window.HKU_DATA = {"meta":{"source":"https://dms.mech.hku.hk/dms2/projectList/displayProjectList (Project List all)","acadYear":"2026-27","applicationWindow":"2026-09-05 00:00 ~ 2026-09-08 23:59","records":391,"note":"本地快照仅供浏览参考；中文字段为机器翻译；申请请回学校系统。","buckets":[{"k":"AI","label":"人工智能 / AI","count":100},{"k":"CS","label":"计算机与软件 / Computing","count":56},{"k":"ROB","label":"机器人 / Robotics","count":115},{"k":"AERO","label":"无人机与航空 / UAV & Aerospace","count":61},{"k":"ELE","label":"电子与传感 / Electronics & Sensing","count":48},{"k":"CTRL","label":"控制与自动化 / Control & Automation","count":22},{"k":"MECH","label":"机械与力学 / Mechanics","count":29},{"k":"DES","label":"设计与制造 / Design & Manufacturing","count":82},{"k":"MAT","label":"材料 / Materials","count":71},{"k":"ENE","label":"能源与热流体 / Energy & Thermofluids","count":47},{"k":"FLU","label":"流体与海洋 / Fluids & Ocean","count":21},{"k":"BIO","label":"生物医学与健康 / Biomedical & Healthcare","count":36},{"k":"MGT","label":"管理与教育 / Management & Education","count":20},{"k":"OTH","label":"其它 / Others","count":20}]},"projects":[{"ref":"2026-27-XIDAO-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"吸光油墨（消光涂料）多领域应用市场调研","titleEn":"Market Research on Multi-field Applications of Light Absorbing Ink (Matting Paint)","areaZh":"电子；材料；技术市场分析","areaEn":"Electronics; Materials; Technology Market Analysis","companyZh":"深圳西岛科技有限公司","companyEn":"Shenzhen Xidao Technologies Co., Ltd.","companySupervisor":"Li  CAO","univSupName":"Professor XU Lizhi","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"1 Project Overview\nThis project centers on the company’s Xiguang Ink (matting paint) and conducts market research covering four major sectors: automotive electronics, medical devices, optical components and aerospace. The research results will support the company’s customer expansion, business development and the commercialization of new application scenarios. As a functional optical light-absorbing coating with excellent absorption capacity for visible and infrared light, Xiguang Ink acts as the core product of the enterprise’s optoelectronic new material division.\n2 Research Content and Scope\nThe research shall be carried out in four core phases under the guidance of project supervisors: \na. Sorting of application scenarios: Sort out components and terminal products applicable to Xiguang Ink in various target fields, and clarify the optical indicators, compatible substrates and process adaptation limits of products; \nb. Research on competitive product solutions: Investigate mainstream light-absorbing and matte coating solutions as well as similar optical coating products on the market, and sort out the technical development paths, performance shortcomings and common industrial pain points of each type of solution; \nc. Inventory of market competitors: Sort out core manufacturers of functional optical light-absorbing coatings in the industry, and summarize each manufacturer’s product portfolio, customer positioning and market layout characteristics; \nd. Research and judgment of industry trends: Focus on various segmented application tracks of matte coatings, analyze the current status, demand characteristics and sales prospects of each segmented market, and study and judge the overall market development potential.\nQuantitative data such as market volume and profit margin of segmented sectors are not mandatory research items, and students may supplement them selectively according to the availability of public materials. The company will provide complete product brochures and technical documents throughout the project, and internal technical specialists are available for professional consultation at any time.\n3 Required Competencies for Students\nTo ensure smooth research progress and deliver satisfactory practical outcomes, participating students are expected to meet the following basic requirements:\na. Possess basic knowledge and research interest in optical components, automotive electronics and functional coatings; \nb. Master fundamental methods for Chinese and English industrial literature retrieval, information integration and logical analysis, capable of independently collecting, summarizing and organizing public industrial data; \nc. Have standard English writing proficiency to independently compose well-structured and logically coherent English market research reports; \nd. Demonstrate strong self-motivation and teamwork awareness, able to solve various research problems independently under supervisors’ guidance.\n4 Expected Deliverables\nA complete English market research report shall be submitted. The report shall fully cover product introduction, full-scenario application sorting, comparative analysis of competing products, industry competition landscape and medium-to-long-term development trends. It shall feature detailed content and standardized frameworks, with both academic rigor and practical industrial reference value.\n5 On-site Attendance Arrangement\nThe project is primarily conducted remotely online, with three mandatory on-site sessions scheduled throughout the cycle:\na. Opening meeting at the project initiation stage to clarify research frameworks and data submission requirements;\nb. Mid-term review meeting to synchronize research progress and address challenges;\nc. Final presentation for deliverable evaluation and acceptance upon project completion.\nAll on-site meetings are held on the 4th Floor, Block A, Jiada R&D Building. Daily inquiries can be resolved instantly via online meetings.\n6 Detailed Product Information & Reference Materials\n6.1 Matting and Light Absorption Mechanism of the Product\nXiguang Ink is a functional matting coating designed to suppress stray light in optical systems, which can absorb both visible and infrared light simultaneously for matte surface treatment of optical baffles, lens barrels, light shields and other components. Formulated with eco-friendly special resin and low-hazard solvents, the coating is non-toxic and harmless, and delivers outstanding resistance to high temperature, ultraviolet radiation and chemical corrosion. Compatible with automated spray mass production, it forms a delicate matte black film with strong adhesion to various substrates after curing. The overall processing workflow is simple with high production efficiency, meeting the demands of mass industrial manufacturing.\nThe coating is compounded with matting powder and carbon black light-absorbing fillers. After being sprayed onto substrates to form a uniform wet film, the coating shrinks gradually as solvents evaporate continuously, pushing matting particles to accumulate on the surface and forming a dry film with tiny uneven microstructures upon full curing. When light hits the uneven micro-surface, directional specular reflection is eliminated, and light scatters in all directions to achieve a high-matte appearance. Combined with the visible and infrared light absorption effect of carbon black fillers, the two mechanisms work synergistically to drastically reduce the overall reflectivity of the coating, suppress stray light interference inside optical equipment, and guarantee the working precision of imaging and ranging devices.\n6.2 Formulation Classification and Standard Construction Specifications\nThe product has two mature formulation systems: water-based and oil-based, with obvious differences in diluents, curing processes and construction parameters. The oil-based Xiguang Ink adopts propylene glycol methyl ether as diluent and requires high-temperature baking at 150 °C for 20 minutes to complete curing; the water-based version is diluted with pure water and fully cured after low-temperature baking at 75 °C for 30 minutes.\nGeneral construction requirements are listed below for reference: The mixed coating must be filtered through a 100-mesh screen before spraying with a 1.5-caliber spray gun, and the dry film thickness can be controlled within the range of 40–170 μm. The blended coating must be used within 2 to 3 hours at 25 °C ambient temperature to avoid uneven spraying caused by powder sedimentation. Workpieces after spraying shall be placed at room temperature for 48 hours before reliability testing. For urgent inspection, place the workpieces in an oven at 60°C for 3 hours to accelerate film stabilization.\n6.3 Optical, Physical, Chemical and Environmental Protection Performance\nAll product performances are verified by spectrophotometer tests and full-set reliability tests conducted by third-party laboratories. Key indicators are listed as follows: \na. Optical Reflectance and Gloss Performance: For the visible light band of 400–780 nm, the reflectance of all models is stably controlled between 1% and 2.2%. The infrared band (905–1550 nm) reflectance of lidar-specialized models is ≤1.8%, and the gloss of general models reaches as low as 0.1 when measured at an 85° angle. Different product categories adopt inconsistent gloss test standards: 80° test angle for lidar products and 60° test angle for LED mask products, which constitutes an irregularity in parameter labeling. \nb. Coating Adhesion Performance: In accordance with the ISO cross-hatch test standard, a 100-square grid is scratched on the substrate surface, and no coating peeling occurs after rapid tearing with 3M tape, reaching Grade 5B, the highest qualified grade in the industry. \nc. Environmental Resistance: The coating has passed a series of rigorous tests including long-term high-temperature baking at 200 °C, low-temperature storage at -40 °C, high-temperature storage at 80 °C, QUV UV aging cycles, high temperature & high humidity (70 °C / 90% RH) exposure for 72 hours, 48-hour salt spray test, and thermal shock cycling between -40 °C and 80 °C. No defects such as blistering, cracking, peeling or discoloration appear after all tests.\nd. Environmental Compliance Indicators: All raw materials comply with RoHS 2.0 control standards, and the formulation contains no heavy metal hazardous fillers, making it suitable for application scenarios with strict environmental requirements such as medical and automotive industries.\n6.4 Substrate Compatibility and Product Application Advantages\nThis coating is compatible with mainstream substrates in the optical industry including ABS, ABS+PC plastics, aluminum, tinplate and composite panels, and the film thickness can be flexibly adjusted according to component structures, delivering wide processing adaptability. Compared with conventional black covering paint on the market, Xiguang Ink relies on special optical powder to achieve low reflectivity without generating light refraction spots, thus stably securing the measurement precision of optical equipment. The cured coating features high hardness and long-lasting adhesion, resisting paint peeling, whitening and other quality defects during component assembly and long-term service.\nBenefiting from its comprehensive performance advantages, the ink has been mass-matched with optical components such as automotive HUD light shields, lidars, LED masks, optical brackets and lens barrel inner walls, covering multiple fields including automotive electronics, medical devices, aerospace optics and 5G optoelectronics.","descZh":"1 项目概述\n本项目围绕公司的 Xiguang Ink（消光漆）开展，覆盖汽车电子、医疗器械、光学元件与航空航天四大领域进行市场调研。调研成果将支撑公司的客户拓展、业务发展以及新应用场景的商业化落地。作为一款对可见光与红外光具有优异吸收能力的功能性光学吸光涂层，Xiguang Ink 是企业光电新材料事业部的核心产品。\n2 调研内容与范围\n调研将在项目导师的指导下，按四个核心阶段开展：\na. 应用场景梳理：梳理 Xiguang Ink 在各目标领域可适用的元器件与终端产品，明确产品的光学指标、兼容基材与工艺适配边界；\nb. 竞品方案调研：调研市场上主流的吸光、消光涂层方案及同类光学涂层产品，梳理各类方案的技术发展路径、性能短板与行业普遍痛点；\nc. 市场竞争对手盘点：梳理行业内功能性光学吸光涂层的核心厂商，归纳各厂商的产品组合、客户定位与市场布局特点；\nd. 行业趋势研判：聚焦消光涂层的各细分应用赛道，分析各细分市场的现状、需求特征与销售前景，研判整体市场的发展潜力。\n细分领域的市场规模、利润率等定量数据为非必研项，学生可根据公开资料的可获得性酌情补充。项目期间公司将提供完整的产品手册与技术文档，并有内部技术专家随时提供专业咨询。\n3 对学生能力的要求\n为确保调研顺利推进并取得满意的实践成果，参与学生应满足以下基本要求：\na. 对光学元件、汽车电子及功能性涂层具备基础知识与研究兴趣；\nb. 掌握中英文行业文献检索、信息整合与逻辑分析的基本方法，能独立搜集、归纳、整理公开行业数据；\nc. 具备规范的英文写作能力，能独立撰写结构完整、逻辑连贯的英文市场调研报告；\nd. 具备较强的自驱力与团队协作意识，能在导师指导下独立解决各类调研问题。\n4 预期交付物\n需提交一份完整的英文市场调研报告。报告须全面涵盖产品介绍、全场景应用梳理、竞品对比分析、行业竞争格局以及中长期发展趋势，内容详实、框架规范，兼具学术严谨性与行业实用参考价值。\n5 现场到场安排\n本项目以线上远程开展为主，整个周期内安排三次必须到场的现场会议：\na. 项目启动阶段的开题会议，明确调研框架与资料提交要求；\nb. 中期评审会议，同步调研进展并解决遇到的困难；\nc. 项目结束时的结题汇报，进行成果评估与验收。\n所有现场会议均在嘉达研发大厦（Jiada R&D Building）A座4楼举行。日常问题可通过线上会议即时沟通解决。\n6 产品详情与参考资料\n6.1 产品的消光与吸光机理\nXiguang Ink 是一款用于抑制光学系统杂散光的功能性消光涂层，可同时吸收可见光与红外光，用于光学遮光罩、镜筒、遮光片等元器件的消光表面处理。该涂层采用环保型特种树脂与低危害溶剂配制，无毒无害，并具备出色的耐高温、耐紫外辐射与耐化学腐蚀性能。可与自动化喷涂量产工艺兼容，固化后形成细腻的哑光黑膜，对多种基材具有强附着力。整体加工流程简单、生产效率高，满足工业化大批量制造的需求。\n该涂层由消光粉与炭黑吸光填料复合配制。喷涂到基材上形成均匀湿膜后，随着溶剂不断挥发，涂层逐渐收缩，推动消光颗粒在表面聚集，完全固化后形成具有细微凹凸微观结构的干膜。当光线照射到凹凸的微观表面时，定向的镜面反射被消除，光线向各个方向散射，实现高消光外观。结合炭黑填料对可见光与红外光的吸收作用，两种机理协同作用，大幅降低涂层整体反射率，抑制光学设备内部的杂散光干扰，保障成像与测距设备的工作精度。\n6.2 配方分类与标准施工规范\n该产品拥有水性、油性两套成熟配方体系，在稀释剂、固化工艺与施工参数上存在明显差异。油性 Xiguang Ink 采用丙二醇甲醚作为稀释剂，需在 150°C 高温烘烤 20 分钟完成固化；水性版本以纯水稀释，在 75°C 低温烘烤 30 分钟后完全固化。\n一般施工要求列举如下以供参考：混合后的涂料须先经 100 目筛网过滤，再用 1.5 口径喷枪喷涂，干膜厚度可控制在 40–170 μm 范围内。调配好的涂料在 25°C 环境温度下须在 2 至 3 小时内用完，以免粉末沉降导致喷涂不均。喷涂后的工件应常温放置 48 小时再进行可靠性测试。如需紧急检验，可将工件置于 60°C 烘箱中 3 小时，加速漆膜稳定。\n6.3 光学、物理、化学与环保性能\n所有产品性能均经分光光度计测试及第三方实验室的全套可靠性测试验证。关键指标列举如下：\na. 光学反射率与光泽度性能：在 400–780 nm 可见光波段，各型号反射率稳定控制在 1% 至 2.2% 之间。激光雷达专用型号的红外波段（905–1550 nm）反射率 ≤1.8%，一般型号在 85° 角测量时光泽度低至 0.1。不同产品类别采用不一致的光泽度测试标准：激光雷达产品采用 80° 测试角，LED 掩膜产品采用 60° 测试角，这构成参数标注上的一处不规范。\nb. 涂层附着力性能：依据 ISO 百格（划格）测试标准，在基材表面划出 100 格方格，用 3M 胶带快速撕拉后涂层无剥落，达到行业最高合格等级 5B。\nc. 耐环境性能：涂层已通过一系列严苛测试，包括 200°C 长期高温烘烤、-40°C 低温存储、80°C 高温存储、QUV 紫外老化循环、高温高湿（70°C / 90% RH）暴露 72 小时、48 小时盐雾测试，以及 -40°C 至 80°C 之间的冷热冲击循环。所有测试后均无起泡、开裂、剥落或变色等缺陷。\nd. 环保合规指标：所有原材料符合 RoHS 2.0 管控标准，配方中不含重金属有害填料，适用于医疗、汽车等对环保要求严格的行业应用场景。\n6.4 基材兼容性与产品应用优势\n该涂层兼容光学行业主流基材，包括 ABS、ABS+PC 塑料、铝、马口铁及复合板材，膜厚可根据元器件结构灵活调整，具有广泛的加工适应性。与市面上常规的黑色覆盖漆相比，Xiguang Ink 依靠特殊光学粉实现低反射率，且不产生折光斑点，从而稳定保障光学设备的测量精度。固化后的涂层硬度高、附着力持久，能抵抗元器件装配及长期使用过程中的掉漆、发白等质量缺陷。\n凭借全面的性能优势，该油墨已与汽车 HUD 遮光罩、激光雷达、LED 掩膜、光学支架、镜筒内壁等光学元器件批量配套，覆盖汽车电子、医疗器械、航空航天光学及 5G 光电等多个领域。","cats":["ELE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-NANJING-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"氢燃料电池与锂电池混合动力无人机控制设计与能量管理","titleEn":"Hydrogen Fuel Cell and Lithium Battery Hybrid Power UAV Control Design and Energy Management","areaZh":"控制系统；能量管理；无人机（UAV）；机电一体化","areaEn":"Control Systems; Energy Management; Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs); Mechatronics","companyZh":"南京奥宁数据科技有限公司","companyEn":"Nanjing Oning Data Technology Co., LTD","companySupervisor":"CAI Chenxiao","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Background and Objectives:\nWith the rapid development of the low-altitude economy, heavy-payload and long-endurance industrial Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) are in high demand. Proton Exchange Membrane Fuel Cells (PEMFC) paired with lithium batteries offer a promising hybrid power solution to overcome the endurance bottlenecks of traditional battery-powered UAVs. However, the Hydrogen-Electric Hybrid Power System is a highly complex, multi-physics coupled system. The delayed dynamic response of the fuel cell during aggressive flight maneuvers can easily cause \"fuel starvation\" and irreversible stack damage. This project, derived from an ongoing industrial R&D program targeting 5kg to 30kg payload hydrogen-powered UAVs (H-05 and H-30 series), aims to develop an intelligent flight control and robust energy management architecture to optimize fuel efficiency, extend battery/fuel cell lifespan, and ensure flight stability under dynamic payload variations. \nProject Tasks:\n1. Establish a control-oriented dynamic model of the PEMFC and Lithium-ion battery hybrid power system.\n2. Analyze the 6-DOF flight dynamics of a multi-rotor UAV, considering the time-varying mass and center-of-gravity shifts due to continuous hydrogen consumption.\n3. Design an advanced Energy Management System (EMS) (e.g., using Model Predictive Control or equivalent algorithms) to optimally distribute power demands between the fuel cell and the lithium battery during various flight phases (take-off, hovering, maneuvering).\n4. Conduct Software-in-the-Loop (SIL) simulations using MATLAB/Simulink to validate the control logic and energy efficiency.\nPreferred Skills:\n1. Solid foundation in Control Theory (e.g., Robust Control, MPC, PID).\n2. Proficiency in MATLAB/Simulink modeling and simulation.\n3. Basic understanding of UAV flight dynamics and electrochemistry (fuel cells/batteries).\n4. Programming skills in C/C++ or Python are highly desirable.\nExpected Outcome:\n1. A comprehensive simulation model of the hybrid-powered UAV.\n2. A validated Energy Management Strategy and control algorithm demonstrating improved fuel efficiency and stable voltage bus tracking.\n3. A complete MSc dissertation detailing the modeling, controller design, and simulation results.\n4. (Optional) Potential co-authorship on a conference/journal paper.\nOnsite Frequency:\n1. Flexible. Weekly online meetings are required.\n2. Onsite visits to the company's R&D lab for hardware testing or data collection are highly welcomed but optional (1-2 times per month, subject to the student's availability and location).","descZh":"背景与目标：随着低空经济快速发展，大载荷、长航时的工业无人机（UAV）需求旺盛。质子交换膜燃料电池（PEMFC）与锂电池相结合，为克服传统电池无人机续航瓶颈提供了有前景的混合动力方案。然而，氢电混合动力系统是高度复杂、多物理场耦合的系统。激烈飞行机动中燃料电池的延迟动态响应容易造成\"燃料饥饿\"并导致电堆不可逆损坏。本项目源自一项面向 5kg~30kg 载荷氢动力无人机（H-05 与 H-30 系列）的在研工业研发计划，旨在开发智能飞行控制与稳健能量管理架构，以优化燃料效率、延长电池/燃料电池寿命，并在动态载荷变化下确保飞行稳定。\n项目任务：\n1. 建立 PEMFC 与锂离子电池混合动力系统的面向控制动态模型；\n2. 分析多旋翼无人机的六自由度飞行动力学，考虑连续氢气消耗引起的时变质量与质心偏移；\n3. 设计先进的能量管理系统（EMS）（如采用模型预测控制或等效算法），在起飞、悬停、机动等各飞行阶段最优分配燃料电池与锂电池之间的功率需求；\n4. 使用 MATLAB/Simulink 开展软件在环（SIL）仿真，验证控制逻辑与能量效率。\n偏好技能：\n1. 扎实的控制理论基础（如鲁棒控制、MPC、PID）；\n2. 熟练进行 MATLAB/Simulink 建模与仿真；\n3. 基本理解无人机飞行动力学与电化学（燃料电池/电池）；\n4. 具备 C/C++ 或 Python 编程能力者优先。\n预期成果：\n1. 完整的混合动力无人机仿真模型；\n2. 经过验证的能量管理策略与控制算法，展示燃油效率提升与稳定电压母线跟踪；\n3. 完整硕士论文，详述建模、控制器设计与仿真结果；\n4.（可选）有机会共同署名会议/期刊论文。\n现场频率：\n1. 灵活，每周线上会议为必需；\n2. 强烈欢迎但不强制前往公司研发实验室进行硬件测试或数据采集（每月 1-2 次，视学生时间与地点而定）。","cats":["AERO","CTRL","ENE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-NANJING-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"100kg级重载无人机多冗余容错飞行控制与可靠通信系统","titleEn":"Multi-Redundancy Fault-Tolerant Flight Control and Reliable Communication System for 100kg-Class Heavy-Lift UAVs","areaZh":"容错控制；多传感器融合；通信网络；无人机（UAV）","areaEn":"Fault-Tolerant Control; Multi-Sensor Fusion; Communication Networks; Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs)","companyZh":"南京奥宁数据科技有限公司","companyEn":"Nanjing Oning Data Technology Co., LTD","companySupervisor":"CAI Chenxiao","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Background and Objectives:\nThe demand for 100kg-class heavy-lift UAVs is growing rapidly in fields such as aerial logistics, emergency rescue, and agricultural spraying. Unlike light commercial drones, heavy-lift UAVs pose severe safety risks in the event of system failures. Therefore, a highly reliable flight control system with multi-redundancy (sensors and actuators) and a robust communication link are critical to ensuring flight safety. This project is based on an ongoing Proof-of-Concept (PoC) industrial program targeting 100kg-class UAVs. The objective is to design, simulate, and validate a multi-redundancy fault-tolerant control (FTC) strategy and evaluate the reliability of the communication system under complex low-altitude environments (e.g., analyzing Channel State Information - CSI and Signal-to-Noise Ratio - SNR). \nProject Tasks:\n1. Dynamic Modeling: Establish a 6-DOF nonlinear dynamic model for the 100kg-class multi-rotor UAV, considering heavy payload swing effects and actuator dynamic responses.\n2. Multi-Sensor Fusion & Fault Detection: Design a state estimator combining redundant sensors (e.g., dual/triple IMU, GNSS) to detect and isolate sensor faults in real-time.\n3. Fault-Tolerant Control (FTC) Design: Develop an active fault-tolerant flight controller that can automatically reallocate control efforts among redundant actuators when partial motor/propeller failures occur, ensuring the heavy-lift UAV maintains stability and safely lands.\n4. Communication Reliability Analysis: Evaluate the UAV's communication link robustness by analyzing dynamic channel metrics (such as CSI) under low-altitude multi-path interference.\n5. Simulation & Validation: Validate the proposed FTC algorithms using MATLAB/Simulink and/or a Software-in-the-Loop (SIL) environment.\nPreferred Skills:\n1. Strong background in Modern Control Theory (e.g., Robust Control, Fault-Tolerant Control, Kalman Filtering).\n2. Proficiency in MATLAB/Simulink for flight dynamics modeling.\n3. Basic understanding of signal processing, wireless communications, or multi-sensor data fusion.\n4. Experience with Pixhawk/PX4 or ROS is a plus but not strictly required.\nExpected Outcome:\n1. A robust fault-tolerant control algorithm capable of handling sensor/actuator failures for heavy-lift UAVs.\n2. A comprehensive simulation demonstrating the stability and redundancy management of the UAV.\n3. A complete MSc dissertation summarizing the modeling, controller design, and simulation results.\n4. Insightful analysis of the low-altitude communication link reliability for industrial applications.\nOnsite Frequency:\n1. Flexible. Weekly online progress meetings are expected.\n2. Onsite visits to the company's R&D facilities for observing heavy-lift UAV flight tests or hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) simulations are highly welcomed but optional (1-2 times per month, depending on student availability).","descZh":"背景与目标：\n100 公斤级大载重无人机在航空物流、应急救援与农业喷洒等领域的需求正在快速增长。与轻型消费级无人机不同，大载重无人机一旦发生系统故障将带来严重的安全风险。因此，具备多重冗余（传感器与执行器）的高可靠性飞行控制系统以及稳健的通信链路，对于保障飞行安全至关重要。本项目基于一项正在进行的、面向 100 公斤级无人机的概念验证（PoC）工业项目。目标是设计、仿真并验证一种多重冗余容错控制（FTC）策略，并评估通信系统在复杂低空环境下的可靠性（例如分析信道状态信息 CSI 与信噪比 SNR）。\n项目任务：\n1. 动力学建模：为 100 公斤级多旋翼无人机建立 6 自由度（6-DOF）非线性动力学模型，考虑重载吊挂摆动效应与执行器动态响应。\n2. 多传感器融合与故障检测：设计一个融合冗余传感器（如双/三 IMU、GNSS）的状态估计器，实时检测并隔离传感器故障。\n3. 容错控制（FTC）设计：开发一种主动容错飞行控制器，能够在部分电机/螺旋桨失效时自动在冗余执行器之间重新分配控制量，确保大载重无人机保持稳定并安全着陆。\n4. 通信可靠性分析：通过分析低空多径干扰下的动态信道指标（如 CSI），评估无人机通信链路的稳健性。\n5. 仿真与验证：使用 MATLAB/Simulink 和/或软件在环（SIL）环境验证所提出的 FTC 算法。\n优先技能：\n1. 扎实的现代控制理论基础（如鲁棒控制、容错控制、卡尔曼滤波）。\n2. 熟练使用 MATLAB/Simulink 进行飞行动力学建模。\n3. 对信号处理、无线通信或多传感器数据融合有基本了解。\n4. 具有 Pixhawk/PX4 或 ROS 经验者优先，但不作硬性要求。\n预期成果：\n1. 一种能够应对大载重无人机传感器/执行器故障的稳健容错控制算法。\n2. 一套展示无人机稳定性与冗余管理的完整仿真。\n3. 一篇完整的硕士论文，总结建模、控制器设计与仿真结果。\n4. 对工业应用中低空通信链路可靠性的深入分析。\n现场频次：\n1. 灵活安排。预计每周举行线上进展会议。\n2. 非常欢迎到公司研发设施现场观摩大载重无人机飞行测试或硬件在环（HIL）仿真（每月 1–2 次，视学生时间而定），但属可选。","cats":["AERO","CTRL","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-AGILIS-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"基于机器学习的手术机器人任务分类","titleEn":"Surgical Robot Task Classification Using Machine Learning","areaZh":"手术机器人；人工智能（AI）","areaEn":"Surgical Robotics; Artificial Intelligent (AI)","companyZh":"Agilis Robotics Limited","companyEn":"Agilis Robotics Limited","companySupervisor":"Ka-Wai KWOK","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project Description:\nThe project focuses on training and testing a deep learning model to automatically classify intraoperative actions and tasks in pre-recorded endoscopic videos. Tasks include but not limited to endoscope navigation, tissue grasping, dissection, lens cleaning, hemostasis, and specimen extraction. The video datasets were recorded in Minimally Invasive Surgical procedures performed using highly miniaturized and flexible robotic instruments developed by Agilis Robotics. \nResponsibilities: \n• Annotate endoscopic surgical videos to build ground-truth datasets for model training. \n• Test and evaluate model performance. \n• Optimize model accuracy by adjusting model training strategies. \n• Potentially incorporate surgical robot task classification into Agilis Robotics’s Intilume System. \nRequirement: \n• Basic programming skills (e.g. Python, C++, MATLAB). \n• Passionate about machine learning or surgical robotics. \n• Eagerness to solve problems and willingness to learn. \n• Able to work independently and as part of a team. \n• Able to work onsite at company facilities 8 to 16 hours per week. \n• Not bothered by the sight of blood or animal/human tissues. \nExpected Outcome: \n• An algorithm capable of classifying surgical robot actions and tasks from endoscopic video data.","descZh":"项目描述：本项目聚焦于训练与测试一个深度学习模型，用于自动分类预录内镜视频中的术中动作与任务。任务包括（但不限于）内镜导航、组织抓取、剥离、镜头清洗、止血与标本取出。视频数据集来自使用 Agilis Robotics 开发的高度微型化、柔性机器人器械实施的微创手术。\n职责：\n• 对内镜手术视频进行标注，构建用于模型训练的真值数据集；\n• 测试与评估模型性能；\n• 通过调整模型训练策略优化模型精度；\n• 有可能将手术机器人任务分类集成到 Agilis Robotics 的 Intilume 系统。\n要求：\n• 基础编程能力（如 Python、C++、MATLAB）；\n• 对机器学习或手术机器人有热情；\n• 乐于解决问题、愿意学习；\n• 能独立工作并参与团队协作；\n• 能每周在公司现场工作 8~16 小时；\n• 不惧看到血液或动物/人体组织。\n预期成果：一个能从内镜视频数据中分类手术机器人动作与任务的算法。","cats":["AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-AGILIS-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"腔内手术机器人模拟器开发","titleEn":"Endoluminal Surgical Robot Simulator Development","areaZh":"手术机器人；虚拟模拟器","areaEn":"Surgical Robotics; Virtual Simulator","companyZh":"Agilis Robotics Limited","companyEn":"Agilis Robotics Limited","companySupervisor":"Ka-Wai KWOK","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project Description:\nThe aim of the project is to develop a Unity-based endoluminal surgical robotic simulation platform for Agilis Robotics’s Intilume system. There are two main modules. First, a pair of handheld controllers is to be fabricated. Using camera vision to estimate the poses of the controllers, Master-Slave control is achieved to actuate a virtual robotic arm in simulation. Second, a physical endoscope holder is to be integrated into the simulation. Pose of the holder is then estimated with encoders, enabling a simulation of endoscopic view adjustments during a surgery. \nResponsibilities: \n• Design tracking markers and implement computer vision algorithms. \n• Develop surgical simulation scenes in Unity. \n• Extract and process the controller live pose data and feed it into the simulation. \n• Extract and process the encoder data from a physical endoscope holder and feed it into the simulation. \nRequirements: \n• Foundation in Forward and Inverse Kinematics. \n• Basic programming skills (e.g. Python, C++, MATLAB). \n• Passionate about computer vision and real-time tracking systems. \n• Able to work independently and as part of a team. \n• Eagerness to solve problems and willingness to learn. \n• Able to work onsite at company facilities 8 to 16 hours per week. \n• Not bothered by the sight of blood or animal/human tissues. \nExpected Outcome: \n• A computer vision-based tracking algorithm that captures 6-DoF poses of the controllers. \n• Virtual robotic arm actuation via Master-Slave control. \n• A physical endoscope holder equipped with encoder or other tracking devices. \n• Simulation of endoscopic view adjustments during a surgery.","descZh":"项目描述：本项目旨在为 Agilis Robotics 的 Intilume 系统开发一套基于 Unity 的腔内手术机器人仿真平台，包含两大模块。其一，制作一对手持控制器；利用相机视觉估计控制器位姿，实现主从控制以驱动仿真中的虚拟机械臂。其二，将实体内窥镜支架集成到仿真中；通过编码器估计支架位姿，实现手术中内镜视角调整的仿真。\n职责：\n• 设计跟踪标记并实现计算机视觉算法；\n• 在 Unity 中开发手术仿真场景；\n• 提取并处理控制器实时位姿数据并输入仿真；\n• 提取并处理实体内窥镜支架的编码器数据并输入仿真。\n要求：\n• 具备正运动学与逆运动学基础；\n• 基础编程能力（如 Python、C++、MATLAB）；\n• 对计算机视觉与实时跟踪系统有热情；\n• 能独立工作并参与团队协作；\n• 乐于解决问题、愿意学习；\n• 能每周在公司现场工作 8~16 小时；\n• 不惧看到血液或动物/人体组织。\n预期成果：\n• 能捕获控制器六自由度位姿的计算机视觉跟踪算法；\n• 通过主从控制实现虚拟机械臂驱动；\n• 配备编码器或其他跟踪装置的实体内窥镜支架；\n• 手术中内镜视角调整的仿真。","cats":["CS","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DKCHO-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向定制化精准口腔手术的任务自主机器人注射系统：基于体模的初步研究","titleEn":"Task-Autonomous Robotic Injection System for Customised Accurate Oral Surgery: A Phantom-Based Pilot","areaZh":"机器人系统；牙科手术；物理智能（Physical-AI）","areaEn":"Robotic system; Dental Surgery; Physical-AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor Kiho CHO","moderator":"Professor SHIN Dong-myeong","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-03 18:09:32","descriptionText":"The primary objectives of the study are:\n1. To develop and validate an integrated robotic pipeline for scan-guided, task-autonomous deposition of flowable materials into the medical/dental surgical area, encompassing scanning, target identification, toolpath generation, and robotic execution.\n2. To conduct a comparative phantom-based pilot study evaluating the accuracy and consistency of robotic deposition against the manual freehand clinical standard.\nHardware setup: The robotic system comprised a JAKA Zu5 collaborative 6-axis robot, MUSASHI SuperHiJet precision dispenser, 3D surface scanner, and high accuracy camera vision system. \nSoftware setup: In the preparation stage, the lab site (phantom, marker, and calibration plot) is scanned using the intraoral 3D scanner, then an STL mesh is created using the MEDIT Link software. The marker is used to compute the transformation matrix between the scan coordinate system and the robot coordinate system. The calibration block is a straight line and is used to calibrate the position of the robot arm with a laser pointer prior to any trial. \nData communication: The generated path waypoints are transformed from the scan coordinate system or the robot coordinate system using the transformation matrix. Communication with the robot was achieved via TCP/IP.","descZh":"本研究的主要目标：\n1. 开发并验证一条集成的机器人流程，用于在扫描引导下、以任务自主方式将可流动材料沉积到医疗/牙科手术区域，涵盖扫描、目标识别、工具路径生成与机器人执行；\n2. 开展一项基于模型（phantom）的对比先导研究，评估机器人沉积相对于手动徒手临床金标准的精度与一致性。\n硬件配置：机器人系统由 JAKA Zu5 协作式六轴机器人、MUSASHI SuperHiJet 精密点胶机、三维表面扫描仪与高精度相机视觉系统组成。\n软件配置：准备阶段使用口内 3D 扫描仪扫描实验场地（模型、标记点与标定图），随后使用 MEDIT Link 软件生成 STL 网格。标记点用于计算扫描坐标系与机器人坐标系之间的变换矩阵。标定块为一条直线，用于在任何试验前借助激光笔标定机械臂的位置。\n数据通信：生成的路径航点通过变换矩阵从扫描坐标系转换到机器人坐标系。与机器人的通信通过 TCP/IP 实现。","cats":["BIO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DKCHO-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"配备纳米金刚石光学应变传感器的3D打印智能正畸矫治器","titleEn":"3D-Printed Smart Orthodontic Appliance Equipped with Nanodiamond Optical Strain Sensor","areaZh":"光学力传感器；FEA；正畸学","areaEn":"Optical force sensor; FEA; Orthodontics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor Kiho CHO","moderator":"Professor SHIN Dong-myeong","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-03 18:09:20","descriptionText":"Objectives: This study aims to develop and characterize a novel 3D-printed clear resin equipped with surface-modified fluorescent nanodiamonds (SA-FNDs) dot arrays for optical strain detection and real-time force monitoring while maintaining mechanical performance and biocompatibility. This study develops a feasible optical strain detection method for real-time orthodontic force monitoring. The integration of SA-FNDs into 3D-printed clear resin creates a biocompatible material with combined mechanical reinforcement and optical fluorescence detecting capability. The technology offers a promising optical, wireless, and biocompatible approach for real-time orthodontic force monitoring, with potential to improve treatment predictability and clinical outcomes.\nMethods: Fluorescent nanodiamonds are surface-modified through mixed-acid oxidation and silane grafting, and SA-FNDs are characterized by FTIR, XRD, SEM, TEM, EDX, sedimentation testing, and fluorescence spectroscopy. The resin containing SA-FNDs is printed with designed dot array structure to develop the optical micro-force sensor. Displacement of the printed dots on the sensor when external force is applied is tracked using ImageJ and digital image correlation system. The accurately detected displacement is used to calculate the strain and stress which are used to calculate the applied force and direction. \nData acquisition: The 3D-printed SA-FND dot arrays enabled accurate non-contact displacement tracking under tensile loading, achieving a strong linear correlation between optically calculated force and machine load force suitable for orthodontic force monitoring. Experimental result will be compared with computation simulation studies (e.g.: FEA)","descZh":"目标：本研究旨在开发并表征一种新型 3D 打印透明树脂——其中嵌入经表面修饰的荧光纳米金刚石（SA-FND）点阵，用于光学应变检测与实时力监测，同时保持力学性能与生物相容性。研究开发一种可行的光学应变检测方法，用于实时正畸力监测。将 SA-FND 集成到 3D 打印透明树脂中，可形成兼具力学增强与光学荧光检测能力的生物相容材料。该技术为正畸力实时监测提供一种有前景的光学、无线、生物相容途径，有望提高治疗可预测性与临床效果。\n方法：荧光纳米金刚石经混酸氧化与硅烷接枝进行表面修饰；SA-FND 采用 FTIR、XRD、SEM、TEM、EDX、沉降测试与荧光光谱进行表征。含 SA-FND 的树脂以设计的点阵结构打印，开发光学微力传感器。施加外力时传感器上打印点阵的位移利用 ImageJ 与数字图像相关系统跟踪；精确检测到的位移用于计算应变与应力，进而计算所施加力的大小与方向。\n数据采集：3D 打印的 SA-FND 点阵在拉伸载荷下实现精确的非接触位移跟踪，光学计算力与试验机载荷力之间呈现强线性相关，适用于正畸力监测。实验结果将与计算仿真研究（如 FEA）进行比较。","cats":["BIO","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZHUBH-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于学习动力学的机器人非线性跟踪控制","titleEn":"Nonlinear Tracking Control of Robotics via Learning-Based Dynamics","areaZh":"全驱动系统；基于学习的建模；基于优函数的控制；非线性跟踪控制","areaEn":"Fully Actuated Systems; Learning-Based Modeling; Majorant-Based Control; Nonlinear Tracking Control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. ZHU Bohao","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Description : \n In recent years, the integration of data-driven modeling and\r\nadvanced nonlinear control techniques has emerged as a promising paradigm in\r\nmodern robotics. Traditional model-based control strategies often rely heavily\r\non precise physical modeling, which can be challenging to obtain for complex\r\nrobotic systems with unmodeled dynamics, parameter uncertainties, or complex\r\nphysical interactions. To address this limitation, learning-based approaches,\r\nsuch as deep neural networks, are increasingly utilized to construct accurate\r\nsystem models directly from experimental data. \n However, ensuring stability and precise tracking performance for\r\nlearning-based dynamic models remains a critical challenge. This project aims\r\nto bridge the gap between machine learning modeling and rigorous control\r\ntheory. Students will investigate nonlinear tracking control strategies, specifically\r\non fully actuated control frameworks or majorant-based system approaches, for\r\nrobotic systems whose dynamics are represented by neural network models. By\r\ncombining state-space representations derived from data-driven models with\r\nformal nonlinear control theory, this project seeks to guarantee reference\r\ntracking and system stability under non-ideal modeling conditions. \n The specifications are given as follows: \n 1. \r\n Study neural network\r\narchitectures for dynamic system modeling and construct data-driven\r\nrepresentations for target robotic platforms. \n 2. \r\n Formulate tracking control\r\nstrategies based on fully actuated system approaches or majorant-based\r\ntechniques for the learned neural network models. \n 3. \r\n Validate and evaluate the\r\ntracking performance, robustness, and stability of the proposed control\r\nframework via numerical simulations. \n Pre- and co-requisites : \n 1. \r\n Basic concepts of classical and\r\nmodern control theory (e.g., state-space representation, nonlinear control\r\nbasics) and machine learning fundamentals. \n 2. \r\n Solid programming proficiency\r\nin Python and/or MATLAB/Simulink for modeling and controller implementation. \n Special note: \n Students who have an interest in combining machine learning with\r\ntheoretical control methods, as well as those interested in numerical\r\nsimulation and dynamic modeling of robotic systems, should find this project interesting.","descZh":"描述：近年来，数据驱动建模与先进非线性控制技术的结合已成为现代机器人学的有前景范式。传统基于模型的控制策略往往高度依赖精确物理建模，而对具有未建模动态、参数不确定性或复杂物理交互的机器人系统而言，精确模型难以获得。为克服这一局限，深度神经网络等基于学习的方法越来越多地被用于直接从实验数据构建精确系统模型。\n然而，确保基于学习的动力学模型的稳定性与精确跟踪性能仍是关键挑战。本项目旨在弥合机器学习建模与严格控制理论之间的差距。学生将研究针对动力学由神经网络模型表示的机器人系统的非线性跟踪控制策略，具体聚焦于全驱动控制框架或基于主导系统（majorant-based）的方法。通过将数据驱动模型导出的状态空间表示与形式化非线性控制理论相结合，本项目力求在非理想建模条件下保证参考跟踪与系统稳定性。\n具体要求：\n1. 研究用于动态系统建模的神经网络架构，并为目标机器人平台构建数据驱动表示；\n2. 针对所学的神经网络模型，基于全驱动系统方法或主导（majorant-based）技术构建跟踪控制策略；\n3. 通过数值仿真验证并评估所提控制框架的跟踪性能、鲁棒性与稳定性。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备经典与现代控制理论的基本概念（如状态空间表示、非线性控制基础）及机器学习基础；\n2. 扎实的 Python 和/或 MATLAB/Simulink 编程能力，用于建模与控制器实现。\n特别说明：对将机器学习与理论控制方法结合感兴趣，以及对机器人系统数值仿真与动态建模感兴趣的学生会觉得该项目颇具吸引力。","cats":["CTRL","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZHUBH-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"多智能体系统探索与利用的数据驱动双重控制","titleEn":"Data-Driven Dual Control of Exploration and Exploitation for Multi-Agent Systems","areaZh":"数据驱动控制；探索与利用多智能体系统双重控制；强化学习","areaEn":"Data-Driven Control; Dual Control of Exploration and Exploitation Multi-Agent Systems; Reinforcemen","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. ZHU Bohao","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Description : \n In complex multi-agent systems (MAS), agents must coordinate their\r\nactions to achieve global objectives under high operational uncertainties and\r\nunknown dynamic environments. Traditional control designs often separate system\r\nidentification (exploration) from control execution (exploitation). However,\r\nDual Control of Exploration and Exploitation (DCEE) provides a unified\r\nframework where control actions are explicitly dual-purposed: simultaneously\r\nprobing the environment to learn uncertain dynamics and steering the system\r\ntoward desired target states. \n This project focuses on developing data-driven DCEE strategies for\r\nmulti-agent systems by leveraging state-of-the-art reinforcement learning\r\ntechniques. Students will investigate how learning-based mechanisms can\r\napproximate optimal dual control signals in high-dimensional, uncertain\r\nenvironments where explicit analytical dual controllers are intractable. The\r\ngoal is to formulate a data-driven framework that balances active\r\nparameter/state exploration and cooperative task exploitation, ensuring\r\nefficient learning, dynamic stabilization, and optimal coordination among\r\nmultiple autonomous agents. \n The specifications are given as follows: \n 1. \r\n Study the mathematical\r\nformulations of dual control theory and deep/multi-agent reinforcement learning\r\nalgorithms. \n 2. \r\n Formulate a data-driven DCEE\r\narchitecture for multi-agent dynamic systems to actively balance joint\r\nexploration and exploitation. \n 3. \r\n Construct a multi-agent\r\nsimulation benchmark and evaluate the performance, convergence, and robustness\r\nof the proposed framework against standard baselines. \n Pre- and co-requisites : \n 1. \r\n Basic understanding of\r\nprobability theory, state-space control theory, and reinforcement learning\r\nconcepts. \n 2. \r\n Solid programming proficiency\r\nin Python and/or MATLAB/Simulink. \n Special note: \n Students who are interested in multi-agent learning, theoretical\r\ncontrol frameworks, active exploration, and reinforcement learning applications\r\nshould find this project particularly engaging and challenging.","descZh":"描述：在复杂多智能体系统（MAS）中，智能体必须在高运行不确定性与未知动态环境下协调行动以实现全局目标。传统控制设计常将系统辨识（探索）与控制执行（利用）分开。然而，探索-利用双重控制（DCEE）提供统一框架，其中控制动作具有明确的双重目的：既探测环境以学习不确定动力学，又将系统导向期望的目标状态。\n本项目聚焦于利用最先进的强化学习技术，为多智能体系统开发数据驱动的 DCEE 策略。学生将研究基于学习的机制如何在解析式双重控制器难以处理的、高维不确定环境中近似最优双重控制信号。目标是构建一个数据驱动的框架，平衡主动参数/状态探索与协作任务利用，确保多个自主智能体间的高效学习、动态稳定与最优协调。\n具体要求：\n1. 研究双重控制理论与深度/多智能体强化学习算法的数学表述；\n2. 为多智能体动态系统构建数据驱动的 DCEE 架构，主动平衡联合探索与利用；\n3. 构建多智能体仿真基准，并与标准基线比较，评估所提框架的性能、收敛性与鲁棒性。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 对概率论、状态空间控制理论与强化学习概念有基本理解；\n2. 扎实的 Python 和/或 MATLAB/Simulink 编程能力。\n特别说明：对多智能体学习、理论控制框架、主动探索与强化学习应用感兴趣的学生会觉得该项目尤其有吸引力且富有挑战。","cats":["CTRL","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_CXCAI-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向无人机航拍图像的轻量化开放词汇小目标检测","titleEn":"Lightweight Open-vocabulary Small-object Detection for UAV Aerial Imagery","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"CAI Chenxiao","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nImages captured by Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) typically feature a wide field of view, small object sizes, substantial scale variations, and frequent occlusions. Conventional object detection models are generally limited to categories predefined during training. When previously unseen categories appear in practical applications or detection requirements change, additional annotated data must be collected to retrain the model. Open-vocabulary object detection addresses this issue by establishing correspondences between visual features and textual category descriptions, enabling the detection of novel objects using natural-language prompts. This provides a more flexible perception approach for UAV inspection, search-and-rescue operations, and scene understanding.\nHowever, most existing open-vocabulary detection models are designed for ground-level viewpoints or general-purpose images. When directly applied to UAV aerial imagery, their performance is often severely hindered by insufficient visual information from small objects, dramatic viewpoint differences, and cross-domain feature shifts. Furthermore, large-scale Vision-Language Models (VLMs) often fail to satisfy the real-time requirements of UAV onboard systems due to limited computational resources.\nBased on existing open-vocabulary detection frameworks, this project aims to investigate lightweight vision-language feature alignment and specific detection methods tailored for small objects in aerial imagery. The primary objective is to enhance the detection accuracy of unseen categories while maintaining a compact model size and efficient inference speed.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Configure and reproduce a baseline open-vocabulary object detection model, developing a solid understanding of current image-text feature alignment mechanisms.\n2. Develop and integrate advanced multi-scale feature extraction and cross-modal feature fusion modules to tackle the challenges of small objects and complex backgrounds in UAV imagery.\n3. Train and evaluate the proposed models on public aerial-image datasets (e.g., VisDrone, DOTA, or UAVDT).\n4. Implement model compression techniques, such as lightweight network architectures, knowledge distillation, or model pruning, to reduce computational complexity.\n5. Systematically compare different improvement strategies, evaluating their performance in terms of detection accuracy (mAP), unseen-category recognition capability, and real-time inference efficiency (FPS).\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Academic Background: A solid foundation in machine learning, deep learning, and computer vision, including basic knowledge of Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), Vision Transformers (ViTs), and object detection pipelines.\n2. Programming Skills: Proficiency in Python and the PyTorch framework. Ability to perform data processing, model training, and experimental analysis in a Linux environment.\n3. Experience: Previous experience with YOLO-based detectors, Vision-Language Models (VLMs), or aerial-image processing is highly desirable. Students should possess the capability to utilize GPUs for training and fine-tuning medium-scale deep learning models.","descZh":"项目描述：无人机（UAV）拍摄的图像通常具有宽视场、物体尺寸小、尺度变化大与频繁遮挡等特点。常规目标检测模型一般局限于训练期间预定义的类别。当实际应用中出现未见类别或检测需求变化时，必须采集额外标注数据重新训练模型。开放词汇目标检测通过在视觉特征与文本类别描述之间建立对应关系解决这一问题，可用自然语言提示检测新物体，为无人机巡检、搜救作业与场景理解提供更灵活的感知方式。\n然而，多数现有开放词汇检测模型针对地面视角或通用图像设计。直接应用于无人机航空影像时，其性能常因小物体视觉信息不足、视角差异巨大与跨域特征偏移而严重受限。此外，大规模视觉-语言模型（VLM）因计算资源有限，往往无法满足无人机机载系统的实时性要求。\n基于现有开放词汇检测框架，本项目旨在研究面向航空影像小物体的轻量视觉-语言特征对齐与特定检测方法。主要目标是在保持紧凑模型规模与高效推理速度的同时，提升未见类别的检测精度。\n具体要求：\n1. 配置并复现基线开放词汇目标检测模型，形成对当前图像-文本特征对齐机制的深入理解；\n2. 开发并集成先进多尺度特征提取与跨模态特征融合模块，应对无人机影像中小物体与复杂背景的挑战；\n3. 在公开航空影像数据集（如 VisDrone、DOTA 或 UAVDT）上训练与评估所提模型；\n4. 实现模型压缩技术（如轻量网络架构、知识蒸馏或模型剪枝），降低计算复杂度；\n5. 系统比较不同改进策略，从检测精度（mAP）、未见类别识别能力与实时推理效率（FPS）方面评估性能。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 学术背景：扎实的机器学习、深度学习与计算机视觉基础，包括卷积神经网络（CNN）、视觉 Transformer（ViT）与目标检测流程的基础知识；\n2. 编程技能：熟练掌握 Python 与 PyTorch 框架；能在 Linux 环境下进行数据处理、模型训练与实验分析；\n3. 经验：有 YOLO 类检测器、视觉-语言模型（VLM）或航空图像处理经验者优先；学生应具备利用 GPU 训练与微调中等规模深度学习模型的能力。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_CXCAI-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"动态环境下基于ORB-SLAM3的鲁棒视觉定位与建图","titleEn":"Robust Visual Localization and Mapping with ORB-SLAM3 in Dynamic Environments","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"CAI Chenxiao","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nVisual Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (vSLAM) systems are generally developed under the assumption of a static environment, where most feature points observed in consecutive frames originate from stationary background objects. However, in practical indoor and outdoor scenarios containing frequently moving pedestrians, vehicles, or other dynamic elements, features extracted from these objects may be incorrectly utilized for camera pose estimation. This violation of the static assumption often leads to severe trajectory drift, ghosting artefacts in the reconstructed map, and, in worst-case scenarios, complete tracking failure.\nWhile ORB-SLAM3 provides a state-of-the-art and comprehensive framework for feature tracking, local mapping, loop closure detection, and map management, its front-end still relies primarily on conventional feature matching and robust estimation (e.g., RANSAC) to handle outliers. Consequently, its localization and mapping performance deteriorates significantly when dynamic objects occupy a large proportion of the field of view or exhibit complex motion patterns.\nThis project aims to develop robust visual localization and mapping algorithms tailored for dynamic environments, building upon the ORB-SLAM3 framework. Students will investigate the influence of dynamic features on camera tracking and map updating. The core approach will involve combining deep-learning-based semantic information (e.g., instance segmentation or object detection) with multi-frame geometric motion cues to identify potentially dynamic regions. Subsequently, features involved in pose estimation and map construction will be actively filtered or adaptively weighted to ensure robustness.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Configure and reproduce the standard ORB-SLAM3 system, conducting a baseline analysis of how dynamic objects negatively impact visual localization and map construction.\n2. Develop an outlier-rejection module by integrating semantic information (e.g., YOLO or Mask R-CNN) and geometric constraints (e.g., epipolar geometry or optical flow) to accurately identify and remove dynamic feature points.\n3. Incorporate the proposed dynamic-feature processing module into the ORB-SLAM3 tracking thread to achieve robust visual localization and mapping in highly dynamic environments.\n4. Conduct comprehensive experiments and quantitative evaluations on public dynamic datasets, such as the TUM RGB-D dataset and the Bonn RGB-D Dynamic dataset.\n5. Systematically compare different dynamic-feature processing strategies, evaluating their performance in terms of Absolute Trajectory Error (ATE), tracking robustness, and real-time computational efficiency.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Academic Background: A solid foundation in computer vision, state estimation, and robotic localization. Basic knowledge of feature matching, epipolar geometry, camera models, and the standard vSLAM pipeline is required.\n2. Programming Skills: Strong proficiency in C++ and/or Python. Ability to configure, compile, and debug complex software projects (e.g., using CMake/Make) in a Linux environment. Familiarity with the Robot Operating System (ROS) is highly preferred.\n3. Experience: Previous experience with the ORB-SLAM framework, deep learning-based object detection, or semantic segmentation is desirable.","descZh":"项目描述：视觉同步定位与建图（vSLAM）系统通常在静态环境假设下开发——连续帧中观察到的大部分特征点来自静止背景物体。然而，在包含频繁移动行人、车辆或其他动态元素的现实室内外场景中，从这些物体提取的特征可能被错误地用于相机位姿估计。这种对静态假设的违背常导致严重轨迹漂移、重建地图中的鬼影伪影，最坏情况下完全跟踪失败。\n尽管 ORB-SLAM3 为特征跟踪、局部建图、回环检测与地图管理提供了先进且全面的框架，其前端仍主要依赖常规特征匹配与稳健估计（如 RANSAC）处理外点。因此，当动态物体占据视野的很大比例或表现出复杂运动模式时，其定位与建图性能显著下降。\n本项目旨在基于 ORB-SLAM3 框架，开发面向动态环境的稳健视觉定位与建图算法。学生将研究动态特征对相机跟踪与地图更新的影响。核心方法将基于深度学习的语义信息（如实例分割或目标检测）与多帧几何运动线索相结合，识别潜在动态区域；随后主动滤除或自适应加权参与位姿估计与地图构建的特征，以确保鲁棒性。\n具体要求：\n1. 配置并复现标准 ORB-SLAM3 系统，基线分析动态物体对视觉定位与地图构建的负面影响；\n2. 通过集成语义信息（如 YOLO 或 Mask R-CNN）与几何约束（如对极几何或光流），开发外点剔除模块，准确识别并去除动态特征点；\n3. 将所提动态特征处理模块融入 ORB-SLAM3 跟踪线程，在高度动态环境中实现稳健的视觉定位与建图；\n4. 在 TUM RGB-D 数据集与 Bonn RGB-D Dynamic 数据集等公开动态数据集上开展全面实验与定量评估；\n5. 系统比较不同动态特征处理策略，从绝对轨迹误差（ATE）、跟踪鲁棒性与实时计算效率方面评估性能。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 学术背景：扎实的计算机视觉、状态估计与机器人定位基础；需具备特征匹配、对极几何、相机模型与标准 vSLAM 流程的基础知识；\n2. 编程技能：较强的 C++ 和/或 Python 能力；能在 Linux 环境中配置、编译与调试复杂软件项目（如使用 CMake/Make）；熟悉机器人操作系统（ROS）者优先；\n3. 经验：有 ORB-SLAM 框架、基于深度学习的物体检测或语义分割经验者优先。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_CXCAI-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"香港无人机城市物流配送的多任务点位路径规划","titleEn":"Multi-task Point Route Planning for UAV Urban Logistics Delivery in Hong Kong","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"CAI Chenxiao","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nThe rapid growth in demand for timely and flexible urban delivery makes Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) a highly promising option for last-mile logistics. However, implementing UAV delivery in Hong Kong presents unique challenges due to the city's exceptionally dense high-rise environment, complex restricted airspaces, and strict delivery time windows. These factors make it exceedingly difficult for a UAV to safely and punctually serve multiple pickup and drop-off locations within a single flight mission.\nThis project investigates the multi-task point route planning problem for logistics UAVs in complex urban environments. It aims to jointly determine the optimal task service sequence (macroscopic scheduling) and generate feasible collision-free 3D flight trajectories (microscopic path planning). The planning must comprehensively account for task priorities, strict time windows, delay penalties, building obstacles, and no-fly zones (NFZs). Students will explore UAV motion modelling, graph-search algorithms, and heuristic optimization, ultimately designing and evaluating their algorithms within a rigorous simulation environment.\nSpecific tasks include:\n1. Construct a simulated 3D Hong Kong urban delivery scenario, modeling depots, randomly distributed pickup/drop-off nodes, task priorities, delivery time windows, and typical building obstacles/NFZs.\n2. Formulate a comprehensive mathematical model for the UAV multi-task route-planning problem, aiming to minimize the total route cost and delay penalties while satisfying task-precedence, time-window, battery capacity, and safety constraints.\n3. Develop a hybrid planning algorithm: utilizing a meta-heuristic approach (e.g., Genetic Algorithm or Ant Colony Optimization) for optimal task sequencing, integrated with a 3D pathfinding algorithm (e.g., A* or RRT*) for collision-free trajectory generation between nodes.\n4. Implement the proposed algorithms in MATLAB or Python.\n5. Systematically compare the proposed hybrid method against baseline approaches (e.g., nearest-neighbor routing, standard A*) based on key performance metrics: total route length, accumulated delay, on-time completion rate, energy consumption, and obstacle-avoidance safety.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Academic Background: A solid foundation in optimization theory, algorithms, control systems, or robotics. Basic knowledge of Vehicle Routing Problems (VRP) and standard path-planning algorithms is required.\n2. Programming Skills: Proficiency in Python, MATLAB, or C++. Experience with data structures, graph theory implementation, or path-planning libraries is a significant advantage.\n3. Experience: Familiarity with simulation platforms such as MATLAB/Simulink, ROS, AirSim, or Gazebo is highly preferred but not mandatory.","descZh":"项目描述：城市即时灵活配送需求的快速增长，使无人机（UAV）成为末端物流极具前景的选项。然而，在香港实施无人机配送面临独特挑战：城市超高密度的楼宇环境、复杂的限制空域与严格的配送时间窗。这些因素使无人机很难在单次飞行任务中安全、准时地为多个取送点服务。\n本项目研究复杂城市环境中物流无人机多任务点航线规划问题。目标是在宏观调度层面联合确定最优任务服务顺序，并在微观路径规划层面生成可行的无碰撞三维飞行轨迹。规划须全面考虑任务优先级、严格时间窗、延误惩罚、楼宇障碍与禁飞区（NFZ）。学生将探索无人机运动建模、图搜索算法与启发式优化，最终在严谨的仿真环境中设计并评估其算法。\n具体任务：\n1. 构建香港三维城市配送仿真场景，建模枢纽站、随机分布的取送节点、任务优先级、配送时间窗与典型楼宇障碍/NFZ；\n2. 为无人机多任务航线规划问题构建综合数学模型，目标是在满足任务先后关系、时间窗、电池容量与安全约束的同时，最小化总航线成本与延误惩罚；\n3. 开发混合规划算法：采用元启发式方法（如遗传算法或蚁群优化）进行最优任务排序，并集成三维寻路算法（如 A* 或 RRT*）生成节点间无碰撞轨迹；\n4. 在 MATLAB 或 Python 中实现所提算法；\n5. 基于关键性能指标（总航线长度、累计延误、按时完成率、能耗与避障安全性），将所提混合方法与基线方法（如最近邻路由、标准 A*）进行系统比较。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 学术背景：扎实的优化理论、算法、控制系统或机器人学基础；需具备车辆路径问题（VRP）与标准路径规划算法的基础知识；\n2. 编程能力：熟练掌握 Python、MATLAB 或 C++；有数据结构、图论实现或路径规划库经验者为重要加分项；\n3. 经验：熟悉 MATLAB/Simulink、ROS、AirSim 或 Gazebo 等仿真平台者优先，但非必需。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_CXCAI-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向鲁棒RGB-T目标检测的可靠性感知动态融合","titleEn":"Reliability-aware Dynamic Fusion for Robust RGB-T Object Detection","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"CAI Chenxiao","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nRGB-T object detection combines visible-spectrum imagery with thermal infrared data to provide complementary appearance and thermal information. This multi-modal approach is particularly valuable for night-time perception, adverse-weather monitoring, intelligent transportation, surveillance, search-and-rescue, and unmanned-system applications. Compared with single-modality detection, RGB-T systems can theoretically preserve target visibility when one sensor becomes unreliable.\nHowever, many existing RGB-T detectors adopt fixed fusion strategies, operating under the ideal assumption that both modalities are clean, perfectly synchronized, and equally informative. In realistic operational environments, factors such as low illumination, thermal crossover, motion blur, sensor noise, occlusions, sensor saturation, and spatial misalignment cause the quality of the two modalities to vary substantially. Under these conditions, static or indiscriminate fusion can introduce misleading features and severely degrade overall detection performance, especially for small or heavily occluded objects. Furthermore, typical two-stream network architectures often incur considerable computational costs, limiting their deployment on resource-constrained edge devices.\nBuilding upon contemporary two-stream detection frameworks, this project aims to investigate a reliability-aware and computationally efficient RGB-T fusion methodology. The proposed model will estimate the relative usefulness of visible and thermal features in real-time, dynamically regulate cross-modal information exchange, and effectively exploit multi-scale complementary cues. The primary objective is to improve both baseline accuracy under clean conditions and detection robustness under modality degradation, while maintaining a compact model size and practical inference speed.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Configure and reproduce representative baseline models for RGB-T object detection (e.g., early-, middle-, and feature-level fusion networks), establishing standard training and evaluation protocols.\n2. Construct controlled modality-degradation test sets (simulating low light, blur, noise, occlusion, and partial sensor corruption). Develop reliability or utility estimators capable of quantifying the real-time contribution of visible versus thermal features.\n3. Design and integrate a quality-aware dynamic fusion module and multi-scale feature interaction networks to enhance the detection of small, occluded, and weak-contrast targets.\n4. Train and rigorously evaluate the proposed methods on public RGB-T datasets (e.g., FLIR, LLVIP, M3FD, or KAIST) under both standard and degraded testing conditions.\n5. Systematically compare the proposed dynamic fusion strategies against baselines using comprehensive metrics: detection accuracy (mAP@50, mAP@50:95), robustness scores under degradation, parameter count, computational complexity (GFLOPs), and inference speed (FPS).\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Academic Background: A solid foundation in machine learning, deep learning, and computer vision. Essential knowledge includes Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), Vision Transformers (ViTs), feature pyramids, and standard object detection pipelines.\n2. Programming Skills: Strong proficiency in Python and the PyTorch framework. Ability to perform data pre-processing, model training, debugging, and experimental analysis in a Linux environment.\n3. Experience: Previous experience with YOLO-based detectors, multi-modal learning, infrared-image processing, or GPU-based model training is highly desirable. Familiarity with model profiling and reproducible experimentation practices is an advantage.","descZh":"项目描述：RGB-T 目标检测将可见光谱图像与热红外数据相结合，提供互补的外观与热信息。这种多模态方法对夜间感知、恶劣天气监测、智能交通、安防监控、搜救与无人系统应用尤其有价值。与单模态检测相比，RGB-T 系统在一传感器不可靠时理论上可保持目标可见性。\n然而，许多现有 RGB-T 检测器采用固定融合策略，运行于两模态干净、完美同步且信息量相等的理想假设下。在现实运行环境中，低照度、热交叉、运动模糊、传感器噪声、遮挡、传感器饱和与空间错位等因素使两种模态的质量差异显著。在此条件下，静态或无差别融合会引入误导特征并严重损害整体检测性能，尤其对小型或重度遮挡物体。此外，典型双流网络架构常带来可观计算成本，限制其在资源受限边缘设备上的部署。\n在当代双流检测框架基础上，本项目旨在研究可靠性感知且计算高效的 RGB-T 融合方法。所提模型将实时估计可见与热特征的相对有用性、动态调节跨模态信息交换，并有效利用多尺度互补线索。主要目标是提升干净条件下的基线精度与模态退化下的检测鲁棒性，同时保持紧凑模型规模与实用推理速度。\n具体要求：\n1. 配置并复现代表性 RGB-T 目标检测基线模型（如早期、中期与特征级融合网络），建立标准训练与评估协议；\n2. 构建受控模态退化测试集（模拟低光、模糊、噪声、遮挡与部分传感器损坏）；开发能实时量化可见与热特征贡献的可靠性/效用估计器；\n3. 设计并集成质量感知动态融合模块与多尺度特征交互网络，增强对小目标、遮挡目标与弱对比目标的检测；\n4. 在公开 RGB-T 数据集（如 FLIR、LLVIP、M3FD 或 KAIST）上，于标准与退化测试条件下训练并严谨评估所提方法；\n5. 用全面指标系统比较所提动态融合策略与基线：检测精度（mAP@50、mAP@50:95）、退化下鲁棒性评分、参数量、计算复杂度（GFLOPs）与推理速度（FPS）。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 学术背景：扎实的机器学习、深度学习与计算机视觉基础；必备知识包括卷积神经网络（CNN）、视觉 Transformer（ViT）、特征金字塔与标准目标检测流程；\n2. 编程技能：较强的 Python 与 PyTorch 框架能力；能在 Linux 环境进行数据预处理、模型训练、调试与实验分析；\n3. 经验：有 YOLO 类检测器、多模态学习、红外图像处理或基于 GPU 的模型训练经验者优先；熟悉模型性能剖析与可复现实验实践者更佳。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_CXCAI-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向无人机动态避障的风险敏感安全强化学习","titleEn":"Risk-sensitive Safe Reinforcement Learning for UAV Dynamic Obstacle Avoidance","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"CAI Chenxiao","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nDeep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) has shown great potential in enabling real-time UAV navigation amid moving obstacles. However, standard DRL training typically relies on simple, conflict-free samples, lacking sufficient exposure to rare, safety-critical edge cases—such as head-on encounters, cross-traffic conflicts, sudden obstacle trajectory changes, and simultaneous multi-obstacle threats. Consequently, such standard policies may achieve high average mission success rates but fail to guarantee reliability and safety under extreme, dangerous scenarios.\nThis project investigates risk-sensitive safe reinforcement learning for the local dynamic obstacle avoidance of UAVs. The obstacle avoidance problem is formulated as a Constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP) to explicitly decouple mission performance from safety metrics. In this framework, the reward function evaluates task progress, path efficiency, and control smoothness, while the safety cost function quantifies collision risks and separation loss based on relative distance and time-to-collision (TTC). Crucially, the Conditional Value-at-Risk (CVaR) is adopted to constrain the high-risk tail of the safety cost distribution, preventing severe, low-probability failure risks from being masked by good average performance.\nThe proposed framework integrates a CVaR-constrained Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) algorithm with risk-guided curriculum learning, gradually increasing the complexity and density of dynamic obstacles during training. The core research objective is to verify whether this risk-sensitive framework improves UAV safety in unseen critical scenarios without causing excessive conservatism or degrading overall mission efficiency. The research will adopt a kinematic single-quadrotor model with continuous control commands, focusing entirely on in-flight collision prevention.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Formulate the UAV dynamic obstacle avoidance problem as a CMDP, defining the state space (local relative observations), continuous action space, reward function (mission efficiency), and cost function (collision risk and TTC).\n2. Implement a baseline DRL algorithm (e.g., standard PPO or SAC) in a simulation environment (e.g., Python-Gymnasium, ROS-Gazebo, or AirSim) containing randomly moving obstacles.\n3. Develop and integrate the Conditional Value-at-Risk (CVaR) constraints into the policy optimization process, constructing the risk-sensitive safe RL framework.\n4. Design a risk-guided curriculum learning strategy to systematically expose the agent to increasingly complex safety-critical edge cases (e.g., head-on and multi-obstacle intersection scenarios).\n5. Rigorously validate and compare the proposed CVaR-constrained policy against the baseline algorithm. Evaluate performance using metrics such as collision rate, tail-risk cost (CVaR estimation), path optimality, and mission success rate under unseen extreme scenarios.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Academic Background: Solid foundations in Automatic Control, Robotics, and Probability & Statistics. A clear understanding of Markov Decision Processes (MDP) and deep learning concepts.\n2. Programming Skills: Proficient in Python programming. Hands-on experience with PyTorch and standard Reinforcement Learning libraries (e.g., Stable-Baselines3, Ray RLlib) is highly preferred.\n3. Experience: Prior knowledge of safe reinforcement learning, constrained optimization, or robot motion planning is a significant advantage.","descZh":"项目描述：深度强化学习（DRL）在实现无人机面对移动障碍物的实时导航方面显示出巨大潜力。然而，标准 DRL 训练通常依赖简单、无冲突的样本，缺乏对罕见安全关键边缘情形（如对头相遇、交叉交通冲突、障碍物轨迹突变与多障碍物同时威胁）的充分暴露。因此，此类标准策略可能在平均任务成功率上表现良好，却无法保证极端危险场景下的可靠性与安全性。\n本项目研究用于无人机局部动态避障的风险敏感安全强化学习。避障问题被表述为约束马尔可夫决策过程（CMDP），以显式解耦任务性能与安全指标。该框架中，奖励函数评估任务进展、路径效率与控制平滑性；安全成本函数基于相对距离与碰撞时间（TTC）量化碰撞风险与间隔损失。关键的是，采用条件风险价值（CVaR）约束安全成本分布的高风险尾部，防止严重、低概率的失效风险被良好平均表现掩盖。\n所提框架集成 CVaR 约束的近端策略优化（PPO）算法与风险引导课程学习——训练过程中逐步增加动态障碍的复杂度与密度。核心研究目标是验证该风险敏感框架是否能在未见关键场景中提升无人机安全性，而不造成过度保守或损害整体任务效率。研究将采用连续控制指令的单一四旋翼运动学模型，完全聚焦飞行中碰撞预防。\n具体要求：\n1. 将无人机动态避障问题表述为 CMDP，定义状态空间（局部相对观测）、连续动作空间、奖励函数（任务效率）与成本函数（碰撞风险与 TTC）；\n2. 在含随机移动障碍物的仿真环境（如 Python-Gymnasium、ROS-Gazebo 或 AirSim）中实现基线 DRL 算法（如标准 PPO 或 SAC）；\n3. 开发并将条件风险价值（CVaR）约束集成到策略优化过程中，构建风险敏感安全强化学习框架；\n4. 设计风险引导课程学习策略，系统性地让智能体暴露于日益复杂的安全关键边缘情形（如对头与多障碍物交叉场景）；\n5. 严谨验证并将所提 CVaR 约束策略与基线算法比较。在未见极端场景下，用碰撞率、尾部风险成本（CVaR 估计）、路径最优性与任务成功率等指标评估性能。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 学术背景：扎实的自动控制、机器人学与概率统计基础；清晰理解马尔可夫决策过程（MDP）与深度学习概念；\n2. 编程技能：熟练使用 Python；有 PyTorch 与标准强化学习库（如 Stable-Baselines3、Ray RLlib）动手经验者优先；\n3. 经验：有安全强化学习、约束优化或机器人运动规划知识者为重要加分项。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_CXCAI-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"氢锂混合动力旋翼无人机分层能量管理策略","titleEn":"Hierarchical Energy Management Strategy for Hydrogen-lithium Hybrid Rotary-wing UAVs","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"CAI Chenxiao","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nHydrogen fuel-cell/lithium-battery hybrid power systems combine the high specific energy and long endurance of fuel cells with the high power density and rapid response of lithium batteries. This combination is attractive for long-endurance rotary-wing Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs), whose propulsion demand changes sharply during take-off, climbing, hovering, maneuvering, payload variation, and wind disturbance. Fuel cells, however, respond relatively slowly and must operate within efficiency, temperature, hydrogen-supply, and current-slew constraints, while excessive battery peak-current support accelerates degradation and reduces emergency reserve.\nConventional energy-management strategies commonly use fixed thresholds or predefined power-split rules. They cannot fully exploit mission-level information while adapting to real operating deviations. A strategy optimized only for a planned profile may become inefficient when actual power demand, wind, payload, component efficiency, battery state, or thermal condition differs from the prediction; a purely reactive controller may instead lack a global view of the remaining mission.\nThis project aims to develop a two-layer hierarchical energy management strategy. The first layer uses reference information, including the mission profile, planned trajectory, payload, predicted propulsion power, initial battery state of charge, remaining hydrogen, efficiency maps, and safety-reserve requirements, to generate reference fuel-cell power, battery power, and energy-state trajectories. The second layer uses measured states and actual flight conditions to fine-tune these references in real time, compensate for disturbances and modeling errors, enforce operating constraints, and maintain DC-bus power balance. The objective is to improve endurance and efficiency while reducing battery stress and preserving emergency reserve.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Establish models of the fuel cell, lithium battery, DC/DC converters, propulsion system, and rotary-wing UAV mission power demand. Reproduce a conventional rule-based power-split strategy as the baseline.\n2. Develop the first-layer reference-generation method using mission and reference information. Candidate approaches may include optimized rule-based control, equivalent-consumption minimization, dynamic programming, or model predictive optimization.\n3. Develop the second-layer real-time adjustment method using measured power demand, battery SOC/SOH, DC-bus voltage, fuel-cell temperature, hydrogen pressure, and flight-state feedback. The controller should correct reference values under disturbances, prediction errors, and component constraints.\n4. Evaluate the strategy through simulation and, where facilities permit, hardware-in-the-loop or powertrain test-bench experiments under take-off, climbing, hovering, cruising, rapid maneuvering, payload changes, and wind disturbances.\n5. Compare the proposed method with rule-based and representative single-layer strategies in terms of hydrogen consumption, battery energy throughput and peak current, SOC tracking, DC-bus power error, computational cost, safety-reserve retention, and achievable endurance.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Academic Background: A solid foundation in control systems, optimization, energy systems, and basic UAV dynamics. Knowledge of fuel cells, lithium batteries, power electronics, or hybrid-electric propulsion is desirable.\n2. Programming Skills: Proficiency in MATLAB/Simulink and/or Python, with the ability to construct dynamic models, implement control algorithms, perform numerical optimization, and analyze simulation data.\n3. Experience: Previous experience with model predictive control, optimal control, battery SOC estimation, fuel-cell systems, embedded control, or hardware-in-the-loop experimentation is highly desirable.","descZh":"项目描述：氢燃料电池/锂电池混合动力系统将燃料电池的高比能与长续航，与锂电池的高功率密度与快速响应相结合。这种组合对长航时旋翼无人机（UAV）颇具吸引力——其推进需求在起飞、爬升、悬停、机动、载荷变化与风扰动期间急剧变化。然而，燃料电池响应相对缓慢，须在效率、温度、氢气供给与电流变化率约束内运行；而过度依赖电池峰值电流支持会加速退化并减少应急储备。\n常规能量管理策略通常使用固定阈值或预定义功率分配规则，无法在适应实际运行偏差的同时充分利用任务级信息。仅针对计划剖面优化的策略，在实际功率需求、风况、载荷、部件效率、电池状态或热状况偏离预测时可能变得低效；纯反应式控制器则可能缺乏对剩余任务的全局视野。\n本项目旨在开发一种双层分层能量管理策略。第一层使用参考信息（包括任务剖面、规划轨迹、载荷、预测推进功率、电池初始荷电状态、剩余氢气、效率图谱与安全储备要求），生成参考燃料电池功率、电池功率与能量状态轨迹。第二层使用实测状态与实际飞行条件实时微调这些参考值、补偿扰动与建模误差、执行运行约束并维持直流母线功率平衡。目标是提升续航与效率，同时减轻电池压力并保留应急储备。\n具体要求：\n1. 建立燃料电池、锂电池、DC/DC 变换器、推进系统与旋翼无人机任务功率需求的模型；复现常规基于规则的功率分配策略作为基线；\n2. 利用任务与参考信息开发第一层参考生成方法；候选方法可包括优化规则控制、等效消耗最小化、动态规划或模型预测优化；\n3. 利用实测功率需求、电池 SOC/SOH、直流母线电压、燃料电池温度、氢气压力与飞行状态反馈开发第二层实时调整方法；控制器应在扰动、预测误差与部件约束下修正参考值；\n4. 通过仿真并在设施允许条件下开展硬件在环或动力系统台架实验，在起飞、爬升、悬停、巡航、快速机动、载荷变化与风扰动条件下评估策略；\n5. 将所提方法与基于规则及代表性单层策略比较，指标包括氢气消耗、电池能量吞吐与峰值电流、SOC 跟踪、直流母线功率误差、计算成本、安全储备保持与可达续航。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 学术背景：扎实的控制系统、优化、能源系统与基础无人机动力学基础；了解燃料电池、锂电池、电力电子或混合电推者优先；\n2. 编程技能：熟练掌握 MATLAB/Simulink 和/或 Python，能构建动态模型、实现控制算法、进行数值优化与分析仿真数据；\n3. 经验：有模型预测控制、最优控制、电池 SOC 估计、燃料电池系统、嵌入式控制或硬件在环实验经验者优先。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_SLIANG-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向高比例可再生能源系统的基于AI的风电与光伏发电预测","titleEn":"AI-based Wind and Solar Power Forecasting for High-Renewable Energy Systems","areaZh":"人工智能；智能电网","areaEn":"Artificial Intelligence; Smart Grid","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"LIANG Shi","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nAccurate wind and solar power forecasting is essential for grid stability and renewable energy integration. However, renewable generation is highly uncertain because it depends strongly on changing weather conditions.\nThis project will develop AI-based forecasting methods using meteorological data and historical power output. Students may explore deep learning, hybrid models, graph neural networks, or physics-informed methods to improve prediction accuracy, robustness, and operational feasibility.\nThe specifications are given as follows:\nPreprocess wind, solar, weather, and historical power data.\nDevelop and compare AI-based forecasting models.\nIncorporate physical or operational constraints where possible.\nEvaluate and visualise forecasting performance.\nOptional: Strong results may lead to a publishable research paper.\nPre- and Co-requisites\nBasic knowledge of machine learning or time-series modelling.\nExperience with Python and PyTorch or TensorFlow.\nInterest in renewable energy and artificial intelligence.","descZh":"项目描述：准确的风电与光伏功率预测对电网稳定与可再生能源消纳至关重要。然而，可再生能源发电高度不确定，因为其强烈依赖多变的天气状况。\n本项目将利用气象数据与历史功率数据，开发基于 AI 的预测方法。学生可探索深度学习、混合模型、图神经网络或物理信息方法，以提升预测精度、鲁棒性与工程可用性。\n项目要求如下：\n- 预处理风、光、气象与历史功率数据；\n- 开发并比较基于 AI 的预测模型；\n- 尽可能融入物理或运行约束；\n- 评估并可视化预测性能；\n- 可选：成果优异可争取发表研究论文。\n先修/并修要求：机器学习或时间序列建模基础知识；熟悉 Python 与 PyTorch 或 TensorFlow；对可再生能源与人工智能有兴趣。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_SLIANG-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于AI的股票表现预测","titleEn":"AI-based Stock Performance Prediction","areaZh":"人工智能；金融数据分析","areaEn":"Artificial Intelligence; Financial Data Analytics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"LIANG Shi","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description: \nIf you had 10 million dollars, how would you allocate stocks as a quantitative fund manager? Consider this challenge in the context of China's A-share market, where there are about 5,000 stocks to choose from—this is no easy task (the same applies to the U.S. or Hong Kong stock markets as well). With the help of artificial intelligence, this project focuses on predicting future stock performance using deep learning models based on historical price and volume data (X). The goal is to forecast the weekly return rate for the next trading week (5 days), then categorize the 5,000 stocks into different tiers through regression or classification, ultimately making a sound and informed investment choice.\nThe specifications are given as follows:\n1. Preprocess historical stock data for model input.\n2. Develop a deep learning model for predicting weekly stock returns.\n3. Classify stocks into tiers based on predicted performance.\n4.（Optionnal） Publish a paper in an SCI-indexed journal\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic understanding of machine learning.\n2. Some experience with Python programming\n3. Interest in financial data and prediction.","descZh":"项目描述：如果你有一千万美元，作为量化基金经理你会如何配置股票？请在中国 A 股市场背景下思考这一挑战——约 5000 只股票可供选择，绝非易事（美股或港股市场亦如此）。在人工智能的帮助下，本项目聚焦于利用基于历史价格与成交量数据（X）的深度学习模型预测股票未来表现。目标是预测下一交易周（5 天）的周收益率，再通过回归或分类将这约 5000 只股票划分为不同层级，最终做出合理、有依据的投资选择。\n具体要求如下：\n1. 对历史股票数据进行预处理作为模型输入；\n2. 开发用于预测周股票收益率的深度学习模型；\n3. 根据预测表现对股票进行分层；\n4.（可选）在 SCI 收录期刊发表论文。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 对机器学习有基本理解；\n2. 有一定 Python 编程经验；\n3. 对金融数据与预测有兴趣。","cats":["AI","CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_XCXIE-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于运动预测与控制的移动机器人预测性避障","titleEn":"Predictive Obstacle Avoidance for Mobile Robots via Motion Prediction and Control","areaZh":"机器人与控制；控制工程","areaEn":"Robotics and Control; Control Engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"XIE Xiaochen","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description: \nMobile robots are increasingly deployed alongside human workers in warehouses and industrial settings. Safe and efficient navigation in such environments requires more than reactive braking or stopping when an obstacle is detected -- such passive strategies cannot distinguish between static and dynamic obstacles, nor can they cope with the uncertainty of future obstacle movements. Therefore, an approach that anticipates multiple possible future trajectories of moving obstacles and proactively plans the robot's path is essential for improving both safety and operational efficiency in human-robot shared workspaces. Research aim and objectives: \n- To predict multiple plausible future positions of dynamic obstacles by training a neural network on bird's eye-view images, using a specialised loss function that prevents mode collapse and captures the inherent multimodality of human/vehicle motion.\n- To characterise the uncertainty of these predictions online and represent them as probabilistic occupancy regions (e.g., ellipses) that can be directly used as safety constraints.\n- To embed these constraints into a predictive controller that computes control commands, allowing the robot to track a desired path while proactively avoiding areas that dynamic obstacles are likely to occupy in the near future.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\nThe project will be implemented in a simulation environment (e.g., Gazebo or Coppeliasim) featuring a warehouse-like scenario with a mobile robot and multiple dynamic agents representing human workers or other vehicles. An overhead camera will provide bird's eye-view images of the scene as the primary perceptual input. The deep learning module will be implemented in PyTorch or TensorFlow, trained offline on simulated trajectory data. The control module will be implemented using a nonlinear MPC solver (e.g., CasADi with IPOPT) running at a specified control frequency. Performance will be evaluated against reactive baselines using metrics such as collision rate, path-tracking error, and average travel time.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nPre-requisites: Working knowledge of Python programming and common scientific libraries (NumPy, SciPy, Matplotlib); Familiarity with robot simulation environments (e.g., Gazebo, Coppeliasim, or similar) and the Robot Operating System (ROS) for integrating perception, planning, and control modules. Basic understanding of deep learning frameworks (PyTorch or TensorFlow) - enough to train and evaluate a neural network on recorded trajectory data. Some exposure to optimisation and control concepts (e.g., Model Predictive Control in simulation) is helpful, but detailed theoretical background is not mandatory.\nCo-requisites (to be taken alongside the project): Hands-on experience with simulation tools for mobile robots (e.g., setting up a warehouse‑like scenario with dynamic agents); Practical skills in data processing for bird's eye-view representations from simulated camera feeds; Ability to implement online uncertainty quantification (e.g., clustering and Gaussian fitting) in Python.","descZh":"项目描述：移动机器人正越来越多地与人类工人在仓库与工业环境中协同作业。在此类环境中实现安全高效导航，需要的远不止检测到障碍物时的反应式制动或停车——这类被动策略既无法区分静态与动态障碍物，也无法应对未来障碍物运动的不确定性。因此，一种能预测移动障碍物多种可能的未来轨迹、并主动规划机器人路径的方法，对提升人机共享工作空间的安全性与运行效率至关重要。\n研究目标：\n- 通过在鸟瞰图像上训练神经网络，预测动态障碍物的多个可能未来位置，使用可防止模式坍缩并捕捉人/车运动固有模态多样性的专用损失函数；\n- 在线表征这些预测的不确定性，并将其表示为可直接用作安全约束的概率占据区域（如椭圆）；\n- 将这些约束嵌入预测控制器以计算控制指令，使机器人能够在主动避开动态障碍物近期可能占据区域的同时跟踪期望路径。\n具体要求：项目将在仿真环境（如 Gazebo 或 Coppeliasim）中实现，场景为类似仓库的环境，含一台移动机器人与代表工人或其他车辆的多台动态智能体。俯视相机提供场景鸟瞰图像作为主要感知输入。深度学习模块将用 PyTorch 或 TensorFlow 实现，在模拟轨迹数据上离线训练。控制模块将使用非线性 MPC 求解器（如 CasADi + IPOPT）以指定控制频率运行。将使用碰撞率、路径跟踪误差与平均行驶时间等指标，与反应式基线评估对比。\n先修/并修要求：\n先修：具备可用的 Python 编程与常用科学库（NumPy、SciPy、Matplotlib）知识；熟悉机器人仿真环境（如 Gazebo、Coppeliasim 或类似）与机器人操作系统（ROS），用于集成感知、规划与控制模块；对深度学习框架（PyTorch 或 TensorFlow）有基本理解，足以在记录的轨迹数据上训练与评估神经网络；有一定优化与控制概念接触（如仿真中的模型预测控制）会有帮助，但详细理论背景非必需。\n并修（与项目同期开展）：具备移动机器人仿真工具的动手经验（如搭建带动态智能体的仓库场景）；具备从模拟相机馈送处理鸟瞰表示的实用数据技能；能用 Python 实现在线不确定性量化（如聚类与高斯拟合）。","cats":["CTRL","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_XQSONG-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"AI增强的弹性飞行：无人机混合数据驱动故障检测","titleEn":"AI-enhanced Resilient Flight: Hybrid Data-driven Fault Detection for Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs)","areaZh":"人工智能；机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Artificial Intelligence:  Robotics; drones; and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"SONG Xiaoqi","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nThe reliability and safety of UAV systems are critical for their widespread applications in areas such as logistics, transportation, surveying, and emergency rescue. Sudden failures of actuators, sensors, or the propulsion system during flight can lead to mission failure and even catastrophic crashes, resulting in significant economic and safety risks.\nThis project aims to explore artificial intelligence in flight control. Data-driven fault detection will be considered to achieve online health monitoring during flights. Model-based fault detection strategies can also be integrated with data-driven algorithms for improved diagnosis performance. The core objective is to enhance UAV resilience and operational safety.\nThis project involves the development of a data-driven fault detection module for UAVs that can identify and isolate common faults, thereby preventing system degradation and improving overall mission success rates.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Data collection & simulation setup: A simulation environment for a UAV will be established using platforms such as MATLAB/Simulink or a similar simulator. Data on flight dynamics, control inputs, and system states will be collected under both normal and various fault cases.\n2. Intelligent fault detection algorithm development: Data-driven algorithms will be developed by processing the control error signals recorded in the flight data and generating signals of fault occurrence or scores related to the likelihood of different types of faults. \nThe approach can also explore a hybrid framework, including data-driven techniques (e.g., reinforcement learning, neural networks, regression algorithms) and model-based strategies (e.g., observer-based, parameter identification methods).\n3. Evaluation: The developed fault detection algorithms will be implemented and integrated into the UAV simulation environment to create an intelligent fault diagnosis module. The performance and robustness of the system will be quantitatively evaluated.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nBasic knowledge of feedback control, filtering, and signal processing.\nProgramming knowledge of MATLAB/Simulink, Python or C++. \nFamiliarity with AI and intelligent control algorithms is a plus.\nExcellent problem-solving and analytical skills.","descZh":"项目描述：无人机系统的可靠性与安全性对其在物流、交通、测绘与应急救援等领域的广泛应用至关重要。飞行中执行器、传感器或推进系统的突发故障可能导致任务失败甚至灾难性坠机，带来重大经济与安全风险。\n本项目旨在探索人工智能在飞行控制中的应用。将考虑采用数据驱动故障检测实现飞行中的在线健康监测；也可将基于模型的故障检测策略与数据驱动算法集成，以提升诊断性能。核心目标是增强无人机的韧性与运行安全性。\n本项目涉及开发面向无人机的数据驱动故障检测模块，能够识别并隔离常见故障，从而防止系统性能退化并提升整体任务成功率。\n具体要求：\n1. 数据采集与仿真搭建：使用 MATLAB/Simulink 或类似仿真器建立无人机仿真环境；在正常工况与多种故障工况下采集飞行动力学、控制输入与系统状态数据；\n2. 智能故障检测算法开发：通过处理飞行数据中记录的控制误差信号、生成故障发生信号或各类故障可能性得分，开发数据驱动算法。该方法还可探索混合框架，结合数据驱动技术（如强化学习、神经网络、回归算法）与基于模型的策略（如基于观测器、参数辨识方法）；\n3. 评估：将所开发的故障检测算法实现并集成到无人机仿真环境中，构建智能故障诊断模块，定量评估系统的性能与鲁棒性。\n先修/并修要求：具备反馈控制、滤波与信号处理基础知识；掌握 MATLAB/Simulink、Python 或 C++ 编程；熟悉 AI 与智能控制算法者优先；具备出色的问题解决与分析能力。","cats":["AERO","AI","CTRL"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_CCFAN-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向安全人机交互的基于学习的控制","titleEn":"Learning-based Control for Safe Human-robot Interaction","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制；人工智能","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control; Artificial intelligence","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"FAN Chenchen","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nLearning-based control, such as reinforcement learning (RL), has emerged as a powerful technique for solving complex optimal control problems by leveraging machine learning. It has demonstrated notable success in various robotic applications, ranging from autonomous navigation to industrial automation. At the same time, ensuring safe control under uncertainty is critical in automation, particularly in human–machine collaborative environments where dynamic interactions introduce unpredictable risks. Safe control has been extensively studied, leading to approaches such as control barrier functions. Therefore, it is important to integrate safety requirements into learning-based control frameworks, especially for human-robot interaction, where both safety and efficiency are essential for real-world deployment in applications such as manufacturing, medical, and rehabilitation robotics.\nThis project will investigate learning-based safe control strategies for human-robot interaction scenarios. The objective is to combine learning-based approaches with control barrier functions to ensure operational safety under physical constraints and interaction requirements, while achieving specified control objectives.\nThe specifications are given as follows:\nStudents are expected to produce the following outcomes: \n1. Analyze and model the dynamics of human-robot interaction scenarios.\n2. Design learning-based control strategies that guarantee safety requirements.\n3. Evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed safe control schemes through simulation studies.\n4. The project will primarily involve numerical simulations, though experimental studies may also be conducted (if the student is interested).\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic knowledge of machine learning and robotics.\n2. Programming experience with MATLAB, Python, ROS or similar tools.","descZh":"项目描述：基于学习的控制（如强化学习 RL）已成为利用机器学习求解复杂最优控制问题的强大技术，在从自主导航到工业自动化的各类机器人应用中取得显著成功。同时，在不确定性下确保安全控制对自动化至关重要，尤其在人机协作环境中——动态交互会引入不可预测的风险。安全控制已被广泛研究，催生了控制屏障函数等方法。因此，将安全要求纳入基于学习的控制框架十分重要，尤其在人机交互中——安全与效率对制造、医疗与康复机器人等实际部署都不可或缺。\n本项目将研究面向人机交互场景的基于学习的安全控制策略。目标是将基于学习的方法与控制屏障函数相结合，在物理约束与交互要求下确保运行安全，同时实现既定控制目标。\n具体要求：学生预期产出以下成果：\n1. 分析并建模人机交互场景的动力学；\n2. 设计能保证安全要求的基于学习的控制策略；\n3. 通过仿真研究评估所提安全控制方案的有效性；\n4. 项目以数值仿真为主（若学生有兴趣，也可开展实验研究）。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备机器学习与机器人学基础知识；\n2. 有 MATLAB、Python、ROS 或类似工具的编程经验。","cats":["AERO","AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_CCFAN-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"故障弹性多智能体系统中的任务重分配与协调","titleEn":"Task Reallocation and Coordination in Failure-resilient Multi-agent Systems","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"FAN Chenchen","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nIn multi-agent systems, the ability to dynamically coordinate and reallocate tasks in response to agent failures is essential for maintaining continuous and efficient operation. However, many traditional task allocation strategies assume that all agents remain fully functional, which can lead to performance degradation or even complete system failure when faults occur. Therefore, it is important to develop scalable and effective control strategies that can handle failure-induced changes in interaction dynamics and communication topologies. Such strategies are critical for enabling robust coordination and task reallocation, thereby supporting more reliable multi-robot system applications. \nThis project will apply switched system methods to capture the changes in interaction dynamics and communication topology following agent failures. Hierarchical control framework may be developed to enable decision-making for task allocation and coordination among the remaining agents. \nThe specifications are given as follows:\nStudents are expected to produce the following outcomes: \n1. Model the dynamics of multi-agent systems with agent failures using switched system methods.\n2. Design hierarchical control strategies to handle dynamics and communication changes while enabling task allocation for effective coordination.\n3. Evaluate the performance of the proposed hierarchical control strategies through simulation studies.\n4. The project will primarily involve numerical simulations, though experimental studies may also be conducted (if the student is interested).\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic knowledge of control theory and optimization.\n2. Programming experience with MATLAB, Python or similar tools.","descZh":"项目描述：在多智能体系统中，针对智能体故障进行动态协调与任务再分配的能力，对维持持续高效运行至关重要。然而，许多传统任务分配策略假定所有智能体始终功能完好，导致故障发生时性能退化甚至系统完全失效。因此，开发能够应对故障引起的交互动力学与通信拓扑变化、可扩展且有效的控制策略十分重要。此类策略对实现稳健协调与任务再分配至关重要，可支撑更可靠的多机器人系统应用。\n本项目将采用切换系统方法刻画智能体故障后交互动力学与通信拓扑的变化；可开发层级化控制框架，为剩余智能体间的任务分配与协调提供决策支持。\n具体要求：学生预期产出以下成果：\n1. 采用切换系统方法对带智能体故障的多智能体系统进行动力学建模；\n2. 设计层级化控制策略，处理动力学与通信变化，同时支持任务分配以实现有效协调；\n3. 通过仿真研究评估所提层级化控制策略的性能；\n4. 项目以数值仿真为主（若学生有兴趣，也可开展实验研究）。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备控制理论与优化的基础知识；\n2. 有 MATLAB、Python 或类似工具的编程经验。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_CCFAN-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"具有AI感知或控制的智能系统安全分析与控制","titleEn":"Safe Analysis and Control for Intelligent Systems with AI Perception or Control","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制；人工智能","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control; Artificial intelligence","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"FAN Chenchen","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nWith the increasing adoption of AI-driven technologies, intelligent autonomous systems are incorporating more AI-based perception and control components, leading to a growing number of AI-in-the-loop systems. However, these AI modules often introduce uncertainties and errors that can compromise system reliability. Ensuring the safety and dependability of such systems is critical for real-world deployment. In this context, reachable set theory offers a promising framework for both safety analysis and the design of safe control strategies.\nThis project aims to apply reachable set methods to evaluate the safety of closed-loop intelligent systems that rely on AI perception or control. Based on the safety assessment results, adjusted control strategies will be developed to enhance the reliability and safety of AI-in-the-loop systems. \nThe specifications are given as follows:\nStudents are expected to deliver the following outcomes:\n1. Develop a dynamic model of the closed-loop system incorporating AI perception or control components.\n2. Compute or estimate the reachable set boundaries for the AI-in-the-loop intelligent system.\n3. Design control strategies to ensure the safety of the closed-loop system.\n4. The project will primarily involve numerical simulations, though experimental studies may also be conducted (if the student is interested).\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic knowledge of control theory, AI, and machine learning.\n2. Programming experience with MATLAB, Python or similar tools.","descZh":"项目描述：随着 AI 驱动技术的日益普及，智能自主系统集成了更多基于 AI 的感知与控制组件，导致 AI 在环系统数量不断增长。然而，这些 AI 模块常引入不确定性并可能出错，从而损害系统可靠性。确保此类系统的安全性与可靠性对其实际部署至关重要。在此背景下，可达集理论为安全分析与安全控制策略设计提供了有前景的框架。\n本项目旨在应用可达集方法评估依赖 AI 感知或控制的闭环智能系统的安全性；并基于安全评估结果，开发调整后的控制策略，以提升 AI 在环系统的可靠性与安全性。\n具体要求如下：学生预期交付以下成果：\n1. 建立包含 AI 感知或控制组件的闭环系统动力学模型；\n2. 计算或估计 AI 在环智能系统的可达集边界；\n3. 设计控制策略确保闭环系统安全；\n4. 项目以数值仿真为主（若学生有兴趣，也可开展实验研究）。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备控制理论、AI 与机器学习的基础知识；\n2. 有 MATLAB、Python 或类似工具的编程经验。","cats":["AERO","AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_PSLI-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于MPC的全向移动底盘集成规划与控制","titleEn":"Integrated Planning and Control for Omnidirectional Mobile Chassis via MPC","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"LI Panshuo","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Description: \nRobots equipped with an omnidirectional mobile chassis can translate in any directio, benefiting from their characteristics. Taking advantage of this feature, they can navigate flexibly in highly constrained, narrow, or dynamically changing environments. In traditional navigation architectures, the system usually relies on a decoupled pipeline: generating a collision-free geometric path first, and then utilizing a low-level controller to track it. However, in complex dynamic environments, simultaneously considering trajectory planning, actuator physical limits, and optimal control commands within a unified optimization framework can significantly improve agility, safety, and tracking precision. How to formulate the dynamic obstacle avoidance, state constraints, and actuator saturation into a unified model predictive control (MPC) problem, while ensuring real-time computational feasibility and driving smoothness, remains an open challenge.\nThe specifications are given as follows:\n1.Establish the kinematic and error dynamics models of the omnidirectional mobile chassis system in both the body and Earth frames.\n2.Formulate the MPC-based integrated planning and control framework. Design the cost function to optimize tracking accuracy and control smoothness, while strictly satisfying control input bounds (e.g., maximum wheel speed/acceleration) as constraints.\n3.When the chassis operates in dynamic environments, design a real-time collision avoidance strategy within the MPC formulation (e.g., utilizing dynamic distance constraints or control barrier functions) to adaptively re-plan optimal commands for moving obstacles.\n4.Utilize Matlab/Simulink, ROS/Gazebo softwares to implement and simulate the integrated MPC algorithm. Through extensive simulation experiments and analysis, assess the algorithm's real-time solving frequency, tracking error, and the allowable velocity limit of dynamic obstacles under tight maneuvering constraints.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1.A foundational understanding of mobile robot kinematics, MPC theory, and numerical optimization.\n2.Programming knowledge of Matlab and ROS/Gazebo (experience with optimization solvers such as CasADi or OSQP is a plus).","descZh":"描述：\n配备全向移动底盘的机器人可以朝任意方向平移，这得益于其自身特性。利用这一特点，它们能够在高度受限、狭窄或动态变化的环境中灵活导航。在传统导航架构中，系统通常依赖一条解耦的流水线：先规划出一条无碰撞的几何路径，再利用底层控制器对其进行跟踪。然而，在复杂的动态环境中，在一个统一的优化框架内同时考虑轨迹规划、执行器物理极限和最优控制指令，可以显著提升敏捷性、安全性和跟踪精度。如何将动态避障、状态约束和执行器饱和表述为一个统一的模型预测控制（MPC）问题，同时保证实时计算可行性和行驶平顺性，仍然是一个开放性挑战。\n规格要求如下：\n1. 在机体坐标系和大地坐标系中建立全向移动底盘系统的运动学和误差动力学模型。\n2. 建立基于 MPC 的一体化规划与控制框架。设计代价函数以优化跟踪精度和控制平顺性，同时作为约束严格满足控制输入界限（例如最大轮速/轮加速度）。\n3. 当底盘在动态环境中运行时，在 MPC 框架内设计实时避碰策略（例如利用动态距离约束或控制障碍函数，control barrier functions），以针对移动障碍物自适应地重新规划最优指令。\n4. 利用 Matlab/Simulink、ROS/Gazebo 软件实现并仿真一体化 MPC 算法。通过大量的仿真实验与分析，评估算法在严苛机动约束下的实时求解频率、跟踪误差，以及动态障碍物的允许速度上限。\n先修/并修课程：\n1. 对移动机器人运动学、MPC 理论和数值优化有基础了解。\n2. 具备 Matlab 和 ROS/Gazebo 的编程知识（有 CasADi 或 OSQP 等优化求解器的使用经验更佳）。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_PSLI-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"未知环境下多移动机器人在线覆盖路径规划","titleEn":"Online Coverage Path Planning for Multiple Mobile Robots in Unknown Environments","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"LI Panshuo","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description: \nMulti-robot coverage path planning (MCPP) enables multiple robots to cooperatively cover an entire accessible area and is widely used in environmental monitoring, inspection, cleaning, and search-and-rescue tasks. In unknown environments, robots operate without a complete prior map. During execution, they perceive the surroundings, update the map, identify uncovered areas, and adjust task assignments and coverage paths online. However, incomplete environmental information and continuous map updates may result in repeated coverage, unbalanced workloads, path conflicts, and inefficient robot motion. How to coordinate online area allocation and path replanning while ensuring complete coverage and reducing the overall completion time remains an open challenge.\nThe specifications are given as follows:\n1.Establish the motion and sensing models of the multi-robot system, and construct and update the \nenvironmental map using the sensing information collected during robot operation.\n2.Detect newly discovered free areas and generate coverage tasks online. Design a multi-robot task \nallocation strategy considering travel cost, remaining workload, and repeated coverage.\n3.Replan the coverage paths when new obstacles or free areas are detected, while reducing workload \nimbalance, repeated motion, and conflicts among robots.\n4.Implement and evaluate the proposed method through simulation and physical multi-robot \nexperiments. Assess its performance in terms of coverage completion time, path length, repeated \ncoverage, and workload balance.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1.A foundational understanding of online coverage path planning, multi-robot task allocation, and \nmobile robot navigation.\n2.Programming experience with Python, C++, MATLAB, or ROS.","descZh":"项目描述：多机器人覆盖路径规划（MCPP）使多台机器人能够协作覆盖整个可到达区域，广泛用于环境监测、巡检、清洁与搜救任务。在未知环境中，机器人没有完整先验地图。执行过程中，它们感知周围环境、更新地图、识别未覆盖区域，并在线调整任务分配与覆盖路径。然而，环境信息不完整与地图持续更新可能导致重复覆盖、负载不均、路径冲突与机器人运动低效。如何协调在线区域分配与路径重规划，同时确保完整覆盖并缩短整体完成时间，仍是开放挑战。\n具体要求：\n1. 建立多机器人系统的运动与感知模型，并利用机器人运行期间采集的感知信息构建与更新环境地图；\n2. 检测新发现的自由区域并在线生成覆盖任务；设计考虑行驶成本、剩余工作量与重复覆盖的多机器人任务分配策略；\n3. 在检测到新障碍或自由区域时重规划覆盖路径，同时减少机器人间的工作量失衡、重复运动与冲突；\n4. 通过仿真与实物多机器人实验实现并评估所提方法，从覆盖完成时间、路径长度、重复覆盖与工作量平衡等方面评估性能。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 对在线覆盖路径规划、多机器人任务分配与移动机器人导航有基础理解；\n2. 有 Python、C++、MATLAB 或 ROS 编程经验。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_JRLIU-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"受牧羊启发的规模化无人地面车辆编队导引控制","titleEn":"Shepherding-inspired Guidance Control for Large-scale UGV Formation","areaZh":"网络化系统控制","areaEn":"Networked Systems Control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"LIU Jinrong","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nWith the rapid growth of autonomous systems, large-scale unmanned ground vehicle (UGV) formations are increasingly applied in logistics, surveillance, agriculture, and disaster response. However, achieving and maintaining a cohesive, stable, and flexible formation remains challenging, especially in dynamic, unstructured environments. Traditional methods often cause the formation to break into subgroups and leave uneven gaps due to limited global coordination, leading to inefficiency and safety risks.\nInspired by natural shepherding behaviors where a few boundary agents (shepherds) guide the rest (sheep) into a cohesive group, this project will propose a novel shepherding-inspired guidance control framework for large-scale UGV formations. In this context, UGVs are dynamically assigned roles as herding or herded vehicles based on their spatial position. Herding UGVs influence the movement of herded UGVs by guiding them toward a virtual group center. This center is estimated through local negotiation among neighboring vehicles, enabling the entire formation to self-organize into a cohesive structure.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\nDuring the project, a distributed protocol will first be developed to allow each UGV to estimate the virtual group center using only local information. Second, a shepherding-inspired control law will be designed to guide the formation toward the negotiated center while maintaining uniformity and avoiding collisions. Third, simulations will be conducted to evaluate the performance of the proposed strategy under various initial conditions and dynamic scenarios. \nThis work aims to deliver a robust, scalable guidance framework for large-scale UGV formations, with potential applications in coordinated ground surveillance and disaster response.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nThe project is recommended for students with strong backgrounds in Advanced Mathematics, Linear Algebra, Control Engineering, and Programming (C/C++/MATLAB), and who can commit sufficient time and effort. Participants will receive comprehensive research training to enhance academic and problem-solving skills.","descZh":"项目描述：随着自主系统的快速发展，大规模无人地面车辆（UGV）编队越来越多地应用于物流、监视、农业与灾害响应。然而，在动态、非结构化环境中实现并保持凝聚、稳定且灵活的编队仍具挑战。传统方法常因全局协调有限而导致编队分裂成子群并留下不均匀间隙，带来效率低下与安全风险。\n受自然放牧行为启发——少数边界个体（牧羊犬）引导其余个体（羊）聚成一群——本项目将为大规模 UGV 编队提出一种新颖的类放牧引导控制框架。在此背景下，UGV 根据空间位置被动态分配为\"放牧\"或\"被放牧\"角色。放牧 UGV 通过将\"被放牧\"UGV 引向虚拟群中心来影响其运动；该中心通过相邻车辆间的局部协商估计，使整个编队自组织为凝聚结构。\n具体要求：项目期间，将首先开发一种分布式协议，使每台 UGV 仅利用局部信息估计虚拟群中心；其次，设计类放牧控制律，在保持均匀性与避免碰撞的同时引导编队趋向协商中心；最后，开展仿真，评估所提策略在不同初始条件与动态场景下的性能。\n本工作旨在为大规模 UGV 编队提供稳健、可扩展的引导框架，可应用于协调地面监视与灾害响应。\n先修/并修要求：建议具有扎实的高等数学、线性代数、控制工程与编程（C/C++/MATLAB）背景、并能投入充足时间与精力的学生参加。参与者将获得全面的研究训练，以提升学术与问题解决能力。","cats":["CTRL"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_YKCUI-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向具身无人机导航的先想象后行动VLA模型","titleEn":"Imagine-then-act VLA Models for Embodied UAV Navigation","areaZh":"AI；机器人","areaEn":"AI; Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"CUI Yukang","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nThis project investigates an imagine-then-act vision-language-action framework for\nembodied UAV navigation. Conventional VLA policies directly map the current observation\nand language instruction to an action, which can be brittle in occluded, unfamiliar, or\ndynamically changing environments. The proposed approach introduces an explicit\npredictive stage: before acting, the UAV imagines a task-relevant future visual state, latent\nstate, semantic occupancy map, or intermediate spatial goal, and then generates an action\nchunk that moves the vehicle toward that predicted future. The project is inspired by\nCoT-VLA: Visual Chain-of-Thought Reasoning for Vision-Language-Action Models (Zhao et\nal., 2025), which predicts future visual goals before actions; V-JEPA 2: Self-Supervised Video\nModels Enable Understanding, Prediction and Planning (Assran et al., 2025), which\nperforms action-conditioned planning in latent space; and BagelVLA: Enhancing\nLong-Horizon Manipulation via Linguistic Planning and Visual Forecasting (RSS 2026),\nwhich unifies planning, forecasting, and action generation.\nThe student will develop a UAV navigation pipeline that takes onboard images, vehicle\nstates, and natural-language instructions as inputs. A predictive module will generate one or\nmore candidate future representations. A policy or planner will then evaluate whether each\nimagined future is instruction-consistent, collision-free, and dynamically feasible, and will\ngenerate the corresponding short-horizon flight commands. The project may compare\npixel-space future prediction, latent-space prediction, and 3D semantic-goal prediction. A\nlightweight safety layer can reject implausible predictions and trigger replanning or hovering.\nExperiments will focus on urban canyons, indoor corridors, partial occlusion, viewpoint\nchanges, and unseen target configurations.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Review VLA models, visual chain-of-thought, video world models, latent predictive models,\nand UAV planning.\n2. Implement a direct-action UAV-VLA or vision-language navigation baseline.\n3. Add a future-goal prediction module and connect it to action generation or\nmodel-predictive planning.\n4. Compare imagined RGB goals, latent goals, and semantic spatial goals where feasible.\n5. Evaluate task success, collision rate, path efficiency, prediction consistency, inference\nlatency, and unseen-environment generalization.\nRepresentative references:\n[1] Zhao et al., CoT-VLA, 2025.\n[2] Assran et al., V-JEPA 2, 2025.\n[3] BagelVLA, RSS 2026.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nBasic Python programming is required. Prior knowledge of computer vision, robotics,\nmachine learning, PyTorch","descZh":"项目描述：本项目研究一种\"先想象、再行动\"的视觉-语言-动作框架，用于具身无人机导航。传统 VLA 策略将当前观测与语言指令直接映射为动作，在遮挡、陌生或动态变化环境中可能较为脆弱。所提方法引入显式预测阶段：行动之前，无人机先想象与任务相关的未来视觉状态、隐状态、语义占据图或中间空间目标，然后生成将该飞行器导向所预测未来的动作序列。本项目受以下工作启发：CoT-VLA（Zhao 等，2025）——在动作前预测未来视觉目标；V-JEPA 2（Assran 等，2025）——在隐空间进行动作条件规划；以及 BagelVLA（RSS 2026）——统一语言规划、视觉预测与动作生成。\n学生将开发一条无人机导航流程，以机载图像、飞行器状态与自然语言指令为输入。预测模块生成一个或多个候选未来表示；策略或规划器随后评估每个想象未来是否与指令一致、无碰撞且动力学可行，并生成相应的短时域飞控指令。项目可比较像素空间未来预测、隐空间预测与三维语义目标预测。轻量安全层可拒绝不合理预测并触发重规划或悬停。\n实验将聚焦城市峡谷、室内走廊、部分遮挡、视角变化与未见目标配置。\n具体要求：\n1. 综述 VLA 模型、视觉思维链、视频世界模型、隐式预测模型与无人机规划；\n2. 实现直接动作的 UAV-VLA 或视觉-语言导航基线；\n3. 添加未来目标预测模块，并将其连接到动作生成或模型预测规划；\n4. 在可行时比较想象的 RGB 目标、隐目标与语义空间目标；\n5. 评估任务成功率、碰撞率、路径效率、预测一致性、推理时延与未见环境泛化。\n代表性参考文献：\n[1] Zhao 等，CoT-VLA，2025；\n[2] Assran 等，V-JEPA 2，2025；\n[3] BagelVLA，RSS 2026。\n先修/并修要求：需具备基础 Python 编程能力；最好具备计算机视觉、机器人、机器学习、PyTorch 方面的先前知识。","cats":["AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_YKCUI-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"具有失败记忆与在线纠错的自改进无人机VLA模型","titleEn":"Self-improving UAV VLA Models with Failure Memory and Online Correction","areaZh":"AI；机器人","areaEn":"AI; Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"CUI Yukang","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nThis project investigates a self-improving UAV vision-language-action system that learns\nfrom deployment failures rather than relying only on fixed offline demonstrations. Real UAV\nmissions inevitably produce long-tail failures, including incorrect target grounding, unsafe\nwaypoint selection, visual occlusion, wind-induced tracking error, action drift, and infeasible\nlanguage commands. A robust embodied agent should detect these failures, store structured\nfailure memories, generate corrective behavior, and reuse past experience when similar\nsituations reappear. The project is motivated by RoboMonkey: Scaling Test-Time Sampling\nand Verification for Vision-Language-Action Models (Kwok et al., CoRL 2025), RISE:\nSelf-Improving Robot Policy with Compositional World Model (Yang et al., RSS 2026),\nSelf-Correcting VLA: Online Action Refinement via Sparse World Imagination (Liu et al.,\n2026), and pi*_0.6: a VLA That Learns From Experience (Amin et al., RSS 2026). These\nworks indicate a shift from static behavior cloning toward verification, imagined rollouts,\nreinforcement learning, and continual post-training.\nThe student will build a closed-loop UAV learning framework with four modules: failure\ndetection, failure diagnosis and memory, online correction, and policy improvement. During\nnavigation, the system will monitor progress, visual-language consistency, collision risk,\ntracking error, and uncertainty. When a failure or near-failure is detected, it will save the\nobservation-action-task context and retrieve similar failures from an episodic memory. A\nverifier, world model, safety planner, or residual policy will generate a correction such as\nstopping, hovering, backtracking, changing viewpoint, or replanning. Successful correction\ntrajectories can then be used for lightweight online adaptation, replay-based post-training, or\npolicy selection without retraining the full VLA backbone.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Review VLA verification, self-correcting policies, failure analysis, world-model-based\nimprovement, and continual robot learning.\n2. Implement a UAV navigation baseline and define a taxonomy of reproducible failure\nmodes.\n3. Develop failure detection and episodic retrieval using visual, state, task, and action\nfeatures.\n4. Implement at least one online correction method and one replay-based or residual policy\nimprovement method.\n5. Evaluate first-attempt success, recovery success, repeated-failure rate, adaptation speed,\nsafety, and retention of previously learned behavior.\nRepresentative references:\n[1] Kwok et al., RoboMonkey, CoRL 2025.\n[2] Yang et al., RISE, RSS 2026.\n[3] Liu et al., Self-Correcting VLA, 2026.\n[4] Amin et al., pi*_0.6, RSS 2026.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nBasic Python programming is required. Prior knowledge of robotics, reinforcement learning,\ncontinual learning, PyTorch, ROS, would be advantageous.","descZh":"项目描述：\n本项目研究一个自我改进的无人机（UAV）视觉-语言-动作（vision-language-action，VLA）系统，该系统从部署失败中学习，而非仅依赖固定的离线示范。真实的无人机任务不可避免地会产生长尾失败，包括错误的目标定位（grounding）、不安全的航点选择、视觉遮挡、风致跟踪误差、动作漂移以及不可行的语言指令。一个稳健的具身智能体应能检测这些失败、存储结构化的失败记忆、生成纠正性行为，并在类似情形再次出现时复用过往经验。本项目的动机来源于以下工作：RoboMonkey: Scaling Test-Time Sampling and Verification for Vision-Language-Action Models（Kwok et al., CoRL 2025）、RISE: Self-Improving Robot Policy with Compositional World Model（Yang et al., RSS 2026）、Self-Correcting VLA: Online Action Refinement via Sparse World Imagination（Liu et al., 2026）以及 pi*_0.6: a VLA That Learns From Experience（Amin et al., RSS 2026）。这些工作表明，研究正从静态行为克隆转向验证、想象推演（imagined rollouts）、强化学习和持续后训练。\n学生将构建一个闭环无人机学习框架，包含四个模块：失败检测、失败诊断与记忆、在线纠正以及策略改进。在导航过程中，系统将监控进度、视觉-语言一致性、碰撞风险、跟踪误差和不确定性。当检测到失败或接近失败时，系统将保存观测-动作-任务上下文，并从情节记忆（episodic memory）中检索相似的失败。验证器、世界模型、安全规划器或残差策略将生成纠正行为，例如停止、悬停、后退、改变视角或重新规划。成功的纠正轨迹随后可用于轻量级在线适应、基于回放的后训练或策略选择，而无需重新训练完整的 VLA 骨干网络。\n规格要求如下：\n1. 综述 VLA 验证、自纠正策略、失败分析、基于世界模型的改进以及持续机器人学习。\n2. 实现一个无人机导航基线，并定义一套可复现失败模式的分类体系。\n3. 使用视觉、状态、任务和动作特征开发失败检测与情节检索。\n4. 实现至少一种在线纠正方法，以及一种基于回放或残差策略的改进方法。\n5. 评估首次尝试成功率、恢复成功率、重复失败率、适应速度、安全性以及对先前已学习行为的保持。\n代表性参考文献：\n[1] Kwok et al., RoboMonkey, CoRL 2025.\n[2] Yang et al., RISE, RSS 2026.\n[3] Liu et al., Self-Correcting VLA, 2026.\n[4] Amin et al., pi*_0.6, RSS 2026.\n先修及并修要求：\n需要具备基本的 Python 编程能力。具备机器人学、强化学习、持续学习、PyTorch、ROS 等方面的先验知识将是有利的。","cats":["AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_YKCUI-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"从互联网飞行视频学习隐式动作以实现具身无人机控制","titleEn":"Latent Action Learning from Internet Flight Videos for Embodied UAV Control","areaZh":"AI；机器人","areaEn":"AI; Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"CUI Yukang","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nLarge robot foundation models are constrained by the scarcity and cost of action-labeled\nrobot trajectories. In contrast, the Internet contains abundant first-person drone videos,\naerial inspection videos, and flight recordings without corresponding thrust, body-rate,\nvelocity, or waypoint commands. This project investigates whether reusable motion\nknowledge can be discovered from such videos as latent actions and transferred to\nembodied UAV navigation and control. The project is motivated by LAPA, which learns latent\nactions from unlabeled videos; GR-2, which uses web-scale video dynamics for robot policy\nlearning; V-JEPA 2, which learns predictive latent representations for planning; and FAST,\nwhich develops efficient action tokenization for VLA models.\nThe student will develop a video-pretrained UAV learning pipeline. First, public or Internet\nflight videos will be curated into short temporal clips. A latent-action encoder or tokenizer will\nthen infer compact discrete or continuous motion representations from transitions between\nobservations. The learned latent actions will be conditioned on current visual observations\nand optional language or task goals. Using a relatively small amount of action-labeled data\ngenerated in AirSim, Isaac Sim, or ROS/Gazebo, a lightweight decoder will map latent\nactions to executable waypoint, velocity, or body-rate commands. A conventional flight\ncontroller or safety planner may be retained as the low-level execution layer.\nThe project may compare discrete action tokens, continuous latent dynamics, and temporally\nchunked actions. Experimental tasks may include visual target following, obstacle avoidance,\nlanguage-conditioned waypoint navigation, and transfer to unseen environments or UAV\ndynamics. The main question is whether video pretraining can reduce the amount of\naction-labeled UAV data required while improving generalization. Performance will be\nevaluated using task success rate, collision rate, trajectory error, labeled-data efficiency,\ntransfer performance, and inference latency.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Review representative work on latent-action pretraining, video world models, action\ntokenization, VLA models, and learning-based UAV control.\n2. Curate public flight videos and generate a small action-labeled UAV dataset in simulation.\n3. Implement a latent-action discovery model and an action decoder for UAV control.\n4. Compare video-pretrained policies with behavior-cloning baselines trained from scratch.\nRepresentative references:\n[1] Ye et al., LAPA: Latent Action Pretraining from Videos, 2024.\n[2] Wu et al., GR-2: A Generative Video-Language-Action Model, 2024.\n[3] Assran et al., V-JEPA 2, 2025.\n[4] Black et al., FAST: Efficient Action Tokenization for VLA Models, 2025.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nExperience in machine learning, PyTorch, ROS/Gazebo, or UAV control would be advantageous.","descZh":"项目描述：\n大型机器人基础模型受到带动作标注的机器人轨迹稀缺且成本高昂的制约。相比之下，互联网上存在大量第一人称无人机视频、航拍巡检视频和飞行录像，但它们没有对应的推力、机体角速率、速度或航点指令。本项目研究能否从这些视频中发现可复用的运动知识，将其作为潜在动作（latent actions），并迁移到具身无人机（UAV）导航与控制中。本项目的动机来自：LAPA（从无标注视频中学习潜在动作）；GR-2（利用网络规模视频动力学进行机器人策略学习）；V-JEPA 2（学习用于规划的可预测潜在表征）；以及 FAST（为 VLA 模型开发高效的动作分词化）。\n学生将开发一条以视频预训练的无人机学习流水线。首先，将公开或互联网上的飞行视频整理成短时片段。随后，一个潜在动作编码器或分词器将从观测之间的状态转移中推断出紧凑的离散或连续运动表征。所学到的潜在动作将以当前视觉观测以及可选的语言或任务目标为条件。利用在 AirSim、Isaac Sim 或 ROS/Gazebo 中生成的相对少量的带动作标注数据，一个轻量级解码器将把潜在动作映射为可执行的航点、速度或机体角速率指令。可以保留一个传统的飞控或安全规划器作为底层执行层。\n本项目可以比较离散动作 token、连续潜在动力学以及时间分块动作。实验任务可包括视觉目标跟随、避障、语言条件化的航点导航，以及向未见环境或无人机动力学的迁移。核心问题是：视频预训练能否在提升泛化能力的同时，减少所需的带动作标注无人机数据量。性能将使用任务成功率、碰撞率、轨迹误差、标注数据效率、迁移性能和推理延迟来评估。\n规格要求如下：\n1. 综述潜在动作预训练、视频世界模型、动作分词化、VLA 模型以及基于学习的无人机控制方面的代表性工作。\n2. 整理公开飞行视频，并在仿真中生成一个小规模带动作标注的无人机数据集。\n3. 实现一个潜在动作发现模型和一个用于无人机控制的动作解码器。\n4. 将视频预训练策略与从头训练的模仿学习（behavior-cloning）基线进行比较。\n代表性参考文献：\n[1] Ye et al., LAPA: Latent Action Pretraining from Videos, 2024.\n[2] Wu et al., GR-2: A Generative Video-Language-Action Model, 2024.\n[3] Assran et al., V-JEPA 2, 2025.\n[4] Black et al., FAST: Efficient Action Tokenization for VLA Models, 2025.\n先修/并修课程：\n具有机器学习、PyTorch、ROS/Gazebo 或无人机控制方面的经验将是加分项。","cats":["AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YAMINW-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"低空姿态约束下无人机的隐私保护分布式优化","titleEn":"Privacy-preserving Distributed Optimization for UAVs Subject to Low Attitude","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"WANG Yamin","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nOperating UAV swarms in low-altitude environments (e.g., urban canyons or cluttered terrain) presents unique challenges. Signal occlusion from buildings often makes centralized control unreliable, necessitating distributed optimization (DO) where UAVs coordinate locally to achieve global goals like formation maintenance or coverage. However, the frequent exchange of trajectory or sensing data between neighbors exposes the swarm to eavesdropping and inference attacks.\nThe objective of this project is to design DO algorithms that function robustly under the communication constraints of low-altitude flight while providing formal privacy guarantees. You will implement baseline solvers (e.g., decentralized gradient descent) and integrate privacy mechanisms (such as Differential Privacy) to ensure that sensitive mission data—such as flight paths or target locations—remains secure during consensus. \nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Problem formulation: Model the DO problem incorporating constraints specific to low-altitude environments (e.g., limited line-of-sight communication or obstacle avoidance).\n2. Algorithm design: Integrate privacy-preserving mechanisms into the distributed solver.\n3. Performance analysis: Analyze the trade-offs between privacy levels, convergence speed, and the communication bandwidth required.\n4. Simulation: Evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed algorithms in simulated low-altitude scenarios (e.g., formation flying through obstacles).\nPreferred Skills:\n1. Basic knowledge in feedback control, probability theory, graph theory, or optimization.\n2. Familiarity with MATLAB or Python programming languages.","descZh":"项目描述：在低空环境（如城市峡谷或杂乱地形）中运行无人机集群面临独特挑战。建筑物的信号遮挡往往使集中控制不可靠，因此需要分布式优化（DO）——无人机通过局部协调实现编队保持或覆盖等全局目标。然而，邻居间频繁交换轨迹或传感数据使集群暴露于窃听与推断攻击之下。\n本项目目标是设计在低空飞行通信约束下稳健运行、同时提供形式化隐私保证的 DO 算法。你将实现基线求解器（如去中心化梯度下降），并集成差分隐私等隐私机制，确保飞行路径或目标位置等敏感任务数据在共识过程中保持安全。\n具体要求：\n1. 问题构建：对融入低空环境特有约束（如有视线限制的通信或避障）的 DO 问题进行建模；\n2. 算法设计：将隐私保护机制集成到分布式求解器中；\n3. 性能分析：分析隐私水平、收敛速度与所需通信带宽之间的权衡；\n4. 仿真：在模拟低空场景（如穿越障碍物的编队飞行）中评估所提算法的有效性。\n偏好技能：\n1. 具备反馈控制、概率论、图论或优化的基础知识；\n2. 熟悉 MATLAB 或 Python 编程语言。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YAMINW-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"隐私保护分布式优化：在人体活动识别中的应用","titleEn":"Privacy-preserving Distributed Optimization: An Application to Human Activity Recognition","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制；人工智能","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control; Artificial intelligent","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"WANG Yamin","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nWearable devices and mobile sensors are increasingly used for human activity recognition in applications such as healthcare monitoring, rehabilitation, and context-aware services. In practice, sensor data are distributed across different users and may contain sensitive information about their behaviors and physical conditions. Transferring all data to a central server may therefore raise privacy concerns and create communication bottlenecks.\nThis project considers a network of edge devices that collaboratively train a human activity recognition model using only locally stored data and neighbor-to-neighbor communication. Each device represents an individual user and participates in the learning process through distributed optimization. Although raw data remain on local devices, the exchanged gradients or model parameters may still reveal sensitive information to curious participants or external observers.\nThe objective of this project is to develop and evaluate privacy-preserving distributed optimization algorithms for decentralized AI. The student will first implement a non-private decentralized learning method, such as decentralized stochastic gradient descent, and then incorporate a differential-privacy mechanism into the exchanged updates. The threat model and privacy metric will be clearly defined to quantify the level of privacy protection.\nThe proposed algorithms will be evaluated using a public human activity recognition dataset under different user data distributions and simulated communication-network topologies. The project will investigate the trade-offs among classification accuracy, privacy level, convergence performance, network connectivity, and communication cost.\nThe specifications are given as follows:\n1. Formulate decentralized human activity recognition as a distributed optimization problem over a communication network, where each agent has access only to its locally stored sensor data.\n2. Implement a baseline decentralized learning algorithm, such as decentralized stochastic gradient descent, and develop a privacy-preserving variant using differential privacy. Clearly define the threat model and privacy metric.\n3. Analyze or empirically evaluate the effects of privacy level, data heterogeneity, and network connectivity on classification accuracy, convergence performance, and communication cost.\n4. Validate the proposed algorithms using a public human activity recognition dataset under different data distributions and communication topologies. Compare the results with suitable benchmarks, such as centralized training, local-only learning, and non-private decentralized learning.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic knowledge in machine learning, probability theory, graph theory, and optimization.\n2. Familiarity with MATLAB and Python programming languages.","descZh":"项目描述：可穿戴设备与移动传感器越来越多地用于人体活动识别，应用于医疗监测、康复与环境感知服务等场景。实践中，传感器数据分布于不同用户，且可能包含关于其行为与身体状况的敏感信息。将所有数据传输至中心服务器可能引发隐私顾虑并造成通信瓶颈。\n本项目考虑一个边缘设备网络，仅利用本地存储数据与邻居间通信协作训练人体活动识别模型。每台设备代表一个用户，通过分布式优化参与学习过程。尽管原始数据保留在本地设备上，但交换的梯度或模型参数仍可能向好奇的参与者或外部观察者泄露敏感信息。\n本项目目标是开发并评估面向去中心化 AI 的隐私保护分布式优化算法。学生将首先实现非隐私的去中心化学习方法（如去中心化随机梯度下降），然后将差分隐私机制融入所交换的更新。将明确定义威胁模型与隐私度量，以量化隐私保护水平。\n将使用公开人体活动识别数据集，在不同用户数据分布与模拟通信网络拓扑下评估所提算法。项目将研究分类精度、隐私水平、收敛性能、网络连通性与通信成本之间的权衡。\n具体要求：\n1. 将去中心化人体活动识别构建为通信网络上的分布式优化问题，其中每个智能体只能访问其本地存储的传感器数据；\n2. 实现基线去中心化学习算法（如去中心化随机梯度下降），并使用差分隐私开发隐私保护变体；明确定义威胁模型与隐私度量；\n3. 分析或实证评估隐私水平、数据异构性与网络连通性对分类精度、收敛性能与通信成本的影响；\n4. 使用公开人体活动识别数据集，在不同数据分布与通信拓扑下验证所提算法，并与集中式训练、纯本地学习与非隐私去中心化学习等合适基准比较。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备机器学习、概率论、图论与优化的基础知识；\n2. 熟悉 MATLAB 与 Python 编程语言。","cats":["AERO","AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YAMINW-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"隐私约束下的在线学习：一种分布式优化方法","titleEn":"Online Learning with Privacy Constraints: A Distributed Optimization Approach","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制；人工智能","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control; Artificial intelligence","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"WANG Yamin","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nOnline learning has found its way into numerous applications, from autonomous vehicles to federated healthcare systems. This technique enables systems to adapt in real time by updating models as fresh data arrives. However, in practice, data often sits on multiple devices or within different organizations, and moving it to a single location is either too costly or violates privacy regulations. Even sharing intermediate results, like gradients or model parameters, can disclose sensitive information. \nTo address this challenge, this project will explore distributed optimization algorithms for online learning that can learn collaboratively while ensuring privacy protection. The goal is to strike the right balance between data confidentiality and model accuracy.\nThe specifications are given as follows:\n1. Formulate the online learning problem over a communication network. \n2. Implement distributed optimization methods such as Online Gradient Descent or Distributed ADMM. Integrate different privacy-preserving mechanisms into the design of the algorithms.\n3. Analyze the performance of the proposed algorithms.\n4. Evaluated the effectiveness of the algorithms through simulation.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic knowledge in probability theory, graph theory, and optimization theory.\n2. Familiarity with MATLAB and Python programming languages.","descZh":"项目描述：\n在线学习（online learning）已进入众多应用领域，从自动驾驶汽车到联邦式医疗系统。这项技术通过在新数据到来时更新模型，使系统能够实时适应。然而在实践中，数据往往分布在多个设备上或不同组织内部，将其集中到单一地点要么成本过高，要么违反隐私法规。即使共享梯度或模型参数等中间结果，也可能泄露敏感信息。\n为应对这一挑战，本项目将探索用于在线学习的分布式优化算法，使其能够在确保隐私保护的同时进行协作学习。目标是在数据机密性和模型精度之间取得恰当的平衡。\n规格要求如下：\n1. 在通信网络上形式化表述在线学习问题。\n2. 实现诸如在线梯度下降（Online Gradient Descent）或分布式 ADMM 等分布式优化方法。将不同的隐私保护机制集成到算法设计中。\n3. 分析所提出算法的性能。\n4. 通过仿真评估算法的有效性。\n先修及并修要求：\n1. 具备概率论、图论和优化理论的基础知识。\n2. 熟悉 MATLAB 和 Python 编程语言。","cats":["AERO","AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_LLIN-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"有限域上一致性网络的模型降阶","titleEn":"Model Reduction of Consensus Networks over Finite Fields","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Lin Lin","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description: \n Model reduction for large-scale dynamical systems constructs lower-dimensional representations while preserving essential behaviors. This project focuses on consensus problems in finite field networks, where agent states take values from a finite field and exact finite-time agreement is required. The state space grows exponentially with the number of nodes, making direct analysis prohibitive. Recent results show that consensus in finite-field networks can be reduced to a quotient transition system with far fewer nodes, and further collapses into a single rooted tree structure when network matrices satisfy certain conditions. This project aims to develop and analyze such tree-structured reduced-order models, characterize reduction conditions, and evaluate performance across different network configurations. Tools include algebraic graph theory, linear algebra over finite fields, and quotient-based reduction methods.\n Specifications are given as follows:\n 1. Study consensus protocols over finite fields and the structural conditions for network matrices.\n 2. Construct the coarsest equivalence relation for consensus reduction.\n 3. Derive the tree-structured quotient system and establish property equivalence.\n 4. Evaluate reduction effectiveness via numerical simulations on random networks.\n Pre- and co-requisites:\n 1. Basic graph theory, linear algebra, and discrete dynamical systems.\n 2. Python programming for simulation.\n Special note:\n Students interested in algebraic system theory and network science with a balance of theory and computation should find this project appealing.","descZh":"项目描述：大规模动力学系统的模型降阶是在保留关键行为的前提下构建低维表示。本项目聚焦有限域网络中的共识问题——智能体状态取自有限域，并要求在精确有限时间内达成一致。状态空间随节点数呈指数增长，使直接分析不可行。近期结果表明，有限域网络中的共识问题可约化为节点数少得多的商转移系统，且当网络矩阵满足一定条件时可进一步坍缩为单一有根树结构。本项目旨在开发并分析此类树结构降阶模型，刻画降阶条件，并评估不同网络配置下的性能。所用手法包括代数图论、有限域上的线性代数与基于商系统的降阶方法。\n具体要求：\n1. 研究有限域上的共识协议及网络矩阵的结构条件；\n2. 构建用于共识降阶的最粗等价关系；\n3. 推导树结构商系统并建立性质等价性；\n4. 通过随机网络上的数值仿真评估降阶有效性。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备基础图论、线性代数与离散动力学系统知识；\n2. 会用 Python 编程进行仿真。\n特别说明：对代数系统理论与网络科学感兴趣、兼顾理论与计算的学生会觉得该项目颇具吸引力。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_LLIN-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"扰动通信网络的一致性","titleEn":"Consensus of Perturbed Communication Networks","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Lin Lin","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\n Consensus in multi-agent systems enables distributed agents to reach agreement through local information exchange over a communication network. The convergence of consensus protocols is determined by the algebraic properties of the network topology. In ideal networks, convergence is well understood and guaranteed under mild connectivity conditions. However, real-world networks are subject to various perturbations, including link failures, transmission delays, and switching topologies, which may slow down convergence, cause oscillations, or even lead to divergence. This project focuses on the convergence analysis of consensus dynamics in perturbed networks, aiming to characterize conditions for asymptotic agreement and establish performance bounds under different types of perturbations. We will employ algebraic graph theory, matrix perturbation analysis, and dynamical systems theory to investigate the robustness of consensus dynamics.\n Specifications:\n 1. Study convergence conditions for consensus in ideal networks using algebraic graph theory.\n 2. Formulate the perturbed consensus problem with common network imperfections.\n 3. Analyze how perturbations affect eigenvalues and eigenvectors that govern convergence.\n 4. Evaluate theoretical results via numerical simulations under varying perturbation levels.\n Pre- and co-requisites:\n 1. Basic graph theory, linear algebra, and ordinary differential equations.\n 2. Python programming for simulation and visualization.\n Special note:\n Students interested in the intersection of control theory and network science with a balance of theory and computation should find this project appealing.","descZh":"项目描述：多智能体系统中的共识使分布式智能体能够通过通信网络上的局部信息交换达成一致。共识协议的收敛性由网络拓扑的代数性质决定。在理想网络中，收敛在温和连通条件下已被充分理解并得到保证。然而，真实网络会受到各种扰动，包括链路故障、传输时延与拓扑切换，这些扰动可能减缓收敛、引起振荡，甚至导致发散。本项目聚焦受扰动网络中共识动力学的收敛分析，旨在刻画渐近一致的达成条件，并建立不同类型扰动下的性能界。我们将采用代数图论、矩阵摄动分析与动力系统理论来研究共识动力学的鲁棒性。\n具体要求：\n1. 利用代数图论研究理想网络中共识的收敛条件；\n2. 结合常见网络缺陷构建受扰动共识问题；\n3. 分析扰动如何影响决定收敛的特征值与特征向量；\n4. 通过不同扰动水平下的数值仿真评估理论结果。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备基础图论、线性代数与常微分方程知识；\n2. 会用 Python 编程进行仿真与可视化。\n特别说明：对控制理论与网络科学交叉感兴趣、兼顾理论与计算的学生会觉得该项目颇具吸引力。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_LLIN-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"扰动通信网络的最小化恢复","titleEn":"Minimal Recovery for Perturbed Communication Networks","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Lin Lin","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description: \n Communication networks are fundamental to modern infrastructure, supporting data exchange in transportation, power grids, and multi-robots swarm systems. However, these networks are often subject to perturbations—such as link errors, node failures, or malicious attacks—that disrupt their functionality. Ensuring rapid and efficient recovery while minimizing resource expenditure is crucial for maintaining network resilience. This project focuses on developing control strategies for minimal recovery in perturbed communication networks, where the goal is to restore network functionality with the least possible modifications (e.g., minimal edge additions, weight adjustments, or topology changes). We will employ graph-theoretic methods, optimization techniques, and control theory to formulate and solve the recovery problem.\n Specifications are given as follows:\n 1. Study the characterization of communication networks.\n 2. Formulate the perturbation and recovery problem in communication networks.\n 3. Leverage algebraic graph theory to design minimal recovery algorithms.\n 4. Evaluate minimal recovery strategies via some realistic models (e.g., ,multi-agent systems and social networks).\n Pre- and co-requisites:\n 1. Basic concepts of network science, graphical algorithms, and control strategies.\n 2. Familiarity with Python for algorithm implementation.\n Special note:\n Students who like numerical simulation and theoretical study should find the project interesting.","descZh":"项目描述：通信网络是现代基础设施的基础，支撑着交通、电网与多机器人集群系统中的数据交换。然而，这些网络常受链路差错、节点故障或恶意攻击等扰动影响，导致功能中断。在最小化资源消耗的同时实现快速高效恢复，对维持网络韧性至关重要。本项目聚焦于为受扰动通信网络开发最小恢复控制策略，目标是以尽可能少的修改（如最少边添加、权重调整或拓扑变化）恢复网络功能。我们将采用图论方法、优化技术与控制理论来构建并求解该恢复问题。\n具体要求：\n1. 研究通信网络的特征刻画；\n2. 构建通信网络中的扰动与恢复问题；\n3. 利用代数图论设计最小恢复算法；\n4. 通过若干真实模型（如多智能体系统与社会网络）评估最小恢复策略。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备网络科学、图算法与控制策略的基本概念；\n2. 熟悉 Python 以实现算法。\n特别说明：喜欢数值仿真与理论研究的学生会发现该项目颇具吸引力。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_LLIN-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"有限域上多智能体系统中的基于学习的控制","titleEn":"Learning-based Control in Multi-Agent Systems over Finite Fields","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Lin Lin","moderator":"Professor LAM James","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description: \n Multi-agent systems are widely used in robotics, distributed computing, and smart grids, where agents must cooperate under communication and computational constraints. Traditional control strategies often rely on real-valued states, but many practical systems—such as digital networks, error-correcting codes, and cryptographic protocols—operate over finite fields, where states and operations are discrete. This project explores learning-based control strategies for multi-agent systems over finite fields, combining reinforcement learning, algebraic graph theory, and distributed control. The goal is to develop adaptive control policies that enable agents to achieve coordination, consensus, or task allocation despite limited communication and discrete state constraints.\n Specifications are given as follows:\n 1. Study algebraic representations of agent dynamics under finite-field constraints.\n 2. Analyze the controllability of multi-agent systems over finite fields.\n 3. Design reinforcement learning policies adapted for finite-field operations.\n 4. Implement learning-based control algorithms on some mathematical models.\n Pre- and co-requisites:\n Basic concepts of reinforcement learning, algebraic graph theory, and state-space control theory.\n Special note:\n Students who like artificial intelligence and theoretical study should find the project interesting.","descZh":"项目描述：多智能体系统广泛应用于机器人学、分布式计算与智能电网等领域，其中智能体须在通信与计算约束下协作。传统控制策略常依赖实值状态，但许多实际系统——如数字网络、纠错码与密码协议——运行在有限域上，其状态与运算是离散的。本项目探索有限域上多智能体系统的基于学习的控制策略，融合强化学习、代数图论与分布式控制。目标是开发自适应控制策略，使智能体在有限通信与离散状态约束下实现协调、共识或任务分配。\n具体要求：\n1. 研究有限域约束下智能体动力学的代数表示；\n2. 分析有限域上多智能体系统的可控性；\n3. 设计适用于有限域运算的强化学习策略；\n4. 在若干数学模型上实现基于学习的控制算法。\n先修/并修要求：具备强化学习、代数图论与状态空间控制理论的基本概念。\n特别说明：喜欢人工智能与理论研究的学生会发现该项目颇有吸引力。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-XULIZHI-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"可编程形变材料","titleEn":"Programmable Shape Morphing Materials","areaZh":"材料；力学；机器人","areaEn":"Materials; Mechanics; Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor XU Lizhi","moderator":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Programmable shape morphing materials serve\r\nas critical components for next-generation human-interfacing robotics. This project aims to fabricate\r\nsilk fibroin hydrogels with gradient network structures using a low electric\r\nfield. You will study how the applied voltage and duration control the bending\r\ncurvature and 3D deformation of hydrogel strips and patterned sheets. The goal\r\nis to demonstrate reversible, reprogrammable shape changes (e.g., bending,\r\ntwisting, kirigami structures) and explore their potential as soft actuators\r\nfor robotics or flexible electronics. You will gain hands-on experience in\r\nhydrogel fabrication, electric-field-induced structuring, and mechanical\r\ncharacterization, with final outputs as photo/video records of various morphing\r\nconfigurations.","descZh":"可编程形状变形材料是下一代人机交互机器人的关键组成部分。本项目旨在利用低电场制备具有梯度网络结构的丝素蛋白水凝胶。你将研究施加电压与时长如何控制水凝胶条带及图案化薄片的弯曲曲率与三维形变。目标是演示可逆、可重编程的形状变化（如弯曲、扭转、kirigami 剪纸结构），并探索其作为机器人或柔性电子软体驱动器的潜力。你将获得水凝胶制备、电场诱导结构成形与力学表征的实操经验，最终以多种变形构型的照片/视频记录作为成果。","cats":["MAT","MECH","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-XULIZHI-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于可穿戴交互的柔性导电水凝胶传感器","titleEn":"Flexible conductive hydrogel sensor for wearable interaction","areaZh":"生物医学工程；电子；材料","areaEn":"Biomedical Engineering; Electronics; Materials","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor XU Lizhi","moderator":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-07 12:34:01","descriptionText":"Flexible\r\nsensors can detect human movement, pressure, and touch, making them useful for\r\nwearable electronics, health monitoring, and human – machine interaction. Hydrogels\r\nare promising materials for flexible sensors because they are soft,\r\nstretchable, and similar to biological tissues. \n In this\r\nproject, students will prepare conductive hydrogels using water-rich polymer\r\nnetworks and conductive components. They will investigate how pressing,\r\nstretching, and bending the hydrogel affect its electrical resistance. \n Students\r\nwill connect the hydrogel sensor to a simple circuit or microcontroller and\r\ndevelop a prototype such as a touch-sensitive pad, finger-bending sensor, or\r\npressure-controlled light. This project will provide experience in\r\nbiomaterials, basic electronics, electrical measurements, data analysis, and\r\nwearable device design.","descZh":"柔性传感器能够检测人体运动、压力与触摸，因而可用于可穿戴电子设备、健康监测与人机交互。水凝胶是柔性传感器的理想材料，因为它们柔软、可拉伸且与生物组织相似。\n在本项目中，学生将使用富水聚合物网络与导电组分制备导电水凝胶。他们将研究按压、拉伸与弯折水凝胶如何影响其电阻。\n学生将把水凝胶传感器连接到简单的电路或微控制器上，并开发一个原型，如触敏垫、手指弯曲传感器或压力控制灯。本项目将提供生物材料、基础电子学、电气测量、数据分析与可穿戴设备设计方面的经验。","cats":["BIO","ELE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-XULIZHI-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"由吸水驱动的仿生水凝胶软体抓手","titleEn":"Bio-inspired hydrogel soft gripper driven by water absorption","areaZh":"机器人；材料；力学","areaEn":"Robotics; Materials; Mechanics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor XU Lizhi","moderator":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-07 12:34:01","descriptionText":"Many plants can change\r\ntheir shapes in response to water. For example, flowers open, leaves bend, and\r\nseed pods curl because different parts of their structures absorb water at\r\ndifferent rates. This natural mechanism can inspire the design of simple soft\r\nrobots. \n In this project,\r\nstudents will fabricate hydrogel structures that bend or curl when placed in\r\nwater. They will investigate how material composition, layer thickness, shape,\r\nand environmental conditions affect the bending speed and deformation of the\r\nhydrogel. \n Students will use simple\r\nmolds or 3D-printed structures to construct a flower-like actuator or a soft\r\ngripper capable of picking up lightweight objects. The project will help\r\nstudents understand smart materials, mechanical deformation, biomimetic design,\r\nand soft robotics.","descZh":"许多植物会随水分变化改变自身形状。例如，花朵开放、叶片弯曲、种荚卷曲，这是因为其结构的不同部分以不同速率吸水。这一自然机制可为简单软体机器人的设计提供灵感。\n在本项目中，学生将制备入水后弯曲或卷曲的水凝胶结构，研究材料成分、层厚、形状与环境条件如何影响水凝胶的弯曲速度与形变。\n学生将使用简易模具或 3D 打印结构，构建花形驱动器或可拾取轻质物体的软体夹爪。项目将帮助学生理解智能材料、机械变形、仿生设计与软体机器人。","cats":["MAT","MECH","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-XULIZHI-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"自变形的3D微传感器","titleEn":"Self-Transforming 3D Microsensors","areaZh":"电子；制造；MEMS；机器人；材料；力学","areaEn":"Electronics; Manufacuring; MEMS; Robotics; Materials; Mechanics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor XU Lizhi","moderator":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-07 12:34:01","descriptionText":"Conventional microelectronic devices are\r\npredominantly fabricated in planar formats, which limits their ability to\r\ninterface with curved surfaces, confined spaces, and dynamic environments.\r\nThree-dimensional microsystems offer a promising route towards more adaptable\r\nand sensitive devices for future applications in wearable electronics,\r\nbiomedical technologies, soft robotics, and intelligent machines. \n In this project, you will investigate\r\nself-transforming MEMS structures enabled by stress-engineered PVD thin films.\r\nBy integrating strain-sensing elements, the device can convert mechanical\r\ndeformation into measurable electrical signals. The project will explore the\r\nrelationship between structural transformation and sensing performance, with\r\nthe aim of developing new three-dimensional sensors beyond conventional planar\r\ndesigns. \n Successful completion of this project will\r\nprovide experience in MEMS, thin-film engineering, microfabrication, and sensor\r\ncharacterization.","descZh":"传统微电子器件主要以平面形式制造，这限制了它们与曲面、受限空间和动态环境交互的能力。三维微系统为可穿戴电子、生物医学技术、软体机器人和智能机器等未来应用中开发更具适应性、更灵敏的器件提供了一条有前景的路径。在本项目中，你将研究由应力工程化 PVD 薄膜驱动的自变形 MEMS 结构。通过集成应变传感元件，器件可将机械形变转化为可测量的电信号。项目将探究结构形变与传感性能之间的关系，目标是在传统平面设计之外开发出新型三维传感器。顺利完成本项目将为你积累 MEMS、薄膜工程、微加工与传感器表征方面的经验。","cats":["DES","ELE","MAT","MECH","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-XULIZHI-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向生物医学应用的水凝胶","titleEn":"Hydrogels for biomedical applications","areaZh":"生物医学工程；材料；力学","areaEn":"Biomedical Engineering; Materials; Mechancis","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor XU Lizhi","moderator":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-07 12:34:01","descriptionText":"In the realm of healthcare, the\r\ndevelopment of innovative materials and technologies is crucial for improving\r\npatient outcomes and enhancing the overall quality of care. One area of\r\nsignificant interest is the creation of advanced biomaterials that can be used\r\nto replace damaged tissues, such as hydrogels. However, their mechanical\r\nstrength is often a limiting factor, which can compromise their performance and\r\nlongevity in healthcare settings. To address this challenge, this project seeks\r\nto investigate various methods for enhancing the strength of hydrogels. Moreover,\r\nthis hydrogel can also be used as flexible wearable electronics that can\r\nmonitor vital signs and other health metrics in real-time, making it a\r\npotentially powerful tool for healthcare. Successful demonstration of this\r\nproject will help you enhance your skills in biomaterials engineering and the\r\ndevelopment of biomedical technologies.","descZh":"在医疗健康领域，创新材料与技术的开发对改善患者疗效和提升整体护理质量至关重要。一个备受关注的领域是开发可用于替换受损组织的先进生物材料，如水凝胶。然而，其力学强度常常成为限制因素，可能削弱其在医疗场景中的性能与使用寿命。为应对这一挑战，本项目旨在研究多种增强水凝胶强度的方法。此外，该水凝胶还可用于可实时监测生命体征与健康指标的柔性可穿戴电子器件，有望成为医疗健康的强大工具。成功完成本项目将有助于你提升生物材料工程与生物医学技术开发方面的技能。","cats":["BIO","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-XULIZHI-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向软体机器人具身智能的智能材料逻辑门设计","titleEn":"Designing smart-materials-based logic gates for embodied intelligence in soft robotics","areaZh":"机器人；设计；材料；力学","areaEn":"Robotics; Design; Materials; Mechanics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor XU Lizhi","moderator":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-07 12:34:01","descriptionText":"Logic processing is the foundation of\r\ndecision-making in both biological organisms and autonomous systems, enabling\r\norganisms to perceive environmental signals and produce complex adaptive\r\nbehaviors. Artificial systems rely heavily on rigid electronic microcontrollers,\r\nwhich face challenges in flexibility, miniaturization, and operation under\r\nextreme or power-limited environments. Stimuli-responsive smart materials, such\r\nas hydrogels, elastomers, and shape memory alloys, offer a transformative\r\nparadigm by performing \"material-level computation\" or embodied\r\nlogic. These materials can directly process physical and chemical inputs (e.g.,\r\nlight, heat, pH, mechanical strain) into logical mechanical or chemical outputs\r\nwithout electronic components. In this project, you will investigate design\r\nprinciples and fabrication strategies to construct basic and multi-input logic\r\ngates (e.g., AND, OR, NOT) using stimuli-responsive materials. You will\r\nintegrate these material-based logic systems into smart robotic systems to\r\nenable environment-conditioned decision-making and self-guided actuation.\r\nSuccessful demonstration of this project will help you gain key capabilities in\r\nadvanced functional materials, smart structure design, and innovative control\r\nstrategies in the next-generation of robotic systems.","descZh":"逻辑处理是生物体与自主系统决策的基础，使生物能够感知环境信号并产生复杂的适应性行为。人工系统高度依赖刚性电子微控制器，其在柔性、微型化以及在极端或受限功率环境下的运行面临挑战。刺激响应型智能材料（如水凝胶、弹性体与形状记忆合金）通过执行\"材料级计算\"或具身逻辑，提供了一种变革性范式。这类材料无需电子元件，即可直接将物理与化学输入（如光、热、pH、机械应变）处理为逻辑化的机械或化学输出。在本项目中，你将研究利用刺激响应型材料构建基本与多输入逻辑门（如与门、或门、非门）的设计原理与制备策略，并将这些基于材料的逻辑系统集成到智能机器人系统中，实现环境条件决策与自主引导驱动。成功完成本项目将帮助你在下一代机器人系统中获得先进功能材料、智能结构设计与创新控制策略方面的关键能力。","cats":["DES","MAT","MECH","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-XULIZHI-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"3D打印承载点阵超材料的设计","titleEn":"Engineering of 3D-Printed Load-Bearing Lattice Metamaterials","areaZh":"材料；力学；制造","areaEn":"Materials; Mechanics; Manufacturing","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor XU Lizhi","moderator":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-07 12:34:01","descriptionText":"Lightweight design holds extensive application prospects\r\nacross aerospace, automotive, and advanced engineering fields. One promising\r\napproach to achieving lightweight yet highly robust engineering materials is\r\nthe development of mechanical metamaterials. These materials derive their\r\nexceptional properties not just from their bulk composition, but from their\r\ncarefully engineered internal architectures. Recent research has focused on\r\nutilizing these structures to create advanced compressive materials with\r\nunprecedented strength-to-weight ratios. In this project, you will investigate\r\nthe design and mechanical properties of compressive lattice structures based on\r\n3D printing (additive manufacturing) technology. You are expected to\r\ncomputationally design lattice models, experimentally fabricate specimens using\r\n3D printing processes, and conduct rigorous mechanical testing and failure\r\nanalysis. Successful demonstration of this project will enhance your\r\ncapabilities in structural design, advanced manufacturing, and solid mechanics.","descZh":"轻量化设计在航空航天、汽车与先进工程领域具有广泛的应用前景。实现轻质且高强度的工程材料，一个有前景的途径是开发力学超材料。这类材料不仅依靠本体成分，更依靠精心设计的内部构型获得优异性能。近期研究聚焦于利用这些结构制造具有空前强度-重量比的先进抗压材料。在本项目中，你将研究基于 3D 打印（增材制造）技术的抗压晶格结构的设计与力学性能。你需要对晶格模型进行计算设计、用 3D 打印工艺实验制造试样，并开展严格的力学测试与失效分析。成功完成本项目将提升你在结构设计、先进制造与固体力学方面的能力。","cats":["DES","MAT","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-XULIZHI-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于柔性生物电子器件的导电聚合物阻尼器","titleEn":"Conductive polymer damper for soft bioelectronics","areaZh":"微电子；制造；材料；力学","areaEn":"Microeletronics; Manufacturing; Materials; Mechanics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor XU Lizhi","moderator":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-07 12:34:01","descriptionText":"Soft\r\nbioelectronic devices can be interfaced with anatomically curved organs, such\r\nas the heart, brain and skin, to provide continuous analysis of physiological\r\ninformation. Conductive polymer dampers can mechanically filter out unnecessary\r\nvibrations to maintain stable electrode contact, thereby ensuring stable signal\r\nacquisition. In this project, you will systematically design and characterize a\r\nnovel conductive polymer damping material, study its mechanical and electrical\r\ncoupling response, and apply it to robustly and low-noise acquisition of\r\nvarious bioelectric signals. This work connects materials science, circuit\r\ndesign, and biomedical applications, providing you with practical experience in\r\ninterdisciplinary engineering.","descZh":"软体生物电子器件可与心脏、大脑、皮肤等具有解剖曲面的器官连接，提供生理信息的持续分析。导电聚合物阻尼层可通过机械方式滤除不必要的振动，维持稳定的电极接触，从而确保稳定的信号采集。在本项目中，你将系统设计与表征一种新型导电聚合物阻尼材料，研究其力电耦合响应，并将其应用于多种生物电信号的稳健、低噪声采集。这项工作连接材料科学、电路设计与生物医学应用，为你提供跨学科工程的实践机会。","cats":["DES","MAT","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-XULIZHI-9","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"静电纺丝纤维上的图案化液态金属用于柔性电子器件","titleEn":"Patterned liquid metal on electrospun fibers for flexible electronic devices","areaZh":"电子；制造；材料","areaEn":"Electronics; Manufacturing; Materials","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor XU Lizhi","moderator":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-07 12:34:01","descriptionText":"Liquid\r\nmetals and electrospun thermoplastic polyurethane (TPU) fibers are promising\r\nmaterials for flexible electronics. In this project, the focus will be on\r\nexploring liquid metal patterning methods on electrospun TPU substrates and\r\nimproving the adhesion and stability of conductive patterns under mechanical\r\ndeformation. Different device designs will be investigated to achieve either\r\nstrain-insensitive conductive pathways for stretchable interconnects or\r\nstrain-sensitive electrical responses for mechanical sensing. The aim is to\r\ndevelop flexible devices with reliable performance during bending and\r\nstretching, with potential applications in stretchable circuits, strain\r\nsensors, ECG, or EMG monitoring. Successful demonstration of this project will\r\nenhance skills in electrospinning, liquid metal processing, device fabrication,\r\nand electromechanical characterization, while deepening the understanding of\r\nthe design principles and applications of strain-insensitive and\r\nstrain-sensitive electronics.","descZh":"液态金属与静电纺丝热塑性聚氨酯（TPU）纤维是柔性电子的有前景材料。本项目将聚焦于探索在静电纺丝 TPU 基材上的液态金属图案化方法，并提升导电图案在机械形变下的附着性与稳定性。将研究不同的器件设计，以实现面向可拉伸互连的应变不敏感导电路径，或面向机械传感的应变敏感电响应。目标是开发在弯折与拉伸过程中性能可靠的柔性器件，潜在应用包括可拉伸电路、应变传感器、心电图（ECG）或肌电（EMG）监测。成功完成本项目将提升你在静电纺丝、液态金属加工、器件制造与机电表征方面的技能，并加深对应变不敏感与应变敏感电子学的设计原理和应用的理解。","cats":["DES","ELE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-XULIZHI-10","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向可穿戴传感器与软体机器人的智能褶皱表面","titleEn":"Smart wrinkle surfaces for wearable sensors and soft robots","areaZh":"材料；力学；机器人","areaEn":"Materials; Mechanics; Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor XU Lizhi","moderator":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-07 12:34:01","descriptionText":"Humidity-responsive\r\nflexible surfaces can exhibit visible pattern changes in response to variations\r\nin the surrounding humidity, including those caused by human breathing or the\r\nproximity of a finger. Such smart surfaces have potential applications in flexible\r\nsensors, wearable devices, and artificial skins for soft robots. In this\r\nproject, you will make a thin gelatin coating on a soft silicone sheet. Because\r\ngelatin takes up and releases water, tiny wrinkles on the surface can appear,\r\ndisappear, or change shape as the humidity varies. You will observe these\r\nchanges using a camera or microscope and use straightforward image analysis to\r\ncompare how quickly and reliably different samples respond. Based on the\r\nresults, you will design a small demonstration device, such as a visual\r\nhumidity indicator, a touch-free sensor, a smart wearable patch, or a\r\nresponsive skin for a soft robot. You are expected to fabricate\r\nhumidity-responsive samples, study their wrinkle patterns under different\r\nhumidity conditions, and explore their potential sensing applications. The\r\nproject provides practical training in smart\r\nmaterials, flexible sensors, and their practical applications.","descZh":"湿度响应型柔性表面可随周围湿度（包括人体呼吸或手指靠近引起的湿度变化）显示可见的图案变化。此类智能表面在柔性传感器、可穿戴设备与软体机器人人造皮肤方面具有潜在应用。在本项目中，你将在软性硅胶片上制备一层明胶薄涂层。由于明胶会吸收和释放水分，表面会随湿度变化出现、消失或改变形状的微小褶皱。你将用相机或显微镜观察这些变化，并通过简单的图像分析比较不同样品的响应速度与可靠性。基于结果，你将设计一个小型演示装置，如可视化湿度指示器、非接触传感器、智能可穿戴贴片或软体机器人的响应式皮肤。你需要制备湿度响应样品，研究其在不同湿度条件下的褶皱图案，并探索其潜在的传感应用。项目将提供智能材料、柔性传感器及其实际应用的实践训练。","cats":["MAT","MECH","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-XULIZHI-11","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于早期检测隐性出血的智能静脉监测设备","titleEn":"A Smart Venous Monitoring Device for Early Detection of Hidden Bleeding","areaZh":"生物医学工程；材料；制造；电子","areaEn":"Biomedical Engineering; Materials; Manufacuring; Electronics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor XU Lizhi","moderator":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-07 12:34:01","descriptionText":"Hidden internal bleeding can be hard to notice\r\nin the early stage, but late detection may lead to shock and serious\r\ncomplications. This project aims to develop a small, minimally invasive venous\r\nmonitoring device that can continuously collect useful blood-related\r\ninformation in real time. The device will be designed with soft materials,\r\nprotective coatings, and simple sensing components to detect changes in\r\nphysiological and biochemical signals. Possible indicators include lactate\r\nlevel, blood pressure waveform changes, and bleeding-related biomarkers. By\r\ncombining these signals, the system may provide simple warning levels to help\r\ndoctors identify bleeding risk earlier. Through this project, you will learn\r\nbasic knowledge of biomaterials, medical device design, biosensing, and early\r\nwarning systems.","descZh":"隐匿性内出血早期难以察觉，而发现过晚可能导致休克与严重并发症。本项目旨在开发一种小型、微创的静脉监测装置，可实时持续采集有用的血液相关信息。该装置将采用软体材料、保护涂层与简单传感元件设计，用于检测生理与生化信号的变化。可能的指标包括乳酸水平、血压波形变化以及与出血相关的生物标志物。通过综合这些信号，系统可提供简单的预警等级，帮助医生更早识别出血风险。通过本项目，你将学习生物材料、医疗器械设计、生物传感与早期预警系统的基础知识。","cats":["BIO","DES","ELE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-XULIZHI-12","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向关节与脊柱植入物的坚韧水凝胶设计","titleEn":"Designing tough hydrogels for joint and spinal implants","areaZh":"材料；力学；生物医学工程","areaEn":"Materials; Mechanics; Biomedical Engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor XU Lizhi","moderator":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-07 12:34:01","descriptionText":"As populations age, more people experience joint\r\ndegeneration and spinal-disc problems, increasing the need for durable and\r\nbiocompatible materials for treatments such as joint replacements and\r\nartificial intervertebral discs. Hydrogels contain large amounts of water and\r\ncan resemble soft biological tissues, making them promising for these\r\napplications. However, many hydrogels are still not strong or reliable enough\r\nfor long-term load-bearing use. In this project, you will explore how changing\r\nthe solvent environment affects the formation and mechanical performance of\r\nfiber-based hydrogels. You will prepare samples, test how they respond to\r\ncompression and deformation, and identify conditions that make them tougher and\r\nmore reliable. The project connects materials science with real healthcare\r\nneeds and will help you understand how an ageing society shapes demand for\r\nmedical technologies. You will also gain practical experience in hydrogel\r\npreparation, mechanical testing, and interpreting experimental results.","descZh":"随着人口老龄化，越来越多的人出现关节退行性病变与椎间盘问题，这增加了对关节置换、人工椎间盘等治疗用耐用、生物相容材料的需求。水凝胶含有大量水分，可模拟软组织生物学特性，因此在上述应用中前景广阔。然而，许多水凝胶的强度与可靠性仍不足以长期承载负荷。在本项目中，你将探索改变溶剂环境如何影响纤维基水凝胶的形成与力学性能。你将制备样品，测试其对压缩与形变的响应，并找出使其更强韧、更可靠的条件。项目将材料科学与真实医疗需求联系起来，帮助你理解老龄化社会如何塑造对医疗技术的需求；你还将获得水凝胶制备、力学测试与实验结果解读的实践技能。","cats":["BIO","MAT","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-AKFPANG-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"客流量与列车行车间隔的实时分析以预测并缓解拥堵问题","titleEn":"Real-time Analytics of Passenger Flow and Train Headway to Predict and Alleviate  Congestion Problem","areaZh":"人工智能","areaEn":"Artificial intelligence","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Mr. PANG Kai Fai Andy","moderator":"Dr. LEUNG Kin Man","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-10 16:10:53","descriptionText":"This is a collaborated dissertation project with the MTR Corporation. Student will use the real-time passenger flow data in a metro station from the MTR Corporation and develop a big data algorithm to predict and alleviate passenger congestion problems (i.e., to ease off the passengers crowded at the platforms).\n Continuous annual increase in patronage of a metro line has always been a challenging problem to the railway operator. Passenger flow analysis study for a metro station is essential to finding ways to predict and alleviate the passenger congestion problems. \n This project involves the development of a real-time analytics system that can predict passenger flow and train headway to alleviate passenger congestion problems in train systems and provide actionable insights to optimize the transportation system.\n Students are expected to produce the following outcomes:\n 1. Data collection: Data on passenger flow and train headway will be collected from a selected train system. The data will be collected using sensors and other data collection tools.\n 2. Real-time analytics algorithm development: Real-time analytics algorithms will be developed to process and analyze the collected data, and to predict passenger flow and train headway. The algorithms will be designed to provide insights into passenger flow and train headway, and to identify potential areas of congestion.\n 3. Simulation environment development: The analytics system will be implemented in a simulation environment to evaluate its effectiveness in predicting passenger flow and train headway, and in reducing passenger congestion.\n 4. Evaluation: The effectiveness of the analytics system will be evaluated based on the accuracy of its predictions and the reduction in passenger congestion observed in the simulation environment.\n Pre- and co-requisites:\n Students should have good knowledge in programming for preparing algorithm using Python or C++.","descZh":"这是一个与港铁公司（MTR Corporation）合作的毕业论文项目。学生将使用港铁公司提供的某地铁站的实时客流数据，开发一种大数据算法，以预测和缓解乘客拥堵问题（即疏散站台上拥挤的乘客）。\n地铁线路客流量的逐年持续增长，一直是轨道交通运营商面临的一大难题。地铁站的客流分析研究对于找到预测和缓解乘客拥堵问题的方法至关重要。\n本项目涉及开发一套实时分析系统，能够预测客流与列车行车间隔，以缓解列车系统中的乘客拥堵问题，并为优化交通系统提供可操作的见解。\n预期学生产出以下成果：\n1. 数据采集：将从选定的列车系统采集客流与列车行车间隔数据。数据将使用传感器及其他数据采集工具进行收集。\n2. 实时分析算法开发：开发实时分析算法，对采集到的数据进行处理与分析，并预测客流与列车行车间隔。算法将设计用于提供关于客流与列车行车间隔的见解，并识别潜在的拥堵区域。\n3. 仿真环境开发：该分析系统将在仿真环境中实现，以评估其在预测客流与列车行车间隔以及减少乘客拥堵方面的有效性。\n4. 评估：将根据其预测的准确性以及在仿真环境中观察到的乘客拥堵减少程度，评估该分析系统的有效性。\n先修与同修要求：\n学生应具备良好的编程知识，以使用 Python 或 C++ 编写算法。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-AKFPANG-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"铁路服务器与通信设备的智能温度监测与优化","titleEn":"Intelligent Temperature Monitoring and Optimization for Railway Server and Telecom Equipment","areaZh":"人工智能","areaEn":"Artificial intelligence","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Mr. PANG Kai Fai Andy","moderator":"Dr. LEUNG Kin Man","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-10 16:11:10","descriptionText":"This is a collaborated dissertation project with the MTR Corporation. Students will apply the relevant big data analytics from the MTR Corporation to develop an intelligent temperature monitoring and optimization solution for the Railway Equipment Room.\n The reliability and resilience of railway systems are heavily dependent on the performance and condition of their supporting infrastructure, including the equipment rooms for critical system, it is of importance in maintaining optimal temperature conditions within the rooms and enclosures housing these vital components.\n The proposed project aims to develop and implement an intelligent temperature monitoring and optimization solution to enhance the reliability and resilience of the railway, ensuring the continuous and efficient operation of its rail network. The expected outcomes include a Comprehensive Temperature Monitoring System, AI-powered Temperature Trend Analysis, Temperature Threshold Determination, Automated Temperature Monitoring and Alerting and provide Predictive Maintenance and Optimization Strategies This project enables the MTR Corporation to enhance the reliability, resilience, and efficiency of its critical railway servers and telecommunication infrastructure, ultimately supporting the continuous and safe operation of its rail network.\n Students are expected to produce the following outcomes:\n 1. Sensor Installation and Data Collection: Install temperature sensors inside and outside the server and telecommunication equipment room. Collect real-time temperature data from the installed sensors over an extended period. \n 2. AI-based Temperature Trend Analysis: Develop an AI-powered data analysis model to evaluate the trends in temperature, both inside and outside the rooms. Identify patterns, fluctuations, and anomalies in the temperature data using advanced machine learning techniques.\n 3. Temperature Monitoring and Alerting System: Establish temperature thresholds for the optimal operation of the telecom and signaling equipment. Implement a real-time monitoring system that continuously tracks the sensor data and compares it against the established thresholds. Implement an automated alert system to notify the relevant personnel when the temperature exceeds the predefined critical levels.\n 4. Predictive Maintenance and Optimization: Utilize the temperature trend analysis to develop predictive models that can forecast potential equipment failures or performance degradation. Provide recommendations for proactive maintenance schedules and equipment upgrades to prevent temperature-related issues. Suggest optimization strategies, such as improved room insulation, ventilation, or cooling systems, to maintain the optimal temperature conditions for the telecom and signaling equipment.\n Pre- and co-requisites:\n Experience in one or more of the following sets of tools and technologies is preferred:\n • Cloud computing technology (i.e. AWS);\n • Database architectures and Big Data technologies;\n • Programming languages (e.g. Python);\n • Data visualization tools (e.g. Power BI, Tableau)","descZh":"这是一个与港铁公司（MTR Corporation）合作的毕业论文项目。学生将应用港铁公司提供的相关大数据分析，为铁路设备房开发一套智能温度监测与优化解决方案。\n铁路系统的可靠性与韧性在很大程度上取决于其配套基础设施的性能与状态，包括关键系统的设备房；在这些容纳关键部件的机房与机柜内维持最佳温度条件至关重要。\n本项目旨在开发并实施一套智能温度监测与优化解决方案，以提升铁路的可靠性与韧性，确保其铁路网络的持续高效运营。预期成果包括一套综合温度监测系统、AI 驱动的温度趋势分析、温度阈值确定、自动化温度监测与告警，以及提供预测性维护与优化策略。本项目使港铁公司能够提升其关键铁路服务器和电信基础设施的可靠性、韧性与效率，最终支撑其铁路网络的持续安全运营。\n学生应产出以下成果：\n1. 传感器安装与数据采集：在服务器和电信设备房内外安装温度传感器。在较长时段内采集来自已安装传感器的实时温度数据。\n2. 基于 AI 的温度趋势分析：开发一个 AI 驱动的数据分析模型，评估机房内外的温度趋势。使用先进的机器学习技术识别温度数据中的模式、波动和异常。\n3. 温度监测与告警系统：为电信和信号设备的最佳运行确定温度阈值。实施一个实时监测系统，持续跟踪传感器数据并与已确定的阈值进行比较。实施一套自动告警系统，在温度超过预定义的关键水平时通知相关人员。\n4. 预测性维护与优化：利用温度趋势分析开发预测模型，以预测潜在的设备故障或性能退化。提供有关主动维护计划和设备升级的建议，以防止与温度相关的问题。提出优化策略（例如改进机房隔热、通风或冷却系统），以为电信和信号设备维持最佳温度条件。\n先修及并修要求：\n优先考虑具备以下一组或多组工具与技术经验者：\n• 云计算技术（如 AWS）；\n• 数据库架构与大数据技术；\n• 编程语言（如 Python）；\n• 数据可视化工具（如 Power BI、Tableau）","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-AKFPANG-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"智慧与绿色公共交通系统运维的最佳工程实践","titleEn":"Best Engineering Practices for Operation and Maintenance of Smart and Green Mass Transit Systems","areaZh":"工程管理","areaEn":"Engineering management","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Mr. PANG Kai Fai Andy","moderator":"Dr. LEUNG Kin Man","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-10 16:11:23","descriptionText":"This research project focuses on identifying and analyzing the best engineering practices for the operation and maintenance of smart and green mass transit systems, particularly in urban environments like East Kowloon, Kai Tak, and Hung Shui Kiu. The study will cover the following key areas:\n Operational Efficiency: Evaluating strategies to optimize service delivery, including scheduling, resource allocation, and real-time monitoring.\n Maintenance Strategies: Investigating preventive, predictive, and corrective maintenance practices to enhance system reliability and reduce downtime.\n Smart Technologies: Analyzing the role of Internet of Things (IoT) and data analytics in improving operational performance and maintenance workflows.\n Safety and Compliance: Assessing safety protocols and regulatory compliance measures to ensure passenger safety and operational integrity.\n Sustainability Practices: Evaluating eco-friendly maintenance practices that minimize environmental impact.\n Students may choose to focus on specific aspects of operation and maintenance or address them collectively across the different transit systems. The project will primarily rely on a literature review and case studies, supplemented by data analysis.\n Key Objectives:\n To identify best practices in the operation and maintenance of smart and green mass transit systems applicable to East Kowloon, Kai Tak, and Hung Shui Kiu.\n To evaluate the impact of smart technologies on operational efficiency and maintenance effectiveness.\n To provide actionable recommendations for enhancing the reliability and sustainability of transit operations in the selected areas.\n Students can pick 2 to 3 of the above areas for the study. The main parts of the project is mainly done through literature review. The students’ study shall be deep and thorough enough to achieve the MSc dissertation standard. Computation to enrich the study is highly recommended. As an alternative, students may choose to carry out the research study into the best international engineering practice in the design, planning and operation of a main railway line. The study areas are similar to the above.\n Pre- and co-requisites:\n No pre-requisite. Prefer to undertake at least 1 MSc railway engineering course.","descZh":"本研究项目聚焦于识别并分析智能绿色公共交通系统运营与维护的最佳工程实践，尤其针对东九龙、启德与洪水桥等城市区域。研究将涵盖以下关键领域：\n- 运营效率：评估优化服务交付的策略，包括调度、资源配置与实时监测；\n- 维护策略：研究预防性、预测性与纠正性维护实践，以提升系统可靠性并减少停机；\n- 智能技术：分析物联网（IoT）与数据分析在改善运营绩效与维护流程中的作用；\n- 安全与合规：评估安全规程与监管合规措施，确保乘客安全与运营完整性；\n- 可持续实践：评估最大限度减少环境影响的环境友好维护实践。\n学生可选择聚焦运营与维护的特定方面，或对多个不同轨道系统进行综合研究。项目将主要依托文献综述与案例研究，辅以数据分析。\n关键目标：\n- 识别适用于东九龙、启德与洪水桥的智能绿色公共交通系统运营与维护最佳实践；\n- 评估智能技术对运营效率与维护有效性的影响；\n- 为提升所选区域轨道运营的可靠性与可持续性提供可操作建议。\n学生可选上述领域中的 2~3 项进行研究。项目主体主要通过文献综述完成，研究需足够深入透彻，达到硕士论文水准；强烈建议用计算手段丰富研究。作为备选，学生也可选择对一条干线铁路的设计、规划与运营最佳国际工程实践开展研究，研究领域与上述类似。\n先修/并修要求：无先修要求；建议至少修读一门硕士轨道交通课程。","cats":["MGT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_XCZHU-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"多种不确定性下四旋翼无人机高精度轨迹跟踪控制","titleEn":"High-Precision Trajectory Tracking Control of a Quadrotor UAV Subject to Various Uncertainties","areaZh":"无人机控制；控制理论与应用","areaEn":"Control of UAVs; Control theory and applications","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. ZHU Xiaocong","moderator":"Professor HUANG Lixi","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Compared with traditional fixed-wing aircraft or manned platforms, quadrotor unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) offer significant advantages, including compact structure, flexible hovering capability, strong maneuverability, excellent environmental adaptability, and low operation and maintenance costs. Therefore, they have demonstrated broad application prospects in fields of military reconnaissance, industrial inspection, disaster monitoring, and logistics delivery. However, in actual flight environments, the UAV system is often confronted with unknown disturbances due to varying wind resistance, fluctuations in air density, and variant payload etc. Meanwhile, they are susceptible to external disturbances including gusts and electromagnetic interference. These factors introduce significant uncertainties into the dynamic model of the UAV, making it difficult to maintain desirable tracking performance with traditional controller design based on precise models.\n This project focuses on the trajectory tracking control of a quadrotor UAV. A precision motion control method for the UAV adapt to complex environments will be developed to enhance its autonomous operation capability. The main work includes: (1) Establish a dynamic model with multi-source uncertainty for the quadrotor UAV system, considering parametric perturbations, unmodeled dynamics and external disturbances etc., (2) Design an extended state observer (ESO) based on the active disturbance rejection control (ADRC) technique for achieving real-time estimation and active compensation of parametric uncertainties, (3) Synthesize an adaptive robust control strategy integrated with extended state observation to achieve stable tracking of the UAV under different working conditions, (4) Develop a simulation platform for the UAV to conduct performance tests under multiple working conditions. \n The student is expected to have a basic background of modeling and control in mechanical and electrical systems. The pre-requisite courses include theoretical mechanics, dynamics of mechanical systems, fundamental of control engineering etc. And MATLAB/Simulink software will be used in the project. It is recommended to take the MOOC course \"Modeling, Identification and Control of Mechatronic Systems\" starting in September.","descZh":"与传统固定翼飞行器或有人平台相比，四旋翼无人机（UAV）具有显著优势，包括结构紧凑、灵活的悬停能力、强机动性、优异的环境适应性以及低运行维护成本。因此，其在军事侦察、工业巡检、灾害监测与物流配送等领域展现出广阔应用前景。然而，在实际飞行环境中，无人机系统常面临风阻变化、空气密度波动与载荷变化等带来的未知扰动；同时易受阵风、电磁干扰等外部扰动影响。这些因素给无人机动力学模型引入显著不确定性，使基于精确模型的传统控制器设计难以保持理想的跟踪性能。\n本项目聚焦四旋翼无人机的轨迹跟踪控制。将开发一种适应复杂环境的精密运动控制方法，提升其自主运行能力。主要工作包括：(1) 建立考虑参数摄动、未建模动态与外部扰动等多源不确定性的四旋翼无人机系统动力学模型；(2) 基于自抗扰控制（ADRC）技术设计扩张状态观测器（ESO），实现对参数不确定性的实时估计与主动补偿；(3) 综合集成扩张状态观测的自适应鲁棒控制策略，实现无人机在不同工况下的稳定跟踪；(4) 开发无人机仿真平台，开展多工况性能测试。\n学生应具备机电系统建模与控制的基础背景。先修课程包括理论力学、机械系统动力学、控制工程基础等。项目将使用 MATLAB/Simulink 软件。建议自九月起学习 MOOC 课程《机电一体化系统的建模、辨识与控制》。","cats":["AERO","CTRL"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_XCZHU-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"EMB系统夹紧力估计与转矩控制策略","titleEn":"Clamping Force Estimation and Torque Control Strategy for an EMB system","areaZh":"机电控制；控制理论与应用；EMB控制","areaEn":"Mechatronic Control; Control theory and applications; Control of EMB","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. ZHU Xiaocong","moderator":"Professor HUANG Lixi","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"The response speed and control accuracy of the clamping force in electromechanical brakes (EMB) serve as core indicators for evaluating braking performance, which will directly affect vehicle safety and ride comfort. In traditional control of EMB clamping force, the measured values from force sensors are typically relied upon as feedback signals in the closed-loop system. However, the brake pads and brake discs are subject to prolonged exposure to high temperatures, intense friction, and continuous wear during frequent braking maneuvers in actual operating environments. This not only poses difficulties in sensor installation and thermal protection due to space constraints, but also renders the measured values of clamping force susceptible to drift caused by temperature variation, zero-point shift, and mechanical deformation. As a result, the long-term reliability and measurement accuracy of the sensors cannot be effectively guaranteed. Therefore, it is necessary to explore an accurate estimation method for the clamping force that rely only on measurable electrical and motion signals of the brushless motor. On this basis, a precision torque control strategy with fast response should be designed to ensure that the EMB system can operate stably, reliably, and efficiently under various complex operating conditions.\n This project focus on the accurate estimation and control of braking force for the EMB system. The main work includes: (1) Analyze the working principle and the typical operating conditions of the braking brushless DC motor, and establish its dynamic model that comprehensively considers the friction effects, wear, temperature etc. (2) Analyze the intrinsic relationship between the clamping force and motor angle, output torque, and ball screw displacement, and establish a dynamic observer equation for the clamping force. (3) Construct an extended dynamic model integrating with the dynamic clamping force observer and the dynamics of brushless DC motor, and design a clamping-force observer based adaptive robust controller to achieve good braking effect with fast-response. (4) Conduct simulation tests to evaluate the effectiveness of the clamping force estimation and the torque control strategy under various operating conditions. \n The student is expected to have a basic background of modeling and control in mechanical and electrical systems. The pre-requisite courses include theoretical mechanics, dynamics of mechanical systems, fundamental of control engineering etc. And MATLAB/Simulink software will be used in the project. It is recommended to take the MOOC course \"Modeling, Identification and Control of Mechatronic Systems\" starting in September.","descZh":"机电制动器（EMB）夹紧力的响应速度与控制精度是评价制动性能的核心指标，直接影响车辆安全与乘坐舒适性。在 EMB 夹紧力的传统控制中，通常依赖力传感器测量值作为闭环系统的反馈信号。然而，实际运行环境中频繁制动时，刹车片与刹车盘长期暴露于高温、强烈摩擦与持续磨损之下；这不仅因空间限制给传感器安装与热防护带来困难，也使夹紧力测量值易受温度变化、零点漂移与机械变形影响而产生漂移。因此，传感器的长期可靠性与测量精度无法有效保证，有必要探索一种仅依赖无刷电机可测电气与运动信号的夹紧力精确估计方法。在此基础上，应设计响应快速的精密扭矩控制策略，确保 EMB 系统在多种复杂工况下稳定、可靠、高效运行。\n本项目聚焦 EMB 系统制动力的精确估计与控制。主要工作包括：(1) 分析制动用无刷直流电机的工作原理与典型工况，建立综合考虑摩擦效应、磨损、温度等因素的动力学模型；(2) 分析夹紧力与电机转角、输出扭矩与滚珠丝杠位移之间的内在关系，建立夹紧力动态观测器方程；(3) 构建集成夹紧力动态观测器与无刷直流电机动力学的扩展动态模型，设计基于夹紧力观测器的自适应鲁棒控制器，实现响应快速的良好制动效果；(4) 开展仿真测试，评估各种工况下夹紧力估计与扭矩控制策略的有效性。\n学生应具备机电系统建模与控制的基础背景。先修课程包括理论力学、机械系统动力学、控制工程基础等。项目将使用 MATLAB/Simulink 软件。建议自九月起学习 MOOC 课程《机电一体化系统的建模、辨识与控制》。","cats":["CTRL"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_XCZHU-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"线控转向无刷直流电机系统的快速高精度控制","titleEn":"Fast and High-Precision Control of A Steer-by-Wire Brushless DC Motor System","areaZh":"机电控制；控制理论与应用；EMB控制","areaEn":"Mechatronic Control; Control Theory and Applications; Control of EMB","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. ZHU Xiaocong","moderator":"Professor HUANG Lixi","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"With the rapid development of intelligent chassis technologies (such as steer-by-wire chassis and skateboard chassis) and high-performance robotics, the brushless DC motor control system serves as the power core of actuation mechanisms. In practical applications, motor loads often exhibit wide-range with rapidly time-varying characteristics. And the operating environment may involve high temperatures, vibrations, and spatial constraints. Meanwhile, nonlinear friction in the transmission mechanism and electromagnetic parameter perturbations are coupled with each other. These factors cause the controlled plant to possess strong nonlinearity, strong coupling, and strong uncertainty. Traditional linear control strategies cannot simultaneously guarantee both fast response and steady-state accuracy across the full range of operating conditions. Therefore, it is necessary to investigate control methods of brushless DC motors to achieve fast and high-precision performance with capable of handling complex operating conditions.\n This project aims to design an adaptive robust controller for the brushless DC motor in steer-by-wire system to achieve its precise tracking control with fast response under large disturbances and physical constraints. The main work includes: (1) Analyze the operating mechanism of the steer-by-wire motor and establish the dynamic model of the brushless DC motor system, considering the effects of nonlinear friction, time-varying parameters due to temperature, and electromagnetic torque ripple. (2) Design a current-loop decoupling control method for achieving effective separation of the flux component and the torque component, thereby improve the current-loop response bandwidth and disturbance rejection performance. (3) Based on the current-loop decoupling, design a model-compensation-based adaptive robust control strategy to achieve fast and precision tracking control of the steer-by-wire system. (4) Build a simulation platform to test the performance of the steer-by-wire motor control system under multiple operating conditions.\n The student is expected to have a basic background of modeling and control in mechanical and electrical systems. The pre-requisite courses include theoretical mechanics, dynamics of mechanical systems, fundamental of control engineering etc. And MATLAB/Simulink software will be used in the project. It is recommended to take the MOOC course \"Modeling, Identification and Control of Mechatronic Systems\" starting in September.","descZh":"随着智能底盘技术（如线控转向底盘和滑板底盘）以及高性能机器人技术的快速发展，无刷直流电机控制系统成为执行机构（actuation mechanisms）的动力核心。在实际应用中，电机负载往往具有宽范围、快速时变的特性。而运行环境可能涉及高温、振动和空间约束。同时，传动机构中的非线性摩擦与电磁参数摄动相互耦合。这些因素使得被控对象具有强非线性、强耦合和强不确定性。传统的线性控制策略无法在全工况范围内同时保证快速响应和稳态精度。因此，有必要研究无刷直流电机的控制方法，以实现快速、高精度的性能，并能够应对复杂工况。\n本项目旨在为线控转向系统中的无刷直流电机设计一种自适应鲁棒控制器，以在大扰动和物理约束下实现其快速响应的精确跟踪控制。主要工作包括：(1) 分析线控转向电机的工作机理，建立无刷直流电机系统的动力学模型，并考虑非线性摩擦、由温度引起的时变参数以及电磁转矩脉动的影响。(2) 设计电流环解耦控制方法，实现磁通分量和转矩分量的有效分离，从而提高电流环的响应带宽和抗扰动性能。(3) 在电流环解耦的基础上，设计基于模型补偿的自适应鲁棒控制策略，实现线控转向系统的快速、精确跟踪控制。(4) 搭建仿真平台，在多种工况下测试线控转向电机控制系统的性能。\n学生应具备机电系统建模与控制方面的基本背景。先修课程包括理论力学、机械系统动力学、控制工程基础等。项目中将使用 MATLAB/Simulink 软件。建议修读 9 月开课的 MOOC 课程“机电系统建模、辨识与控制”（Modeling, Identification and Control of Mechatronic Systems）。","cats":["CTRL"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_XCZHU-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向气浮气动伺服平台的基于DSP的先进压力控制器开发","titleEn":"Development of DSP-based Advanced Pressure Controller for the Air Flotation Pneumatic Servo Platform","areaZh":"机电控制；控制理论与应用；气动控制","areaEn":"Mechatronic Control; Control Theory and Applications; Pneumatic Control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. ZHU Xiaocong","moderator":"Professor HUANG Lixi","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"An air flotation pneumatic servo platform system has the characteristics of low heat generation and ultra-low friction, making it suitable for potential applications in various fields such as semiconductors, biomedical engineering and the nuclear industry etc. However, several factors—including the complex behavior of air flotation, inherent parametric uncertainties from system modeling, and uncertain nonlinearities due to sudden disturbances—pose significant challenges to achieve precise control of the system. Therefore, it is necessary to develop an advanced pressure tracking controller for the air flotation pneumatic servo platform.\n In this project, an advanced controller for the air flotation pneumatic platform will be developed. The dynamic model of the system will be analyzed. A software and hardware solution for a DSP-based control system will be designed. The research work includes: (1) modeling of the air flotation pneumatic platform with strong nonlinearities and various uncertainties, (2) designing an intelligent control strategy for pressure control of the air flotation pneumatic platform, (3) developing the hardware and software framework for the DSP-based controller (STM 32 series). (4) simulating the pressure control system using MATLAB-DSP tools, and experimentally validating the motion performance of the system.\n The student is expected to have a basic background of modeling and control in mechanical and electrical systems. The pre-requisite courses include theoretical mechanics, dynamics of mechanical systems, fundamental of control engineering etc. And MATLAB/Simulink and CCS tools software will be used in the project. It is recommended to take the MOOC course \"Modeling, Identification and Control of Mechatronic Systems\" starting in September.","descZh":"气浮气动伺服平台系统具有发热低、摩擦极小的特点，适合用于半导体、生物医学工程与核工业等领域的潜在应用。然而，气浮行为的复杂性、系统建模固有的参数不确定性以及突发扰动带来的不确定非线性等因素，为系统精确控制带来重大挑战。因此，有必要为气浮气动伺服平台开发先进的压力跟踪控制器。\n本项目将为气浮气动平台开发先进控制器，分析系统动力学模型，并设计基于 DSP 的控制系统软硬件方案。研究工作包括：(1) 对具有强非线性与多种不确定性的气浮气动平台建模；(2) 设计面向气浮气动平台压力控制的智能控制策略；(3) 开发基于 DSP（STM32 系列）控制器的软硬件框架；(4) 使用 MATLAB-DSP 工具对压力控制系统仿真，并对系统运动性能进行实验验证。\n学生应具备机电系统建模与控制的基础背景。先修课程包括理论力学、机械系统动力学、控制工程基础等。项目将使用 MATLAB/Simulink 与 CCS 工具软件。建议自九月起学习 MOOC 课程《机电一体化系统的建模、辨识与控制》。","cats":["CTRL"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MMCHOY-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"MR暗棋","titleEn":"MR dark chess","areaZh":"混合现实；跟踪问题","areaEn":"Mixed Reality; tracking problem","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"Dr. ALI Zulfiqar","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 09:10:13","descriptionText":"This is an individual project. Capacity = 2 means two students can work on this independently with their own approach. \n The setup \n A two-player mixed-reality version of Dark C hess that is played on a real board with real physical pieces. Both players wear a Meta Quest 3 headset (other brand is ok). The pieces themselves are blank (i.e. no characters printed on them), and the headset renders the Chess-character glyph on top of each piece when it should be visible. \n Because Dark Chess hides piece identities until a piece is moved, each player sees a diﬀerent view of the same physical board: the pieces you have revealed show their glyphs to you but remain blank to your opponent, and vice versa. This kind of per-player private information layered onto shared physical objects is something only a headset can do. \n What to do \n Design the physical pieces and board, build the MR application. Place an additional camera on the top, overseeing the whole board, projecting the real time image together with Chess characters on a screen, so that viewers can see the whole picture while the two players are enjoying the Dark Chess.\n Addition: can consider adding levels of difficulities, such as suggesting which piece to move.","descZh":"这是一个个人项目。容量 2 表示两名学生可各自独立、以各自方式开展此项目。\n项目设定：一种双人混合现实版\"暗棋\"，在真实棋盘上用真实实体棋子进行。两名玩家各佩戴一台 Meta Quest 3 头显（其他品牌亦可）。棋子本身为空白（未印任何字符），头显会在棋子应可见时把象棋字符字形渲染到每颗棋子之上。\n由于暗棋在棋子移动前隐藏其身份，两名玩家看到的是同一实体棋盘的不同视图：你已翻开的棋子在你看来自带字形，而对对手仍为空白，反之亦然。这种将玩家各自的私人信息叠加于共享实体物体之上的方式，只有头显能够实现。\n任务内容：设计实体棋子与棋盘，构建 MR 应用；在顶部额外放置一台相机，俯瞰整个棋盘，将实时画面连同象棋字符投影到屏幕上，使观众能在两名玩家对弈暗棋时看到全景。可选加项：可考虑加入难度层级，例如提示该走哪颗棋子。","cats":["CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MMCHOY-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向非政府组织的云端部署员工请假与薪资管理系统","titleEn":"A Cloud-Deployed Staff Leave and Payroll Management System for an NGO","areaZh":"全栈应用","areaEn":"full stack application","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"Dr. ALI Zulfiqar","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-09 15:34:02","descriptionText":"A Cloud-Deployed Staff Leave and Payroll Management System for an NGO \n (This is an individual full-stack project. estimated from start to end about 30 weeks)\n PROBLEM \n Small NGOs run staff leave on spreadsheets, paper forms and email. Entitlements are miscalculated, approvals leave no audit trail, and the monthly hand-off of leave data \n into payroll is re-keyed by hand. Commercial HR suites are too costly and too rigid for NGO-specific leave types and approval hierarchies. \n AIM \n Build, deploy and evaluate a web platform in which an administrator configures leave types, entitlement rules, approval routing and payroll rules WITHOUT code changes. \n CORE FEATURES \n Roles and access - staff, first approver, second approver, HR/payroll admin. IT admin set and name the roles and access (e.g. staff is just a general name, there might be cleaning staff, supervisor staff which have different access to the system).\n Configurable approval engine - 0, 1, 2 or n approval levels, set per role, per leave type or per individual; delegation and escalation on timeout. THIS IS THE TECHNICAL CORE OF YOUR DISSERTATION - build it generic, not hard-coded.\n Entitlement engine - full-time, part-time, compansation leave, annual leave, sick leave, other leave, carry forward and expiry.\n Payroll module - no-pay leave deduction, statutory sickness allowance, full-time pay, part-time pay, compensation-leave offset, year-end encashment provision. Payroll periods lock on close; corrections are adjustment entries, never edits to history.\n Reporting - individual leave statement, departmental utilisation, leave liability, absence patterns, and a CSV/Excel payroll export.\n Staff portal, approver dashboard, admin console, leave calendar, email alerts. \n Take care of public holidays in HK and observe HK labour laws relate to annual leave, sick leave, etc. for full-time and part-time employee. (e.g. ref. hk labour law 468) \n UI is web based but responsive for mobile\n UAT is a must\n MANDATORY : RENT A SERVER - NO LOCALHOST DEMOS\n You must run this as a live service on a rented VPS or cloud instance (can be reimbursed). Required and assessed:\n - public domain or subdomain, HTTPS with auto-renewing certificate\n - reverse proxy (Nginx/Caddy), Docker Compose or a documented provisioning script\n - separate staging and production environments\n - scheduled automated backups PLUS a demonstrated restore test\n - firewall, key-only SSH, no root login, access and application logging, uptime monitor\n - a Deployment and Operations chapter, an uptime/incident log for the last 8 weeks, and a live URL with a read-only examiner account \n Do not put real personal data on the server; use a synthetic demonstration dataset.\n USER ACCEPTANCE TESTING (assessed separately from your own testing)\n - 6 to 10 participants covering all roles, NGO staff preferred; proxies allowed but must be declared as a limitation. Confirm participants by week 20. \n - Written UAT plan with role-specific test scripts traceable to your requirement list: preconditions, steps, expected result, pass/fail/pass-with-comment. \n - Participants work UNAIDED on the live server. It is not a guided demo.\n - Measure: pass rate per requirement, task time, error count, System Usability Scale, short interview. Participants must check one full payroll period against an \n independent manual calculation.\n - Agree sign-off criteria with the supervisor BEFORE UAT starts (e.g. all must-have requirements pass, no open critical/major defects, System Usability Scale ≥ 68). \n - Report initial results AND post-fix results. Include the sign-off as an appendix.\n DELIVERABLES \n Live system on the rented server (public URL + examiner account), source repository with README and deployment runbook, requirements and design documentation, test report, UAT plan, scripts, results, sign-off. dissertation and live oral demo.\n HOW YOU WILL BE MARKED \n Functional coverage of must-have requirements\n correctness of the approval, entitlement and payroll engines\n configurability (demonstrate by reconfiguring the system for a SECOND, different leave and pay policy with no code change)\n UAT results and usability\n quality of the production deployment, code and security quality.\n THINGS THAT LOSE MARKS \n 1. Demonstrating on localhost.\n 2. Hard-coding \"one approver\" or \"two approvers\" instead of a configurable engine.\n 3. Testing your rule engine with test cases derived from your own code - that is circular. Derive test cases from the policy documents and have them reviewed \n before you implement the module.\n 4. Claiming payroll compliance, or claiming the NGO has adopted the system. Claim only what you built and tested: a prototype with an evaluated deployment. \n AI USE \n AI may assist with language, structure and summarising your own material. It must not g enerate your data, technical results or references. Complete the AI declaration in \n full - an incomplete declaration is recorded separately from your academic grade.","descZh":"面向 NGO 的云端员工请假与薪资管理系统\n（这是一个个人全栈项目，预计从开始到结束约 30 周）\n问题：小型 NGO 目前用电子表格、纸质表格与电子邮件管理员工请假。应享假期计算错误、审批无审计轨迹，每月将请假数据转交薪资计算时靠人工重复录入。商用 HR 套件对 NGO 特有的请假类型与审批层级而言成本过高且过于僵化。\n目标：构建、部署并评估一个 Web 平台——管理员可在平台中配置请假类型、应享规则、审批路由与薪资规则，而无需改代码。\n核心功能：\n- 角色与访问：员工、一级审批人、二级审批人、HR/薪资管理员。IT 管理员设置并命名角色与访问权限（如\"员工\"只是通用名称，可能有保洁员工、主管员工等对系统拥有不同访问权限）。\n- 可配置审批引擎：0、1、2 或 n 级审批，可按角色、按请假类型或按个人设置；超时委托与升级。这是你论文的技术核心——要做得通用，而非硬编码。\n- 应享引擎：全职、兼职、补休、年假、病假、其他假期、结转与过期。\n- 薪资模块：无薪假扣款、法定疾病津贴、全职薪资、兼职薪资、补休抵扣、年终折现计提。薪资期结算后锁定；更正用调整分录实现，绝不直接修改历史。\n- 报表：个人请假结算单、部门使用率、假期负债、缺勤模式与 CSV/Excel 薪资导出。\n- 员工门户、审批人仪表板、管理控制台、请假日历、邮件提醒。\n- 考虑香港公众假期，并遵守与年假、病假等相关的香港劳工法（涵盖全职与兼职员工，如参考香港劳工法例第 57 章/相关条文）。\n- UI 为 Web 但需移动端响应式。\n- 必须做用户验收测试（UAT）。\n强制要求：租用服务器——禁止仅在本机（localhost）演示。\n你必须将其作为已租用 VPS 或云实例（费用可报销）上的线上服务运行。需要且将被评估：\n- 公共域名或子域名、带自动续期证书的 HTTPS；\n- 反向代理（Nginx/Caddy）、Docker Compose 或文档化的部署脚本；\n- 独立的预发布与生产环境；\n- 定时自动备份及一次演示还原测试；\n- 防火墙、仅密钥 SSH、禁止 root 登录、访问与应用日志、在线率监控；\n- 一篇部署与运维章节、最近 8 周的在线率/事故日志，以及带只读考官账号的线上 URL。\n不得在服务器上放置真实个人数据；请使用合成演示数据集。\n用户验收测试（与自测分开评估）：\n- 6~10 名覆盖所有角色的参与者（NGO 员工优先；可用代理参与者但须声明为局限）。第 20 周前确认参与者。\n- 书面 UAT 计划，含可追溯至需求列表的角色特定测试脚本：前置条件、步骤、预期结果、通过/失败/带备注通过。\n- 参与者在线上服务器上独立完成，不作引导演示。\n- 测量：每项需求通过率、任务时间、错误数、系统可用性量表（SUS）与简短访谈。参与者必须对照独立的人工计算核查一个完整薪资期。\n- 在 UAT 开始前与导师达成签字验收标准（如所有必备需求通过、无未关闭的关键/重大缺陷、SUS ≥ 68）。\n- 报告初次结果与修复后结果，将签字验收作为附录。\n交付成果：租用服务器上的线上系统（公共 URL + 考官账号）；含 README 与部署手册的源代码仓库；需求与设计文档；测试报告；UAT 计划、脚本、结果与签字；论文与现场口头演示。\n评分标准：必备需求的功能覆盖；审批、应享与薪资引擎的正确性；可配置性（演示在零代码改动下为第二种不同的请假与薪资政策重新配置系统）；UAT 结果与可用性；生产部署质量、代码与安全质量。\n失分点：\n1. 在 localhost 上演示；\n2. 将\"一个审批人\"或\"两个审批人\"硬编码，而非可配置引擎；\n3. 用源自自己代码的测试用例测试规则引擎——那是循环论证。测试用例应从政策文档推导，并在实现模块前请人评审；\n4. 声称符合薪资法规，或声称 NGO 已采纳该系统。只声称你构建并测试的内容：一个经过部署评估的原型。\nAI 使用：AI 可协助语言、结构与归纳你自己的材料，不得生成你的数据、技术结果或参考文献。请完整填写 AI 声明——声明不完整将单独记录，与学业成绩分开。","cats":["CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MMCHOY-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"云端部署的教会网络平台：策略驱动访问控制、房间预订与容量管理注册","titleEn":"A Cloud-Deployed Church Web Platform with Policy-Driven Access Control, Room Booking and Capacity-Managed Registration","areaZh":"全栈应用","areaEn":"full stack application","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"Dr. ALI Zulfiqar","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-09 15:29:21","descriptionText":"A Cloud-Deployed Church Web Platform with Policy-Driven Access Control, Room Booking and Capacity-Managed Registration \n (This is an individual full-stack project. Estimated from start to end about 30 weeks)\n PROBLEM \n A church is a public organisation and a membership organisation at the same time. Service times and sermons must be open; member directories, small-group rosters, pastoral records and Sunday school registers must not. Most churches run this on a static site, messaging groups, paper sign-up sheets and a shared booking spreadsheet - producing double-booked rooms, over-subscribed events, directories circulating as unprotected files, etc.\n AIM \n Build, deploy and evaluate a platform where visibility rules, booking constraints and registration policies are set by an ADMINISTRATOR AS CONFIGURATION and enforced by a central engine - not scattered through your code as if-statements.\n THE THREE THINGS THAT MAKE THIS AN MSc PROJECT \n Read this first. \"A church website\" is not a master's dissertation. These are:\n 1. POLICY ENGINE (your primary contribution)\n Every content item and every action carries an admin-defined access policy:\n public / any member / attribute-based (e.g. attended course XXX) / role-based (ministry leader) / relationship-based (guardian of THIS child). All of it evaluated at ONE enforcement point. List endpoints filter through the same engine, so a member never receives a record they cannot see - not merely \"cannot open it\". Provide an admin rule editor and a \"view as this role\" simulator. \n 2. SCHEDULING AND CONFLICT ENGINE \n room bookings (single or recurring), buffer/setup time between bookings, room capacity, equipment requirements, approval workflow for restricted rooms. Conflict detection = expand the recurrence series into an occupancy table, then interval-overlap query with buffers applied.\n 3. CAPACITY ALLOCATION UNDER CONCURRENCY \n Two users click \"register\" for the last activity place at the same instant. The correct result is ONE registration and ONE waitlist entry. Enforce capacity at \n the database level (transaction or constrained counter), then PROVE it with a concurrent load experiment. This is an excellent evaluation section - do not skip it. \n REMAINING FUNCTIONS (supporting infrastructure, not the contribution)\n - Public site: pages, announcements, sermons + media, service times, calendar, scheduled publish/expire. \n - Members: self-service profile and household, ministry group membership, cell groups, volunteer serving with availability and clash detection against bookings.\n - Events: capacity, waitlist with auto-promotion and a time-limited claim window, registration windows, eligibility (age band, membership status, prerequisite course), register several from one household in one transaction, fee recorded (NOT collected). \n - Safeguarding: guardian-verified registration, medical/allergy notes with restricted visibility and their own audit trail, check-in/check-out with a matching code. \n - Admin: content management, membership setup, role and policy configuration, audit log.\n MANDATORY: RENT A SERVER - NO LOCALHOST DEMOS \n Live public service on a rented VPS from about week 10 (can be reimbursed). Required and assessed:\n - public domain, HTTPS with auto-renewing certificate\n - separate staging and production\n - automated database AND media backups PLUS a demonstrated restore test\n - firewall, key-only SSH, rate limiting on login and registration endpoints\n - access and application logging, uptime monitoring\n - Deployment and Operations chapter, uptime/incident log for the final 8 weeks, live URL + read-only examiner account + one test account PER ROLE \n ABSOLUTE RULE ON DATA \n No real personal data. The demonstration dataset is synthetic and the supervisor checks it b efore UAT. This is not negotiable. \n USER ACCEPTANCE TESTING \n - 8 to 12 participants covering EVERY role: public visitor, ordinary member, parent of a child, ministry leader, booking approver, activity organizer, general admin, super user (can view/edit everything).\n - Written UAT plan, role-specific scripts traceable to your requirement list, executed UNAIDED on the live server. Pass / fail / pass-with-comment per step. \n - NEGATIVE TEST SCRIPTS - required. e.g. Participants deliberately try to reach what they must not see. \n - Also measure: task time, error and assisted-task counts, System Usability Scale (SUS), short interview, and a WCAG 2.1 AA check on the public pages. \n - Sign-off criteria agreed with the supervisor BEFORE UAT: all must-have requirements pass, ZERO access-control failures (one is critical), no open critical/major defects, \n SUS ≥ 68. Report initial AND post-fix results; sign-off goes in an appendix.\n DELIVERABLES \n Live platform (public URL, examiner account, one test account per role)\n repository with README, deployment runbook and synthetic dataset\n requirements, access-rule catalogue and design documentation\n test report with the FULL access-control matrix and concurrency results\n UAT plan, scripts, results, sign-off\n HOW YOU WILL BE MARKED \n Functional coverage\n access-control correctness (complete role × resource × action matrix, every cell asserted and passing)\n booking-engine correctness\n concurrency safety\n configurability (reconfigure for a SECOND church with different roles and visibility rules, with NO code change)\n UAT and usability\n quality of the production deployment\n code and security quality.\n THINGS THAT LOSE MARKS \n 1. Demonstrating on localhost.\n 2. Writing access checks as scattered if-statements instead of one policy engine. If your authorisation logic appears in more than one place, the contribution is gone. \n 3. Ignoring concurrency because \"it works when I click it myself\".\n 4. Deriving your test cases from your own code. That is circular. Derive them from the requirement specification and have them reviewed before you implement the module. \n 5. Presenting this as a finished system for a real congregation. Claim only what you built and tested: a prototype with an evaluated deployment. \n AI USE \n AI may assist with language, structure and summarising your own material. It must not g enerate your data, technical results or references. Complete the AI declaration in \n full - an incomplete declaration is recorded separately from your academic grade.","descZh":"云端部署的教会网站平台：策略驱动访问控制、房间预订与容量受管报名\n（这是一个个人全栈项目，预计从开始到结束约 30 周）\n问题：教会同时是公共组织与会员组织。礼拜时间与讲道必须公开；会员名录、小组名单、教牧记录与主日学名册则不可公开。多数教会用静态网站、聊天群组、纸质报名表与共享预订表格来管理——造成房间重复预订、活动超额报名、名录以未受保护文件传播等问题。\n目标：构建、部署并评估一个平台——可见性规则、预订约束与报名政策由管理员以配置方式设定，并由中央引擎强制执行——而不是散落在代码中的 if 语句。\n使这成为硕士项目的三件事（请先读）：\n\"教会网站\"不是硕士论文。以下才是：\n1. 策略引擎（你的主要贡献）：每个内容项与每个动作都携带管理员定义的访问策略：公开 / 任一会员 / 基于属性（如\"修读过课程 XXX\"）/ 基于角色（事工领袖）/ 基于关系（某位孩子的监护人）。全部在单一执行点评估。列表端点也经同一引擎过滤，因此会员永远不会收到他无权查看的记录——而不仅仅是\"无法打开它\"。提供管理员规则编辑器与\"以该角色查看\"模拟器。\n2. 排程与冲突引擎：房间预订（单次或周期）、预订间缓冲/准备时间、房间容量、设备要求、受限房间的审批流程。冲突检测 = 将周期序列展开为占用表，再以缓冲时间做区间重叠查询。\n3. 并发下的容量分配：两名用户在同一瞬间点击\"报名\"最后一个活动名额。正确结果是一个报名与一个候补记录。必须在数据库层面（事务或受约束计数器）强制容量，然后用并发负载实验证明。这是很好的评估章节——不要跳过。\n其余功能（支撑性基础设施，非核心贡献）：\n- 公共网站：页面、公告、讲道与媒体、礼拜时间、日历、定时发布/过期；\n- 会员：自助档案与家庭、事工组会员资格、小组、志愿服务（含可用性与与预订的冲突检测）；\n- 活动：容量、自动递补与限时认领窗口的候补名单、报名窗口、资格（年龄段、会员身份、先修课程）、一笔事务为同一家庭多人报名、记录费用（不收款）；\n- 保护：监护人核验报名、限制可见且有独立审计轨迹的医疗/过敏备注、凭配对码的签到/签退；\n- 管理：内容管理、会员设置、角色与策略配置、审计日志。\n强制要求：租用服务器——禁止本机演示。\n约第 10 周起在已租用 VPS 上运行线上公共服务（费用可报销）。需要且将被评估：\n- 公共域名、带自动续期证书的 HTTPS；\n- 独立预发布与生产环境；\n- 自动化数据库与媒体备份及一次演示还原测试；\n- 防火墙、仅密钥 SSH、登录与注册端点限流；\n- 访问与应用日志、在线率监控；\n- 部署与运维章节、最后 8 周的在线率/事故日志、线上 URL + 只读考官账号 + 每个角色一个测试账号。\n数据绝对规则：不得使用真实个人数据。演示数据集为合成数据，导师在 UAT 前检查。这没有商量余地。\n用户验收测试：\n- 8~12 名覆盖每个角色的参与者：公开访客、普通会员、孩子家长、事工领袖、预订审批人、活动组织者、普通管理员、超级用户（可查看/编辑一切）；\n- 书面 UAT 计划、可追溯至需求列表的角色特定脚本，在线上服务器上独立执行。每步记录通过/失败/带备注通过；\n- 必须有负向测试脚本——例如参与者故意尝试访问其不应看到的内容；\n- 另测量：任务时间、错误数与获助任务数、系统可用性量表（SUS）、简短访谈，以及对公共页面做 WCAG 2.1 AA 检查；\n- 在 UAT 前与导师商定签字验收标准：所有必备需求通过、零访问控制失败（一次即为关键缺陷）、无未关闭的关键/重大缺陷、SUS ≥ 68。分别报告初次与修复后结果；签字验收放入附录。\n交付成果：线上平台（公共 URL、考官账号、每角色一个测试账号）；含 README、部署手册与合成数据集的仓库；需求、访问规则目录与设计文档；含完整访问控制矩阵与并发结果的测试报告；UAT 计划、脚本、结果与签字。\n评分标准：功能覆盖；访问控制正确性（完整角色 × 资源 × 动作矩阵，每格均被断言且通过）；预订引擎正确性；并发安全；可配置性（在零代码改动下为第二个拥有不同角色与可见性规则的教会重新配置）；UAT 与可用性；生产部署质量；代码与安全质量。\n失分点：\n1. 在 localhost 上演示；\n2. 将访问检查写成散落的 if 语句而非统一策略引擎。若你的授权逻辑出现在不止一处，该贡献即告消失；\n3. 因\"我自己点它时能工作\"而忽视并发；\n4. 用源自自己代码的测试用例。那是循环论证。测试用例应从需求规格推导，并在实现模块前请人评审；\n5. 把它作为面向真实会众的成品系统呈现。只声称你构建并测试的内容：一个经过部署评估的原型。\nAI 使用：使用 AI 工具帮助编写代码、起草文字或理解论文是允许的且必须声明。这与在系统内部署 AI 组件完全不同——本项目不这样做。请不要在论文中混淆二者。","cats":["CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CHLIU-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"钝体空气动力学的 CFD 分析","titleEn":"CFD Analysis of Bluff-Body Aerodynamics","areaZh":"航空航天工程；设计 & 制造；热流体","areaEn":"Aerospace engineering; Design & manufacture; and Thermofluids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LIU Chun Ho","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"his group of dissertations investigates the aerodynamics of bluff bodies using computational fluid dynamics (CFD), aiming to enhance understanding of flow behavior and vortex shedding phenomena in order to improve the aerodynamic performance.\n Bluff bodies, characterized by their non-streamlined shapes, induce complex flow patterns including flow separation, wake formation, and unsteady vortex shedding, which significantly influence drag forces and structural stability. The study employs high-resolution CFD simulations, utilizing turbulence models, such as k-ω SST turbulence models and/or large eddy simulation (LES), to capture the flow dynamics around various bluff body geometries. Parametric analyses explore the effects of shape, size, and flow velocity on vortex shedding frequency, drag coefficients, and pressure distributions.\n The findings contribute to optimizing bluff body designs in various engineering applications such as flight and/or vehicle aerodynamics, where reducing drag and controlling vortex-induced vibrations are critical. In short, this work enhances the predictive capabilities of CFD for bluff body aerodynamics, offering valuable guidance for engineers and researchers to improve structural performance and aerodynamic efficiency by refined innovative design.\n Examples of bluff bodies\n 1. Vehicles\n 2. Heavy-duty trucks\n 3. Sports cars or race cars\n 4. unmanned aircraft\n 5. submarines\n Pre-requisites\n Base computer skill, applied maths, and data analysis.\n Co-requisites\n Computational fluid dynamics (CFD)","descZh":"本组论文利用计算流体力学（CFD）研究钝体空气动力学，旨在加深对流动行为与涡脱落现象的理解，以提升气动性能。\n钝体以其非流线型外形为特征，会诱发复杂的流动模式，包括流动分离、尾流形成与非定常涡脱落，这些现象显著影响阻力与结构稳定性。研究采用高分辨率 CFD 仿真，利用 k-ω SST 湍流模型和/或大涡模拟（LES）等湍流模型，捕捉多种钝体几何周围的流动动力学。参数化分析探讨形状、尺寸与流速对涡脱落频率、阻力系数与压力分布的影响。\n研究结果有助于在飞行器和/或车辆空气动力学等各类工程应用中优化钝体设计——在这些场景中，减阻与控制涡激振动至关重要。总之，本工作提升了 CFD 对钝体空气动力学的预测能力，为工程师与研究人员通过精细创新设计提升结构性能与气动效率提供了宝贵指导。\n钝体示例：1. 车辆；2. 重型卡车；3. 跑车或赛车；4. 无人机；5. 潜艇。\n先修要求：基础计算机技能、应用数学与数据分析。\n并修要求：计算流体力学（CFD）。","cats":["AERO","CS","DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CHLIU-9","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"特斯拉阀的实验室实验","titleEn":"Laboratory Experiment for Tesla Valve","areaZh":"设计 & 制造；热流体","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; and thermofluilds","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LIU Chun Ho","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-06 10:34:01","descriptionText":"These MSc dissertations focus on the experimental investigations of Tesla Valve, a passive, check-valve-like device designed by Nikola Tesla that allows fluid flow predominantly in one direction without any moving parts.\n Tesla Valve operates based on intricate geometrical features that creates varying flow resistances depending on the flow direction, making it an innovative solution to fluid control in various engineering applications. These projects involve designing and fabricating a scaled model of Tesla Valve for a series of laboratory experiments to analyze its flow characteristics, performance, pressure drops, and efficiency under different operating conditions.\n Using flow visualization techniques and precise measurement instruments, the study aims to quantify the valve's unidirectional flow behavior and assess its potential advantages/disadvantages over traditional check valves. This work not only enhances the understanding of Tesla Valve and fluid dynamics but also explores its practical viability in systems requiring reliable, maintenance-free flow control. The findings could contribute to advancements in microfluidics, thermal management, and hydraulic systems, where passive, no-moving-part valves are highly desirable. Overall, the dissertations combine theoretical analysis with empirical data to evaluate the performance of Tesla Valve performance, providing valuable insights into its potential applications and future improvements.\n Pre-requisites\n Fluid mechanics, applied maths, and data analysis/processing\n Co-requisites\n Basic laboratory instrumentation","descZh":"本组硕士论文聚焦于特斯拉阀的实验研究——这是尼古拉·特斯拉设计的一种无源、类止回阀装置，无需任何运动部件即可使流体主要沿一个方向流动。\n特斯拉阀基于精巧的几何特征运行，随流向不同产生变化的流阻，是多种工程应用中流体控制的创新方案。本项目涉及设计并制造特斯拉阀缩尺模型，开展一系列实验室实验，分析其在不同运行条件下的流动特性、性能、压降与效率。\n研究利用流动可视化技术与精密测量仪器，旨在量化该阀的单向流动行为，并评估其相对于传统止回阀的潜在优劣。本工作不仅加深对特斯拉阀与流体动力学的理解，还探索其在需要可靠、免维护流体控制的系统中的实际可行性。研究成果可为微流控、热管理与液压系统作出贡献——这些领域非常需要无运动部件的无源阀门。总体而言，本论文将理论分析与实验数据相结合，评估特斯拉阀的性能，为其潜在应用与未来改进提供有价值的洞见。\n先修要求：流体力学、应用数学与数据分析/处理。\n并修要求：基础实验室仪器操作。","cats":["CS","DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DMSHIN-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"含凝胶聚合物电解质的透明湿气发电器件","titleEn":"Transparent moisture electricity generator comprising gel polymer electrolyte","areaZh":"能源工程；材料 & 固体","areaEn":"Energy Engineering; Materials & Solids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SHIN Dong-myeong","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-03 13:07:02","descriptionText":"Sustainable electric power generation from ambient energy has become one of the desired technologies in the world as a countermeasure to excessive energy consumption as advanced technologies permeate our daily lives. Moisture electricity generators (MEGs) have emerged as one of the promising candidates for fulfilling energy demands. This project aims to develop a transparent MEGs using gel polymer electrolytes in which hydrogels serve as both the functional active medium and the electrode interface. Conventional MEGs often require high relative humidity, suffer high internal resistance, short-lived output, and are opaque or mechanically rigid, hindering practical deployment. Here, we engineer an optically clear, ionically conductive hydrogel integrated with transparent conductive networks to combine high transparency with low electronic and ionic losses. The device operation exploits continuous hygroscopic water uptake from ambient air and controlled surface evaporation to establish a persistent moisture gradient and steady ion flux, producing long-lasting electrical output. Expected outcomes are a proof-of-concept transparent MEG that functions over a broad humidity range and delivers sustained, stable power through continuous water uptake–evaporation cycles, suitable for integration into windows, wearable optics, and transparent sensor platforms. This work will advance the materials- and device-level understanding of hydrogel-based moisture-to-electricity transduction and provide a practical route toward visually unobtrusive ambient-energy harvesting.","descZh":"随着先进技术渗透日常生活，可持续地从环境能量发电已成为全球理想的技术方向，以应对过度能源消耗。湿气发电机（MEG）已成为满足能源需求的有前景候选之一。本项目旨在利用凝胶聚合物电解质开发透明 MEG，其中水凝胶既充当功能性活性介质又充当电极界面。传统 MEG 往往需要高相对湿度、存在高内阻、输出寿命短，且不透明或机械刚硬，阻碍实际部署。在此，我们工程化一种光学透明、离子导电的水凝胶，并与透明导电网络集成，将高透明度与低电子/离子损耗相结合。器件运行利用从环境空气中持续吸湿以及受控表面蒸发，建立持久的湿气梯度与稳定离子通量，产生持久电输出。预期成果是一个可在宽湿度范围内工作、通过持续吸水-蒸发循环提供稳定持久功率的概念验证透明 MEG，适合集成到窗户、可穿戴光学器件与透明传感器平台。本工作将推进基于水凝胶的\"湿气-电\"转换在材料与器件层面的理解，并为视觉上不显眼的环境能量收集提供一条实用途径。","cats":["ENE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DMSHIN-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"不受湿度影响的旋转式摩擦纳米发电机（r-TENG）","titleEn":"Humidity-independent rotational triboelectric nanogenerators (r-TENGs)","areaZh":"能源工程；材料 & 固体","areaEn":"Energy Engineering; Materials & Solids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SHIN Dong-myeong","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-03 13:07:02","descriptionText":"Rotational triboelectric nanogenerators (r-TENGs) are promising candidates for powering Internet of Things devices, owing to their ability to convert continuous mechanical motion into electricity. Nevertheless, improving their electrical output often comes at the cost of increased frictional degradation, which limits overall energy-conversion efficiency and long-term durability. In this proposal, we embark on the development of aerodynamic triboelectric nanogenerators for airflow energy harvesting. The proposed research will focus on: (1) Design and fabrication of aerodynamic TENGs to scavenge the wind energy with humidity-independent materials, and (2) Characterization and optimization of electrical outputs, durability, and sustainability of the resulting devices at strong and unstable airflow stimuli.","descZh":"旋转式摩擦纳米发电机（r-TENG）因能将连续机械运动转化为电能，被视为为物联网设备供电的有前景方案。然而，提升其电输出往往以加剧摩擦退化、限制整体能量转换效率与长期耐久性为代价。在本方案中，我们着手开发用于气流能量收集的气动式摩擦纳米发电机。拟开展的研究将聚焦：(1) 设计与制造气动式 TENG，采用与湿度无关的材料收集风能；(2) 表征并优化所得器件在强而不稳定气流激励下的电输出、耐久性与可持续性。","cats":["ENE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DMSHIN-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向无芯片可穿戴电源模块应用的无整流器摩擦纳米发电机","titleEn":"Rectifier-Free Triboelectric Nanogenerators towards chip-less wearable power module applications","areaZh":"能源工程；材料 & 固体","areaEn":"Energy Engineering; Materials & Solids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SHIN Dong-myeong","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-03 13:07:02","descriptionText":"A conceptual shift toward next-generation wearable electronics is driving research into self-powered electronics technologies that can be independently operated without plugging into the grid for external power feeding. Triboelectric nanogenerators (TENGs) are emerging as a key component of self-powered electronics, but a power type mismatch between supply and demand limits their direct implementation into wearable self-powered electronics. We envision a TENG with switchable power mode capability where the charge flow direction is modulated over the course of slow and random mechanical stimuli, with exceptional rectification, stable outputs over the cycles, and design flexibility in strechable platforms. Importantly, the remarkable switchable power generation with fabric counter materials illuminates a new path for the smooth integration of stretchable TENGs into wearable self-powered electronics.","descZh":"向下一代可穿戴电子的概念转变，正在推动自供电电子技术的研究——这类器件可独立运行，无需接入电网供电。摩擦纳米发电机（TENG）正成为自供电电子的关键组成部分，但供-需之间的功率类型不匹配限制了其直接应用于可穿戴自供电电子设备。我们设想一种具有可切换功率模式能力的 TENG：在缓慢、随机的机械激励过程中调控电荷流向，具备优异的整流能力、跨周期稳定输出，以及在可拉伸平台上的设计灵活性。重要的是，采用织物对摩材料实现的显著可切换发电，为将可拉伸 TENG 平滑集成到可穿戴自供电电子设备中开辟了新路径。","cats":["ENE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DMSHIN-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"利用单离子导电聚合物电解质助力锂金属电池技术","titleEn":"Leveraging Single-Ion Conducting Polymer Electrolytes for Li metal Battery Technologies","areaZh":"能源工程；材料 & 固体","areaEn":"Energy Engineering; Materials & Solids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SHIN Dong-myeong","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-03 13:07:02","descriptionText":"High energy-density Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) can increase the range of electric vehicles as well as reduce the cost. Lithium–metal batteries (LMBs) can further increase the specific energy by 35% and energy density by 50% at the cell level by eliminating the conventional graphite anode electrodes. Moreover, anode-free LMBs deliver 60% higher energy density compared to traditional LIBs by using bare Cu foil with no anode-active material. But, inherent limitations of LMBs make it difficult for Li-ion chemistries to deliver high energy densities. Lithium metal batteries that contain solid polymer electrolyte and a high voltage cathode are one of the favored choices for next-generation Li batteries. However, solid polymer electrolytes are generally difficult to meet the requirements of both excellent electrochemical properties and thermal stability. In particular, single-ion conductors, in which anion groups are tethered in the network polymer, offer many advantages over traditional liquid and solid electrolytes, including improved electrochemical stability, minimal polarization across the separator, and high resistance to dendrite growth; however, their low ionic conductivity and the necessity for organic solvents limit their potential in commercial devices. The proposed research will implement a new class of porous materials that can serve as a solvent-free single-ion conducting polymer electrolyte for use in Li-batteries with improved electrochemical performance and safety. We envision two different routes to customize the porous materials for solvent-free single-ion conducting electrolyte applications: 1) implementation of branched organic bridging linkers and 2) insertion of charged organic bridging linkers. By carefully designing intrinsic material properties, we will incorporate these materials into battery prototypes and evaluate their ability to be used as an all-solid-state battery.","descZh":"高能量密度锂离子电池（LIBs）既能增加电动汽车续航，也能降低使用成本。锂金属电池（LMBs）通过取消传统石墨负极，可将电池单体层面的比能量进一步提升约35%、能量密度提升约50%。此外，无负极锂金属电池使用裸铜箔、不含负极活性材料，相比传统锂离子电池可将能量密度提升60%。然而，LMBs 的固有局限使其难以凭借锂离子化学体系实现高能量密度。采用固态聚合物电解质与高压正极的锂金属电池是下一代锂电池的优选方案之一。但固态聚合物电解质通常难以同时满足优异的电化学性能与热稳定性。尤其是单离子导体——其阴离子基团被锚定在网络聚合物中——相比传统液态与固态电解质具有诸多优势，包括更好的电化学稳定性、隔膜两端极化最小化以及较强的抗枝晶生长能力；但其离子电导率低，且需要使用有机溶剂，限制了其在商业化器件中的应用潜力。本研究将实现一类新型多孔材料，用作锂电池用无溶剂单离子导电聚合物电解质，以改善电化学性能与安全性。我们设想两条定制化路线来使多孔材料适用于无溶剂单离子导电电解质应用：1) 引入支化有机桥联连接基；2) 插入带电荷的有机桥联连接基。通过精心设计材料本征特性，我们将把这些材料集成到电池原型中，评估其作为全固态电池电解质的可用性。","cats":["ENE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DMSHIN-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于采集人体能量的可穿戴摩擦纳米发电机","titleEn":"Wearable Triboelectric Nanogenerators for Harvesting Body Energy","areaZh":"能源工程；材料 & 固体","areaEn":"Energy Engineering; Materials & Solids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SHIN Dong-myeong","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-03 13:07:02","descriptionText":"Internet of Things (IoT) technology is emerging to take an essential role in smart cities, where advanced electronics exchange information without requiring human intervention. Although IoT holds the promise of enriching the lives of humans, the technology is expected to encounter massive power consumption issues from the billions of advanced electronics connected through the network. Endeavors to meet the energy demands have focused on developing alternative powering technology, which harvests the abandoned energy resources in an independent, sustainable, and maintenance-free manner. In particular, triboelectric nanogenerators (TENGs) have been fostered as a state-of-the-art energy scavenging technology for self-powered electronics. Indeed, TENGs offer many advantages over conventional energy harvesting technologies, including their light weight, simple fabrication, material selection diversity, and high energy conversion efficiency. In this proposal, we embark on the development of wearable triboelectric nanogenerators for breath energy harvesting. The proposed research will focus on: (1) Design and fabrication of humidity inactive TENGs to scavenge the wind energy from the mouth, and (2) Characterization and optimization of electrical outputs, durability, and sustainability of the resulting devices.","descZh":"物联网（IoT）技术正成为智慧城市中的关键角色——先进电子产品无需人工干预即可交换信息。尽管物联网有望丰富人类生活，但该技术预计将面临数十亿联网先进电子产品带来的巨大功耗问题。为满足能源需求，各方致力于开发替代供电技术，以独立、可持续、免维护的方式收集废弃能源。其中，摩擦纳米发电机（TENG）已被培育为面向自供电电子的前沿能量收集技术。事实上，TENG 相比传统能量收集技术具有诸多优势，包括重量轻、制造简单、材料选择多样与能量转换效率高。在本方案中，我们着手开发用于呼吸能量收集的可穿戴摩擦纳米发电机。拟开展的研究将聚焦：(1) 设计与制造湿度不敏感型 TENG，以收集口部呼出的风能；(2) 表征并优化所得器件的电输出、耐久性与可持续性。","cats":["ENE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DMSHIN-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于手语翻译的摩擦纳米发电机手套","titleEn":"Triboelectric Nanogenerators Glove for Sign Language Translation","areaZh":"能源工程；材料 & 固体","areaEn":"Energy Engineering; Materials & Solids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SHIN Dong-myeong","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-03 13:07:02","descriptionText":"Wearable ultra-sensitivity sensors are imperatively necessary technology to give shape to innovative healthcare systems, especially for physically challenged people. Triboelectric technology is one of the remarkable candidates to realize these applications. This is because it not only has a self-power function without external electrical suppliers such as batteries but also is suitable to fabricate wearable devices consisting of bio-friendly materials which can be directly touched with human skin without any toxicity risk. The triboelectric generators are composed of two materials with different triboelectric polarities, so they can induce and transfer surface charge during mechanical motions such as rubbing, twisting, touching, banding, and compressing. Therefore, the generators can be utilized as sensors for detecting slight movements. However, if the intensity of the electrical signals is too weak, they would be hard to discriminate clearly, which can cause a critical malfunction of gesture analysis. Hence, one of the critical key points for making high-sensing performance is how to generate obvious and distinguishable electrical signals. In this work, we will develop sign language recognition and translation system by using the triboelectric generators attached to gloves.","descZh":"可穿戴超高灵敏度传感器是塑造创新型医疗健康体系的必要技术，对肢体残障人群尤其如此。摩擦电技术是实现这类应用的重要候选之一，因为它不仅具有无需电池等外部供电设备的自供电功能，而且适合制造由生物友好材料构成、可直接接触人体皮肤且无毒性风险的可穿戴器件。摩擦发电机由两种摩擦电极性不同的材料构成，在摩擦、扭转、触碰、弯折与压缩等机械运动过程中可感应并转移表面电荷。因此，发电机可用作检测细微运动的传感器。然而，若电信号强度过弱，将难以清晰区分，可能导致手势分析的严重误判。因此，实现高传感性能的关键点之一是如何产生明显且可区分的电信号。在本工作中，我们将利用附着于手套上的摩擦发电机，开发手语识别与翻译系统。","cats":["ENE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DMSHIN-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向先进离子电导率的高熵选择性离子导电网络聚合物","titleEn":"High-Entropy Selective Ion-Conducting Network Polymers for Advanced Ionic Conductivity","areaZh":"能源工程；材料 & 固体","areaEn":"Energy Engineering; Materials & Solids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SHIN Dong-myeong","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-03 13:07:02","descriptionText":"State-of-the-art selective ion-conducting polymers show room‑temperature ionic conductivities <10−5 S cm−1, far below practical requirements. Adding plasticizers yields gel electrolytes with higher conductivity, but introduces flammability and poor mechanical strength (often >80wt% organic solvent). A key challenge is the trade‑off between ion transport and mechanical robustness; decoupling ion conduction pathways from polymer microstructures is essential for viable solid electrolytes. This proposal aims to implement high-entropy design principles into selective ion-conducting network polymers to decouple the ion transport from the structural framework of network polymers to achieve high ionic conductivity at room temperature. The project is centered around three primary objectives.\nIn the first objective, we will design a family of high-entropy selective ion-conducting (SIC) network polymers with controlled local disorder and repeating‑unit distortion. We propose that controlled local symmetry breaking—introducing configurational disorder of ionic and non‑ionic units in highly crosslinked networks—will decouple ion conduction from mechanical integrity. We will vary repeating‑unit disorder and network connectivity to determine the effects on ion transport. Design strategies include (1) tuning local arrangement via stereoisomers, (2) disrupting diamondoid symmetry with length‑mismatched bridges, and (3) varying crosslink density. From this objective, we aim to achieve increased local segmental mobility, lower ion-conduction activation energy, and preserved macroscopic mechanical stability.","descZh":"目前最先进的选择性离子传导聚合物在室温下的离子电导率 <10⁻⁵ S cm⁻¹，远低于实际应用要求。添加增塑剂可得到电导率更高的凝胶电解质，但会引入可燃性并导致机械强度较差（通常含 >80 wt% 有机溶剂）。一个关键挑战在于离子传输与机械稳健性之间的权衡；将离子传导路径与聚合物微观结构解耦，对于获得可行的固态电解质至关重要。本提案旨在将高熵设计原理引入选择性离子传导网络聚合物，将离子传输与网络聚合物的结构骨架解耦，从而在室温下实现高离子电导率。项目围绕三个主要目标展开。\n在第一个目标中，我们将设计一族具有可控局部无序与重复单元畸变的高熵选择性离子传导（SIC）网络聚合物。我们提出，受控的局部对称性破缺——在高度交联的网络中引入离子单元与非离子单元的构型无序——将使离子传导与机械完整性解耦。我们将改变重复单元的无序程度与网络连通性，以确定其对离子传输的影响。设计策略包括：(1) 通过立体异构体调节局部排列，(2) 用长度不匹配的桥破坏类金刚石（diamondoid）对称性，以及 (3) 改变交联密度。通过该目标，我们期望实现局部链段运动性增强、离子传导活化能降低，并保持宏观机械稳定性。","cats":["ENE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DMSHIN-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"利用高度有序多孔骨架实现全面的材料间离子传输","titleEn":"Exploiting highly-ordered porous frameworks for comprehensive inter-material ionic transfers","areaZh":"能源工程；材料 & 固体","areaEn":"Energy Engineering; Materials & Solids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SHIN Dong-myeong","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-03 13:07:02","descriptionText":"Electrostatic charging derived from charge transfer throughout friction is commonplace, but understanding charge species transferred still remains a debated and contradictory issue. The electron has been widely believed to be the dominant charge carrier for contact electrification between solids, liquids, and gases. However, emerging evidence points to the trait that mobile ions can also contribute to the process, making it more complicated to elucidate even in polymer-polymer contact electrification. This proposal aims to delve into the intricate workings of ionomers and their role in the field of triboelectricity, specifically focusing on the inter-material ion transfer in polymer-ionomer contact electrification. The project is centered around three primary objectives. First, we aim to design and synthesize a series of model ionomers with controlled ion density and ion/electron binding affinity. The second objective is to investigate the inter-material ion transfer during the contact electrification of ionomers/polymer systems. The final objective is to establish a new model for inter-material ion transfer during contact electrification with ionomers.","descZh":"摩擦过程中电荷转移引起的静电充电十分常见，但对转移电荷种类的理解仍存在争议与矛盾。电子被广泛认为是固体、液体与气体间接触起电的主要电荷载体。然而，新出现的证据表明，可移动离子也可能参与该过程，使即便聚合物-聚合物接触起电的机理也难以阐明。本方案旨在深入探究离聚物及其在摩擦电领域的复杂作用，特别聚焦于聚合物-离聚物接触起电中的材料间离子转移。项目围绕三个主要目标展开：其一，设计并合成一系列具有可控离子密度与离子/电子结合亲和力的模型离聚物；其二，研究离聚物/聚合物体系接触起电过程中的材料间离子转移；其三，建立离聚物接触起电中材料间离子转移的新模型。","cats":["ENE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DMSHIN-9","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向安全可持续电池应用的选择性离子导电自修复网络聚合物","titleEn":"Selective-Ion Conducting Self-Healable Network Polymers for Safe and Sustainable Battery Application","areaZh":"能源工程；材料 & 固体","areaEn":"Energy Engineering; Materials & Solids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SHIN Dong-myeong","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-03 13:07:02","descriptionText":"Li-ion batteries featuring liquid electrolytes are ubiquitous across a wide range of applications, the technology still suffers from safety hazards and high costs, hindering their application in systems that require high energy density. Especially, dendrite growth arising from the reaction of liquid electrolytes with Li-metal results in an internal short-circuit, leading to possible thermal runaway and combustion of the battery. The need to supplement battery packs with engineering controls to prevent such short circuits increases battery costs and lowers the specific energy density of the overall device. The selective-ion conducting (SIC) polymer electrolytes can overcome many current issues with liquid electrolytes, and they offer possibilities for the development of new battery technologies. Indeed, the SIC polymer electrolytes showcased a high ionic selectivity in conduction, a wide electrochemical stability window, and improved resistance to dendrite formation. However, further studies are required to address the low ionic conductivity as well as crack formation during battery cycling.\nOur primary hypothesis is that the self-healability of polymer electrolytes provides autonomous repair of the cracks formed over the course of charging/discharging, improving their ionic conductivity and mechanical stability. Our preliminary studies document the functionality of SIC polymer electrolytes in Li-metal batteries. But, we found that the defects generated in the membrane fabrication lower the ionic conductivity and resistance to the dendrite growth. Our finding implies that a membrane without SIC defects (or cracks) improves the ionic conduction and safety of electrolytes, but our preliminary work has limitations as the creation and spread of cracks are unavoidable in the given copious parasitic reaction during battery operation.\nWe propose to embark on a program to systematically test our hypothesis by implementing self-healing moieties into SIC network polymers. We will establish the experimental routes to install dynamic hydrogen bondings as a self-healing motif to our SIC network polymers that exhibit a moderate cationic conductivity with exceptional selectivity, an outstanding electrochemical and thermal stability. Dynamic hydrogen bonding moieties possess directionality and high concentration per volume, providing acceptable autonomous repair of the cracks. After carefully designing materials, we will develop methods for crack-free membrane processing to incorporate these materials into battery prototypes, permitting effective functioning of electrolytes in the battery. A new class of polymer electrolytes could substantially enhance the safety and sustainability of Li-metal batteries. Our strategies are designed to facilitate the seamless integration of Li-metal batteries into industrial applications. Furthermore, the development of such energy storage technologies will bolster Hong Kong's status as a pioneering clean energy city, distinguished by its innovative and cutting-edge technology.","descZh":"采用液态电解质的锂离子电池虽广泛应用于各类领域，但仍存在安全危害与高成本问题，阻碍其在需要高能量密度的系统中的应用。尤其是液态电解质与锂金属反应引起的枝晶生长会导致内部短路，可能引发热失控与电池燃烧。为防此类短路而给电池组增补工程控制措施，提高了电池成本并降低了整体器件的比能量密度。选择性离子传导（SIC）聚合物电解质可克服液态电解质的诸多现有问题，并为新电池技术的发展提供可能。事实上，SIC 聚合物电解质在传导中展现出高离子选择性、宽电化学稳定窗口与更好的抗枝晶形成能力。然而，仍需进一步研究以解决离子电导率低以及电池循环中开裂的问题。\n我们的核心假设是：聚合物电解质的自愈合能力可自动修复充放电过程中形成的裂纹，从而提升其离子电导率与机械稳定性。我们的初步研究记录了 SIC 聚合物电解质在锂金属电池中的功能。但我们发现，膜制造中产生的缺陷会降低离子电导率与抗枝晶能力。我们的发现意味着：无 SIC 缺陷（或裂纹）的膜可改善电解质的离子传导与安全性；但我们的初步工作存在局限，因为在电池运行中大量寄生反应下，裂纹的产生与扩展难以避免。\n我们提议开展一项计划，通过将自愈合基团引入 SIC 网络聚合物，系统验证我们的假设。我们将建立实验路线，将动态氢键作为自愈合基元安装到我们的 SIC 网络聚合物中——该聚合物具有适度的阳离子电导率与卓越的选择性，以及优异的电化学与热稳定性。动态氢键基元具有方向性与高体积浓度，可提供可接受的裂纹自主修复。在精心设计材料后，我们将开发无裂纹膜加工方法，将这些材料集成到电池原型中，使电解质在电池中有效工作。一类新型聚合物电解质可大幅提升锂金属电池的安全性与可持续性。我们的策略旨在促进锂金属电池无缝集成到工业应用中。此外，此类储能技术的发展将巩固香港作为以创新前沿技术著称的清洁能源先锋城市的地位。","cats":["ENE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DMSHIN-10","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"利用电源管理系统定制摩擦纳米发电机的电输出","titleEn":"Customizing Electrical Outputs of Triboelectric Nanogenerators using Power Management System","areaZh":"能源工程；材料 & 固体","areaEn":"Energy Engineering; Materials & Solids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SHIN Dong-myeong","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-03 13:07:02","descriptionText":"In the era of the internet of things, wearable electronic technology can help citizens to avail advanced features and characteristics in different facets of their daily life. These wearable electronics are powered by current energy storage devices that need regular recharging, but the increasing demand for continuous operation is driving research into new power supplies that can deliver stable electricity. One major development has been a conceptual shift away from grid supply charging toward self-powering, in which the devices continuously operate without external power feeding. Triboelectric nanogenerators (TENGs) are emerging as a power supply for self-powered electronics due to their light weight, simple fabrication, diversity in material selection, and high energy conversion efficiency, but the power output of TENGs needs to be trimmed to stably power the electronics. There is a need for non-rigid flexible power management circuits for self-powered electronic applications, particularly for wearable devices.\nWe propose to embark on a program to develop a new class of flexible power management components that can modulate the electrical output of TENGs to stably power wearable electronics. We envision two main routes for achieving an efficient power management system for flexible TENGs: (1) minimization of circuit components to decrease power loss in the circuit components, and (2) modification of the structural design for flexible TENGs. The key design routes include incorporating air gaps into TENGs for DC voltage, multilayering the coil in the induction transformer for voltage stepping, and fiber supercapacitor for stable power to realize flexible textile devices. We will incorporate the resulting components into an all-in-one TENGs and evaluate their output performance in parametric studies. We believe the successful development of a flexible power management system will contribute to making Hong Kong as a world-class smart city.","descZh":"在物联网时代，可穿戴电子技术可帮助市民在日常生活各层面享受先进功能与特性。这些可穿戴电子由需要定期充电的储能器件供电，而对持续运行的日益增长的需求正推动研究能够提供稳定电能的新型电源。一项重要进展是概念上从电网供电转向自供电——器件无需外部供电即可持续运行。摩擦纳米发电机（TENG）正因其重量轻、制造简单、材料选择多样与能量转换效率高而成为自供电电子的电源，但 TENG 的功率输出需要调节才能稳定为电子器件供电。自供电电子应用（尤其可穿戴设备）需要非刚性、柔性的功率管理电路。\n我们提议开展一项计划，开发一类可调制 TENG 电输出以稳定驱动可穿戴电子的新型柔性功率管理组件。我们设想实现高效柔性 TENG 功率管理系统的两条主要路线：(1) 最小化电路元件以降低电路元件中的功率损耗；(2) 修改柔性 TENG 的结构设计。关键设计路线包括在 TENG 中引入气隙以获得直流电压、在感应变压器中将线圈多层化以实现升压，以及使用纤维超级电容器提供稳定功率以实现柔性织物器件。我们将把所得组件集成为一体化 TENG，并通过参数化研究评估其输出性能。我们相信，柔性功率管理系统的成功开发将有助于把香港打造为世界级智慧城市。","cats":["ENE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"中国内地与香港应对气候变化影响的工程、城市与政策响应","titleEn":"Engineering, Urban and Policy Responses to Climate Change Impacts in China and Hong Kong","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Climate change is contributing to more frequent and severe extreme weather events, including heatwaves, intense rainfall, flooding, storm surges, droughts, and coastal risks. Traditionally temperate regions are increasingly experiencing prolonged periods of high temperature, while densely populated coastal cities face growing exposure to sea-level rise and extreme storm events. In addition to long-term anthropogenic climate change, natural climate variability such as El Niño can further intensify temperature and rainfall anomalies in some regions.\n China and Hong Kong provide important case studies for examining practical climate change responses. China is the world’s largest emitter of greenhouse gases but is also a major investor in renewable energy, electric transport, green infrastructure, and carbon neutrality policies. Hong Kong, as a compact and highly urbanised coastal city, faces specific risks from rising temperatures, urban heat island effects, heavy rainfall, flooding, typhoons, storm surges, and sea-level rise.\n The purpose of this project is to investigate practical initiatives that can reduce greenhouse gas emissions and/or improve resilience to climate change impacts. Students may focus on mitigation strategies, adaptation strategies, or an integrated approach. The project should examine technical feasibility, environmental benefits, implementation barriers, policy relevance, and potential application in the local or national context of China and/or Hong Kong.\n Students may select a specific dissertation title within the broad theme, depending on their academic background and research interests. For example:\n - Renewable Energy Integration into Existing Power Grids\n - Climate-Resilient Design of Urban Drainage Systems \n - Life-Cycle Carbon Assessment of Sustainable Building Materials \n - Design and Optimisation of Energy-Efficient Buildings in a Warming Climate \n - Assessment of Coastal Infrastructure Vulnerability to Sea-Level Rise","descZh":"气候变化正导致更频繁、更严重的极端天气事件，包括热浪、强降雨、洪涝、风暴潮、干旱与沿海风险。传统温带地区正经历更长的高温期，而人口密集的沿海城市面临海平面上升与极端风暴事件的更大风险。除长期人为气候变化外，厄尔尼诺等自然气候变率还会进一步加剧部分地区的气温与降水异常。\n中国与香港为考察实际气候变化应对提供了重要案例。中国是全球最大温室气体排放国，但同时也是可再生能源、电动交通、绿色基础设施与碳中和政策的重要投资方。香港作为紧凑且高度城市化的沿海城市，面临升温、城市热岛效应、强降雨、洪涝、台风、风暴潮与海平面上升带来的特定风险。\n本项目旨在调研可减少温室气体排放和/或提升对气候变化影响韧性的务实举措。学生可聚焦减缓战略、适应战略或综合方法。项目应考察技术可行性、环境效益、实施障碍、政策相关性，以及在中国和/或香港本地或国家背景下的潜在应用。\n学生可根据自身学术背景与研究兴趣，在大主题内选择具体论文题目。例如：\n- 可再生能源接入现有电网；\n- 城市排水系统的气候韧性设计；\n- 可持续建筑材料生命周期碳评估；\n- 变暖气候下节能建筑的设计与优化；\n- 沿海基础设施对海平面上升脆弱性评估。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"从数据到决策：面向智能工厂预测性维护的智能体 AI","titleEn":"From Data to Decisions: Agentic AI for Predictive Maintenance in Smart Factories","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Modern factories generate large volumes of data from sensors, machines, production lines and maintenance records. Traditionally, this data is used to detect faults or support scheduled maintenance, but agentic AI introduces a new possibility: AI systems that can not only analyse data but also plan maintenance actions, recommend repairs, prioritise work orders and interact with digital maintenance systems.\n This project investigates how agentic AI could be used to support predictive maintenance in industrial environments. The student may examine how AI agents can detect early signs of machine failure, decide whether maintenance is urgent, suggest spare parts, and help maintenance teams reduce downtime. This is an interesting project because predictive maintenance is already a major industrial application of AI, while agentic AI represents the next step towards more autonomous industrial decision-making.\n Possible case studies could include manufacturing equipment, conveyor systems, pumps, motors, HVAC systems, robotic arms or production-line machinery.","descZh":"现代工厂从传感器、机器、生产线与维护记录中产生海量数据。传统上，这些数据用于故障检测或支持计划性维护；而智能体 AI 带来了新的可能：AI 系统不仅能分析数据，还能规划维护行动、建议维修方案、确定工单优先级并与数字化维护系统交互。\n本项目考察如何利用智能体 AI 支持工业环境中的预测性维护。学生可研究 AI 智能体如何检测机器故障的早期迹象、判断维护是否紧急、建议备件，并帮助维护团队减少停机时间。这是一个有价值的课题，因为预测性维护已是 AI 的重要工业应用，而智能体 AI 代表着迈向更自主的工业决策的下一步。\n可选案例包括制造设备、输送系统、泵、电机、暖通空调（HVAC）系统、机械臂或生产线机械。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"自主工厂：利用智能体 AI 优化工业流程","titleEn":"The Autonomous Factory: Using Agentic AI to Optimise Industrial Processes","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Industrial processes often involve many variables, such as temperature, pressure, production speed, energy consumption, material flow and quality control. Human operators and conventional control systems may struggle to optimise all these variables in real time. Agentic AI could support industrial optimisation by monitoring process conditions, identifying inefficiencies, testing alternative strategies and recommending operational changes.\n This project explores whether agentic AI can improve the efficiency, productivity and sustainability of industrial processes. The student could investigate applications in manufacturing, logistics, food processing, chemical production, semiconductor production or energy-intensive industries. The project is potentially interesting because many industries are under pressure to reduce costs, save energy, improve quality and lower carbon emissions. Agentic AI may become a powerful tool in achieving these objectives.\n The project could be based on a literature review, case study analysis, simulation, or the design of a conceptual agentic AI workflow.","descZh":"工业过程常涉及许多变量，如温度、压力、生产速度、能耗、物料流与质量控制。人工操作员与常规控制系统可能难以实时优化所有这些变量。智能体 AI 可通过监测过程条件、识别低效环节、测试替代策略并建议运行调整来支持工业优化。\n本项目探讨智能体 AI 能否提升工业过程的效率、生产力与可持续性。学生可研究制造、物流、食品加工、化工生产、半导体生产或高耗能行业中的应用。该项目值得研究，因为许多行业面临降低成本、节约能源、提升质量与降低碳排放的压力，智能体 AI 可能成为实现这些目标的强大工具。\n项目可基于文献综述、案例研究、仿真，或智能体 AI 工作流的概念设计。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"工业中的自主 AI 可信吗？智能体 AI 系统的安全与治理","titleEn":"Can We Trust Autonomous AI in Industry? Safety and Governance of Agentic AI Systems","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"As AI systems become more autonomous, important questions arise: Who is responsible if an AI agent makes a poor decision? How can organisations prevent AI-generated mistakes? How much human supervision is necessary? These questions are especially important in industrial environments where incorrect decisions can cause equipment damage, production losses, safety incidents or environmental harm.\n This project focuses on the safety, risk and governance of agentic AI in industrial operations. The student may examine how agentic AI should be controlled, monitored and audited before being deployed in safety-critical settings. The project could use risk assessment tools such as Failure Mode and Effects Analysis, bow-tie analysis, HAZOP-style review or AI governance frameworks.\n This is an interesting project for students who are interested not only in AI technology but also in practical implementation, safety management, regulation, ethics and accountability.","descZh":"随着 AI 系统自主性增强，一系列重要问题随之出现：若 AI 智能体作出糟糕决策，责任在谁？组织如何预防 AI 产生的失误？需要多大程度的人类监督？这些问题在工业环境中尤为重要——错误的决策可能造成设备损坏、生产损失、安全事故或环境损害。\n本项目聚焦工业运行中智能体 AI 的安全、风险与治理。学生可研究在安全关键环境中部署智能体 AI 之前，应如何对其进行控制、监测与审计。项目可采用失效模式与影响分析（FMEA）、领结（bow-tie）分析、HAZOP 式评审或 AI 治理框架等风险评估工具。\n该项目适合不仅对 AI 技术感兴趣，也对实际落地、安全管理、法规、伦理与问责制感兴趣的学生。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"供应链中的 AI 智能体：构建更智能、更具韧性的工业物流","titleEn":"AI Agents in the Supply Chain: Building Smarter and More Resilient Industrial Logistics","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Recent global disruptions, including pandemics, geopolitical tensions, port congestion and material shortages, have shown that industrial supply chains can be fragile. Companies need better tools to monitor risks, predict delays, identify alternative suppliers and optimise logistics decisions. Agentic AI could help by continuously scanning information, analysing supply-chain risks, proposing alternative actions and supporting procurement or scheduling decisions.\n This project investigates how agentic AI can be used to improve industrial supply chain resilience and efficiency. The student may focus on manufacturing supply chains, aviation parts logistics, construction materials, electronics, automotive components or cross-border logistics in the Greater Bay Area.\n The topic is interesting because supply chain management is no longer just about cost reduction; it is now also about resilience, speed, transparency and risk management. Agentic AI could play an important role in the next generation of intelligent supply-chain systems.","descZh":"包括疫情、地缘政治紧张、港口拥堵与材料短缺在内的近期全球性扰动表明，工业供应链可能十分脆弱。企业需要更好的工具来监测风险、预测延误、识别替代供应商并优化物流决策。智能体 AI 可通过持续扫描信息、分析供应链风险、提出替代行动方案并支持采购或调度决策来提供帮助。\n本项目考察如何利用智能体 AI 提升工业供应链的韧性与效率。学生可聚焦制造供应链、航空零部件物流、建材、电子产品、汽车零部件或大湾区的跨境物流。\n该主题颇具价值，因为供应链管理已不再只是降低成本，还关乎韧性、速度、透明度与风险管理。智能体 AI 有望在下一代智能供应链系统中发挥重要作用。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"腾飞：大湾区低空经济的机遇","titleEn":"Taking Off: Opportunities for the Low Altitude Economy in the Greater Bay Area","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"The low altitude economy refers to economic activities that use low-altitude airspace, including drones, eVTOL aircraft, air taxis, aerial logistics, infrastructure inspection, emergency response and low-altitude tourism. Mainland China is actively promoting the development of this sector, and the Guangdong-Hong Kong-Macao Greater Bay Area could become an important testing ground due to its dense cities, strong technology base, logistics demand and advanced manufacturing capabilities.\n This project investigates the opportunities and barriers for developing the low altitude economy in the Greater Bay Area. The student could examine market potential, policy support, airspace management, safety regulation, infrastructure needs and Hong Kong’s possible role.\n This is a highly topical project because the low altitude economy is now seen as an emerging growth sector in China. It is suitable for students interested in aviation, transport planning, smart cities, logistics, innovation policy or regional development.\n Students choosing this topic may have an opportunity to visit Xpeng Aridge on their eVTOL development in Guangzhou.","descZh":"低空经济指利用低空空域的经济活动，包括无人机、eVTOL 飞行器、空中出租车、空中物流、基础设施巡检、应急响应与低空旅游。内地正在积极推进这一领域的发展，而粤港澳大湾区凭借密集城市、雄厚的科技基础、物流需求与先进制造能力，有望成为重要试验场。\n本项目考察在大湾区发展低空经济的机遇与障碍。学生可研究市场潜力、政策支持、空域管理、安全监管、基础设施需求以及香港可能扮演的角色。\n这是一个热点课题，因为低空经济目前被视为中国新兴的增长领域，适合对航空、交通规划、智慧城市、物流、创新政策或区域发展感兴趣的学生。选择该主题的学生有机会参观小鹏汇天（Xpeng Aridge）在广州的 eVTOL 研发。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"空中配送：密集城市中基于无人机的末端物流可行性","titleEn":"Delivery from the Sky: Feasibility of Drone-Based Last-Mile Logistics in Dense Cities","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Drone delivery has been tested in many parts of the world for parcels, food, medicine and emergency supplies. However, dense urban environments such as Hong Kong, Shenzhen and Guangzhou create special challenges, including limited landing space, tall buildings, complex airspace, noise concerns, privacy issues, safety risks and unpredictable weather.\n This project assesses the feasibility of drone-based last-mile delivery in dense urban areas. The student may compare drone delivery with conventional road-based delivery in terms of cost, speed, emissions, reliability and public acceptance. The project could also examine suitable use cases, such as medical delivery, urgent documents, remote island delivery or emergency supplies.\n This is an interesting project because it addresses a real question: can drones genuinely improve urban logistics, or are they only suitable for selected niche applications?\n Students choosing this topic may have an opportunity to visit Xpeng Aridge on their eVTOL development in Guangzhou.","descZh":"无人机配送已在世界多地就包裹、食品、药品与应急物资进行测试。然而，香港、深圳、广州等密集城市环境带来特殊挑战，包括有限的起降空间、高楼、复杂空域、噪声顾虑、隐私问题、安全风险与多变天气。\n本项目评估在密集城市区域开展无人机末端配送的可行性。学生可就成本、速度、排放、可靠性与公众接受度，将无人机配送与传统道路配送进行对比；也可考察合适的应用场景，如医疗配送、紧急文件、离岛配送或应急物资。\n这是一个有趣的项目，因为它回应一个真实问题：无人机能否真正改善城市物流，还是仅适用于特定的细分场景？选择该主题的学生有机会参观小鹏汇天（Xpeng Aridge）在广州的 eVTOL 研发。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"管理天空：低空经济的空域安全与交通管制","titleEn":"Managing the Sky: Airspace Safety and Traffic Control for the Low Altitude Economy","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"If drones, eVTOL aircraft and other low-altitude vehicles become common, cities will need systems to manage low-altitude traffic safely. Unlike traditional aviation, low-altitude operations may involve many small aircraft flying at low levels, often close to buildings, people and infrastructure. This creates challenges for air traffic management, communication, collision avoidance, emergency response and regulation.\n This project investigates airspace management and safety challenges associated with the low altitude economy. The student may examine unmanned traffic management systems, geofencing, detect-and-avoid technologies, digital flight approval systems and integration with existing aviation regulations.\n This topic is particularly interesting because low-altitude airspace is becoming a new type of transport infrastructure. The project is suitable for students interested in aviation safety, smart mobility, regulation, systems engineering or digital infrastructure.\n Students choosing this topic may have an opportunity to visit Xpeng Aridge on their eVTOL development in Guangzhou.","descZh":"如果无人机、eVTOL 飞行器及其他低空飞行器变得普及，城市将需要系统来安全管理低空交通。与传统航空不同，低空运营可能涉及大量小型飞行器在低空飞行，且往往靠近建筑物、人群与基础设施。这给空中交通管理、通信、避撞、应急响应与监管带来了挑战。\n本项目研究低空经济相关的空域管理与安全挑战。学生可考察无人交通管理系统、地理围栏、探测与避让（detect-and-avoid）技术、数字化飞行审批系统，以及与现有航空法规的整合。\n该课题特别有意义，因为低空空域正在成为一种新型交通基础设施。本项目适合对航空安全、智慧出行、监管、系统工程或数字基础设施感兴趣的学生。\n选择本课题的学生可能有机会到广州参观 Xpeng Aridge 的 eVTOL 研发。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-9","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"无人机救援：面向应急响应与灾害管理的低空航空","titleEn":"Drones to the Rescue: Low-Altitude Aviation for Emergency Response and Disaster Management","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Drones are increasingly being used in emergency response, including search and rescue, firefighting support, flood monitoring, landslide assessment, medical delivery and post-disaster damage inspection. They can reach areas that are difficult, dangerous or slow for humans to access. In cities exposed to typhoons, flooding, hill fires or coastal risks, low-altitude aviation could become an important emergency management tool.\n This project examines how drones and other low-altitude aircraft can support disaster management and emergency response. The student may analyse case studies, compare response times, evaluate operational benefits, and identify regulatory or technical limitations.\n This is an interesting and socially meaningful project because it focuses on how aviation technology can save lives, improve resilience and support public safety.\n Students choosing this topic may have an opportunity to visit Xpeng Aridge on their eVTOL development in Guangzhou.","descZh":"无人机正越来越多地用于应急响应，包括搜救、消防支援、洪水监测、滑坡评估、医疗配送与灾后损毁检查。它们可以到达人类难以、危险或缓慢才能到达的区域。在易受台风、洪水、山火或沿海灾害影响的城市，低空航空可能成为重要的应急管理工具。\n本项目考察无人机及其他低空飞行器如何支持灾害管理与应急响应。学生可分析案例、比较响应时间、评估运行效益，并识别监管或技术限制。\n这是一个具有社会意义的有趣项目，聚焦航空技术如何拯救生命、提升韧性并支持公共安全。选择该主题的学生有机会参观小鹏汇天（Xpeng Aridge）在广州的 eVTOL 研发。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-10","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"建设低空城市：垂直起降场、充电网络与数字基础设施","titleEn":"Building the Low-Altitude City: Vertiports, Charging Networks and Digital Infrastructure","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"For the low altitude economy to develop, cities will need more than aircraft. They will require vertiports, drone landing areas, charging or battery-swapping stations, maintenance facilities, weather monitoring systems, communications networks and digital traffic management platforms. Poor infrastructure planning could limit the commercial success and safety of low-altitude aviation.\n This project investigates the infrastructure required to support the low altitude economy. The student may focus on vertiport site selection, charging network planning, integration with public transport, rooftop landing facilities, or logistics hubs. A case study could be conducted for Hong Kong, Shenzhen, Guangzhou or another urban area.\n This project is attractive because it links aviation with urban planning, infrastructure engineering, smart cities and future mobility.\n Students choosing this topic may have an opportunity to visit Xpeng Aridge on their eVTOL development in Guangzhou.","descZh":"低空经济要发展，城市需要的不只是航空器，还需要垂直起降场（vertiport）、无人机起降区、充电或换电站、维护设施、气象监测系统、通信网络与数字化交通管理平台。糟糕的基础设施规划可能限制低空航空的商业成功与安全性。\n本项目考察支撑低空经济所需的基础设施。学生可聚焦垂直起降场选址、充电网络规划、与公共交通的衔接、屋顶起降设施或物流枢纽；可针对香港、深圳、广州或其他城市开展案例研究。\n该项目将航空与城市规划、基础设施工程、智慧城市与未来出行联系起来，颇具吸引力。选择该主题的学生有机会参观小鹏汇天（Xpeng Aridge）在广州的 eVTOL 研发。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-11","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"防患于未然：飞机系统的预测性维护","titleEn":"Fix It Before It Fails: Predictive Maintenance for Aircraft Systems","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Aircraft maintenance is traditionally based on scheduled inspections, regulatory requirements and component life limits. However, modern aircraft generate large volumes of operational and health-monitoring data. Predictive maintenance uses this data to detect early signs of component degradation and predict failures before they happen.\n This project investigates how predictive maintenance can improve aircraft reliability, safety and availability. The student may focus on aircraft engines, landing gear, hydraulic systems, avionics, braking systems, auxiliary power units or environmental control systems. The project could examine machine learning methods, condition-monitoring data, maintenance records or published case studies.\n This is a strong topic because airlines and maintenance organisations are under constant pressure to reduce delays, lower maintenance costs and improve safety.\n Students choosing this topic may have an opportunity to visit Guangzhou Aircraft Maintenance Engineering Company Limited ( GAMECO ).","descZh":"飞机维护传统上基于定期检查、法规要求与部件寿命限制。然而，现代飞机产生大量运行与健康监测数据。预测性维护利用这些数据检测部件退化的早期迹象，并在故障发生前进行预测。\n本项目考察预测性维护如何提升飞机的可靠性、安全性与可用性。学生可聚焦飞机发动机、起落架、液压系统、航电、制动系统、辅助动力装置（APU）或环境控制系统；项目可研究机器学习方法、状态监测数据、维护记录或已发表的案例研究。\n这是一个有力课题，因为航空公司和维修机构始终面临减少延误、降低维护成本与提升安全的压力。选择该主题的学生有机会参观广州飞机维修工程有限公司（GAMECO）。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-12","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"虚拟飞机：面向更智能维护的数字孪生技术","titleEn":"The Virtual Aircraft: Digital Twin Technology for Smarter Maintenance","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"A digital twin is a virtual representation of a physical asset that can be updated with real-time or operational data. In aircraft maintenance, digital twins could be used to monitor component condition, simulate wear and tear, predict failures, support troubleshooting and optimise maintenance planning.\n This project explores how digital twin technology can support aircraft maintenance and reliability improvement. The student may focus on a specific component, such as an engine, landing gear, aircraft structure, battery system or avionics unit. The project could develop a conceptual digital twin framework and assess implementation challenges.\n This topic is interesting because digital twins are becoming important in aviation, manufacturing and asset management. It is suitable for students interested in aircraft engineering, data analytics, maintenance management or Industry 4.0.\n Students choosing this topic may have an opportunity to visit Guangzhou Aircraft Maintenance Engineering Company Limited ( GAMECO ).","descZh":"数字孪生是物理资产的虚拟表示，可利用实时或运行数据进行更新。在飞机维护中，数字孪生可用于监测部件状态、模拟磨损、预测故障、支持排障并优化维护计划。\n本项目探索数字孪生技术如何支持飞机维护与可靠性提升。学生可聚焦某一具体部件，如发动机、起落架、飞机结构、电池系统或航电单元；项目可开发概念性数字孪生框架，并评估实施挑战。\n该主题颇具价值，因为数字孪生在航空、制造与资产管理中正变得越来越重要，适合对飞机工程、数据分析、维护管理或工业 4.0 感兴趣的学生。选择该主题的学生有机会参观广州飞机维修工程有限公司（GAMECO）。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-13","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"维修差错为何发生：飞机维修安全中的人为因素","titleEn":"Why Maintenance Errors Happen: Human Factors in Aircraft Maintenance Safety","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Aircraft maintenance is safety-critical, but maintenance errors still occur. These errors may be caused by fatigue, time pressure, poor communication, inadequate documentation, shift handover problems, training gaps, tool control issues or organisational culture. Understanding human factors is essential because aviation accidents and incidents are rarely caused by technical faults alone.\n This project investigates human factors contributing to aircraft maintenance errors. The student could analyse accident and incident reports, apply human factors models such as SHELL or HFACS, and propose strategies to reduce risk. If access is available, the student may also conduct interviews or surveys with maintenance personnel.\n This is a valuable project because it focuses on aviation safety, people and organisational systems. It is especially suitable for students interested in maintenance management, safety culture and human performance.\n Students choosing this topic may have an opportunity to visit Guangzhou Aircraft Maintenance Engineering Company Limited ( GAMECO ).","descZh":"飞机维护属于安全关键领域，但维护差错仍会发生。这些差错可能源于疲劳、时间压力、沟通不畅、文档不完善、交接班问题、培训不足、工具管控问题或组织文化。理解人为因素至关重要，因为航空事故与事件很少仅由技术故障引起。\n本项目调研导致飞机维护差错的人为因素。学生可分析事故与事件报告，应用 SHELL 或 HFACS 等人为因素模型，并提出降低风险的策略；如条件允许，还可对维护人员进行访谈或问卷调查。\n这是一个有价值的项目，因为它聚焦航空安全、人员与组织系统，尤其适合对维护管理、安全文化与人的表现感兴趣的学生。选择该主题的学生有机会参观广州飞机维修工程有限公司（GAMECO）。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-14","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"恰到好处的零件：飞机维修中的备件管理","titleEn":"The Right Part at the Right Time: Spare Parts Management in Aircraft Maintenance","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Aircraft spare parts are expensive, highly regulated and often have long lead times. Keeping too much inventory increases cost, but insufficient stock can cause aircraft-on-ground situations and major operational disruption. Effective spare parts management is therefore essential for airlines and MRO organisations.\n This project examines how aircraft spare parts inventory can be optimised. The student may investigate demand forecasting, criticality classification, inventory cost, supplier lead time, pooling strategies and digital inventory systems. Methods such as ABC analysis, VED analysis or inventory modelling could be applied.\n This is an interesting project because spare parts management is a hidden but crucial part of aviation reliability and airline profitability.\n Students choosing this topic may have an opportunity to visit Guangzhou Aircraft Maintenance Engineering Company Limited ( GAMECO ).","descZh":"飞机备件价格昂贵、监管严格，且通常交货周期长。库存过多会增加成本，但库存不足又可能导致飞机停场（AOG）与重大运营中断。因此，有效的备件管理对航空公司和维修机构（MRO）至关重要。\n本项目考察如何优化飞机备件的库存。学生可研究需求预测、关键性分级、库存成本、供应商交货周期、共享策略与数字化库存系统；可采用 ABC 分析、VED 分析或库存建模等方法。\n这是一个有价值的项目，因为备件管理是航空可靠性与航空公司盈利能力中隐蔽但关键的一环。选择该主题的学生有机会参观广州飞机维修工程有限公司（GAMECO）。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-15","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"让无人机机队持续飞行：面向低空机队维护的智能体 AI","titleEn":"Keeping Drone Fleets Flying: Agentic AI for Low-Altitude Fleet Maintenance","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"As drone fleets expand for logistics, inspection, surveillance, agriculture and emergency response, operators will need efficient ways to manage maintenance. Unlike traditional aircraft, drones may operate in large numbers, with frequent short missions, battery degradation, motor wear, sensor faults and software updates. Manual maintenance planning may become inefficient as fleets scale up.\n This project investigates how agentic AI could support maintenance management for drone fleets. AI agents could monitor fleet health, schedule inspections, identify high-risk drones, recommend spare parts and optimise availability. The student may develop a conceptual framework for AI-supported drone maintenance.\n This is an exciting project because it combines three fast-growing areas: autonomous AI, drone operations and aviation maintenance.\n Students choosing this topic may have an opportunity to visit Xpeng Aridge on their eVTOL development in Guangzhou.","descZh":"随着无人机机队不断扩展，用于物流、巡检、监视、农业与应急响应，运营者需要高效的维护管理方式。与传统飞机不同，无人机可能数量庞大、任务短促频繁，并伴随电池衰减、电机磨损、传感器故障与软件更新。随着机队规模扩大，人工维护规划可能变得低效。\n本项目考察智能体 AI 如何支持无人机机队的维护管理。AI 智能体可监测机队健康、安排检查、识别高风险无人机、推荐备件并优化可用性。学生可开发一套 AI 支持的无人机维护概念框架。\n这是一个令人兴奋的课题，因为它结合了自主 AI、无人机运行与航空维护三大快速增长领域。选择该主题的学生有机会参观小鹏汇天（Xpeng Aridge）在广州的 eVTOL 研发。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-16","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"空中的自主 AI：AI 控制低空作业的安全治理","titleEn":"Autonomous AI in the Sky: Safety Governance for AI-Controlled Low-Altitude Operations","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Low-altitude aviation may eventually involve AI-supported flight planning, route optimisation, traffic management, emergency decision-making and fleet maintenance. If agentic AI systems are allowed to make or recommend operational decisions, safety governance becomes critical. A failure in AI decision-making could affect aircraft, people on the ground and urban infrastructure.\n This project examines safety and regulatory challenges associated with agentic AI in low-altitude aviation. The student may investigate how AI agents should be supervised, certified, audited and constrained. The project could compare aviation safety principles with emerging AI governance frameworks.\n This is an important topic because low-altitude aviation will require high public trust. Safe AI governance may become a key condition for commercial success.\n Students choosing this topic may have an opportunity to visit Xpeng Aridge on their eVTOL development in Guangzhou.","descZh":"低空航空未来可能涉及 AI 支持的飞行规划、航线优化、交通管理、应急决策与机队维护。如果允许智能体 AI 系统作出或建议运行决策，安全保障治理将变得至关重要。AI 决策失误可能影响航空器、地面人员与城市基础设施。\n本项目考察智能体 AI 在低空航空中的安全与监管挑战。学生可研究 AI 智能体应如何被监督、认证、审计与约束；项目可将航空安全原则与新兴 AI 治理框架进行对比。\n这是一个重要课题，因为低空航空需要高度公众信任，安全的 AI 治理可能成为商业成功的关键条件。选择该主题的学生有机会参观小鹏汇天（Xpeng Aridge）在广州的 eVTOL 研发。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FKLUNG-17","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"作为低空经济试验场的大湾区","titleEn":"The Greater Bay Area as a Testbed for the Low Altitude Economy","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LUNG Francis","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"The Greater Bay Area has strong potential for low-altitude aviation due to its dense urban centres, advanced manufacturing base, logistics demand and policy support for innovation. This project examines whether the region could become a leading testbed for drone delivery, aerial inspection, emergency response and eVTOL mobility.\n The student could analyse policy frameworks, infrastructure needs, airspace constraints, market opportunities and Hong Kong’s possible role. This topic is attractive because it is highly relevant to current regional development and could produce practical recommendations for policymakers and industry.\n Students choosing this topic may have an opportunity to visit Xpeng Aridge on their eVTOL development in Guangzhou.","descZh":"粤港澳大湾区凭借密集的城市中心、先进的制造基础、物流需求与创新政策支持，在发展低空航空方面潜力巨大。本项目考察该区域能否成为无人机配送、空中巡检、应急响应与 eVTOL 出行的领先试验场。学生可分析政策框架、基础设施需求、空域约束、市场机会以及香港可能扮演的角色。该主题之所以吸引人，是因为它与当前区域发展高度相关，并可为政策制定者与产业界提出务实建议。选择该主题的学生有机会参观小鹏汇天（Xpeng Aridge）在广州的 eVTOL 研发。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"在物理仿真中学习机械臂的物体抓取与操作","titleEn":"Learning Object Grasping and Manipulation for a Robotic Arm in Physics\rSimulation","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; Drones and Control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nThis project aims to teach a simulated robotic arm how to pick up and move a ball using\nreinforcement learning. The student will build the whole training pipeline inside a physics simulator,\ndesign the reward system that tells the robot what good behaviour looks like, and then run\nexperiments to see how well the learned policy generalises to new situations.\nThe robot used is a Franka Emika Panda, a widely-used 7-joint robotic arm with a parallel-jaw\ngripper. It is a standard platform in manipulation research and comes with ready-to-use models in the\nsimulator. The simulator is MuJoCo (Multi-Joint dynamics with Contact), which is free, open-source,\nand known for fast and accurate physics simulation, especially for contact-rich tasks like grasping.\nThe student will set up a tabletop scene in MuJoCo with the Panda arm, a ball, and a target zone. The\ntask is: given the ball at some position on the table, the robot must reach out, grasp it, lift it, and place\nit at the target. The student will train the robot using Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), a popular\ndeep reinforcement learning algorithm that is easy to implement using the Stable-Baselines3 Python\nlibrary.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Set up the MuJoCo environment with the Panda robot, a ball, and a table.\n2. Define the observation space (joint angles, gripper state, ball position, target position) and the\naction space (joint or end-effector velocity commands, gripper open/close).\n3. Design a reward function that gives positive feedback for: approaching the ball, touching it, lifting\nit off the table, and bringing it close to the target.\n4. Train a policy using PPO and tune the hyperparameters.\n5. Evaluate the trained policy: measure success rate, completion time, and how well it handles unseen\nball positions or slightly different ball sizes.\n6. Optionally apply domain randomisation (randomly varying friction, mass, and other physics\nparameters during training) to make the policy more robust.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nProgramming skills in Python.\nBasic understanding of robotics concepts (joints, links, forward kinematics).\nFamiliar with machine learning (neural networks, training loops, gradient descent).","descZh":"项目描述：本项目旨在教会一台仿真机械臂使用强化学习抓取并移动一个球。学生将在物理仿真器中构建完整训练流程，设计告诉机器人什么是良好行为的奖励系统，然后运行实验，考察所学策略对新情况的泛化效果。\n所用机器人为 Franka Emika Panda——一台广泛使用的七关节机械臂，带平行夹爪，是操作研究中的标准平台，仿真器中带有现成模型。仿真器为 MuJoCo（带接触的多关节动力学），免费开源，以快速、精确的物理仿真著称，尤其适合抓取等接触丰富任务。\n学生将在 MuJoCo 中搭建包含 Panda 机械臂、一个球与目标区域的桌面场景。任务为：给定桌上某位置的球，机器人须伸手、抓取、提起并将其放到目标位置。学生将使用近端策略优化（PPO）训练机器人——这是一种流行的深度强化学习算法，可借助 Python 库 Stable-Baselines3 轻松实现。\n具体要求：\n1. 搭建含 Panda 机器人、球与桌面的 MuJoCo 环境；\n2. 定义观测空间（关节角、夹爪状态、球位置、目标位置）与动作空间（关节或末端速度指令、夹爪开合）；\n3. 设计奖励函数，对接近球、触碰球、将球提起离桌以及将其带近目标给予正反馈；\n4. 使用 PPO 训练策略并调优超参数；\n5. 评估训练好的策略：测量成功率、完成时间，以及其对未见球位置或略不同球尺寸的处理能力；\n6.（可选）应用域随机化（训练中随机改变摩擦、质量等物理参数）以增强策略鲁棒性。\n先修/并修要求：具备 Python 编程能力；对机器人学基本概念（关节、连杆、正运动学）有基本理解；熟悉机器学习（神经网络、训练循环、梯度下降）。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-13","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"空地协同编队控制","titleEn":"Air&#8211;ground Collaborative Formation Control","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description: \nAir–ground collaborative formation refers to the coordinated operation of unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) and unmanned ground vehicles (UGVs) within a unified formation framework. This heterogeneous architecture exploits the complementary strengths of the two platforms: UAVs offer broad aerial views, rapid deployment, and agile maneuverability, while UGVs provide sustained ground-level interaction, precise manipulation, and extended operational endurance. The synergy between aerial and ground domains enables more comprehensive mission execution across a variety of scenarios, including search and rescue, precision agriculture, infrastructure inspection, and disaster response. Nevertheless, achieving effective air–ground formation control presents substantial challenges. These include the inherent kinematic and dynamic discrepancies between heterogeneous agents, mismatched communication protocols, and the stringent requirement for real-time coordination in cluttered and dynamic environments. Overcoming these obstacles demands the design of robust control laws, adaptive task allocation strategies, and seamless communication architectures that jointly guarantee both safety and efficiency in cooperative operations. Recent progress in artificial intelligence and multi-agent systems has demonstrated considerable potential in advancing intelligent air–ground collaboration, charting a promising path toward more autonomous, resilient, and heterogeneous robotic systems.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Formulate the dynamic models for heterogeneous UAV and UGV platforms, and establish the communication topology and environmental representation. \n2. Define the mission scenario, specify the formation pattern, and derive the relative position constraints among heterogeneous agents. \n3. Design the distributed formation control law with collision avoidance.\n4. Validate the proposed algorithm through numerical simulations, and analyze the robustness.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic knowledge of algorithm and graph theory.\n2. Programming knowledge of Matlab.\nSpecial note:\nThis project is suitable for students with a strong interest in multi-agent systems and formation control. A solid foundation in control theory and MATLAB programming is expected. The primary research work will involve heterogeneous system modeling, formation control law design, and simulation-based validation. As an optional extension, the project can be expanded to include hardware experiments, communication delay compensation, or reinforcement learning for adaptive formation control.","descZh":"项目描述：空地协同编队指无人机（UAV）与无人地面车辆（UGV）在统一编队框架内的协同运行。这种异构架构发挥两类平台的互补优势：无人机提供广阔空中视野、快速部署与敏捷机动；UGV 提供持续的近地交互、精密操作与更长运行续航。空域与地面域的协同使搜救、精准农业、基础设施巡检与灾害响应等多种场景中的任务执行更加全面。然而，实现有效的空地编队控制面临重大挑战，包括异构智能体间固有的运动学与动力学差异、通信协议不匹配，以及杂乱动态环境中实时协调的严格要求。克服这些障碍需要设计稳健控制律、自适应任务分配策略与无缝通信架构，共同保证协同作业的安全与效率。近期人工智能与多智能体系统的进展已展现出推动智能空地协作的巨大潜力，为更自主、更有韧性、更多样的异构机器人系统开辟了有前景的路径。\n具体要求：\n1. 构建异构 UAV 与 UGV 平台的动力学模型，并建立通信拓扑与环境表示；\n2. 定义任务场景、指定编队构型，并推导异构智能体间的相对位置约束；\n3. 设计带避碰的分布式编队控制律；\n4. 通过数值仿真验证所提算法并分析鲁棒性。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备算法与图论基础知识；\n2. 掌握 MATLAB 编程。\n特别说明：本项目适合对多智能体系统与编队控制有浓厚兴趣的学生；期望具备扎实的控制理论与 MATLAB 编程基础。主要研究工作涉及异构系统建模、编队控制律设计与基于仿真的验证。作为可选扩展，项目可加入硬件实验、通信时延补偿或用于自适应编队控制的强化学习。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-14","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"预定时间编队控制","titleEn":"Predefined-time Formation Control","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description: \nPredefined-time formation control is an emerging paradigm that requires a multi-robot system to achieve and maintain a desired formation pattern within an arbitrarily user-specified time, with the convergence time upper bound independent of initial conditions. This provides deterministic timing guarantees, which facilitate precise mission scheduling. Unlike finite-time control, the upper bound of the settling time is adjustable via control parameters and does not depend on initial states, offering superior design flexibility and predictability. This capability is critical in time-sensitive scenarios such as search and rescue, coordinated strikes, dynamic target encirclement, and automated warehouse coordination, where formations must be established reliably within strict deadlines.\nHowever, practical implementation faces several challenges, such as actuator saturation caused by unbounded nonlinear gains, sensitivity to disturbances and model uncertainties, and vulnerability to communication delays and packet losses. Ongoing research focuses on adaptive control, disturbance observers, and event-triggered strategies to address these limitations.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Formulate the dynamic models of individual agents, capturing their kinematic and kinetic characteristics using a unified state-space representation.\n2. Define the formation geometry and error dynamics by establishing desired relative positions and orientations.\n3. Design the distributed formation control law based on predefined-time stability theory.\n4. Validate the control framework through simulations or experiments, assessing whether the formation achieves the desired geometry precisely at the prescribed time, and evaluating convergence accuracy, transient performance, and robustness under various scenarios.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic knowledge of algorithm and graph theory.\n2. Programming knowledge of Matlab.\nSpecial note:\nThis project is suitable for students with a strong interest in multi-agent systems and formation control. A solid foundation in control theory and MATLAB programming is expected. The primary research work will involve system modeling, formation control law design, and simulation-based validation. As an optional extension, the project can be expanded to include hardware experiments.","descZh":"项目描述：预定时间编队控制是一种新兴范式，要求多机器人系统在用户任意指定的时间内达成并保持期望编队构型，且收敛时间上界与初始条件无关。这提供了确定性时间保证，便于精确任务调度。与有限时间控制不同，其收敛时间上界可通过控制参数调节，且不依赖于初始状态，具有更优的设计灵活性与可预测性。该能力在搜救、协同打击、动态目标包围与自动化仓库协调等时间敏感场景中至关重要——这些场景必须在严格期限内可靠建立编队。\n然而，实际实现面临若干挑战，如无界非线性增益引起的执行器饱和、对扰动与模型不确定性的敏感性，以及对通信时延与丢包的脆弱性。当前研究聚焦自适应控制、扰动观测器与事件触发策略以应对这些局限。\n具体要求：\n1. 构建各智能体的动力学模型，用统一状态空间表示刻画其运动学与动力学特征；\n2. 通过设定期望相对位置与姿态定义编队几何与误差动力学；\n3. 基于预定时间稳定性理论设计分布式编队控制律；\n4. 通过仿真或实验验证控制框架，评估编队是否在规定时刻精确达成期望几何，并评估收敛精度、暂态性能与不同场景下的鲁棒性。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备算法与图论基础知识；\n2. 掌握 MATLAB 编程。\n特别说明：本项目适合对多智能体系统与编队控制有浓厚兴趣的学生；期望具备扎实的控制理论与 MATLAB 编程基础。主要研究工作涉及系统建模、编队控制律设计与基于仿真的验证。作为可选扩展，项目可加入硬件实验。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"遭受网络攻击的无人自主系统的安全状态估计","titleEn":"Secure State Estimation for Unmanned Autonomous Systems Subject to Cyber-attacks","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nIn practical applications, unmanned surface vehicles (USVs) usually work with a communication network to achieve data transmission. The measurement signals from sensors mounted on USVs are transmitted through communication network channels to the remote control station to complete specific tasks. However, the communication network in USV systems is vulnerable to interference and cyber-attacks from malicious adversaries. Secure state estimation is a concept within control theory and its applications that concentrates on accurately estimating the true states of physical systems subject to malicious cyber-attacks. This project aims to develop a secure remote state estimation strategy for a USV system subject to cyber-attacks, employing zonotopic set theory.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Modelling the dynamics of the unmanned surface vehicle system.\n2. Design a secure state estimation strategy for the considered USV system subject to cyber-attacks.\n3. Evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed strategy through numerical simulation using MATLAB.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic knowledge of set theory, state-space control theory, and optimization.\n2. Programming knowledge of MATLAB.\nSpecial note:\nStudents who like numerical simulation and theoretical study should find the project interesting.","descZh":"项目描述：在实际应用中，无人水面艇（USV）通常需要借助通信网络实现数据传输。搭载于 USV 上的传感器测量信号通过通信网络信道传输至遥控站以完成特定任务。然而，USV 系统中的通信网络易受干扰及恶意对手的网络攻击。安全状态估计是控制理论及其应用中的一个概念，关注在恶意网络攻击下准确估计物理系统真实状态。本项目旨在采用 zonotope 集合理论，为遭受网络攻击的 USV 系统开发一种安全远程状态估计策略。\n具体要求：\n1. 对无人水面艇系统动力学进行建模；\n2. 为所研究的受攻击 USV 系统设计安全状态估计策略；\n3. 使用 MATLAB 进行数值仿真，评估所提策略的有效性。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备集合论、状态空间控制理论与优化的基础知识；\n2. 掌握 MATLAB 编程。\n特别说明：喜欢数值仿真与理论研究的学生会发现该项目颇有吸引力。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于 Zonotope 方法的约束控制性能下主动攻击隔离","titleEn":"Active Attack Isolation with Constrained Control Performance based on Zonotopic Approach","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nIn modern cyber‑physical systems, malicious adversaries may compromise sensors, actuators, or communication channels, threatening system reliability and safety. Active attack isolation employs carefully designed system inputs to expose and distinguish attack channels, enabling timely identification of compromised components. This project aims to develop a zonotopic set‑based framework for active attack isolation with constrained control performance, ensuring that the system maintains acceptable tracking behavior during the isolation process. The approach will integrate set‑theoretic uncertainty representation with input design strategies to achieve robustness against disturbances and attacks. Numerical simulations in MATLAB will be conducted to demonstrate the effectiveness of the method and to analyze trade‑offs between isolation speed and control quality.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Model the dynamics of a representative cyber‑physical system subject to adversarial attacks.\n2. Design system inputs to achieve active attack isolation while ensuring constrained tracking control performance.\n3. Evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed approach through numerical simulation using MATLAB.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic knowledge of set theory, state‑space control theory, and optimization.\n2. Programming knowledge of MATLAB.\nSpecial note:\nStudents who enjoy combining theoretical analysis with numerical simulation should find the project engaging.","descZh":"项目描述：在现代信息物理系统中，恶意对手可能攻陷传感器、执行器或通信信道，威胁系统的可靠性与安全性。主动攻击隔离利用精心设计的系统输入来暴露并区分攻击通道，从而及时识别受攻陷的部件。本项目旨在开发一种基于 zonotope 集合理论的主动攻击隔离框架，并约束控制性能，确保系统在隔离过程中保持可接受的跟踪行为。该方法将集合论不确定性表示与输入设计策略相结合，以增强对扰动与攻击的鲁棒性。将使用 MATLAB 数值仿真验证该方法的有效性，并分析隔离速度与控制质量之间的权衡。\n具体要求：\n1. 对受对抗攻击的代表性信息物理系统进行动力学建模；\n2. 设计系统输入以实现主动攻击隔离，同时保证带约束的跟踪控制性能；\n3. 使用 MATLAB 数值仿真评估所提方法的有效性。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备集合论、状态空间控制理论与优化的基础知识；\n2. 掌握 MATLAB 编程。\n特别说明：喜欢将理论分析与数值仿真相结合的学生会认为该项目颇具吸引力。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"自动驾驶汽车的可达性分析与安全评估","titleEn":"Reachablity Analysis and Safety Assessment of Autonomous Cars","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description: \nThe safety of vehicles is becoming increasingly critical with the development of autonomous driving technology. Due to unpredictable uncertainties in the real world such as modeling errors, bumps on the road surface and measurement noise, the safety of vehicles cannot be strictly guaranteed through trajectory simulations since only a single solution out of infinitely many possibilities can be checked. Reachability analysis can establish reliable boundaries for system behaviors, which provides a powerful tool to assess the safety of vehicles. In this project, students will conduct reachability analysis based on state space models and set-based techniques, thereby assessing the safety of autonomous vehicles.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Modeling the dynamics or kinematics of vehicles (e.g., differential drive kinematics, single-track kinematic, 2-DOF bicycle dynamics, and 3-DOF vehicle dynamics). Establishing a state-space model through linearization and discretization.\n2. Computing reachable sets of vehicles based on certain set representations, such as ellipsoids, zonotopes and polytopes.\n3. Evaluating the safety of vehicles through numerical simulations.\n4. Experimental studies may be conducted (optional, if students are interested).\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic knowledge of control theory and linear algebra. \n2. Programming experience with MATLAB or Python (simulation), and ROS (if interested in experiments).\nSpecial note:\nSuitable for students who prefer numerical simulations and theoretical studies. Students who like control theory and it's applications might enjoy this project.","descZh":"项目描述：随着自动驾驶技术的发展，车辆安全日益关键。由于现实世界中存在建模误差、路面颠簸与测量噪声等不可预测的不确定性，车辆安全性无法通过轨迹仿真严格保证——因为无穷多种可能性中只能检验单一解。可达性分析可为系统行为建立可靠边界，为评估车辆安全提供有力工具。在本项目中，学生将基于状态空间模型与集合技术开展可达性分析，从而评估自动驾驶车辆的安全性。\n具体要求：\n1. 对车辆动力学或运动学进行建模（如差速驱动运动学、单轨运动学、二自由度自行车动力学与三自由度车辆动力学），通过线性化与离散化建立状态空间模型；\n2. 基于椭球、zonotope、多面体等集合表示计算车辆可达集；\n3. 通过数值仿真评估车辆安全性；\n4.（可选）若学生有兴趣，可开展实验研究。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备控制理论与线性代数基础知识；\n2. 有 MATLAB 或 Python（仿真）编程经验；若对实验有兴趣，需了解 ROS。\n特别说明：适合偏好数值仿真与理论研究的学生；喜欢控制理论及其应用的学生会乐在其中。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"自动驾驶汽车的基于集合的状态估计与故障检测","titleEn":"Set-Based State Estimation and Fault Detection of Autonomous Cars","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description: State estimation and fault detection constitute an important technique for ensuring the safety of autonomous vehicles. Stochastic approaches, such as the Kalman filter, which provide point-by-point state estimation and probabilistic detection results, have been extensively studied over the past few decades. Recently, set-based approaches that model uncertainties as unknown but bounded sets have attracted increasing attention in state estimation and fault detection, owing to their robustness and ability to handle worst-case uncertainties. In this project, students will design set-based observers to compute compact bounds for internal states of vehicles, and develop algorithms to determine whether vehicle systems have malfunctioned.\nThe specifications are given as follows:\n1. Modeling the dynamics or kinematics of vehicles (e.g., differential drive kinematics, single-track kinematic, 2-DOF bicycle dynamics, and 3-DOF vehicle dynamics) under both healthy and fault modes. Establishing a state-space model through linearization and discretization.\n2. Designing a set-valued observer based on certain set representations, such as ellipsoids, zonotopes and polytopes.\n3. Conducting fault detection for the vehicles through numerical simulations.\n4. Experimental studies may be conducted (optional, if students are interested).\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic knowledge of control theory, optimization and linear algebra. \n2. Programming experience with of MATLAB or Python (simulation), and ROS (if interested in experiments).\nSpecial note:\nSuitable for students who prefer numerical simulations and theoretical studies. Students who like control theory and it’s applications might enjoy this project.","descZh":"项目描述：状态估计与故障检测是保障自动驾驶汽车安全的重要技术。卡尔曼滤波等随机方法可提供逐点状态估计与概率检测结果，在过去几十年被广泛研究。近年来，将不确定性建模为未知但有界集合的集合方法，因其鲁棒性与处理最坏情况不确定性的能力，在状态估计与故障检测中受到越来越多关注。在本项目中，学生将设计集合观测器，计算车辆内部状态的紧凑界，并开发判定车辆系统是否发生故障的算法。\n具体要求：\n1. 在健康与故障模式下对车辆动力学或运动学进行建模（如差速驱动运动学、单轨运动学、二自由度自行车动力学与三自由度车辆动力学），通过线性化与离散化建立状态空间模型；\n2. 基于椭球、zonotope、多面体等集合表示设计集值观测器；\n3. 通过数值仿真开展车辆故障检测；\n4.（可选）若学生有兴趣，可开展实验研究。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备控制理论、优化与线性代数基础知识；\n2. 有 MATLAB 或 Python（仿真）编程经验；若对实验有兴趣，需了解 ROS。\n特别说明：适合偏好数值仿真与理论研究的学生；喜欢控制理论及其应用的学生会乐在其中。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"连接 LiDAR 与视觉：基于语义边缘的外参标定","titleEn":"Bridging LiDAR and Vision: Semantic Edge-Based Extrinsic Calibration","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nThis project aims to estimate the six-degree-of-freedom extrinsic transformation between a LiDAR and a camera by matching edge features extracted from the two sensing modalities.\nThe student will use a public LiDAR–camera dataset or optionally collect data using a self-built sensor platform. Pre-trained segmentation models, such as SAM, Semantic-SAM, or SegFormer, will be applied to camera images and LiDAR range or intensity images to obtain object or semantic masks. Edge features will then be extracted from these masks to establish correspondences between the two modalities. Training a segmentation model from scratch is not required.\nGiven an approximate initial calibration, the student will project the LiDAR edge points onto the camera image and optimise the extrinsic parameters by minimising the alignment error between the projected LiDAR edges and the corresponding image edges. The proposed method will be evaluated under different initial calibration errors and scene conditions.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Set up a public LiDAR–camera dataset, or optionally collect data using a self-built sensor platform.\n2. Generate LiDAR range or intensity images and apply pre-trained segmentation models to both sensing modalities.\n3. Extract semantic or object boundaries and establish edge correspondences between LiDAR and camera data.\n4. Design an optimisation method to estimate the six-degree-of-freedom extrinsic parameters.\n5. Evaluate the method using rotation error, translation error, edge-alignment error, convergence rate, and computation time.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nProgramming skills in Python or C++.\nBasic understanding of linear algebra, coordinate transformations, and camera projection models.\nBasic knowledge of computer vision or robotics.","descZh":"项目说明：\n本项目旨在通过匹配从两种传感模态中提取的边缘特征，估计 LiDAR 与相机之间的六自由度外参变换。\n学生将使用公开的 LiDAR–相机数据集，或可选择使用自建传感器平台采集数据。将把预训练分割模型（如 SAM、Semantic-SAM 或 SegFormer）应用于相机图像以及 LiDAR 距离图或强度图，以获得物体或语义掩膜。随后从这些掩膜中提取边缘特征，以建立两种模态之间的对应关系。无需从零开始训练分割模型。\n给定一个近似的初始标定，学生将把 LiDAR 边缘点投影到相机图像上，并通过最小化投影 LiDAR 边缘与对应图像边缘之间的配准误差来优化外参数。所提出的方法将在不同的初始标定误差与场景条件下进行评估。\n规格说明如下：\n1. 搭建一个公开的 LiDAR–相机数据集，或可选择使用自建传感器平台采集数据。\n2. 生成 LiDAR 距离图或强度图，并将预训练分割模型应用于两种传感模态。\n3. 提取语义或物体边界，并在 LiDAR 与相机数据之间建立边缘对应关系。\n4. 设计一种优化方法以估计六自由度外参数。\n5. 使用旋转误差、平移误差、边缘配准误差、收敛速度与计算时间来评估该方法。\n先修与同修要求：\n具备 Python 或 C++ 编程技能。\n对线性代数、坐标变换与相机投影模型有基本了解。\n具备计算机视觉或机器人学的基础知识。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-11","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"四旋翼无人机的安全关键轨迹跟踪控制","titleEn":"Safety-Critical Trajectory Tracking Control for Quadrotor UAVs","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nQuadrotor unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) have been widely employed in applications such as urban air mobility, autonomous inspection, search and rescue, and environmental monitoring, owing to advantages including simple structure, high agility, and ease of maintenance. However, in dynamic and cluttered environments, quadrotors face significant challenges in simultaneously achieving precise trajectory tracking while maintaining robust safety guarantees due to complex physical constraints, unpredictable external disturbances, and complex interactions with obstacles. Traditional control approaches often lack formal safety guarantees under severe model uncertainties or dynamic environment changes. This project aims to design a safety-critical trajectory tracking control framework for quadrotor UAVs, ensuring robust tracking performance and guaranteed safety constraint satisfaction in complex dynamic environments.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1.⁠ ⁠Modelling the dynamics and kinematics of the UAVs and designing different control schemes.\n2.⁠ ⁠Analysing the tracking performance of the UAVs and guaranteeing unsafe region avoidance.\n3.⁠ ⁠The effectiveness of the different control schemes is evaluated by simulations.\n4.⁠ ⁠The project involves primarily numerical simulation. ROS simulation studies may be conducted (if the student is interested)\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1.⁠ ⁠Basic knowledge of feedback control and optimization\n2.⁠ ⁠Programming knowledge of MATLAB and Python","descZh":"项目描述：四旋翼无人机（UAV）凭借结构简单、机动性高与易于维护等优势，已广泛用于城市空中交通、自主巡检、搜救与环境监测等应用。然而，在动态且杂乱的环境中，由于复杂的物理约束、不可预测的外部扰动以及与障碍物的复杂交互，四旋翼在同时实现精确轨迹跟踪与保持稳健安全保障方面面临重大挑战。传统控制方法在严重模型不确定性或动态环境变化下往往缺乏形式化安全保障。本项目旨在为四旋翼无人机设计一种安全关键的轨迹跟踪控制框架，确保其在复杂动态环境中的稳健跟踪性能与有保证的安全约束满足。\n具体要求：\n1. 对无人机动力学与运动学建模并设计不同控制方案；\n2. 分析无人机跟踪性能并保证避开不安全区域；\n3. 通过仿真评估不同控制方案的有效性；\n4. 项目以数值仿真为主（若学生有兴趣，也可开展 ROS 仿真研究）。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备反馈控制与优化的基础知识；\n2. 掌握 MATLAB 与 Python 编程。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-12","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"机器人机械臂的安全关键控制","titleEn":"Safety-Critical Control for Robotics Manipulators","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nRobotic manipulators are increasingly deployed in human-centric, collaborative, and unstructured environments for applications such as industrial assembly, medical surgery, and service robotics. In these scenarios, ensuring physical safety, such as joint position and\nvelocity limits, actuator torque saturation bounds, workspace boundaries, and real-time human/obstacle avoidance, is paramount, as constraint violations can cause catastrophic hardware damage or severe physical injury. Traditional control methods often lack formal safety guarantees, especially under model uncertainties and disturbances or in environments with obstacles. This project\naims to design a safety-critical control framework for robotic\nmanipulators, ensuring robust task performance and guaranteed safety constraint satisfaction in complex dynamic environments.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1.⁠ ⁠Modelling the dynamics and kinematics of the manipulators, and designing different control schemes.\n2.⁠ ⁠Analysing the task performance of the manipulators and guaranteeing unsafe region avoidance.\n3.⁠ ⁠The effectiveness of the different control schemes is evaluated by simulations.\n4.⁠ ⁠The project involves primarily numerical simulation. ROS simulation studies may be conducted (if the student is interested)\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1.⁠ ⁠Basic knowledge of feedback control and optimization\n2.⁠ ⁠Programming knowledge of MATLAB and Python","descZh":"项目描述：机械臂正越来越多地部署在以人为中心、协作性与非结构化的环境中，用于工业装配、医疗手术与服务机器人等应用。在这些场景中，确保物理安全（如关节位置与速度限制、执行器扭矩饱和边界、工作空间边界以及实时的人/障碍物避让）至关重要，因为违反约束可能造成灾难性的硬件损坏或严重的人身伤害。传统控制方法往往缺乏形式化的安全保障，尤其是在存在模型不确定性、扰动或环境中存在障碍物的情况下。本项目旨在为机械臂设计一种安全关键控制框架，在复杂动态环境中确保稳健的任务性能与有保证的安全约束满足。\n具体要求：\n1. 对机械臂的动力学与运动学进行建模，并设计不同的控制方案；\n2. 分析机械臂的任务性能并保证避开不安全区域；\n3. 通过仿真评估不同控制方案的有效性；\n4. 项目以数值仿真为主（若学生有兴趣，也可开展 ROS 仿真研究）。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备反馈控制与优化的基础知识；\n2. 掌握 MATLAB 与 Python 编程。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"多智能体系统的基于一致性的分布式状态估计","titleEn":"Consensus-based Distributed State Estimation for Multi-agent Systems","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description: \nTo address the ever-increasing demands for accuracy, reliability, robustness, cost-effectiveness, and scalability in target detection and tracking tasks, and in line with the current trend of multi-agent collaboration, multi-agent system cooperative state estimation technology has emerged. Multi-source information fusion is the core of collaborative state estimation, which can be divided into two categories: centralized state estimation and distributed state estimation. The data-parallel processing architecture of distributed methods gives them superior robustness, reliability, and scalability, but their accuracy is generally lower than that of centralized fusion methods. In practical applications, considering the limited network resources, cooperative state estimation methods are usually designed based on distributed frameworks. \nThe state estimation of the same system obtained by different agents based on local observation may differ from each other. The core scientific problem we aims to solve is how to design a distributed fusion method to effectively integrate dispersed, dynamic, heterogeneous, and multi-source information, so that the cooperative system can balance consensus, compatibility, and scalability, while also balancing system performance and computational efficiency.\nSpecifications are given as follows: \nBased on the knowledge of Cubature Information Filter to design a distributed state estimator, which can realize consensus estimation.\nPre- and co-requisites: \nSome knowledge of Kalman Filter, Ability to program with MATLAB.","descZh":"项目描述：为满足目标检测与跟踪任务对精度、可靠性、鲁棒性、经济性与可扩展性日益增长的需求，并顺应多智能体协作的趋势，多智能体系统协同状态估计技术应运而生。多源信息融合是协同状态估计的核心，可分为集中式状态估计与分布式状态估计两类。分布式方法的数据并行处理架构赋予其优越的鲁棒性、可靠性与可扩展性，但其精度通常低于集中式融合方法。实际应用中，考虑到网络资源有限，协同状态估计方法通常基于分布式框架设计。\n不同智能体基于本地观测对同一系统得到的状态估计可能互不相同。我们试图解决的核心科学问题是：如何设计一种分布式融合方法，有效整合分散、动态、异构、多源的信息，使协同系统在共识性、兼容性与可扩展性之间取得平衡，同时在系统性能与计算效率之间取得平衡。\n具体要求：基于容积信息滤波（Cubature Information Filter）的知识设计一种分布式状态估计器，实现共识估计。\n先修/并修要求：具备一定卡尔曼滤波知识，能使用 MATLAB 编程。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"智能机器人：为 6 自由度机械臂设计抗扰管理控制器","titleEn":"Smart Robotics: Designing Disturbance-Management Controllers for 6-DOF Manipulators","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nRobotic manipulators are the backbone of modern automation, critical to everything from industrial manufacturing and space exploration to delicate medical surgeries. However, getting a robot to move with high precision in the real world is surprisingly difficult.\nWhile modern robots have multiple Degrees of Freedom (DoFs) that make them incredibly flexible, this high-dimensional dynamics makes them mathematically complex to model exactly. In real-world scenarios, robots face \"lumped uncertainties\"—unpredictable variables like shifting payloads, sudden friction, or accidental physical contact. Standard control systems often struggle to handle these disturbances efficiently, losing precision or becoming too computationally heavy to deploy.\nYour Role in this Project: This project aims to bridge the gap between complex robotics and practical, high-accuracy control. You will investigate and design efficient solutions that allow high-DoF robots to maintain perfect motion tracking even when subjected to unknown physical disturbances. If you are interested in advanced robotics, practical algorithm design, and making machines work seamlessly in unpredictable environments, this project is for you.\nKey Objectives (Specifications)\nThroughout this project, you will:\n1. Map the System: Study the kinematic and dynamic models of a 6-DOF robotic manipulator, specifically identifying real-world sources of physical disturbances and how they impact motion.\n2. Define Success Metrics: Conduct a targeted literature review to establish exactly how the robotics industry assesses controller performance (e.g., standard indicators and experimental designs).\n3. Build the Baseline: Implement and test prevalent industry-standard control algorithms (such as PID and Impedance control) to establish a baseline for fair experimental comparison.\n4. Innovate and Test: Develop a novel controller utilizing a disturbance management strategy, and validate its effectiveness against the baseline through hands-on experiments.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Core Knowledge: A solid grasp of fundamental feedback control, state-space control, and basic optimization principles.\n2. Technical Skills: Functional programming skills in MATLAB/Simulink and/or C#.\n3. Mindset: Strong self-motivation and a strong sense of ownership over your research and dissertation. Encourage independent thinking and not relying on AI.\nReference:\n[1] Z. Chen, J. Fu, F. Huang, X. Zhang and W. Chen, \"Disturbance Observer-Based Trajectory Generator for Fast and Accurate Motion Tracking of Robotic Manipulator With Kinematic and Dynamic Constraints,\" in IEEE/ASME Transactions on Mechatronics, doi: 10.1109/TMECH.2026.3694874.\n[2] X. Jia, J. Yang, T. Shi, W. Wang, Y. Pan and H. Yu, \"Robust Precision Motion Control Based on Enhanced Unknown System Dynamics Estimator for High-DoF Robot Manipulators,\" in IEEE/ASME Transactions on Mechatronics, vol. 30, no. 1, pp. 357-368, Feb. 2025, doi: 10.1109/TMECH.2024.3385785.","descZh":"项目描述：机械臂是现代自动化的支柱，从工业制造与太空探索到精细医疗手术，处处至关重要。然而，让机器人在真实世界高精度运动出乎意料地困难。\n现代机器人拥有多个自由度（DoF），使其极其灵活，但高维动力学使其在数学上难以精确建模。真实场景中，机器人面临\"集总不确定性\"——载荷变化、突发摩擦或意外物理接触等不可预测变量。标准控制系统往往难以高效应对这些扰动，要么损失精度，要么计算量过大而无法部署。\n你在本项目中的角色：本项目旨在弥合复杂机器人学与实用高精度控制之间的差距。你将研究并设计高效方案，使高自由度机器人在遭受未知物理扰动时仍能保持完美运动跟踪。如果你对先进机器人学、实用算法设计以及让机器在不可预测环境中无缝工作感兴趣，本项目适合你。\n关键目标（要求）：\n1. 系统建模：研究六自由度机械臂的运动学与动力学模型，特别识别真实世界中物理扰动的来源及其对运动的影响；\n2. 定义成功指标：开展针对性文献综述，确定机器人行业如何评估控制器性能（如标准指标与实验设计）；\n3. 构建基线：实现并测试 PID、阻抗控制等主流工业标准控制算法，为公平实验比较建立基线；\n4. 创新与测试：开发采用扰动管理策略的新型控制器，并通过动手实验验证其相对基线的有效性。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 核心知识：扎实掌握基础反馈控制、状态空间控制与基本优化原理；\n2. 技术技能：能使用 MATLAB/Simulink 和/或 C# 进行功能编程；\n3. 心态：具备强烈自我驱动力与对研究与论文的主人翁意识；鼓励独立思考，不要依赖 AI。\n参考文献：\n[1] Z. Chen 等，\"Disturbance Observer-Based Trajectory Generator for Fast and Accurate Motion Tracking of Robotic Manipulator With Kinematic and Dynamic Constraints,\" IEEE/ASME Transactions on Mechatronics（附 DOI）\n[2] X. Jia 等，\"Robust Precision Motion Control Based on Enhanced Unknown System Dynamics Estimator for High-DoF Robot Manipulators,\" IEEE/ASME Transactions on Mechatronics, vol. 30, no. 1, pp. 357-368, Feb. 2025（附 DOI）","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-9","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"通过抗扰管理实现永磁同步电机驱动的鲁棒速度跟踪","titleEn":"Robust Speed Tracking for Permanent Magnet Synchronous Motor Drives via Disturbance Management","areaZh":"声学；振动 & 控制","areaEn":"Sound; vibration & control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nFrom high-speed electric vehicles and industrial automation to advanced robotics, permanent magnet synchronous motors (PMSMs) are the primary driving force behind modern clean technology. They are widely adopted across the engineering sector for good reason: high efficiency, high power density, and rapid dynamic response.\nHowever, operating electric motors in demanding, unpredictable real-world environments is far from simple. Changes in physical loads, dynamic friction, temperature shifts, and internal parameter fluctuations introduce unknown disturbances that degrade precision control. This challenge multiplies in modern industrial setups—such as paper processing plants, gantries, and multi-motor EV drivetrains—where multiple motors must operate in perfect, dynamic synchronization under variable external forces.\nYour Role in this Project: In this project, you will tackle the core challenge of precision motor control: keeping multi-motor systems running in flawless lockstep, even when unexpected physical forces try to throw them off course. You will study cutting-edge disturbance observers, benchmark standard consensus control techniques, and ultimately develop a novel disturbance-managed consensus control algorithm. If you are interested in power electronics, control theory, electric vehicles, and hands-on simulation and experimental validation, this project offers high-impact research with direct industry application.\nKey Objectives (Specifications)\nThroughout this project, you will:\n1. Map & Validate Physical Disturbances: Conduct a literature review to identify the primary disturbances affecting single PMSM drives, classify their physical characteristics, and validate these behavior profiles through real-world experiments. Additionally, map out standard load-torque characteristics across key industrial applications like electric vehicles and robotics.\n2. Benchmark Advanced Observer Techniques: Investigate state-of-the-art disturbance estimation methods—specifically Predefined-Time disturbance observers and extended state observers. Replicate these observers using MATLAB/Simulink and physical hardware, conducting a comparative analysis between simulation and experimental results to evaluate their real-world strengths and limitations\n3. Explore Multi-Motor Synchronization: Conduct a targeted literature review on consensus speed control for multi-PMSM systems, analyzing how localized disturbances propagate and impact overall dynamic synchronization.\n4. Establish the Baseline: Replicate existing consensus control algorithms in MATLAB/Simulink and experimental testbeds, evaluating their performance limits and shortcomings under real-world perturbations.\n5. Innovate and Validate: Develop a novel consensus control algorithm rooted in a comprehensive disturbance management strategy for multi-PMSM systems, validating its superiority over baseline methods through rigorous experimental testing.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Core Knowledge: A solid grasp of fundamental feedback control, state-space control, and basic optimization principles.\n2. Technical Skills: Functional programming skills in MATLAB/Simulink.\n3. Mindset: Strong self-motivation and a strong sense of ownership over your research and dissertation. Encourage independent thinking and not relying on AI.\nReference: \n[1] J. Sun, S. Xu, S. Ding, Z. Pu and J. Yi, \"Adaptive Conditional Disturbance Negation-Based Nonsmooth-Integral Control for PMSM Drive System,\" in IEEE/ASME Transactions on Mechatronics, vol. 29, no. 5, pp. 3602-3613, Oct. 2024, doi: 10.1109/TMECH.2023.3348247.\n[2] L. Hou, X. Lan, Y. Ren and P. Zhang, \"Fixed-Time Consensus Protocols of Multiagent Systems Based Speed Coordinated Control for Multiple Permanent Magnet Synchronous Motors Under Vector Control System,\" in IEEE Transactions on Power Electronics, vol. 40, no. 12, pp. 17844-17857, Dec. 2025, doi: 10.1109/TPEL.2025.3590572.","descZh":"项目描述：从高速电动汽车、工业自动化到先进机器人，永磁同步电机（PMSM）是现代清洁技术的主要驱动力。其凭借高效率、高功率密度与快速动态响应等优势被工程领域广泛采用。\n然而，在严苛、不可预测的真实环境中运行电机绝非易事。物理负载变化、动态摩擦、温度变化与内部参数波动都会引入破坏精密控制的未知扰动。这一挑战在现代工业装置中成倍放大——如造纸厂、龙门架与多电机 EV 动力系统——其中多台电机必须在变化的内部作用力下保持完美的动态同步。\n你在本项目中的角色：你将攻克电机精密控制的核心挑战——即便意外物理力试图使多电机系统偏离轨迹，也要让其保持完美同步运行。你将研究前沿扰动观测器、基准测试标准共识控制技术，并最终开发一种以全面扰动管理策略为基础的新型扰动管理共识控制算法。如果你对电力电子、控制理论、电动汽车以及动手仿真与实验验证感兴趣，本项目将提供对行业有直接应用的高影响力研究。\n关键目标（要求）：\n1. 建模与验证物理扰动：开展文献综述，识别影响单台 PMSM 驱动的主要扰动，对其物理特性分类，并通过真实实验验证这些行为画像；此外，梳理电动汽车与机器人等关键工业应用的标准负载扭矩特性；\n2. 基准测试先进观测器技术：研究最先进的扰动估计方法——特别是预定时间扰动观测器与扩张状态观测器。使用 MATLAB/Simulink 与物理硬件复现这些观测器，对仿真与实验结果进行比较分析，评估其真实优势与局限；\n3. 探索多电机同步：对多 PMSM 系统共识速度控制开展针对性文献综述，分析局部扰动如何传播并影响整体动态同步；\n4. 建立基线：在 MATLAB/Simulink 与实验台架上复现现有共识控制算法，评估其在真实扰动下的性能极限与不足；\n5. 创新与验证：开发一种以全面扰动管理策略为基础的多 PMSM 系统新型共识控制算法，通过严格实验测试验证其优于基线方法。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 核心知识：扎实掌握基础反馈控制、状态空间控制与基本优化原理；\n2. 技术技能：能使用 MATLAB/Simulink 进行功能编程；\n3. 心态：具备强烈自我驱动力与对研究与论文的主人翁意识；鼓励独立思考，不要依赖 AI。\n参考文献：\n[1] J. Sun 等，\"Adaptive Conditional Disturbance Negation-Based Nonsmooth-Integral Control for PMSM Drive System,\" IEEE/ASME Transactions on Mechatronics, vol. 29, no. 5, pp. 3602-3613, Oct. 2024（附 DOI）\n[2] L. Hou 等，\"Fixed-Time Consensus Protocols of Multiagent Systems Based Speed Coordinated Control for Multiple Permanent Magnet Synchronous Motors Under Vector Control System,\" IEEE Transactions on Power Electronics, vol. 40, no. 12, pp. 17844-17857, Dec. 2025（附 DOI）","cats":["CTRL","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-10","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"通过抗扰管理实现主动车辆悬架的鲁棒控制","titleEn":"Robust Control of Active Vehicle Suspensions via Disturbance Management","areaZh":"声学；振动 & 控制","areaEn":"Sound; vibration & control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nWhether driving a high-performance Electric Vehicle (EV) or riding in an autonomous shuttle, a smooth, stable ride is the benchmark of modern automotive engineering. At the heart of this experience is the vehicle suspension system, which mitigates harsh road impacts to maximize both passenger comfort and tire road-holding safety.\nHistorically, vehicles relied on passive suspensions with fixed spring and damper settings. Semi-active systems (such as Skyhook control) introduced dynamic adjustment, but their adaptability remains confined to narrow ranges. To unlock true ride comfort across highly unpredictable road conditions, modern engineering relies on Active Suspension Systems (ASSs)—advanced setups capable of generating dynamic forces to actively counteract vehicle vibration.\nHowever, designing active suspension controllers is challenging. Road elevation variations are severe external disturbances, and dynamic vehicle uncertainties—such as shifting passenger loads, tire wear, and velocity changes—make ideal control difficult to achieve.\nYour Role in this Project: In this project, you will bridge mechanical vehicle dynamics and cutting-edge control theory. You will study road profile disturbance modeling, establish baseline controller performance, and develop a novel disturbance-managed robust controller designed to deliver an exceptionally smooth ride over harsh, unpredictable terrain. If you are passionate about automotive systems, control theory, vehicle dynamics, and practical MATLAB/Simulink modeling, this project offers high-impact research with direct applications in the EV and autonomous vehicle industries.\nKey Objectives (Specifications)\nThroughout this project, you will:\n 1. Model & Characterize Road Disturbances: Study the dynamic model of an ASS, identify real-world road profiles and system uncertainties (e.g., potholes, changing passenger mass), and analyze their physical impact on vehicle motion.\n 2. Define Performance Metrics: Conduct a targeted literature review to define industry-standard control objectives and performance indicators (e.g., vertical acceleration for ride comfort, suspension deflection limits, and tire load variation for safety).\n 3. Benchmark Standard Controllers: Implement and evaluate baseline control strategies in MATLAB/Simulink and/or experimental setups to establish a clear comparative standard across varied road surfaces.\n 4. Innovate & Validate: Develop a novel controller grounded in a disturbance management strategy, validating its superior vibration suppression and robustness against established baseline controllers through rigorous simulation and physical experiments.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Core Knowledge: A solid grasp of fundamental feedback control, state-space control, and basic optimization principles.\n2. Technical Skills: Functional programming skills in MATLAB/Simulink.\n3. Mindset: Strong self-motivation and a strong sense of ownership over your research and dissertation. Encourage independent thinking and not relying on AI.","descZh":"项目描述：无论是驾驶高性能电动汽车（EV）还是乘坐自动驾驶穿梭车，平稳舒适的乘坐体验都是现代汽车工程的标杆。这一体验的核心是车辆悬架系统——它缓解恶劣道路冲击，以最大限度提升乘客舒适性与轮胎路面附着安全。\n历史上，车辆依赖具有固定弹簧与减振器设定的被动悬架。半主动系统（如天棚控制）引入了动态调节，但其适应性仍局限于较窄范围。为在高度不可预测的道路条件下释放真正的乘坐舒适性，现代工程依赖主动悬架系统（ASS）——能够产生动态力主动抵消车辆振动的先进装置。\n然而，设计主动悬架控制器颇具挑战：路面起伏是强烈的外部扰动，而动态车辆不确定性（如乘客载荷变化、轮胎磨损与速度变化）使理想控制难以实现。\n你在本项目中的角色：你将桥接机械车辆动力学与前沿控制理论。你将研究路面轮廓扰动建模、建立基线控制器性能，并开发一种以扰动管理策略为基础的新型鲁棒控制器，旨在严苛、不可预测地形上实现异常平稳的行驶。如果你对汽车系统、控制理论、车辆动力学与实用的 MATLAB/Simulink 建模充满热情，本项目将提供对 EV 与自动驾驶汽车行业具有直接应用的高影响力研究。\n关键目标（要求）：\n1. 建模与表征道路扰动：研究主动悬架系统的动力学模型，识别真实路面轮廓与系统不确定性（如坑洼、乘客质量变化），并分析其对车辆运动的物理影响；\n2. 定义性能指标：开展针对性文献综述，定义行业标准的控制目标与性能指标（如用于乘坐舒适性的垂向加速度、悬架变形限制与用于安全性的轮胎载荷变化）；\n3. 基准标准控制器：在 MATLAB/Simulink 和/或实验装置中实现并评估基线控制策略，在不同路面建立清晰比较标准；\n4. 创新与验证：开发一种基于扰动管理策略的新型控制器，通过严谨仿真与物理实验，验证其相对基线控制器的优越振动抑制能力与鲁棒性。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 核心知识：扎实掌握基础反馈控制、状态空间控制与基本优化原理；\n2. 技术技能：能使用 MATLAB/Simulink 进行功能编程；\n3. 心态：具备强烈自我驱动力与对研究与论文的主人翁意识；鼓励独立思考，不要依赖 AI。","cats":["CTRL","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-15","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"自动驾驶汽车的运动规划、跟踪控制与可达性分析","titleEn":"Motion Planning, Tracking Control, and Reachability Analysis of Automated Vehicle","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description:\nMotion planning is a fundamental technology for the applications of automated vehicles. The generated trajectory should be feasible for the vehicle to implement, which requires sufficient smoothness on the trajectory. To guarantee safety when running in a complex environment, the trajectory should not collide with any obstacles, which is a main concern in the trajectory optimization problem. Due to modelling errors and external disturbances, perfect trajectory tracking is impossible, so a robust tracking controller is required to attenuate deviations. Furthermore, reachability analysis is integrated to formally verify that the vehicle's entire possible state set, considering bounded tracking errors, remains within safe operational envelopes, thereby providing provable safety guarantees beyond planning alone.\nReachability analysis is used to verify strict safety for the planning and control of automated vehicles. By explicitly incorporating the bounded position errors arising from the tracking controller's performance into the reachable set formulation, we can mathematically over-approximate the forward reachable sets of the vehicle. This integration allows us to rigorously verify whether the vehicle's evolving state envelope remains strictly within the protected road boundaries and collision-free zones, even in worst-case disturbances.\nSpecifications are given as follows:\n1. Using some search-based method to get a coarse path from the start to the end. Safe corridors can be constructed from the map information based on the coarse path to avoid collisions from obstacles. \n2. The smooth trajectory is yielded by solving an optimization problem, in which the safe corridors are applied in the constraints of collision avoidance.\n3. The motion planning and tracking control method is evaluated by numerical simulation.\n5. Using an open-source toolkit, Continuous Reachability Analyzer (CORA), to implement the reachability analysis of a vehicle system. \n6. Experimental studies may be conducted (if the student is interested).\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic knowledge of control theory and optimization.\n2. Programming knowledge of MATLAB for simulations, robot operating system (ROS) and Python (or C++) for experiments.","descZh":"项目描述：运动规划是自动驾驶应用的基础技术。生成的轨迹应可行，可供车辆实际执行，这就要求轨迹具有足够的平滑度。为保证复杂环境下的运行安全，轨迹不得与任何障碍物碰撞——这是轨迹优化问题的主要关切。由于建模误差与外部扰动，完美轨迹跟踪不可能实现，因此需要鲁棒跟踪控制器来抑制偏差。此外，集成可达性分析，可在考虑有界跟踪误差的情况下形式化验证车辆全部可能状态集始终保持在安全运行包络内，从而提供超越单一规划的、可证明的安全保证。\n可达性分析用于对自动驾驶车辆的规划与控制进行严格安全验证。通过将跟踪控制器性能所引起的有界位置误差显式纳入可达集表述，我们可在数学上对车辆的前向可达集进行过近似。这种集成使我们能够严格验证：即使在最坏情况扰动下，车辆的状态演化包络是否始终保持在被保护的道路边界与无碰撞区域内。\n具体要求：\n1. 采用某种基于搜索的方法获得从起点到终点的粗略路径；可依据粗略路径结合地图信息构建安全走廊，以避免与障碍物碰撞；\n2. 通过求解优化问题生成平滑轨迹，其中安全走廊被用作避碰约束；\n3. 通过数值仿真评估运动规划与跟踪控制方法；\n4. 使用开源工具包 CORA（Continuous Reachability Analyzer）实现车辆系统的可达性分析；\n5.（可选）若学生有兴趣，可开展实验研究。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备控制理论与优化的基础知识；\n2. 会使用 MATLAB 编程进行仿真，实验需了解 ROS（机器人操作系统）与 Python（或 C++）。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-16","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于测量的超视距雷达多目标跟踪","titleEn":"Measurement based multi-target tracking with over-the-horizon radars","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description: Multi-target tracking (MTT) technology can estimate the states of multiple targets in the observation scene, which is widely used in military and civil fields, such as target monitoring, precision guidance and air defense. In real practices, the target usually gets multiple measurements for the shape and size of the target and the multiple reflection paths in the environment. Over-the-horizon radar (OTHR) is a widely used multiple observation models sensor, which can track the target at a long distance and is commonly used for marine target tracking. However, OTHR needs to solve the problem of measurement-observation model-target matching, which causes huge computational burden especially for the case with dense clutters. With the increasing demand of marine target tracking and the complexity of the environment, multi-detection multi-target tracking (MDMTT) using OTHR with dense clutters is a hot topic. \nOur goal is to propose an algorithm that utilizes OTHR observations (which include a large number of clutter observations and observations from different models of different targets) to accurately estimate the state of multiple targets while reducing the computational burden. This requires matching a large number of observations with multiple targets, which is the challenge of this project.\nPre- and co-requisites: Some knowledge of Kalman Filter, Ability to program with MATLAB.","descZh":"项目描述：多目标跟踪（MTT）技术可估计观测场景中多个目标的状态，广泛应用于军事与民用领域，如目标监视、精确制导与防空。实际中，目标通常会因目标形状尺寸及环境中多条反射路径而获得多个量测。超视距雷达（OTHR）是一种广泛使用的多观测模型传感器，可远距离跟踪目标，常用于海上目标跟踪。然而，OTHR 需要解决\"量测-观测模型-目标\"匹配问题，这在杂波密集的情况下会造成巨大计算负担。随着海上目标跟踪需求增长与环境复杂化，使用密集杂波下 OTHR 的多检测多目标跟踪（MDMTT）成为研究热点。\n我们的目标是提出一种算法，利用 OTHR 观测（包含大量杂波观测以及来自不同目标不同模型的观测）准确估计多目标状态，同时降低计算负担。这需要将大量观测与多个目标进行匹配，即本项目的主要挑战。\n先修/并修要求：具备一定卡尔曼滤波知识，能使用 MATLAB 编程。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LEUNGKM2-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"下一代高速列车（或地铁列车）","titleEn":"The Next Generation High-speed Trains (or Metro trains)","areaZh":"高速列车文献综述与 CFD 研究","areaEn":"Literature review and CFD study of high-speed train","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. LEUNG Kin Man","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-01 02:04:02","descriptionText":"Rolling stock manufacturers worldwide are focusing intensively their research and development efforts in improving the operating and environmental performance of the high-speed trains and metro trains. The latest design of the high-speed trains, in fact, shapes the rapid development of the high-speed transport links across the globe. \nThis project involves a research study into the state-of-the-art design of high-speed trains especially on their aerodynamic performance to enhance their operation & headway, speed (train cab and train aerodynamic design), passenger carrying capacity, energy efficiency, environmental performance, traction drive, traction control technology, train mass management, ride comfort and wheel-rail interaction over the next 10 to 20 years. \nFor the train aerodynamic study, students will apply computerized CFD modelling for the aerodynamic designs of the high-speed trains (or metro trains) involving its aerodynamic drag forces, drag coefficients, pressure and air flow distributions, skin frictions, Reynolds number and other key design parameters. Students will use a CFD program (e.g. SimScale, Simcenter STAR-CCM+, ANSYS Fluent (2022R1) or equivalent for CFD analysis; SolidWorks (2022), Pro/Engineer or equivalent for 3-D modelling or other similar proprietary software) for the aerodynamic simulation to simulate the drag force and drag coefficient of the cab under different operating speed.\nAs an alternative, students may also choose to carry out the research study into the design of metro trains. The project will involve the future advancement of the metro trains’ operating performance, passenger carrying capacity, energy efficiency, environmental performance, traction drive, traction control technology or ride comfort.","descZh":"全球轨道交通装备制造商正集中研发力量，提升高速列车与地铁列车的运行与环境性能。事实上，最新设计的高速列车正塑造着全球高速交通连接的快速发展。\n本项目对高速列车的最先进设计进行研究，尤其聚焦其空气动力学性能，以提升未来 10~20 年内的运行与发车间隔、速度（司机室与列车空气动力学设计）、载客能力、能源效率、环境性能、牵引驱动、牵引控制技术、列车重量管理、乘坐舒适性与轮轨相互作用。\n就列车空气动力学研究而言，学生将应用计算机 CFD 建模开展高速列车（或地铁列车）的气动设计，涵盖气动阻力、阻力系数、压力与气流分布、表面摩擦、雷诺数及其他关键设计参数。学生将使用 CFD 程序（如用于 CFD 分析的 SimScale、Simcenter STAR-CCM+、ANSYS Fluent (2022R1) 或等效软件；用于 3D 建模的 SolidWorks (2022)、Pro/Engineer 或等效或其他类似商业软件）进行气动仿真，模拟司机室在不同运行速度下的阻力与阻力系数。\n作为备选，学生也可选择开展地铁列车设计研究。项目将涉及地铁列车运行性能、载客能力、能源效率、环境性能、牵引驱动、牵引控制技术或乘坐舒适性的未来进步。","cats":["FLU"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LEUNGKM2-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"地铁站客流仿真分析","titleEn":"Simulation Analysis of passenger flows at a Metro Station","areaZh":"使用 AnyLogic 仿真铁路车站客流","areaEn":"Simulation of railway station passenger flows using AnyLogic","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. LEUNG Kin Man","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-01 02:04:02","descriptionText":"Continuous increase in patronage of a metro line has always been a challenging problem to the railway operator and the local government. Passenger flow analysis study for a metro station is an essential and scientific way to understand and alleviate the passenger congestion problems. Engineering consultants are doing a lot of the station passenger flow analysis studies in Hong Kong.\nThis project involves the development of a mathematical model to simulate passenger flows of a station (or a railway terminal) at peak operating conditions. The model shall encompass the station areas and facilities like station entrances/exits, concourse, automatic fare collection gates (and ticketing gates), retail shops areas, waiting areas, staircases, passenger lifts, escalators, cross-passage tunnel/edit/walkway/footbridge and platforms, etc. A consultancy study on the passenger flow analysis of a station in the railway sector normally costs approximately HK$1 to 5 million if it is done by an engineering consultancy firm.\nFor this project, students will need to find out the input data including the layout of the station and the peak passenger flows data. Students are recommended to apply proprietary software program (e.g. AnyLogic 8.8, AnyLogic Cloud or equivalent) that is available in the market for the simulation, and they will undertake verification and validation processes (calculations using basic principles) to verify the accuracy of the proprietary software.","descZh":"地铁线路客流持续增长一直是轨道交通运营方与地方政府面临的难题。地铁车站的客流分析研究是理解并缓解乘客拥堵问题的必要且科学的手段。香港的工程顾问公司开展了大量车站客流分析研究。\n本项目涉及开发一个数学模型，用于仿真车站（或铁路枢纽）在高峰运行条件下的客流。模型应涵盖车站区域与设施，如出入口、站厅、自动售检票闸机（与售票闸机）、商铺区域、等候区、楼梯、无障碍电梯、自动扶梯、跨线隧道/通道/走廊/天桥与站台等。在香港，由工程顾问公司开展的轨道交通车站客流分析咨询研究通常花费约 100 万至 500 万港元。\n本项目学生需获取输入数据，包括车站布局与高峰客流数据。建议学生使用市面上可获得的商业软件（如 AnyLogic 8.8、AnyLogic Cloud 或同类软件）进行仿真，并开展验证与校核过程（用基本原理进行计算），以核实商业软件的准确性。","cats":["FLU"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LEUNGKM2-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"高速列车气动性能的 CFD 建模","titleEn":"CFD modelling of the aerodynamic performance of a high-speed train","areaZh":"高速列车（或地铁列车）的 CFD 建模","areaEn":"CFD modelling of high-speed trains (or metro trains)","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. LEUNG Kin Man","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-01 02:04:02","descriptionText":"Minimizing the passenger journey time is the vision of all well-known train manufacturers worldwide. One of the critical design criteria of the high-speed trains is to optimize the operating speed with reduced electricity consumption. For a higher running speed, the corresponding traction power consumption and the vibration levels will increase (leading to fatigue). \nIn the recent decades, the high-speed train manufacturers have all performed in-depth research and development in improving the aerodynamic design of their high-speed trains by optimizing the shape and construction of the train cab, cab nose, car-to-car gap and pantograph in order to minimize its electricity costs during operation. Scale-down high-speed train model tests in wind tunnels have been well developed to assist the research. As a result, the train cab noses are lengthened and the train cab and car bodies are streamlined to minimize the aerodynamic drag and skin frictions.\nThis project involves a literature study and/or computerized CFD modelling of the aerodynamic designs of the high-speed trains (or metro trains) involving its aerodynamic drag forces, drag coefficients, pressure and air flow distributions, skin frictions, Reynolds number and other key design parameters. Students will apply the CFD modelling (e.g. SimScale, Simcenter STAR-CCM+, ANSYS Fluent (2022R1) or equivalent for CFD analysis; SolidWorks (2022), Pro/Engineer or equivalent for 3-D modelling or other similar proprietary software) for the aerodynamic simulation. \nThe study shall also involve wind tunnel tests of HST (or metro train) with scale-down models and comparison of different models of high-speed trains depending on the student’s choice","descZh":"缩短乘客旅程时间是全球知名列车制造商共同的愿景。高速列车的一个关键设计标准是优化运行速度并降低电力消耗。运行速度越高，相应的牵引功耗与振动水平也越高（从而导致疲劳）。\n近几十年来，高速列车制造商均在改善高速列车空气动力学设计方面开展了深入研发——通过优化列车司机室、司机室车头、车厢间缝隙与受电弓的形状与构造，最大限度降低运行电费。风洞缩比模型试验已得到充分发展以辅助研究。由此，列车车头被加长，司机室与车体被流线化，以最小化气动阻力与表面摩擦。\n本项目涉及高速列车（或地铁列车）空气动力学设计的文献研究与/或计算机 CFD 建模，涵盖其气动阻力、阻力系数、压力与气流分布、表面摩擦、雷诺数及其他关键设计参数。学生将应用 CFD 建模（如 SimScale、Simcenter STAR-CCM+、ANSYS Fluent (2022R1) 或等效 CFD 分析软件；SolidWorks (2022)、Pro/Engineer 或等效 3D 建模软件或其他类似商业软件）进行气动仿真。\n研究还将涉及 HST（或地铁列车）缩比模型风洞试验，并可根据学生选择对不同型号高速列车进行比较。","cats":["FLU"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LEUNGKM2-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"现代高速铁路线（或现代地铁线）的最佳智能工程实践","titleEn":"Best Intelligent Engineering Practices for a modern High-speed Rail Line (or a modern Metro Line)","areaZh":"最佳智能与国际工程实践的文献综述","areaEn":"Literature review of best intelligent and international engineering practices","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. LEUNG Kin Man","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-07-30 21:07:01","descriptionText":"This is a research project into the best intelligent and international engineering practices in the design, planning and operation of a modern high-speed rail line. The study areas include the railway line alignment planning, station and platform design, infrastructure design (for track works, overhead lines, etc.), high-speed train design (for traction drives and traction control, etc.) or design of railway engineering systems (for traction supply system, signaling system, train management system) etc. Students may apply Digital Twin, Abaqus or similar proprietary software to enrich the study. \nStudents shall pick 2 to 3 relevant areas for the study to ensure good coverage. The study shall be deep and thorough enough to achieve the MSc dissertation standard. As an alternative, students may choose to carry out the research study into the best international engineering practice in the design, planning and operation of a modern metro line. The study areas are similar to the above.","descZh":"这是一项关于现代高速铁路线路设计、规划与运营中最佳智能与国际工程实践的研究项目。研究领域包括：铁路线路走向规划、车站与站台设计、基础设施设计（轨道工程、接触网等）、高速列车设计（牵引驱动与牵引控制等）或铁路工程系统设计（牵引供电系统、信号系统、列车管理系统）等。学生可应用数字孪生、Abaqus 或类似专有软件来丰富研究。\n学生应从上述领域中选取 2~3 个相关方向进行研究，以保证覆盖面；研究需深入、透彻，达到硕士论文水准。作为备选，学生也可选择对现代地铁线路设计、规划与运营的最佳国际工程实践开展研究，研究领域与上述类似。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YUECHEN-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"储能材料的仿真与建模","titleEn":"Simulation and modeling of energy storage materials","areaZh":"材料科学与工程","areaEn":"Materials Science and Engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHEN Yue","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Materials properties are essential to the performance of batteries. To improve safety and energy density, new materials are continuously being explored. Compared to experimental investigations, material simulation and modeling offer a cost-effective and efficient approach for screening potential candidates. In this project, students will employ first-principles methods to study structural stability, ionic adsorption, and diffusion. High-performance computing resources will be utilized to conduct the research. The predictive capabilities of the simulations will be validated against experimental results. The goal of this project is to enhance understanding of how materials modeling can contribute to the development of new battery materials.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nMaterials science and engineering","descZh":"材料性能对电池表现至关重要。为提升安全性与能量密度，人们不断探索新材料。与实验研究相比，材料模拟与建模为筛选潜在候选材料提供了经济高效的途径。在本项目中，学生将采用第一性原理方法研究结构稳定性、离子吸附与扩散。研究将利用高性能计算资源开展；仿真的预测能力将与实验结果进行对照验证。项目目标是加深对材料建模如何助力新电池材料开发的理解。\n先修/并修要求：材料科学与工程。","cats":["MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YUECHEN-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"使用 AI 模型预测储能材料性能","titleEn":"Predicting the performance of energy storage materials using AI models","areaZh":"AI & 材料科学","areaEn":"AI & Materials Science","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHEN Yue","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Estimating the remaining lifetime and state of health of batteries is crucial for various applications. With the advancement of artificial intelligence (AI) models, it has become possible to reliably predict battery performance using minimal experimental data. In this project, students will employ different AI techniques to analyze the state of health and remaining useful life of various batteries. High-performance computing resources will be utilized to conduct the research. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy measurements of the batteries will be performed experimentally, and the resulting data will be used to train the AI models. The goal of this project is to deepen the understanding of the predictive capabilities of different AI models and explore their potential application for effective battery management.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nNil","descZh":"估算电池的剩余寿命与健康状态（SOH）对多种应用至关重要。随着人工智能（AI）模型的发展，利用极少的实验数据可靠预测电池性能已成为可能。在本项目中，学生将采用不同的 AI 技术分析多种电池的健康状态与剩余使用寿命。研究将利用高性能计算资源开展；实验上将对电池进行电化学阻抗谱测量，所得数据用于训练 AI 模型。项目目标是加深对不同 AI 模型预测能力的理解，并探索其在有效电池管理中的应用潜力。\n先修/并修要求：无。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YUECHEN-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"AI 原子间势能及其在分子动力学模拟中的应用","titleEn":"AI interatomic potentials and their applications in molecular dynamics simulations","areaZh":"AI & 材料科学","areaEn":"AI & Materials Science","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHEN Yue","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Direct calculation of atomic interaction in materials can be extremely challenging due to complex nature of chemical bonding. With the development of artificial intelligence, it is possible to reliably evaluate atomic interaction without the knowledge of electronic structure. In this project, students will use established generic machine learning models to study the dynamics of crystal lattice, and evaluate the thermodynamic properties of materials. High-performance computer systems will be applied to carry out the research. The predictive power of different machine learning models will be evaluated by comparing the results with those obtained from accurate first-principles calculations. The aim of this project is to better understand how AI technology can advance computational research in materials science and engineering. \nPre- and co-requisites:\nMaterials science and engineering","descZh":"由于化学成键的复杂性，直接计算材料中的原子相互作用极具挑战。随着人工智能的发展，无需电子结构知识即可可靠评估原子相互作用已成为可能。在本项目中，学生将使用成熟的通用机器学习模型研究晶格动力学，并评估材料的热力学性质。研究将利用高性能计算机系统开展；不同机器学习模型的预测能力将通过与精确第一性原理计算结果的对比加以评估。项目旨在更好地理解 AI 技术如何推动材料科学与工程中的计算研究。\n先修/并修要求：材料科学与工程。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YUECHEN-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"材料微观结构、热学与电学性能的实验研究","titleEn":"Experimental study of the microstructure, thermal, and electrical properties of materials","areaZh":"能源 & 材料","areaEn":"Energy & Materials","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHEN Yue","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Thermoelectric materials can directly convert heat into electricity or vice versa. They can be used in various applications including energy harvesting and solid-state cooling. Although thermoelectric effect was discovered many years ago, it has garnered significant research attention in recent years due to increasing interest in sustainable energy technologies. In this project, students will synthesized thermoelectric materials using high-temperature melting, high-energy ball milling, and induction hot press methods. Students will learn to characterize the microstructures of materials, and perform measurements of the electrical and thermal properties at different temperatures.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nMaterials science and engineering","descZh":"热电材料可直接将热能转化为电能，或反之。它们可用于能量收集与固态制冷等多种应用。尽管热电效应早在多年前就被发现，但近年来由于对可持续能源技术兴趣的增长，重新获得了大量研究关注。在本项目中，学生将采用高温熔炼、高能球磨与感应热压等方法合成热电材料；学习表征材料微观结构，并在不同温度下测量材料的电学与热学性能。\n先修/并修要求：材料科学与工程。","cats":["MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YUECHEN-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"热能转换与热电模块","titleEn":"Thermal energy conversion and thermoelectric modules","areaZh":"能源；材料与器件","areaEn":"Energy; Materials and Devices","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHEN Yue","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Thermoelectric materials can directly convert heat into electricity or vice versa. For practical applications, n-type and p-type thermoelectric materials need to be connected to form a thermoelectric module. The power output and energy conversion efficiency of the module are affected by different factors including the dimensions of legs, the contact materials, the solders, and the selections of the n-type and p-type materials. Measurements of the power output and the energy conversion efficiency are critical for the evaluation of the module performance. In this project, students will learn to fabricate the thermoelectric module and measure the contact resistance and study the effects of different factors on the power output and energy conversion efficiency.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nMaterials science and engineering\nDesign and manufacture","descZh":"热电材料可直接将热转化为电，或反之。实际应用中，需要将 n 型与 p 型热电材料连接起来构成热电模块。模块的功率输出与能量转换效率受多种因素影响，包括热电腿尺寸、接触材料、焊料以及 n/p 型材料的选择。功率输出与能量转换效率的测量对评估模块性能至关重要。在本项目中，学生将学习制造热电模块、测量接触电阻，并研究不同因素对功率输出与能量转换效率的影响。\n先修/并修要求：材料科学与工程；设计与制造。","cats":["ENE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZULFIALI-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"使用 Arduino 微控制器板自动检测雨或阳光的智能天篷","titleEn":"Smart canopy for automatically detecting rain or sunlight using an Arduino\rmicrocontroller board.","areaZh":"自动化；智能系统；微电子；创新设计；机电一体化","areaEn":"Automation; Smart System; Microelectronics; Innovative Design; Electromechanics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. ALI Zulfiqar","moderator":"","capacity":8,"availCap":8,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"In this project, the student is tasked with developing an intelligent canopy that automatically opens or\ncloses based on external environmental conditions. The canopy features sensors that detect weather\nconditions to determine whether it should be open or closed. The student will select appropriate sensors, such as temperature, humidity, rain, and light sensors, and incorporate an OLED display to show real time data like temperature, humidity, and light levels. When the rain sensor detects rainfall, the canopy will automatically open; it will close once the rain stops, indicating the area is dry. The opening and closing actions should occur automatically without manual intervention. This canopy is designed to be large and suitable for installation in open spaces such as courtyards, parks, beaches, and outdoor cafes. It can be positioned near benches, providing a comfortable shelter for people to sit and seek refuge from the rain during inclement weather.","descZh":"在本项目中，学生需开发一种可根据外部环境条件自动开合的智能遮阳棚。遮阳棚配备可检测天气状况的传感器，以决定应开启还是关闭。学生将选择合适的传感器（如温度、湿度、雨水与光照传感器），并集成 OLED 显示屏实时显示温度、湿度与光照等数据。当雨量传感器检测到降雨时，遮阳棚将自动打开；雨停后（表明地面已干）自动关闭。开合动作应完全自动，无需人工干预。该遮阳棚设计为大型装置，适合安装于庭院、公园、海滩与户外咖啡厅等开放空间，可置于长椅旁，为人们在恶劣天气下提供舒适的避雨休憩之所。","cats":["CTRL","DES","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZULFIALI-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"盲人智能手杖","titleEn":"Smart Cane for Blind People","areaZh":"自动化；智能系统；微电子学；创新设计；机电一体化","areaEn":"Automation; Smart System; Microelectronics; Innovative Design; Electromechanics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. ALI Zulfiqar","moderator":"","capacity":8,"availCap":8,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"The smart cane allows users to move forward smoothly, much like a conventional cane,making it easy for visually impaired individuals to adopt. Additionally, the cane features 360° obstacle detection using LiDAR, enabling it to automatically turn right or left when obstacles are detected, enhancing safety and navigation.","descZh":"这款智能导盲杖让用户能够像使用传统手杖一样顺畅前行，易于视障人士接受。此外，导盲杖采用 LiDAR 实现 360° 障碍物检测，在检测到障碍物时可自动左右转向，提升安全性与导航能力。","cats":["CTRL","DES","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZULFIALI-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于Arduino的智能家居系统","titleEn":"Smart Home System Based on Arduino","areaZh":"自动化；智能系统；微电子学；创新设计；机电一体化","areaEn":"Automation; Smart System; Microelectronics; Innovative Design; Electromechanics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. ALI Zulfiqar","moderator":"","capacity":8,"availCap":8,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"With the advancement of technology, 'Smart Home' has become a trend nowadays. The 'Smart Home' goal is to provide a comfortable, convenient, efficient lifestyle; enhance safety; be environmentally friendly and energy-saving; and create an environment for the residents by automating and centralizing the controls of all the appliances in homes.​We want to design a smart home system based on temperature and humidity sensors, water drop sensors, photoresistor sensors, etc., which uses a microcontroller board to obtain some environmental information, as well as to send commands to some actuators, such as a servo motor and a cooling fan. Meanwhile, the window will be closed automatically when it rains, or the lights will be turned on automatically when it is dark. In the meantime, the power supply system supports solar energy to charge a lithium battery, which means that the home will not just stop from shutting down due to a blackout but also helps the global ecosystem in general by taking its tiny part in helping reduce CO₂ on Earth. So all of these sensors will help construct a comfortable, smart, and safe system. Also, Bluetooth will be used for transferring the sensor data without an the internet connection.","descZh":"随着技术进步，\"智能家居\"已成为当今潮流。智能家居的目标是通过对家中所有电器的自动化与集中控制，为居住者提供舒适、便利、高效的生活方式，提升安全性，并做到环保节能。我们想要设计一套基于温度/湿度传感器、水滴传感器、光敏电阻传感器等的智能家居系统，利用微控制器板采集环境信息，并向舵机、冷却风扇等执行器发送指令。同时，下雨时窗户将自动关闭，天黑时灯光将自动开启。此外，电源系统支持用太阳能为锂电池充电——这意味着家居不仅不会因停电而停摆，还能为减少地球二氧化碳排放贡献一份力量。这些传感器共同构建一个舒适、智能、安全的系统；同时将使用蓝牙在无互联网连接的情况下传输传感器数据。","cats":["CTRL","DES","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI翻牌显示板（英语/粤语/普通话）","titleEn":"AI Split-flap board (English/Cantonese/Mandarin)","areaZh":"设计与制造；AI大语言模型；令牌与API编程","areaEn":"Design and Manufacture; AI LLM; token and API programming","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 21:36:30","descriptionText":"The student will develop a physical 100cmx60cm, multilingual AI interaction system: users speak into a wireless handheld phone in English, Cantonese, or Mandarin; the system sends the query to Gemini, Perplexity, or DeepSeek; and a concise answer appears on a split-flap board for 10 seconds before returning to its idle display. The system supports English, Cantonese and Mandarin, with speech-to-text (STT) and speech-to-speech (STS) interaction.\n1) Preferred skills\nMechanical design and CAD modelling for enclosures, mounts and moving display mechanisms\nElectronics and embedded systems: Raspberry Pi/Arduino/ESP32, sensors, motors, relays and power systems\nBasic programming for hardware control, voice processing and AI API integration\nExperience with prototyping\nInterest in multilingual voice AI, user-centred product design and hardware–software troubleshooting\n2) Expected outcomes\nFunctional wireless handheld telephone-style for voice prompts\nEnglish, Cantonese and Mandarin voice-query capability\nGemini, Perplexity and/or DeepSeek integration for AI-generated answers\nSplit-flap board automatically displays a concise answer for 10 seconds, then returns to idle mode\nComplete prototype package: CAD design, wiring/system diagram, source code, bill of materials, testing results and final demonstration\n3) Onsite frequency\n1 onsite day (3hr) per week for prototype assembly, hardware integration and system testing","descZh":"学生将开发一个 100cm×60cm 的多语言 AI 交互系统：用户对着无线手持电话机用英语、粤语或普通话说话；系统将提问发送给 Gemini、Perplexity 或 DeepSeek；简洁的回答显示在翻页板（split-flap board）上 10 秒，然后回到空闲显示。系统支持英语、粤语与普通话，具备语音转文本（STT）与语音到语音（STS）交互能力。\n1) 偏好技能：外壳、支架与活动显示机构的机械设计与 CAD 建模；电子与嵌入式系统：Raspberry Pi/Arduino/ESP32、传感器、电机、继电器与电源系统；面向硬件控制、语音处理与 AI API 集成的基础编程；有原型制作经验；对多语言语音 AI、以用户为中心的产品设计以及软硬件排障有兴趣。\n2) 预期成果：可工作的无线手持电话式语音提示终端；英语、粤语与普通话语音提问能力；集成 Gemini、Perplexity 和/或 DeepSeek 生成 AI 回答；翻页板自动显示简洁答案 10 秒后返回空闲模式；完整原型包：CAD 设计、接线/系统图、源代码、物料清单、测试结果与最终演示。\n3) 现场频率：每周 1 天现场（3 小时），用于原型组装、硬件集成与系统测试。","cats":["AI","CS","DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"搜寻走失人员的AI户外设备（英语/粤语/普通话）","titleEn":"AI outdoor device for finding lost people (English/Cantonese/Mandarin)","areaZh":"设计与制造；AI大语言模型；令牌与API编程；LoRa","areaEn":"Design and Manufacture; AI LLM; token and API programming; Lora","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 21:07:02","descriptionText":"A rugged, walkie-talkie-style outdoor device that supports multilingual AI queries through 3 LLMs, presents information on a low-power 3-inch e-ink display, and includes LoRa-based signalling/location tracking plus a camera for image identification. The system supports English, Cantonese and Mandarin, with speech-to-text (STT) interaction.\n1) Preferred skills\nMechanical design and CAD modelling for rugged, weather-resistant enclosures, mounting points and durable button interfaces\nEmbedded systems and low-power electronics, including microcontrollers, battery management systems, e-ink displays and power optimisation\nLoRa/LoRaWAN, GPS/GNSS and wireless communication for outdoor signalling, location tracking and emergency-status functions\nProgramming for AI API integration, multilingual voice/text interaction, camera input and image-identification workflows\nExperience with outdoor-device testing, including water/dust resistance, drop protection, thermal conditions, usability and field troubleshooting\n2) Expected outcomes\nA functional heavy-duty, walkie-talkie-style AI outdoor device with a 3-inch e-ink screen and long-life rechargeable battery\nThree-language operation—English, Cantonese and Mandarin—with three selectable or automatically routed LLM services, such as Gemini, Perplexity and DeepSeek\nLoRa-based outdoor communication for location/status signalling, with GPS/GNSS tracking and an SOS or check-in function\nIntegrated camera that captures an image and sends it to an AI vision model for basic identification, such as plants, objects, signs or equipment\nComplete prototype package: CAD enclosure files, electronics/wiring diagram, source code, bill of materials, battery-life and field-test results, user guide and final demonstration\n3) Onsite frequency\n1 onsite day (3hr) per week for enclosure fabrication, electronics assembly, antenna integration and outdoor-device testing","descZh":"一款坚固耐用的对讲机式户外设备：通过 3 个 LLM 支持多语言 AI 查询，在低功耗 3 英寸电子墨水屏上呈现信息，并具备基于 LoRa 的信令/位置跟踪以及用于图像识别的相机。系统支持英语、粤语与普通话，具备语音转文本（STT）交互能力。\n1) 偏好技能：面向坚固、防风雨外壳、安装点与耐用按键界面的机械设计与 CAD 建模；嵌入式系统与低功耗电子，包括微控制器、电池管理系统、电子墨水屏与功耗优化；面向户外信令、位置跟踪与紧急状态功能的 LoRa/LoRaWAN、GPS/GNSS 与无线通信；面向 AI API 集成、多语言语音/文本交互、相机输入与图像识别工作流的编程；有户外设备测试经验，包括防水/防尘、跌落防护、热环境、可用性与现场排障。\n2) 预期成果：带 3 英寸电子墨水屏与长效可充电电池的可工作重型对讲机式 AI 户外设备；支持英语、粤语与普通话三种语言运行，可选用三个 LLM 服务（如 Gemini、Perplexity、DeepSeek）或自动路由；基于 LoRa 的户外通信用于位置/状态信令，具备 GPS/GNSS 跟踪与 SOS 或签到功能；集成相机可拍摄图像并发送至 AI 视觉模型进行基础识别（如植物、物体、标识或设备）；完整原型包：CAD 外壳文件、电子/接线图、源代码、物料清单、电池续航与现场测试结果、用户指南与最终演示。\n3) 现场频率：每周 1 天现场（3 小时），用于外壳制作、电子装配、天线集成与户外设备测试。","cats":["AI","CS","DES","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-3","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI学习设备（英语/粤语/普通话）","titleEn":"AI study device (English/Cantonese/Mandarin)","areaZh":"设计与制造；AI大语言模型；令牌与API编程","areaEn":"Design and Manufacture; AI LLM; token and API programming","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 21:07:02","descriptionText":"A compact multilingual study device that provides AI-supported question answering and image understanding in English, Cantonese and Mandarin. It uses a 5-inch e-ink display, physical scroll control and 2 buttons, enabling focused learning without reliance on a smartphone. Powered by type-C charging. The system supports English, Cantonese and Mandarin, with speech-to-text (STT) and speech-to-speech (STS) interaction\n1) Preferred skills\nMechanical design and CAD modelling for a compact, ergonomic handheld enclosure with durable buttons and camera placement\nEmbedded systems and electronics, including microcontrollers, 5-inch e-ink displays, buttons, camera modules, battery systems and low-power design\nProgramming for physical interface control, display formatting, wireless connectivity and AI API integration\nFamiliarity with multilingual speech/text interaction and LLM services such as Gemini, Perplexity and DeepSeek\nComputer-vision for camera-based recognition, description and educational content support\n2) Expected outcomes\nA working handheld AI study device with a 5-inch e-ink screen and physical up/down scroll buttons\nEnglish, Cantonese and Mandarin support for AI-based learning questions and answers\nA dedicated Ask AI button that sends learner questions to one of three selectable LLM platforms—Gemini, Perplexity and DeepSeek—and displays concise, structured responses until next question.\nA 5 MP camera with a dedicated image-recognition button that returns an AI-generated identification or description\nComplete prototype package: CAD files, enclosure design, wiring/system diagram, source code, bill of materials, battery and usability test results, user guide and final demonstration\n3) Onsite frequency\n1 onsite day (3hr) per week for device assembly, enclosure prototyping, e-ink display integration and camera/button testing","descZh":"一种紧凑型多语言学习设备，以英语、粤语与普通话提供 AI 支持的问答与图像理解。设备采用 5 英寸电子墨水屏、实体滚轮控制与 2 个按键，无需依赖智能手机即可专注学习；由 Type-C 供电。系统支持英语、粤语与普通话，具备语音转文本（STT）与语音到语音（STS）交互能力。\n1) 偏好技能：面向紧凑、人体工学手持外壳（含耐用按键与相机放置）的机械设计与 CAD 建模；嵌入式系统与电子，包括微控制器、5 英寸电子墨水屏、按键、相机模块、电池系统与低功耗设计；面向实体界面控制、显示排版、无线连接与 AI API 集成的编程；熟悉多语言语音/文本交互及 Gemini、Perplexity、DeepSeek 等 LLM 服务；具备用于相机识别、描述与学习内容支持的计算机视觉能力。\n2) 预期成果：带 5 英寸电子墨水屏与实体上下滚动按键的可工作手持 AI 学习设备；支持英语、粤语与普通话的 AI 学习问答；专用\"Ask AI\"按键将学习者问题发送至三个可选的 LLM 平台（Gemini、Perplexity、DeepSeek）之一，并显示简洁、结构化的回答直至下个问题；带专用图像识别按键的 5 MP 相机，可返回 AI 生成的识别或描述；完整原型包：CAD 文件、外壳设计、接线/系统图、源代码、物料清单、电池与可用性测试结果、用户指南与最终演示。\n3) 现场频率：每周 1 天现场（3 小时），用于设备组装、外壳原型制作、电子墨水屏集成与相机/按键测试。","cats":["AI","CS","DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-4","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI天气ESG设备","titleEn":"AI weather ESG device","areaZh":"设计与制造；AI大语言模型；令牌与API编程","areaEn":"Design and Manufacture; AI LLM; token and API programming","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 21:07:02","descriptionText":"A multilingual environmental monitoring device that captures live weather and ESG-related data, displays it on a 5-inch e-ink dashboard, and uses AI for data interpretation and camera-based environmental observation. The system supports English, Cantonese and Mandarin, with speech-to-text (STT) and speech-to-speech (STS) interaction.\n1) Preferred skills\nEmbedded systems and sensor integration, including temperature, humidity, CO₂, O₂ and CH₄ sensors, microcontrollers, power management and wireless connectivity\nMechanical design and CAD modelling for a durable sensor enclosure, airflow design, camera mounting and environmental protection\nProgramming for live sensor data collection, database/cloud storage, dashboard development and data analysis\nFamiliarity with AI API integration across three LLMs, such as Gemini, Perplexity and DeepSeek, including multilingual prompt routing\n2) Expected outcomes\nA working AI weather and ESG device with five integrated sensors: temperature, humidity, CO₂, O₂ and CH₄\nA 5-inch e-ink “live-time” dashboard showing current readings, sensor status, trends, alerts and concise ESG/environmental insights\nA 5 MP camera connected to three LLM or vision-AI services for image recognition and environmental scene description\nEnglish, Cantonese and Mandarin interaction using STT for voice input and TTS for spoken AI responses\nA complete data workflow that saves timestamped sensor readings and images, analyses trends, flags unusual readings, and produces AI-generated summaries, supported by CAD files, wiring diagrams, source code, bill of materials, test data and a final demonstration\n3) Onsite frequency\nOne onsite day (3hr) per week for sensor installation, enclosure development, display/camera integration and calibration testing","descZh":"一种多语言环境监测设备：捕获实时天气与 ESG 相关数据，显示在 5 英寸电子墨水仪表盘上，并利用 AI 进行数据解读与基于相机的环境观测。系统支持英语、粤语与普通话，具备语音转文本（STT）与语音到语音（STS）交互能力。\n1) 偏好技能：嵌入式系统与传感器集成，包括温度、湿度、CO₂、O₂ 与 CH₄ 传感器、微控制器、电源管理与无线连接；面向耐用传感器外壳、气流设计、相机安装与环境保护的机械设计与 CAD 建模；面向实时传感器数据采集、数据库/云存储、仪表盘开发与数据分析的编程；熟悉 Gemini、Perplexity、DeepSeek 三个 LLM 的 AI API 集成，包括多语言提示词路由。\n2) 预期成果：一台可工作的 AI 天气与 ESG 设备，集成五个传感器：温度、湿度、CO₂、O₂、CH₄；一个显示当前读数、传感器状态、趋势、告警与简洁 ESG/环境洞察的 5 英寸电子墨水\"实时\"仪表盘；一台连接三个 LLM 或视觉 AI 服务的 5 MP 相机，用于图像识别与环境场景描述；支持英语、粤语与普通话交互，使用 STT 进行语音输入、TTS 进行 AI 语音应答；一套完整数据工作流，保存带时间戳的传感器读数与图像、分析趋势、标记异常读数并生成 AI 摘要；配套 CAD 文件、接线图、源代码、物料清单、测试数据与最终演示。\n3) 现场频率：每周 1 天现场（3 小时），用于传感器安装、外壳开发、显示/相机集成与标定测试。","cats":["AI","CS","DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MESSAGE-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI就绪数字服务生态","titleEn":"AI-Ready Digital Service Ecosystem","areaZh":"AI；工程管理；AI搜索；SEO；服务设计；工作流自动化","areaEn":"AI; Engineering Management; AI Search; SEO; Service Design; Workflow Automation","companyZh":"Message In A Dish","companyEn":"Message In A Dish","companySupervisor":"Channing Lee","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-09 21:04:01","descriptionText":"1/ Project Background \nMessage In A Dish is a Hong Kong-based artist-led creative enterprise founded by artist, educator and theologian Channing Lee. The company creates personalised art experiences, customised gifts, workshops and storytelling-based creative encounters that transform memories, relationships and emotions into meaningful physical experiences.\nUnlike conventional e-commerce businesses, Message In A Dish delivers highly personalised creative services that require significant trust-building, customer communication and founder involvement throughout the customer journey. Each project involves consultation, storytelling, creative interpretation, quotation preparation, production, manual payment, workshop delivery and long-term relationship building.\nAs the business grows, the founder seeks to redesign the entire digital ecosystem so that discovery, enquiry, booking, payment, service delivery and post-experience engagement become more integrated, scalable and operationally efficient while preserving the personalised and human-centred nature of the business.\nThis project provides an opportunity to investigate how digital transformation, AI-assisted discovery, information architecture, workflow automation and service system design can support the sustainable growth of an independent artist-led creative enterprise.\nBeyond improving operational efficiency, the project will explore how a creative business can establish a stronger digital knowledge presence so that its services, founder profile and expertise are accurately represented across both traditional search engines and emerging AI-assisted discovery platforms.\n 2/ Project Aim \nThe project aims to design an integrated AI-ready digital service ecosystem that improves discoverability, customer experience, operational efficiency and long-term business sustainability for Message In A Dish.\nRather than focusing solely on website redesign, students are expected to examine the complete end-to-end service journey—from initial discovery through Google Search and AI-assisted discovery platforms, to enquiry, quotation, booking, payment, workshop participation, customer retention and referral.\nThe project should generate practical recommendations that are technically feasible, scalable and implementable within the operational constraints of a small artist-led enterprise.\n 3/ Research Objectives \nStudents are expected to investigate and propose practical solutions across the following four interconnected workstreams.\n[Workstream 1]\nAI-Assisted Discovery, Search Systems and Digital Knowledge Visibility\nEvaluate how an artist-led creative enterprise can improve discoverability across traditional search engines and emerging AI-assisted discovery platforms.\nPossible areas include:\n- SEO audit\n- Google Search optimisation\n- AI Search Optimisation (AEO)\n- Generative Engine Optimisation (GEO)\n- Google Business Profile optimisation\n- Local SEO\n- Search intent mapping\n- Structured data (Schema.org)\n- Entity-based SEO\n- Knowledge Graph optimisation\n- AI-readable content architecture\n- Knowledge representation for AI systems\n- Google Search Console\n- Google Analytics\n- AI discoverability across Google AI Overviews, ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude and Perplexity\n[Workstream 2]\nInformation Architecture, UX and Digital Experience Design\nReview the existing Wix website and propose a scalable digital architecture that supports customer experience, business growth and AI-assisted discovery.\nStudents may investigate:\n- Information architecture\n- Website navigation\n- UX/UI\n- Mobile-first experience\n- Content hierarchy\n- Semantic content organisation\n- Landing page strategy\n- Portfolio presentation\n- Service pages\n- Workshop booking experience\n- Conversion optimisation\n- Founder story architecture\n- FAQ architecture\n- CMS organisation\n- Accessibility\n- Feasible implementation within the Wix ecosystem\nThe proposed architecture should also support future expansion into the founder's wider artistic, educational and cultural practices.\n[Workstream 3]\nDigital Operations, Workflow Automation and Business Process Optimisation\nA major objective is to redesign the operational workflow currently used by the founder.\nStudents should examine how enquiries, quotations, workshop bookings, customer records, manual payments and follow-up communication can be connected into a more efficient digital workflow.\nPossible topics include:\n- Wix CMS\n- Wix Forms\n- Wix Automations\n- Wix Velo\n- Jotform\n- Airtable\n- Google Sheets\n- CRM\n- Low-code automation\n- API integration\n- Workflow automation\n- Internal dashboards\n- Booking management\n- Customer records\n- Knowledge management\n- AI-assisted administrative workflow\nThe project should evaluate whether Wix can become the primary integrated platform for website management, enquiry forms, booking workflow and content management, thereby reducing dependence on multiple third-party services.\nWhere native Wix functionality is insufficient, students may evaluate lightweight custom development, Wix Velo, APIs or suitable third-party integrations.\nRecommendations should improve operational efficiency while preserving personalised customer service.\n[Workstream 4] \nService System Design and Customer Experience Engineering\nAs a high-involvement creative service, Message In A Dish requires customers to make purchasing decisions under conditions of uncertainty, limited standardisation and extensive personalisation.\nStudents should evaluate how digital service systems can reduce uncertainty, improve customer confidence and create a coherent end-to-end customer experience.\nPossible areas include:\n- Customer journey mapping\n- Service blueprint design\n- Digital touchpoint design\n- Multi-channel communication architecture\n- Customer onboarding workflow\n- Trust signal design\n- Social proof mechanisms\n- WhatsApp Business workflow\n- Review management system\n- Online-to-offline service integration\n- QR code integration\n- Post-service engagement\n- Customer retention mechanisms\n- Cross-service recommendation strategy\n 4/ Special Local Context - Payment UX \nUnlike many international e-commerce businesses, customers in Hong Kong frequently prefer PayMe, FPS or bank transfer instead of credit card payments.\nStudents should redesign a frictionless manual payment workflow that minimises transaction costs while maintaining a professional customer experience.\nThe proposed workflow may include:\n- quotation approval\n- payment instructions\n- payment confirmation\n- receipt upload\n- booking confirmation\n- automated reminders\n- customer notifications\nThe objective is to improve customer confidence while reducing administrative workload.\n 5/ Optional Innovation Component \nStudents may explore how generative AI can assist, rather than replace, personalised creative services.\nPossible applications include:\n- AI-assisted enquiry intake\n- Story collection\n- Creative brief generation\n- Internal proposal drafting\n- Recommended follow-up questions\n- Customer segmentation\n- AI-ready knowledge base\n- Knowledge architecture for AI-assisted discovery\n- Prompt libraries for business operations\n- AI-assisted content generation\nThe emphasis should remain on supporting human creativity rather than replacing it.\n 6/ Expected Deliverables \nInstead of producing purely theoretical recommendations, students are encouraged to deliver implementable prototypes wherever feasible.\nExpected outcomes may include:\n1. Digital Transformation Strategy\n2. AI-Assisted Discovery, Google Search and Knowledge Visibility Audit\n3. Website Information Architecture\n4. SEO, AEO/GEO and Knowledge Graph Optimisation Framework\n5. UX/UI Improvement Recommendations\n6. Customer Journey Map\n7. Service Blueprint\n8. Business Process Analysis and Workflow Redesign\n9. Booking and Payment Experience Framework\n10. CRM and Knowledge Management Framework\n11. WhatsApp Business Workflow Design\n12. Implementation-ready Wix Architecture and Technical Recommendations\n13. Prototype designs or proof-of-concept implementations where feasible\n14. Prioritised implementation roadmap (Quick Wins, Medium-term and Long-term)\n15. AI-ready Content and Knowledge Architecture Guidelines\n16. Recommendations for future digital scaling\n 7/ Preferred Skills \nStudents with one or more of the following backgrounds are welcome:\n- Engineering Management\n- Digital Transformation\n- Artificial Intelligence\n- AI-assisted Discovery\n- Search Engine Optimisation (SEO)\n- Generative Engine Optimisation (GEO)\n- UX/UI Design\n- Human-Centred Design\n- Service Design\n- Information Systems\n- Business Process Optimisation\n- Workflow Automation\n- Low-code Development\n- CRM\n- Website Design\n- Data Analytics\n- Digital Strategy\nExperience with Wix, Wix Velo, Google Analytics, Google Search Console, Google Business Profile, Airtable or workflow automation tools would be advantageous but is not essential.\n 8/ Company Support \nThe company can provide students with access to:\n- Existing Wix website\n- Existing booking workflow\n- Existing enquiry forms\n- Google Analytics (if available)\n- Google Search Console (if available)\n- Google Business Profile\n- Social media channels\n- Anonymised customer enquiries\n- Brand assets\n- Founder interviews\n- Existing operational workflow\n- Workshop observations where appropriate\nStudents will receive regular feedback throughout the project and may validate recommendations within a live creative business environment.\n 9/ Project Significance \nThis project offers students a live industry case at the intersection of Engineering Management, Digital Transformation, AI-assisted discovery, Information Architecture, Service Design and Creative Entrepreneurship.\nRather than focusing solely on website redesign or digital marketing, the project investigates how an independent artist-led enterprise can design an integrated digital service ecosystem that enhances discoverability, customer experience, operational efficiency and long-term sustainability in an increasingly AI-assisted digital environment.\nThe project will produce practical and implementable recommendations while contributing broader insights into the digital transformation of small creative enterprises.","descZh":"1 项目背景\nMessage In A Dish 是一家总部位于香港、由艺术家主导的创意企业，由艺术家、教育工作者及神学家 Channing Lee 创立。该公司创造个性化的艺术体验、定制礼物、工作坊以及基于讲故事的创意互动，将记忆、关系与情感转化为有意义的实体体验。\n与传统电子商务企业不同，Message In A Dish 提供高度个性化的创意服务，在整个客户旅程中需要大量的信任构建、客户沟通以及创始人参与。每个项目都涉及咨询、讲故事、创意诠释、报价准备、制作、人工收款、工作坊交付以及长期关系建立。\n随着业务的发展，创始人希望重新设计整个数字生态系统，使发现、咨询、预订、支付、服务交付和体验后互动变得更加整合、可扩展且运营高效，同时保留该业务个性化和以人为本的特质。\n本项目提供了一个机会，用以研究数字化转型、AI 辅助的发现、信息架构、工作流自动化以及服务系统设计如何能够支持一家独立的、由艺术家主导的创意企业的可持续发展。\n除了提升运营效率之外，本项目还将探索一家创意企业如何建立更强的数字知识存在感，使其服务、创始人形象和专业知识能够在传统搜索引擎以及新兴的 AI 辅助发现平台上得到准确的呈现。\n2 项目目标\n本项目旨在为 Message In A Dish 设计一个整合的、AI 就绪的数字服务生态系统，以提升其可发现性、客户体验、运营效率和长期业务可持续性。\n学生不应仅仅关注网站改版，而应审视从通过 Google Search 和 AI 辅助发现平台进行初次发现，到咨询、报价、预订、支付、工作坊参与、客户留存和转介绍的完整端到端服务旅程。\n本项目应产出切实可行的建议，这些建议在技术上是可行的、可扩展的，并且能够在小型艺术家主导企业的运营约束下落地实施。\n3 研究目标\n学生应围绕以下四个相互关联的工作流进行调查并提出切实可行的解决方案。\n[工作流 1]\nAI 辅助的发现、搜索系统与数字知识可见性\n评估一家由艺术家主导的创意企业如何在传统搜索引擎和新兴的 AI 辅助发现平台上提升可发现性。\n可能的领域包括：\n- SEO 审计\n- Google Search 优化\n- AI 搜索优化（AEO）\n- 生成式引擎优化（GEO）\n- Google 商家资料（Google Business Profile）优化\n- 本地 SEO\n- 搜索意图映射\n- 结构化数据（Schema.org）\n- 基于实体的 SEO\n- 知识图谱优化\n- AI 可读的内容架构\n- 面向 AI 系统的知识表示\n- Google Search Console\n- Google Analytics\n- 在 Google AI Overviews、ChatGPT、Gemini、Claude 和 Perplexity 中的 AI 可发现性\n[工作流 2]\n信息架构、用户体验与数字体验设计\n审查现有 Wix 网站，并提出一个可扩展的数字架构，以支持客户体验、业务增长和 AI 辅助的发现。\n学生可以研究：\n- 信息架构\n- 网站导航\n- UX/UI\n- 移动优先体验\n- 内容层级\n- 语义化内容组织\n- 落地页策略\n- 作品集展示\n- 服务页面\n- 工作坊预订体验\n- 转化率优化\n- 创始人故事架构\n- FAQ 架构\n- CMS 组织\n- 无障碍访问\n- 在 Wix 生态系统内可行的实施\n所提出的架构还应支持未来向创始人更广泛的艺术、教育和文化实践扩展。\n[工作流 3]\n数字运营、工作流自动化与业务流程优化\n一个主要目标是重新设计创始人目前使用的运营工作流。\n学生应研究如何将咨询、报价、工作坊预订、客户记录、人工收款以及后续沟通连接成一个更高效的数字工作流。\n可能的主题包括：\n- Wix CMS\n- Wix 表单（Wix Forms）\n- Wix 自动化（Wix Automations）\n- Wix Velo\n- Jotform\n- Airtable\n- Google 表格（Google Sheets）\n- CRM\n- 低代码自动化\n- API 集成\n- 工作流自动化\n- 内部仪表盘\n- 预订管理\n- 客户记录\n- 知识管理\n- AI 辅助的行政工作流\n本项目应评估 Wix 是否能够成为网站管理、咨询表单、预订工作流和内容管理的主要整合平台，从而减少对多个第三方服务的依赖。\n在 Wix 原生功能不足的情况下，学生可以评估轻量级定制开发、Wix Velo、API 或合适的第三方集成。\n建议应在提升运营效率的同时保留个性化的客户服务。\n[工作流 4]\n服务系统设计与客户体验工程\n作为一项高度参与型的创意服务，Message In A Dish 要求客户在不确定性、标准化程度有限以及高度个性化的条件下做出购买决策。\n学生应评估数字服务系统如何能够减少不确定性、提升客户信心，并创造连贯的端到端客户体验。\n可能的领域包括：\n- 客户旅程地图\n- 服务蓝图设计\n- 数字触点设计\n- 多渠道沟通架构\n- 客户入门（onboarding）工作流\n- 信任信号设计\n- 社会证明机制\n- WhatsApp Business 工作流\n- 评价管理系统\n- 线上到线下（O2O）服务整合\n- 二维码（QR code）集成\n- 服务后互动\n- 客户留存机制\n- 跨服务推荐策略\n4 特殊本地情境——支付用户体验\n与许多国际电子商务企业不同，香港的客户通常更偏好 PayMe、FPS（转数快）或银行转账，而非信用卡支付。\n学生应重新设计一个顺畅无摩擦的人工收款工作流，在保持专业客户体验的同时最小化交易成本。\n所提出的工作流可以包括：\n- 报价确认\n- 付款说明\n- 付款确认\n- 收据上传\n- 预订确认\n- 自动提醒\n- 客户通知\n目标是提升客户信心，同时减少行政工作量。\n5 可选创新部分\n学生可以探索生成式 AI 如何能够辅助（而非取代）个性化的创意服务。\n可能的应用包括：\n- AI 辅助的咨询受理\n- 故事采集\n- 创意简报生成\n- 内部提案起草\n- 推荐的后续追问\n- 客户细分\n- AI 就绪的知识库\n- 面向 AI 辅助发现的知识架构\n- 面向业务运营的提示词库\n- AI 辅助的内容生成\n重点应始终放在支持人类的创造力上，而非取代它。\n6 预期交付成果\n学生不应只产出纯理论性的建议，而应在可行的情况下尽可能交付可实施的雏形（prototype）。\n预期成果可以包括：\n1. 数字化转型策略\n2. AI 辅助发现、Google Search 与知识可见性审计\n3. 网站信息架构\n4. SEO、AEO/GEO 与知识图谱优化框架\n5. UX/UI 改进建议\n6. 客户旅程地图\n7. 服务蓝图\n8. 业务流程分析与工作流重新设计\n9. 预订与支付体验框架\n10. CRM 与知识管理框架\n11. WhatsApp Business 工作流设计\n12. 可实施的 Wix 架构与技术建议\n13. 在可行情况下的雏形设计或概念验证实施\n14. 按优先级排序的实施路线图（速赢项、中期项和长期项）\n15. AI 就绪的内容与知识架构指南\n16. 面向未来数字规模扩展的建议\n7 优先技能\n欢迎具备以下一项或多项背景的学生：\n- 工程管理\n- 数字化转型\n- 人工智能\n- AI 辅助的发现\n- 搜索引擎优化（SEO）\n- 生成式引擎优化（GEO）\n- UX/UI 设计\n- 以人为本的设计\n- 服务设计\n- 信息系统\n- 业务流程优化\n- 工作流自动化\n- 低代码开发\n- CRM\n- 网站设计\n- 数据分析\n- 数字策略\n具有 Wix、Wix Velo、Google Analytics、Google Search Console、Google 商家资料（Google Business Profile）、Airtable 或工作流自动化工具方面的经验将是有利的，但并非必需。\n8 公司支持\n公司可以向学生提供以下内容的访问权限：\n- 现有 Wix 网站\n- 现有预订工作流\n- 现有咨询表单\n- Google Analytics（如可用）\n- Google Search Console（如可用）\n- Google 商家资料（Google Business Profile）\n- 社交媒体渠道\n- 匿名化的客户咨询\n- 品牌资产\n- 创始人访谈\n- 现有运营工作流\n- 在适当情况下的工作坊观察\n学生将在整个项目期间获得定期反馈，并可在真实的创意企业环境中验证建议。\n9 项目意义\n本项目为学生提供了一个位于工程管理、数字化转型、AI 辅助的发现、信息架构、服务设计与创意创业交叉领域的真实行业案例。\n本项目并非仅仅关注网站改版或数字营销，而是研究一家独立的艺术家主导企业如何能够设计一个整合的数字服务生态系统，从而在日益 AI 辅助的数字环境中提升可发现性、客户体验、运营效率和长期可持续性。\n本项目将产出切实可行且可实施的建议，同时为小型创意企业的数字化转型贡献更广泛的洞见。","cats":["AI","CTRL","DES","MGT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-FIBOCOM-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"智能体运行时与多智能体协作平台","titleEn":"Agent Runtime &amp; Multi-Agent Collaboration Platform","areaZh":"智能体","areaEn":"Agent","companyZh":"广和通","companyEn":"Fibocom","companySupervisor":"Crest.Li","univSupName":"Dr. ZHANG Cuiping","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Build a unified Agent Runtime platform to implement Agent lifecycle management, supporting a six-stage cycle of perception, thinking, planning, execution, observation, and reflection. Support automatic task decomposition, state management, context sharing, tool invocation, long-task recovery, exception handling, human intervention, and multi-Agent collaborative scheduling. Establish a unified communication protocol to enable collaborative completion of complex R&D tasks by Planning Agent, Development Agent, Review Agent, Testing Agent, and other roles, providing a unified operational foundation for all enterprise AI Agents.\nTechnical Metrics:\n• Support 100+ Agent instances running simultaneously\n• Support no fewer than 10 Agent roles\n• Average Agent scheduling latency ≤ 100 ms\n• Long-task checkpoint recovery success rate ≥ 95%\n• Multi-Agent collaboration success rate ≥ 90%\n• Provide complete SDK and development documentation","descZh":"构建统一的 Agent 运行时（Agent Runtime）平台，实现 Agent 生命周期管理，支持\"感知、思考、规划、执行、观察、反思\"六阶段循环。支持自动任务分解、状态管理、上下文共享、工具调用、长任务恢复、异常处理、人工介入与多 Agent 协作调度。建立统一通信协议，使规划 Agent、开发 Agent、评审 Agent、测试 Agent 等角色协同完成复杂研发任务，为企业全部 AI Agent 提供统一的运行基础。\n技术指标：\n• 支持 100+ 个 Agent 实例同时运行\n• 支持不少于 10 种 Agent 角色\n• Agent 平均调度时延 ≤ 100 ms\n• 长任务断点恢复成功率 ≥ 95%\n• 多 Agent 协作成功率 ≥ 90%\n• 提供完整 SDK 与开发文档","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-FIBOCOM-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"智能体安全与多租户管理","titleEn":"Agent Security &amp; Multi-Tenant Management","areaZh":"智能体","areaEn":"Agent","companyZh":"广和通","companyEn":"Fibocom","companySupervisor":"Crest.Li","univSupName":"Dr. ZHANG Cuiping","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Build an enterprise-grade Agent security platform implementing unified identity authentication, RBAC permission management, multi-tenant isolation, model permission management, tool permission management, knowledge base permission management, code sandboxing, prompt attack protection, data desensitization, security auditing, behavior tracking, and risk interception. Support independent Agent deployment by different departments, projects, and teams to achieve enterprise-level security management.\nTechnical Metrics:\n• Support 100+ user management\n• Support three-tier permissions: organization, role, and tenant\n• Prompt attack detection rate ≥ 95%\n• 100% audit trail for all operations\n• Zero data leakage in data isolation\n• Pass all security vulnerability scans","descZh":"构建一个企业级的智能体（Agent）安全平台，实现统一身份认证、RBAC 权限管理、多租户隔离、模型权限管理、工具权限管理、知识库权限管理、代码沙箱化、提示词攻击防护、数据脱敏、安全审计、行为跟踪和风险拦截。支持不同部门、项目和团队独立部署 Agent，以实现企业级的安全管理。\n技术指标：\n• 支持 100+ 用户管理\n• 支持组织、角色和租户三层权限\n• 提示词攻击检测率 ≥ 95%\n• 对所有操作实现 100% 审计追踪\n• 数据隔离中实现零数据泄露\n• 通过所有安全漏洞扫描","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-FIBOCOM-3","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"技能中心与智能体能力生态","titleEn":"Skill Hub &amp; Agent Capability Ecosystem","areaZh":"智能体","areaEn":"Agent","companyZh":"广和通","companyEn":"Fibocom","companySupervisor":"Crest.Li","univSupName":"Dr. ZHANG Cuiping","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Build an enterprise Skill Hub platform implementing Skill development, publishing, review, installation, version management, dependency management, security review, quality scoring, statistical analysis, and marketplace management. Support automatic Skill composition to generate workflows, and support automatic generation of Skills and Agents from individual knowledge, forming an enterprise capability-sharing platform.\nTechnical Metrics:\n• Support 500+ Skill management\n• Skill installation time ≤ 10 seconds\n• Skill version rollback supported\n• Department-level statistical analysis supported\n• Skill invocation success rate ≥ 99%\n• Support automated testing and security review","descZh":"构建企业级 Skill Hub 平台，实现技能（Skill）的开发、发布、评审、安装、版本管理、依赖管理、安全评审、质量评分、统计分析与应用市场管理。支持技能自动编排以生成工作流，支持从个人知识自动生成 Skills 与 Agents，形成企业能力共享平台。\n技术指标：\n• 支持 500+ 技能管理\n• 技能安装时间 ≤ 10 秒\n• 支持技能版本回滚\n• 支持部门级统计分析\n• 技能调用成功率 ≥ 99%\n• 支持自动化测试与安全评审","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-FIBOCOM-4","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI研发智能体平台（全周期研发智能体平台）","titleEn":"AI R&amp;D Agent Platform (Full-Cycle R&amp;D Agent Platform)","areaZh":"智能体","areaEn":"Agent","companyZh":"广和通","companyEn":"Fibocom","companySupervisor":"Crest.Li","univSupName":"Dr. ZHANG Cuiping","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Build an AI R&D platform enabling full-cycle Agent collaboration across requirements analysis, architecture design, code generation, code modification, compilation & build, log analysis, automated code review, automated testing, and Git commit. Integrate capabilities such as CloudCode to achieve R&D process automation and improve R&D efficiency.\nTechnical Metrics:\n• Support automatic Git repository integration\n• Single-file code generation accuracy ≥ 90%\n• Automated review coverage ≥ 95%\n• Automated compilation success rate ≥ 100%\n• Automated testing pass rate ≥ 90%\n• R&D efficiency improvement of more than 30%","descZh":"构建一个 AI 研发平台，支持需求分析、架构设计、代码生成、代码修改、编译构建、日志分析、自动化代码评审、自动化测试与 Git 提交等全周期 Agent 协作。集成 CloudCode 等能力，实现研发流程自动化并提升研发效率。\n技术指标：\n• 支持 Git 仓库自动集成\n• 单文件代码生成准确率 ≥ 90%\n• 自动化评审覆盖率 ≥ 95%\n• 自动化编译成功率 ≥ 100%（原文如此）\n• 自动化测试通过率 ≥ 90%\n• 研发效率提升超过 30%","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-FIBOCOM-5","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"研发流程自动化智能体","titleEn":"R&amp;D Process Automation Agent","areaZh":"智能体","areaEn":"Agent","companyZh":"广和通","companyEn":"Fibocom","companySupervisor":"Crest.Li","univSupName":"Dr. ZHANG Cuiping","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Build an R&D process automation Agent to implement requirements checking, issue ticket tracking, automatic version number updates, automated compilation, compilation verification, automatic Release Note generation, self-test report aggregation, automatic test submission, and workflow approval, achieving automated R&D delivery.\nTechnical Metrics:\n• Automatically complete more than 90% of the release process\n• Release Note auto-generation rate: 100%\n• Automated issue ticket completion check rate: 100%\n• Automated compilation success rate ≥ 95%\n• Reduce handoff-to-testing time by 50%","descZh":"构建一个研发流程自动化 Agent，实现需求校验、问题单（issue ticket）跟踪、版本号自动更新、自动化编译、编译验证、自动生成 Release Note、自测报告汇总、测试自动提交与流程审批，达成研发交付自动化。\n技术指标：\n• 自动完成超过 90% 的发布流程\n• Release Note 自动生成率：100%\n• 问题单完成校验自动化率：100%\n• 自动化编译成功率 ≥ 95%\n• 交付测试交接时间降低 50%","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZOOMVISI-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"算法工程师（决策调度）","titleEn":"Algorithm Engineer (Decision Scheduling)","areaZh":"算法","areaEn":"Algorithm","companyZh":"Zoomvision Technology (Shenzhen) Co., Ltd.","companyEn":"Zoomvision Technology (Shenzhen) Co., Ltd.","companySupervisor":"Tmac WANG","univSupName":"Dr. ZHANG Cuiping","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"About Us\nWe are a smart wearable optics company. Our product is in mass production and in the hands of real users. We are now iterating on the device's core decision-making algorithms using large-scale real-world usage data, so that the device responds accurately and naturally across different users and scenarios.\nProject Background\nFocus adjustment is fully automatic: no buttons, no gestures, no conscious input from the wearer. The device continuously reads several onboard sensors, infers moment to moment whether the wearer is looking near or far, and drives the optical system to change focus accordingly.\nThe product is already in mass production and worn by real users; the optics and hardware pipeline work. This project is therefore not about proving the concept — it is about improving the strategy that decides when focus should change. That strategy is what determines whether a wearer comes to rely on the glasses or takes them off after a week.\nFour things make the strategy hard, and they form the substance of the project:\n1、Errors are felt immediately — a focus change that should have happened and didn't leaves the wearer squinting; one that fires when it shouldn't is worse, blurring the world for no reason. In the user's perception there is no such thing as \"slightly wrong.\"\n2、Acting has a real cost — every focus change consumes power and takes physical settling time. The strategy cannot react to every sensor fluctuation; it must judge whether a change is worth making.\n3、Modules conflict — several algorithm modules produce judgments in parallel from different signal sources, and they routinely disagree. Something has to fuse them and arbitrate into a single decision.\n4、The budget is tight — everything runs on-device, within a wearable's compute and power envelope, under hard real-time constraints.\nYou will have access to real-world usage data returning from production devices, the existing algorithm codebase, and test equipment, and will work directly with the algorithm engineers who own the module.\nProject Objectives\nBy the end of the project, you are expected to have produced:\n1、A failure-mode analysis — working from real-world usage data, characterising where and why today's decisions go wrong: which scenarios, which signal patterns.\n2、An improved decision scheduling strategy — with the problem properly modelled (control-theoretic, state-machine-based, learning-based, or a hybrid; the choice is yours to argue for), covering both when to act and by how much and along what transition curve.\n3、A fusion and arbitration mechanism — defining how conflicting module outputs resolve into a single stable decision that feels natural rather than merely metric-optimal.\n4、An offline simulation and evaluation framework — replaying real data through candidate strategies so that any change can be quantified before it touches hardware, together with the evaluation set you construct.\n5、A written report documenting the method, experiments, quantified comparison against the production baseline, and on-device results — suitable for submission as your dissertation or project report.\nObjectives 1–3 are the core; 4 and 5 accompany them. The scope can be adjusted with your supervisor to match your programme's requirements.\nWhat You'll Do\n1. Strategy modelling and simulation\nMine bad cases from returning field data; trace root causes, reproduce them, and classify them into the few failure modes that dominate the experience problem.\nBuild and implement the decision scheduling model, covering trigger timing, action magnitude, and transition curve.\nDesign the fusion and priority-arbitration logic across modules, handling signal conflicts and edge cases.\nBuild the offline simulation and evaluation framework, and define quantitative metrics that actually track subjective experience, enabling comparison across strategy revisions.\n2. On-device validation and iteration\nDeploy your strategy onto physical glasses and validate it under real wearing conditions.\nRun comparative testing against the production baseline and quantify the improvement on key metrics.\nDiagnose discrepancies between on-device behaviour and simulation predictions, and feed the conclusions back into the model.\nParticipate in trade-off analysis across on-device compute, power, and real-time constraints, confirming the strategy fits within the device budget.\nWho This Suits\n1、Master's student in Automation / Control, Computer Science / AI, or a related field.\n2、Solid grounding in at least one of: control theory and closed-loop system design, state machines and decision logic modelling, filtering and state estimation, time-series modelling, or multi-source information fusion.\n3、Strong programming fundamentals and engineering discipline (no specific language or framework required) — able to take an algorithm from modelling through simulation to validation independently.\n4、Strong problem decomposition: given a vague complaint that \"it doesn't feel right,\" you can narrow it to a specific stage, parameter, or class of input.\n5、Comfortable working directly with hardware, mechanical, and optical teams, and with the constraint that a strategy must run within a real device's compute and power budget.\nNice to Have\n1、Algorithm work deployed on real-time embedded systems — wearables, robotics, autonomous driving, or drones.\n2、Experience with human-in-the-loop systems, where the output is directly perceived by a person and metric optimality is not the same as a good experience.\n3、Having taken an algorithm from simulation to real hardware and corrected the model against measured data.\n4、Experience cleaning and labelling real-world data and constructing evaluation sets.\n5、Publications, patents, competitions, or open-source work in a related area.\nWorking Arrangement\n1、Remote-first. Data analysis, strategy modelling, simulation, and evaluation are all done remotely. There is no fixed number of office days.\n2、On-site as the project requires. You come in when the work genuinely needs the hardware — on-device integration, measurement, and design reviews — roughly 3 to 5 times across the project. These sessions are scheduled with you in advance rather than imposed on a weekly cadence, and typically cluster into short, concentrated blocks. On-site work takes place at our Shenzhen office or a Hong Kong meeting point.\n3、Timeline follows your programme's project schedule.\nThe project is supervised directly by our algorithm engineering team, with a named contact responsible for data, device, and technical support. Your work feeds into the actual product rather than a separate exercise.","descZh":"关于我们：我们是一家智能可穿戴光学公司。我们的产品已量产并交付真实用户使用。我们正利用大规模真实使用数据迭代设备的核心决策算法，使设备在不同用户与场景下准确、自然地响应。\n项目背景：对焦调节完全自动：无需按钮、手势或佩戴者的有意输入。设备持续读取多个机载传感器，实时推断佩戴者是在看近还是看远，并相应驱动光学系统改变焦点。\n产品已量产并被真实用户佩戴，光学与硬件链路工作正常。因此本项目不是证明概念——而是改进决定\"何时应变焦\"的策略。该策略决定了佩戴者会依赖这副眼镜，还是一周后将其摘下。\n四件事使该策略变得困难，构成本项目的主要内容：\n1. 错误会被立即感知——本应变焦而未变焦会让佩戴者眯眼；不应触发却触发更糟，无端模糊世界。在用户感知中不存在\"稍微不对\"这回事。\n2. 动作有真实成本——每次变焦都消耗电力并需物理稳定时间。策略不能对每次传感器波动都反应，必须判断一次变焦是否值得。\n3. 模块冲突——多个算法模块并行从不同信号源产生判断，且经常相互分歧。必须有某种机制融合它们并将仲裁为一个决策。\n4. 预算紧张——一切都须在设备上运行，处于可穿戴设备的计算与功耗包络内，且满足严格实时约束。\n你将可以使用从量产设备返回的真实使用数据、现有算法代码库与测试设备，并直接与拥有该模块的算法工程师共事。\n项目目标：项目结束时，你预期产出：\n1. 失效模式分析——基于真实使用数据，刻画当今决策在哪里与为何出错：哪些场景、哪些信号模式；\n2. 改进的决策调度策略——对问题作恰当建模（控制理论、状态机、基于学习或混合方式，由你论证选择），覆盖何时行动、行动幅度与沿何种过渡曲线；\n3. 融合与仲裁机制——定义冲突模块输出如何化解为单一稳定决策，使感觉自然而不仅是指标最优；\n4. 离线仿真与评估框架——将真实数据回放通过候选策略，使任何改动在触碰硬件前即可量化，连同你构建的评估集；\n5. 书面报告——记录方法、实验、与生产基线的量化对比及设备端结果，适合作为论文或项目报告提交。\n目标 1-3 为核心，4 与 5 为辅。范围可与导师调整，以匹配你项目的课程要求。\n你将做什么：\n1. 策略建模与仿真：从返回的现场数据中挖掘糟糕案例；追溯根因、复现并归为少数主导体验问题的失效模式；构建并实现决策调度模型，覆盖触发时机、动作幅度与过渡曲线；设计跨模块的融合与优先级仲裁逻辑，处理信号冲突与边界情形；构建离线仿真与评估框架，并定义真正追踪主观体验的定量指标，支持跨策略版本比较。\n2. 设备端验证与迭代：将策略部署到实体眼镜上，在真实佩戴条件下验证；对照生产基线运行对比测试，量化关键指标改进；诊断设备端行为与仿真预测之间的差异，将结论反馈到模型中；参与设备端计算、功耗与实时约束的权衡分析，确认策略适配设备预算。\n适合人群：\n1. 自动化/控制、计算机科学/AI 或相关领域的硕士生；\n2. 至少扎实掌握以下之一：控制理论与闭环系统设计、状态机与决策逻辑建模、滤波与状态估计、时间序列建模或多源信息融合；\n3. 扎实的编程基础与工程纪律（不限特定语言或框架），能独立将算法从建模经仿真推进到验证；\n4. 出色的问题分解能力：面对\"感觉不对\"的模糊抱怨，能将其缩小到特定阶段、参数或输入类别；\n5. 能自如地与硬件、机械与光学团队直接协作，并接受\"策略必须运行在真实设备的计算与功耗预算内\"这一约束。\n加分项：\n1. 有在实时嵌入式系统（可穿戴、机器人、自动驾驶或无人机）上部署算法工作的经验；\n2. 有人机在环系统经验，深知输出直接被人类感知时，指标最优不等于体验好；\n3. 曾将算法从仿真带到真实硬件并用实测数据校正模型；\n4. 有清洗与标注真实数据并构建评估集的经验；\n5. 有相关领域的论文、专利、竞赛或开源工作。\n工作安排：\n1. 远程优先。数据分析、策略建模、仿真与评估均远程完成，无固定办公室天数；\n2. 按项目需要现场。当工作确实需要硬件（设备端集成、测量与设计评审）时你到场——整个项目约 3~5 次。这些场次会提前与你约定而非强加固定周频率，并通常集中为短而密集的时段。现场工作在我们的深圳办公室或香港会面点进行；\n3. 时间线遵循你课程的项目安排。\n本项目由我们的算法工程团队直接指导，并有指定联系人负责数据、设备与技术支持。你的工作将输入真实产品而非独立练习。","cats":["CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZOOMVISI-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"光学工程师","titleEn":"Optical Engineer","areaZh":"智能变焦眼镜的光学设计优化","areaEn":"Optical Design Optimization for Smart Varifocal Eyewear","companyZh":"Zoomvision Technology (Shenzhen) Co., Ltd.","companyEn":"Zoomvision Technology (Shenzhen) Co., Ltd.","companySupervisor":"Tmac WANG","univSupName":"Dr. ZHANG Cuiping","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"About Us\nWe are a smart wearable optics company. Our product is a pair of smart varifocal glasses that adjusts its optical power automatically for the wearer, removing the need to switch between separate pairs of prescription lenses. It is a real hardware programme in active development, and this project sits inside it.\nProject Background\nThe glasses change optical power by mechanically shifting a pair of freeform optical elements relative to each other. A working prototype already exists and delivers the intended zoom range, so this project is not about proving the concept — it is about closing the gap between the optical performance on paper and what the assembled product actually delivers.\nThat gap comes from two sources, and they form the substance of the project:\n1、Surface figure vs. manufacturing tolerance — how far the manufactured optical elements deviate from the designed surface, and how sensitive image quality is to that deviation.\n2、Displacement accuracy and assembly alignment — how relative displacement error, decenter, and tilt between the two elements degrade image quality across the zoom range.\nYou will have access to the existing prototype, its design files, and the company's optical test equipment, and will work alongside the optical, mechanical, and process engineers who built it.\nProject Objectives\nBy the end of the project, you are expected to have produced:\n1、A validated simulation model of the varifocal optical system that reproduces the measured performance of the current prototype across the full zoom range.\n2、A tolerance and sensitivity analysis ranking surface-figure parameters, displacement error, decenter, and tilt by their impact on image quality, identifying the few that dominate.\n3、A tolerance budget that translates the above into actionable limits for manufacturing and assembly.\n4、A measurement dataset and test method covering the full zoom range, reusable for comparing future prototype revisions.\n5、A written report documenting the method, results, and recommended design changes — suitable for submission as your dissertation or project report.\nObjectives 1–3 are the core; 4 and 5 accompany them. The scope can be adjusted with your supervisor to match your programme's requirements.\nWhat You'll Do\n1. Optical design and simulation\nBuild and maintain the simulation model of the varifocal optical system, and evaluate imaging performance across the full zoom range.\nPerform tolerance and sensitivity analysis on the freeform surfaces, identifying the surface parameters and manufacturing deviations that dominate image-quality degradation.\nEstablish quantitative relationships between displacement error, decenter/tilt, and aberrations, and produce a tolerance budget that guides assembly and mechanical design.\nParticipate in design iterations and assess manufacturability together with the mechanical and process teams.\n2. Optical testing and characterization\nSet up and maintain test benches, and measure MTF, wavefront, distortion, transmittance, and related metrics.\nInspect surface figure on manufactured parts and analyze deviations against design values.\nDefine test procedures and data-recording conventions covering the full zoom range, enabling comparison across prototype revisions.\nDiagnose discrepancies between measured prototype performance and simulation predictions, and feed the conclusions back into the design.\nWho This Suits\n1、Master's student in Optical Engineering, Optoelectronics, Physics, Precision Instruments, or a related field.\n2、Solid grounding in geometrical optics and aberration theory, with a working understanding of MTF, wavefront error, and tolerance analysis.\n3、Able to independently model and optimize in at least one optical design package (Zemax / CODE V or equivalent).\n4、Hands-on experience building optical setups and operating measurement instruments, or a strong willingness to do experimental work.\n5、Basic data-processing skills (Python / MATLAB for batch simulation, curve fitting, and visualization).\n6、Comfortable working directly with mechanical, process, and algorithm teams, and with the constraint that a design must end up as a manufacturable part.\nNice to Have\n1、Experience designing or analyzing freeform optics (freeform surfaces, off-axis systems).\n2、Familiarity with injection-molded / molded optics and their typical surface-figure defects.\n3、Background in visual optical systems, eyewear, head-mounted displays, or optometry.\n4、Experience operating interferometers, profilometers, or MTF test stations.\n5、Experience automating simulation via Zemax ZOS-API or macro scripting.\nWorking Arrangement\n1、Remote-first. Simulation, tolerance analysis, and data work are all done remotely. There is no fixed number of office days.\n2、On-site as the project requires. You come in when the work genuinely needs the hardware — prototype measurement, bench setup, and design reviews. These sessions are scheduled with you in advance rather than imposed on a weekly cadence, and typically cluster into short, concentrated blocks.\n3、Timeline follows your programme's project schedule.\nThe project is supervised directly by our optical engineering team, and your work feeds into the actual product rather than a separate exercise.","descZh":"关于我们：我们是一家智能可穿戴光学公司。我们的产品是一副智能变焦眼镜，可为佩戴者自动调节光学度数，无需在不同处方式镜片之间切换。这是一项正在积极开发中的真实硬件项目，本项目是其一部分。\n项目背景：眼镜通过机械方式相对移动一对自由曲面光学元件来改变光学度数。可工作的原型已存在并达到预期变焦范围，因此本项目不是证明概念——而是要弥合\"纸上光学性能\"与\"组装后产品实际表现\"之间的差距。\n这一差距来自两个来源，构成本项目的主要内容：\n1. 面形与制造公差——制造出的光学元件与设计表面的偏差有多大，图像质量对该偏差有多敏感；\n2. 位移精度与装配对准——两元件间的相对位移误差、偏心与倾斜如何在变焦范围内降低图像质量。\n你将可以使用现有原型、其设计文件与公司的光学测试设备，并与打造它的光学、机械与工艺工程师并肩工作。\n项目目标：项目结束时，你预期产出：\n1. 一个经验证的变焦光学系统仿真模型，能在整个变焦范围复现当前原型的实测性能；\n2. 公差与灵敏度分析，按对图像质量的影响对表面面形参数、位移误差、偏心与倾斜排序，识别少数主导因素；\n3. 将上述转化为制造与装配可执行限值的公差预算；\n4. 覆盖整个变焦范围的测量数据集与测试方法，可复用于比较未来原型版本；\n5. 记录方法、结果与建议设计变更的书面报告，适合作为论文或项目报告提交。\n目标 1-3 为核心，4 与 5 为辅。范围可与导师调整，以匹配你项目的课程要求。\n你将做什么：\n1. 光学设计与仿真：构建并维护变焦光学系统的仿真模型，评估整个变焦范围的成像性能；对自由曲面开展公差与灵敏度分析，识别主导图像质量退化的表面参数与制造偏差；建立位移误差、偏心/倾斜与像差之间的定量关系，并产出指导装配与机械设计的公差预算；参与设计迭代，与机械与工艺团队共同评估可制造性。\n2. 光学测试与表征：搭建并维护测试台架，测量 MTF、波前、畸变、透射率及相关指标；检查制造件的表面面形并对照设计值分析偏差；定义覆盖整个变焦范围的测试程序与数据记录规范，支持跨原型版本比较；诊断原型实测性能与仿真预测之间的差异，并将结论反馈到设计中。\n适合人群：\n1. 光学工程、光电子、物理、精密仪器或相关领域的硕士生；\n2. 扎实的几何光学与像差理论基础，对 MTF、波前误差与公差分析有实用理解；\n3. 能至少在一个光学设计软件包（Zemax/CODE V 或等效）中独立建模与优化；\n4. 有搭建光学装置与操作测量仪器的动手经验，或有强烈意愿做实验工作；\n5. 基础数据处理技能（Python/MATLAB 用于批量仿真、曲线拟合与可视化）；\n6. 能自如地与机械、工艺与算法团队直接协作，并接受\"设计最终必须成为可制造零件\"这一约束。\n加分项：\n1. 有自由曲面光学（自由曲面、离轴系统）设计或分析经验；\n2. 熟悉注塑/模压光学件及其典型面形缺陷；\n3. 有目视光学系统、眼镜、头戴显示或验光背景；\n4. 有操作干涉仪、轮廓仪或 MTF 测试台经验；\n5. 有用 Zemax ZOS-API 或宏脚本自动化仿真的经验。\n工作安排：\n1. 远程优先。仿真、公差分析与数据处理均远程完成，无固定办公室天数；\n2. 按项目需要现场。当工作确实需要硬件（原型测量、台架搭建与设计评审）时你到场。这些场次会提前与你约定，而非强加固定周频率，并通常集中为短而密集的时段；\n3. 时间线遵循你课程的项目安排。\n本项目由我们的光学工程团队直接指导，你的工作将输入真实产品而非独立练习。","cats":["CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-HKSTP-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"面向快速诊断的先进微流控POCT试剂盒设计与制造优化","titleEn":"Advanced Microfluidic POCT Cartridge Design and Manufacturing Optimization for Rapid Diagnostics","areaZh":"创新与设计；生物医学工程；先进制造","areaEn":"Innovation and design; biomedical engineering; advanced manufacturing","companyZh":"Orange Biotech Limited","companyEn":"Orange Biotech Limited","companySupervisor":"Dr Chris Chan","univSupName":"Professor CHOW Philip Chi Yung","moderator":"","capacity":6,"availCap":6,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Orange Biotech Limited is offering an intensive, hands-on internship experience at the Hong Kong Science Park. As an MSc student, you will join our multidisciplinary R&D team to bridge the gap between theoretical mechanical engineering and the commercial realization of Point-of-Care Testing (POCT) devices. This project focuses on the end-to-end development of microfluidic diagnostic platforms. You will be involved in the design, simulation, and prototyping of intricate device components. A core component of this project is applying Design for Manufacturing (DFM) principles to transition prototypes into mass-producible plastic parts using injection molding techniques. You will also assist in the rigorous testing of fluidic, thermal, and optical subsystems to ensure clinical-grade performance.\nRequirements:\n • Currently studying Mechanical Engineering or a related discipline.\n • Proficient with CAD software such as SolidWorks.\n • Good practical problem-solving skills and willingness to work hands-on.\n • Good communication and ability to work independently on assigned tasks.\nAdvantageous:\n • Previous industrial experience (especially in relevant areas).\n • Experience with manufacturing, plastic part design, or injection molding.\n • Knowledge of optics, microfluidics, medical devices, or instrumentation.\n • Interest in pursuing a career in medical devices, biotechnology, diagnostics, or related industries.\nWhat We Offer:\n • Practical experience in real medical-device product development.\n • Exposure to engineering, manufacturing, microfluidics, optics, and diagnostic technologies.\n • Opportunity to work within a growing biotechnology company\nLocation:\n • This is an onsite position located at the Hong Kong Science Park.","descZh":"Orange Biotech Limited 正在香港科学园提供高强度、动手实践的实习机会。作为硕士生，你将加入我们多学科研发团队，弥合理论机械工程与即时检测（POCT）器件商业实现之间的差距。本项目聚焦微流控诊断平台的端到端开发。你将参与复杂器件部件的设计、仿真与原型制作。项目核心之一是应用面向制造的设计（DFM）原则，通过注塑工艺将原型转化为可批量生产的塑料部件。你还将协助对流体、热与光学子系统开展严格测试，确保达到临床级性能。\n要求：\n• 目前攻读机械工程或相关学科；\n• 熟练使用 SolidWorks 等 CAD 软件；\n• 良好的实际问题解决能力，愿意动手实践；\n• 良好的沟通能力，能独立完成分配任务。\n加分项：\n• 有工业经验（尤其在相关领域）；\n• 有制造、塑料件设计或注塑经验；\n• 了解光学、微流控、医疗器械或仪器；\n• 有志于在医疗器械、生物技术、诊断或相关行业从事职业。\n我们能提供：\n• 真实的医疗器械产品开发实践经验；\n• 接触工程、制造、微流控、光学与诊断技术；\n• 在成长中的生物技术公司工作的机会。\n地点：本岗位位于香港科学园，需现场办公。","cats":["BIO","DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-GEM-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"基于深度学习的退役电池视觉缺陷检测与分选模型研究","titleEn":"Research on Vision&#8209;Based Defect Detection and Sorting Model for Retired Batteries Using Deep Learning","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"武汉动力电池再生技术有限公司","companyEn":"Wuhan Power Battery Recycling Technology Co., Ltd.","companySupervisor":"Yuping Zhang","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"After disassembly, retired power batteries require visual inspection and initial health screening. Currently, this process relies on manual visual inspection, which is inefficient and lacks standardised criteria. This study aims to develop a deep‑learning‑based defect detection model for battery appearance, capable of automatically identifying typical surface defects such as corrosion, scratches, dents, and swelling. The student will collect battery sample images, build an annotated dataset, train lightweight classification/detection models (e.g., YOLO), evaluate the model’s recognition accuracy under actual production-line lighting conditions, and output deployable model weights and inference code.\nRequired Skills: Python programming, basic knowledge of PyTorch or TensorFlow, fundamental concepts of image processing\nExpected Deliverables: One annotated dataset, one trained defect detection model (accuracy ≥ 95%), one set of model deployment and inference code, and one technical report\nOn-site Frequency: 5 days per week for 3 months","descZh":"退役动力电池在拆解后需进行外观检测与初步健康筛查。目前该过程依赖人工目视检查，效率低且缺乏标准化判据。本研究旨在开发基于深度学习的电池外观缺陷检测模型，可自动识别锈蚀、划痕、凹坑、鼓包等典型表面缺陷。学生将采集电池样本图像、构建带标注数据集、训练轻量化分类/检测模型（如 YOLO），在实际产线光照条件下评估模型识别精度，并输出可部署的模型权重与推理代码。\n必备技能：Python 编程、PyTorch 或 TensorFlow 基础知识、图像处理基本概念。\n预期交付：一套带标注数据集、一个训练好的缺陷检测模型（精度 ≥ 95%）、一套模型部署与推理代码、一份技术报告。\n现场频率：每周 5 天，为期 3 个月。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-GEM-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"面向拆解部件智能分选的末端执行器夹具设计与抓取策略优化","titleEn":"Design of End-Effector Grippers and Optimisation of Grasping Strategies for Intelligent Sorting of Disassembled Components","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"武汉动力电池再生技术有限公司","companyEn":"Wuhan Power Battery Recycling Technology Co., Ltd.","companySupervisor":"Yuping Zhang","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"After disassembly of retired battery packs, a wide variety of components are produced (copper busbars of different specifications, wire harnesses, connectors, module housings, etc.). The grippers of existing parallel‑robot sorting systems have limited adaptability to components of varying shapes and materials, and the grasping success rate needs improvement. This study focuses on 3-5 typical disassembled components, designing an adaptive flexible end‑effector gripper. Combined with AI-based visual recognition results, the project will optimise grasping pose and force control strategies. The student will complete 3D modelling and prototyping of the gripper, collect and analyse experimental grasping data, and propose an optimised grasping strategy.\nRequired Skills: Basic knowledge of SolidWorks or similar CAD software, fundamental robotics or mechanics concepts, hands‑on experimental ability\nExpected Deliverables: One set of 3D design drawings for the flexible gripper, one prototype, one experimental data and analysis report, and one proposal for grasping strategy optimisation\nOn-site Frequency: 5 days per week for 3 months","descZh":"退役电池组拆解后会产生多种部件（不同规格的铜排、线束、连接器、模组外壳等）。现有并联机器人分拣系统的夹爪对不同形状与材料部件的适应性有限，抓取成功率有待提高。本研究聚焦 3~5 种典型拆解部件，设计一种自适应柔性末端夹爪；结合基于 AI 的视觉识别结果，优化抓取姿态与力控制策略。学生将完成夹爪的三维建模与原型制作，采集并分析抓取实验数据，并提出优化后的抓取策略。\n必备技能：SolidWorks 或类似 CAD 软件基础知识、基础机器人学或力学概念、动手实验能力。\n预期交付：柔性夹爪 3D 设计图纸一套、原型一个、实验数据与分析报告一份、抓取策略优化方案一份。\n现场频率：每周 5 天，为期 3 个月。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-GEM-3","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"基于数字孪生的电池拆解线可视化监控界面原型设计","titleEn":"Prototype Design of a Visualisation Monitoring Interface for Battery Disassembly Lines Based on Digital Twin","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"武汉动力电池再生技术有限公司","companyEn":"Wuhan Power Battery Recycling Technology Co., Ltd.","companySupervisor":"Yuping Zhang","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Our intelligent disassembly line has already deployed information systems such as MES/WMS, but currently lacks an intuitive 3D visualisation monitoring interface, making it difficult for managers to perceive the line’s operational status in real time. This study aims to design a prototype visualisation monitoring interface for the disassembly line based on the digital twin concept. Using the line layout and equipment operation data, the student will build a digital model of the production line with a 3D engine (Unity or UE), design visualisation methods for key information such as equipment status, production progress, and fault alarms, and develop an interactive prototype that can interface with backend data.\nRequired Skills: Basic knowledge of Unity or UE, fundamental 3D modelling, UI/UX design concepts, basic ability to interface with data\nExpected Deliverables: One operational prototype of the 3D visualisation monitoring interface (with a live demo), one interface design document, and one user manual\nOn-site Frequency: 5 days per week for 3 months","descZh":"我们的智能拆解线已部署 MES/WMS 等信息系统，但目前缺少直观的三维可视化监控界面，管理者难以实时感知产线运行状态。本研究旨在基于数字孪生理念，为拆解线设计一个可视化监控界面原型。学生将利用产线布局与设备运行数据，使用三维引擎（Unity 或 UE）构建产线的数字模型，设计设备状态、生产进度、故障告警等关键信息的可视化方式，并开发可对接后端数据的交互原型。\n必备技能：Unity 或 UE 基础知识、基础三维建模、UI/UX 设计概念、基本的数据对接能力。\n预期交付：一个可运行的三维可视化监控界面原型（含现场演示）、一份界面设计文档、一份用户手册。\n现场频率：每周 5 天，为期 3 个月。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-GEM-4","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"退役动力电池在低空经济场景中再利用的适应性研究","titleEn":"Adaptability Study on Reusing Retired Power Batteries in Low-Altitude Economy Scenarios","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"武汉动力电池再生技术有限公司","companyEn":"Wuhan Power Battery Recycling Technology Co., Ltd.","companySupervisor":"Yuping Zhang","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"With the rapid development of eVTOL and drone industries, there is a growing demand for cost‑effective energy storage systems in low-altitude aircraft. After disassembly and reorganisation, retired power batteries could theoretically serve as energy storage units for low-altitude infrastructure (e.g., drone charging station storage, emergency backup power for take-off/landing sites). This study aims to evaluate the suitability of disassembled cells for low-altitude applications, including the matching analysis between cell state-of-health (SOH) grading standards and low-altitude usage scenarios, assessment of thermal management requirements for repurposed battery modules, and a preliminary economic analysis for typical application scenarios. Based on actual cell inspection data from the disassembly line, the student will build a suitability evaluation framework.\nRequired Skills: Basic principles of batteries, data analysis fundamentals (Excel or Python), report writing ability\nExpected Deliverables: One SOH grading and scenario‑matching matrix for low-altitude applications, one thermal management requirements assessment report, and one economic analysis report for second-life utilisation\nOn-site Frequency: 5 days per week for 3 months","descZh":"随着 eVTOL 和无人机产业的快速发展，低空飞行器对高性价比储能系统的需求日益增长。退役动力电池经过拆解和重组后，理论上可作为低空基础设施的储能单元（例如无人机充电站储能、起降场地的应急备用电源）。本研究旨在评估拆解电芯对低空应用的适用性，包括电芯健康状态（SOH）分级标准与低空使用场景之间的匹配分析、对梯次利用（repurposed）电池模组热管理需求的评估，以及典型应用场景的初步经济分析。学生将基于拆解线的实际电芯检测数据，构建一个适用性评估框架。\n所需技能：电池基本原理、数据分析基础（Excel 或 Python）、报告撰写能力\n预期交付物：一份面向低空应用的 SOH 分级与场景匹配矩阵、一份热管理需求评估报告，以及一份梯次利用经济分析报告\n到岗频率：每周 5 天，持续 3 个月","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-MMCHOY-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"FitMate 2.0：面向安全约束运动方案推荐系统的会员视频链接审核与激励设计","titleEn":"FitMate 2.0: Moderated Member-Submitted Video Links and Incentive Design for a Safety-Constrained Exercise Programme Recommender","areaZh":"全栈应用","areaEn":"full stack application","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"Dr. ALI Zulfiqar","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-09 15:46:35","descriptionText":"FitMate 2.0: Moderated Member-Submitted Video Links and Incentive Design for a Safety-Constrained Exercise Programme Recommender \n (Individual full-stack project, estimated from start to end about 30 weeks. This is a continuation of FitMate 1.0)\n WHERE YOU ARE STARTING FROM \n FitMate 1.0 (Spring Boot + PostgreSQL + Elasticsearch + LangChain4j) already delivers:\n bilingual natural-language need parsing, hybrid lexical + vector retrieval over a CURATED catalogue, defence-in-depth injury safety (pre-filters, post-filters, ranking \n penalties, explanations), transactional plan persistence, a feedback loop, and an evaluation harness kept outside production code. It scored Recall@3 0.760 / nDCG@3 \n 0.805 on an 8-case fixture set with a 60-scenario weighting ablation.\n Four recorded limitations of 1.0 are YOUR starting worklist: \n (a) the labelled evaluation set was too small;\n (b) the catalogue was closed and could not grow;\n (c) the feedback rule can raise impact LOW→MEDIUM even where LOW was set for injury sensitivity - this erodes a SAFETY constraint and must be fixed with a \n regression test;\n (d) not production-hardened: CSRF disabled, secrets in env files, no rate limiting, no audit logging. \n WHAT 2.0 ADDS \n Membership. Members SUBMIT A LINK to an existing exercise video - they do not upload files, and the platform stores no video. An admin/moderator reviews and confirms each submission; confirmed items earn bonus points AND enter the recommendable catalogue. A member states a need and the system assembles a tailored multi-week programme from that catalogue. Engagement features encourage continued exercise. \n Note what this design choice really means: your catalogue is an EXTERNALLY OWNED resource you do not control and cannot repair. That is the source of most of the \n interesting work below.\n THE FOUR THINGS THAT MAKE THIS AN MSc PROJECT \n Read this before you write any code. \"1.0 with a login page\" will not pass.\n 1. SAFE RECOMMENDATION OVER UNTRUSTED CONTENT (your primary contribution) \n 1.0 could assume the catalogue was correct. 2.0 cannot. A submitted link arrives with no reliable muscle group, equipment, difficulty or contraindication metadata - yet your inherited injury filters depend entirely on that metadata. Build: a verification state machine (pending / verified / deprecated / withdrawn), a per-contributor trust score from acceptance history, and moderator quality labels. C RITICAL DESIGN RULE: verification is a HARD FILTER applied BEFORE ranking, not a ranking signal. An unverified item must be unreachable, not merely down-weighted. Trust score may reorder items that are ALREADY verified. Nothing more. \n 2. ASSISTED METADATA EXTRACTION WITH A HUMAN DECISION-MAKER\n A link comes with a title, description, duration, channel and often captions. Pass these through the language components already in 1.0 to PROPOSE target area, \n equipment, difficulty, impact and likely contraindications, presented as a pre-filled, EDITABLE moderation form. The moderator's decision is always what gets \n stored. Record both the proposal and the final decision so you can measure accuracy afterwards. This gives you the strongest single result available in this project: \n per-field accuracy against moderator ground truth, plus MODERATOR SECONDS PER ITEM WITH AND WITHOUT ASSISTANCE. Design for this measurement from week 6 - you cannot reconstruct it later. \n 3. LINK ROT AND PROGRAMME REPAIR\n A verified item can be deleted, made private, region-blocked, age-restricted or have embedding disabled - at any time, silently, AFTER a moderator approved it and AFTER \n it was prescribed into someone's active programme. Build a scheduled re-validation job: detect the change, quarantine the item, find every active programme that \n references it, substitute an equivalent verified item satisfying the same constraints, notify the member, and claw back points if the item is permanently gone. Measure detection latency, repair rate and member-visible breakage rate. \n 4. INCENTIVE DESIGN THAT SURVIVES ADVERSARIAL USE\n Pasting a link takes seconds, so paying points for submissions is a wide-open attack surface. Points on VERIFICATION only, never on submission. Per-period caps, \n diminishing returns, duplicate detection by CANONICAL VIDEO ID (the same video has many URL forms - normalise before comparing), channel-flooding limits, moderator \n override, clawback on withdrawal or link death, and an append-only points ledger so any balance can be explained. Then RUN AN EXPERIMENT: attack your own system with defined strategies (bulk low-effort submissions, the same video re-submitted under different URL forms, flooding from one external channel) and report detection rate, \n points wrongly awarded, and moderator workload per 100 submissions.\n TERMS OF SERVICE - A HARD CONSTRAINT, NOT A DETAIL \n The media belongs to other people. Embed ONLY through the official player. NEVER download, re-host or proxy the video. Honour the embedding-disabled flag. Display \n attribution to the original channel. Work within the data API quota using caching and graceful degradation - exhausting your quota disables submission AND link checking at the same time. A student who downloads video to the server has created a copyright problem, not a feature. \n ENGAGEMENT: GROUND IT IN THEORY OR LOSE MARKS \n Points, streaks, badges and leaderboards bolted on by intuition are the single most common weakness in projects like this. Map EVERY engagement feature to a named \n behaviour change technique and to self-determination theory (goal setting, self-monitoring, feedback on behaviour, effort-contingent reward, optional social \n comparison). Also record what you deliberately EXCLUDED and why - e.g. public leaderboards, which can demotivate less able members. \n MANDATORY: RENT A SERVER - NO LOCALHOST DEMOS \n Live service on a rented VPS (can be reimbursed). Required and assessed:\n - domain, HTTPS with auto-renewing certificate\n - separate staging and production\n - CSRF ENABLED, secrets in a secret store (not a plain .env), rate limiting on login, submission and points-earning endpoints, access + application + moderation audit \n logs.\n - automated database backups PLUS a demonstrated restore test\n - access and application logging, uptime monitoring\n ETHICS AND SAFETY - THIS IS A GATE, NOT PAPERWORK \n - Because members submit links rather than recordings of themselves, nobody is filmed and your ethical burden is lighter than it would otherwise be. But the \n engagement study still collects behavioural data from real people. \n - Members follow your programmes with their own bodies: readiness screen, explicit non-medical disclaimer, conservative default when a constraint is uncertain, an \n obvious \"report unsafe item\" path. Make NO clinical or rehabilitation claim.\n - Submissions can point at inappropriate material. They stay invisible to other members until verified. Moderation is mandatory before publication. Implement and \n TEST report, take-down and points clawback.\n - Health and injury profile data are restricted resources with their own audit trail. Implement the retention and deletion policy, do not merely describe it. \n USER ACCEPTANCE TESTING \n - 8 to 12 participants covering member, contributor, moderator and administrator - INCLUDING at least two with a stated injury or physical limitation, because injury \n sensitivity is the inherited core of this system. \n - Role-specific scripts traceable to your requirement list, executed UNAIDED on the live server, pass / fail / pass-with-comment per step, PLUS a 2–3 week free-use \n period to collect the engagement measures.\n - Measure: pass rate, task time, error and assisted-task counts, System Usability Scale, Intrinsic Motivation Inventory (or comparable validated measure), interview. \n - Engagement measures: sessions logged per week, programme adherence, submission rate, retention. STATE PLAINLY that these are engagement results from a short \n uncontrolled study with a small self-selected sample - NOT evidence of behaviour change, fitness improvement or health benefit. That would need a controlled trial. \n - Sign-off criteria agreed BEFORE UAT: all must-have requirements pass, ZERO unverified or withdrawn items reachable in any generated programme, no open \n critical/major defects, SUS ≥ 68. Report initial AND post-fix results.\n HOW YOU WILL BE MARKED \n Advance over 1.0 (inherited-vs-new boundary documented; all four 1.0 limitations addressed)\n safety under untrusted content\n programme quality against an ENLARGED labelled set, compared with the 1.0 baseline\n reward integrity experiment\n assisted classification accuracy and moderator time saved\n catalogue reliability under link rot\n theoretical grounding of the engagement layer\n UAT and usability\n quality of the production deployment, code and security quality.\n THINGS THAT LOSE MARKS \n 1. Demonstrating on localhost.\n 2. Delivering 1.0 with a login page. Your contribution is safe recommendation over UNTRUSTED, EXTERNALLY OWNED content plus measured reward integrity - foreground it. \n 3. Treating verification as a ranking signal instead of a hard pre-ranking filter. One unverified item appearing in a generated programme is a critical failure. \n 4. Downloading or re-hosting the videos. Embed through the official player only.\n 5. Reusing 1.0 code without documenting the inherited-versus-new boundary. Reuse is expected and legitimate; hiding it is not. \n 6. Adding points and badges with no behavioural rationale.\n 7. Claiming the system increased physical activity, fitness or health. You are running a short uncontrolled study. Claim engagement and usability, nothing more. \n 8. Leaving the evaluation set as small as 1.0's eight fixture cases. Enlarging and re-labelling it is one of your named objectives. \n AI USE \n AI may assist with language, structure and summarising your own material. It must not g enerate your data, technical results or references. Note that the assisted metadata \n extraction inside your SYSTEM is a designed artefact you are evaluating - that is not AI assistance with your dissertation and must not be confused with it in your \n declaration. Complete the AI declaration in full - an incomplete declaration is recorded separately from your academic grade.","descZh":"FitMate 2.0：经审核的会员提交视频链接，以及面向受安全约束的运动计划推荐系统的激励机制设计\n（个人全栈项目，预计从开始到结束约 30 周。这是 FitMate 1.0 的延续）\n你的起点\nFitMate 1.0（Spring Boot + PostgreSQL + Elasticsearch + LangChain4j）已实现：双语自然语言需求解析、在精选（CURATED）目录上的混合词法 + 向量检索、纵深防御式的损伤安全机制（预过滤、后过滤、排序惩罚、解释说明）、事务性的计划持久化、反馈闭环，以及被置于生产代码之外的评估测试套件（evaluation harness）。它在 8 个用例的固定测试集（fixture set）上，通过 60 个场景的加权消融实验（weighting ablation），取得了 Recall@3 0.760 / nDCG@3 0.805 的成绩。\n1.0 记录在案的四个局限就是你的起始工作清单：\n(a) 标注评估集太小；\n(b) 目录是封闭的、无法增长；\n(c) 反馈规则即使在因损伤敏感性（injury sensitivity）而设定为 LOW 的情况下，仍可能将影响等级从 LOW 提升为 MEDIUM——这会侵蚀一条安全约束，必须修复并配上回归测试；\n(d) 尚未达到生产级加固：CSRF 被禁用、密钥存放在环境变量文件中、没有限流、没有审计日志。\n2.0 新增了什么\n会员机制（Membership）。会员提交一个指向既有运动视频的链接——他们不上传文件，平台也不存储任何视频。管理员/审核员审查并确认每一份提交；经确认的条目可获得奖励积分，并进入可推荐目录。会员陈述一个需求，系统从该目录中为其组装一份量身定制的多周训练计划。互动（engagement）功能鼓励持续锻炼。\n请注意这一设计选择真正意味着什么：你的目录是一个由外部拥有的资源，你无法控制，也无法修复。这正是下文大部分有意思工作的来源。\n使本项目成为一个硕士（MSc）项目的四件事\n在写任何代码之前先读这一节。“带登录页的 1.0”是通不过的。\n1. 针对不受信内容的安全推荐（你的主要贡献）\n1.0 可以假设目录是正确的，2.0 不能。一条被提交的链接到达时，没有可靠的肌群、器械、难度或禁忌症（contraindication）元数据——然而你继承的损伤过滤机制完全依赖这些元数据。需要构建：一个验证状态机（pending / verified / deprecated / withdrawn，即待处理/已验证/已弃用/已撤回）、基于录用历史的每位贡献者信任评分，以及审核员质量标签。关键设计规则：验证是在排序之前应用的硬过滤器，而不是一个排序信号。未经验证的条目必须不可达，而不仅仅是降权。信任评分只能对已经验证过的条目进行重新排序，仅此而已。\n2. 带有人类决策者的辅助元数据提取\n一条链接带有标题、描述、时长、频道，且通常还有字幕。把这些信息通过 1.0 中已有的语言组件，来提议（PROPOSE）目标部位、器械、难度、影响等级以及可能的禁忌症，并以一份预填充、可编辑的审核表单呈现。审核员的决定才是最终被存储的内容。同时记录提议和最终决定，以便事后衡量准确率。这会给你带来本项目所能获得的最强有力的单一成果：相对于审核员真实标注（ground truth）的逐字段准确率，外加在有辅助和无辅助两种情况下“每个条目的审核员耗时（秒）”。从第 6 周起就要为这一测量做好设计——你无法在事后补做。\n3. 链接失效（link rot）与计划修复\n一条已验证条目可能被删除、设为私密、被区域封锁、被年龄限制，或禁用嵌入——而且可能在任何时候、悄无声息地发生，发生时机可能是在审核员批准之后、在它被写入某人的进行中计划之后。构建一个定时重新验证任务：检测变更、隔离（quarantine）该条目、找出所有引用它的进行中计划、替换为一条满足相同约束的等价已验证条目、通知会员，并在条目永久消失时追回积分。测量检测延迟、修复率以及会员可见的失效率。\n4. 能在对抗性使用下存活的激励设计\n粘贴一个链接只需几秒钟，因此为提交行为发放积分就是一个完全敞开的攻击面。积分只能在验证时发放，绝不能按提交发放。要有：按周期上限、收益递减、通过规范视频 ID（canonical video ID）进行重复检测（同一个视频有多种 URL 形式——在比较之前要先规范化）、频道刷屏限制、审核员覆写、在撤回或链接失效时的追回，以及一个只追加（append-only）的积分账本，使任何余额都能被解释。然后运行一个实验：用明确定义的策略攻击你自己的系统（批量低质量提交、同一个视频以不同 URL 形式重复提交、来自单一外部频道的刷屏），并报告检测率、被错误发放的积分，以及每 100 份提交的审核员工作量。\n服务条款——硬约束，而非细节\n这些媒体属于他人。只能通过官方播放器进行嵌入（embed）。绝不下载、重新托管（re-host）或代理这些视频。遵守“禁止嵌入”标志。展示对原始频道的署名。利用缓存和优雅降级在数据 API 配额内运作——耗尽配额会同时禁用提交功能和链接检查功能。把视频下载到服务器的学生制造的是版权问题，而不是功能。\n互动设计：扎根于理论，否则会丢分\n靠直觉堆砌积分、连胜（streaks）、徽章和排行榜，是此类项目中最常见的弱点。把每一个互动功能映射到一个有名称的行为改变技术（behaviour change technique）以及自我决定理论（self-determination theory：目标设定、自我监控、行为反馈、随努力而定的奖励、可选的社会比较）。同时记录你有意排除的内容及其原因——例如公开排行榜，它可能打击能力较弱的会员的积极性。\n强制要求：租一台服务器——不允许 localhost 演示\n在租用的 VPS 上运行线上服务（费用可报销）。以下为必需且会被考核：\n- 域名、带自动续期证书的 HTTPS\n- 独立的预发布（staging）与生产环境\n- 启用 CSRF、密钥存放在密钥存储（secret store）中（而不是明文 .env）、对登录、提交和积分获取端点进行限流、访问 + 应用 + 审核审计日志\n- 自动化数据库备份，外加一次演示的恢复测试\n- 访问与应用日志、运行状态（uptime）监控\n伦理与安全——这是门槛，不是文书工作\n- 由于会员提交的是链接而非本人的录像，没有人被拍摄，你的伦理负担因此比原本更轻。但互动研究仍然会从真实人群收集行为数据。\n- 会员用自己的身体执行你的计划：要有就绪筛查（readiness screen）、明确的无医疗性质免责声明、当约束不确定时采用保守默认值，以及一条显而易见的“举报不安全条目”路径。不要作任何临床或康复方面的声明。\n- 提交内容可能指向不适当的材料。在验证之前，它们对其他会员不可见。发布前必须进行审核。实现并测试举报、下架和积分追回。\n- 健康和损伤档案数据是受限资源，要有自己的审计轨迹。落实保留与删除策略，而不仅仅是描述它。\n用户验收测试（UAT）\n- 8 到 12 名参与者，涵盖会员、贡献者、审核员和管理员角色——其中至少包括两名声明有损伤或身体受限的人，因为损伤敏感性是本系统继承的核心。\n- 与你的需求列表可追溯的角色专属脚本，在线上服务器上以无辅助（UNAIDED）方式执行，每一步记录 pass / fail / pass-with-comment（通过/失败/通过但有意见），外加一段 2–3 周的免费使用期以收集互动指标。\n- 测量：通过率、任务时长、错误与受辅助任务次数、系统可用性量表（System Usability Scale）、内在动机量表（Intrinsic Motivation Inventory，或可比的经过验证的量表）、访谈。\n- 互动指标：每周记录的会话次数、计划依从率、提交率、留存率。要明确声明：这些是来自一个短期、无对照、小规模自选样本研究的互动结果——并非行为改变、体能改善或健康收益的证据。要证明那一点需要对照试验。\n- 在 UAT 之前商定的验收（sign-off）标准：所有“必须满足”需求通过、在任何生成的计划中都没有可达的未验证或已撤回条目、没有未关闭的严重/主要缺陷、SUS ≥ 68。报告初始结果和修复后的结果。\n评分方式\n- 相对 1.0 的进展（记录继承与新增的边界；1.0 的四个局限全部解决）\n- 不受信内容下的安全性\n- 在扩大后的标注集上的计划质量，并与 1.0 基线对比\n- 奖励完整性实验\n- 辅助分类准确率与节省的审核员时间\n- 链接失效下的目录可靠性\n- 互动层的理论依据\n- UAT 与可用性\n- 生产部署、代码与安全质量。\n会丢分的事项\n1. 在 localhost 上演示。\n2. 交付一个带登录页的 1.0。你的贡献是针对不受信的、外部拥有的内容的安全推荐，加上经过测量的奖励完整性——要把这一点突出呈现。\n3. 把验证当作排序信号而不是排序前的硬过滤器。一个未验证条目出现在生成的计划中，就是严重失败。\n4. 下载或重新托管视频。只能通过官方播放器嵌入。\n5. 复用了 1.0 代码却没有记录继承与新增的边界。复用是预期且正当的；隐藏它则不然。\n6. 添加积分和徽章却没有任何行为学上的依据。\n7. 声称系统提升了身体活动量、体能或健康。你运行的是一个短期无对照研究。只宣称互动性和可用性，此外别无其他。\n8. 让评估集仍然像 1.0 的八个固定用例那样小。扩大并重新标注评估集是你明确的目标之一。\nAI 使用\nAI 可以协助语言、结构以及对你自己的材料进行概括。它不得生成你的数据、技术结果或参考文献。注意：你的系统内部的辅助元数据提取是你正在评估的一个经过设计的人工产物——这不是对你的学位论文的 AI 协助，在你的声明中不得将二者混淆。完整填写 AI 使用声明——不完整的声明会与你的学业成绩分开记录。","cats":["CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-URXUAN-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"解析电荷密度波超晶格中的晶格-电子关联","titleEn":"Deciphering lattice-electron correlation in a charge density wave superlattice","areaZh":"原子电子断层成像；电子叠层衍射成像；电荷密度波；密度泛函理论","areaEn":"Atomic Electron Tomography; Electron Ptychography; Charge Density Waves; Density Functional Theory","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"LIAO Yuxuan","moderator":"Professor SONG Weixin","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-14 12:34:01","descriptionText":"Project Description:\nIn the 1950s, Peierls pointed out that a one-dimensional metallic chain undergoes a spontaneous lattice distortion at low temperatures, resulting in the formation of a charge density wave (CDW) accompanied by a corresponding lattice distortion, known as Peierls distortion. The Peierls distortion effectively doubles the lattice periodicity, creating a CDW superlattice and transforming the metallic material into an insulator or semiconductor(Grüner 1988). The profound lattice-electron correlations inherent in this phenomenon have inspired extensive research on CDWs and CDW superlattice structures, ranging from early theoretical frameworks(Grüner 1988), and experimental observations unitizing neutron scattering and X-ray diffraction(Moncton, Axe et al. 1977, Kiryukhin, Casa et al. 1998), to subsequent studies on transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs)(Wilson, Di Salvo et al. 1975, Coleman, Giambattista et al. 1988). \nRecently, advancements in high-resolution imaging and spectroscopy have begun to reveal atomic-scale features of CDWs and the associated lattice distortions(Zeljkovic, Xu et al. 2012, Chen, Ruan et al. 2020, Ruan, Chen et al. 2021, Hu, Zhang et al. 2022, Song, Liu et al. 2022, Hart, Pan et al. 2024). These studies have provided valuable insight into the local structural distortions and CDWs. However, current techniques, such as scanning tunneling microscopy (STM), are primarily limited to two-dimensional mapping of surface atoms and are unable to resolve the full three-dimensional (3D) atomic positions of CDW superlattices. This limitation poses a significant barrier to fully understanding the intricate relationship between CDW lattice structures and their electronic properties. \nAtomic Electron Tomography (AET) offers a promising approach to overcome these limitations. AET combines the high spatial resolution of scan transmission electron microscopy (STEM) or 4D-STEM ptychography with our sophisticated tomographic reconstruction algorithm, RESIRE(Pham, Yuan et al. 2020), enabling direct visualization of 3D atomic positions of CDW superlattice at picometer-scale precision. This technique can reveal the subtle lattice distortions that characterize CDW phases, providing a more comprehensive understanding of the structural origins of their electronic properties.\nIn this project, we aim to utilize AET to resolve the 3D atom positions of CWD superlattice for TMD material of 1T-TaS2. The AET measurements will be conducted at several specific temperatures within 180 K-350 K to probe different CDW phases. The 3D atomic configurations obtained from AET will serve as input for density function theory (DFT) calculations to analyze local CDW variations at atomic scales. Through this approach, we seek to answer fundamental questions about the correlation between lattice distortions and electronic properties in CDW systems. \nPreferred Skills (Any of the Following):\n(1) A background in materials science, physics, mechanical engineering, or a related discipline. \n(2) Basic knowledge of crystallography, solid-state physics, or charge-density-wave phenomena. \n(3) A strong interest in transmission electron microscopy, electron ptychography, or other structural characterization techniques. \n(4) A strong interest in density functional theory (DFT) or other first-principles calculation methods. \n(5) A strong interest in atomic-scale characterization, computational analysis, and advanced electron microscopy techniques.","descZh":"项目描述：20 世纪 50 年代，Peierls 指出一维金属链在低温下会发生自发晶格畸变，形成伴随相应晶格畸变的电荷密度波（CDW），即 Peierls 畸变。Peierls 畸变使晶格周期性有效加倍，形成 CDW 超晶格，并将金属材料转变为绝缘体或半导体（Grüner 1988）。这一现象中深刻的晶格-电子关联激发了关于 CDW 与 CDW 超晶格结构的广泛研究——从早期理论框架（Grüner 1988）、利用中子散射与 X 射线衍射的实验观测（Moncton, Axe 等 1977；Kiryukhin, Casa 等 1998），到随后对过渡金属二硫族化物（TMD）的研究（Wilson, Di Salvo 等 1975；Coleman, Giambattista 等 1988）。\n近期，高分辨率成像与光谱学进展开始揭示 CDW 及相关晶格畸变的原子尺度特征（Zeljkovic, Xu 等 2012；Chen, Ruan 等 2020；Ruan, Chen 等 2021；Hu, Zhang 等 2022；Song, Liu 等 2022；Hart, Pan 等 2024）。这些研究为局部结构畸变与 CDW 提供了宝贵洞见。然而，扫描隧道显微术（STM）等现有技术主要局限于表面原子的二维映射，无法分辨 CDW 超晶格的完整三维（3D）原子位置。这一局限对全面理解 CDW 晶格结构与其电子性质之间的复杂关系构成重大障碍。\n原子电子断层成像（AET）为克服这些局限提供了有前景的方法。AET 将扫描透射电子显微术（STEM）或 4D-STEM 叠层衍射成像的高空间分辨率与我们精密的断层重建算法 RESIRE（Pham, Yuan 等 2020）相结合，能够在皮米级精度直接可视化 CDW 超晶格的三维原子位置。该技术可揭示 CDW 相特征的细微晶格畸变，从而更全面地理解其电子性质的结构起源。\n本项目旨在利用 AET 解析 TMD 材料 1T-TaS2 的 CDW 超晶格三维原子位置。AET 测量将在 180 K~350 K 范围内的若干特定温度下开展，以探测不同 CDW 相。AET 获得的三维原子构型将作为密度泛函理论（DFT）计算的输入，分析原子尺度的局部 CDW 变化。通过这一方法，我们寻求回答 CDW 系统中晶格畸变与电子性质之间关联的基本问题。\n偏好技能（以下任一）：(1) 具备材料科学、物理、机械工程或相关学科背景；(2) 具备晶体学、固态物理或电荷密度波现象基础知识；(3) 对透射电子显微术、电子叠层衍射或其他结构表征技术有浓厚兴趣；(4) 对密度泛函理论（DFT）或其他第一性原理计算方法有浓厚兴趣；(5) 对原子尺度表征、计算分析与先进电子显微技术有浓厚兴趣。","cats":["MAT","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-URXUAN-10","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"深度学习多切片电子叠层衍射成像","titleEn":"Deep-learning multislice electron ptychography","areaZh":"深度学习；电子衍射成像；电子叠层衍射成像；计算成像","areaEn":"Deep Learning; Electron Diffractive Imaging; Electron Ptychography; Computational Imaging","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"LIAO Yuxuan","moderator":"Professor SONG Weixin","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-14 12:34:01","descriptionText":"Project Description:\nDeep learning (DL) phase retrieval has seen rapid advancements in electron ptychography, transforming the reconstruction of complex structures from 4D electron diffraction data. By leveraging advanced neural network architectures—including convolutional neural networks, autoencoders, and generative adversarial networks—along with effective learning strategies, these methods efficiently model the intricate relationship between diffraction patterns and object transmission functions. Unlike traditional iterative algorithms, which often suffer from slow convergence, sensitivity to noise, and dependence on prior assumptions, DL-based approaches offer improved computational efficiency, greater adaptability to large-scale datasets, and enhanced robustness in challenging imaging conditions.\nDespite recent success(Babu, Zhou et al. 2023, Chang, O’Leary et al. 2023), DL phase retrieval methods still face significant challenges. One of the primary limitations is their sensitivity to variations in experimental conditions and specimen properties. Since DL models rely heavily on the similarity between training data and real experimental observations, they often struggle to generalize when experimental conditions deviate from those seen during training. This can lead to reconstruction failures or artifacts when applied to new datasets, limiting their robustness in practical applications. Another major challenge is the collection of large, diverse training datasets. High-quality phase retrieval typically requires extensive ptychographic measurements across a wide range of specimens and experimental conditions, which is both time-consuming and costly. The high data acquisition burden makes it difficult to generate sufficiently diverse training datasets, restricting the ability of DL models to generalize across different imaging scenarios.\nTo overcome these limitations, researchers have proposed physics-constrained unsupervised deep learning approaches. Unlike traditional supervised learning methods, which require large labeled datasets, these approaches integrate physical models directly into the training process. By enforcing physics-based constraints, such methods improve the model’s ability to generalize across different experimental conditions while reducing dependence on extensive training data. This has led to promising results in improving reconstruction accuracy and robustness, particularly in scenarios with limited or highly variable data. In this project, we combine deep learning with adaptive physical modeling to enhance both accuracy and generalizability in electron ptychography imaging applications.\nPreferred Skills (Any of the Following):\n(1) A background in materials science, physics, mechanical engineering, or a related discipline. \n(2) Basic knowledge of crystallography, solid-state physics, or wave transport phenomena. \n(3) A strong interest in transmission electron microscopy, electron ptychography, or other structural characterization techniques. \n(4) A strong interest in atomic-scale characterization, computational analysis, and advanced electron microscopy techniques.","descZh":"项目描述：深度学习（DL）相位恢复在电子叠层衍射成像（ptychography）中取得快速发展，变革了从 4D 电子衍射数据重建复杂结构的方式。通过利用先进神经网络架构（包括卷积神经网络、自编码器与生成对抗网络）及有效学习策略，这些方法高效建模衍射图案与物体透射函数之间的复杂关系。与传统迭代算法（常受收敛慢、对噪声敏感与依赖先验假设困扰）相比，基于 DL 的方法提供更高计算效率、对大规模数据集更强的适应性，以及在严苛成像条件下更强的鲁棒性。\n尽管近期取得成功（Babu, Zhou 等 2023；Chang, O'Leary 等 2023），DL 相位恢复方法仍面临重大挑战。主要局限之一是对实验条件与样品性质变化的敏感性：由于 DL 模型高度依赖训练数据与真实实验观测之间的相似性，当实验条件偏离训练所见时往往难以泛化，应用于新数据集时可能导致重建失败或伪影，限制其实际应用鲁棒性。另一重大挑战是采集大规模、多样的训练数据集：高质量相位恢复通常需要对广泛样品与实验条件进行大量叠层衍射测量，既耗时又昂贵。高数据采集负担使生成足够多样的训练数据集困难重重，限制 DL 模型跨不同成像场景的泛化能力。\n为克服这些局限，研究者提出物理约束的无监督深度学习方法。与需要大量标注数据的传统监督学习方法不同，这些方法将物理模型直接集成到训练过程中。通过施加基于物理的约束，此类方法提升模型跨不同实验条件的泛化能力，同时减少对大量训练数据的依赖。在有限或高度可变数据场景下，其在提升重建精度与鲁棒性方面已取得有前景的成果。本项目将深度学习与自适应物理建模相结合，提升电子叠层衍射成像应用的精度与泛化能力。\n偏好技能（以下任一）：(1) 具备材料科学、物理、机械工程或相关学科背景；(2) 具备晶体学、固态物理或波输运现象基础知识；(3) 对透射电子显微术、电子叠层衍射或其他结构表征技术有浓厚兴趣；(4) 对原子尺度表征、计算分析与先进电子显微技术有浓厚兴趣。","cats":["AI","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-17","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"仿生机器鱼的在线具身动力学学习与自适应控制","titleEn":"Online Embodied Dynamics Learning and Adaptive Control for Biomimetic Robotic Fish","areaZh":"AI；机器人学；机器鱼","areaEn":"AI; robotics; Robotic fisher","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 12:04:02","descriptionText":"Research Background\nBiomimetic robotic fish provide an attractive platform for studying adaptive locomotion, embodied intelligence, and learning-based control. Unlike conventional rigid robots, their motion is governed by complex interactions among flexible body structures, oscillatory actuation, and surrounding fluid. As a result, the dynamics of a robotic fish can vary significantly when its morphology, hydrodynamic environment, or actuator condition changes. Typical variations include changes in tail stiffness, fin geometry, payload distribution, external water flow, actuator efficiency, and structural damage. Accurately modeling these effects using analytical hydrodynamic equations is challenging because fluid-structure interactions, compliant deformation, actuator nonlinearities, and environmental disturbances are difficult to identify precisely. Conventional controllers are usually designed based on a fixed analytical or experimentally identified model and may therefore suffer substantial performance degradation when the physical configuration or operating condition changes.\nContent:\nThis project aims to develop a low-cost open-source biomimetic robotic fish platform and investigate learning-based approaches for online dynamics understanding and adaptive locomotion control. The robotic platform will include a motor-driven oscillatory tail, compliant tail structure, replaceable caudal fins, onboard sensing and control electronics, wireless communication, and external visual tracking. The project will investigate how recent state-action trajectories can be used to infer changes in the robot's effective dynamics without explicitly measuring the underlying physical parameters. JEPA-style latent predictive models, recurrent dynamics models, neural state-space models, or related self-supervised world-model architectures may be developed to predict future robot motion over multiple time horizons.\nExpected Outcomes:\n1. Biomimetic Robotic Fish Platform: A functional low-cost robotic fish platform will be fabricated, assembled, waterproofed, calibrated, and equipped with sensing, actuation, communication, and motion-tracking capabilities. \n2. Dynamics Learning Model: Conventional and learning-based dynamics models will be implemented and compared, potentially including analytical or identified models, MLP predictors, recurrent networks, neural state-space models, and JEPA-style latent predictive architectures. \n3. Adaptive Locomotion Control: Learned dynamics models will be integrated with adaptive control frameworks such as MPC or MPPI and compared with conventional PID or fixed-model controllers. Evaluation tasks may include straight swimming, speed regulation, heading stabilization, waypoint navigation, circular trajectories, and figure-eight trajectory tracking under changing dynamics.\n4. Systematic Experimental Analysis: The project will analyze how morphology, environmental disturbances, and mechanical degradation affect locomotion dynamics, model prediction accuracy, and control performance.\nPreferred Skills:\n• Basic knowledge of robotics, system dynamics, and control\n• Proficiency in Python and basic experience with PyTorch\n• Familiarity with Arduino, ESP32, STM32, or similar embedded platforms\n• Basic experience with CAD, 3D printing, mechanical fabrication, and electronics","descZh":"研究背景：仿生机器鱼为研究自适应运动、具身智能与基于学习的控制提供了有吸引力的平台。与常规刚性机器人不同，其运动受柔性身体结构、振荡驱动与周围流体之间复杂相互作用支配。因此，当形态、水动力环境或执行器状态改变时，机器鱼的动力学可能显著变化。典型变化包括尾柄刚度改变、鱼鳍几何变化、载荷分布变化、外部水流、执行器效率变化与结构损伤。用解析水动力方程精确建模这些效应颇具挑战，因为流固耦合、柔顺变形、执行器非线性与环境扰动难以精确辨识。常规控制器通常基于固定的解析或实验辨识模型设计，当物理构型或运行条件变化时可能遭受显著性能退化。\n内容：本项目旨在开发低成本开源的仿生机器鱼平台，并研究基于学习的在线动力学理解与自适应运动控制方法。机器人平台将包括电机驱动的振荡尾柄、柔顺尾结构、可更换尾鳍、机载传感与控制电子、无线通信与外部视觉跟踪。项目将研究如何利用最近的状态-动作轨迹推断机器人有效动力学的变化，而无需显式测量底层物理参数。可开发 JEPA 式隐式预测模型、循环动力学模型、神经状态空间模型或相关自监督世界模型架构，预测机器人未来多个时域的运动。\n预期成果：\n1. 仿生机器鱼平台：制造、组装、防水、标定一台可工作的低成本机器鱼平台，配备传感、驱动、通信与运动跟踪能力；\n2. 动力学学习模型：实现并比较常规与基于学习的动力学模型，可能包括解析或辨识模型、MLP 预测器、循环网络、神经状态空间模型与 JEPA 式隐式预测架构；\n3. 自适应运动控制：将所学动力学模型与 MPC 或 MPPI 等自适应控制框架集成，并与常规 PID 或固定模型控制器比较；评估任务可包括在动力学变化下的直线游泳、速度调节、航向稳定、航点导航、圆形轨迹与八字轨迹跟踪；\n4. 系统实验分析：分析形态、环境扰动与机械退化如何影响运动动力学、模型预测精度与控制性能。\n偏好技能：\n• 具备机器人、系统动力学与控制基础知识；\n• 熟练掌握 Python 并有 PyTorch 基础经验；\n• 熟悉 Arduino、ESP32、STM32 或类似嵌入式平台；\n• 有 CAD、3D 打印、机械制造与电子基础经验。","cats":["AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-18","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"AeroHand：面向遥操作与模仿学习的机械手平台","titleEn":"AeroHand: A Robotic Hand Platform for Teleoperation and Imitation Learning","areaZh":"机器人学 & 控制；人工智能；设计 & 制造","areaEn":"Robotics & Control; Artificial Intelligence; Design & Manufacture","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 12:04:02","descriptionText":"Pre- and co-requisites:\nNo specific pre- or co-requisites are required.\nIntroduction significance:\nThis project aims to develop a robotic hand system for real-time teleoperation and imitation learning. Human hand motions captured by Meta Quest 3 will be mapped to the open-source Aero Hand platform, providing an intuitive control interface and demonstration data for robotic manipulation. The project will offer an accessible and reproducible platform for learning robotic hand development, teleoperation, and skill learning.\nContent:\nThe student will assemble and calibrate the Aero Hand, implement its motor-control and communication interface, and acquire hand-tracking data from Meta Quest 3. A motion-mapping method will be implemented to convert human hand poses into robotic hand commands for real-time teleoperation. Teleoperation demonstrations will then be collected to train a lightweight behavior-cloning model that reproduces a small set of predefined hand motions or simple grasping actions. The complete system will be evaluated through a hardware demonstration.\nOutcomes:\nThe student is expected to produce the following outcomes:\n1. A fully assembled and calibrated Aero Hand with a reliable motor-control interface.\n2. A real-time pipeline that maps Meta Quest 3 hand motions to the robotic hand.\n3. A lightweight model that reproduces selected hand motions or simple grasping actions from teleoperation demonstrations.\n4. Experimental results on motion-tracking accuracy, control latency, repeatability, and task success, together with a final hardware demonstration.\nPreferred Skills:\n1. Programming experience in Python and/or C++.\n2. Basic knowledge of robotics, control, mechatronics, or machine learning.\n3. Interest in robotic hardware integration and system development.\n4. Experience with ROS, Unity, OpenXR, embedded systems, or CAD is advantageous.","descZh":"先修/并修要求：无特定先修或并修要求。\n引言与意义：本项目旨在开发一套用于实时遥操作与模仿学习的机械手系统。由 Meta Quest 3 捕获的人手运动将映射到开源的 Aero Hand 平台，为机器人操作提供直观的控制界面与示范数据。项目将为学习机械手开发、遥操作与技能学习提供可及且可复现的平台。\n内容：学生将组装并标定 Aero Hand，实现其电机控制与通信接口，并从 Meta Quest 3 获取手部跟踪数据。将实现一种运动映射方法，把人体手部姿态转换为机械手指令以进行实时遥操作。随后采集遥操作示范，训练轻量行为克隆模型，复现一小组预定义手部动作或简单抓取动作。完整系统将通过硬件演示评估。\n成果：学生预期产出：\n1. 组装并标定完成、带可靠电机控制接口的 Aero Hand；\n2. 将 Meta Quest 3 手部运动映射到机械手的实时流程；\n3. 从遥操作示范复现选定手部动作或简单抓取动作的轻量模型；\n4. 关于运动跟踪精度、控制时延、可重复性与任务成功率的实验结果，以及最终硬件演示。\n偏好技能：\n1. 有 Python 和/或 C++ 编程经验；\n2. 具备机器人、控制、机电一体化或机器学习基础知识；\n3. 对机器人硬件集成与系统开发有兴趣；\n4. 有 ROS、Unity、OpenXR、嵌入式系统或 CAD 经验者优先。","cats":["AI","DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-19","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于DJI单无人机平台的自主视觉导航与避障","titleEn":"Autonomous Visual Navigation and Obstacle Avoidance Based on the DJI Single-Drone Platform","areaZh":"AI；飞行机器人；机器人学习","areaEn":"AI; Flying robotics; robot learning","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 12:04:02","descriptionText":"Project Overview & Research Objectives:\nThis project aims to develop an autonomous visual navigation, target tracking, and dynamic obstacle avoidance system utilizing a Commercial Off-The-Shelf (COTS) DJI drone platform. By combining the DJI Mobile SDK (MSDK v5) with an NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano edge computing board, the system offloads high-level perception and trajectory planning algorithms to the edge computer while maintaining real-time, low-latency flight control via Virtual Stick commands.\nKey Project Goals:\nAPI Integration: Establish a stable ROS 2 communication bridge between the Android MSDK bridge app and the edge processor.\nEdge AI Perception: Deploy quantized computer vision models (YOLOv8 + TensorRT) for real-time target detection and 3D position estimation.Closed-Loop Control: Implement closed-loop flight control (e.g., PID/MPC) utilizing DJI's IVirtualStickManager interface.\nAutonomous Trajectory Generation: Execute local path planning (e.g., A*, RRT*, EGO-Planner) to achieve real-time dynamic obstacle avoidance.\nSystem Data Pipeline & Architecture:\nVideo & Sensor Streaming: The drone streams real-time video and IMU flight telemetry to the controller.\nBridge Transceiver: The Android bridge app decodes the feed and broadcasts image and state data as ROS 2 topics over a high-bandwidth Wi-Fi network.\nEdge Processing: The NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano processes the vision pipeline, calculates object 3D coordinates, and generates velocity/yaw rate setpoints.\nClosed-Loop Execution: Control commands are pushed back to the MSDK IVirtualStickManager interface, adjusting the aircraft's position in real time.\nReferences:\n1. DJI SDK & Flight Control: https://github.com/dji-sdk/Mobile-SDK-Android\n2. NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano Developer Guide: https://developer.nvidia.com/embedded/jetson-orin-nano-developer-kit\n3.ZJU FAST-Lab: EGO-Planne: https://github.com/ZJU-FAST-Lab/ego-planner","descZh":"项目概述与研究目标：本项目旨在利用商用现货（COTS）大疆（DJI）无人机平台，开发自主视觉导航、目标跟踪与动态避障系统。通过将 DJI Mobile SDK（MSDK v5）与 NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano 边缘计算板结合，系统将高层感知与轨迹规划算法卸载到边缘计算机，同时通过 Virtual Stick 指令保持实时、低时延的飞行控制。\n关键项目目标：\n- API 集成：在 Android MSDK 桥接应用与边缘处理器之间建立稳定的 ROS 2 通信桥；\n- 边缘 AI 感知：部署量化计算机视觉模型（YOLOv8 + TensorRT），实现实时目标检测与三维位置估计；\n- 闭环控制：利用大疆 IVirtualStickManager 接口实现闭环飞行控制（如 PID/MPC）；\n- 自主轨迹生成：执行 A*、RRT*、EGO-Planner 等局部路径规划，实现实时动态避障。\n系统数据管线与架构：\n- 视频与传感器流：无人机将实时视频与 IMU 飞行遥测流式传输至控制器；\n- 桥接收发：Android 桥接应用解码数据流，并通过高带宽 Wi-Fi 网络将图像与状态数据以 ROS 2 话题形式广播；\n- 边缘处理：NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano 处理视觉管线、计算物体三维坐标并生成速度/偏航率设定值；\n- 闭环执行：控制指令推送回 MSDK IVirtualStickManager 接口，实时调整飞行器位置。\n参考资料：\n1. DJI SDK 与飞控：https://github.com/dji-sdk/Mobile-SDK-Android\n2. NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano 开发者指南：https://developer.nvidia.com/embedded/jetson-orin-nano-developer-kit\n3. 浙大 FAST-Lab：EGO-Planner：https://github.com/ZJU-FAST-Lab/ego-planner","cats":["AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KYEUNG-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"SmartLift——视觉驱动的动态电梯调度","titleEn":"SmartLift – Vision-Driven Dynamic Elevator Scheduling","areaZh":"传感器技术；优化；仿真","areaEn":"Sensor technologies; optimization; simulations","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor YEUNG LK","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Objective: Optimize multi-elevator efficiency and passenger experience in commercial buildings using real-time surveillance data.\n Key Methodology:\n Data Collection: Use lobby cameras to extract passenger arrival rates, floor demand, and waiting queues without collecting Personally Identifiable Information . \n Algorithm Design: Implement a multi-objective dispatcher that balances passenger wait/travel time, stop frequency, and lift energy consumption.\n Capacity Awareness: Dynamically bypass floors when lifts reach capacity and pre-dispatch cabins during predicted traffic surges.\n Expected Outcome: A simulated scheduling prototype demonstrating reduced peak-hour passenger wait times and minimized empty stop cycles.","descZh":"目标：利用实时监控数据优化商业建筑中多部电梯的运行效率与乘客体验。\n关键方法：\n- 数据采集：利用大厅摄像头提取乘客到达率、楼层需求与排队情况，且不采集个人身份信息（PII）；\n- 算法设计：实现多目标调度器，在乘客等待/行程时间、停靠次数与电梯能耗之间取得平衡；\n- 满载感知：电梯满载时动态跳过楼层，并在预测到客流高峰前预派轿厢；\n- 预期成果：一个仿真调度原型，展示高峰时段乘客等待时间缩短与空停循环最小化。","cats":["CS","ELE","FLU"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KYEUNG-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"SmartLift——Wi-Fi感知驱动的动态电梯调度","titleEn":"SmartLift – Wi-Fi-Sensing-Driven Dynamic Elevator Scheduling","areaZh":"传感器技术；优化；仿真","areaEn":"Sensor technologies; optimization; simulations","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor YEUNG LK","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Objective: Optimize multi-elevator efficiency and passenger experience in commercial buildings using passive, privacy-preserving Wi-Fi sensing data.\n Key Methodology:\n Data Collection: Extract Channel State Information (CSI) and RF perturbation signals across lobby transceivers to estimate passenger arrival rates, floor demand, and waiting queue headcounts without collecting Personally Identifiable Information or visual feeds.\n Algorithm Design: Implement a multi-objective dispatcher that balances passenger wait/travel time, stop frequency, and lift energy consumption.\n Capacity Awareness: Dynamically bypass floors when lifts reach capacity and pre-dispatch cabins during predicted traffic surges.\n Expected Outcome: A simulated scheduling prototype demonstrating reduced peak-hour passenger wait times and minimized empty stop cycles while ensuring complete privacy compliance.","descZh":"目标：利用被动的、保护隐私的 Wi-Fi 感知数据，优化商业建筑中多部电梯的效率和乘客体验。\n核心方法：\n数据采集：提取大堂各收发器之间的信道状态信息（CSI）和射频（RF）扰动信号，以估计乘客到达率、楼层需求和候梯人数，而无需采集个人身份信息（PII）或视频画面。\n算法设计：实现一个多目标调度器，在乘客等待/行程时间、停靠频率和电梯能耗之间取得平衡。\n容量感知：当电梯满载时动态跳过楼层，并在预测到客流高峰时预先调度轿厢。\n预期成果：一个仿真调度原型，展示高峰时段乘客等待时间的降低和空停循环的最小化，同时确保完全符合隐私要求。","cats":["CS","ELE","FLU"]},{"ref":"2026-27-VTAM-01","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"构建基于生成式AI的智能私人教练应用平台","titleEn":"Building a GenAI-Based Platform for An Intelligent Personal Trainer Application","areaZh":"电子竞技；生成式AI；人机交互（HCI）；传感器技术","areaEn":"e-Sports; GenAI; Human-Computer Interaction (HCI); Sensor Technologies.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. TAM W.L. Vincent","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-15 12:41:35","descriptionText":"With more emphases on healthy living in recent years - especially after the COVID, people of all ages are more concerned about exercises and workouts. Thus, personal trainers are in great demand all over the world. This project is focused on the potential applications of GenAI techniques, particularly the various popular Large Language Models like the ChatGPT, DeepSeek, Gemini, etc. and/or machine learning (ML) approaches, for finding the most appropriate personal training programme for each individual based on his/her health record, physical fitness condition, personal preference and goals, etc. to customise for a personalised training programme in exercises such as indoor bikes, gymnastic, running, etc. for general health or even the HYROX training program that is recently very popular in Hong Kong or overseas.\nThe scope of this project is focused on the investigation of various Gen-AI (such as the Large Language Model (LLM) platforms and/or trained machine learning (ML) framework on different sports like indoor bikes, weight training, running, and their combinations for personalised fitness condition and goals. Essentially, after collecting the relevant and real-time biological data from various sensors such as the heart-beat rate (HBR) sensor, temperature sensor, etc. for each individual user, the connected LLM models and/or trained ML models will work as a multi-agent framework to provide the most optimal training plans/schedules for the individual as based on his/her current biological condition and training goals.\nThe student should have prior knowledge and good programming skills in Python as the pre-requisite. Specifically, the student should have mastered good knowledge and programming skills with Python programming for GenAI and/or ML, and also good analytical skills. \nFieldValueDefault: With more emphases on healthy living in recent years - especially after the COVID, people of all ages are more concerned about exercises and workouts. Thus, personal trainers are in great demand. This project is focused on the potential applications of GenAI techniques, particularly the various popular Large Language Models like the ChatGPT, DeepSeek, Gemini, etc. and/or machine learning (ML) approaches, for finding the most appropriate personal training programme for each person based on his/her health record, physical fitness condition, personal preference and goals, etc. to customise for a personalised training programme in exercises such as indoor bikes, gymnastic, running, etc. \nThe scope of this project can basically be split into two parts: A) to be focused on the investigation of various Gen-AI (such as the Large Language Model (LLM) platforms and/or trained machine learning (ML) framework on different sports like indoor bikes, weight training, running, and their combinations for personalised fitness condition and goals. The second part as B) will be focused on the design and development of a web-based or mobile app platform to make use of the backend GenAI-based techniques, with integration of smart bands/watches and/or other sensors, to provide a all-in-one portal for each individual to achieve personal goals in fitness while maintaining healthy diets and also living styles to develop and ultimately achieve such goals. \nBoth students should have prior knowledge and good programming skills in Python. The student working on A) should have mastered good knowledge and programming skills with Python programming for GenAI and/or ML, and also good analytical skills. The student working on B) should have good knowledge on the design and programming skills with Python programming for web-based or mobile app platform, including but not limited to the user interface design and development in the field of human-computer interaction (HCI), the design and setup of database for the storage and retrieval of personal data, etc.","descZh":"近年来，随着人们对健康生活愈发重视——尤其是疫情之后——各年龄段人群更加关注运动锻炼。因此，私人教练在全球范围内需求旺盛。本项目聚焦于 GenAI 技术（尤其是 ChatGPT、DeepSeek、Gemini 等流行大语言模型）和/或机器学习（ML）方法的潜在应用——根据每个人的健康记录、体能状况、个人偏好与目标等，为其寻找最合适的个人训练方案，定制个性化训练计划，涵盖室内单车、体操、跑步等一般健康训练，甚至包括近期在香港及海外非常流行的 HYROX 训练项目。\n本项目范围聚焦于研究各类 GenAI（如大语言模型（LLM）平台和/或针对室内单车、力量训练、跑步及其组合等不同运动训练的已训练机器学习（ML）框架），用于个性化体能状况与目标。本质上，在从心率（HBR）传感器、温度传感器等各种传感器收集每位用户的实时生物数据后，所连接的 LLM 模型和/或已训练 ML 模型将作为多智能体框架，根据个体当前生物状况与训练目标提供最优训练计划/安排。\n学生应具备 Python 基础知识与良好的编程能力，作为先修条件。具体而言，学生应熟练掌握面向 GenAI 和/或 ML 的 Python 编程知识与技能，并具备良好的分析能力。\n字段补充：随着人们对健康生活愈发重视——尤其是疫情之后——各年龄段人群更加关注运动锻炼，私人教练需求旺盛。本项目聚焦 GenAI 技术（尤其是 ChatGPT、DeepSeek、Gemini 等各类流行大语言模型）和/或机器学习（ML）方法的潜在应用——根据每个人的健康记录、体能状况、个人偏好与目标等，为其寻找最合适的个人训练方案，定制个性化训练计划，涵盖室内单车、体操、跑步等训练。\n本项目范围基本可分为两部分：A) 聚焦研究各类 GenAI（如大语言模型（LLM）平台和/或针对室内单车、力量训练、跑步及其组合等不同运动训练的已训练机器学习（ML）框架），用于个性化体能状况与目标；B) 聚焦设计并开发一个 Web 或移动应用平台，利用后端 GenAI 技术，集成智能手环/手表和/或其他传感器，为每个人提供一个一体化门户，帮助其在保持健康饮食与生活方式的同时实现个人健身目标。\n两名学生都应具备 Python 基础知识与良好的编程能力。负责 A) 的学生应熟练掌握面向 GenAI 和/或 ML 的 Python 编程知识与技能，并具备良好的分析能力；负责 B) 的学生应掌握面向 Web 或移动应用平台的 Python 设计编程技能，包括但不限于人机交互（HCI）领域的用户界面设计与开发、用于个人数据存储与检索的数据库设计与搭建等。","cats":["AI","CS","ELE","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-VTAM-02","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"构建可定制交互式智能私人教练应用平台","titleEn":"Building a Customisable and Interactive Platform for An Intelligent Personal Trainer Application","areaZh":"电子竞技；生成式AI；人机交互（HCI）；传感器技术","areaEn":"e-Sports; GenAI; Human-Computer Interaction (HCI); Sensor Technologies.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. TAM W.L. Vincent","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-15 12:42:12","descriptionText":"With more emphases on healthy living in recent years - especially after the COVID, people of all ages are more concerned about exercises and workouts. Thus, personal trainers (PT) are in great demand all over the world. This project is focused on the potential applications of GenAI techniques, particularly the various popular Large Language Models like the ChatGPT, DeepSeek, Gemini, etc. and/or machine learning (ML) approaches, for finding the most appropriate personal training programme for each individual based on his/her health record, physical fitness condition, personal preference and goals, etc. to customise for a personalised training programme in exercises such as indoor bikes, gymnastic, running, etc. for general health or even the HYROX training program that is recently very popular in Hong Kong or overseas.\nThe scope of this project is focused on the design and development of a highly customisable and smart web-based or mobile app platform for connecting to the backend GenAI-based techniques as intelligent PT advisors, with integration of smart bands/watches and/or other sensors, to provide a all-in-one portal for each individual to achieve personal goals in fitness while maintaining healthy diets and also living styles to develop and ultimately achieve such goals. \nThe student should have prior knowledge and good programming skills in Python as the pre-requisite. Specifically, the student should have good knowledge on the design and programming skills with Python programming for web-based or mobile app platform, including but not limited to the user interface design and development in the field of human-computer interaction (HCI), the design and setup of database for the storage and retrieval of personal data, etc.","descZh":"近年来，随着人们对健康生活愈发重视——尤其是疫情之后——各年龄段人群更加关注运动锻炼。因此，私人教练（PT）在全球范围内需求旺盛。本项目聚焦于 GenAI 技术（尤其是 ChatGPT、DeepSeek、Gemini 等流行大语言模型）和/或机器学习（ML）方法的潜在应用——根据每个人的健康记录、体能状况、个人偏好与目标等，为其寻找最合适的个人训练方案，定制个性化的训练计划，涵盖室内单车、体操、跑步等一般健康训练，甚至包括近期在香港及海外非常流行的 HYROX 训练项目。\n本项目范围聚焦于设计并开发一个高度可定制、智能的 Web 或移动应用平台，连接后端基于 GenAI 的技术充当智能 PT 顾问，并集成智能手环/手表和/或其他传感器，为每个人提供一个一体化门户，帮助其在保持健康饮食与生活方式的同时实现个人健身目标。\n学生应具备 Python 基础知识与良好的编程能力，作为先修条件。具体而言，学生应掌握面向 Web 或移动应用平台的 Python 设计编程技能，包括但不限于人机交互（HCI）领域的用户界面设计与开发、用于个人数据存储与检索的数据库设计与搭建等。","cats":["AI","CS","ELE","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-VTAM-03","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"构建基于生成式AI的虚拟现实（VR）面试练习仿真平台","titleEn":"Building A GenAI-Based Simulation Platform with Virtual Reality (VR) for Practising Interviews","areaZh":"生成式AI；人机交互（HCI）；传感器技术","areaEn":"GenAI; Human-Computer Interaction (HCI); Sensor Technologies.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. TAM W.L. Vincent","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-15 12:42:44","descriptionText":"Many personal development units in universities or private sectors have recruited many different experts in helping to train students attending job or university admission interviews. With the fast development of GenAI and sensor technologies such as the META Quest VR headsets, it is very potential and useful to investigate on how GenAI - especially the various large language models (LLMs) - and the VR headsets may help to simulate the various real-world interviewing scenarios with interviewers of different characters to allow students practising their interviewing skills/techniques based on their personal capabilities with different strengths and weakness. \nAccordingly, the scope of this project is focused on the investigation of various GenAI-based LLMs for simulating the different interviewing scenarios such as those for job interviews, promotion interviews, or graduate school admission interviews, etc. with multiple interviewers of different characters sitting in the panel. The simulated characters and features of the virtual interviewers will then be displayed in the simulated environment as the virtual reality part of the META Quest VR headsets, e.g. possibly through the Unity game engine and libraries.\nThe student should have prior knowledge and good programming skills in Python as the pre-requisite for this project. Specifically, the student should have mastered good knowledge and programming skills with Python programming for GenAI-based LLMs platforms, and also good analytical skills.","descZh":"许多大学或私营机构的个人发展部门聘请了众多不同领域的专家，帮助训练参加求职或大学录取面试的学生。随着 GenAI 与 META Quest VR 头显等传感器技术的快速发展，研究 GenAI——尤其是各类大语言模型（LLM）——与 VR 头显如何帮助模拟各种真实面试场景（由不同性格的面试官组成）非常有潜力且实用，可让学生根据自身不同强弱项练习面试技巧。\n因此，本项目范围聚焦于研究基于 GenAI 的各类 LLM 来模拟不同面试场景，如求职面试、晋升面试或研究生录取面试等，面试委员会中设有多位性格各异的面试官。虚拟面试官的模拟角色与特征将作为 META Quest VR 头显的虚拟现实部分呈现在模拟环境中，例如可通过 Unity 游戏引擎及其库实现。\n学生应具备 Python 基础知识与良好的编程能力，作为本项目先修条件。具体而言，学生应熟练掌握面向 GenAI LLM 平台的 Python 编程知识与技能，并具备良好的分析能力。","cats":["AI","CS","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-VTAM-04","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"开发集成VR头显的交互式面试练习仿真平台","titleEn":"Developing An Interactive Simulation Platform Integrated with VR Headsets for Practising Interviews","areaZh":"生成式AI；人机交互（HCI）；传感器技术","areaEn":"GenAI; Human-Computer Interaction (HCI); Sensor Technologies.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. TAM W.L. Vincent","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-15 12:43:26","descriptionText":"Many personal development units in universities or private sectors have recruited many different experts in helping to train students attending job or university admission interviews. With the fast development of GenAI and sensor technologies such as the META Quest VR headsets, it is very potential and useful to investigate on how GenAI - especially the various large language models (LLMs) - and the VR headsets may help to simulate the various real-world interviewing scenarios with interviewers of different characters to allow students practising their interviewing skills/techniques based on their personal capabilities with different strengths and weakness. \nAccordingly, the scope of this project is focused on the design and development of a VR-based application running on the desktop or tablet PC platform connecting to the backend GenAI-based platforms, with integration of VR headsets, to provide a all-in-one portal for each individual to practise their interviewing skills under different real-world scenarios. \nThe student should have prior knowledge and good programming skills in Python as the pre-requisite for this project. Specifically, the student should have good knowledge on the design and programming skills with Python programming for VR-based platform/tool such as the META Quest VR headsets and development tools, including but not limited to the user interface design and development of VR applications, the design and setup of database for the storage and retrieval of personal data, etc.","descZh":"大学或私营机构的许多个人发展部门已聘请了众多不同领域的专家，帮助培训参加求职或大学入学面试的学生。随着生成式人工智能（GenAI）以及 META Quest VR 头显等传感器技术的快速发展，研究 GenAI——尤其是各种大语言模型（LLM）——与 VR 头显如何帮助模拟各类真实世界面试场景（由不同性格的面试官参与），从而让学生能够根据自身能力与不同的优劣势练习面试技巧/技法，具有很大的潜力与实用价值。\n据此，本项目的研究范围聚焦于设计与开发一款运行在台式机或平板电脑平台上的基于 VR 的应用，该应用连接后端基于 GenAI 的平台，并集成 VR 头显，为每个人提供一个一体化的门户，使个人能够在不同的真实世界场景下练习面试技巧。\n学生应具备 Python 方面的先修知识与良好的编程技能，作为本项目的先修要求。具体而言，学生应熟练掌握针对 VR 平台/工具（如 META Quest VR 头显及开发工具）的 Python 设计与编程技能，包括但不限于 VR 应用的用户界面设计与开发、用于存储与检索个人数据的数据库设计与搭建等。","cats":["AI","CS","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ALANCHT-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"使用介电弹性体驱动器的软体游泳机器人","titleEn":"A Soft Swimming Robot using Dielectric Elastomer Actuators","areaZh":"软体机器人；软材料","areaEn":"Soft Robotics; Soft material","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor TSANG Alan Cheng Hou","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 17:07:01","descriptionText":"Dielectric elastomer actuators (DEAs) is a new type of material recently used for the development of soft robotics. DEAs consist of an incompressible elastic layer sandwiched between two flexible electrodes. When an electric potential difference is applied between the two electrodes, electrostatic stress causes the elastomer to deform, and the resulting mechanical strain can be used for actuation. Traditional DEA require applying a large voltage to both ends of the flexible electrodes to drive the DEAs, and most DEAs require a pre-stretching step to reduce the thickness of the elastomer and minimize the influence of electromechanical instability (EMI) on the actuator. Although pre-stretching can effectively produce high-quality thin films and reduce the driving voltage, the pre-stretching process requires the installation of cumbersome support structures in the actuator, thereby reducing the payload of the robot. \nThis project presents a low-cost, low-voltage, long-life, high-power-density DEA, which is fabricated using a new multi-layer structure manufacturing method and electrode material optimization technology. Through improvements in spin coating and vacuum filtration processes, our method can produce 10 layers high-quality 50 µm thick elastomer films and conductive carbon black materials as flexible electrodes, enabling it to function at voltages lower than 500V. Based on this DEA, we will design and develop a peristaltic pump for soft robot driving as well as a soft swimming robot, demonstrating the potential of this novel material for serving as deformable components for soft robotic applications.\nThe project will be supported by PhD student mentors from Prof. Alan Tsang's lab.","descZh":"介电弹性体执行器（DEA）是近年来用于软体机器人开发的新型材料。DEA 由夹在两柔性电极之间的不可压缩弹性层构成。当两电极间施加电势差时，静电应力使弹性体形变，产生的机械应变可用于驱动。传统 DEA 需在柔性电极两端施加高电压驱动，且多数 DEA 需要预拉伸步骤以减薄弹性体并最小化机电不稳定性（EMI）对执行器的影响。尽管预拉伸能有效制备高质量薄膜并降低驱动电压，但预拉伸工艺需要在执行器中安装笨重的支撑结构，从而降低机器人的有效载荷。\n本项目提出一种低成本、低电压、长寿命、高功率密度的 DEA，采用新型多层结构制造方法与电极材料优化技术制备。通过改进旋涂与真空过滤工艺，本方法可生产 10 层、厚度 50µm 的高质量弹性体薄膜，并以导电炭黑材料作为柔性电极，使其能在低于 500V 电压下工作。基于该 DEA，我们将设计并开发用于软体机器人驱动的蠕动泵以及软体游动机器人，展示这种新材料作为软体机器人可变形部件的潜力。\n本项目将获得曾志伟（Alan Tsang）教授课题组博士生的指导支持。","cats":["MAT","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ALANCHT-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向自适应流动整流的仿生软体微流控二极管","titleEn":"Bioinspired Soft Microfluidic Diodes for Adaptive Flow Rectification","areaZh":"仿生系统；智能制造","areaEn":"Bioinspired systems; Smart manufacturing","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor TSANG Alan Cheng Hou","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 17:07:01","descriptionText":"Inspired by the spiral intestinal structures of sharks, this project aims to develop a soft on-chip microfluidic diode for preferential liquid transport without moving valves or electronic control. Inclined helical structures will be integrated into elastomeric microchannels to reproduce the biological principle of asymmetric intestinal flow. CAD and CFD simulations will assess how spiral pitch, inclination angle, opening size, and number of turns affect forward and reverse hydraulic resistance. Selected designs will be fabricated using 3D printing and soft lithography. Pressure–flow measurements and optical imaging will compare rigid and deformable channels, quantify flow diodicity, and determine how flow-induced deformation enables adaptive rectification. The project provides a manageable MSc research cycle covering biomimetic design, simulation, advanced manufacturing, soft material testing, and data analysis. The work is divided into manageable stages that can be completed alongside regular coursework. The resulting device may support backflow prevention, sample transport, and simplified fluidic control in diagnostics, chemical processing, and laboratory automation, while providing career-relevant training in microfluidics, soft materials, advanced manufacturing, and computational engineering.\nThe project will be supported by PhD student mentors from Prof. Alan Tsang's lab.","descZh":"本项目受鲨鱼螺旋肠结构的启发，旨在开发一种软体片上微流控二极管，无需移动阀或电子控制即可实现优先液体输运。将倾斜螺旋结构集成到弹性体微通道中，以复现不对称肠流的生物学原理。将利用 CAD 与 CFD 仿真评估螺旋节距、倾斜角、开口尺寸与圈数如何影响正向与反向流阻。选定的设计将采用 3D 打印与软光刻工艺制造。通过压力-流量测量与光学成像，比较刚性通道与可变形通道，量化流体二极性，并确定流动引起的变形如何实现自适应整流。本项目提供了一个可控的硕士研究周期，涵盖仿生设计、仿真、先进制造、软材料测试与数据分析；工作分为若干可在常规课程学习之余完成的阶段。所开发器件可在诊断、化工处理与实验室自动化中支持防回流、样品输运与简化流体控制，同时在微流控、软材料、先进制造与计算工程方面提供与职业发展相关的训练。\n本项目将获得曾志伟（Alan Tsang）教授课题组博士生的指导支持。","cats":["DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ALANCHT-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"仿生刚毛微结构：设计与多功能应用","titleEn":"Bio-inspired Bristled Microstructures: Design and Multifunctional Application","areaZh":"软体机器人；软材料","areaEn":"Soft robotics; Soft material","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor TSANG Alan Cheng Hou","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"In nature, many miniature insects (e.g., parasitoid wasps, thrips) rely on bristled wings—sparse arrays of rigid bristles rather than solid membranes—to achieve efficient flight at low Reynolds numbers (Re < 100). These wings exhibit a unique porosity effect: the inter-bristle gaps form a “virtual fluid barrier” that effectively prevents airflow leakage while significantly reducing structural mass and inertia. More importantly, by tailoring geometric parameters (bristle density, diameter, spacing, and arrangement), one can precisely control fluid resistance, lift, damping, and resonance characteristics. This porosity effect is not limited to micro aerial vehicles; it can be exploited in both forward and inverse manners across diverse engineering scenarios:\n• In micro aerial vehicles, porous structures can generate sufficient lift with reduced power consumption;\n• In sensors, the damping properties of porous structures can be used to measure flow velocity, pressure, or acoustic waves;\n• In actuators/valves, the deformability of porous structures enables fluid channel control;\n• In energy harvesting, flow-induced vibrations of porous structures can capture ambient energy.\nThis project aims to systematically investigate and utilize the porosity effect of bristled microstructures to develop functional devices or microsystems applicable to the aforementioned scenarios and beyond.\nThe project will be supported by PhD student mentors from Prof. Alan Tsang's lab.","descZh":"在自然界中，许多微型昆虫（如寄生蜂、蓟马）依靠刚毛翼——由稀疏刚性刚毛阵列而非实体膜构成——在低雷诺数（Re < 100）条件下实现高效飞行。这种翅膀表现出独特的孔隙效应：刚毛间隙形成\"虚拟流体屏障\"，能有效阻止气流泄漏，同时大幅降低结构质量与惯性。更重要的是，通过定制几何参数（刚毛密度、直径、间距与排布），可精确控制流体阻力、升力、阻尼与共振特性。这种孔隙效应并不限于微型飞行器，还可在多种工程场景中以正演与逆演两种方式加以利用：\n• 在微型飞行器中，多孔结构可在更低功耗下产生足够升力；\n• 在传感器中，多孔结构的阻尼特性可用于测量流速、压力或声波；\n• 在执行器/阀门中，多孔结构的可变形性可实现流体通道控制；\n• 在能量收集中，多孔结构的流致振动可捕获环境能量。\n本项目旨在系统研究并利用刚毛微结构的孔隙效应，开发适用于上述场景及更广领域的功能器件或微系统。\n本项目将获得曾志伟（Alan Tsang）教授课题组博士生的指导支持。","cats":["MAT","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ALANCHT-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向微液滴动力学的创新微流控平台","titleEn":"Innovative Microfluidic Platforms for Microdroplet Dynamics","areaZh":"微流控；先进制造；生物医学应用","areaEn":"Microfluidics; Advanced Manufacturing; Biomedical Applications","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor TSANG Alan Cheng Hou","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 17:07:01","descriptionText":"Microdroplet dynamics plays an important role in microfluidics, bioanalysis, drug screening, emulsion engineering, inkjet printing, surface engineering, and advanced materials processing. \nThis project will focus on the development of a PDMS-based microfluidic platform and the characterization of droplet dynamics. Combining microfluidic chip design, softlithography fabrication, two-phase flow control, and high-speed imaging analysis, it will systematically study the key physical mechanisms of microdroplet generation, collision, and impact processes.\nStudents will first systematically learn the design of microfluidic PDMS chips and standard soft lithography fabrication processes. Building upon this foundation, they will design and fabricate PDMS microfluidic chips for droplet generation, observing the kinetics of droplet formation by varying parameters such as flow ratio, liquid viscosity, and liquid-liquid interfacial tension.\nSubsequent phases of the project will involve building an optical system and combining it with high-speed imaging technology to further investigate the spreading, rebounding, deposition, or splashing behavior of droplets impacting different solid-liquid surfaces, analyzing the effects of impact velocity, surface wettability, liquid properties, and film thickness on droplet impact dynamics.\nThis project aims to establish a complete experimental chain from microfluidic chip fabrication to droplet dynamics analysis, providing fundamental research support for areas such as lab-on-a-chip technology, biomanufacturing, inkjet printing, surface functionalization, and soft material devices. Students will receive comprehensive training in micro/nano fabrication, experimental design, high-speed microscopic imaging, and quantitative data analysis.\nThe project will be supported by PhD student mentors from Prof. Alan Tsang's lab.","descZh":"微液滴动力学在微流控、生物分析、药物筛选、乳液工程、喷墨打印、表面工程与先进材料加工中发挥重要作用。\n本项目将聚焦于开发基于 PDMS 的微流控平台并表征液滴动力学。结合微流控芯片设计、软光刻加工、两相流控制与高速成像分析，系统研究微液滴生成、碰撞与撞击过程的关键物理机制。\n学生将首先系统学习 PDMS 微流控芯片设计与标准软光刻加工流程。在此基础上，设计并制造用于液滴生成的 PDMS 微流控芯片，通过改变流量比、液体粘度与液-液界面张力等参数观察液滴形成动力学。\n项目后续阶段将搭建光学系统并结合高速成像技术，进一步研究液滴撞击不同固-液表面时的铺展、回弹、沉积或飞溅行为，分析撞击速度、表面润湿性、液体性质与液膜厚度对液滴撞击动力学的影响。\n本项目旨在建立从微流控芯片制造到液滴动力学分析的完整实验链，为芯片实验室技术、生物制造、喷墨打印、表面功能化与软材料器件等领域提供基础研究支撑。学生将获得微/纳加工、实验设计、高速显微成像与定量数据分析的全面训练。\n本项目将获得曾志伟（Alan Tsang）教授课题组博士生的指导支持。","cats":["BIO","CS","DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ALANCHT-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于强化学习的风场下能量感知无人机航迹规划","titleEn":"UAV Route Planning under Wind Fields with Energy Awareness using Reinforcement Learning","areaZh":"无人机；强化学习","areaEn":"UAV; Reinforcement learning","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor TSANG Alan Cheng Hou","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This project involves the development of a real-time route planning system that can predict UAV energy consumption and propose a safe, energy-efficient flight path under different wind/weather conditions\nOutcomes:\nStudents are expected to produce the following outcomes\n1. Simulation environment building: \n1.1 The terrain environment representing an urban and suburban region with possible obstacles such as buildings and no-fly zones should be built.\n1.2 A wind field model that assigns wind vectors (speed and direction). This module can be derived from simple mathematical models or open-source data.\n1.3 A basic energy consumption model, relating energy usage to speed, maneuvers or hovering with/without windy condition.\n2. Route planning algorithm development:\nThe students are expected to design and implement reinforcement-learning based algorithms (PPO, DQN and etc.) to lean energy-efficient, obstacles-free flight policies under different wind conditions. This algorithm should also balance the flight time and energy loss of the UAV.\n3. Evaluations: The performance of the algorithm will be evaluated based on:\n3.1 Total and average energy consumption for different routes under different wind conditions.\n3.2 Success rate for reaching the targets under limited energy supply and travel time.\n3.3 Robustness to changes in unexpected wind conditions, such as a sudden increase in wind intensity. \nThe evaluations should highlight the trade‑offs between energy efficiency, travel time and success rate of reaching the targets.","descZh":"本项目涉及开发一套实时航线规划系统，可在不同风况/天气条件下预测无人机能耗并提出安全、节能的飞行路径。\n成果：学生预期产出以下成果：\n1. 仿真环境搭建：\n1.1 构建代表城市与郊区的环境（含建筑、禁飞区等可能障碍物）；\n1.2 建立赋予风矢量（速度与方向）的风场模型（可由简单数学模型或开源数据推导）；\n1.3 建立基础能耗模型，将能耗与速度、机动或悬停（有/无风条件）关联起来。\n2. 航线规划算法开发：学生须设计并实现基于强化学习的算法（PPO、DQN 等），学习不同风况下节能且避障的飞行策略；算法还应在飞行时间与无人机能量损失之间取得平衡。\n3. 评估：算法性能将基于以下指标评估：\n3.1 不同航线与不同风况下的总能耗与平均能耗；\n3.2 在有限能量供给与行程时间约束下到达目标点的成功率；\n3.3 对突发风况（如风速骤增）变化的鲁棒性。\n评估应突出能效、飞行时间与到达成功率之间的权衡。","cats":["AERO","AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ALANCHT-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"通信冷却系统液-汽两相传热过程的CFD研究","titleEn":"CFD Study of Liquid-Vapor Two-Phase Heat Transfer Process for Telecommunication Cooling Systems","areaZh":"计算流体力学；传热","areaEn":"Computational Fluid Dynamics; Heat Transfer","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor TSANG Alan Cheng Hou","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 17:07:01","descriptionText":"The escalating power density of telecommunication equipment, driven by 5G base stations and edge data centres, has rendered single-phase air cooling increasingly inadequate. Two-phase cooling exploits latent heat transport to achieve markedly higher heat transfer coefficients under identical operating conditions, and is realized either passively, through two-phase closed thermosiphons (TPCTs), or actively, through flow boiling and flow condensation in minichannel heat exchangers. This project will employ ANSYS Fluent to investigate the liquid–vapour two-phase heat transfer characteristics of such systems. The volume of fluid multiphase model with proper mass transfer assumption will be adopted to simulate the two-phase heat transfer behavior. The work may be divided among students according to configuration and phase-change mode (e.g. flow boiling, flow condensation, and thermosiphon operation). Systematic parametric studies will then quantify the influence of structural parameters, mass flux, vapour quality, saturation temperature, heat load and filling ratio on the heat transfer characteristics. Particular attention will be given to the relationship between flow pattern and heat transfer performance. The outcome will be a validated numerical framework and practical design guidance for two-phase cooling of telecommunication equipment.\nThe project will be supported by PhD student mentors from Prof. Alan Tsang's lab.","descZh":"受 5G 基站与边缘数据中心驱动的通信设备功率密度不断攀升，使单相风冷日益力不从心。两相冷却利用潜热输运，在相同运行条件下可实现显著更高的传热系数；其实现方式包括无源的两相闭式热虹吸管（TPCT），以及有源的小通道换热器内流动沸腾与流动冷凝。本项目将采用 ANSYS Fluent 研究此类系统的液-汽两相传热特性，将采用流体体积（VOF）多相模型并结合适当的质量传递假设对两相传热行为进行仿真。工作可按构型与相变模式（如流动沸腾、流动冷凝与热虹吸运行）在多名学生之间划分。随后将开展系统参数化研究，量化结构参数、质量流量、干度、饱和温度、热负荷与充液率对传热特性的影响，尤其关注流型与传热性能之间的关系。成果将是一套经过验证的数值框架，以及面向通信设备两相冷却的实用设计指南。\n本项目将获得曾志伟（Alan Tsang）教授课题组博士生的指导支持。","cats":["ENE","FLU"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ALANCHT-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"电子设备热管理中电流体动力传导泵的CFD研究","titleEn":"CFD Study of Electrohydrodynamic Conduction Pumping for Electronic Device Thermal Management","areaZh":"计算流体力学；传热","areaEn":"Computational Fluid Dynamics; Heat Transfer","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor TSANG Alan Cheng Hou","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 17:07:01","descriptionText":"This project aims to numerically investigate the potential of Electrohydrodynamic (EHD) conduction pumping for improving thermal management in high heat flux electronic systems. A 2D horizontal plate model with a uniformly heated patch will be simulated using Computational Fluid Dynamic tools such as OpenFOAM® or Ansys Fluent. The study will explore how flow induced by space charge mechanisms interacts with natural convection, under varying Rayleigh and electric Reynolds numbers. The objective is to analyze how EHD conduction pumping alters flow characteristics and enhances heat transfer, providing insights into its viability for compact liquid-based cooling applications in electronics. The findings from this project are expected to inform the design of advanced cooling solutions for high-performance electronic devices, such as data centers, power electronics, and battery. The combined system can improve device reliability, reduce energy consumption, and enable higher operational heat fluxes without the need for bulky or complex cooling infrastructure. Ultimately, this will contribute to the advancement of sustainable and compact thermal management technologies in the electronics industry.\nThe project will be supported by PhD student mentors from Prof. Alan Tsang's lab.","descZh":"本项目旨在数值研究电流体动力学（EHD）传导泵送在高热流密度电子系统热管理中的潜力。将使用 OpenFOAM® 或 Ansys Fluent 等计算流体力学工具，对带均匀加热斑块的二维水平板模型进行仿真。研究将探讨空间电荷机制引起的流动如何在变化的瑞利数与电雷诺数下与自然对流相互作用。目标是分析 EHD 传导泵送如何改变流动特性并增强传热，为电子设备紧凑型液冷应用的可行性提供见解。预期研究成果可为数据中心、电力电子与电池等高性能电子设备的先进冷却方案设计提供依据。该系统可提升器件可靠性、降低能耗，并在无需笨重复杂冷却基础设施的情况下支持更高的工作热流密度。最终将推动电子行业可持续、紧凑型热管理技术的进步。\n本项目将获得曾志伟（Alan Tsang）教授课题组博士生的指导支持。","cats":["ENE","FLU"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ALANCHT-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"电池系统或数据中心的热管理仿真","titleEn":"Thermal Management Simulation for Battery System or Data Center","areaZh":"计算流体力学；传热","areaEn":"Computational Fluid Dynamics; Heat Transfer","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor TSANG Alan Cheng Hou","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 17:07:01","descriptionText":"In this project, students will design and implement a simulation model to study the thermal behavior of computer heat dissipation components, EV battery systems, and data center or high‑performance computing (HPC) racks. The goal is to understand the factors that influence heat dissipation and develop strategies to optimize cooling for improved performance and reliability. Students will use computational fluid dynamics (CFD) techniques to model heat transfer, airflow, and temperature distribution within these systems. The project will involve data collection, model development, simulation, and analysis of the results to identify potential design improvements, including the evaluation of cold‑aisle/hot‑aisle configurations, rack‑level airflow organization, and the mitigation of thermal hot spots in large‑scale computing environments.\nThe project will be supported by PhD student mentors from Prof. Alan Tsang's lab.","descZh":"在本项目中，学生将设计并实现一个仿真模型，研究计算机散热部件、电动汽车电池系统以及数据中心或高性能计算（HPC）机柜的热行为。目标是理解影响散热的各种因素，并制定优化冷却的策略以提升性能与可靠性。学生将使用计算流体力学（CFD）技术，对这些系统内的传热、气流与温度分布进行建模。项目将涉及数据采集、模型开发、仿真与结果分析，以识别潜在的设计改进，包括评估冷/热通道布置、机柜级气流组织以及大规模计算环境中热点抑制。\n本项目将获得曾志伟（Alan Tsang）教授课题组博士生的指导支持。","cats":["ENE","FLU"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ALANCHT-9","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"刚毛翼结构对微型扑翼无人机的气动优势","titleEn":"Aerodynamic Advantages of Bristled-Wing Structures for Miniaturized Flapping-Wing Drone","areaZh":"航空航天；设计与制造；材料与固体力学；机器人学；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Aerospace; Design and manufacture; Materials and Solids; Robotics; Drones and Control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor TSANG Alan Cheng Hou","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Introduction:\nThe miniaturization of flapping-wing micro aerial vehicles (FWMAVs) presents significant aerodynamic challenges at sub-centimeter scales, where flight enters a low Reynolds number regime dominated by viscous effects and diminished lift. Inspired by the flight capabilities of tiny insects like parasitoid wasps, this project explores the use of bio-inspired bristled wing microstructures to enhance aerodynamic performance under these demanding conditions. The development of such biomimetic structures holds the potential to enable stable flight and complex maneuvers for millimeter-scale FWMAVs, opening new possibilities for applications in micro-robotics, environmental monitoring, and search-and-rescue operations in confined spaces.\nContent:\nThis project focuses on the design, fabrication, and experimental testing of bio-inspired bristled wings for tailless FWMAVs operating in low Reynolds number environments (Re < 100). The objective is to investigate how specific geometric parameters of bristled microstructures influence aerodynamic efficiency, lift generation, and flight stability, thereby advancing the frontiers of micro-aerial vehicle technology.\nOutcomes:\nStudents are expected to produce the following outcomes:\n1. Bio-inspired Wing Design and Modeling:\n1.1 Design a series of bristled wing prototypes with varying geometric parameters (e.g., bristle density, length, spacing) using CAD software, drawing inspiration from biological models like parasitoid wasps.\n1.2 Develop a simplified aerodynamic model to simulate and predict the performance of these bristled wing designs under low Reynolds number conditions.\n2. Prototype Fabrication and Testing:\n2.1 Fabricate the designed wing prototypes using suitable materials and techniques, such as 3D printing or micro-machining.\n2.2 Develop an experimental testbed (e.g., a tethered flight setup or a wind tunnel adapted for low Reynolds number flows) to evaluate the lift force, power consumption, and flight stability of the fabricated wings.\n3. Performance Analysis and Optimization:\n3.1 Quantify the correlation between bristled wing geometric parameters and key aerodynamic performance metrics.\n3.2 Propose an optimized wing design that demonstrates improved aerodynamic efficiency or enhanced flight stability compared to a baseline solid-wing design under the same conditions.\nEvaluations:\nThe success of the project will be evaluated based on:\n1 The measured lift-to-power ratio of the fabricated bristled wings, indicating aerodynamic efficiency.\n2 The observed flight stability and maneuverability of the wing prototypes in the experimental testbed.\n3 A comparative analysis demonstrating the performance advantages of the optimized bristled wing design over a conventional solid-wing design in low Reynolds number conditions. The evaluation should highlight the trade-offs between structural integrity, weight, and aerodynamic performance.\nThe project will be supported by PhD student mentors from Prof. Alan Tsang's lab.","descZh":"引言：扑翼微型飞行器（FWMAV）的微型化在亚厘米尺度面临显著气动挑战——飞行进入以粘性效应为主、升力下降的低雷诺数区间。受寄生蜂等微型昆虫飞行能力启发，本项目探索利用仿生刚毛翼微结构，在此严苛条件下增强气动性能。此类仿生结构的开发有望使毫米级 FWMAV 实现稳定飞行与复杂机动，为微机器人、环境监测与受限空间搜救应用开启新可能。\n内容：本项目聚焦在低雷诺数环境（Re<100）中运行的无尾 FWMAV 仿生刚毛翼的设计、制造与实验测试。目标是研究刚毛微结构的特定几何参数如何影响气动效率、升力生成与飞行稳定性，从而推进微型飞行器技术前沿。\n成果：学生预期产出：\n1. 仿生机翼设计与建模：\n1.1 以寄生蜂等生物为灵感，使用 CAD 设计一系列具有不同几何参数（如刚毛密度、长度、间距）的刚毛翼原型；\n1.2 开发简化气动模型，在低雷诺数条件下仿真并预测这些刚毛翼设计的性能。\n2. 原型制造与测试：\n2.1 采用 3D 打印或微加工等合适材料与工艺制造设计好的机翼原型；\n2.2 开发实验台架（如系留飞行装置或适配低雷诺数流动的风洞），评估所制机翼的升力、功耗与飞行稳定性。\n3. 性能分析与优化：\n3.1 量化刚毛翼几何参数与关键气动性能指标之间的相关性；\n3.2 提出优化的机翼设计，展示在相同条件下相比基线实心翼设计具有更高气动效率或更强飞行稳定性。\n评估：项目成功与否将基于：\n1. 所制刚毛翼实测升力-功率比（反映气动效率）；\n2. 实验台架上机翼原型表现出的飞行稳定性与机动性；\n3. 对比分析，证明低雷诺数条件下优化刚毛翼设计相对传统实心翼设计的性能优势。评估应突出结构完整性、重量与气动性能之间的权衡。\n本项目将获得曾志伟（Alan Tsang）教授课题组博士生的指导支持。","cats":["AERO","DES","MAT","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ALANCHT-10","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"蝴蝶启发的扑翼微型无人机稳定飞行控制","titleEn":"Stable Flight Control of a Butterfly-Inspired Flapping-Wing Micro Drones","areaZh":"航空航天 | 设计与制造；材料与固体力学；机器人学；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Aerospace | Design and manufacture; Materials and Solids; Robotics; Drones and Control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor TSANG Alan Cheng Hou","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Introduction:\nButterflies exhibit unique flight characteristics, such as exceptionally low flapping frequencies (~10 Hz) and significant wing-body coupling, which contribute to their remarkable agility and long-distance migration capabilities. These traits present a promising yet underexplored model for developing low-noise, highmaneuverability micro aerial vehicles (MAVs). This project aims to decode the flight mechanics of butterflies and translate them into engineering principles for bio-inspired drone design, with a focus on achieving auditory stealth through low-frequency flapping.\nContent:\nThis project involves the study of butterfly flight mechanics and the development of a butterfly-inspired flapping-wing micro aerial vehicle (FWMAV). The core work includes simplifying the complex wing-body coupling observed in butterflies into a practical mechanical model, and utilizing this low-frequency flapping strategy to design, build, and test a prototype MAV that prioritizes controlled, stable, and quiet flight.\nOutcomes:\nStudents are expected to produce the following outcomes:\n1. Bio-mechanical Analysis and Simplification:\n1.1 Conduct a thorough analysis of butterfly flight kinematics (e.g., wing stroke amplitude, body oscillation)\nbased on existing literature and video data.\n1.2 Propose a simplified and mechanically feasible wing-body coupling mechanism that captures the\nessential aerodynamics of butterfly flight.\n2. Prototype Design and Fabrication:\n2.1 Design a butterfly-inspired FWMAV prototype using CAD software, incorporating the simplified\ncoupling mechanism and focusing on low-frequency flapping actuation.\n2.2 Fabricate the key components of the MAV, including the wing structures and transmission system, using appropriate materials and manufacturing techniques.\n3. Flight Testing and Performance Validation:\n3.1 Develop a test platform to evaluate the prototype's flight stability, basic maneuverability, and noise levels.\n3.2 Demonstrate controlled flight and quantify the noise reduction achieved by the low-frequency flapping approach compared to conventional high-frequency MAVs.\nEvaluations:\nThe success of the project will be evaluated based on:\n1 The achievement of stable, controlled flight in the prototype MAV.\n2 Measured noise levels demonstrating a significant reduction attributable to the low-frequency flapping design.\n3 The prototype's agility in performing basic maneuvers, validating the effectiveness of the simplified wingbody coupling mechanism. The evaluation will highlight the trade-offs between mechanical complexity, flight stability, and auditory stealth.\nThe project will be supported by PhD student mentors from Prof. Alan Tsang's lab.","descZh":"引言：蝴蝶具有独特的飞行特性，如极低的扑翼频率（约 10Hz）与显著的翼-体耦合，这造就了其非凡的敏捷性与长距离迁徙能力。这些特性为开发低噪声、高机动性微型飞行器（MAV）提供了有前景但尚未充分开发的模型。本项目旨在破解蝴蝶的飞行力学，并将其转化为仿生无人机设计的工程原理，重点是通过低频扑翼实现听觉隐身。\n内容：本项目涉及蝴蝶飞行力学研究，以及仿蝴蝶扑翼微型飞行器（FWMAV）的开发。核心工作包括将蝴蝶复杂的翼-体耦合简化为实用机械模型，并利用这种低频扑翼策略设计、制造与测试一款优先实现可控、稳定且安静飞行的 MAV 原型。\n成果：学生预期产出：\n1. 生物力学分析与简化：\n1.1 基于现有文献与视频数据，对蝴蝶飞行运动学（如扑翼幅度、机体振荡）进行深入分析；\n1.2 提出一种简化且机械可行的翼-体耦合机构，捕捉蝴蝶飞行的基本空气动力学。\n2. 原型设计与制造：\n2.1 使用 CAD 软件设计仿蝴蝶 FWMAV 原型，融入简化耦合机构并聚焦低频扑翼驱动；\n2.2 使用合适材料与制造工艺制造 MAV 的关键部件，包括机翼结构与传动系统。\n3. 飞行测试与性能验证：\n3.1 开发测试平台，评估原型的飞行稳定性、基本机动性与噪声水平；\n3.2 演示受控飞行，并量化与常规高频 MAV 相比、低频扑翼方案实现的降噪效果。\n评估：项目成功与否将基于：\n1. 原型 MAV 是否实现稳定、受控飞行；\n2. 实测噪声水平是否证明低频扑翼设计带来显著降噪；\n3. 原型执行基本机动时的敏捷性，验证简化翼-体耦合机构的有效性。评估将突出机械复杂度、飞行稳定性与听觉隐身之间的权衡。\n本项目将获得曾志伟（Alan Tsang）教授课题组博士生的指导支持。","cats":["AERO","MAT","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ALANCHT-11","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"参数化低空飞行器与eVTOL构型的CFD驱动稳定性分析","titleEn":"CFD-Driven Stability Analysis for Parametric Low-Altitude Aircraft and eVTOL\rConfigurations","areaZh":"航空航天；热流体","areaEn":"Aerospace; Thermofluids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor TSANG Alan Cheng Hou","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Introduction:\nContinuous annual growth in lowaltitude operations poses challenges to aircraft configuration design and control robustness. A stability analysis study based on parametric geometry and CFDdriven aerodynamic models is essential for predicting and alleviating potential stability and control risks across flight envelopes.\nIn this project, the student will use CFD-based numerical simulation method (OpenVSP and its integrated VSPAERO aerodynamic solver) to build parametric aircraft geometries and develop a streamlined numerical analysis pipeline to predict and assess static stability and control characteristics of fixedwing and eVTOL “Lift + Cruise” configurations. The objective is to provide fast, lowcost, and repeatable earlystage stability evaluations that support configuration selection and control surface sizing through CFD simulations.\nContent:\nThis project involves the development of a fast, repeatable OpenVSP/VSPAERO-based analysis system that can:\n1. Build parametric aircraft models (wing, fuselage, tail including V-tail, control surfaces) and reference an eVTOL “Lift + Cruise” example.\n2. Compute longitudinal and lateral–directional static stability derivatives and control derivatives.\n3. Diagnose cross-coupling effects (e.g., adverse yaw, ruddervator interactions) and quantify sensitivity to CG location and geometry.\n4. Provide actionable guidance for early-stage design and control surface sizing.\nOutcomes:\nStudents are expected to produce the following outcomes:\n1.Geometry and data setup: OpenVSP parametric models (.vsp3) for at least three configurations: baseline fixed-wing, V-tail variant, and an eVTOL Lift + Cruise layout with nacelles and actuator disk placeholders.\n2. Stability analytics algorithm development: VSPAERO batch run scripts to sweep angles (, ) and control deflections (flap, aileron, ruddervator). Post-processing algorithms to read .polar outputs and compute stability/control derivatives (CL, CMy, CY, CMx, CMz, dC/d).\n3. Simulation environment and validation: Sensitivity analysis to mesh resolution, wake parameters, control gain conventions, and CG shifts. Optional actuator disk on/off studies to observe qualitative propulsion\n4. Evaluation: Accuracy and robustness evaluated by internal consistency (mesh/wake independence) and theoretical trends (e.g., Roskam formulas, lifting-line).\nThe project will be supported by PhD student mentors from Prof. Alan Tsang's lab.","descZh":"引言：\n低空运营的持续逐年增长，给飞行器构型设计与控制稳健性带来挑战。基于参数化几何与 CFD 驱动气动模型的稳定性分析研究，对于预测与缓解整个飞行包线内潜在的稳定性与控制风险至关重要。\n在本项目中，学生将使用基于 CFD 的数值仿真方法（OpenVSP 及其集成的 VSPAERO 气动求解器）构建参数化飞行器几何模型，并开发一套精简的数值分析流程，以预测和评估固定翼与 eVTOL“Lift + Cruise（升力+巡航）”构型的静稳定性与控制特性。目标是提供快速、低成本、可重复的早期稳定性评估，通过 CFD 仿真为构型选择与操纵面尺寸确定提供支撑。\n内容：\n本项目涉及开发一套快速、可重复的基于 OpenVSP/VSPAERO 的分析系统，该系统能够：\n1. 构建参数化飞行器模型（机翼、机身、尾翼，含 V 型尾翼、操纵面），并参考一个 eVTOL“Lift + Cruise（升力+巡航）”示例。\n2. 计算纵向与横航向静稳定导数及操纵导数。\n3. 诊断交叉耦合效应（如不利偏航、方向升降舵（ruddervator）相互作用），并量化对重心（CG）位置与几何形状的敏感性。\n4. 为早期设计与操纵面尺寸确定提供可操作的指导。\n成果：\n预期学生产出以下成果：\n1. 几何与数据设置：至少三种构型的 OpenVSP 参数化模型（.vsp3）：基准固定翼、V 型尾翼变体，以及带有短舱与作动盘占位符的 eVTOL 升力+巡航布局。\n2. 稳定性分析算法开发：用于扫描角度（α、β）与操纵面偏转（襟翼、副翼、方向升降舵）的 VSPAERO 批量运行脚本。用于读取 .polar 输出并计算稳定性/操纵导数（CL、CMy、CY、CMx、CMz、dC/d）的后处理算法。\n3. 仿真环境与验证：对网格分辨率、尾流参数、操纵增益约定及重心偏移的敏感性分析。可选的作动盘开/关研究，以观察定性推进效应。\n4. 评估：通过内部一致性（网格/尾流无关性）与理论趋势（如 Roskam 公式、升力线）评估精度与稳健性。\n本项目将由 Prof. Alan Tsang 实验室的博士生导师提供支持。","cats":["AERO","ENE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JLAM-20","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"智能人体跟随气瓶搬运车","titleEn":"Smart Human Following Gas Cylinder Carrier","areaZh":"机器人学；控制","areaEn":"Robotics; control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LAM James","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-17 17:34:01","descriptionText":"Project Description \nThis project aims to develop a prototype trolley capable of safely transporting a standard industrial gas cylinder (approx. 80 kg, 1.6 m tall, 250 mm diameter). The trolley will be battery powered and demonstrate two modes of operation:\n Manual control via a handheld controller\n Autonomous following, where the trolley tracks a designated person while avoiding obstacles\n The prototype should travel at least 1 km per charge and climb ramps with a gradient of 1/10 on uneven concrete surfaces.\n Team Structure \nThe project will be completed by two students:\n Mechanical Design Student: Responsible for SolidWorks design, cylinder handling mechanism, and terrain capable chassis.\n Software Student: Responsible for refining existing navigation software, adjusting parameters for cylinder weight, dimensions, and terrain conditions, and ensuring smooth integration with the mechanical system.\n The Department will support fabrication of mechanical parts based on the SolidWorks design. Students are encouraged to source and adapt existing trolley or carrier products from the market as a base platform, modifying them to meet the project requirements.\n Key Features \n Cylinder Handling: Secure pickup and anchorage system for a 60 kg cylinder.\n Mobility: Reliable operation on uneven concrete, ramp climbing up to 1/10 gradient.\n Human Following Navigation: Person tracking with obstacle avoidance, refined for the designated route.\n Deliverables \n1. Mechanical Deliverables\n CAD model of trolley frame and cylinder holder in SolidWorks.\n Fabricated chassis and anchorage system (with departmental support).\n Load test with a standard cylinder to confirm stability and ramp climbing.\n 2. Software Deliverables\n Adaptation of existing human following program.\n Parameter tuning for cylinder weight, height, and terrain.\n Integration test: trolley follows a staff member along a short route.\n 3. Final Demonstration\n Safely load and secure a cylinder.\n Transport cylinder from Haking Wong Building to Yam Pak Building.\n Show both manual and autonomous modes under specified terrain conditions.\n Prerequisites \n Proficiency in C programming (for refining navigation and control).\n Proficiency in SolidWorks mechanical design (for chassis and cylinder handling).","descZh":"项目描述：本项目旨在开发一台原型运输小车，能够安全运输标准工业气瓶（约 80kg、高 1.6m、直径 250mm）。小车采用电池供电，需演示两种运行模式：\n- 通过手持控制器手动控制；\n- 自主跟随：小车跟踪指定人员并避障。\n原型应每次充电至少行驶 1km，并能在不平整混凝土表面攀爬 1/10 坡度的斜坡。\n团队结构：项目由两名学生完成：\n- 机械设计学生：负责 SolidWorks 设计、气瓶搬运机构与适应地形的底盘；\n- 软件学生：负责优化现有导航软件、针对气瓶重量/尺寸与地形条件调整参数，并确保与机械系统顺畅集成。\n院系将根据 SolidWorks 设计支持机械零件制造。鼓励学生采购并改造市面上现成的运输小车或搬运产品作为基础平台，改装以满足项目要求。\n关键特性：\n- 气瓶搬运：为 60kg 气瓶提供可靠的取放与固定系统；\n- 移动性：在不平整混凝土上可靠运行，可攀爬最高 1/10 坡度；\n- 人形跟随导航：人员跟踪并避障，针对指定路线优化。\n交付成果：\n1. 机械交付：SolidWorks 中小车车架与气瓶固定器的 CAD 模型；制造完成的底盘与固定系统（院系支持）；使用标准气瓶进行载荷测试，确认稳定性与爬坡能力。\n2. 软件交付：改造现有人员跟随程序；针对气瓶重量、高度与地形进行参数调优；集成测试：小车沿短路线跟随一名工作人员。\n3. 最终演示：安全装载并固定气瓶；将气瓶从黄克竞楼（Haking Wong Building）运至任白楼（Yam Pak Building）；在指定地形条件下演示手动与自主两种模式。\n先修要求：熟练掌握 C 语言编程（用于优化导航与控制）；熟练掌握 SolidWorks 机械设计（用于底盘与气瓶搬运）。","cats":["CTRL","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JWZHOU-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"数据中心冷却的热阻与能效表征","titleEn":"Characterization of thermal resistance and energy efficiency for data center cooling","areaZh":"热建模；材料表征；电子设备冷却","areaEn":"Thermal modeling; Material characterization; Electronics cooling","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-14 17:07:02","descriptionText":"As microelectronic chips continue to develop towards smaller dimensions and data centers shift towards higher performance and greater density, their electricity consumption increases significantly. Local hotspots and excessive temperatures have therefore become major concerns in today's computing and AI infrastructure.\nHeat generated inside a chip must travel through semiconductor materials, dielectric layers, metallic thin films, thermal interface materials, and packaging structures. This project will employ thermal characterization techniques to quantitatively measure the thermal conductivity and interfacial thermal resistance in the heat dissipation pathway and identify the cooling bottleneck. Students will learn the basic principles and experimental procedures of thermal measurement, such as thermoreflectance methods, transient plane source method, and steady state methods. Students will conduct sample measurements, process the experimental data, and extract the relevant thermal properties through model fitting.\nWith the measured thermal properties, the effects of thermal conductivity, interfacial thermal resistance, and material layout on chip temperature rise and hotspot temperature will be investigated. Based on the experimental and modelling results, students will learn strategies for improving heat-transfer pathways and the energy performance of electronic chips and high-density data centers.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nWe invite responsible and motivated students with an interest in experimental research and data center thermal management to participate in this project. Basic knowledge of heat transfer, engineering thermodynamics, or the thermal properties of materials would be helpful. Experience in experimental work, data processing, MATLAB, or Python would be advantageous but is not essential.","descZh":"随着微电子芯片持续向更小尺寸发展，数据中心向更高性能与更高密度迈进，其电力消耗显著增加。局部热点与过高温度因此成为当今计算与 AI 基础设施的主要关切。\n芯片内部产生的热量必须依次穿过半导体材料、介电层、金属薄膜、热界面材料与封装结构。本项目将采用热表征技术，定量测量散热通路中的热导率与界面热阻，并识别散热瓶颈。学生将学习热测量（如热反射法、瞬态平面热源法与稳态法）的基本原理与实验流程；开展样品测量、处理实验数据，并通过模型拟合提取相关热学性质。\n利用测得的热学性质，将研究热导率、界面热阻与材料布局对芯片温升与热点温度的影响。基于实验与建模结果，学生将学习改善电子芯片与高密度数据中心传热路径与能耗表现的策略。\n先修/并修要求：我们欢迎对实验研究与数据中心热管理感兴趣、认真负责且有动力的学生参与本项目。具备传热、工程热力学或材料热学性质基础知识会有帮助；有实验、数据处理、MATLAB 或 Python 经验者优先，但非必需。","cats":["ELE","ENE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-JWZHOU-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"具有优异能量输运性能半导体材料的数据驱动学习","titleEn":"Data-driven learning of semiconductor materials with exceptional energy transport properties","areaZh":"计算材料科学；电子材料；数据驱动建模","areaEn":"Computational materials science; Electronic materials; Data-driven modelling","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHOU Jiawei","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-14 19:34:01","descriptionText":"Semiconductor materials sit at the core of all semiconductor technologies. Among all physical properties, the energy transport properties, such as the mobility and conductivity, are the most critical properties of the semiconductor materials. However, it is currently challenging to accurately simulate these properties and to engineer them at will.\n This project aims to develop a reproducible, physics-informed workflow to connect fundamental material structure data to energy transport properties. We aim to use machine learning to train interpretable surrogate models for rapid materials screening. By investigating the fundamental material descriptors for energy transport in semiconductor materials, we aim to accelerate the material discovery process.\n Pre- and co-requisites:\n We are looking for students who have interests in applying data science and machine learning concepts to semiconductor materials and/or devices. The following skills will be preferred: 1Basic materials science, solid-state physics, engineering maths, or Python desirable; Training will be provided regarding basic material database extraction and machine learning.","descZh":"半导体材料是所有半导体技术的核心。在所有物理性质中，能量输运性质（如迁移率与电导率）是半导体材料最关键的性质。然而，目前精确模拟这些性质并随心所欲地对其进行工程调控仍具挑战。\n本项目旨在开发一种可复现、物理信息驱动的工作流程，将基础材料结构数据与能量输运性质连接起来。我们希望利用机器学习训练可解释的代理模型，用于快速材料筛选；通过研究半导体材料能量输运的基础材料描述符，加速材料发现过程。\n先修/并修要求：我们希望招收对将数据科学与机器学习方法应用于半导体材料和/或器件的学生。以下技能更受欢迎：具备基础材料科学、固态物理、工程数学或 Python 能力为佳；将就基础材料数据库提取与机器学习提供培训。","cats":["ELE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PCYC-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向低空卫星与无人机的轻量化太阳能电池板","titleEn":"Lightweight solar panels for low-altitude satellites and drones","areaZh":"航空航天；低空技术；能源","areaEn":"Aerospace; LAT; Energy","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHOW Philip Chi Yung","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This MSc project primarily involves conducting a thorough literature review on lightweight solar panel technologies suitable for low-altitude satellites and drones, with the aim of synthesizing current advancements, challenges, and potential future directions in the field. The review will cover materials, structural design considerations, and integration techniques that contribute to reducing weight and enhancing efficiency in small-scale aerospace applications. Additionally, there is the potential to complement the literature review with practical laboratory work, such as testing lightweight solar cell samples, characterization of photovoltaic performance, or exploring fabrication methods, to supplement the theoretical findings and provide practical insights.\nCandidates should have a solid background in electrical engineering, materials science, or aerospace engineering, along with a keen interest in renewable energy and solar technologies. Prior experience with laboratory experiments related to photovoltaics, as well as familiarity with research tools like CAD software or analysis programs such as MATLAB or Python, will be advantageous (but not required). Strong analytical abilities, excellent report-writing skills, and the ability to critically evaluate scientific literature are essential. This project offers a valuable opportunity to develop comprehensive knowledge in lightweight solar energy systems, which could inform future experimental investigations or development initiatives in the field.","descZh":"这一硕士项目主要涉及对适用于低轨道卫星和无人机的轻量化太阳能电池板技术进行全面文献综述，旨在综合该领域当前的进展、挑战以及潜在的未来方向。综述将涵盖有助于在小型航空航天应用中减轻重量并提升效率的材料、结构设计考虑以及集成技术。此外，还有可能辅以实际的实验室工作来补充文献综述，例如测试轻量化太阳能电池样品、表征光伏性能，或探索制造方法，以补充理论发现并提供实际见解。\n候选人应具备电气工程、材料科学或航空航天工程方面的扎实背景，并对可再生能源和太阳能技术有浓厚兴趣。具有光伏相关实验室实验经验，以及熟悉 CAD 软件或 MATLAB、Python 等分析程序等研究工具者，将更具优势（但非必需）。扎实的分析能力、出色的报告撰写能力以及批判性评价科学文献的能力是必不可少的。本项目提供了宝贵的机会，帮助学生建立轻量化太阳能系统的全面知识，这可为该领域未来的实验研究或开发计划提供参考。","cats":["AERO","ENE","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PCYC-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"柔性、轻量化且可印刷的太阳能光伏器件","titleEn":"Flexible, lightweight and printed solar photovoltaic devices","areaZh":"太阳能；先进制造","areaEn":"Solar energy; advanced manufacturing","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHOW Philip Chi Yung","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 17:07:01","descriptionText":"Developing efficient and low-cost solar PV cells are very important for renewable energy and mitigating the effect of climate change. Current solar PV cells available in the market are based on inorganic crystalline silicon. These inorganic cells are bulky and inflexible, thus restricting their use and installation especially in city areas such as Hong Kong. Furthermore, they are not biodegradable and it takes a lot of energy to produce them. Therefore, the development of new materials for next-generation solar cells that promise to be low-cost, flexible, lightweight and biodegradable has attracted much attentions from scientists and engineers all over the world. Furthermore, lightweight solar cells may also be useful for powering drones. \nRecently, a number of promising new solar cell materials and technologies have emerged, including polymer (plastic) solar cells, metal halide perovskite solar cells, quantum dot solar cells, etc. In this project, each student will carry out an in-depth literature search about recent research progress about one of these new technologies, provide a critical overview about the future directions of solar cell material research, learn how to fabricate working solar cells in our laboratory as well as detailed characterization of the photovoltaic performance of these devices.","descZh":"开发高效低成本的太阳能光伏电池对可再生能源发展及减缓气候变化至关重要。目前市场上的太阳能光伏电池基于无机晶体硅，笨重且缺乏柔韧性，限制了其在香港等城市地区的使用与安装；而且它们不可生物降解，生产耗能大。因此，开发有望实现低成本、柔性、轻质与可生物降解的下一代太阳能电池新材料，引起了全世界科学家与工程师的广泛关注。此外，轻质太阳能电池也可能用于为无人机供电。\n近期已涌现多种有前景的新型太阳能电池材料与技术，包括聚合物（塑料）太阳能电池、金属卤化物钙钛矿太阳能电池、量子点太阳能电池等。在本项目中，每名学生将就其中一种新技术的最新研究进展开展深入文献调研，就太阳能电池材料研究的未来方向提供批判性综述，学习在实验室中制备可工作的太阳能电池，并对这些器件的光伏性能进行详细表征。","cats":["DES","ENE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PCYC-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向自旋调控光子器件与激光应用的金属卤化物钙钛矿","titleEn":"Metal halide perovskites for spin-controlled photonic devices and lasing applications","areaZh":"光子学；光电子学；量子器件","areaEn":"Photonics; optoelectronics; quantum devices","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHOW Philip Chi Yung","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 17:07:01","descriptionText":"Background & Project Overview:\nTransition metal doping in halide perovskites and diluted magnetic semiconductor structures has emerged as a compelling route to introduce strong carrier-dopant exchange interactions, tunable spin polarization, and novel magneto-optical phenomena. By coupling spin degrees of freedom with the optical cavity, spin lasers offer unique advantages such as ultra-low threshold pumping, high-speed polarization modulation, and polarization-controlled coherent emission. This project aims to explore the spin-dependent optical properties, energy-transfer kinetics, and spin-lasing potential of transition metal-doped metal halide perovskite materials integrated with optical microcavity architectures.\nKey Student Tasks & Scope of Work:\n 1. Literature Review: Perform a comprehensive state-of-the-art literature review on spin-polarized optical processes, dopant-mediated energy transfer pathways, magnetic circular dichroism/polarization memory, and cavity-enhanced spin lasing architectures in halide perovskites.\n 2. Optical & Material Characterization: Analyze optical datasets (e.g., steady-state/time-resolved photoluminescence, absorption, spin-polarized optical pumping thresholds, and degree of circular polarization) collected in the optical laboratory.\n 3. Device & Cavity Analysis: Evaluate cavity designs (e.g., Fabry–Pérot, distributed feedback, or microdisk resonators) to model optical feedback, modal gain, and spin-flip relaxation dynamics necessary for room-temperature spin-polarized lasing.\n 4. Final Reporting: Synthesize findings into a rigorous dissertation and presentation, highlighting key device optimization strategies and future integration pathways for spin-photonic systems.\nPre-requisites / Co-requisites:\nUndergraduate-level background in Materials Science, Physics, Mechanical Engineering, Electrical Engineering, or related fields. Basic familiarity with semiconductor device physics or physical optics would be helpful.","descZh":"背景与项目概述：在卤化物钙钛矿与稀磁半导体结构中进行过渡金属掺杂，已成为引入强载流子-掺杂剂交换相互作用、可调自旋极化与新颖磁光现象的有力途径。通过将自旋自由度与光学腔耦合，自旋激光提供超低阈值泵浦、高速偏振调制与偏振可控相干发射等独特优势。本项目旨在探索与光学微腔架构集成的过渡金属掺杂金属卤化物钙钛矿材料的自旋相关光学性质、能量转移动力学与自旋激光潜力。\n主要学生任务与工作范围：\n1. 文献综述：对卤化物钙钛矿中的自旋极化光学过程、掺杂剂介导的能量转移路径、磁圆二色性/偏振记忆与腔增强自旋激光架构开展全面的前沿文献综述；\n2. 光学与材料表征：分析光学实验室采集的光学数据集（如稳态/时间分辨光致发光、吸收、自旋极化光泵浦阈值与圆偏振度）；\n3. 器件与腔体分析：评估腔体设计（如法布里-珀罗、分布式反馈或微盘谐振腔），对实现室温自旋极化激光所需的光反馈、模式增益与自旋翻转弛豫动力学进行建模；\n4. 最终报告：将研究结论整理为严谨的论文与演示，突出关键器件优化策略及自旋光子系统的未来集成路径。\n先修/并修要求：具备材料科学、物理、机械工程、电气工程或相关领域的本科背景；了解半导体器件物理或物理光学基础会有帮助。","cats":["ELE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PCYC-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向太阳能制氢与生物成像的有机纳米颗粒","titleEn":"Organic nanoparticles for solar hydrogen production and bioimaging","areaZh":"材料科学；生物成像；光子学","areaEn":"Material science; bioimaging; photonics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHOW Philip Chi Yung","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 17:07:01","descriptionText":"Organic semiconductors (OSCs) exhibit exceptional potential due to their ability to simultaneously absorb near-infrared (NIR) light and have tunable energy levels through precise molecular engineering. These unique properties make them highly promising for applications in solar-driven hydrogen (H₂) production and NIR imaging-guided surgery. This project aims to design, fabricate, and deploy organic nanoparticles (NPs) tailored for both efficient solar H₂ generation and advanced biomedical imaging.\nBy processing OSCs into water-dispersible nanoparticles, we seek to significantly enhance their biocompatibility and photocatalytic performance in aqueous environments. Through strategic modification of their chemical structures and energy alignments, we will develop customizable NP systems optimized for either photocatalytic hydrogen evolution or biomedical imaging. This targeted nanomaterial approach offers versatile solutions that bridge renewable energy conversion and precision healthcare, advancing sustainable energy technologies and innovative medical diagnostics.\nIn this project, MSc students will first conduct a literature review. Then the students will assist or participate in synthesizing organic NPs, evaluating their photocatalytic H₂ evolution and bioimaging performance, as well as conducting basic optical characterizations. This hands-on research offers training in organic nanomaterial synthesis, spectroscopy analysis, and interdisciplinary applications in renewable energy and biomedical imaging. Through this project, the students will gain comprehensive training in organic optoelectronic materials, spectroscopic analysis, and interdisciplinary energy-biomedical applications. Additionally, each student will complete an individual project report and deliver a presentation, if needed.\nPreferred skills: Basic background knowledge/experience in chemical synthesis and optical spectroscopy are useful but not required for this project.","descZh":"有机半导体（OSC）展现出非凡潜力：其能同时吸收近红外（NIR）光，并可通过精确的分子工程调谐能级。这些独特性质使其在太阳能驱动制氢（H₂）与 NIR 成像引导手术等应用中前景广阔。本项目旨在设计、制造并部署定制化的有机纳米粒子（NP），同时用于高效太阳能 H₂ 生成与先进生物医学成像。\n通过将 OSC 加工为水分散纳米粒子，我们力求显著提升其在水环境中的生物相容性与光催化性能。通过战略性修饰其化学结构与能级排列，我们将开发可定制的 NP 系统，针对光催化析氢或生物医学成像进行优化。这种靶向纳米材料方法提供兼顾可再生能源转换与精准医疗的通用方案，推动可持续能源技术与创新医学诊断的发展。\n在本项目中，硕士生将首先开展文献综述，随后协助或参与有机 NP 的合成、评估其光催化 H₂ 析出与生物成像性能，并进行基础光学表征。这项实践研究提供有机纳米材料合成、光谱分析以及可再生能源与生物医学成像跨学科应用的训练。通过本项目，学生将获得有机光电材料、光谱分析及能源-生物医学跨学科应用的全面训练。此外，每名学生将完成一份个人项目报告，并在需要时进行展示。\n偏好技能：具备化学合成与光学光谱的基础知识/经验会有帮助，但非本项目必需。","cats":["BIO","ELE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YHCHEN-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"连续体机械臂的力学特性表征与设计改进","titleEn":"Mechanical Characterization and Design Improvement of a continuum robotic arm","areaZh":"机器人学","areaEn":"Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHEN Yong Hua","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 12:04:01","descriptionText":"This project will establish a reproducible mechanical test program for an existing continuum robotic arm and determine how one selected operating variable, such as tendon pretension or compression state will influence its mechanical performance. This project requires collaborative work with a robotic company in HK Science park.\n Selected references\n [1] Lin et al. \"3D Printing of a Modular Antagonistic Variable Stiffness Bellow Continuum Robot\", Proc. IEEE ICARM 2025\n [2] Zhang et al. \"Design and stiffness Control of a variable length Continuum Robot for Endoscopic Surgery\". IEEE Trans. Autom. Sci. Eng. 2025.\n Bruder et al. \"Increasing the Payload Capacity of Soft Robotic Arms by Localizing Stiffening\", Sci Robot, 2023.","descZh":"本项目将为现有连续型（连续体）机械臂建立一套可复现的力学测试程序，并确定所选的单个运行变量（如肌腱预紧力或压缩状态）如何影响其力学性能。本项目需要与香港科学园的一家机器人公司协作开展。\n参考（部分）：\n[1] Lin 等，\"3D Printing of a Modular Antagonistic Variable Stiffness Bellow Continuum Robot\", Proc. IEEE ICARM 2025\n[2] Zhang 等，\"Design and stiffness Control of a variable length Continuum Robot for Endoscopic Surgery\", IEEE Trans. Autom. Sci. Eng. 2025\nBruder 等，\"Increasing the Payload Capacity of Soft Robotic Arms by Localizing Stiffening\", Sci Robot, 2023","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YHCHEN-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"连续体机械臂的实验运动学与闭环位置控制","titleEn":"Experimental Kinematics and Closed-Loop Position Control of a Continuum Robotic Arm","areaZh":"机器人学","areaEn":"Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHEN Yong Hua","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 12:04:01","descriptionText":"Use a fixed camera with markers, or an equivalent external method to measure two-dimensional tip position. Collect tendon-command, pretension or stiffness state, and tip-position data over a defined workspace, fit a practical mapping, and implement a controller that updates tendon commands from measured position error. Open loop and close loop performance will be compared on the same target set.\n This is a collaborative project with a robotic company in HK Science Park.","descZh":"使用带标记点的固定相机或等效的外部方法测量二维末端位置。在定义的工作空间内采集肌腱指令、预紧力/刚度状态与末端位置数据，拟合实用的映射关系，并实现一个控制器，根据测得的位置误差更新肌腱指令。将在同一组目标上比较开环与闭环性能。\n本项目是与香港科学园一家机器人公司合作的协作项目。","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YHCHEN-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"自适应变刚度夹具的设计与评估","titleEn":"Design and Evaluation of an Adaptive Variable stiffness Fixture","areaZh":"机器人学；设计与制造","areaEn":"Robotics; Design and Manufacturing","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHEN Yong Hua","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 11:50:42","descriptionText":"This project will design and evaluate an adaptive fixture module that uses a compliant contact interface and vacuum driven particle jamming. The fixture should conforn to a component in its compliant state, stiffen for secure holding, and release to component without permanent deformation.\n This project is a collaborative work with a robotic company in HK Science Park.\n References\n 1. Kiyokawa et al.\" Soft-jig-Driven Assembly Operations\", Pro. IEEE ICRA 2021\n 2. Brigido et al. \"Flexural Models for Vacuum Packed Particles as Variable stiffness Mechanism in Smart Structures\", Phys. Rev. Applied 2022.","descZh":"本项目将设计并评估一种自适应夹具模块，它采用柔顺接触界面和真空驱动的颗粒阻塞（particle jamming）。该夹具应能在柔顺状态下贴合（conform to）零件，在需要牢固夹持时变硬，并在释放零件时不产生永久变形。\n本项目是与香港科学园一家机器人公司的合作项目。\n参考文献\n1. Kiyokawa et al. “Soft-jig-Driven Assembly Operations”, Proc. IEEE ICRA 2021\n2. Brigido et al. “Flexural Models for Vacuum Packed Particles as Variable stiffness Mechanism in Smart Structures”, Phys. Rev. Applied 2022.","cats":["DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YHCHEN-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"软体机械手的外部可穿戴位形感知","titleEn":"External Wearable Configuration Sensing for a Soft Robotic Hand","areaZh":"机器人学","areaEn":"Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHEN Yong Hua","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 11:51:44","descriptionText":"This project will develop or select a low cost wearable sensing glove or sleeve that is mounted on the soft robotic hand itself. The sensor signal will be calibrated against an independent ground truth measurement and mapped to a defined robot configuration variable, such as finger bending angle or fingertip displacement.\n This is a collaborative work with a robotic company in HK Science Park.\n References\n Tashakori et al. \"Capturing Complex Hand Movements and Object Interaction Using Machine Learning-Powered Stretchable Smart Textile Gloves\", Nat. Mach. Intell. 2024.","descZh":"本项目将开发或选用一种低成本可穿戴传感手套/护套，安装在软体机械手本体上。传感器信号将与独立的真值测量进行标定，并映射到定义好的机器人构型变量（如手指弯曲角度或指尖位移）。\n本项目是与香港科学园一家机器人公司的协作项目。\n参考文献：\nTashakori 等，\"Capturing Complex Hand Movements and Object Interaction Using Machine Learning-Powered Stretchable Smart Textile Gloves\", Nat. Mach. Intell. 2024.","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YHCHEN-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"软体机械手的触觉感知与抓取状态识别","titleEn":"Tactile Sensing and Grasp-State Recognition for a Soft Robotic Hand","areaZh":"机器人学","areaEn":"Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHEN Yong Hua","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 11:52:43","descriptionText":"This project will integrate existing pressure, force or flexible tactile sensors at selected contact regions of a soft robotic hand and use their signals to recognize contact and trial-level grasp outcome for a small set of standard objects such as sphere, cylinder, and cuboid etc.\n This is a collaborative work with a robotic company in HK Science Park.\n References\n Zhao et al. \"Embedding High Resolution Touch Across Robotic Hands Enables Adaptive Human Like Grasping\". Nat. Mach Intell. 2025.","descZh":"本项目将在软体机器人手选定接触区域集成现有的压力、力或柔性触觉传感器，并利用其信号识别一小批标准物体（如球体、圆柱体与长方体等）的接触情况与试次级别的抓取结果。\n这是与香港科学园一家机器人公司的合作项目。\n参考文献\nZhao et al. “Embedding High Resolution Touch Across Robotic Hands Enables Adaptive Human Like Grasping”. Nat. Mach Intell. 2025.","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YHCHEN-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"设计与开发3D可打印肌腱驱动机械手","titleEn":"Design and Develop 3D Printable Tendeon Driven Robotic Hand","areaZh":"机器人学","areaEn":"Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHEN Yong Hua","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 12:04:01","descriptionText":"This project requires the design and development of a robotic hand with 5 fingers, each finger has three joints. The design should be 3D printable without the need of assembly. Tendon routing and servo actuation should consider both one directional driving and bi-directional driving. A prototype must be made at the end of the project.","descZh":"本项目要求设计与开发一只具有 5 根手指的机械手，每根手指有 3 个关节。设计应可通过 3D 打印直接制成，无需装配。肌腱走线与舵机驱动需同时考虑单向驱动与双向驱动两种方式。项目结束时必须制作出原型。","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YHCHEN-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"设计与开发液体驱动机械手","titleEn":"Design and develop a liquid driven robotic hand","areaZh":"机器人学","areaEn":"Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor CHEN Yong Hua","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 12:04:01","descriptionText":"This project requires the design and development of a liquid driven robotic hand. Elastic tube and constraint sleeve are combined with selective pumps are the major components to be used in the project. The development must be prototyped with proper experimental characterization such as stiffness, liquid pressure and finger bending angle. The robotic hand must be controllable.","descZh":"本项目要求设计与开发一只液驱机械手。主要组成部分为弹性管、约束套筒与选配的泵相结合。该开发成果必须制作原型，并进行相应的实验表征，如刚度、液体压力与手指弯曲角度等。机械手必须是可控的。","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YLIN-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"3D打印技术在医疗健康领域的应用探索","titleEn":"Exploration of 3D printing technology in healthcare applications","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体力学","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LIN Yuan","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This project explores the application of advanced 3D printing (additive manufacturing) technologies to develop innovative tools for healthcare. The work integrates computational design (CAD), material science (selection and processing of biocompatible resins), and high-resolution printing techniques (such as stereolithography - SLA or Two-Photon Polymerization - TPP) to create functional prototypes. These devices will undergo rigorous characterization, including microscopic analysis, mechanical testing, and biological validation. \nThe project environment provides access to advanced fabrication and characterization facilities (3D printers, cell culture labs, mechanical testers, microscopes) under expert mentorship in biofabrication and translational medicine.","descZh":"本项目探索先进 3D 打印（增材制造）技术在开发创新医疗工具中的应用。该工作融合了计算设计（CAD）、材料科学（生物相容性树脂的选择与加工）以及高分辨率打印技术（如立体光刻 SLA 或双光子聚合 TPP），以制造功能性原型。这些器件将接受严格的表征，包括显微分析、力学测试与生物学验证。\n项目环境提供先进制造与表征设施（3D 打印机、细胞培养实验室、力学试验机、显微镜）的使用机会，并在生物制造与转化医学方面获得专家指导。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YLIN-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"新型MEMS/微流控器件的开发","titleEn":"Development of novel MEMS/microfluidic devices","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；热流体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Thermofluids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LIN Yuan","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This project pioneers the design, fabrication, and validation of innovative microfluidic and/or MEMS platforms for advanced biomedical research. Participants will engineer microscale systems leveraging fluid dynamics, microelectrode array fabrication, surface chemistry, and image analysis knowledge to address challenges such as cellular mechanobiology, high-throughput cell sorting via electrical signatures, or controlled micro-particle formation for drug delivery. The workflow spans computational modeling (e.g., COMSOL for flow/signal optimization), prototyping and fabrication using soft lithography, bio-3D printing and Digital Micromirror Device (DMD)-based lithography, and biological validation with live cells or biomolecules. Outcomes will deliver adaptable, lab-on-a-chip tools that enhance precision in cell separation and cell/tissue testing, and accelerate drug screening and diagnostic applications.","descZh":"本项目开创性地设计、制造并验证用于前沿生物医学研究的创新型微流控和/或 MEMS 平台。参与者将综合运用流体力学、微电极阵列加工、表面化学与图像分析等知识，构建微尺度系统，应对细胞力学生物学、基于电学特征的高通量细胞分选、面向药物递送的可控微粒生成等挑战。工作流程涵盖计算建模（如用 COMSOL 进行流动/信号优化）、采用软光刻、生物 3D 打印与基于数字微镜器件（DMD）的光刻开展原型制作与加工，以及利用活细胞或生物分子进行生物学验证。项目成果将提供适应性强、实验室芯片化的工具，提升细胞分离与细胞/组织测试的精度，并加速药物筛选与诊断应用。","cats":["BIO","DES","ENE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YLIN-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向机器人与外骨骼应用的多响应系统设计与制造","titleEn":"Design and fabrication of multi-responsive systems for robotic and exoskeleton applications","areaZh":"机器人；设计与制造；人工智能；材料与固体力学","areaEn":"Robotics; Design & manufacture; Artificial intelligence; Materials & solids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LIN Yuan","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This project aims at the design, fabrication, and validation of smart structures/systems for robotic applications. Specifically, by utilizing light- or magnetic-responsive materials, proper structural and functional design will be conducted for developing novel actuators and sensors. The prototypes, to be used as artificial muscles or in miniature robots and exoskeletons, will then be fabricated and systematically tested. Machine-learning based algorithms will also be explored to achieve better control and functioning of the developed systems.","descZh":"本项目旨在面向机器人应用设计、制造并验证智能结构/系统。具体而言，将利用光响应或磁响应材料，通过合理的结构与功能设计，开发新型驱动器与传感器。随后将制作并系统测试相关原型，这些原型可用作人工肌肉，或用于微型机器人与外骨骼。还将探索基于机器学习的方法，以实现对所开发系统更好的控制与功能发挥。","cats":["AI","DES","MAT","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"软件编程：基于Type-C USB3.0固件设计的高速移动充电","titleEn":"Software Programming: Exciting-Fast Mobile-Charging by Type-C USB3.0 Firmware Design","areaZh":"软件编程","areaEn":"Software Programming","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 12:34:01","descriptionText":"This dissertation addresses the growing demand for fast and efficient mobile charging driven by the rise of larger-capacity batteries in modern devices. Standards such as USB Type-C Power Delivery and proprietary protocols like Qualcomm Quick Charge aim to minimize charging time while ensuring device safety and user convenience.\n The project centers on developing firmware that can parse and implement multiple fast-charging protocols, enabling a charging system to dynamically adjust voltage and current delivery according to device requirements. Using 8051 microcontrollers, the firmware will be programmed to support USB 3.0 Type-C Power Delivery specifications and interact directly with the power converter hardware.\n Deliverables include building a working prototype that establishes communication between the mobile device and the charging system to initiate and control fast-charging sequences. Extensions may involve designing a companion mobile application for IoT-enabled smart home integration, showcasing how protocol libraries can enable broader use cases beyond personal electronics.\n This research contributes to the advancement of high-speed power delivery systems by bridging firmware design with practical power electronics applications. The outcome demonstrates how embedded programming plays a crucial role in enabling universal, reliable, and safe fast-charging technologies for next-generation devices.\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"本论文回应现代设备大容量电池兴起所推动的对快速高效移动充电日益增长的需求。USB Type-C 电力传输等标准以及高通 Quick Charge 等专有协议，旨在最大限度缩短充电时间，同时确保设备安全与用户便利。\n项目核心是开发可解析并实现多种快充协议的固件，使充电系统能根据设备需求动态调整电压与电流输出。固件将基于 8051 微控制器编程，支持 USB 3.0 Type-C PD 规范，并直接与电源转换硬件交互。\n交付成果包括搭建一个可工作原型，建立移动设备与充电系统之间的通信，以启动并控制快充流程。扩展方向可包括设计配套移动应用以实现物联网智能家居集成，展示协议库如何为个人电子之外的更广泛用例提供支持。\n本研究通过连接固件设计与实际电力电子应用，为高速供电系统的发展作出贡献；成果展示嵌入式编程在实现面向下一代设备的通用、可靠且安全的快充技术中的关键作用。\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"模拟IC设计项目：具有电流驱动能力的亚1V带隙基准设计","titleEn":"Analog IC Design Project: Sub 1-V Bandgap Design with Current Driving Capability","areaZh":"模拟IC设计","areaEn":"Analog IC Design","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 12:34:01","descriptionText":"This dissertation explores the design of low-voltage pure CMOS bandgap references (BGRs) with current driving capability, a critical component for battery-operated mobile and IoT devices. Conventional CMOS BGRs, often derived from parasitic vertical substrate structures in digital CMOS processes, face challenges when directly sourcing or sinking current. Even at small loads (≈10 µA), the output reference voltage collapses due to high output impedance, making them unsuitable for noise-sensitive applications such as high-speed ADCs or SMPSs.\n While some CMOS BGRs can function below 1 V, they typically cannot deliver the required current levels. Sub-1 V BGRs with low output impedance and robust current driving capability would therefore be of high value, enabling practical integration in advanced analog and mixed-signal systems. However, design is difficult due to the intrinsic bandgap voltage of silicon (~1.25 V) and the stringent input common-mode range requirements of the error amplifier, which must ensure stability and accuracy across supply variations.\n The project focuses on designing and simulating sub-1 V CMOS BGR circuits, beginning with a low-dropout regulator (LDO) reference under a 1 V supply. Initial designs may emphasize temperature-independent voltage references without current drive, followed by extended designs incorporating current-driving capability in LDO or Source Follower modes. LTSpice will be the primary simulation tool, with opportunities to learn analog IC layout if time allows.\n Ultimately, this work aims to contribute design techniques that enhance the practicality and robustness of sub-1 V CMOS BGRs for next-generation low-power electronic systems\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"本论文探讨具有电流驱动能力的低压纯 CMOS 带隙基准源（bandgap reference，BGR）的设计，这是电池供电的移动设备和物联网设备中的一个关键组件。传统 CMOS BGR 通常源自数字 CMOS 工艺中的寄生纵向衬底结构，在直接提供（source）或吸收（sink）电流时面临挑战。即使在小负载（约 10 µA）下，由于高输出阻抗，输出基准电压也会崩溃，使其不适用于高速 ADC 或 SMPS 等对噪声敏感的应用。\n虽然一些 CMOS BGR 可以在 1 V 以下工作，但它们通常无法提供所需的电流水平。因此，具有低输出阻抗和稳健电流驱动能力的 1 V 以下（sub-1 V）BGR 将具有很高的价值，能够在先进的模拟和混合信号系统中实现实际集成。然而，由于硅的固有带隙电压（约 1.25 V）以及误差放大器对输入共模范围的严格要求（其必须在电源变化范围内确保稳定性和精度），设计十分困难。\n本项目专注于设计和仿真 1 V 以下的 CMOS BGR 电路，从 1 V 电源下的低压差稳压器（LDO）基准源开始。初始设计可以侧重于无电流驱动能力的温度无关电压基准，随后是扩展设计，在 LDO 或源极跟随器（Source Follower）模式下加入电流驱动能力。LTSpice 将是主要的仿真工具，如果时间允许，还有机会学习模拟 IC 版图设计。\n最终，本工作旨在贡献设计技术，以提升面向下一代低功耗电子系统的 1 V 以下 CMOS BGR 的实用性和稳健性\n对 Dr. David Ng 的项目感兴趣者，请发送电子邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"模拟IC设计：DC-DC变换器设计","titleEn":"Analog IC Design:  DC-DC Converter Design","areaZh":"模拟IC设计","areaEn":"Analog IC Design","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 12:34:01","descriptionText":"This dissertation focuses on the design of DC–DC converters, essential circuits for managing power in portable electronic devices such as smartphones, laptops, and IoT systems. These devices often rely on batteries, which provide limited and varying voltage as energy drains. Since subsystems within a device demand different voltage levels, efficient DC–DC conversion is critical to ensure proper operation and prolonged battery life.\n The study explores switched-mode DC–DC converters, which step voltages up or down to meet system requirements without the inefficiencies of using multiple batteries. Emphasis is placed on understanding power management from system-level architecture down to transistor-level implementation. Through detailed analysis and circuit simulations, the work investigates key design blocks, including op-amp control, bandgap reference circuits, and feedback mechanisms for voltage/current regulation.\n Deliverables include developing both transistor-level and behavioral models (e.g., using Verilog-A), enabling comparisons between theoretical hand calculations and simulation results. Students will gain foundational knowledge in analog and mixed-signal IC design, with opportunities to progress toward layout-level design. Ultimately, this research highlights design trade-offs in efficiency, stability, and scalability, offering insight into how robust DC–DC converters can be integrated into future low-power systems.\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"本论文聚焦 DC-DC 变换器设计——这是管理智能手机、笔记本电脑与物联网系统等便携式电子设备电源的关键电路。此类设备通常依赖电池供电，而电池随能量消耗会提供有限且变化的电压。由于设备内各子系统需要不同电压水平，高效的 DC-DC 变换对于确保正常运行与延长电池寿命至关重要。\n研究探索开关模式 DC-DC 变换器，其可升降电压以满足系统需求，而无需使用多块电池的低效做法。重点在于从系统级架构到晶体管级实现理解电源管理。通过详细分析与电路仿真，工作考察关键设计模块，包括运放控制、带隙基准电路以及用于电压/电流调节的反馈机制。\n交付成果包括开发晶体管级与行为级模型（如使用 Verilog-A），实现理论手算与仿真结果的对比。学生将获得模拟与混合信号 IC 设计的基础知识，并有向版图级设计进阶的机会。最终，本研究揭示效率、稳定性与可扩展性之间的设计权衡，为如何将稳健的 DC-DC 变换器集成到未来低功耗系统中提供洞见。\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"模拟IC设计：SAR模数转换器","titleEn":"Analog IC Design: SAR Analog-to-Digital Converte","areaZh":"模拟IC设计","areaEn":"Analog IC Design","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 12:34:01","descriptionText":"This dissertation focuses on the design and analysis of a successive-approximation register (SAR) analog-to-digital converter (ADC), a widely used architecture in mixed-signal systems. A SAR ADC converts a continuous analog input into a discrete digital output by iteratively narrowing the quantization range through a binary search algorithm. This approach strikes a balance between accuracy, speed, and power efficiency, making SAR ADCs an essential building block in modern electronics.\n Common applications include medical imaging, industrial process control, optical communication, and power management, where large numbers of sensor outputs must be digitized efficiently. In these contexts, SAR ADCs are preferred due to their relatively low power consumption and moderate-to-high resolution capabilities.\n The project involves exploring SAR ADC design at both the transistor and behavioral modeling levels. Key blocks such as operational amplifiers, bandgap references, and comparators will be designed, simulated, and evaluated for performance. Deliverables include transistor-level circuits and Verilog-A models, with emphasis on comparing analytical hand calculations against simulated results to identify gaps and optimize performance. Students may also extend into analog IC layout, gaining experience with the complete design flow of a mixed-signal integrated circuit.\n Ultimately, this dissertation aims to deepen understanding of SAR ADC architecture and its design trade-offs, contributing insights into how power efficiency, resolution, and speed can be optimized for sensor-rich applications in next-generation systems.\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"本论文聚焦逐次逼近寄存器（SAR）模数转换器（ADC）的设计与分析——这是混合信号系统中广泛使用的架构。SAR ADC 通过二分搜索算法迭代缩小量化范围，将连续模拟输入转换为离散数字输出。该方法在精度、速度与功耗效率之间取得平衡，使 SAR ADC 成为现代电子器件的重要构件。\n常见应用包括医学成像、工业过程控制、光通信与电源管理——这些场景中大量传感器输出需要高效数字化。在此类应用中，SAR ADC 因其相对较低的功耗与中高分辨率能力而受到青睐。\n项目涉及在晶体管级与行为建模层面探索 SAR ADC 设计。运放、带隙基准与比较器等关键模块将被设计、仿真并评估性能。交付成果包括晶体管级电路与 Verilog-A 模型，重点是比较理论手算与仿真结果，以识别差距并优化性能。学生还可延伸至模拟 IC 版图，获得混合信号集成电路完整设计流程的经验。\n最终，本论文旨在加深对 SAR ADC 架构及其设计权衡的理解，为在下一代系统面向传感器密集应用优化功耗效率、分辨率与速度提供洞见。\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"电源设计项目：有源钳位反激（ACF）功率变换器","titleEn":"Power Design Project:\nActive Clamp Fly-back (ACF) power converter","areaZh":"电源设计项目：有源钳位反激（ACF）功率变换器","areaEn":"Power Design Project:\nActive Clamp Fly-back (ACF) power converter","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 12:34:01","descriptionText":"\"This dissertation investigates the design and implementation of an Active Clamp Fly-Back (ACF) power converter, a topology widely recognized for its efficiency, compactness, and suitability for USB Type-C applications. ACF converters achieve high power density and wide output voltage ranges, addressing modern demands for fast-charging and portable power delivery systems.\n The project emphasizes developing a complete ACF converter for USB Type-C standards, with specifications of 90–240 VAC input, 3–20 VDC regulated output, and 65W power delivery. A key design challenge involves transformer implementation, where planar or printed circuit board (PCB)-based transformers are adopted to reduce size and improve performance. By leveraging active clamp techniques, switching losses are minimized, enabling higher efficiency under varying load conditions.\n Deliverables include simulation, design, and construction of the ACF converter, followed by prototype testing to validate output stability, efficiency, and thermal performance. Additional effort will focus on designing the planar transformer or equivalent inductors, integrating them into the system to achieve reliable operation. A functional demo will verify performance, with additional credit awarded for experimental validation.\n This research contributes to advancing high-efficiency, compact power conversion systems, aligning with the growing demand for fast, safe, and standardized power solutions in consumer electronics. The outcomes have practical applications in USB-C chargers, portable adapters, and other high-density power modules.\"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"\"本论文研究有源钳位反激（ACF）电源变换器的设计与实现——该拓扑以其效率、紧凑性及对 USB Type-C 应用的适配性被广泛认可。ACF 变换器可实现高功率密度与宽输出电压范围，满足现代快充与便携供电系统需求。\n项目强调为 USB Type-C 标准开发完整的 ACF 变换器，规格为 90~240 VAC 输入、3~20 VDC 稳压输出、65W 功率输出。关键设计挑战之一是变压器实现：采用平面变压器或基于印刷电路板（PCB）的变压器以减小体积并提升性能。通过利用有源钳位技术，开关损耗被最小化，从而在不同负载条件下实现更高效率。\n交付成果包括 ACF 变换器的仿真、设计与搭建，随后进行原型测试，验证输出稳定性、效率与热性能。额外工作将聚焦于设计平面变压器或等效电感并将其集成到系统中以实现可靠运行。功能演示将验证性能，带实验验证可获得额外加分。\n本研究有助于推进高效、紧凑型电源转换系统的发展，契合消费电子对快速、安全、标准化电源解决方案日益增长的需求。成果在 USB-C 充电器、便携适配器及其他高密度电源模块中具有实际应用价值。\"\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"带空芯变压器的有源钳位反激（ACF）功率变换器","titleEn":"Active Clamp Fly-back (ACF) power converter with Air-Core Transformer","areaZh":"电源设计项目：带空芯变压器的有源钳位反激（ACF）功率变换器","areaEn":"\"Power Design Project:\nActive Clamp Fly-back (ACF) power converter with Air-Core Transformer\"\n","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 13:04:02","descriptionText":"\"This dissertation focuses on the design of an Active Clamp Fly-Back (ACF) power converter with an air-core transformer, a topology well-regarded for its high efficiency, compact size, and wide output voltage range. With the rise of USB Type-C power converters, ACF circuits have become essential for delivering fast, safe, and reliable power in modern electronics.\n The project emphasizes both the ACF converter design and transformer innovation. Specifications target a universal AC input (90–240 VAC), a regulated DC output range of 3–20 VDC, and up to 65W power delivery. A critical element is the development of an air-core transformer using Flexible Printed Circuit (FPC) or equivalent planar transformer technology, designed to minimize losses and improve performance under high-frequency switching conditions.\n Deliverables include circuit design, transformer development, and prototype validation through experimental testing. Students will simulate and implement the complete ACF converter, then verify performance through lab measurements of efficiency, thermal stability, and output regulation. Additional credit is awarded for constructing a functional demo with experimental validation.\n This research highlights the growing role of advanced converter topologies in consumer power electronics, particularly in addressing challenges of power density, heat management, and compliance with USB Type-C standards. The outcomes contribute to the development of compact, efficient power systems for chargers, adapters, and other high-performance portable devices.\"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"\"本论文聚焦带空心变压器（air-core transformer）的有源钳位反激（ACF）电源变换器设计——该拓扑以高效率、小体积与宽输出电压范围著称。随着 USB Type-C 电源变换器的兴起，ACF 电路已成为现代电子设备中实现快速、安全、可靠供电的关键。\n项目同时强调 ACF 变换器设计与变压器创新。规格目标为：通用交流输入（90~240 VAC）、稳压直流输出范围 3~20 VDC、最高 65W 功率输出。关键要素是采用柔性印刷电路（FPC）或等效平面变压器技术开发空心变压器，以在高频开关条件下最大限度降低损耗并提升性能。\n交付成果包括电路设计、变压器开发与通过实验测试进行原型验证。学生将仿真并实现完整 ACF 变换器，再通过实验室测量效率、热稳定性与输出稳压来验证性能。构建带实验验证的功能演示将获得额外加分。\n本研究凸显先进变换器拓扑在消费类电力电子中的日益重要作用，尤其应对功率密度、热管理与 USB Type-C 标准合规等挑战。研究成果将促进充电器、适配器及其他高性能便携设备所用紧凑、高效电源系统的发展。\"\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"数字IC设计：面向AI或快充应用的基于FPGA的CPU（RISC-V）开发","titleEn":"Digital IC Design: Development of FPGA-based CPU (RISC-V) for Al or Fast-Charging Application","areaZh":"数字IC设计","areaEn":"Digital IC Design","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 13:04:02","descriptionText":"\"This dissertation explores the development of an FPGA-based CPU using the RISC-V architecture, integrated with a floating-point unit (FPU) for accelerating computation-intensive applications such as AI inference and fast-charging power control. Conventional microcontrollers, such as 8051 or RISC-V cores without hardware FPUs, rely on software emulation of floating-point arithmetic, which increases latency and limits performance in demanding industrial and embedded systems.\n The project focuses on designing a digital AI IC that integrates a RISC-V or 8051-based microcontroller with an IEEE-754 compliant single-precision FPU. The CPU and FPU are implemented on FPGA, combining modular IP blocks into a synthesizable, high-performance prototype. Applications include pattern recognition, motor control, and advanced power management, where reduced computation time is critical.\n Deliverables include RTL development in Verilog/SystemVerilog, implementation of the IEEE-754 FPU, and the creation of verification testbenches or assertion-based checks to validate compliance with floating-point standards. The design will be synthesized using industry-standard tools such as Synopsys DC or Xilinx Vivado, ensuring proper timing closure and practical hardware readiness. If time allows, optimization of the architecture into a full custom IC may be pursued.\n This research contributes to the advancement of embedded computing by demonstrating how FPGA-based RISC-V systems with hardware FPUs can significantly improve performance and energy efficiency in real-world AI and power applications.\"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"本论文探讨基于 RISC-V 架构的 FPGA CPU 的开发，并集成浮点运算单元（FPU），以加速 AI 推理和快充电源控制等计算密集型应用。传统微控制器（如 8051，或不带硬件 FPU 的 RISC-V 内核）依赖浮点运算的软件仿真，这会增加延迟，并限制在要求苛刻的工业和嵌入式系统中的性能。\n本项目专注于设计一款数字 AI 芯片（IC），将基于 RISC-V 或 8051 的微控制器与符合 IEEE-754 标准的单精度 FPU 集成在一起。CPU 和 FPU 在 FPGA 上实现，将模块化 IP 块组合成一个可综合、高性能的原型。应用包括模式识别、电机控制和先进电源管理，在这些应用中缩短计算时间至关重要。\n交付成果包括使用 Verilog/SystemVerilog 进行 RTL 开发、实现 IEEE-754 FPU，以及创建验证测试平台（testbench）或基于断言的检查，以验证对浮点标准的符合性。该设计将使用 Synopsys DC 或 Xilinx Vivado 等行业标准工具进行综合，确保恰当的时序收敛和实际硬件就绪度。如果时间允许，可以进一步将架构优化为完整的定制 IC。\n本研究通过展示带有硬件 FPU 的基于 FPGA 的 RISC-V 系统如何在实际 AI 和电源应用中显著提升性能和能效，为嵌入式计算的发展做出贡献。\n对 Dr. David Ng 的项目感兴趣者，请发送电子邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"数字IC设计项目：基于CNN的人脸识别/目标检测/自然语言处理","titleEn":"Digital IC Design Project:\r\nCNN-Based Face Recognition/Object Detection/Natural Language Processing","areaZh":"数字IC设计","areaEn":"Digital IC Design","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 13:04:02","descriptionText":"\"This dissertation explores the implementation of convolutional neural networks (CNNs) for intelligent applications such as face recognition, object detection, and natural language processing (NLP). With the rise of deep neural networks and high-performance computing, AI systems have evolved from handcrafted models to machine-learned algorithms that continuously improve through training with large-scale datasets. Applications such as YOLO-based video analytics, speech understanding, and biometric recognition are now central to smart city and edge intelligence systems.\n Although GPUs and NPUs dominate current AI computation, there remains a demand for embedded hardware solutions with higher flexibility, lower cost/performance ratios, and reduced power consumption. This project addresses that gap by designing FPGA-based hardware accelerators that implement CNN architectures for real-time AI at the edge.\n The project deliverables include implementing CNN algorithms in software, then accelerating them using Verilog/SystemVerilog RTL mapped onto FPGA. A new RISC-V core will also be designed and integrated with the AI accelerator to demonstrate scalability. Verification testbenches with randomized data will be developed to validate correctness and robustness.\n This research contributes to the development of efficient, embedded AI hardware that brings intelligence closer to users. By combining CNN models with FPGA-based design, the work highlights how edge platforms can achieve low-latency inference, energy efficiency, and adaptability in next-generation smart applications.\"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"“本论文探讨卷积神经网络（CNN）在智能应用（如人脸识别、目标检测与自然语言处理（NLP））中的实现。随着深度神经网络与高性能计算的兴起，AI 系统已从手工构建的模型演进为通过大规模数据集训练而不断改进的机器学习算法。诸如基于 YOLO 的视频分析、语音理解与生物特征识别等应用，如今已成为智慧城市与边缘智能系统的核心。\n尽管 GPU 与 NPU 在当前 AI 计算中占据主导地位，但市场仍对具有更高灵活性、更优成本/性能比以及更低功耗的嵌入式硬件解决方案存在需求。本项目通过设计基于 FPGA 的硬件加速器来填补这一空白，这些加速器实现用于边缘实时 AI 的 CNN 架构。\n项目交付物包括：先用软件实现 CNN 算法，再使用映射到 FPGA 上的 Verilog/SystemVerilog RTL 对其进行加速。还将设计一个新的 RISC-V 内核，并与 AI 加速器集成，以展示可扩展性。将开发带有随机化数据的验证测试平台（testbench），以验证正确性与稳健性。\n本研究有助于开发高效、嵌入式的 AI 硬件，使智能更贴近用户。通过将 CNN 模型与基于 FPGA 的设计相结合，该工作凸显了边缘平台如何在下一代智能应用中实现低延迟推理、高能效与自适应性。”\n对 Dr. David Ng 的项目感兴趣者，请发送邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-9","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"数字IC设计项目：基于深度神经网络的投资组合优化AI金融科技","titleEn":"Digital IC Design Project: AI Fintech for Investment Portfolio Optimization using Deep Neural Networks","areaZh":"数字IC设计","areaEn":"Digital IC Design","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 13:04:02","descriptionText":"\"This dissertation investigates the use of hardware-accelerated deep neural networks (DNNs) for financial technology (Fintech) applications, focusing on portfolio optimization and investment decision-making. Traditional market analysis is computationally intensive, requiring large-scale data processing and evaluation of economic indicators, industry trends, and company performance metrics. Hardware acceleration offers the potential to significantly reduce processing time, enabling faster and more efficient analysis.\n The project aims to design and implement AI hardware capable of running trained DNN models to predict stock price movements, assess risk, and optimize investment portfolios based on Markowitz’s model. By considering expected returns, volatility, and inter-stock correlations, the system can achieve accurate profit maximization and risk minimization.\n Deliverables include developing DNN-based predictive models, implementing common AI algorithms in FPGA using Verilog/SystemVerilog RTL, and designing a RISC-V core integrated with the FPGA platform. Verification testbenches will be used to validate accuracy and performance using randomized input data.\n This research demonstrates how combining AI models with digital hardware design can accelerate financial analysis, making it possible to evaluate large datasets and optimize portfolios in minutes rather than hours. The outcomes highlight the role of embedded AI hardware in Fintech, offering scalable and efficient solutions for real-world investment decision-making.\"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"\"本论文研究硬件加速深度神经网络（DNN）在金融科技（Fintech）中的应用，聚焦投资组合优化与投资决策。传统市场分析计算密集，需要大规模数据处理并评估经济指标、行业趋势与企业绩效指标。硬件加速有望大幅缩短处理时间，实现更快、更高效的分析。\n项目旨在设计并实现能够运行已训练 DNN 模型的 AI 硬件，用于预测股价走势、评估风险，并基于马科维茨模型优化投资组合。通过考虑预期收益、波动率与个股间相关性，系统可实现精确的利润最大化与风险最小化。\n交付成果包括开发基于 DNN 的预测模型、使用 Verilog/SystemVerilog RTL 在 FPGA 上实现常见 AI 算法，以及设计与 FPGA 平台集成的 RISC-V 内核。将使用验证测试平台并借助随机输入数据校验精度与性能。\n本研究展示将 AI 模型与数字硬件设计相结合如何加速金融分析，使大规模数据集的评估与投资组合优化从数小时缩短至数分钟。成果凸显嵌入式 AI 硬件在金融科技中的作用，为真实世界投资决策提供可扩展、高效的解决方案。\"\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-10","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"模拟与数字IC设计：面向存内计算的电路（数字或模拟方案）","titleEn":"Analog &amp; Digital IC Design: Circuit for in-memory computing, from a Digital or Analog Approach","areaZh":"模拟与数字IC设计","areaEn":"Analog & Digital IC Design","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 13:04:02","descriptionText":"\"This dissertation focuses on circuits for in-memory computing, an emerging paradigm in artificial intelligence and machine learning under Industry 4.0. Unlike traditional computing, which separates memory and processing, in-memory computing integrates computation within memory arrays, thereby reducing data movement, energy consumption, and latency. This makes it highly suitable for edge applications where efficiency is critical.\n The project explores both analog and digital approaches to in-memory design. Key elements include efficient analog-to-digital converters (ADCs) with high linearity, low power, and compact area, such as successive approximation register (SAR) ADCs. Memory devices such as SRAM, Flash, DRAM, or RRAM will also be investigated for their suitability in storing and processing data simultaneously. Selected devices will be modeled, designed, and tested to evaluate performance trade-offs.\n Deliverables include circuit-level implementations for in-memory computing, as well as digital design approaches that interface with RISC-V cores to explore architectural integration. Simulation using LTSpice and digital verification will provide comparative insights, while advanced students may extend the work into analog IC layout.\n This research highlights the potential of in-memory computing to enable next-generation low-power AI accelerators, offering practical insights into circuit-level innovations that support scalable, energy-efficient architectures for data-intensive applications.\"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"\"本论文聚焦存内计算电路——这是工业 4.0 背景下人工智能与机器学习的一种新兴范式。与传统将存储与处理分离的计算不同，存内计算将计算集成到存储阵列中，从而减少数据搬运、能耗与时延，非常适合对效率要求极高的边缘应用。\n项目探索存内设计的模拟与数字两种路径。关键要素包括高线性度、低功耗、小面积的高效模数转换器（ADC），如逐次逼近寄存器（SAR）ADC；还将研究 SRAM、Flash、DRAM 或 RRAM 等存储器件在同时存储与处理数据方面的适用性。将对选定的器件进行建模、设计与测试，以评估性能权衡。\n交付成果包括面向存内计算的电路级实现，以及对接 RISC-V 内核、探索架构集成的数字设计方法。使用 LTSpice 仿真与数字验证可获得对比洞见，进阶学生可将工作延伸至模拟 IC 版图。\n本研究凸显存内计算支撑下一代低功耗 AI 加速器的潜力，为支撑数据密集型应用的可扩展、节能架构提供电路级创新的实用洞见。\"\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-11","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"软件编程项目：面向物联网与智能家居氛围灯光音乐的AI驱动RGB-LED系统","titleEn":"Software Programming Project: AI-Driven RGB-LED System for IoT and Smart Home Ambient Light Music Applications","areaZh":"软件编程","areaEn":"Software Programming","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 13:04:02","descriptionText":"\"This dissertation investigates the development of an AI-driven RGB-LED lighting system for smart home and IoT applications. With the expansion of the Internet of Things (IoT), embedded systems are increasingly required to connect, process, and respond intelligently to environmental data. Smart lighting is one application that enhances living spaces, creating responsive and energy-efficient environments.\n The project focuses on designing a digitally controlled RGB-LED driver capable of adjusting light colors and patterns in real time. AI algorithms will be applied to synchronize lighting with inputs such as background music or environmental conditions. The system may be implemented using microcontrollers (MCUs) or FPGAs, along with sensors for feedback and pattern generation.\n Deliverables include building a hardware prototype, developing firmware for LED control, and implementing wireless interfaces such as Bluetooth or Wi-Fi for mobile app control. The system will also connect to a web server for data collection and visualization using open-source hardware and software libraries. Testing will be performed in real ambient environments, validating the AI algorithms and lighting control. Additionally, the integration of USB Type-C fast-charging power delivery technology is recommended to provide an efficient power supply.\n This research demonstrates how embedded AI and IoT technologies can be combined to create intelligent home lighting systems. By enabling dynamic light control, wireless connectivity, and real-time responsiveness, the project contributes to advancing smart home applications and improving user experience.\"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"\"本论文研究面向智能家居与物联网应用的 AI 驱动 RGB-LED 照明系统的开发。随着物联网（IoT）的扩展，嵌入式系统日益需要连接、处理并智能响应环境数据。智能照明是提升居住空间的典型应用之一，可营造响应式、节能的环境。\n项目聚焦于设计一个可实时调节光色与光效图案的数字控制 RGB-LED 驱动器。将应用 AI 算法，使照明与背景音乐或环境条件等输入同步。系统可采用微控制器（MCU）或 FPGA 实现，并配备传感器用于反馈与图案生成。\n交付成果包括搭建硬件原型、开发 LED 控制固件，以及实现用于移动应用控制的蓝牙或 Wi-Fi 等无线接口。系统还将连接 Web 服务器，利用开源软硬件库进行数据采集与可视化。测试将在真实环境环境中进行，验证 AI 算法与照明控制。此外，建议集成 USB Type-C 快充供电技术以提供高效电源。\n本研究展示如何将嵌入式 AI 与物联网技术相结合，创造智能家居照明系统。通过实现动态光控、无线连接与实时响应，项目有助于推进智能家居应用并改善用户体验。\"\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-12","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"软件编程项目：USB Type-C PD可充电电池应用","titleEn":"Software Programming Project:\r\nUSB Type-C PD Rechargeable Battery Applications","areaZh":"软件编程","areaEn":"Software Programming","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-14 10:04:01","descriptionText":"Description\n Since the USB Type-C 3.1 Specification introduction in 2016 by the USB Implementers Forum (USB-IF), the development of new USB Type-C battery packs has been accelerating and migrating legacy USB Type-A battery pack designs to USB Type-C.\n The new USB Type-C PD standard allows chargers, batteries, mobile devices and hubs etc. to negotiate in a variety of power schemes to support high-current fast charge and high-power output.\n The candidate will be provided with an USB Type-C PD rechargeable battery application board and a lithium-ion battery for applications development.\n Deliverables\n Develop a dual-role port (DRP) rechargeable battery controller with USB Type-C power delivery (PD) protocol to power smartphones, tablets, laptops, headphones, and other portable devices.\n References\n USB Type-C Reference Design from Silicon Labs - YouTube\n SLRDK1000A Silicon Labs, Reference Design, USB Type-C Rechargeable Battery Pack, EFM8BB3 Busy Bee MCU | Farnell\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"项目描述：自 2016 年 USB 实施者论坛（USB-IF）发布 USB Type-C 3.1 规范以来，新型 USB Type-C 电池组开发不断加快，传统 USB Type-A 电池组设计正加速向 USB Type-C 迁移。新的 USB Type-C PD 标准允许充电器、电池、移动设备与集线器等通过多种功率方案协商，以支持大电流快充与高功率输出。\n将向候选学生提供一块 USB Type-C PD 可充电电池应用板与一节锂离子电池，用于应用开发。\n交付成果：开发一个带 USB Type-C 电力传输（PD）协议的双角色端口（DRP）可充电电池控制器，可为智能手机、平板、笔记本、耳机及其他便携设备供电。\n参考：Silicon Labs 的 USB Type-C 参考设计（YouTube）；Farnell 网站 SLRDK1000A Silicon Labs 参考设计（USB Type-C 可充电电池组，EFM8BB3 Busy Bee MCU）。\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-13","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"机器人与无人机在现代应用中的运用","titleEn":"Robots-Drone in Modern Applications","areaZh":"机器人与无人机","areaEn":"Robots-Drone","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-14 10:04:12","descriptionText":"\"This dissertation explores the development and applications of robotic and drone systems, ranging from quadruped robots and aerial drones to humanoid platforms. The aim is to analyze their design principles, functional capabilities, and impact on real-world applications such as surveillance, search and rescue, logistics, healthcare, and personal assistance.\n The research can take one of three approaches: simulation and theoretical modeling, simulation combined with prototype implementation, or practical implementation complemented by theoretical insights. Each pathway enables a comprehensive evaluation of how robotic systems integrate mechanics, control, sensors, and AI to achieve autonomous operation.\n Deliverables include detailed simulations of robotic or drone dynamics, prototype designs, performance testing, and an analytical report on applications and challenges. Depending on the chosen scope, the study may focus on efficiency, adaptability, and safety in practical use cases.\n This project contributes to the growing field of robotics by bridging theoretical concepts with practical engineering solutions. By evaluating emerging technologies and their adaptability to societal needs, it emphasizes both innovation and application, ensuring the findings are relevant to modern robotics research and deployment.\"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"\"本论文探讨机器人与无人机系统的开发与应用，涵盖四足机器人、空中无人机直至人形平台。旨在分析其设计原理、功能能力，以及对监视、搜救、物流、医疗健康与个人辅助等实际应用的影响。\n研究可采取三种路径之一：仿真与理论建模；仿真结合原型实现；或实践实现辅以理论洞察。每种路径都能全面评估机器人系统如何集成机械、控制、传感器与 AI 以实现自主运行。\n交付成果包括机器人或无人机动力学的详细仿真、原型设计、性能测试，以及关于应用与挑战的分析报告。根据所选范围，研究可聚焦于实际用例中的效率、适应性与安全性。\n本项目通过连接理论概念与工程实践方案，为日益壮大的机器人领域作出贡献。通过评估新兴技术及其对社会需求的适应性，研究同时强调创新与应用，确保成果与当代机器人研究与部署相关。\"\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-14","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"智能机器狗的研发与分析","titleEn":"Development and Analysis of an Intelligent Robot Dog","areaZh":"机器人","areaEn":"robot","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-14 10:34:01","descriptionText":"\"This dissertation focuses on the development and evaluation of an autonomous drone platform using different embedded systems such as Raspberry Pi, NVIDIA Jetson Nano, and Arduino. The study emphasizes navigation, obstacle avoidance, power efficiency, and the integration of AI algorithms, including large language models (LLMs), to enhance interactivity and task adaptability. The aim is to determine the optimal embedded system for balancing intelligence, real-time processing, energy efficiency, and reliability in autonomous flight.\n The project includes literature review, hardware prototyping, software development, and performance benchmarking. Hardware implementation involves building the drone platform with multiple embedded boards and sensors for navigation and control. Software development will focus on flight stabilization, obstacle detection, autonomous navigation, and potentially LLM-based interactions for command execution and mission planning.\n Deliverables include:\n Literature Review: Overview of drone systems and embedded platforms.\n Hardware Development: Building the drone with multiple embedded boards and components.\n Software Development: Implementing navigation, balance, obstacle avoidance, and AI-enabled features.\n Performance Testing: Comparing computational efficiency, power consumption, flight stability, and responsiveness across boards.\n Final Report: Analyzing results to recommend the most suitable embedded system.\n Prototype Demonstration: Showcasing drone performance with various embedded systems.\n This research contributes to advancing autonomous aerial robotics by evaluating trade-offs between intelligence, efficiency, and adaptability, offering practical insights for real-world applications such as surveillance, logistics, and environmental monitoring.\"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"\"本论文聚焦使用 Raspberry Pi、NVIDIA Jetson Nano、Arduino 等多种嵌入式系统开发并评估自主无人机平台。研究强调导航、避障、功耗效率以及包括大语言模型（LLM）在内的 AI 算法集成，以增强交互性与任务适应性。目标是为自主飞行中的智能性、实时处理、能效与可靠性权衡，确定最优嵌入式系统。\n项目包括文献综述、硬件原型制作、软件开发与性能基准测试。硬件实现涉及用多块嵌入式板与导航控制传感器搭建无人机平台。软件开发聚焦于飞行稳定、障碍检测、自主导航，并可能实现基于 LLM 的指令执行与任务规划交互。\n交付成果包括：\n- 文献综述：无人机系统与嵌入式平台概述；\n- 硬件开发：用多块嵌入式板与部件搭建无人机；\n- 软件开发：实现导航、平衡、避障与 AI 增强功能；\n- 性能测试：比较各板的计算效率、功耗、飞行稳定性与响应性；\n- 最终报告：分析结果并推荐最合适的嵌入式系统；\n- 原型演示：展示无人机在各类嵌入式系统下的性能。\n本研究通过评估智能性、效率与适应性之间的权衡，为推进自主空中机器人作出贡献，为监视、物流与环境监测等实际应用提供实用洞见。\"\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-15","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向边缘端目标检测的各类半导体IC市场调研","titleEn":"Marketing Research on Different Types of Semiconductor ICs for Edge-Side Object Detection","areaZh":"半导体IC","areaEn":"Semiconductor ICs","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-14 10:34:01","descriptionText":"\"This dissertation investigates the market landscape and technical performance of various semiconductor integrated circuits (ICs) for edge-side object detection. The IC categories under study include System-on-Chips (SoCs), Application-Specific Integrated Circuits (ASICs), Field-Programmable Gate Arrays (FPGAs), and Power Management ICs (PMICs). With the rise of robotics, autonomous vehicles, IoT, and surveillance, these hardware platforms play a critical role in supporting efficient, low-latency, and cost-effective AI processing at the edge.\n The research will evaluate computational efficiency, power consumption, cost-effectiveness, and compatibility with machine learning models such as YOLO, SSD, and Faster R-CNN. A particular focus is placed on PMICs, which optimize energy delivery and extend device battery life, making them essential for long-term deployment in edge AI systems.\n Deliverables include a comprehensive market analysis of IC adoption trends, a technical review comparing efficiency and power optimization, and case studies of real-world deployments. Additional outputs include a cost-benefit analysis of different ICs in small- and large-scale applications, alongside recommendations on the most suitable solutions for industry-specific needs.\n By integrating technical benchmarking with market insights, this dissertation provides a dual perspective on both performance and commercial viability. The findings will contribute to understanding how different IC technologies—particularly PMICs—can best support scalable and energy-efficient edge-side object detection across industries.\"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"\"本论文调研用于边缘侧物体检测的各类半导体集成电路（IC）的市场格局与技术性能。所研究的 IC 类别包括片上系统（SoC）、专用集成电路（ASIC）、现场可编程门阵列（FPGA）与电源管理 IC（PMIC）。随着机器人、自动驾驶、物联网与安防监控的兴起，这些硬件平台在支持边缘高效、低时延、经济高效的 AI 处理方面扮演关键角色。\n研究将评估计算效率、功耗、经济性以及对 YOLO、SSD、Faster R-CNN 等机器学习模型的兼容性。重点之一是 PMIC——它可优化能量供给并延长设备电池寿命，对边缘 AI 系统的长期部署至关重要。\n交付成果包括 IC 采用趋势的全面市场分析、比较效率与功耗优化的技术综述，以及实际部署的案例研究。此外还包括小型与大规模应用中不同 IC 的成本效益分析，以及针对行业特定需求的最合适解决方案建议。\n通过将技术基准测试与市场洞察相结合，本论文提供了性能与商业可行性双重视角。研究结果将有助于理解不同 IC 技术（尤其是 PMIC）如何最好地支撑跨行业的可扩展、节能型边缘侧物体检测。\"\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-16","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"自主无人机的研发与分析","titleEn":"Development and Analysis of an Autonomous Drone","areaZh":"自主无人机","areaEn":"Autonomous Drone","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-14 10:34:01","descriptionText":"\"Employing autonomous drone for delivery and passenger transport is no longer a dream, thanks to cutting-edge technology in Edge AI processors for sensor fusion (image sensor, gyroscope, accelerometer, range sensor), path planning, obstacle avoidance, fluid dynamics, and auto-balancing. Students will test and develop using embedded boards (e.g., Raspberry Pi, NVIDIA Jetson Nano, ESP32-S3, Arduino) for system control, AI computations, connectivity, and sensor interfacing, ensuring robust autonomous navigation and stability through simulation and real-world testing. \n Deliverables: \n Literature Review: Analyze drone technologies, sensor fusion, path planning, fluid dynamics. \n Hardware Development: Integrate sensors, processors (Raspberry Pi, Jetson Nano, ESP32-S3, Arduino). \n Software Development: Code sensor fusion, navigation, obstacle avoidance using Python, firmware. \n Software Simulation Development: Validate algorithms in Gazebo/ROS for navigation, stability. \n Performance Testing: Evaluate accuracy, stability in simulated, real-world scenarios. \n Final Report and Demonstration: Document results, demonstrate autonomous flight. \"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"\"借助用于传感器融合（图像传感器、陀螺仪、加速度计、测距传感器）、路径规划、避障、流体动力学与自动平衡的前沿边缘 AI 处理器技术，将自主无人机用于配送与载客已不再是梦想。学生将使用嵌入式板卡（如 Raspberry Pi、NVIDIA Jetson Nano、ESP32-S3、Arduino）进行系统控制、AI 计算、连接与传感器接口的开发与测试，并通过仿真与实际测试确保自主导航的稳健性与稳定性。\n交付成果：\n- 文献综述：分析无人机技术、传感器融合、路径规划与流体动力学；\n- 硬件开发：集成传感器与处理器（Raspberry Pi、Jetson Nano、ESP32-S3、Arduino）；\n- 软件开发：使用 Python 与固件编写传感器融合、导航与避障代码；\n- 软件仿真开发：在 Gazebo/ROS 中验证导航与稳定性算法；\n- 性能测试：在仿真与实际场景中评估精度与稳定性；\n- 最终报告与演示：记录结果并演示自主飞行。\"\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["AERO"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-17","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于RISC-V的存内计算（CIM）AI加速","titleEn":"Compute-In-Memory (CIM) AI Acceleration with RISC-V","areaZh":"存内计算（CIM）AI","areaEn":"Compute-In-Memory (CIM) AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-14 10:34:01","descriptionText":"\"AI demands energy-efficient accelerators. Compute-in-Memory (CIM) chips overcome the traditional von Neumann architectures, optimizing power consumption and enabling high-speed matrix multiply-accumulate for deep learning. This project extends RISC-V with custom instructions, allowing TensorFlow/PyTorch users to access CIM hardware for vision and NLP tasks. It involves hardware-software co-design, making Analog AI accessible to a broader community of practitioners.\n Deliverables\n Literature Review Report: Detailed survey with references (e.g., IEEE/ACM papers) and proposed instruction set design.\n Hardware Prototype: Functional FPGA/MCU implementation of the extended RISC-V core with CIM interface, including Verilog code and synthesis reports.\n Software Middleware: Open-source library/codebase for TensorFlow/PyTorch integration, with documentation and examples.\n Performance Evaluation Report: Benchmark results presented in tables/graphs, analyzing trade-offs (e.g., energy savings vs. accuracy loss).\n Final Thesis Report: Comprehensive document integrating all phases, with appendices for code, schematics, and raw data.\n Prototype Demonstrations: Working demos for vision (e.g., image classification pipeline) and NLP (e.g., language model inference), deployable on the hardware platform.\n Code Repository: GitHub repo with all source code, simulations, and setup instructions for replication.\"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"AI 需要高能效的加速器。存内计算（Compute-in-Memory，CIM）芯片克服了传统的冯·诺依曼（von Neumann）架构，优化了功耗，并实现了深度学习所需的高速矩阵乘加运算。本项目通过自定义指令扩展 RISC-V，使 TensorFlow/PyTorch 用户能够访问 CIM 硬件以完成视觉和 NLP 任务。该项目涉及软硬件协同设计，使模拟 AI（Analog AI）能够被更广泛的实践者群体所使用。\n交付成果\n文献综述报告：附参考文献（如 IEEE/ACM 论文）的详细综述，以及所提出的指令集设计。\n硬件原型：扩展 RISC-V 内核（带 CIM 接口）的功能性 FPGA/MCU 实现，包括 Verilog 代码和综合报告。\n软件中间件：用于 TensorFlow/PyTorch 集成的开源库/代码库，附文档和示例。\n性能评估报告：以表格/图表形式呈现的基准测试结果，分析权衡（例如节能与精度损失的权衡）。\n最终论文报告：整合所有阶段的综合文档，并附代码、原理图和原始数据的附录。\n原型演示：针对视觉（如图像分类流水线）和 NLP（如语言模型推理）的可运行演示，可部署在硬件平台上。\n代码仓库：包含所有源代码、仿真和复现所需设置说明的 GitHub 仓库。\n对 Dr. David Ng 的项目感兴趣者，请发送电子邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-18","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向EV/汽车电源管理芯片（PMIC）的模拟存内计算芯片技术框架研究","titleEn":"Technical Framework Research for Analog CIM Chips in EV/Automotive PMICs","areaZh":"模拟存内计算芯片","areaEn":"Analog CIM Chips","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-14 10:34:01","descriptionText":"\"This MSc project conducts market research on analog Compute-In-Memory (CIM) chips and EV/automotive power management integrated circuits (PMICs), emphasizing CIM's advantages for high-efficiency, low-power edge AI in auxiliary functions like ADAS sensor fusion, predictive maintenance, voice recognition, and battery optimization. It develops technical implementation via simulations and marketing strategies for OEMs, balancing half technical (advantages, integration, simulations, IEEE paper, testings) and half marketing (research, framework, strategies).\n Objectives:\n Market research on CIM/PMICs, trends in auxiliary edge AI (e.g., ADAS, infotainment, V2X) for automotive / EV\n Evaluate CIM advantages for auxiliary edge AI, Simulate CIM for PMIC functions using NeuroSim/PyTorch; quantify benefits like reduced power in auxiliary computations.\n Design CIM integration for PMICs in auxiliary applications: Model ReRAM-based CIM in multi-rail supplies via MATLAB.\n Develop marketing framework and strategies: Create demos, ROI tools, and targeted campaigns for OEMs.\n Test and deliver IEEE paper (i.e.: Benchmark vs. digital; A/B test marketing; ensure ISO 26262 compliance)\n Technical Focus: Implement CIM for DNNs in PMICs, testing low-power edge AI for auxiliary tasks like driver personalization and emissions optimization, enhancing EV range/safety.\n Outcomes/Deliverables: IEEE paper on implementation/advantages; marketing framework/strategies (whitepapers, tools); simulation testings with metrics.\"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"本项目开展针对模拟存内计算（Compute-In-Memory，CIM）芯片以及电动汽车/汽车电源管理集成电路（PMIC）的市场研究，重点强调 CIM 在辅助功能（如 ADAS 传感器融合、预测性维护、语音识别和电池优化）中实现高效率、低功耗边缘 AI 的优势。项目通过仿真开发技术实现方案，并为整车厂（OEM）制定营销策略，在“一半技术（优势、集成、仿真、IEEE 论文、测试）”与“一半营销（研究、框架、策略）”之间取得平衡。\n目标：\n- 开展 CIM/PMIC 的市场研究，以及汽车/电动汽车辅助边缘 AI（例如 ADAS、信息娱乐、V2X）的趋势调研\n- 评估 CIM 对辅助边缘 AI 的优势，使用 NeuroSim/PyTorch 对 PMIC 功能进行 CIM 仿真；量化其收益，例如降低辅助计算中的功耗。\n- 设计辅助应用中 PMIC 的 CIM 集成方案：通过 MATLAB 在多轨电源中对基于 ReRAM 的 CIM 进行建模。\n- 制定营销框架和策略：为整车厂制作演示、ROI 工具和定向营销活动。\n- 测试并交付 IEEE 论文（即：与数字方案进行基准对比；对营销进行 A/B 测试；确保符合 ISO 26262）\n- 技术重点：在 PMIC 中为深度神经网络（DNN）实现 CIM，测试用于辅助任务（如驾驶员个性化和排放优化）的低功耗边缘 AI，从而提升电动汽车续航/安全性。\n- 成果/交付物：关于实现方案/优势的 IEEE 论文；营销框架/策略（白皮书、工具）；带指标的仿真测试。\n对 Dr. David Ng 的项目感兴趣者，请发送邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":["ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-19","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"模拟存内计算芯片在汽车动力总成燃油/电力管理中的机电一体化集成","titleEn":"Mechanical and Electronic Integration of Analog CIM Chips in Automotive Powertrain Systems for Fuel/Electricity Management","areaZh":"模拟存内计算","areaEn":"Analog CIM","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-14 10:34:01","descriptionText":"\"\n This MSc project designs mechanical and electronic integration of analog Compute-In-Memory (CIM) chips into automotive powertrain systems for EVs, ICE vehicles, and hybrids, enabling edge AI for fuel/electricity management (e.g., fuel injection in ICE, regenerative braking in hybrids, motor control in EVs). It addresses mech (e.g., thermal/vibration management) and electronic (e.g., circuit optimization) challenges for efficiency gains, leveraging trends in automotive electronics for powertrains.\n Objectives:\n Review mech-electronic trends for AI chips in automotive powertrains: Analyze designs for fuel/electricity compatibility in ICE/hybrids.\n Design electronic aspects (e.g., CIM for low-power DNNs in fuel optimization): Simulate using PyTorch/NeuroSim for tasks like hybrid power splitting.\n Develop mechanical integration (e.g., vibration-resistant packaging, cooling): Model via SolidWorks/ANSYS for CIM in powertrain modules.\n Integrate and simulate full system: Combine models in MATLAB/Simulink for scenarios like ICE efficiency or EV range extension.\n Test and deliver IEEE paper: Prototype simulations; benchmark (e.g., energy savings); validate per AEC-Q100 standards.\n Technical Focus: Mech: Thermal/vibration modeling for CIM in engines/motors; Electronic: Circuit integration for edge AI, testing gains in fuel/electricity management like torque vectoring in hybrids.\n Outcomes/Deliverables: IEEE paper on powertrain integration; simulation models/testings; prototype schematics with metrics (e.g., reduced latency, thermal stability).\"\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"\"本硕士项目设计模拟存内计算（CIM）芯片在汽车动力系统（EV、ICE 与混动车）中的机电集成，为燃油/电力管理实现边缘 AI（如 ICE 的燃油喷射、混动的再生制动、EV 的电机控制）。项目处理机械（如热/振动管理）与电子（如电路优化）方面的挑战以获得效率提升，并借力汽车动力系统电子领域的趋势。\n目标：\n- 综述 AI 芯片在汽车动力系统中的机电趋势：分析 ICE/混动中燃油/电力兼容性设计；\n- 设计电子方面（如面向燃油优化的低功耗 DNN 的 CIM）：使用 PyTorch/NeuroSim 针对混动功率分配等任务仿真；\n- 开发机械集成（如抗振封装、冷却）：用 SolidWorks/ANSYS 对动力系统模块中的 CIM 建模；\n- 集成并仿真完整系统：在 MATLAB/Simulink 中组合模型，针对 ICE 效率或 EV 续航扩展等场景；\n- 测试并产出 IEEE 论文：原型仿真；基准测试（如节能）；按 AEC-Q100 标准验证。\n技术重点：机械——发动机/电机中 CIM 的热/振动建模；电子——面向边缘 AI 的电路集成，测试燃油/电力管理（如混动扭矩矢量分配）中的收益。\n成果/交付：关于动力系统集成的 IEEE 论文；仿真模型/测试；带指标（如更低时延、热稳定性）的原型原理图。\"\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-DAVIDN-20","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向机器狗或人形机器人应用的边缘AI足式机器人平台设计与实现","titleEn":"Design and Implementation of an Edge-AI Legged Robot Platform for Robotic Dog or Humanoid Applications","areaZh":"机器人","areaEn":"robot","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. NG David","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-14 10:34:01","descriptionText":"\"This master project aims to design, build, and evaluate a real working Edge-AI legged robot platform, which can be implemented as either a four-legged robotic dog or a two-legged humanoid robot. The project will integrate mechanical design, embedded electronics, IC selection, power regulation, robot control software, sensors, and artificial intelligence into one complete robotic system.\n Students will develop a modular legged robot using smart servo motors, linkage mechanisms, 3D-printed parts, and lightweight structural materials. The robot will include a power management system, such as 24V to 12V DC-DC regulation, or related, together with suitable ICs, microcontrollers, communication modules, sensors, and safety functions.\n The software component will include servo control, inverse kinematics, gait generation, balance control, sensor feedback, and basic autonomous movement. Students will also explore the implementation of edge AI functions, such as computer vision, voice command recognition, local LLM/VLM interaction, and text-to-speech response.\n The final robot should be able to demonstrate basic movement, sensor-based perception, and simple human–robot interaction. The project is suitable for students interested in robotics, embedded systems, mechatronics, AI, and intelligent hardware development.\n Deliverables\n - Working robotic dog or humanoid prototype\n - Mechanical CAD and assembly files\n - Electronics / PCB / wiring documentation\n - Robot control and AI software\n - Testing results and performance analysis\n - Final dissertation and demonstration video\n For those interested in Dr. David Ng's project, please send an email to the address, david2016hku@gmail.com.","descZh":"\"本硕士项目旨在设计、搭建并评估一台真实可工作的边缘 AI 腿足机器人平台，可做成四足机器狗或两足人形机器人。项目将机械设计、嵌入式电子、IC 选型、电源调节、机器人控制软件、传感器与人工智能集成为一个完整的机器人系统。\n学生将使用智能舵机、连杆机构、3D 打印件与轻质结构材料开发模块化腿足机器人。机器人将包含电源管理系统（如 24V 转 12V 的 DC-DC 调节或类似方案），并配备合适的 IC、微控制器、通信模块、传感器与安全功能。\n软件部分将包括舵机控制、逆运动学、步态生成、平衡控制、传感器反馈与基础自主移动。学生还将探索边缘 AI 功能的实现，如计算机视觉、语音指令识别、本地 LLM/VLM 交互与语音合成应答。\n最终机器人应能演示基本移动、基于传感器的感知与简单的人机交互。项目适合对机器人、嵌入式系统、机电一体化、AI 与智能硬件开发感兴趣的学生。\n交付成果：\n- 可工作的机器狗或人形原型\n- 机械 CAD 与装配文件\n- 电子/PCB/接线文档\n- 机器人控制与 AI 软件\n- 测试结果与性能分析\n- 最终论文与演示视频\n有意参与 David Ng 博士项目者，请发邮件至 david2016hku@gmail.com。\"","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YOTONG-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"研究空气动力学、通风与能源的计算流体力学（CFD）方法","titleEn":"Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) approach for studying Aerodynamics, Ventilation, and Energy","areaZh":"航空航天；设计与制造；能源；环境；机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Aerospace; Design & manufacture; Energy; Environment; Robotics; Drones & Control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Ir Dr. TONG N. Y. O.","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) technique is a weighty constituent for design consideration. It is anticipated that the current study can generally contribute to control and optimization strategies of an advanced design over aerodynamics, ventilation and energy performance which are meaningful\noutcomes for both academic research and practical industrial and building communities. It is also anticipated that students would be equipped with both theoretical and practical knowledge and skills for future study and work.\nNo pre- and co-requisites required.","descZh":"计算流体力学（CFD）技术是设计考量的重要组成部分。预期本研究可在空气动力学、通风与能耗性能方面，为先进设计的控制与优化策略作出总体贡献，这些成果对学术研究以及工业界和建筑界实践都具有意义。同时，学生也将获得兼具理论与实践的知识与技能，为今后的学习与工作做好准备。\n无先修或并修课程要求。","cats":["AERO","DES","ENE","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YOTONG-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向冷水机组优化的人工智能或用于故障检测诊断的机器学习","titleEn":"Artificial Intelligence for Chiller Optimization or Machine Learning for Fault Detection Diagnosis","areaZh":"AI；能源；机器人；无人机与控制；设计与制造；管理","areaEn":"AI; Energy; Robotics; Drones and Control; Design and manufacture; Management","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Ir Dr. TONG N. Y. O.","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-15 21:07:01","descriptionText":"Artificial Intelligence (AI) approach will be used to determine energy consumption and particular characteristics for chiller performance optimization. AI approach will be used for prediction model development and comparison against practical performance. It is anticipated that scientific and reasonable strategies for energy management can be resulted in response to the forecast and compared data in this study. It is also anticipated that students would be equipped with both theoretical and practical skills and experience for future study and work.\nFor Fault Detection Diagnosis, it is anticipated that the study on machine learning models and/or ensemble learning can help develop effective maintenance and operation strategies for automation with foreseeable solution for the practical implementation of fault diagnosis. It is also anticipated that students would be equipped with both theoretical and practical knowledge and skills for future study and work.\nNo pre- and co-requisites required.","descZh":"将采用人工智能（AI）方法来确定冷水机组的能耗与特定特性，以实现性能优化。AI 方法将用于预测模型的开发，并与实际性能进行对比。预计本研究可针对预测结果与对比数据，得出科学合理的能源管理策略。同时预计学生将获得理论与实践的技能与经验，为未来的学习与工作做好准备。\n对于故障检测与诊断（Fault Detection Diagnosis），预计对机器学习模型和/或集成学习的研究有助于制定有效的自动化维护与运行策略，并为故障诊断的实际落地提供可预见的解决方案。同时预计学生将获得理论与实践的知识与技能，为未来的学习与工作做好准备。\n无先修与同修要求。","cats":["AERO","AI","DES","ENE","MGT","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YOTONG-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向太阳能电站功率预测与能源管理的机器学习方法","titleEn":"Machine Learning approach for Solar Plant Power Forecasting and Energy Management","areaZh":"AI；能源；机器人；无人机与控制；设计与制造；管理；电厂","areaEn":"AI; Energy; Robotics; Drones and Control; Design and manufacture; Management; Power Plant","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Ir Dr. TONG N. Y. O.","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-15 21:07:01","descriptionText":"Machine Learning approach will be used to forecasting solar plant power and the associated energy management strategies. Renewable power generation and electricity-demand forecasting over multiple horizons are targeted to be covered. It is expected that scientific and reasonable strategies for energy management can be resulted. It is also anticipated that students would be equipped with both theoretical and practical knowledge and skills for future study and work.\nNo pre- and co-requisites required.","descZh":"将采用机器学习方法来预测太阳能电站的发电功率以及相关的能源管理策略。目标涵盖多个时间尺度的可再生能源发电预测和电力需求预测。预期能够得出科学合理的能源管理策略。同时，预期学生将具备面向未来学习和工作的理论与实践知识和技能。\n无需先修及并修要求。","cats":["AERO","AI","DES","ENE","MGT","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YOTONG-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"建筑能耗性能与能效优化的数值分析","titleEn":"Numerical Analysis on the Building Energy Performance and Energy Efficiency Optimization","areaZh":"能源；控制；热流体；建成环境；建筑设备；机电","areaEn":"Energy; Control; Thermofluids; Built Environment; Building Services; Electrical & Mechanical","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Ir Dr. TONG N. Y. O.","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-15 21:07:01","descriptionText":"Numerical analysis will be used to determine the building energy consumption and energy efficiency optimization. Advanced and hybrid simulation skills will be used for model development, design and operational optimization for building energy consumption. It is anticipated that scientific and reasonable strategies for building energy management can be resulted in response to the forecast and compared data in this study. It is also anticipated that students would be equipped with both theoretical and practical knowledge and skills for future study and work.\nNo pre- and co-requisites required.","descZh":"将采用数值分析来确定建筑能耗并优化能效。将使用先进的混合仿真技能进行建筑能耗的模型开发、设计与运行优化。预计本研究可针对预测结果与对比数据，得出科学合理的建筑能源管理策略。同时预计学生将获得理论与实践的知识与技能，为未来的学习与工作做好准备。\n无先修与同修要求。","cats":["CS","CTRL","ENE","MECH","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YOTONG-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向特定工业监测或光伏组件的基于MATLAB的工业机器人仿真","titleEn":"MATLAB-based Simulation for Industrial Robot in Specific Industry Monitoring or Photovoltaic Module","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制；设计与制造；能源；机电","areaEn":"Robotics; Drones and Control; Design and manufacture; Energy; Electrical and Mechanical","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Ir Dr. TONG N. Y. O.","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"The use of industrial robots based on MATLAB simulation for specific industry environment\nmonitoring is to better monitor the particular environment better and reduce monitoring costs. It is\nanticipated that autonomous acquisition process and hierarchical software progression; \narchitecture would be generated. It is also anticipated that students would be equipped with both\ntheoretical and practical knowledge and skills for future study and work.\nFor Photovoltaic Module, it is anticipated that the characteristics of PV modules &amp; associated power generations under different environmental conditions with particular algorithms would be simulated using MATLAB Simulink software. Theoretical calculations and results from MATLAB Simulink would be compared for verification of the design. It is also anticipated that students would be equipped with both theoretical and practical knowledge and skills for future study and work.\nNo pre- and co-requisites required.","descZh":"利用基于 MATLAB 仿真的工业机器人对特定工业环境进行监测，目的在于更好地监测特定环境并降低监测成本。预期将形成自主采集流程与层次化软件架构。同时，学生也将获得兼具理论与实践的知识与技能，为今后的学习与工作做好准备。\n针对光伏组件：预期将使用 MATLAB Simulink 软件，结合特定算法对不同环境条件下光伏组件特性及其发电情况进行仿真；并将 MATLAB Simulink 的理论计算与结果进行比对以验证设计。同时，学生也将获得兼具理论与实践的知识与技能。\n无先修或并修课程要求。","cats":["AERO","DES","ENE","MECH","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YOTONG-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向建筑及/或相关能耗分析的绿色建筑信息模型（BIM）","titleEn":"Green-based Building Information Modelling (BIM) for Buildings and/or Associated Energy Analysis","areaZh":"能源；管理；自然与建成环境；热流体；管理；建筑设备","areaEn":"Energy; Management; Natural & built environment; Thermofluids; Management; Building Services","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Ir Dr. TONG N. Y. O.","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-15 18:34:01","descriptionText":"The outcomes of this study will be meaningful as a partial reference to help achieve an enhanced\nbuilding design. It is anticipated that positive theoretical and practical contributions to engineering\nindustry by green-based BIM modelling framework and/or associated building energy performance analysis can be achieved. It is also\nanticipated that students would be equipped with both theoretical and practical knowledge and\nskills for future study and work.\nNo pre- and co-requisites required.","descZh":"本研究的成果将具有一定参考意义，有助于实现更好的建筑设计。预期能够通过基于绿色的 BIM 建模框架和/或相关的建筑能耗性能分析，为工程行业做出积极的理论与实践贡献。同时，预计学生将同时掌握理论与实践知识与技能，为今后的学习与工作做好准备。本项目无先修课程或并修课程要求。","cats":["CS","ENE","MGT","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZHANGCP8-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"克服色序显示色彩分裂效应的新型图像算法","titleEn":"Novel image algorithm to overcome the color breakup effect of color sequential display","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. ZHANG Cuiping","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-10 21:07:02","descriptionText":"Color sequential display achieves full-color output by rapidly cycling RGB primaries in time sequence, offering three key advantages over traditional color-filter solutions: 1) 2-3x higher optical efficiency (no filter loss); 2) increased pixel density (single-pixel full-color); 3) wider color gamut. The technology has been adopted in DLP projectors, field-sequential LCDs (FS-LCDs), and some Micro LED systems. However, its major challenge is \"color breakup\": when displaying fast-moving content or during eye saccades, temporal separation of RGB components causes observers to perceive colored edges. This phenomenon becomes pronounced when refresh rates fall below 3x the critical fusion frequency (~180Hz), and can be mitigated by higher refresh rates (480Hz+), optimized color sequencing algorithms, or eye-tracking compensation techniques. In recent years, the rapid development of Micro LED technology has enabled significant innovation in color sequential display systems. Through innovative image algorithm design, a novel display architecture can be achieved that features high efficiency, high brightness, and elimination of the color breakup phenomenon. This project is aimed to propose a novel image algorithm to overcome the color breakup effect of color sequential display technology.\nPre Requisite:\n1. Able to use the programming language, such as Matlab, Python and so on.","descZh":"时序彩色显示通过在时间序列上快速循环 RGB 三基色实现全彩输出，相比传统滤色方案具有三大优势：1) 光学效率高 2~3 倍（无滤光损失）；2) 像素密度更高（单像素全彩）；3) 色域更广。该技术已应用于 DLP 投影机、场序液晶（FS-LCD）及部分 Micro LED 系统。然而，其主要挑战是\"彩色分裂\"（color breakup）：显示快速移动内容或眼球扫视时，RGB 分量的时间分离会使观察者感知到彩色边缘。当刷新率低于临界融合频率（约 180Hz）的 3 倍时，该现象会变得明显；可通过更高刷新率（480Hz 以上）、优化的时序算法或眼动跟踪补偿技术加以缓解。近年来，Micro LED 技术的快速发展推动了时序彩色显示系统的重大创新。通过创新的图像算法设计，可实现具有高效率、高亮度并消除彩色分裂现象的新型显示架构。本项目旨在提出一种新颖的图像算法，以克服时序彩色显示技术的彩色分裂效应。\n先修要求：1. 能使用 Matlab、Python 等编程语言。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZHANGCP8-11","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向工程求职者的AI辅助虚拟模拟面试平台开发","titleEn":"Development of an AI-Assisted Virtual Mock Interview Platform for Engineering Job Seekers","areaZh":"AI+教育","areaEn":"AI+Education","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. ZHANG Cuiping","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This project focuses on designing and implementing an immersive virtual mock interview system specialised for certain engineering major (can be determined by students) students preparing for industry recruitment. Combining virtual scene simulation and domain-adapted artificial intelligence, the platform delivers realistic technical interview practices accessible via ordinary PCs, with expandable VR interactive modules for enhanced immersion.\nThe embedded AI interview engine generates adaptive technical questions. It engages in real-time interactive dialogue with candidates, tracks their verbal responses, and assesses the accuracy, depth and logicality of technical answers. The virtual environment reconstructs typical interview scenarios to relieve students’ interview anxiety and help them adapt to formal technical questioning.\nUpon completion of each simulation session, the system generates structured evaluation reports, pinpointing deficiencies in professional knowledge and offering targeted review recommendations. This system fills the gap of discipline-specific technical interview training tools. It verifies how immersive virtual environments combined with domain AI can improve the efficiency of pre-job technical training for engineering undergraduates and postgraduates in microelectronics-related majors.","descZh":"本项目聚焦于设计并实现一个沉浸式虚拟模拟面试系统，专门面向正在为行业招聘作准备的某一工科专业（可由学生自定）学生。平台结合虚拟场景仿真与领域自适应人工智能，通过普通 PC 即可获得逼真的技术面试练习，并配有可扩展的 VR 交互模块以增强沉浸感。\n内嵌 AI 面试引擎可生成自适应的技术问题，与应试者进行实时交互对话，跟踪其口头作答，并评估技术回答的准确性、深度与逻辑性。虚拟环境重构典型面试场景，以缓解学生的面试焦虑，帮助他们适应正式的技术提问。\n每次模拟结束后，系统生成结构化评估报告，指出专业知识方面的不足并给出针对性的复习建议。该系统填补了学科专用技术面试训练工具的空白，并验证了沉浸式虚拟环境结合领域 AI 如何提升微电子相关专业本硕学生岗前技术训练的效率。","cats":["MGT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZHANGCP8-12","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向中学学习的语音交互AI学生教学模拟平台","titleEn":"Voice-Interactive AI Platform Enabling Student Teaching Simulation for Middle School Learning","areaZh":"AI+STEM教育","areaEn":"AI + STEM Education","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. ZHANG Cuiping","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Leveraging the protégé effect, this work designs and implements a voice-interactive AI learning platform for middle school mathematics or physics. The platform provides standard textbook-aligned PPT handouts for students. Learners need to explain academic content verbally to the AI system based on the provided slides.\nThe core innovation lies in the role setting: the AI simulates a student audience rather than an evaluator. While listening to the learner’s explanation, the AI continuously raises follow-up questions to challenge incomplete arguments, ambiguous definitions and logical flaws. The system integrates speech recognition, natural language understanding and voice synthesis to realize seamless oral human-computer dialogue.\nOnce the explanation activity finishes, the platform produces personalised feedback, summarising misconceptions and suggesting targeted review arrangements. Traditional online learning tools mostly adopt question-and-quiz modes. This project builds a new practice channel that encourages students to organise knowledge independently for teaching. The final system will be evaluated to explore its effectiveness in consolidating conceptual mastery and improving students’ critical thinking ability in middle school STEM subjects.","descZh":"本作品利用\"教授他人效应\"（protégé effect），设计并实现一个面向中学数学或物理的语音交互式 AI 学习平台。平台为学生提供与标准教材同步的 PPT 讲义；学习者需要基于所给幻灯片，向 AI 系统口头讲解学科内容。\n核心创新在于角色设定：AI 模拟的是学生听众而非评分者。在聆听学习者讲解的同时，AI 会持续提出追问，质疑不完整的论证、含糊的定义与逻辑漏洞。系统集成语音识别、自然语言理解与语音合成，实现无缝的口头人机对话。\n讲解活动结束后，平台生成个性化反馈，总结概念误区并建议针对性的复习安排。传统在线学习工具多采用问答与测验模式；本项目开辟一种新的练习通道，鼓励学生自主整理知识进行讲授。最终系统将接受评估，以探索其在巩固概念掌握、提升中学生 STEM 学科批判性思维方面的效果。","cats":["MGT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZHANGCP8-13","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向工科学生的带语音交互的AI辅助模拟论文答辩系统","titleEn":"AI-Assisted Simulated Dissertation Defense System with Speech Interaction for Engineering Students","areaZh":"AI+STEM教育","areaEn":"AI + STEM Education","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. ZHANG Cuiping","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This project aims to construct an intelligent simulated defense platform with voice interaction to facilitate engineering students' dissertation presentation preparation. Students upload their defense PPTs and deliver complete oral presentations inside the system. The AI simulates academic examiners, raises relevant questions and engages in interactive Q&A after the presentation.\nThe system automatically analyzes multiple dimensions of the presentation performance. Assessment metrics include speech fluency, accuracy of academic expressions, logical integrity of research narration, and adequacy of answers to academic questions. A personalized feedback report will be produced, highlighting weaknesses such as vague statements, improper terminology and incomplete argumentation, alongside concrete suggestions for content revision and presentation skill improvement.\nUnlike generic speech training software, this platform is tailored for academic dissertation defenses. It leverages text analysis techniques to realize automated evaluation. The system enables students to conduct unlimited self-practice without organizing real review panels. This study validates the value of AI simulation in boosting postgraduates’ presentation proficiency and alleviating pressure during formal thesis defense.","descZh":"本项目旨在构建一个带语音交互的智能模拟答辩平台，帮助工科学生准备学位论文答辩。学生上传答辩 PPT 并在系统内完成完整口头陈述；AI 模拟学术评审专家，在陈述后提出相关问题并进行交互式问答。\n系统自动从多个维度分析陈述表现。评估指标包括语言流利度、学术表达准确性、研究叙述的逻辑完整性以及学术问题回答的充分性。系统将生成个性化反馈报告，指出表述含糊、术语不当、论证不完整等不足，并给出内容修改与表达技能提升的具体建议。\n与通用语音训练软件不同，本平台针对学术论文答辩定制，借助文本分析技术实现自动化评估。系统使学生无需组织真实评审组即可进行不限次数的自我练习。本研究验证了 AI 模拟在提升研究生表达能力、缓解正式答辩压力方面的价值。","cats":["MGT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIXF-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"紧凑型波浪能转换装置的设计与性能优化","titleEn":"Design and Performance Optimization of a Compact Wave Energy Converter","areaZh":"机械设计；工程优化；可再生能源；海洋工程","areaEn":"Mechanical Design; Engineering Optimization; Renewable Energy; Ocean Engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Xiaofan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 18:07:14","descriptionText":"Wave energy is a reliable renewable resource for Hong Kong’s outlying islands and remote coastal sites, yet many existing converters remain too large or costly for practical deployment. This project focuses on the conceptual design and performance optimization of a compact oscillating-body or oscillating-water-column type wave energy converter. You will review key working principles and recent designs, then develop a simplified numerical model in MATLAB to study how geometry, mass, and damping affect power output under typical Hong Kong sea states. The work includes identifying promising design improvements and evaluating technical and economic feasibility for small-scale or hybrid applications. The final deliverable is a clear design framework and performance comparison that can guide future prototype development.","descZh":"波浪能是香港离岛与偏远沿海地区可靠的可再生资源，然而许多现有换能器体积过大或成本过高，难以实际部署。本项目聚焦于一种紧凑型振荡体式或振荡水柱式波浪能装置的方案设计与性能优化。你将梳理关键工作原理与近期设计，随后在 MATLAB 中建立简化数值模型，研究典型香港海况下几何、质量与阻尼对发电功率的影响。工作包括识别有前景的设计改进，并评估其面向小型或混合应用的工程与经济可行性。最终交付一套清晰的设计框架与性能对比，可为未来原型开发提供指导。","cats":["CS","ENE","FLU","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIXF-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"最大化波浪能俘获的最优控制策略","titleEn":"Optimal Control Strategies for Maximizing Wave Energy Capture","areaZh":"机械设计；工程优化；可再生能源；海洋工程","areaEn":"Mechanical Design; Engineering Optimization; Renewable Energy; Ocean Engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Xiaofan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 18:07:39","descriptionText":"The amount of electricity a wave energy converter produces depends strongly on how its motion is controlled. This project explores practical, low-complexity control methods that improve energy capture without requiring expensive real-time sensors or heavy computation.You will study basic power take-off (PTO) systems and reactive/damping control ideas, implement simple algorithms in simulation, and compare energy output under irregular waves. Emphasis is placed on understanding the trade-offs between performance, robustness, and ease of implementation. The outcome is a recommended control approach and a clear evaluation of its potential benefits for real devices.","descZh":"波浪能装置产电量在很大程度上取决于其运动如何被控制。本项目探索实用、低复杂度的控制方法，在无需昂贵实时传感器或重计算的情况下提升能量捕获。你将学习基本的能量摄取（PTO）系统与无功/阻尼控制思路，在仿真中实现简单算法，并比较不规则波浪下的能量输出。重点在于理解性能、鲁棒性与实现简易度之间的权衡。成果为一套推荐的控制方法，以及其对实际装置潜在收益的清晰评估。","cats":["CS","ENE","FLU","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIXF-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"浮式海上风机的振动抑制概念","titleEn":"Vibration Suppression Concepts for Floating Offshore Wind Turbines","areaZh":"机械设计；工程优化；可再生能源；海洋工程","areaEn":"Mechanical Design; Engineering Optimization; Renewable Energy; Ocean Engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Xiaofan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 18:07:53","descriptionText":"Floating offshore wind turbines experience continuous wave and wind loading that can reduce structural life and increase maintenance costs. This project investigates passive and semi-active vibration control ideas including tuned mass damper and tuned mass damper inerter concepts suitable for turbine towers.You will review the main vibration sources, build a simplified dynamic model of a tower, and explore how different damper parameters affect response under typical loading. The work stays at the conceptual and simulation level and ends with practical design guidelines and a feasibility discussion for Hong Kong or Greater Bay Area conditions.","descZh":"浮式海上风机持续承受波浪与风的荷载，可能缩短结构寿命并增加维护成本。本项目研究适用于风机塔筒的无源与半主动减振方案，包括调谐质量阻尼器（TMD）与调谐质量阻尼-惯容器（TMD inerter）概念。你将梳理主要振动来源，建立塔筒的简化动力学模型，并考察典型荷载下不同阻尼器参数对响应的作用。工作停留在概念与仿真层面，最终给出实用设计指南，并就香港或大湾区条件下的可行性进行讨论。","cats":["CS","ENE","FLU","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIXF-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"小型海上风电系统浮式平台的概念设计","titleEn":"Conceptual Design of a Floating Platform for Small Offshore Wind System","areaZh":"机械设计；工程优化；可再生能源；海洋工程","areaEn":"Mechanical Design; Engineering Optimization; Renewable Energy; Ocean Engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Xiaofan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 18:08:02","descriptionText":"Floating platforms open new sites for offshore wind in deeper waters. This project develops a conceptual design for a simple floating platform that could support a small or medium wind turbine.You will study existing platform types including spar, semi-submersible, barge types, evaluate stability and motion characteristics with basic calculations or software, and propose a layout optimized for cost, ease of installation, and Hong Kong sea conditions. The final output is a clear design concept together with a technical and economic feasibility assessment.","descZh":"浮式平台为在更深海域发展海上风电开辟了新场址。本项目为一种可支撑小型或中型风机的简易浮式平台进行概念设计。你将研究包括立柱式（spar）、半潜式、驳船式在内的现有平台类型，通过基础计算或软件评估其稳定性与运动特性，并提出面向成本、安装便利性与香港海况优化的布置方案。最终输出为清晰的设计概念，以及技术与经济可行性评估。","cats":["CS","ENE","FLU","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIXF-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"浮式太阳能平台的概念设计与稳定性分析","titleEn":"Conceptual Design and Stability Analysis of Floating Solar Platforms","areaZh":"机械设计；工程优化；可再生能源；海洋工程","areaEn":"Mechanical Design; Engineering Optimization; Renewable Energy; Ocean Engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Xiaofan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 18:08:12","descriptionText":"Floating photovoltaic (FPV) systems can utilise water surfaces while reducing land-use conflict, yet they face unique challenges from waves, wind, and biofouling. This project focuses on the conceptual design of a modular floating solar platform suitable for sheltered coastal or reservoir environments in Hong Kong. You will examine existing FPV structures, analyze basic hydrodynamic and structural requirements, and explore simple design improvements for stability, maintenance access, and cost. The outcome is a proposed platform concept and a practical evaluation of its suitability for local conditions.","descZh":"水上光伏（FPV）系统可利用水面并减少土地占用冲突，但也面临波浪、风与生物污损带来的独特挑战。本项目聚焦于一种适用于香港受遮蔽水库或近岸环境的模块化水上光伏平台的方案设计。你将研究现有 FPV 结构，分析基本水动力与结构要求，并探索面向稳定性、维护可达性与成本方面的简化改进设计。成果为一份平台概念方案，以及其适用于本地条件的实际评估。","cats":["CS","ENE","FLU","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIXF-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向偏远沿海社区的混合海洋可再生能源系统","titleEn":"Hybrid Marine Renewable Energy Systems for Remote Coastal Communities","areaZh":"机械设计；工程优化；可再生能源；海洋工程","areaEn":"Mechanical Design; Engineering Optimization; Renewable Energy; Ocean Engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Xiaofan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 18:08:22","descriptionText":"Single renewable sources are intermittent; combining wave energy with solar and wind can provide more stable power for islands or remote coastal sites. This project studies the technical and economic feasibility of a hybrid marine renewable energy system. You will review complementary resource characteristics, develop a simplified energy-balance model, and evaluate different sizing and control strategies for a representative Hong Kong outlying-island scenario. The final deliverable is a recommended system configuration and a clear assessment of its advantages over single-source solutions.","descZh":"单一可再生能源具有间歇性；将波浪能与太阳能、风能相结合，可为岛屿或偏远沿海地区提供更稳定的电力。本项目研究混合海洋可再生能源系统的技术与经济可行性。你将梳理各类资源的互补特性，开发简化的能量平衡模型，并针对香港某离岛的代表性场景评估不同的系统规模与运行控制策略。最终交付物为一套推荐的系统配置，以及相对单一能源方案优势的清晰评估。","cats":["CS","ENE","FLU","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIXF-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于海岸波浪衰减的超结构概念","titleEn":"Metastructure Concepts for Coastal Wave Attenuation","areaZh":"机械设计；工程优化；可再生能源；海洋工程","areaEn":"Mechanical Design; Engineering Optimization; Renewable Energy; Ocean Engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Xiaofan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 18:08:32","descriptionText":"Traditional breakwaters are bulky and expensive. Engineered metastructures with periodic or resonant structures can dissipate or redirect wave energy more efficiently at lower material cost. This project investigates simple metastructure concepts for coastal protection.You will review recent metamaterial and metastructure ideas for water waves, analyse basic wave–structure interaction with simplified models, and propose a practical design concept suitable for Hong Kong coastal conditions. Emphasis is on understanding working principles and evaluating feasibility rather than full-scale engineering.","descZh":"传统的防波堤体积庞大且造价高昂。具有周期性或谐振结构的工程化超结构（metastructure）能够以更低的材料成本更高效地耗散或改变波浪能量的传播方向。本项目研究用于海岸防护的简单超结构概念。你将综述近期针对水波的超材料（metamaterial）和超结构构想，利用简化模型分析基本的波浪-结构相互作用，并提出一个适合香港沿海条件的实用设计概念。重点在于理解工作原理和评估可行性，而非进行全尺寸工程。","cats":["CS","ENE","FLU","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIXF-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于水下航行器的仿生涡环推进器","titleEn":"Bio-inspired Vortex-Ring Thruster for Underwater Vehicles","areaZh":"机械设计；工程优化；可再生能源；海洋工程","areaEn":"Mechanical Design; Engineering Optimization; Renewable Energy; Ocean Engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Xiaofan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 18:08:42","descriptionText":"Traditional propellers can be noisy and less efficient at low speeds. Vortex-ring thrusters inspired by squid and jellyfish offer quiet, efficient alternatives for small underwater robots. This project explores the basic design and performance of a simple vortex-ring thruster.You will study the formation and propulsion principles of vortex rings, develop a conceptual thruster layout, and use simplified analytical or CFD models to examine thrust and efficiency under different operating parameters. The outcome is a design concept and performance insights that can support future soft or rigid underwater vehicles.","descZh":"传统螺旋桨在低速时噪声较大且效率较低。受鱿鱼与水母启发的涡环推进器为小型水下机器人提供了安静、高效的替代方案。本项目探索简易涡环推进器的基本设计与性能。你将研究涡环的形成与推进原理，提出推进器概念布局，并使用简化的解析或 CFD 模型考察不同运行参数下的推力与效率。成果为一套设计方案与性能认识，可为未来软体或刚性水下航行器提供支持。","cats":["CS","ENE","FLU","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIXF-9","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向海洋任务的仿生或模块化水下机器人概念设计","titleEn":"Conceptual Design of a Bio-inspired or Modular Underwater Robot for Marine Tasks","areaZh":"机械设计；工程优化；机器人；海洋工程","areaEn":"Mechanical Design; Engineering Optimization; Robotics; Ocean Engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Xiaofan","moderator":"","capacity":8,"availCap":8,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 18:08:52","descriptionText":"Underwater robots are increasingly used for inspection of marine infrastructure, cables, and environmental monitoring. This project focuses on the practical integration of sensors including IMU, camera, sonar etc. and the development of simple motion-control strategies for a small ROV or AUV platform. You will select suitable sensors, study basic state estimation and PID or similar control methods, and evaluate performance through simulation or laboratory-scale tests. The final output is a clear system design and control framework that can support inspection tasks in coastal waters.","descZh":"水下机器人越来越多地用于海洋基础设施、电缆检测与环境监测。本项目聚焦于 IMU、相机、声呐等传感器的工程集成，以及小型 ROV/AUV 平台简单运动控制策略的开发。你将选择合适的传感器，学习基础状态估计与 PID 或类似控制方法，并通过仿真或实验室规模测试评估性能。最终输出为一套清晰可行的系统设计与控制框架，可支持近岸水域的检测任务。","cats":["CS","FLU","MECH","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIXF-10","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"软体机器人夹爪的传感与简易控制策略","titleEn":"Sensing and Simple Control Strategies for Soft Robotic Grippers","areaZh":"机械设计；工程优化；机器人","areaEn":"Mechanical Design; Engineering Optimization; Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Xiaofan","moderator":"","capacity":8,"availCap":8,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 18:09:02","descriptionText":"This is a collaborative project with GLI Tech: http://www.glitech.com/. You will have the opportunity to conduct an intern at their technology base in Shenzhen. \nEffective use of soft robotic hands requires reliable sensing of contact and force, plus straightforward control. This project investigates basic sensing approaches and simple closed-loop or open-loop control methods for soft multi-finger grippers. You will review current sensing and control techniques, propose a practical sensing + control scheme for a representative grasping task, and evaluate it through analysis or limited experiments. The final deliverable is a workable sensing-and-control concept together with implementation guidelines.","descZh":"本项目为与 GLI Tech 的合作项目：http://www.glitech.com/。你将有机会在其深圳技术基地实习。\n有效使用软体机械手需要可靠的接触与力感知，以及直观的控制方式。本项目研究软体多指夹爪的基本感知方案与简单的闭环或开环控制方法。你将梳理当前感知与控制技术，为一项代表性抓取任务提出实用的\"感知+控制\"方案，并通过分析或有限实验加以评估。最终交付物为可行的感知-控制概念与实施指南。","cats":["CS","MECH","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-SWXVIP-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"叠层衍射成像相位图计算","titleEn":"Ptychography Phase Image Computation","areaZh":"计算机科学；材料科学；物理学","areaEn":"computer science; material science; physics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":15,"availCap":15,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project Overview\nThis project focuses on exploring iterative ptychographic reconstruction algorithms to perform atomic-resolution phase image reconstruction and understand their contrast transfer functions. Ptychography is a cutting-edge phase image reconstruction method, aiming to retrieve the phase information of the sample using the electron diffractions collected by the electron detector. The recorded electron diffraction intensity is the modulus square of the Fourier-space exit wavefunction, losing the phase information. Phase shift carries important structural information of the sample, such as small shift from low Z elements, and must be retrieved. Ptychography solved this phase problem by direct or iterative processing of the diffraction patterns in four-dimensional scanning transmission electron microscopy (4D STEM) datasets. However, the transfer function of these well-established iterative algorithms is unclear, posing challenges in interpreting the phase shift retrieved from these methods.\nObjectives:\nYou will leverage existing Python and MATLAB programs for iterative ptychographic phase reconstruction, including py4dstem, Ptychoshelves etc to perform image reconstruction on the simulated and experimental datasets. There are multiple optimization schemes during the iteration to solve the inverse problems, differing in the update rules in the object and probe functions. You will compare the transfer functions of the implemented schemes, including ePIE, gradient descent, and maximum likelihood, to identify any rules. You will also explore the depth resolution that can be achieved by these iterative methods.\nTasks:\n1, Understanding electron-matter interaction fundamentals.\n2, Establishing atom models for 4D STEM dataset simulation.\n3, Running iterative algorithms to reconstruct the phase information of the object.\n4, Running the iterative processing of experimental 4D STEM datasets.\n5, Differing the transfer function in these iterative schemes and depth resolution. \nWhat You'll Gain:\nThis project will equip you with expertise in advanced 4D STEM. You’ll develop skills in simulation software, algorithm development, and quantitative analysis, building a strong portfolio for academic or industry roles. You will have opportunities to be co-supervised by staff from world-leading electron microscopy centres, for example, the University of Oxford. Collaboration with microscopy experts and potential integration with experimental data will enhance your research profile, paving the way for thesis work or PhD opportunities. The project offers opportunities for publication in prestigious journals.\nRequired Skills and Background\n1, Strong understanding of physics, particularly wave optics, quantum mechanics, or electron scattering in solids.\n2, Proficiency in mathematics, including Fourier transforms, and numerical methods.\n3, Solid Python programming skills, with experience in scientific libraries (e.g., NumPy, SciPy, Matplotlib) and ideally image processing or simulation tools.\n4, Basic knowledge of crystallography or electron microscopy is advantageous.\n5, Enthusiasm for computational challenges is essential.","descZh":"项目概述：本项目聚焦探索迭代式叠层衍射（ptychography）重建算法，以实现原子分辨率相位图像重建并理解其衬度传递函数。叠层衍射是一种前沿的相位图像重建方法，旨在利用电子探测器收集的电子衍射数据恢复样品的相位信息。记录的电子衍射强度是傅里叶空间出射波函数的模平方，丢失了相位信息。相移携带着样品的重要结构信息（如低原子序数元素引起的微小偏移），必须被恢复。叠层衍射通过直接或迭代处理四维扫描透射电子显微术（4D STEM）数据集中的衍射图案解决了这一相位问题。然而，这些成熟迭代算法的传递函数尚不清晰，给解读从这些方法恢复的相移带来挑战。\n目标：你将利用现有 Python 与 MATLAB 迭代叠层衍射相位重建程序（包括 py4dstem、Ptychoshelves 等），对仿真与实验数据集进行图像重建。迭代求解逆问题有多种优化方案，其在物体与探针函数的更新规则上有所不同。你将比较所实现方案（包括 ePIE、梯度下降与最大似然）的传递函数，识别任何规律；还将探索这些迭代方法可实现的深度分辨率。\n任务：\n1. 理解电子-物质相互作用基础；\n2. 建立用于 4D STEM 数据集仿真的原子模型；\n3. 运行迭代算法重建物体的相位信息；\n4. 运行实验 4D STEM 数据集的迭代处理；\n5. 区分这些迭代方案中的传递函数与深度分辨率。\n你将获得：本项目将赋予你先进 4D STEM 的专业知识。你将培养仿真软件、算法开发与定量分析技能，为学术或行业岗位打造坚实作品集。你有机会获得牛津大学等世界领先电子显微中心人员的共同指导。与显微专家合作并可能集成实验数据，将提升你的研究资历，为论文工作或博士机会铺路。项目提供在高水平期刊发表的机会。\n所需技能与背景：\n1. 扎实的物理理解，尤其波动光学、量子力学或固体中的电子散射；\n2. 熟练的数学能力，包括傅里叶变换与数值方法；\n3. 扎实的 Python 编程技能，熟悉科学计算库（如 NumPy、SciPy、Matplotlib），最好有图像处理或仿真工具经验；\n4. 具备晶体学或电子显微术基础知识者优先；\n5. 对计算挑战的热情不可或缺。","cats":["CS","MAT","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-SWXVIP-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向高分辨率电子显微镜的AI驱动探针像差校正","titleEn":"AI-driven probe aberration correction for high-resolution electron microscopy","areaZh":"计算机科学；材料科学；物理学","areaEn":"computer science; material science; physics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project Overview\nScanning Transmission Electron Microscopy (STEM) is a powerful tool for atomic-scale imaging, but the resolution is fundamentally limited by aberrations in the electromagnetic probe-forming lenses. These aberrations distort the electron wavefunction, corrupting the phase information carried by scattered electrons and leading to blurred, aberrated images. Current methods for measuring and correcting these aberrations, such as using iterative algorithms, singular value decomposition or hardware correctors, can be slow, sensitive to noise, or prohibitively complex and expensive.\nThis project aims to develop a novel, AI-based framework to automatically, rapidly, and precisely determine lens aberrations directly from experimental data. We will leverage deep convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to learn the complex relationship between aberrated STEM datasets and the underlying aberration coefficients of the electron probe. Once identified, these coefficients will be used to perform computational aberration corrections, enabling the reconstruction of aberration-free, high-fidelity images and quantitative electron ptychographic phase images. This software-based solution has the potential to democratize ultra-high-resolution imaging, making atomic-resolution achievable on a wider range of microscopes without the need for complex hardware correctors.\nObjectives:\n1, Students will leverage existing Python and MATLAB programs (MULTEM) to simulate the four-dimensional scanning transmission electron microscopy (4D STEM) datasets with different types of aberrations.\n2, Students will perform Fourier Transform on the 4D STEM data and obtain the complex phase images at a set of spatial frequencies, distorted by aberrations.\n3, Students will create a training model based on the datasets and tune the model to recognize the aberrations. \n4, Students will utilize the optimized model to generate aberration-free datasets.\n5, Students will use ptychography reconstruction method to use these aberration-free datasets to reconstruct the aberration-free phase image.\nWhat You'll Gain:\n1, This project will equip you with expertise in advanced 4D STEM. \n2, You’ll develop skills in simulation software, AI tools, algorithm development, and quantitative analysis, building a strong portfolio for academic or industry roles. \n3, You will have opportunities to be co-supervised by staff from world-leading electron microscopy centres, for example, the University of Oxford. \n4, Collaboration with microscopy experts and potential integration with experimental data will enhance your research profile, paving the way for thesis work or PhD opportunities. \n5, The project offers opportunities for publication in prestigious journals.\nRequired Skills and Background\n1, Strong understanding of physics, particularly wave optics, quantum mechanics, or electron scattering in solids.\n2, Proficiency in mathematics, including Fourier transforms, and numerical methods.\n3, Solid Python programming skills, with experience in AI tools and ideally image processing or simulation tools.\n4, Basic knowledge of crystallography or electron microscopy is advantageous.\n5, Enthusiasm for computational challenges is essential.","descZh":"项目概述：扫描透射电子显微术（STEM）是原子尺度成像的强大工具，但其分辨率从根本上受电磁探针成形透镜像差限制。这些像差扭曲电子波函数、破坏散射电子所携相位信息，导致图像模糊、畸变。当前测量与校正这些像差的方法（如迭代算法、奇异值分解或硬件校正器）可能缓慢、对噪声敏感，或复杂昂贵得难以企及。\n本项目旨在开发一种新颖的基于 AI 的框架，直接从实验数据自动、快速、精确地确定透镜像差。我们将利用深度卷积神经网络（CNN）学习畸变 STEM 数据集与电子探针潜在像差系数之间的复杂关系。一旦识别出这些系数，将用于执行计算像差校正，从而重建无像差、高保真图像与定量电子叠层衍射相位图像。这种纯软件方案有望使超高分辨率成像平民化，让更多显微设备无需复杂硬件校正器即可实现原子级分辨率。\n目标：\n1. 学生将利用现有 Python 与 MATLAB 程序（MULTEM）仿真具有不同类型像差的 4D-STEM 数据集；\n2. 对 4D STEM 数据执行傅里叶变换，在一组空间频率下获得被像差畸变的复相位图像；\n3. 基于数据集构建训练模型并调参，使其能识别像差；\n4. 利用优化后的模型生成无像差数据集；\n5. 使用叠层衍射重建方法，利用这些无像差数据集重建无像差相位图像。\n你将获得：\n1. 该项目将赋予你先进 4D STEM 的专业知识；\n2. 你将培养仿真软件、AI 工具、算法开发与定量分析技能，为学术或行业岗位打造坚实作品集；\n3. 你有机会获得牛津大学等世界领先电子显微中心人员的共同指导；\n4. 与显微专家合作并可能集成实验数据，将提升你的研究资历，为论文工作或博士机会铺路；\n5. 项目提供在高水平期刊发表的机会。\n所需技能与背景：\n1. 扎实的物理理解，尤其波动光学、量子力学或固体中的电子散射；\n2. 熟练的数学能力，包括傅里叶变换与数值方法；\n3. 扎实的 Python 编程技能，有 AI 工具经验，最好有图像处理或仿真工具经验；\n4. 具备晶体学或电子显微术基础知识者优先；\n5. 对计算挑战的热情不可或缺。","cats":["CS","MAT","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-SWXVIP-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"电子衍射图案的AI增强去噪","titleEn":"AI-enhanced denoising for electron diffraction patterns","areaZh":"计算机科学；材料科学；物理学","areaEn":"computer science; material science; physics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 15:34:01","descriptionText":"Project Overview\nFour-dimensional Scanning Transmission Electron Microscopy (4D-STEM) is a transformative technique that captures the full diffraction pattern at each probe position, enabling powerful imaging modes like ptychography and reciprocal space diffraction processing. However, the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) in these diffraction patterns is a fundamental limitation, especially under low-electron-dose conditions necessary for imaging beam-sensitive materials. Noise arises from stochastic electron scattering, detector readout noise, and spurious backgrounds from effects like thermal diffuse scattering and charge overflow.\nThis project will develop a deep learning-based denoising framework to overcome this barrier. We will train a convolutional neural network (CNN) to distinguish the physically meaningful signal—Bragg disks, diffuse scattering, and inelastic backgrounds—from corrupting noise. The model will be optimized to process entire 4D-STEM datasets, producing clean diffraction patterns that preserve critical structural information. By recovering a high-SNR signal from noisy data, this approach will enable high-fidelity image reconstruction, unlock unprecedented resolution in low-dose experiments, and extend the frontiers of electron microscopy to the study of delicate beam-sensitive materials.\nObjectives:\n1, Students will leverage existing Python and MATLAB programs (MULTEM) to simulate the four-dimensional scanning transmission electron microscopy (4D STEM) datasets with different noise level.\n2, Students will develop AI based tools, such as deep learning and CNN, and/or other fitting algorithms to denoise the data, ideally with clear contributions from the second or high order scattering. \n3, Students will utilize the denoised datasets to identify the effect on the reconstruction, through electron ptychography reconstruction.\nWhat You'll Gain:\n1, This project will equip you with expertise in advanced 4D STEM. \n2, You’ll develop skills in simulation software, AI tools, algorithm development, and quantitative analysis, building a strong portfolio for academic or industry roles. \n3, You will have opportunities to be co-supervised by staff from world-leading electron microscopy centres, for example, the University of Oxford. \n4, Collaboration with microscopy experts and potential integration with experimental data will enhance your research profile, paving the way for thesis work or PhD opportunities. \n5, The project offers opportunities for publication in prestigious journals.\nRequired Skills and Background\n1, Strong understanding of physics, particularly wave optics, quantum mechanics, or electron scattering in solids.\n2, Proficiency in mathematics, including Fourier transforms, and numerical methods.\n3, Solid Python programming skills, with experience in AI tools and ideally image processing or simulation tools.\n4, Basic knowledge of crystallography or electron microscopy is advantageous.\n5, Enthusiasm for computational challenges is essential.","descZh":"项目概述：四维扫描透射电子显微镜（4D-STEM）是一种变革性技术，可在每个探针位置捕获完整衍射图案，支持叠层衍射成像（ptychography）与倒易空间衍射处理等强大成像模式。然而，这些衍射图案中的信噪比（SNR）是根本限制，尤其在成像电子束敏感材料所需的低电子剂量条件下。噪声源于随机电子散射、探测器读出噪声，以及热弥散散射与电荷溢出等效应引起的杂散背景。\n本项目将开发基于深度学习的去噪框架以克服这一障碍。我们将训练卷积神经网络（CNN），区分具有物理意义的信号（Bragg 盘、弥散散射与非弹性背景）与破坏性噪声。模型将针对处理完整 4D-STEM 数据集进行优化，输出保留关键结构信息的干净衍射图案。通过从噪声数据中恢复高信噪比信号，该方法可实现高保真图像重建，解锁低剂量实验前所未有的分辨率，并将电子显微术的前沿扩展到易损电子束敏感材料的研究。\n目标：\n1. 学生将利用现有 Python 与 MATLAB 程序（MULTEM）仿真具有不同噪声水平的 4D-STEM 数据集；\n2. 开发基于 AI 的工具（如深度学习与 CNN）和/或其他拟合算法对数据去噪，理想情况能清晰计入二阶或高阶散射贡献；\n3. 通过电子叠层衍射重构，利用去噪数据集识别其对重建的影响。\n你将获得：\n1. 该项目将赋予你先进 4D-STEM 的专业知识；\n2. 你将培养仿真软件、AI 工具、算法开发与定量分析技能，为学术或行业岗位打造坚实作品集；\n3. 你有机会获得牛津大学等世界领先电子显微中心人员的共同指导；\n4. 与显微专家合作并可能集成实验数据，将提升你的研究资历，为论文工作或博士机会铺路；\n5. 项目提供在高水平期刊发表的机会。\n所需技能与背景：\n1. 扎实的物理理解，尤其波动光学、量子力学或固体中的电子散射；\n2. 熟练的数学能力，包括傅里叶变换与数值方法；\n3. 扎实的 Python 编程技能，有 AI 工具经验，最好有图像处理或仿真工具经验；\n4. 具备晶体学或电子显微术基础知识者优先；\n5. 对计算挑战的热情不可或缺。","cats":["CS","MAT","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-SWXVIP-04","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于电池寿命预测的AI","titleEn":"AI for Battery Lifespan Prediction","areaZh":"计算机科学；材料科学；机械工程","areaEn":"Computing science; material science; mechanical engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This project explores the use of artificial intelligence to predict battery lifetime from early-cycle data. Traditional testing requires months of cycling until end-of-life, but AI models can extract subtle signals from the first ~100 cycles to forecast long-term performance. The pipeline integrates engineered electrochemical features with machine learning models such as ElasticNet, Random Forest, and Gradient Boosting, evaluated under strict cross-validation and statistical guardrails. Results show reliable predictions in matched regimes, while transfer across batches remains challenging. Extensions with electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) further enhance robustness, offering a promising route for faster, more accurate battery screening.\nObjectives (part of them):\n1. Summarize key papers on battery lifetime prediction and machine learning methods.\n2. Train baseline models (linear regression, random forest etc) with clear documentation, reproduce and innovate the models.\n3. Explore multiple features cross-correlation model, including cycling data, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy data, and potentially thermal data, sonication image etc.\nRequirements:\nComputing science, material science, mechanical engineering.","descZh":"本项目探索利用人工智能从早期循环数据预测电池寿命。传统测试需要数月的循环直到寿命终止，而 AI 模型可以从最初约 100 个循环中提取细微信号，以预测长期性能。该流水线将工程化的电化学特征与 ElasticNet、随机森林（Random Forest）和梯度提升（Gradient Boosting）等机器学习模型相结合，并在严格的交叉验证和统计防护下进行评估。结果表明，在匹配的工况下预测可靠，而跨批次迁移仍然具有挑战性。通过电化学阻抗谱（EIS）的扩展进一步增强了稳健性，为更快、更准确的电池筛选提供了一条有前景的途径。\n目标（其中一部分）：\n1. 总结关于电池寿命预测和机器学习方法的关键论文。\n2. 训练基线模型（线性回归、随机森林等），并附清晰的文档，复现并改进这些模型。\n3. 探索多特征互相关模型，包括循环数据、电化学阻抗谱数据，以及可能的热数据、超声成像等。\n要求：\n计算机科学、材料科学、机械工程。","cats":["MAT","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-SWXVIP-05","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"新一代富锂氧化物正极","titleEn":"Next-generation Li-Rich Oxide Cathodes","areaZh":"材料科学；工程","areaEn":"material science; engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project Overview\nNext-generation lithium-ion batteries require cathodes with higher energy density, improved safety, and lower cost. Li-rich layered oxides (LRLOs) are a promising class of materials that meet these criteria, delivering significantly higher capacities than conventional cathodes. This enhanced capacity originates from the unique participation of lattice oxygen in the redox processes, alongside the traditional transition metal redox. However, the very mechanism that enables high capacity also leads to rapid voltage fade and capacity degradation during cycling, driven by irreversible structural and chemical evolution at the atomic scale. This project aims to bridge the gap between macroscopic performance decay and nanoscale structural origins in LRLOs. The primary focus is to develop a fundamental, atomic-level understanding of the degradation mechanisms—such as oxygen release, transition metal migration, and phase transformations—using advanced transmission electron microscopy (TEM). The insights gained will be critical for designing next-generation cathode materials with superior cycling stability.\nObjectives:\n1, Conduct a comprehensive literature review to synthesize current knowledge on the structural and chemical evolution of LRLOs and their link to electrochemical degradation.\n2, Master the fundamental principles of Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM), with a specific focus on the challenges and opportunities of applying TEM techniques (e.g., HRTEM, STEM, EELS) to air- and beam-sensitive battery materials.\n3, Propose and explore novel material engineering strategies—such as surface coating, cation doping, or novel synthesis routes—to mitigate the identified degradation pathways.\n4, Engage in collaborative experimental work with group members to synthesize proposed material variants and characterize their electrochemical performance.\nWhat You'll Gain:\n1, Specialized Knowledge: Deep expertise in state-of-the-art battery materials and advanced characterization techniques, particularly TEM.\n2, Transferable Skills: Proficiency in critical literature review, scientific programming, data analysis, and scientific communication.\n3, Industry-Relevant Experience: The project includes potential for co-supervision by industrial partners from leading battery technology companies, providing valuable industry perspective.\n4, Career Advancement: This research is designed to yield high-impact results, paving the way for publications in prestigious journals and providing an excellent foundation for a PhD or a career in the energy storage industry.\nRequired Skills and Background\n1, Background of condensed matter physics, particularly concepts of optics, electron scattering, and diffraction in TEM.\n2, Demonstrated programming skills in Python, with experience using scientific libraries. \n3, A foundational knowledge of crystallography and/or electron microscopy.\n4, Enthusiasm for tackling complex, interdisciplinary research challenges.","descZh":"（注：正文与 SWXVIP-08 基本相同，另增\"你将获得\"部分）\n项目概述：下一代锂离子电池需要能量密度更高、安全性更好、成本更低的正极材料。富锂层状氧化物（LRLO）是有望满足这些要求的有前景材料类别，其容量显著高于传统正极。这种高容量源于晶格氧与过渡金属氧化还原共同参与氧化还原过程。然而，正是这一高容量机制也导致循环过程中因原子尺度的不可逆结构与化学演化而产生快速电压衰减与容量退化。本项目旨在弥合 LRLO 宏观性能衰减与其纳米级结构起源之间的鸿沟。主要聚焦利用先进透射电子显微镜（TEM），从原子层面理解氧释放、过渡金属迁移与相变等退化机制。所获洞见对设计具有优异循环稳定性的下一代正极材料至关重要。\n目标：\n1. 开展全面文献综述，综合当前关于 LRLO 结构与化学演化及其与电化学退化关联的知识；\n2. 掌握透射电子显微镜（TEM）基本原理，特别关注将 TEM 技术（如 HRTEM、STEM、EELS）应用于对空气与电子束敏感的电池材料的挑战与机遇；\n3. 提出并探索新型材料工程策略（如表面包覆、阳离子掺杂或新型合成路线），以缓解已识别的退化路径；\n4. 与课题组成员开展协作实验，合成所提出的材料变体并表征其电化学性能。\n你将获得：\n1. 专业知识：在前沿电池材料与先进表征技术（尤其 TEM）方面获得深厚专长；\n2. 可迁移技能：熟练掌握批判性文献综述、科学编程、数据分析与科学表达；\n3. 行业相关经验：项目可能由领先电池技术公司的产业伙伴共同指导，提供宝贵行业视角；\n4. 职业发展：研究旨在产出高影响力成果，为在高水平期刊发表铺路，并为攻读博士或从事储能行业提供坚实基础。\n所需技能与背景：\n1. 具备凝聚态物理背景，尤其熟悉 TEM 中的光学、电子散射与衍射概念；\n2. 具有 Python 编程能力，熟悉科学计算库；\n3. 具备晶体学和/或电子显微术基础；\n4. 对攻克复杂跨学科研究挑战有热情。","cats":["MAT","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-SWXVIP-06","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"梯度电池材料","titleEn":"Gradient Battery Materials","areaZh":"材料科学","areaEn":"material science","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project Overview\nThis project aims to understand the design principles of gradient-element structure of Li-rich layered metal oxide cathode materials for lithium-ion batteries and develop optimal gradient structures to improve materials’ performance. Li-rich metal oxides are among the most promising next-generation cathode materials for lithium-ion batteries because they can proceed with both cationic redox and anionic oxygen redox reactions, enabling exceptionally high energy densities. For instance, Li-rich Li1.2Ni0.13Mn0.54Co0.13O2 can deliver a specific capacity beyond 250 mAh/g and a material-based specific energy beyond 1200 Wh/kg. However, these materials suffer from rapid voltage decay and capacity degradation during cycling, primarily due to structural and chemical instabilities. Gradient element distribution where transition metals such as Ni, Mn and Co vary from the particle surface to the interior bulk, has emerged as a potential strategy to mitigate the performance degradation. By tailoring the compositional gradient, it is possible to fully suppress oxygen release, reduce transition metal migration, and stabilize the layered structure during cycling. This project aims to establish a fundamental understanding of how gradient structures influence electrochemical performance and to develop optimized gradient designs for Li-rich cathodes. Through a combination of advanced characterization, computational modelling, and experimental synthesis, the project will provide actionable insights for engineering high-energy, long-life cathode materials. \nObjectives:\n1, Students will conduct an in-depth review of current strategies for improving Li-rich cathode performance, with a focus on gradient element distribution. Students will analyse how compositional gradients influence oxygen redox activity, transition metal migration, and structural stability during cycling. \n2, Students are encouraged to develop and synthesize Li-rich cathode materials with tailored gradient structures using advanced synthesis techniques and perform electrochemical testing (e.g., cyclic voltammetry, galvanostatic cycling) to evaluate performance improvements.\n3, Students are encouraged to utilize state-of-the-art techniques such as STEM, TEM, and X-ray diffraction to investigate the structural and chemical profiles of gradient-designed cathode materials at multiple length scales.\nWhat You'll Gain:\n1, This project will provide expertise in advanced battery technologies.\n2, Students will develop proficiency in literature review and lab work, transferable to both academic and industrial research.\n3, The project offers opportunities for high-impact publications in prestigious journals and provides a strong foundation for pursuing a PhD or a career in energy storage technologies. \nRequired Skills and Background\n1, Background of materials science, mechanical, design, manufacturing.\n2, Proficiency in reading and critically reviewing scientific literature.\n3, Hands-on experience or willingness to learn material synthesis techniques, electrochemical testing, and characterization methods.","descZh":"项目概述：本项目旨在理解锂离子电池富锂层状金属氧化物正极材料梯度元素结构的设计原理，并开发优化梯度结构以提升材料性能。富锂金属氧化物是最有前景的下一代锂离子电池正极材料之一，因为其可同时进行阳离子氧化还原与阴离子氧氧化还原反应，实现极高能量密度。例如，富锂 Li1.2Ni0.13Mn0.54Co0.13O2 可提供超过 250 mAh/g 的比容量与超过 1200 Wh/kg 的材料级比能量。然而，这些材料在循环中因结构与化学不稳定而出现快速电压衰减与容量退化。梯度元素分布——即 Ni、Mn、Co 等过渡金属从颗粒表面到内部体相变化——已成为缓解性能退化的一种潜在策略。通过定制成分梯度，可完全抑制氧释放、减少过渡金属迁移并在循环中稳定层状结构。本项目旨在建立梯度结构如何影响电化学性能的基础认识，并为富锂正极开发优化的梯度设计。通过先进表征、计算建模与实验合成的结合，项目将为工程化高能量、长寿命正极材料提供可操作洞见。\n目标：\n1. 学生将深入综述当前提升富锂正极性能的策略，重点关注梯度元素分布；分析成分梯度如何影响循环中的氧氧化还原活性、过渡金属迁移与结构稳定性；\n2. 鼓励学生使用先进合成技术开发并合成具有定制梯度结构的富锂正极材料，并开展电化学测试（如循环伏安、恒流循环）评估性能改进；\n3. 鼓励学生利用 STEM、TEM 与 X 射线衍射等最先进技术，在多个长度尺度研究梯度设计正极材料的结构与化学分布。\n你将获得：\n1. 项目将提供先进电池技术专长；\n2. 学生将培养文献综述与实验室工作的熟练度，可迁移至学术与工业研究；\n3. 项目提供在高水平期刊发表高影响力论文的机会，为攻读博士或从事储能技术职业奠定坚实基础。\n所需技能与背景：\n1. 具备材料科学、机械、设计、制造背景；\n2. 熟练阅读与批判性评审科研文献；\n3. 有材料合成、电化学测试与表征方法的动手经验或学习意愿。","cats":["MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-SWXVIP-07","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"超级电容器材料","titleEn":"Supercapacitors Materials","areaZh":"材料科学","areaEn":"material science","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 15:34:01","descriptionText":"Project Overview\nThis project aims to develop advanced electrode materials for supercapactors to increase the capacitance while maintaining the superior power capability. Supercapacitors are energy storage devices that bridge the gap between conventional capacitors and batteries, offering high power density, rapid charge–discharge capability, and long cycle life. Two primary types dominate the field and are full of significance in research and application: (i) Electric Double-Layer Capacitors (EDLCs) and (ii) Pseudocapacitors. These materials offer higher energy density but face challenges such as poor electrical conductivity, limited rate capability, and structural degradation during cycling.\nThis project aims to design and optimize advanced electrode architectures that combine the strengths of both systems. By integrating experimental synthesis, advanced characterization, and computational modeling, this research will establish design principles for next-generation supercapacitors with high energy and power density, long cycle life, and cost-effectiveness.\nObjectives:\n1, Students will primarily conduct an in-depth review of current strategies for developing EDLC and pseudocapacitor materials. \n2, Students are encouraged to perform lab work to develop new materials for supercapacitor applications.\n3, Students are encouraged to evaluate their electrochemical performance through lab tests.\nRequired Skills and Background\nBackground on materials, mechanical, design, or manufacturing.","descZh":"项目概述：本项目旨在开发先进的超级电容器电极材料，以提升电容的同时保持优越的功率能力。超级电容器是介于传统电容器与电池之间的储能器件，具有高功率密度、快速充放电能力与长循环寿命。该领域以两大类为主，在研究与应用中意义重大：(i) 双电层电容器（EDLC）与 (ii) 赝电容器。这些材料能量密度更高，但面临导电性差、倍率能力有限与循环中结构退化等挑战。\n本项目旨在设计并优化融合两类体系优势的先进电极结构。通过结合实验合成、先进表征与计算建模，本研究将为兼具高能量与高功率密度、长循环寿命且经济高效的下一代超级电容器建立设计原则。\n目标：\n1. 学生将主要对当前 EDLC 与赝电容材料开发策略进行深入综述；\n2. 鼓励学生开展实验室工作，开发用于超级电容器的新材料；\n3. 鼓励学生通过实验室测试评估其电化学性能。\n所需技能与背景：具备材料、机械、设计或制造背景。","cats":["MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-SWXVIP-08","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"锂离子电池富锂金属氧化物正极的原子尺度认识","titleEn":"Atomic-Scale Understanding of Li-Rich Metal Oxide Cathodes for Li-Ion Batterie","areaZh":"材料科学；工程","areaEn":"material science; engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project Overview\nNext-generation lithium-ion batteries require cathodes with higher energy density, improved safety, and lower cost. Li-rich layered oxides (LRLOs) are a promising class of materials that meet these criteria, delivering significantly higher capacities than conventional cathodes. This enhanced capacity originates from the unique participation of lattice oxygen in the redox processes, alongside the traditional transition metal redox. However, the very mechanism that enables high capacity also leads to rapid voltage fade and capacity degradation during cycling, driven by irreversible structural and chemical evolution at the atomic scale. This project aims to bridge the gap between macroscopic performance decay and nanoscale structural origins in LRLOs. The primary focus is to develop a fundamental, atomic-level understanding of the degradation mechanisms—such as oxygen release, transition metal migration, and phase transformations—using advanced transmission electron microscopy (TEM). The insights gained will be critical for designing next-generation cathode materials with superior cycling stability.\nObjectives:\n1, Conduct a comprehensive literature review to synthesize current knowledge on the structural and chemical evolution of LRLOs and their link to electrochemical degradation.\n2, Master the fundamental principles of Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM), with a specific focus on the challenges and opportunities of applying TEM techniques (e.g., HRTEM, STEM, EELS) to air- and beam-sensitive battery materials.\n3, Propose and explore novel material engineering strategies—such as surface coating, cation doping, or novel synthesis routes—to mitigate the identified degradation pathways.\n4, Engage in collaborative experimental work with group members to synthesize proposed material variants and characterize their electrochemical performance.\nTasks:\n1, Developing a foundational knowledge of TEM theory and practice.\n2, Systematically reviewing and critically analysing scientific literature on LRLO degradation mechanisms.\n3, Formulating material design strategies to enhance structural stability.\n4, Collaborating with PhD students in a wet lab environment to synthesize novel cathode materials and evaluate their electrochemical properties.\nRequired Skills and Background\n1, A solid understanding of condensed matter physics, particularly concepts of optics, electron scattering, and diffraction in TEM.\n2, Demonstrated programming skills in Python, with experience using scientific libraries (e.g., NumPy, SciPy, Matplotlib). \n3, A foundational knowledge of crystallography and/or electron microscopy .\n4, Enthusiasm for tackling complex, interdisciplinary research challenges.","descZh":"项目概述：下一代锂离子电池需要能量密度更高、安全性更好、成本更低的正极材料。富锂层状氧化物（LRLO）是有望满足这些要求的有前景材料类别，其容量显著高于传统正极。这种高容量源于晶格氧与过渡金属氧化还原共同参与氧化还原过程。然而，正是这一高容量机制也导致循环过程中因原子尺度的不可逆结构与化学演化而产生快速电压衰减与容量退化。本项目旨在弥合 LRLO 宏观性能衰减与其纳米级结构起源之间的鸿沟。主要聚焦利用先进透射电子显微镜（TEM），从原子层面理解氧释放、过渡金属迁移与相变等退化机制的基础认识。所获洞见对设计具有优异循环稳定性的下一代正极材料至关重要。\n目标：\n1. 开展全面文献综述，综合当前关于 LRLO 结构与化学演化及其与电化学退化关联的知识；\n2. 掌握透射电子显微镜（TEM）基本原理，特别关注将 TEM 技术（如 HRTEM、STEM、EELS）应用于对空气与电子束敏感的电池材料的挑战与机遇；\n3. 提出并探索新型材料工程策略（如表面包覆、阳离子掺杂或新型合成路线），以缓解已识别的退化路径；\n4. 与课题组成员开展协作实验，合成所提出的材料变体并表征其电化学性能。\n任务：\n1. 建立 TEM 理论与实践的基础知识；\n2. 系统综述并批判性分析关于 LRLO 退化机理的科研文献；\n3. 制定提升结构稳定性的材料设计策略；\n4. 在湿实验室环境中与博士生协作合成新型正极材料并评估其电化学性质。\n所需技能与背景：\n1. 扎实的凝聚态物理理解，尤其熟悉 TEM 中的光学、电子散射与衍射概念；\n2. 具有 Python 编程能力，熟悉科学计算库（如 NumPy、SciPy、Matplotlib）；\n3. 具备晶体学和/或电子显微术基础；\n4. 对攻克复杂跨学科研究挑战有热情。","cats":["MAT","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-SWXVIP-09","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"电池回收","titleEn":"Battery recycling","areaZh":"材料科学","areaEn":"material science","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project Overview\nThis collaborative dissertation project investigates the current solutions and emerging innovations in battery recycling. The rapid expansion of electric vehicles, portable electronics, and renewable energy storage systems has created unprecedented demand for batteries, while simultaneously raising urgent questions about how to responsibly manage their end-of-life stage. Battery recycling is not only a technical challenge but also a multidimensional issue involving environmental sustainability, economic feasibility, and social responsibility. Conventional recycling methods often face limitations in efficiency, cost, and safety, while new approaches — such as direct cathode regeneration, hydrometallurgical and pyrometallurgical processes, and closed-loop recycling — promise more sustainable pathways. This project will provide students with opportunities to gain a holistic understanding of how battery recycling can contribute to a circular economy and support the global transition to clean energy.\nObjectives:\n1, Students will survey current academic and industry ways of battery recycling to compare conventional methods (e.g., pyrometallurgy, hydrometallurgy) with emerging techniques (e.g., direct recycling, solid-state recovery) and identify gas or challenges that remain unresolved.\n2, Students will analyze the technical feasibility of different recycling approaches, including recovery efficiency and material purity, and evaluate environmental impacts such as energy consumption, emissions, and waste generation, as well as economic & social dimensions.\n3, Students will propose evidence-based recommendations for advancing battery recycling technologies and policies, highlighting pathways for industrial adoption and alignment with sustainability goals.\nRequired Skills and Background\nNo.","descZh":"项目概述\n这一合作学位论文项目研究电池回收的现有解决方案和新兴创新。电动汽车、便携式电子产品和可再生能源储能系统的快速扩张，创造了前所未有的电池需求，同时也就如何负责任地管理其报废阶段提出了紧迫的问题。电池回收不仅是一项技术挑战，还是一个涉及环境可持续性、经济可行性和社会责任的多维度议题。传统回收方法往往在效率、成本和安全性方面存在局限，而新方法——例如正极直接再生、湿法冶金和火法冶金工艺以及闭环回收——则有望提供更可持续的路径。本项目将为学生提供机会，全面了解电池回收如何为循环经济作出贡献并支持全球向清洁能源的转型。\n目标：\n1. 学生将调研当前学术界和工业界的电池回收方式，比较传统方法（例如火法冶金、湿法冶金）与新兴技术（例如直接回收、固态回收），并识别尚未解决的差距或挑战。\n2. 学生将分析不同回收方法的技术可行性，包括回收效率和材料纯度，并评估能源消耗、排放和废物产生等环境影响，以及经济和社会维度。\n3. 学生将提出基于证据的建议，以推进电池回收技术和政策，重点指出工业应用路径以及与可持续发展目标的一致性。\n所需技能与背景\n无。","cats":["MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-SWXVIP-10","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"高能量电池技术","titleEn":"High-energy battery technologies","areaZh":"材料科学；工程","areaEn":"material science; engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project Overview\nThis collaborative dissertation project will explore next-generation Li-ion battery technologies with a focus on dry electrode manufacturing processes. Conventional electrode fabrication relies on toxic solvents and limits the achievable mass loading of active materials, constraining the energy density of batteries used in electric transportation and drones. In contrast, dry electrode processes offer a more sustainable and cost-effective pathway by eliminating solvent use and enabling thicker electrodes with higher mass loading.\nThe project will investigate how key parameters influence electrode quality, structure, and performance. Students can engage in literature-based research or potential hands-on experimental work to deepen understanding of the relationship between manufacturing conditions and battery energy density.\nObjectives:\n1, Students will survey current research on dry electrode processes for Li-ion batteries to analyze how binder ratio, temperature, and calendering pressure affect electrode properties. Students will propose recommendations for future research directions and industrial adoption.\n2, (Optional) Students who are interested are expected to prepare electrode films using dry electrode techniques and systematically vary binder ratio, temperature, and calendering pressure to observe changes in electrode quality using characterization facilities. \n3, (Optional) Students who are interested are expected to assemble the dry electrodes into cells and analyze their electrochemical performance. \nRequired Skills and Background\nMechanical engineering, material science","descZh":"项目概述\n本合作毕业论文项目将探索下一代锂离子（Li-ion）电池技术，重点关注干法电极制造工艺。传统的电极制造依赖有毒溶剂，并限制了活性材料可达到的面载量（mass loading），从而制约了用于电动交通和无人机的电池的能量密度。相比之下，干法电极工艺通过消除溶剂的使用，并能够制造更厚、面载量更高的电极，提供了一条更具可持续性和成本效益的途径。\n本项目将研究关键参数如何影响电极的质量、结构和性能。学生可以从事基于文献的研究，或进行潜在的动手实验工作，以加深对制造条件与电池能量密度之间关系的理解。\n目标：\n1、学生将调研当前锂离子电池干法电极工艺的研究，分析粘结剂比例、温度和辊压压力如何影响电极性能。学生将针对未来的研究方向和工业应用提出建议。\n2、（可选）感兴趣的学生需要使用干法电极技术制备电极薄膜，并系统地改变粘结剂比例、温度和辊压压力，利用表征设施观察电极质量的变化。\n3、（可选）感兴趣的学生需要将干法电极组装成电池，并分析其电化学性能。\n所需技能与背景\n机械工程、材料科学","cats":["MAT","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-SWXVIP-11","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"再生制动系统综述与设计","titleEn":"Regenerative Braking System Review and Design","areaZh":"电气工程；机械工程；材料科学","areaEn":"Electrical engineering; mechanical engineering; material science","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project Overview\nRegenerative braking systems (RBS) are an essential innovation in modern transportation, designed to recover kinetic energy during deceleration and convert it into usable electrical energy. Instead of dissipating braking energy as heat, RBS channels it back into storage devices, improving overall efficiency and reducing fuel consumption. \nSupercapacitors play a critical role here: they offer rapid charge–discharge capability, high power density, and long cycle life, making them ideal for capturing short bursts of energy during braking events. In automotive applications, RBS enhances electric and hybrid vehicle range, lowers emissions, and reduces wear on mechanical brakes. In rail systems, regenerative braking contributes to significant energy savings by feeding recovered electricity back into the grid or powering auxiliary systems. Currently, cars such as Toyota hybrids and Tesla EVs, as well as metro trains worldwide, employ RBS to improve sustainability and operational efficiency. However, challenges remain in optimizing energy storage, balancing supercapacitor–battery hybrids, and integrating systems across diverse platforms. \nObjectives (part of them):\n1. Review the principles of regenerative braking.\n2. Analyse the requirements for supercapacitor integration, and propose design strategies that maximize energy recovery and reliability in both automotive and rail applications.\nRequired Skills and Background\nElectrical engineering, mechanical engineering, material science","descZh":"项目概述：再生制动系统（RBS）是现代交通的一项重要创新，旨在减速过程中回收动能并转化为可用的电能。再生制动不将制动能量以热的形式耗散，而是将其回馈到储能器件中，从而提升整体效率、降低燃料消耗。\n超级电容器在此扮演关键角色：其具备快速充放电能力、高功率密度与长循环寿命，非常适合捕获制动过程中的短时能量脉冲。在汽车应用中，RBS 可提升电动与混动汽车续航、降低排放并减少机械制动器磨损；在轨道交通中，再生制动通过将回收电能回馈电网或为辅助系统供电，实现显著的节能。目前，丰田混动、特斯拉电动车以及全球多地的地铁列车均采用 RBS 以提升可持续性与运行效率。然而，在优化储能、平衡超级电容-电池混合系统以及跨平台系统集成方面仍存在挑战。\n目标（部分）：\n1. 综述再生制动的原理；\n2. 分析超级电容器集成的需求，并提出在汽车与轨道交通应用中最大限度提高能量回收与可靠性的设计策略。\n所需技能与背景：电气工程、机械工程、材料科学。","cats":["MAT","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-SWXVIP-12","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"电子显微镜模拟夹具的3D打印","titleEn":"3D printing of dummy electron microscope holders","areaZh":"机械工程；材料科学；物理学；计算机科学","areaEn":"Mechanical engineering; material science; physics; computer science","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 15:34:01","descriptionText":"Project introduction\nTransmission Electron Microscopy (TEM) is a cornerstone technique in materials science, enabling atomic-scale imaging and analysis. However, TEM holders are specialized, costly, and often limited to specific configurations, restricting experimentation and demonstration in teaching environments. To address this, the project proposes the design and fabrication of dummy TEM holders using 3D printing technology. These holders will not be functional for imaging but will replicate the geometry, ergonomics, and handling of real TEM hardware, allowing students to explore the mechanical design and operational principles without risk of damaging expensive equipment. The project leverages departmental 3D printing facilities to provide hands-on experience in CAD design, prototyping, and additive manufacturing. \nObjectives (part of them)\n1. Explore TEM hardware: Study the geometry, functions, and constraints of standard TEM holders.\n2. 3D Design: Create CAD models replicating or modifying TEM holder designs.\n3. Additive Manufacturing: Fabricate prototypes using departmental 3D printing facilities.\n4. Testing & Demonstration: Validate usability for training and demonstration purposes.\nRequirements:\nMechanical engineering, material science, physics, computer science.","descZh":"项目简介：透射电子显微镜（TEM）是材料科学的基石技术，可实现原子尺度成像与分析。然而，TEM 样品杆专用、昂贵，且常局限于特定构型，限制了教学环境中的实验与演示。为解决这一问题，项目提出利用 3D 打印技术设计与制造模拟 TEM 样品杆（dummy holder）。这些样品杆虽不具成像功能，但将复刻真实 TEM 硬件的几何、人体工学与操作方式，使学生能够在不冒损坏昂贵设备风险的情况下，探索其机械设计与操作原理。项目利用院系 3D 打印设施，提供 CAD 设计、原型制作与增材制造方面的实操经验。\n目标（部分）：\n1. 探索 TEM 硬件：研究标准 TEM 样品杆的几何、功能与约束；\n2. 三维设计：创建复刻或改进 TEM 样品杆设计的 CAD 模型；\n3. 增材制造：利用院系 3D 打印设施制造原型；\n4. 测试与演示：验证其用于培训与演示的可用性。\n要求：机械工程、材料科学、物理、计算机科学。","cats":["CS","MAT","MECH","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZLJIANG-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"腹腔镜视频中解剖感知且时序一致的手术安全评估","titleEn":"Anatomy-Aware and Temporally Consistent Surgical Safety Assessment in Laparoscopic Videos","areaZh":"AI；机器人；深度学习","areaEn":"AI; robotics; Deep learning","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","moderator":"","capacity":6,"availCap":6,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 17:07:01","descriptionText":"Research Background\nLaparoscopic cholecystectomy is one of the most commonly performed minimally invasive surgical procedures. To reduce the risk of bile duct injury, surgeons are required to establish the Critical View of Safety (CVS) before clipping and dividing relevant anatomical structures. CVS assessment depends on three clinically defined criteria related to the exposure and separation of the hepatocystic anatomy. However, automatically determining whether these criteria have been satisfied remains highly challenging because of tissue deformation, surgical smoke, bleeding, specular reflections, camera motion, instrument occlusion, and substantial visual differences across surgeons, hospitals, and imaging systems. Contemporary surgical video analysis models commonly formulate CVS assessment as frame-level multi-label classification. Although convolutional neural networks and vision transformers can extract discriminative appearance features, they often fail to explicitly model the anatomical relationships that determine CVS. They may also produce temporally unstable predictions, where the estimated safety status changes abruptly between adjacent frames despite gradual surgical progression. In addition, CVS annotations may contain inter-observer disagreement because different experts can interpret partially exposed anatomy differently. This introduces uncertainty that is not adequately represented by conventional hard-label classification objectives.\nContent:\nThis project aims to develop an anatomy-aware and temporally consistent framework for automatic CVS assessment from laparoscopic cholecystectomy videos. The model will jointly or hierarchically exploit visual appearance, anatomical structure information, and temporal context to determine whether individual CVS criteria have been satisfied. The project will investigate anatomical segmentation as auxiliary supervision or privileged information, causal temporal architectures that only use current and previous video frames, and uncertainty-aware objectives that account for ambiguous or inconsistent expert annotations. The resulting framework should provide stable and interpretable CVS predictions while remaining sufficiently efficient for potential real-time surgical deployment.\nExpected Outcomes:\nThe student is expected to produce the following outcomes:\n1. Dataset Preparation and Benchmark Construction: The student will organize and preprocess laparoscopic video datasets associated with CVS assessment, extract temporally aligned video clips, analyze class imbalance and annotation disagreement, and construct standardized training, validation, and evaluation protocols for CVS recognition.\n2. Model Structure Design and Implementation: The student will design an anatomy-aware surgical video model incorporating a visual encoder, an anatomical representation module, and a causal temporal reasoning component. \n3. Evaluation: The student will compare the proposed approach with convolutional, transformer-based, and surgical video classification baselines; and evaluate classification accuracy, computational efficiency, and robustness under occlusion, smoke, illumination variation, and cross-center domain shifts.\nPreferred Skills:\n•Familiarity with image classification, semantic segmentation, or video understanding architectures\n• Basic knowledge of convolutional neural networks, vision transformers, and temporal modeling\n• Proficiency in PyTorch with experience in video preprocessing and data handling","descZh":"研究背景：腹腔镜胆囊切除术是最常见的微创外科手术之一。为降低胆管损伤风险，外科医生在夹闭与离断相关解剖结构前须建立\"安全关键视野\"（CVS）。CVS 评估取决于与肝胆囊解剖暴露与分离相关的三条临床标准。然而，由于组织变形、手术烟雾、出血、镜面反射、相机运动、器械遮挡，以及不同外科医生、医院与成像系统间的巨大视觉差异，自动判定这些标准是否满足仍极具挑战。当代手术视频分析模型通常将 CVS 评估表述为帧级多标签分类。尽管卷积神经网络与视觉 Transformer 可提取判别性外观特征，它们往往无法显式建模决定 CVS 的解剖关系；还可能产生时间不稳定的预测——手术进程平缓推进，但相邻帧间估计的安全状态却突变。此外，由于不同专家对部分暴露解剖的解释可能不同，CVS 标注可能包含观察者间分歧，这引入了常规硬标签分类目标无法充分表征的不确定性。\n内容：本项目旨在开发一种解剖感知且时间一致的框架，用于从腹腔镜胆囊切除术视频中自动评估 CVS。模型将联合或分层利用视觉外观、解剖结构信息与时间上下文，判定各 CVS 标准是否满足。项目将研究解剖分割作为辅助监督或特权信息、仅使用当前与先前视频帧的因果时间架构，以及考虑模糊或不一致专家标注的不确定性感知目标。所得框架应提供稳定、可解释的 CVS 预测，同时保持足够效率以支持潜在实时手术部署。\n预期成果：学生预期产出：\n1. 数据集准备与基准构建：整理并预处理与 CVS 评估相关的腹腔镜视频数据集，提取时间对齐视频片段，分析类别不平衡与标注分歧，并构建标准化的 CVS 识别训练、验证与评估协议；\n2. 模型结构设计与实现：设计一种解剖感知手术视频模型，包含视觉编码器、解剖表示模块与因果时间推理组件；\n3. 评估：将所提方法与卷积、基于 Transformer 及手术视频分类基线比较；评估分类精度、计算效率，以及在遮挡、烟雾、光照变化与跨中心域偏移下的鲁棒性。\n偏好技能：\n• 熟悉图像分类、语义分割或视频理解架构；\n• 具备卷积神经网络、视觉 Transformer 与时间建模基础知识；\n• 熟练掌握 PyTorch，有视频预处理与数据处理经验。","cats":["AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZLJIANG-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"MuscleEcho：基于学习的可穿戴超声手势识别平台","titleEn":"MuscleEcho: A Wearable Ultrasound Platform for Learning-Based Gesture Recognition","areaZh":"电子器件设计；信号处理；机器学习","areaEn":"Electronic Device Design; Signal Processing; Machine Learning","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 17:07:01","descriptionText":"Pre- and co-requisites:\nNo specific pre- or co-requisites are required.\nIntroduction significance:\nThis project aims to develop an open-source wearable ultrasound system based on WULPUS PRO for real-time hand-gesture recognition. Ultrasound measurements of forearm muscle deformation will provide a compact sensing interface for robotic control and human-machine interaction.\nContent:\nThe project will assemble and configure the WULPUS PRO platform and integrate several A-mode ultrasound transducers into a wearable forearm band. A wireless data-acquisition interface will be developed, and ultrasound signals will be collected for a set of predefined hand gestures using Meta Quest 3 as the reference. A lightweight learning model will be trained to recognize the gestures in real time. The recognized gestures will then be used to control a robotic hand or gripper.\nOutcomes:\nThe project is expected to produce the following outcomes:\n1. A functional wearable ultrasound armband based on WULPUS PRO.\n2. A labelled dataset covering several predefined hand gestures.\n3. A lightweight model for real-time gesture recognition.\n4. Experimental evaluation of recognition accuracy, latency, and repeatability.\n5. A hardware demonstration in which wearable ultrasound signals control a robotic hand or gripper.\nPreferred Skills:\n1. Programming experience in Python, C, or C++.\n2. Basic knowledge of signal processing or machine learning.\n3. Interest in ultrasound sensing, wearable devices, and robotic systems.\n4. Experience with microcontrollers, ROS, or CAD is advantageous.","descZh":"先修/并修要求：无特定先修或并修要求。\n引言与意义：本项目旨在基于 WULPUS PRO 开发一套开源可穿戴超声系统，用于实时手势识别。对前臂肌肉形变的超声测量将提供一种紧凑的传感界面，用于机器人控制与人机交互。\n内容：项目将组装并配置 WULPUS PRO 平台，将若干 A 型超声换能器集成到可穿戴前臂带中；开发无线数据采集接口，并以 Meta Quest 3 作为参照采集一组预定义手势的超声信号；训练一个轻量学习模型实时识别手势；随后将识别到的手势用于控制机械手或夹爪。\n成果：项目预期产出：\n1. 基于 WULPUS PRO 的可工作的可穿戴超声臂带；\n2. 覆盖若干预定义手势的标注数据集；\n3. 用于实时手势识别的轻量模型；\n4. 对识别精度、时延与可重复性的实验评估；\n5. 硬件演示：用可穿戴超声信号控制机械手或夹爪。\n偏好技能：\n1. 有 Python、C 或 C++ 编程经验；\n2. 具备信号处理或机器学习基础知识；\n3. 对超声传感、可穿戴设备与机器人系统有兴趣；\n4. 有微控制器、ROS 或 CAD 经验者优先。","cats":["AI","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZLJIANG-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"开源 FlexiTac 触觉皮肤复现与机器人闭环操控","titleEn":"Open-Source FlexiTac Tactile Skin Reproduction and Closed-Loop Robotic Manipulation","areaZh":"AI；机器人；信号处理；触觉感知","areaEn":"AI; robotics; Signal Processing; tactile sensing","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 17:07:01","descriptionText":"Project Overview & Research Objectives:\nThis project aims to develop a low-cost, ultra-thin, flexible tactile sensing system based on the open-source FlexiTac architecture (a flexible printed circuit, or FPC–Velostat–FPC piezoresistive sandwich structure) for robotic manipulation and feedback control. By combining the flexible piezoresistive array with an MCU-based high-speed ADC scanning unit and a ROS 2 middleware pipeline, the system offloads tactile feature extraction and contact wrench estimation to a host computer while maintaining real-time, low-latency adaptive grasping control on a robotic gripper.\nKey Project Goals:\nHardware Fabrication & Assembly: Replicate the open-source FlexiTac FPC design, integrate Velostat piezoresistive layers, and assemble custom MCU scanning hardware.\nSignal Acquisition & Processing: Develop high-frequency ADC matrix scanning firmware and a ROS 2 bridge driver for real-time tactile array streaming and baseline noise calibration.\nTactile Perception & Calibration: Construct non-linear pressure-resistance mapping curves, render real-time 2D contact pressure heatmaps, and implement slip detection.\nClosed-Loop Robotic Control: Integrate the tactile skin onto a robotic gripper to execute adaptive grasping for delicate or slippery objects based on real-time force feedback.\nSystem Data Pipeline & Architecture:\nTactile Pressure Sensing: External contact pressure deforms the Velostat piezoresistive layer sandwiched between two FPC sheets, inducing local resistance changes across the sensor matrix.\nMCU Array Scanning: An onboard microcontroller (e.g., Teensy 4.1 / STM32) with analog multiplexers scans the piezoresistive grid via ADC, converting voltage drops into raw digital pressure values.\nBridge Transceiver & Streaming: The MCU streams calibrated pressure readings over USB/Serial to the host computer, where a ROS 2 driver decodes the array and publishes tactile topics and 2D pressure heatmaps.\nClosed-Loop Execution: The high-level control node evaluates contact force and slip metrics, sending real-time velocity or torque setpoints to the robotic gripper controller to dynamically adjust clamping force.\nReferences:\nFlexiTac Official Project Page & Open-Source Hardware:https://flexitac.github.io/\nFlexiTac Code Repository & Cad Files:https://github.com/flexitac/flexitac","descZh":"项目概述与研究目标：本项目旨在基于开源的 FlexiTac 架构（一种柔性电路板，即 FPC–Velostat–FPC 压阻三明治结构）开发低成本、超薄、柔性触觉传感系统，用于机器人操作与反馈控制。将柔性压阻阵列与基于 MCU 的高速 ADC 扫描单元及 ROS 2 中间件管线相结合，系统将触觉特征提取与接触力/力矩估计卸载到上位机，同时在机器人夹爪上保持实时、低时延的自适应抓取控制。\n关键项目目标：\n- 硬件制造与装配：复刻开源 FlexiTac FPC 设计、集成 Velostat 压阻层，并组装定制的 MCU 扫描硬件；\n- 信号采集与处理：开发高频 ADC 矩阵扫描固件与 ROS 2 桥接驱动，实现实时触觉阵列数据流与基线噪声标定；\n- 触觉感知与标定：构建非线性压力-电阻映射曲线、渲染实时二维接触压力热图并实现滑移检测；\n- 闭环机器人控制：将触觉皮肤集成到机器人夹爪上，基于实时力反馈实现对精细或易滑物体的自适应抓取。\n系统数据管线与架构：\n- 触觉压力感知：外部接触压力使夹在两层 FPC 之间的 Velostat 压阻层变形，在传感器矩阵上引起局部电阻变化；\n- MCU 阵列扫描：板载微控制器（如 Teensy 4.1 / STM32）配合模拟多路复用器，通过 ADC 扫描压阻网格，将压降转换为原始数字压力值；\n- 桥接收发与数据流：MCU 通过 USB/串口将标定后的压力读数流式传输到上位机，由 ROS 2 驱动解码阵列并发布触觉话题与二维压力热图；\n- 闭环执行：高层控制节点评估接触力与滑移指标，向机器人夹爪控制器发送实时速度或扭矩设定值，动态调整夹持力。\n参考资料：\nFlexiTac 官方项目页与开源硬件：https://flexitac.github.io/\nFlexiTac 代码仓库与 CAD 文件：https://github.com/flexitac/flexitac","cats":["AI","ELE","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZLJIANG-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"模块化蛇形机器人的设计、控制与功能集成","titleEn":"Design, Control and Functional Integration of a Modular Snake Robot","areaZh":"AI；机器人；蛇形机器人","areaEn":"AI; robotics; Snake Robot","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 17:07:01","descriptionText":"Snake robots are highly articulated robotic systems capable of locomotion in confined, irregular and difficult-to-access environments. Their modular structures and flexible motion make them suitable for applications such as inspection, exploration and search tasks. \nThis project will investigate the mechanical design, locomotion control and functional integration of a modular snake robot. Up to two MSc students will work on related but independent topics: \n(1) Mechanical Design and Obstacle Traversal: reproducing and constructing a complete physical snake robot based on an existing modular design, improving its mechanical integration and extending the robot with suitable structural modules to enable traversal of small obstacles and uneven terrain;\n (2) Electronic Control and Functional Integration: developing the multi-servo electronic control system and implementing basic snake locomotion and steering, starting from a small multi-joint prototype before integration with the full robot, and further incorporating functions such as onboard video monitoring and simple sensor-based obstacle avoidance. \nBoth projects will first reproduce the corresponding mechanical or control baseline before integrating additional mature engineering solutions to extend the robot functionality. Each project will include physical implementation, system integration, functional testing and performance evaluation. For baseline, please refer to https://github.com/MECA-SNAKE/flipper-snake.\nPre-requisites / Preferred Skills: \nStudents should have a basic background in mechanical engineering, robotics, mechatronics, electronics, control, or a related discipline. Basic programming and hands-on prototyping experience is preferred. Familiarity with CAD, 3D printing, microcontrollers, Arduino, servo control, or embedded systems would be advantageous but is not required.","descZh":"蛇形机器人是高关节化机器人系统，能够在狭窄、不规则与难以到达的环境中运动。其模块化结构与柔性运动使其适用于巡检、勘探与搜救等任务。\n本项目将研究模块化蛇形机器人的机械设计、运动控制与功能集成。最多两名硕士生将开展相关但独立的研究方向：\n(1) 机械设计与越障：基于现有模块化设计复现并搭建完整的实体蛇形机器人，改进其机械集成，并添加合适的结构模块使机器人能够通过小障碍物与不平地形；\n(2) 电子控制与功能集成：开发多舵机电子控制系统并实现基础蛇形运动与转向，先从小型多关节原型入手，再与完整机器人集成，并进一步加入车载视频监控与基于传感器的简单避障等功能。\n两个项目都将先复现相应的机械或控制基线，再集成成熟工程方案以扩展机器人功能。每个项目均包含物理实现、系统集成、功能测试与性能评估。基线参考：https://github.com/MECA-SNAKE/flipper-snake。\n先修/偏好技能：学生应具备机械工程、机器人、机电一体化、电子、控制或相关学科基础背景；有基础编程与动手原型制作经验者优先；熟悉 CAD、3D 打印、微控制器、Arduino、舵机控制或嵌入式系统者优先，但非必需。","cats":["AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZLJIANG-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"绳驱连续体机器人的建模、设计、控制与感知","titleEn":"Modeling, Design, Control and Perception of Tendon-Driven Continuum Robots","areaZh":"AI；机器人；连续体机器人","areaEn":"AI; robotics; Continuum Robot","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 17:07:01","descriptionText":"Continuum robots are flexible robotic systems capable of large and continuous deformation, offering high dexterity and adaptability in confined and complex environments. Their compliant structures, however, introduce significant challenges in mechanical design, motion control, state estimation, and model calibration. \nThis project will investigate the design, control, sensing and modeling of tendon-driven continuum robots (TDCRs). Up to four MSc students will work on related but independent topics: \n(1) Mechanical Design Optimization: optimizing selected geometric and actuation parameters of the robot to improve workspace coverage and reaching performance under fixed actuation limits;\n(2) Obstacle Avoidance Control: developing a trajectory tracking controller that ensures the robot body maintains safe clearance from obstacles during motion;\n(3) Vision-Based Tip Tracking and Control: estimating the robot tip position from camera measurements and using the estimated state for closed-loop trajectory tracking;\n(4) Parameter Identification: estimating selected geometric and tendon-related parameters from motion data and evaluating how parameter calibration improves model prediction accuracy.\nEach project will include implementation, quantitative evaluation and comparison with a baseline method or model. Students will first become familiar with tendon-driven continuum robot modeling and simulation, followed by method implementation, evaluation, and dissertation preparation. For baseline, please refer to https://github.com/ContinuumRoboticsLab/opencr-mujoco.\nPre-requisites / Preferred Skills:\nStudents should have a basic background in mechanical engineering, robotics, mechatronics, dynamics, control, or a related discipline. Basic programming experience is expected. Familiarity with Python, MATLAB, mujoco, numerical optimization, robot control, computer vision, or system identification would be advantageous but is not required.","descZh":"连续体机器人（continuum robot）是能够产生大幅且连续变形的柔性机器人系统，在狭小和复杂环境中具有高度的灵巧性与适应性。然而，其柔顺结构在机械设计、运动控制、状态估计和模型标定方面带来了显著挑战。\n本项目将研究绳驱动连续体机器人（TDCR）的设计、控制、传感和建模。最多四名理学硕士（MSc）学生将各自从事相关但独立的课题：\n（1）机械设计优化：在固定的驱动限制下，优化机器人的选定几何参数和驱动参数，以改善工作空间覆盖范围和到达性能；\n（2）避障控制：开发一个轨迹跟踪控制器，确保机器人本体在运动过程中与障碍物保持安全距离；\n（3）基于视觉的末端跟踪与控制：根据相机测量估算机器人末端位置，并利用估算状态进行闭环轨迹跟踪；\n（4）参数辨识：从运动数据中估算选定的几何参数和与绳相关的参数，并评估参数标定如何提升模型预测精度。\n每个项目都将包括实现、定量评估以及与基线方法或模型的对比。学生将首先熟悉绳驱动连续体机器人的建模与仿真，随后进行方法实现、评估和论文撰写。有关基线，请参阅 https://github.com/ContinuumRoboticsLab/opencr-mujoco。\n先修要求 / 优先技能：\n学生应具备机械工程、机器人学、机电一体化、动力学、控制或相关学科的基础背景。需要具备基本的编程经验。熟悉 Python、MATLAB、mujoco、数值优化、机器人控制、计算机视觉或系统辨识将是有利的，但并非必需。","cats":["AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZLJIANG-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于 ACE 的低成本机器人遥操作平台开发","titleEn":"Development of a Low-Cost Robot Teleoperation Platform Based on ACE","areaZh":"AI；机器人；机器人学习","areaEn":"AI; robotics; robot learning","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 17:07:01","descriptionText":"Introduction Significance:\nThis project focuses on the development of a low-cost robot teleoperation platform based on ACE (A Cross-Platform Visual-Exoskeleton System for Low-Cost Dexterous Teleoperation: https://github.com/ACETeleop/ACETeleop).\nRobot teleoperation is important for real-world robot control and demonstration data collection. However, existing systems often require expensive motion-capture devices or robot-specific hardware. ACE combines a lightweight exoskeleton with vision-based hand tracking to provide a low-cost and cross-platform teleoperation solution.\nThe goal of this project is to build an ACE-based teleoperation system, integrate it with existing robotic platforms, and validate the system through real-world manipulation and basic robot learning experiments.\nContent:\n1. ACE Hardware Platform Development\nAssemble and configure the exoskeleton, cameras, sensors, controllers, and communication interfaces.\n2. Pose Tracking and Calibration\nImplement and calibrate wrist and hand pose tracking using the exoskeleton and vision system.\n3. Cross-Platform Robot Teleoperation\nMap human hand and wrist motions to different robot arms and end-effectors through workspace mapping and inverse kinematics.\n4. Data Collection\nDevelop a pipeline for recording robot observations, states, and teleoperation commands during manipulation tasks.\n5. Robot Learning Validation\nCollect demonstration data, train a baseline imitation learning policy, and deploy it on the physical robot platform for manipulation experiments.\nOutcomes\nStudents are expected to produce:\nA functional ACE-based teleoperation platform.\nA reliable hand and wrist tracking system.\nA cross-platform robot teleoperation and data collection system.\nA basic imitation learning and autonomous manipulation system.\nPreferred Skills:\n1. Strong interest in hands-on robotics and hardware development.\n2. Experience with mechanical assembly, sensors, cameras, actuators, or embedded systems is preferred.\n3. Basic Python/C++ programming skills.\n4. Experience with Linux, ROS/ROS2, robot kinematics, or control systems is a plus.\n5. Good practical problem-solving, communication, and teamwork skills.","descZh":"引言与意义：本项目聚焦于基于 ACE（一种低成本的跨平台灵巧遥操作视觉外骨骼系统：https://github.com/ACETeleop/ACETeleop）开发低成本机器人遥操作平台。\n机器人遥操作对真实世界机器人控制与示范数据采集十分重要。然而，现有系统往往需要昂贵的动作捕捉设备或机器人专用硬件。ACE 将轻量外骨骼与基于视觉的手部跟踪相结合，提供低成本、跨平台的遥操作方案。\n本项目目标是构建基于 ACE 的遥操作系统、将其与现有机器人平台集成，并通过真实世界操作与基础机器人学习实验验证系统。\n内容：\n1. ACE 硬件平台开发：组装并配置外骨骼、相机、传感器、控制器与通信接口；\n2. 姿态跟踪与标定：利用外骨骼与视觉系统实现并标定腕部与手部姿态跟踪；\n3. 跨平台机器人遥操作：通过工作空间映射与逆运动学，将人手与手腕运动映射到不同机械臂与末端执行器；\n4. 数据采集：开发在操作任务期间记录机器人观测、状态与遥操作指令的流程；\n5. 机器人学习验证：采集示范数据，训练基线模仿学习策略，并在实物机器人平台上部署开展操作实验。\n成果：学生预期产出：可工作的 ACE 遥操作平台；可靠的手腕与手部跟踪系统；跨平台机器人遥操作与数据采集系统；基础的模仿学习与自主操作系统。\n偏好技能：1. 对动手机器人实践与硬件开发有强烈兴趣；2. 有机械装配、传感器、相机、执行器或嵌入式系统经验者优先；3. 基础 Python/C++ 编程能力；4. 有 Linux、ROS/ROS2、机器人运动学或控制系统经验更佳；5. 良好的实际问题解决、沟通与团队协作能力。","cats":["AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ZLJIANG-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于 OpenCat 的低成本四足机器人平台开发","titleEn":"Development of a Low-Cost Quadruped Robotic Platform Based on OpenCat","areaZh":"AI；机器人；机器人学习","areaEn":"AI; robotics; robot learning","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-12 17:07:01","descriptionText":"Introduction Significance:\nThis project focuses on the development of a low-cost robot teleoperation platform based on ACE (A Cross-Platform Visual-Exoskeleton System for Low-Cost Dexterous Teleoperation: https://github.com/ACETeleop/ACETeleop).\nRobot teleoperation is important for real-world robot control and demonstration data collection. However, existing systems often require expensive motion-capture devices or robot-specific hardware. ACE combines a lightweight exoskeleton with vision-based hand tracking to provide a low-cost and cross-platform teleoperation solution.\nThe goal of this project is to build an ACE-based teleoperation system, integrate it with existing robotic platforms, and validate the system through real-world manipulation and basic robot learning experiments.\nContent:\n1. ACE Hardware Platform Development\nAssemble and configure the exoskeleton, cameras, sensors, controllers, and communication interfaces.\n2. Pose Tracking and Calibration\nImplement and calibrate wrist and hand pose tracking using the exoskeleton and vision system.\n3. Cross-Platform Robot Teleoperation\nMap human hand and wrist motions to different robot arms and end-effectors through workspace mapping and inverse kinematics.\n4. Data Collection\nDevelop a pipeline for recording robot observations, states, and teleoperation commands during manipulation tasks.\n5. Robot Learning Validation\nCollect demonstration data, train a baseline imitation learning policy, and deploy it on the physical robot platform for manipulation experiments.\nOutcomes\nStudents are expected to produce:\nA functional ACE-based teleoperation platform.\nA reliable hand and wrist tracking system.\nA cross-platform robot teleoperation and data collection system.\nA basic imitation learning and autonomous manipulation system.\nPreferred Skills:\n1. Strong interest in hands-on robotics and hardware development.\n2. Experience with mechanical assembly, sensors, cameras, actuators, or embedded systems is preferred.\n3. Basic Python/C++ programming skills.\n4. Experience with Linux, ROS/ROS2, robot kinematics, or control systems is a plus.\n5. Good practical problem-solving, communication, and teamwork skills.","descZh":"（与 ZLJIANG-6 文本相同的重复项目）\n引言与意义：本项目聚焦于基于 ACE（A Cross-Platform Visual-Exoskeleton System for Low-Cost Dexterous Teleoperation：https://github.com/ACETeleop/ACETeleop）开发低成本机器人遥操作平台。\n机器人遥操作对真实世界机器人控制与示范数据采集十分重要。然而，现有系统往往需要昂贵的动作捕捉设备或机器人专用硬件。ACE 将轻量外骨骼与基于视觉的手部跟踪相结合，提供低成本、跨平台的遥操作方案。\n本项目目标是构建基于 ACE 的遥操作系统、将其与现有机器人平台集成，并通过真实世界操作与基础机器人学习实验验证系统。\n内容：\n1. ACE 硬件平台开发：组装并配置外骨骼、相机、传感器、控制器与通信接口；\n2. 姿态跟踪与标定：利用外骨骼与视觉系统实现并标定腕部与手部姿态跟踪；\n3. 跨平台机器人遥操作：通过工作空间映射与逆运动学，将人手与手腕运动映射到不同机械臂与末端执行器；\n4. 数据采集：开发在操作任务期间记录机器人观测、状态与遥操作指令的流程；\n5. 机器人学习验证：采集示范数据，训练基线模仿学习策略，并在实物机器人平台上部署开展操作实验。\n成果：学生预期产出：可工作的 ACE 遥操作平台；可靠的手腕与手部跟踪系统；跨平台机器人遥操作与数据采集系统；基础的模仿学习与自主操作系统。\n偏好技能：1. 对动手机器人实践与硬件开发有强烈兴趣；2. 有机械装配、传感器、相机、执行器或嵌入式系统经验者优先；3. 基础 Python/C++ 编程能力；4. 有 Linux、ROS/ROS2、机器人运动学或控制系统经验更佳；5. 良好的实际问题解决、沟通与团队协作能力。","cats":["AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CHUYUWEI-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于城市温室气体与空气污染物检测的低成本移动平台","titleEn":"Low-Cost Mobile Platform for Urban Greenhouse Gas and Air Pollutant Sensing","areaZh":"环境传感；传感器系统；低空技术","areaEn":"Environmental Sensing; Sensor Systems; Low-Altitude Technology","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WEI Chuyu","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Urban greenhouse gas and air-pollutant concentrations can exhibit strong spatial and temporal variations that are difficult to capture using conventional stationary monitoring stations. This project aims to develop a low-cost mobile sensing platform for mapping greenhouse gases and selected air pollutants in urban environments. Depending on the student's interests, the platform may be integrated with a ground vehicle, bicycle, portable unit, or unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV).\nThe project may involve selection and characterization of low-cost sensors for species such as CO₂, CH₄, CO, NO₂, particulate matter, and volatile organic compounds; sensor calibration under controlled conditions; integration with GPS, temperature, humidity, and other environmental sensors; development of a data-acquisition system; and spatial visualization of measured pollutant distributions. Advanced topics may include compensation for environmental interference and sensor drift, sensor-fusion algorithms, machine-learning-based calibration, and identification of potential emission hotspots.\nStudents will gain hands-on experience in environmental sensing, instrumentation, sensor calibration, data acquisition, programming, spatial mapping, and mobile sensing systems.\nPre-/Co-requisites: No specific prerequisite beyond an appropriate engineering or science background. Basic knowledge of instrumentation, electronics, thermofluids, or environmental engineering would be beneficial.\nPreferred Skills: Interest in experimental work and sensing technologies. Experience with Python, MATLAB, electronics, data analysis, or GIS/data visualization is desirable but not required. Students should be willing to conduct laboratory and/or outdoor measurements.\nThe detailed scope can be adjusted according to the student's background and interests. Students are encouraged to contact Prof. Chuyu Wei (chuyuwei@hku.hk) to discuss potential directions before project selection.","descZh":"城市温室气体和空气污染物浓度可能表现出强烈的空间和时间变化，传统的固定监测站难以捕捉这些变化。本项目旨在开发一种低成本移动感知平台，用于绘制城市环境中的温室气体和选定空气污染物的分布图。根据学生的兴趣，该平台可与地面车辆、自行车、便携式设备或无人机（UAV）集成。\n项目可能涉及：针对 CO₂、CH₄、CO、NO₂、颗粒物和挥发性有机化合物等物种的低成本传感器选型与表征；在受控条件下进行传感器标定；与 GPS、温度、湿度和其他环境传感器集成；数据采集系统的开发；以及对测量到的污染物分布进行空间可视化。进阶主题可能包括对环境影响和传感器漂移的补偿、传感器融合算法、基于机器学习的标定，以及潜在排放热点区域的识别。\n学生将获得环境感知、仪器仪表、传感器标定、数据采集、编程、空间制图和移动感知系统方面的实践经验。\n先修/并修课程：除具备适当的工程或科学背景外，没有其他特定先修要求。具备仪器仪表、电子学、热流体或环境工程方面的基础知识将会有所帮助。\n偏好技能：对实验工作和传感技术感兴趣。具有 Python、MATLAB、电子学、数据分析或 GIS/数据可视化方面的经验者优先，但非必需。学生应愿意进行实验室和/或户外测量。\n详细范围可根据学生的背景和兴趣进行调整。鼓励学生在选课前联系 Chuyu Wei 教授（chuyuwei@hku.hk），讨论潜在方向。","cats":["ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CHUYUWEI-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于健康监测与疾病诊断的多组分呼气传感器开发","titleEn":"Development of a Multi-Component Breath Sensor for Health Monitoring and Disease Diagnosis","areaZh":"生物医学传感；仪器仪表；健康监测","areaEn":"Biomedical Sensing; Instrumentation; Health Monitoring","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WEI Chuyu","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Human exhaled breath contains a large number of gaseous compounds whose concentrations can provide information about physiological conditions, metabolism, and potentially disease-related processes. This project aims to investigate and develop a compact multi-component breath-sensing system for non-invasive health monitoring and breath analysis.\nDepending on the project scope, target species may include CO₂, acetone, ammonia, methane, water vapor, carbon monoxide, or other relevant breath biomarkers. The student may investigate suitable sensing technologies such as laser absorption spectroscopy, electrochemical sensors, metal-oxide sensors, or combinations of multiple sensing modalities. The project may include sensor selection, optical or electronic system design, breath-sampling design, calibration, interference analysis, signal processing, and experimental testing using controlled gas mixtures.\nAdvanced projects may explore sensor arrays and machine-learning methods to distinguish multi-component gas signatures or investigate how combinations of breath biomarkers can improve classification compared with individual species. The project will focus primarily on development and characterization of the sensing technology rather than clinical diagnosis.\nStudents will gain experience in gas sensing, spectroscopy or sensor technology, instrumentation, signal processing, experimental design, and quantitative data analysis, with exposure to emerging biomedical sensing applications.\nPre-/Co-requisites: An engineering, physical-science, biomedical-engineering, or related background is suitable. No previous biomedical experience is required. Basic knowledge of optics, electronics, chemistry, or instrumentation would be helpful.\nPreferred Skills: Interest in optical/gas sensors and experimental research. Experience with Python or MATLAB, electronics, data acquisition, spectroscopy, machine learning, or instrumentation is desirable but not essential.\nThe detailed scope can be adjusted according to the student's background and interests. Students are encouraged to contact Prof. Chuyu Wei (chuyuwei@hku.hk) to discuss potential directions before project selection.","descZh":"人体呼出气体含有大量气态化合物，其浓度可提供关于生理状况、新陈代谢以及潜在疾病相关过程的信息。本项目旨在研究并开发一种紧凑型多组分呼气传感系统，用于无创健康监测与呼气分析。\n根据项目范围，目标气体可包括 CO₂、丙酮、氨、甲烷、水蒸气、一氧化碳或其他相关呼气生物标志物。学生可研究激光吸收光谱、电化学传感器、金属氧化物传感器等合适的传感技术，或多种传感模态的组合。项目可包括传感器选型、光学或电子系统设计、呼气采样设计、标定、干扰分析、信号处理以及使用受控混合气体进行实验测试。\n高级项目可探索传感器阵列与机器学习方法，以区分多组分气体特征，或研究呼气生物标志物组合相比单种气体如何提升分类效果。项目将主要聚焦传感技术的开发与表征，而非临床诊断。\n学生将获得气体传感、光谱或传感器技术、仪器、信号处理、实验设计与定量数据分析方面的经验，并接触新兴生物医学传感应用。\n先修/并修要求：具有工程、物理科学、生物医学工程或相关背景即可；无需生物医学经验。具备光学、电子、化学或仪器基础知识会有帮助。\n偏好技能：对光学/气体传感器与实验研究有兴趣；有 Python 或 MATLAB、电子、数据采集、光谱学、机器学习或仪器经验者优先，但非必需。\n详细范围可根据学生背景与兴趣调整。鼓励学生在选课前联系魏楚宇教授（chuyuwei@hku.hk）讨论可能的研究方向。","cats":["BIO","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CHUYUWEI-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"AI 辅助的可持续燃料性质预测光谱方法","titleEn":"AI-Assisted Spectroscopic Method for Sustainable Fuel Property Prediction","areaZh":"人工智能；机器学习；光谱学；可持续燃料","areaEn":"Artificial Intelligence; Machine Learning; Spectroscopy; Sustainable Fuels","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WEI Chuyu","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"The increasing use of sustainable aviation fuels, biofuels, synthetic fuels, and alternative fuel blends creates a need for rapid methods to characterize fuel composition and important physical and chemical properties. Conventional laboratory characterization can require multiple instruments and time-consuming procedures. This project aims to develop an AI-assisted spectroscopic approach for rapid prediction of sustainable-fuel properties from measured optical spectra.\nThe student will investigate relationships between spectroscopic signatures and selected fuel properties. Depending on available samples and data, properties of interest may include fuel composition, density, viscosity, heating value, aromatic content, ignition-related characteristics, or other relevant parameters. Spectroscopic measurements may involve infrared, near-infrared, or other optical techniques.\nThe project may include spectral preprocessing, feature extraction, dimensionality reduction, and machine-learning algorithms such as principal-component regression, partial least-squares regression, support-vector machines, neural networks, or other regression approaches. Particular emphasis will be placed on model validation, generalization to previously unseen fuel samples, uncertainty analysis, and identification of physically meaningful spectral features.\nStudents will gain interdisciplinary experience in spectroscopy, sustainable fuels, artificial intelligence, scientific programming, statistical modeling, and quantitative data analysis.\nPre-/Co-requisites: Basic knowledge of thermodynamics, energy, chemistry or data analysis is helpful but not mandatory. The project can be adapted toward either experimental spectroscopy or computational/data-driven research depending on the student's background.\nPreferred Skills: Programming experience in Python or MATLAB is desirable. Previous exposure to machine learning, statistics, spectroscopy,, fuels, or data science would be advantageous but not required. Students should have an interest in combining physical measurements with AI.\nThe detailed scope can be adjusted according to the student's background and interests. Students are encouraged to contact Prof. Chuyu Wei (chuyuwei@hku.hk) to discuss potential directions before project selection.","descZh":"可持续航空燃料、生物燃料、合成燃料与替代燃料混合燃料的使用日益增多，催生了对快速表征燃料成分及重要物理化学性质方法的需求。常规实验室表征可能需要多种仪器与耗时流程。本项目旨在开发一种 AI 辅助光谱方法，从测得的光学光谱快速预测可持续燃料性质。\n学生将研究光谱特征与选定燃料性质之间的关系。根据可用样品与数据，关注的性质可包括燃料成分、密度、粘度、热值、芳香烃含量、着火相关特性或其他相关参数。光谱测量可涉及红外、近红外或其他光学技术。\n项目可包括光谱预处理、特征提取、降维，以及主成分回归、偏最小二乘回归、支持向量机、神经网络或其他回归方法等机器学习算法。重点将放在模型验证、对未见燃料样品的泛化、不确定性分析以及识别具有物理意义的光谱特征上。\n学生将获得光谱学、可持续燃料、人工智能、科学编程、统计建模与定量数据分析方面的跨学科经验。\n先修/并修要求：具备热力学、能源、化学或数据分析基础知识会有帮助，但非必需。项目可根据学生背景调整为实验光谱方向或计算/数据驱动研究方向。\n偏好技能：有 Python 或 MATLAB 编程经验者优先；接触过机器学习、统计学、光谱学、燃料或数据科学者优先，但非必需。学生应对将物理测量与 AI 结合有兴趣。\n详细范围可根据学生背景与兴趣调整。鼓励学生在选课前联系魏楚宇教授（chuyuwei@hku.hk）讨论可能的研究方向。","cats":["AI","ENE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CHUYUWEI-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"电池热失控期间气体排放与消防安全评估","titleEn":"Evaluation of Gas Emissions and Fire Safety during Battery Thermal Runaway","areaZh":"电池安全；热失控；消防安全；气体排放分析","areaEn":"Battery Safety; Thermal Runaway; Fire Safety; Gas Emission Analysis","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WEI Chuyu","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Lithium-ion batteries are widely used in electric vehicles, consumer electronics, and stationary energy-storage systems, but thermal runaway can lead to rapid heat release, flammable and toxic gas emissions, fire, and explosion hazards. This project aims to experimentally characterize gas emissions and evaluate the associated fire and safety risks during battery thermal runaway.\nThe project may involve controlled thermal-runaway experiments on selected battery cells or modules, together with measurements of temperature, pressure, heat release, and gas composition. Particular attention may be given to the evolution of major flammable, toxic, and greenhouse-gas species during different stages of thermal runaway. Depending on the available instrumentation, gas analysis may be conducted using optical spectroscopy, electrochemical sensors, infrared sensing, gas sampling, or other analytical techniques.\nThe student may further investigate how battery chemistry, state of charge, heating conditions, ventilation, or enclosure configuration influence gas-release characteristics and fire hazards. Quantitative analysis may include gas-generation rates, flammability assessment, ignition potential, thermal hazard indicators, and the development of simplified metrics for evaluating battery safety.\nAdvanced project directions may include real-time detection of early thermal-runaway signatures, correlation between gas emissions and thermal events, development of sensor-based warning strategies, or data-driven classification of battery failure stages.\nStudents will gain experience in battery safety, combustion and fire experiments, gas sensing, instrumentation, thermal measurements, data acquisition, hazard assessment, and quantitative experimental analysis.\nPreferred Skills:Interest in experimental work and safety-related research. Experience with Python or MATLAB, gas sensors, data acquisition, or battery testing would be advantageous but is not essential.\nThe detailed scope can be adjusted according to the student's background and interests. Students are encouraged to contact Prof. Chuyu Wei (chuyuwei@hku.hk) to discuss potential directions before project selection.","descZh":"锂离子电池广泛用于电动汽车、消费电子与固定储能系统，但热失控可能导致快速放热、可燃与有毒气体排放、火灾与爆炸危害。本项目旨在通过实验表征电池热失控过程中的气体排放，并评估相关的火灾与安全风险。\n项目可能涉及对选定电池单体或模组开展受控热失控实验，同时测量温度、压力、放热与气体成分。尤其关注热失控不同阶段主要可燃、有毒与温室气体组分的演化。根据可用仪器，气体分析可采用光学光谱、电化学传感器、红外传感、气体采样或其他分析技术。\n学生还可进一步研究电池化学、荷电状态、加热条件、通风或箱体构型如何影响气体释放特性与火灾危害。定量分析可包括产气速率、可燃性评估、点燃潜力、热危害指标，以及面向电池安全评估的简化指标开发。\n高级方向可包括热失控早期特征信号的实时检测、气体排放与热事件之间的关联、基于传感器的预警策略开发，或电池失效阶段的数据驱动分类。\n学生将获得电池安全、燃烧与火灾实验、气体传感、仪器、热测量、数据采集、危害评估与定量实验分析方面的经验。\n偏好技能：对实验工作与安全相关研究有兴趣；有 Python 或 MATLAB、气体传感器、数据采集或电池测试经验者优先，但非必需。\n详细范围可根据学生背景与兴趣调整。鼓励学生在选课前联系魏楚宇教授（chuyuwei@hku.hk）讨论可能的研究方向。","cats":["ENE","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CHUYUWEI-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于泄漏检测的智能气体成像与源定位系统开发","titleEn":"Development of an Intelligent Gas Imaging and Source Localization System for Leak Detection","areaZh":"人工智能；源定位；图像处理；实验设计","areaEn":"Artificial Intelligence; Source Localization; Image Processing; Experimental Design","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WEI Chuyu","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Rapid detection, visualization, and localization of gas leaks are important for industrial safety, environmental monitoring, and emergency response. Conventional point sensors can detect the presence of a gas but provide limited spatial information, while imaging and distributed sensing approaches can provide richer information about the location, extent, and dynamics of a gas plume. This project aims to develop an intelligent gas leak detection system that combines gas imaging with data-driven or physics-based source localization methods.\nDepending on the student's background and available instrumentation, the project may involve infrared absorption imaging, distributed gas sensors, or a combination of imaging and point-sensing techniques. The student may work on optical-system design, image acquisition, calibration, background correction, plume visualization, and automated leak identification.\nThe project will further investigate algorithms for estimating the location and, where feasible, the release rate of an unknown gas source from spatial gas measurements. Possible approaches include image-based plume tracking, computer vision, optimization-based inverse methods, machine learning, or sensor-fusion techniques. \nStudents will gain hands-on experience in gas sensing, optical imaging, image processing, computer vision, inverse modeling, artificial intelligence, experimental design, and quantitative data analysis. The project can be tailored toward either experimental system development, computational algorithm development, or a combination of both.\nPre-/Co-requisites:Basic engineering or physical-science knowledge is sufficient. Familiarity with optics, fluid mechanics, heat transfer, numerical methods, probability/statistics, or image processing would be beneficial but is not mandatory.\nPreferred Skills:Experience with Python or MATLAB is desirable. Background in image processing, machine learning, numerical optimization, cameras, or data acquisition would be advantageous but not required. Students interested in both experimental work and computational analysis are particularly encouraged to apply.\nThe detailed scope can be adjusted according to the student's background and interests. Students are encouraged to contact Prof. Chuyu Wei (chuyuwei@hku.hk) to discuss potential directions before project selection.","descZh":"气体泄漏的快速检测、可视化与定位对工业安全、环境监测与应急响应十分重要。传统点式传感器可检测气体是否存在，但提供的空间信息有限；而成像与分布式传感方法则可提供关于气体羽流位置、范围与动态的更丰富信息。本项目旨在开发一套智能气体泄漏检测系统，将气体成像与数据驱动或基于物理的源定位方法相结合。\n根据学生背景与可用仪器，项目可涉及红外吸收成像、分布式气体传感器，或成像与点式传感技术相结合。学生可从事光学系统设计、图像采集、标定、背景校正、羽流可视化与自动泄漏识别等工作。\n项目还将研究从空间气体测量中估计未知气源位置（可行时还包括释放速率）的算法。可能的方法包括基于图像的羽流跟踪、计算机视觉、基于优化的反演方法、机器学习或传感器融合技术。\n学生将获得气体传感、光学成像、图像处理、计算机视觉、反演建模、人工智能、实验设计与定量数据分析的动手经验。项目可按实验系统开发、计算算法开发或两者结合进行定制。\n先修/并修要求：具备基础工程或物理科学知识即可。熟悉光学、流体力学、传热、数值方法、概率/统计或图像处理会有帮助，但非必需。\n偏好技能：有 Python 或 MATLAB 经验者优先；有图像处理、机器学习、数值优化、相机或数据采集背景者优先，但非必需。特别鼓励对实验与计算分析都有兴趣的学生申请。\n详细范围可根据学生背景与兴趣调整。鼓励学生在选课前联系魏楚宇教授（chuyuwei@hku.hk）讨论可能的研究方向。","cats":["AI","DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CHUYUWEI-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"阴燃与建筑火灾的早期检测","titleEn":"Early Detection of Smoldering and Building Fire","areaZh":"消防安全；建筑","areaEn":"Fire Safety; Building","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WEI Chuyu","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Smoldering combustion can persist for long periods without a visible flame while continuously producing heat, smoke, and gaseous products. Because conventional fire alarms often respond only after smoke concentration or temperature has increased substantially, there is a strong need for earlier and more reliable detection methods. This project aims to develop and evaluate new sensing approach for the early detection of smoldering and incipient fire.\nThe project may involve controlled smoldering experiments using representative materials such as wood, polymers, textiles, insulation materials, or battery-related components. Multiple sensing techniques may be investigated, including gas sensors for species such as CO, CO₂, volatile organic compounds, and other combustion products, together with temperature, humidity, particulate, infrared, or optical measurements.\nThe student will investigate the temporal evolution of different sensing signals during the transition from normal conditions to smoldering and, where applicable, flaming combustion. Particular emphasis will be placed on identifying early-warning signatures that appear before conventional smoke or temperature alarms are triggered.\nAdvanced project directions may include sensor fusion, signal-processing methods, anomaly detection, and machine-learning algorithms for distinguishing genuine smoldering events from environmental disturbances and reducing false alarms. The project may also explore the development of quantitative warning indicators or a real-time early-warning algorithm based on combinations of gas, thermal, and particulate measurements.\nStudents will gain practical experience in combustion and fire experiments, gas sensing, thermal diagnostics, data acquisition, signal processing, sensor fusion, and artificial intelligence for safety monitoring.\nThe detailed scope can be adjusted according to the student's background and interests. Students are encouraged to contact Prof. Chuyu Wei (chuyuwei@hku.hk) to discuss potential directions before project selection.","descZh":"阴燃可在无明显火焰的情况下持续很长时间，同时不断产生热量、烟雾与气态产物。由于传统火灾报警器往往只在烟雾浓度或温度显著升高后才响应，因此迫切需要更早、更可靠的检测方法。本项目旨在开发并评估用于早期检测阴燃与初起火灾的新型传感方法。\n项目可能涉及使用木材、聚合物、纺织品、保温材料或电池相关部件等代表性材料的受控阴燃实验。可研究多种传感技术，包括针对 CO、CO₂、挥发性有机化合物及其他燃烧产物的气体传感器，并结合温度、湿度、颗粒物、红外或光学测量。\n学生将研究从正常状态过渡到阴燃（以及在适用情况下到明火燃烧）过程中不同传感信号的时间演化，重点识别在常规烟雾或温度报警触发之前出现的早期预警特征。\n高级方向可包括传感器融合、信号处理方法、异常检测与机器学习算法，用于区分真实阴燃事件与环境扰动并减少误报。项目还可探索基于气体、热与颗粒物测量组合的定量预警指标或实时早期预警算法。\n学生将获得燃烧与火灾实验、气体传感、热诊断、数据采集、信号处理、传感器融合以及面向安全监测的人工智能方面的实践经验。\n详细范围可根据学生背景与兴趣调整。鼓励学生在选课前联系魏楚宇教授（chuyuwei@hku.hk）讨论可能的研究方向。","cats":["CS","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YHAOZHOU-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于智能鞋垫的老年人姿态识别与监护","titleEn":"Smart insole-based posture recognition and monitoring for elderly care","areaZh":"可穿戴生物电子；智能鞋垫；姿态识别；老年照护；深度学习","areaEn":"Wearable bioelectronics; Smart insole; Posture recognition; Elderly Care; Deep learning","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHOU Yihao","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 13:34:02","descriptionText":"Posture, balance, and gait are important indicators of mobility and physical health in older adults. Continuous monitoring of these parameters can support fall-risk assessment, rehabilitation, and personalized elderly care. However, conventional posture-monitoring approaches often rely on cameras or laboratory-based motion-capture systems, which are not well suited for continuous monitoring in daily life. This project aims to develop a wireless smart insole that measures plantar pressure distributions and uses deep learning to estimate human posture and movement. The ultimate goal is to explore a wearable and unobtrusive approach for long-term posture and gait monitoring in elderly care. \nThe main objectives of this project are to:\n1. Develop a smart insole capable of measuring spatial plantar pressure distributions.\n2. Establish a wireless interface for transmitting sensor data to a PC or smartphone for real-time monitoring and data collection.\n3. Integrate the smart insole with a depth camera to simultaneously collect plantar pressure and whole-body posture data. \n4. Train deep-learning models to estimate human posture and movement directly from plantar pressure distributions.\n5. Explore potential applications of the system in elderly care and rehabilitation.\nThe students will first develop and integrate the smart-insole sensing hardware, including pressure sensors, data-acquisition electronics, and wireless communication. A graphical interface will also be developed for real-time visualization of plantar pressure distributions on a PC or smartphone. The smart insole will then be used together with a depth camera to establish a multimodal data-collection platform. Participants will perform different postures and activities, such as standing, leaning, walking, and sit-to-stand transitions. The depth camera will provide reference information on whole-body posture, while the smart insole simultaneously records the corresponding plantar pressure distributions. The collected data will be used to train deep-learning models that establish the relationship between foot-pressure patterns and human posture. The trained model will subsequently be evaluated for its ability to recognize or estimate posture using only the smart-insole measurements.\nA key application of the system is fall-risk assessment and prevention. Changes in balance, posture, and plantar pressure distribution may indicate instability or abnormal movement patterns, allowing potential fall risks to be identified at an early stage. Another application is post-stroke rehabilitation monitoring. Changes in gait and weight distribution during rehabilitation may provide quantitative information about functional recovery. Continuous monitoring using the smart insole could therefore help evaluate rehabilitation progress and potentially support more personalized therapy. The student will also be encouraged to identify and explore additional application scenarios in elderly care in discussion with the supervisor.\npre- and co-requisites: Electronics Design, Deep Learning, Programming, Bio-mechanical Sensor Development.\npreferred skills: Biomechanics, Deep Learning, Electronics Design.","descZh":"姿态、平衡与步态是反映老年人活动能力与身体健康的重要指标。持续监测这些参数可支持跌倒风险评估、康复训练与个性化老人照护。然而，常规姿态监测方法往往依赖相机或实验室动作捕捉系统，不适合日常生活中的持续监测。本项目旨在开发一种无线智能鞋垫，测量足底压力分布，并利用深度学习估计人体姿态与运动。最终目标是探索一种可穿戴、不引人注目的方法，用于老年照护中的长期姿态与步态监测。\n本项目主要目标：\n1. 开发能测量空间足底压力分布的智能鞋垫；\n2. 建立将传感器数据传输到 PC 或智能手机的无线接口，用于实时监测与数据采集；\n3. 将智能鞋垫与深度相机集成，同时采集足底压力与全身姿态数据；\n4. 训练深度学习模型，直接从足底压力分布估计人体姿态与运动；\n5. 探索系统在老年照护与康复中的潜在应用。\n学生将首先开发并集成智能鞋垫传感硬件，包括压力传感器、数据采集电子与无线通信；还将开发用于在 PC 或智能手机上实时可视化足底压力分布的图形界面。随后将智能鞋垫与深度相机配合使用，建立多模态数据采集平台。参与者将执行站立、倾斜、行走与从坐到站转换等不同姿态与活动。深度相机提供全身姿态参考信息，智能鞋垫同时记录相应的足底压力分布。采集的数据将用于训练深度学习模型，建立足部压力模式与人体姿态之间的关系；随后评估训练好的模型仅使用智能鞋垫测量值识别或估计姿态的能力。\n系统的一个关键应用是跌倒风险评估与预防。平衡、姿态与足底压力分布的变化可能预示不稳或异常运动模式，从而在早期阶段识别潜在跌倒风险。另一应用是卒中后康复监测。康复过程中步态与体重分布的变化可提供功能恢复的定量信息。使用智能鞋垫持续监测因此可帮助评估康复进展，并可能支持更个性化的治疗。还将鼓励学生在与导师讨论中识别并探索老年照护中的其他应用场景。\n先修/并修要求：电子设计、深度学习、编程、生物力学传感器开发。\n偏好技能：生物力学、深度学习、电子设计。","cats":["AI","BIO","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YHAOZHOU-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于机器鱼闭环控制的仿生人工侧线系统","titleEn":"A bioinspired artificial lateral-line system for closed-loop control of robotic fish","areaZh":"机器人；流体力学；传感器；深度学习","areaEn":"Robotics; Fluid mechanics; Sensors; Deep learning","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHOU Yihao","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-13 13:34:02","descriptionText":"Fish possess a specialised sensory organ known as the \"lateral-line system\", which enables them to detect surrounding water flow, pressure variations, vortices, and other hydrodynamic disturbances. This sensing capability plays an important role in navigation, obstacle avoidance, schooling, and maintaining swimming stability. Unlike visual sensing, the lateral-line system provides direct information about the local fluid environment and remains effective under dark, turbid, or visually obstructed conditions. Inspired by this biological mechanism, this project aims to develop an \"artificial lateral-line system\" for robotic fish, enabling bioinspired perception and closed-loop control in complex aquatic environments.\nThe proposed sensing system will integrate soft magnetic materials with vectorized magnetic sensors. Water-flow-induced deformation of the soft magnetic structures will produce changes in the local magnetic field, which can be detected by nearby magnetic sensors. Multiple sensing units will be distributed along the robotic fish to mimic the spatially distributed sensing architecture of a biological lateral line and provide multidimensional information about the surrounding hydrodynamic environment.\nThe project will consist of three major objectives. \n1. Design and fabricate the artificial lateral-line sensing units. The geometry, mechanical properties, magnetic configuration, and sensor arrangement will be investigated and optimized to achieve sensitive and reliable detection of flow-induced deformation.\n2. Develop a deep-learning-enabled flow perception framework using signals collected from the artificial lateral-line system. The sensors will be characterized under different flow velocities, directions, vortices, and hydrodynamic disturbances. The resulting dataset will be used to train deep learning models that learn the relationship between distributed magnetic sensing signals and the surrounding flow conditions. Depending on project progress, the model may be used to estimate flow velocity and direction, recognize different flow patterns, or reconstruct spatial characteristics of the local flow environment.\n3. Integrate the artificial lateral-line system and the trained perception model with a robotic fish to establish a closed-loop sensing-perception-control framework. Hydrodynamic information inferred by the deep learning model will serve as real-time feedback to guide the swimming direction, posture, or trajectory of the robotic fish. Potential demonstrations include flow-direction tracking, obstacle avoidance, stabilization against disturbances, and adaptive navigation in complex flow environments.\nThrough this project, the student will gain interdisciplinary experience in soft functional materials, magnetic sensing, deep learning, fluid mechanics, and robotic control, while contributing to the development of intelligent bioinspired underwater robotic systems.\npre- and co-requisites: Electronics Design, Deep Learning, Programming, Robotics\npreferred skills: Additive manufacturing, Deep learning, Programming, fluid mechanics","descZh":"鱼类拥有一种称为“侧线系统”（lateral-line system）的特化感觉器官，使它们能够探测周围的水流、压力变化、涡旋以及其他流体动力学扰动。这种感知能力在导航、避障、集群游动以及保持游动稳定性方面发挥着重要作用。与视觉感知不同，侧线系统提供关于局部流体环境的直接信息，并且在黑暗、浑浊或视线受阻的条件下仍然有效。受这一生物机制的启发，本项目旨在为机器鱼开发一套“人工侧线系统”，从而在复杂的水生环境中实现仿生感知和闭环控制。\n所提出的感知系统将把软磁性材料与矢量化的磁传感器集成在一起。水流引起的软磁结构变形会产生局部磁场的变化，这种变化可被附近的磁传感器检测到。多个感知单元将沿机器鱼分布，以模仿生物侧线在空间上分布式的感知架构，并提供关于周围流体力学环境的多维信息。\n本项目由三个主要目标组成。\n1. 设计并制造人工侧线感知单元。将研究和优化单元的几何形状、力学性能、磁学构型以及传感器布置，以实现对流动诱发变形的灵敏而可靠的检测。\n2. 利用人工侧线系统采集的信号，开发一个基于深度学习的流场感知框架。传感器将在不同的流速、流向、涡旋和流体动力学扰动下进行标定。所得数据集将用于训练深度学习模型，学习分布式磁传感信号与周围流动条件之间的关系。根据项目进展，该模型可用于估计流速和流向、识别不同的流型，或重建局部流场的空间特征。\n3. 将人工侧线系统和训练好的感知模型与机器鱼集成，建立一个闭环的“感知–认知–控制”框架。深度学习模型推断出的流体力学信息将作为实时反馈，用于引导机器鱼的游动方向、姿态或轨迹。潜在的演示包括流向追踪、避障、抗扰动稳定，以及复杂流场中的自适应导航。\n通过本项目，学生将获得软功能材料、磁传感、深度学习、流体力学和机器人控制方面的跨学科经验，同时为智能仿生水下机器人系统的发展作出贡献。\n先修/并修课程：电子设计、深度学习、编程、机器人学\n偏好技能：增材制造、深度学习、编程、流体力学","cats":["AI","CS","ELE","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YCARRY-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"基于 AI 的新一代网络平台","titleEn":"New Generation AI based Web platform","areaZh":"AI；智能服务","areaEn":"AI; smart services","companyZh":"Y Carry Limited","companyEn":"Y Carry Limited","companySupervisor":"Jimmy Chan","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-22 14:50:51","descriptionText":"An AI-driven web application for smart locker services streamlines personal storage through intelligent automation, real-time tracking, and modular accessibility. By integrating machine learning with low-power IoT infrastructure, the platform offers predictive locker allocation, automated dynamic pricing based on local demand, and computer vision models that estimate luggage dimensions to recommend the precise compartment size required. Users can seamlessly discover nearby sites, execute contactless unlocking via NFC or local payment options like Octopus, and delegate access permissions through secure, encrypted digital keys.","descZh":"一款面向智能储物柜服务的 AI 驱动 Web 应用，通过智能自动化、实时跟踪与模块化可及性简化个人储物体验。平台将机器学习与低功耗物联网基础设施相结合，提供预测式储物柜分配、基于本地需求的自动动态定价，以及用于估算行李尺寸、推荐精确箱格大小的计算机视觉模型。用户可无缝发现附近站点，通过 NFC 或八达通等本地支付方式实现无接触开锁，并通过安全加密的数字钥匙委派访问权限。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YCARRY-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"新型机器人吸尘器","titleEn":"New Robot Vacuum Clear","areaZh":"机器人","areaEn":"Robotics","companyZh":"Y Carry Limited","companyEn":"Y Carry Limited","companySupervisor":"Jimmy Chan","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-22 14:51:10","descriptionText":"This project introduces an advanced robot vacuum designed to navigate complex spaces and clean floor surfaces without being obstructed by table legs or tight chair clusters. Powered by a dynamic multi-sensor array combining solid-state LiDAR, 3D time-of-flight cameras, and ultrasonic sensors, the vacuum generates high-resolution, real-time spatial maps of every environment. Advanced spatial-AI algorithms enable the machine to identify furniture leg configurations instantly, calculating optimized cleaning paths around high-density obstacle fields. Instead of bumping into table bases or getting trapped beneath dining sets, the robot executes fluid, sub-centimeter maneuvers that maximize coverage while eliminating physical interference and stuck-vehicle errors.\nMechanic Engineering Student, Electronic Engineering Student required.","descZh":"本项目推出一款先进的扫地机器人，旨在复杂空间中导航并清洁地面，而不会被桌腿或密集的椅子组所阻挡。机器人配备由固态 LiDAR、3D 飞行时间相机与超声波传感器构成的动态多传感器阵列，可为每个环境生成高分辨率、实时的空间地图。先进的空间 AI 算法使机器能够即时识别家具腿部构型，在障碍物高密度区域周围计算优化的清洁路径。机器不会撞击桌座或被卡在餐桌之下，而是执行流畅的亚厘米级机动，在最大限度覆盖清扫区域的同时消除物理碰撞与卡困故障。\n本项目面向机械工程专业学生与电子工程专业学生。","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YCARRY-3","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"智能储物柜服务的设计、营销与品牌建设","titleEn":"Design, marketing and branding for Smart Lockers Services","areaZh":"产品营销","areaEn":"Product marketing","companyZh":"Y Carry Limited","companyEn":"Y Carry Limited","companySupervisor":"Jimmy Chan","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-22 14:51:21","descriptionText":"project team for SEA & Japan Expansion to drive market entry and growth for its HKSTP-backed smart locker platform. In this role, you will adapt and execute targeted go-to-market strategies that communicate the value of Y Carry’s modular, low-power, and NFC-enabled locker solutions to enterprise partners—such as transit hubs, shopping centers, and venues—and end-users across Southeast Asia and Japan. You will lead market research, localized positioning, partner marketing, and campaign execution to establish Y Carry as the premier smart locker and urban mobility partner in these key regional markets.","descZh":"加入 SEA（东南亚）与日本拓展项目团队，为获得香港科技园（HKSTP）支持的智能储物柜平台驱动市场进入与增长。在此角色中，你将调整并执行针对性的进入市场策略，向企业合作伙伴（如交通枢纽、购物中心与场馆）以及东南亚和日本市场的终端用户传达 Y Carry 模块化、低功耗、支持 NFC 的储物柜解决方案的价值。你将主导市场调研、本地化定位、伙伴营销与活动执行，使 Y Carry 成为这些重点区域市场中领先的智能储物柜与城市出行合作伙伴。","cats":["MGT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PET-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI 驱动的宠物鼻纹身份识别","titleEn":"AI-Powered Pet Nose Identity Recognition","areaZh":"鼻纹识别；生物特征验证；多模态集成","areaEn":"Nose Pattern Recognition; Biometric Verification; Multi-modal Integration","companyZh":"Pet Pet Club Limited","companyEn":"Pet Pet Club Limited","companySupervisor":"Kev Tsieng","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-20 13:34:03","descriptionText":"Every pet has a unique nose pattern, similar to a human fingerprint. This makes nose-based identification a promising non-invasive method for recognizing individual dogs and cats. Compared with collars, tags, or microchips, nose recognition can provide a contactless and permanent biometric solution that works naturally during daily pet interactions. It can also complement facial identification by offering an additional reliable biometric feature.\nProblem Statement \nPet owners need a contactless and reliable way to identify individual pets using nose patterns.\nDetailed Problem Description \nTraditional pet identification methods such as collars, tags, and microchips may be lost, removed, or inconvenient to use in everyday situations. Nose patterns, however, are unique to each pet and can potentially be captured through close-range images for accurate recognition. This project aims to develop a deep learning model that can detect and recognize individual pets based on nose texture and structure. The system should be robust enough to handle different angles, lighting conditions, fur colors, and partial occlusions, while providing a practical non-invasive identification method for real-world pet care applications.\nExpected outcomes\nA deep learning model for identifying pets using nose patterns.\nA non-invasive and contactless biometric recognition method.\nRobust performance under different angles, lighting, and image quality conditions.\nA complementary identification method that can work with facial recognition.\nA practical prototype for pet security, tracking, or access control applications.","descZh":"每只宠物都有独特的鼻纹，类似人类的指纹。这使得基于鼻纹的识别成为一种有前景的无创方法，可用于识别个体犬猫。与项圈、标牌或微芯片相比，鼻纹识别可提供非接触、永久的生物识别方案，在宠物的日常互动中自然工作；它还可作为面部识别的补充，提供额外可靠的生物特征。\n问题陈述：宠物主人需要一种无接触、可靠的方式，利用鼻纹识别个体宠物。\n详细问题描述：项圈、标牌与微芯片等传统宠物识别方式在日常使用中可能丢失、被摘除或不便使用。而鼻纹对每只宠物独一无二，可通过近距离图像捕捉实现准确识别。本项目旨在开发一个深度学习模型，基于鼻子纹理与结构检测并识别个体宠物。系统应足够稳健，能应对不同角度、光照条件、毛色与部分遮挡，同时为真实宠物护理应用提供实用的无创识别方法。\n预期成果：用于鼻纹识别宠物的深度学习模型；无创、非接触的生物识别方法；在不同角度、光照与图像质量条件下保持稳健性能；可与面部识别协同的补充识别方法；可用于宠物安防、跟踪或门禁控制应用的实用原型。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PET-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI 驱动的物联网智能宠物项圈监测","titleEn":"AI-Powered IoT Smart Pet Collar Monitoring","areaZh":"AI 行为识别；GPS 追踪；轻量硬件；基于物联网的宠物监测设备","areaEn":"AI behavior recognition; GPS tracking; lightweight hardware; IoT-based pet monitoring device","companyZh":"Pet Pet Club Limited","companyEn":"Pet Pet Club Limited","companySupervisor":"Kev Tsieng","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-20 13:34:03","descriptionText":"Project background\nPet owners often lack continuous and reliable data about their pets’ body condition, movement, and whereabouts, especially when pets are outdoors or unattended. The attached reference image presents a smart collar concept with GPS tracking, AI behavior recognition, lightweight hardware, waterproof protection, and safety-focused wearable design, which aligns well with an IoT-based pet monitoring device.Problem Statement \nPet owners lack a simple, affordable device to continuously monitor their pet's temperature, activity, and location in one place.\nDetailed Problem Description\nMost pet care products handle health, movement, and location separately, making full monitoring difficult and inconvenient. This leaves three common problems for pet owners:\nHealth gaps: Illness signs like fever or lethargy often go unnoticed between vet visits. Early detection through continuous monitoring can flag issues days before symptoms appear\nSafety risks: Without real-time location, owners cannot act quickly if a pet strays into a dangerous area\nLimited vet data: Vets only see a pet once in a while. Continuous data sharing helps vets give better, more personalised care\nThis project will design a lightweight IoT collar tag that records temperature, activity, and location continuously, and sends the data to a mobile app and PHealth OS for tracking and alerts.\nExpected outcomes\nA wearable IoT collar prototype recording temperature, activity, and real-time location — tested at Pet Pet Club's AI vet clinic\nSensors including temperature, motion (IMU/accelerometer), and real-time tracking modules\nData sent to a mobile app and PHealth OS, viewable by owners and vets\nAlerts for abnormal temperature, sudden inactivity, and geofence boundary breaches\nLightweight hardware (below 25g), IP67 waterproof, safe for daily pet use\nOn-device activity model identifying rest, walk, run, and distress states to support health monitoring through PHealth OS","descZh":"项目背景：宠物主人往往缺乏关于宠物身体状况、活动与行踪的持续可靠数据，尤其在宠物外出或无人看管时。附图展示了一款智能项圈概念：具备 GPS 跟踪、AI 行为识别、轻量化硬件、防水保护与面向安全的可穿戴设计，与基于物联网的宠物监测设备高度契合。\n问题陈述：宠物主人缺少一款简单、实惠的设备，可在同一处持续监测宠物的体温、活动与位置。\n详细问题描述：大多数宠物护理产品分别处理健康、活动与位置，使全面监测困难不便。这给宠物主人带来三个常见问题：\n健康缺口：发烧或嗜睡等疾病迹象在两次就医之间常被忽略；通过持续监测实现早期发现，可在症状出现前几天发出提示。\n安全风险：没有实时位置，宠物误入危险区域时主人无法快速行动。\n兽医数据有限：兽医只能偶尔见到宠物；持续数据共享有助于兽医提供更好、更个性化的护理。\n本项目将设计一款轻量物联网项圈标签，持续记录体温、活动与位置，并将数据发送到移动应用与 PHealth OS 进行跟踪与告警。\n预期成果：记录体温、活动与实时位置的可穿戴物联网项圈原型——在 Pet Pet Club 的 AI 兽医诊所测试；包括体温、运动（IMU/加速度计）与实时跟踪模块的传感器；数据发送至移动应用与 PHealth OS，主人与兽医均可查看；对异常体温、突然静止与电子围栏越界发出告警；轻量硬件（低于 25g）、IP67 防水，适合宠物日常佩戴；片上活动模型可识别休息、行走、奔跑与痛苦状态，通过 PHealth OS 支持健康监测。","cats":["AI","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PET-3","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI 驱动的多角度宠物面部识别","titleEn":"AI-Powered Pet Facial Identification for Multi-Angle Recognition","areaZh":"多角度宠物面部识别；姿态不变生物特征识别；真实环境部署","areaEn":"Multi-Angle Pet Face Recognition; Pose-Invariant Biometric Identification; Real-World Deployment A","companyZh":"Pet Pet Club Limited","companyEn":"Pet Pet Club Limited","companySupervisor":"Kev Tsieng","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-20 13:34:03","descriptionText":"Project background\nPet identification is important for security, tracking, and personalized pet care, but traditional methods such as collars, tags, and microchips can be lost, removed, or require invasive procedures. Recent pet recognition work shows that dogs and cats have distinctive facial features that can be used for biometric identification, and modern systems are moving toward face-based recognition rather than only tags or implants.\nProblem Statement\nPet owners need a non-invasive way to identify pets accurately from different face angles.\nDetailed Problem Description \nThis project aims to develop an AI model that can recognize individual dogs and cats using facial features such as eye placement, nose shape, ear structure, and fur patterns, even when the face is captured from different angles, lighting conditions, or partial occlusions. Existing research shows that animal face recognition can work well with landmark detection, alignment, and deep learning, but generalization across breeds and poses remains a challenge. The system will focus on robust multi-angle identification so pets can be recognized in real-world settings such as homes, shelters, feeders, or monitoring cameras.\nExpected outcomes\nA deep learning model that can identify individual pets from facial images.\nImproved recognition robustness across different face angles, poses, and lighting conditions.\nNon-invasive biometric identification for dogs and cats.\nA pet recognition system that can support applications such as feeding access control, pet tracking, and lost-pet recovery.\nA practical prototype or demo that shows multi-angle face identification in real-world","descZh":"项目背景：宠物识别对安防、跟踪与个性化宠物护理十分重要，但项圈、标牌与微芯片等传统方法可能丢失、被摘除或需要侵入性操作。近期宠物识别研究表明，犬猫具有可用于生物识别的独特面部特征，现代系统正从仅依赖标签或植入物转向基于面部的识别。\n问题陈述：宠物主人需要一种无创方法，从不同面部角度准确识别宠物。\n详细问题描述：本项目旨在开发一个 AI 模型，利用眼距、鼻形、耳结构与毛色模式等面部特征识别个体犬猫，即使面部以不同角度、光照条件或部分遮挡拍摄也能识别。现有研究表明，借助关键点检测、对齐与深度学习，动物面部识别可取得良好效果，但跨品种与姿态的泛化仍是挑战。系统将聚焦稳健的多角度识别，使宠物能在家庭、收容所、喂食器或监控摄像头等真实场景中被识别。\n预期成果：可从面部图像识别个体宠物的深度学习模型；跨不同面部角度、姿态与光照条件的识别鲁棒性提升；面向犬猫的无创生物识别；可支持喂食门禁、宠物跟踪与寻回走失宠物等应用的宠物识别系统；在真实场景中演示多角度面部识别的实用原型或演示。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PET-4","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI 驱动的多模态宠物情绪识别与健康分析","titleEn":"AI-Powered Multimodal Pet Emotion Recognition and Well-being Analysis","areaZh":"视觉情绪识别；音视频多模态融合；时序情绪趋势分析","areaEn":"Visual Emotion Recognition; Audio-Visual Multimodal Fusion; Temporal Emotion Trend Analysis","companyZh":"Pet Pet Club Limited","companyEn":"Pet Pet Club Limited","companySupervisor":"Kev Tsieng","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-20 13:34:03","descriptionText":"Project background\nPet owners often find it difficult to understand their pets’ emotional states accurately. This can lead to missed signs of stress, fear, pain, happiness, or discomfort, and may affect the pet’s quality of life. This project aims to use AI to analyze pet emotions from video, and optionally sound, to give owners clearer insights into their pets’ psychological well-being.\nProblem Statement \nPet owners lack objective tools to accurately detect and interpret their pets’ emotions.\nDetailed Problem Description\nCurrent pet emotion assessment is mostly subjective and depends on owner observation. This makes it difficult to detect subtle emotional cues such as anxiety, fear, pain, excitement, or contentment. The project will develop a deep learning model that analyzes video footage for facial expressions, body posture, tail movement, ear position, and overall motion patterns. If audio is available, it will also use vocal cues such as barking, meowing, purring, growling, or whining to improve emotion recognition. The system will provide real-time or near real-time emotional analysis and long-term emotional trend insights in a clear, actionable format for pet owners.\nExpected outcomes\nA deep learning model that can classify pet emotional states from video and sound.\nReal-time or near real-time emotion monitoring.\nImproved accuracy through multimodal fusion of visual and audio cues.\nLong-term emotional trend analysis for each pet.\nClear visual outputs that help owners understand emotional changes over time.","descZh":"项目背景：宠物主人往往难以准确理解宠物的情绪状态，这可能导致错过压力、恐惧、疼痛、快乐或不适的迹象，并可能影响宠物的生活质量。本项目旨在利用 AI 从视频（可选加声音）中分析宠物情绪，让主人更清晰地洞察宠物的心理健康。\n问题陈述：宠物主人缺乏客观工具来准确检测与解读宠物情绪。\n详细问题描述：目前宠物情绪评估大多主观，依赖主人观察，难以察觉焦虑、恐惧、疼痛、兴奋或满足等细微情绪线索。项目将开发一个深度学习模型，分析视频中的面部表情、身体姿态、尾巴动作、耳朵位置与整体运动模式；如可获得音频，还将利用吠叫、喵叫、呼噜、低吼或哀鸣等声音线索提升情绪识别。系统将以清晰、可操作的形式，为主人提供实时或近实时情绪分析及长期情绪趋势洞察。\n预期成果：一个可从视频与声音分类宠物情绪状态的深度学习模型；实时或近实时情绪监测；通过视觉与音频线索的多模态融合提升准确率；为每只宠物提供长期情绪趋势分析；清晰的可视化输出，帮助主人理解宠物随时间的情绪变化。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PET-5","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI 驱动的多模态宠物异常行为检测与预警系统","titleEn":"AI-Powered Multimodal Pet Abnormal Behavior Detection and Early Warning System","areaZh":"个体基线学习；多模态异常检测；可解释预警生成","areaEn":"Individual Baseline Learning; Multimodal Anomaly Detection; Explainable Alert Generation","companyZh":"Pet Pet Club Limited","companyEn":"Pet Pet Club Limited","companySupervisor":"Kev Tsieng","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-20 13:34:03","descriptionText":"Project background\nPet owners often fail to notice subtle or sudden behavioral changes that may signal stress, discomfort, illness, or emotional distress. While basic activity monitoring can track routines, it may not fully capture abnormal patterns such as unusual vocalization, restless movement, prolonged inactivity, or disrupted sleep. Research and monitoring systems increasingly use multimodal signals such as behavior, movement, and audio to identify non-routine or anomalous states, and automated vocalization analysis has shown promise for detecting meaningful behavioral changes in animals\nProblem Statement \nPet owners often miss abnormal behavioral changes that may indicate early health or stress issues.\nDetailed Problem Description \nMany pet monitoring systems focus only on routine activity recognition and do not effectively detect abnormal behavior relative to each pet’s personal baseline. As a result, subtle warning signs such as excessive vocalization, unusual inactivity, altered eating or drinking frequency, sleep disruption, or repetitive restlessness may go unnoticed. This project aims to develop a multimodal AI system that combines video, sound, and historical behavioral data to learn each pet’s normal routine, detect deviations, classify abnormal patterns, and generate timely alerts with clear explanations for pet owners.\nExpected outcomes\nA personalized AI model that learns each pet’s normal behavioral baseline.\nReal-time abnormal behavior detection using both video and sound inputs.\nDetection of anomalies such as unusual vocalization, prolonged inactivity, restlessness, altered eating or drinking, and sleep disturbances.\nContextual alerts that explain the detected abnormal pattern in simple language.\nA user-friendly interface showing abnormal events, trend shifts, and early-warning insights.","descZh":"项目背景：宠物主人往往未能注意到可能预示压力、不适、疾病或情绪困扰的细微或突发行为变化。虽然基本活动监测可跟踪日常规律，但可能无法完整捕捉异常模式，如异常发声、躁动、长时间不动或睡眠紊乱。研究与监测系统越来越多地利用行为、运动与音频等多模态信号识别非常规或异常状态，自动发声分析已显示出检测动物有意义行为变化的潜力。\n问题陈述：宠物主人常常错过可能预示早期健康或压力问题的异常行为变化。\n详细问题描述：许多宠物监测系统只关注日常活动识别，无法有效检测相对每只宠物个人基线的异常行为。因此，过度发声、异常不活动、进食或饮水频率改变、睡眠紊乱或反复躁动等细微警示信号可能被忽略。本项目旨在开发一个多模态 AI 系统，结合视频、声音与历史行为数据，学习每只宠物的正常作息、检测偏离、对异常模式分类，并以清晰解释为宠物主人及时生成告警。\n预期成果：学习每只宠物正常行为基线的个性化 AI 模型；同时利用视频与声音输入的实时异常行为检测；检测过度发声、长时间不活动、躁动、进食/饮水改变与睡眠紊乱等异常；以通俗语言解释所检测异常模式的上下文告警；显示异常事件、趋势偏移与早期预警洞察的友好界面。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PET-6","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI 驱动的多模态宠物日常活动监测与健康洞察","titleEn":"AI-Powered Multimodal Pet Daily Activity Monitoring and Wellness Insights","areaZh":"细粒度活动分类；个性化基线建模；健康洞察生成","areaEn":"Fine-Grained Activity Classification; Personalized Baseline Modeling; Wellness Insight Generation","companyZh":"Pet Pet Club Limited","companyEn":"Pet Pet Club Limited","companySupervisor":"Kev Tsieng","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-20 13:34:03","descriptionText":"Project background\nPet owners often struggle to understand their pets’ daily routines in detail, especially eating, drinking, resting, activity, excretion, and grooming behaviors. Current monitoring methods are limited and often miss subtle changes that may indicate health or behavioral issues. This project aims to use AI to combine video and sound analysis for more accurate, continuous, and personalized pet activity tracking.\nProblem Statement \nExisting pet monitoring systems cannot accurately and comprehensively track daily activities and behavioral changes.\nDetailed Problem Description \nCurrent pet monitoring solutions are often camera-only and lack fine-grained analysis of key daily behaviors such as eating, drinking, resting, grooming, and excretion. They also struggle to identify subtle changes over time or provide personalized baseline insights for each pet. This limits owners’ ability to detect early signs of illness, discomfort, or behavioral abnormalities. The project will develop an AI system that integrates visual and audio data to detect pet activities more accurately, generate long-term behavioral patterns, and present meaningful wellness insights in a clear and user-friendly format.\nExpected outcomes\nA multimodal AI model that can classify pet daily activities with higher accuracy.\nReal-time detection of eating, drinking, resting, active behavior, excretion, and grooming.\nPersonalized behavior baselines for each pet.\nLong-term activity summaries and anomaly insights.\nA visual dashboard or interface that makes pet behavior easy for owners to understand.","descZh":"项目背景：宠物主人往往难以细致了解宠物的日常作息，尤其是进食、饮水、休息、活动、排泄与理毛行为。现有监测方法有限，常常遗漏可能预示健康或行为问题的细微变化。本项目旨在利用 AI 结合视频与声音分析，实现更准确、持续且个性化的宠物活动跟踪。\n问题陈述：现有宠物监测系统无法准确、全面地跟踪日常活动与行为变化。\n详细问题描述：当前宠物监测方案往往仅依赖摄像头，缺乏对进食、饮水、休息、理毛、排泄等关键日常行为的细粒度分析；也难以识别随时间发生的细微变化或为每只宠物提供个性化基线洞察。这限制了主人发现疾病、不适或行为异常早期迹象的能力。项目将开发一个集成视觉与音频数据的 AI 系统，更准确地检测宠物活动、生成长期行为模式，并以清晰、易用的形式呈现有意义的健康洞察。\n预期成果：可更高精度分类宠物日常活动的多模态 AI 模型；实时检测进食、饮水、休息、活动、排泄与理毛行为；每只宠物的个性化行为基线；长期活动摘要与异常洞察；让主人易于理解宠物行为的可视化仪表板/界面。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ESSAA-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"跨项目执行管理","titleEn":"Cross-Project Executive","areaZh":"人机交互；系统工程与产品生命周期管理；软件质量","areaEn":"Human-Computer Interaction; Systems Engineering & Product Lifecycle Management; Software Quality","companyZh":"ESSAA Limited","companyEn":"ESSAA Limited","companySupervisor":"Alex Tong","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-20 18:04:02","descriptionText":"Creato is an AI SaaS and educational technology developer. This dissertation project offers 3 intern positions for Cross-Project Executives to drive product development, system testing, user experience (UX) journey engineering, and market execution across Creato's core software solutions. The scope covers two active commercial platforms—\"Seda\" (a student mental health monitoring platform) and \"2tag.ai\" (an AI marketing tool)—alongside two specialized SaaS products tailored for primary and secondary school aviation education programs.\n Scope of Work & Key Responsibilities \n Product Testing & Quality Assurance: Plan and execute functional testing, cross-platform performance checks, edge-case analysis, and bug tracking across Seda, 2tag.ai, and the aviation education platforms.\n User Journey & Workflow Optimization: Map end-user interactions, evaluate user interface (UI/UX) efficiency, and design improved system navigation tailored to students, teachers, and business users.\n Cross-Project Execution & Launch Support: Assist in feature iteration, system execution, user feedback synthesis, and promotional rollout strategies for all four SaaS platforms.\n Aviation EdTech System Integration: Conduct hands-on testing and user workflow development for interactive aviation simulation tools designed for primary and secondary school curricula.\n Preferred Skills \n Academic background in Mechanical Engineering, Industrial Engineering, Systems Engineering, Computer Science, or related technical disciplines.\n Familiarity or strong interest in software quality assurance (QA), product management, or human-computer interaction (HCI).\n Analytical thinking, structured problem-solving skills, and attention to user workflow detail.\n Effective communication in English and Cantonese (Mandarin is a plus) for cross-functional teamwork.\n Expected Outcomes \n Complete test suites, bug tracking records, and system reliability reports for all target SaaS platforms.\n Optimized user journey maps and system workflow specifications for the aviation education software.\n Actionable user feedback analysis to guide upcoming feature releases for Seda and 2tag.ai.\n Onsite Frequency \n 2 to 3 days per week onsite at Creato office (flexible hybrid schedule available to accommodate university lecture timetables)","descZh":"Creato 是一家 AI SaaS 与教育科技开发商。本论文项目提供 3 个\"跨项目执行\"实习岗位，负责推动 Creato 核心软件产品的产品开发、系统测试、用户体验（UX）旅程工程与市场执行。范围涵盖两个在运营的商业平台——\"Seda\"（学生心理健康监测平台）与\"2tag.ai\"（AI 营销工具）——以及两个面向中小学航空教育项目定制的专门 SaaS 产品。\n工作范围与主要职责：\n- 产品测试与质量保障：在 Seda、2tag.ai 与航空教育平台上规划并执行功能测试、跨平台性能检查、边界案例分析、缺陷跟踪；\n- 用户旅程与工作流优化：梳理终端用户交互、评估用户界面（UI/UX）效率，为面向学生、教师与商业用户的系统导航设计改进；\n- 跨项目执行与发布支持：协助四个 SaaS 平台的功能迭代、系统执行、用户反馈综合与推广上线策略；\n- 航空教育科技系统集成：面向中小学课程设计的交互式航空模拟工具，开展实践测试与用户工作流开发。\n偏好技能：\n- 机械工程、工业工程、系统工程、计算机科学或相关技术学科背景；\n- 熟悉或对软件质量保障（QA）、产品管理或人机交互（HCI）有浓厚兴趣；\n- 分析思维、结构化问题解决能力，注重用户工作流细节；\n- 具备英语与粤语的有效沟通能力（普通话为加分项），支持跨职能团队协作。\n预期成果：\n- 完成所有目标 SaaS 平台的测试套件、缺陷跟踪记录与系统可靠性报告；\n- 为航空教育软件提供优化的用户旅程地图与系统工作流规范；\n- 提供可操作的用户反馈分析，指导 Seda 与 2tag.ai 即将推出的功能。\n现场频率：每周 2~3 天在 Creato 办公室现场办公（可根据大学上课时间表灵活安排混合办公）。","cats":["CS","MGT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-INERI-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"带动态 AI 成本优化的可解释 LLM 生成量化交易系统","titleEn":"Interpreted LLM-Generated Quantitative Trading System with Dynamic AI Cost Optimization","areaZh":"人工智能；量化交易；软件工程；成本优化","areaEn":"Artificial Intelligence; Quantitative Trading; Software Engineering; Cost Optimization","companyZh":"Hong Kong International New Economic Research Institute","companyEn":"Hong Kong International New Economic Research Institute","companySupervisor":"FU RAO Tony","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Objective:\nDevelop and evaluate an interpreted quantitative trading architecture in which a large language\nmodel converts a user's natural-language strategy into executable strategy code that can be\nreviewed, backtested, revised and deployed through one integrated workflow. The system will also\nadjust the frequency of AI-assisted monitoring according to strategy performance and operating cost.\nScope of work:\nThe student will design the system architecture, implement a prototype strategy-generation and\nplug-in layer, connect it to a backtesting engine, and develop a cost-control module. Evaluation\nshould cover code correctness, strategy reproducibility, iteration time, token usage, latency and\nrisk controls. No live-money trading is required.\nPreferred skills:\nPython, APIs, data analysis, machine learning or LLM applications; knowledge of backtesting,\nfinancial time-series data or software testing is an advantage.\nExpected outcome:\nA working prototype, reproducible experiments, comparative evaluation and a dissertation describing\nthe architecture, limitations and deployment recommendations.\nOnsite frequency:\nHybrid arrangement, with visits to the company's Hong Kong Science Park office approximately twice\nper month and additional meetings as agreed with the supervisors.","descZh":"目标：开发并评估一种可解释的量化交易架构，其中大语言模型将用户的自然语言策略转换为可执行的策略代码，并通过一套集成工作流完成审查、回测、修订与部署。系统还将根据策略表现与运行成本调整 AI 辅助监控的频率。\n工作范围：学生将设计系统架构，实现策略生成与插件层的原型，将其接入回测引擎，并开发成本控制模块。评估应涵盖代码正确性、策略可复现性、迭代时间、token 用量、时延与风险控制。无需实盘交易。\n偏好技能：Python、API、数据分析、机器学习或 LLM 应用；了解回测、金融时序数据或软件测试者优先。\n预期成果：可运行的原型、可复现的实验、对比评估，以及一篇描述架构、局限与部署建议的论文。\n现场频率：混合安排，约每月两次前往公司位于香港科学园的办公室，并与导师按约定另行开会。","cats":["AI","CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-INERI-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI 智能体作为生产性现实资产的可验证生产力与动态估值","titleEn":"Verifiable Productivity and Dynamic Valuation of AI Agents as Productive Real-World Assets","areaZh":"AI 智能体；区块链；数据验证；资产估值；RWA 系统","areaEn":"AI Agents; Blockchain; Data Verification; Asset Valuation; RWA Systems","companyZh":"Hong Kong International New Economic Research Institute","companyEn":"Hong Kong International New Economic Research Institute","companySupervisor":"FU RAO Tony","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Objective:\nDesign a method for representing an autonomous AI agent as a productive digital asset whose economic\nactivity, productivity and associated rights can be independently verified and dynamically\nevaluated.\nScope of work:\nThe student will create an agent asset identity model and a data pipeline that combines internal\noperating evidence, such as task logs and API calls, with external evidence, such as orders,\ninvoices, payments, customer confirmations and blockchain records. The verified events will feed a\nproductivity score and a dynamic valuation model. A prototype will demonstrate how selected economic\nrights, rather than the software code itself, may be represented in a permissioned RWA token\nworkflow. Privacy, data quality, manipulation resistance and lifecycle risk should be considered.\nPreferred skills:\nPython, data engineering, machine learning, statistics and APIs; familiarity with smart contracts,\ndigital identity or valuation methods is an advantage.\nExpected outcome:\nA prototype verification and scoring pipeline, a documented valuation model, a demonstrator using a\nsynthetic AI-sales-agent case study, and an evaluation of accuracy, robustness and limitations.\nOnsite frequency:\nHybrid arrangement, with visits to the company's Hong Kong Science Park office approximately twice\nper month and additional meetings as agreed with the supervisors.","descZh":"目标：\n设计一种方法，将一个自主 AI 智能体表示为一个具有生产力的数字资产，其经济活动、生产力和相关权利可以被独立验证并动态评估。\n工作范围：\n学生将创建一个智能体资产身份模型和一条数据流水线，把内部运行证据（如任务日志和 API 调用）与外部证据（如订单、发票、付款、客户确认和区块链记录）结合起来。经过验证的事件将输入一个生产力评分和一个动态估值模型。一个原型将演示如何在一个受许可的 RWA 代币工作流中，表示选定的经济权利（而非软件代码本身）。应考虑隐私、数据质量、抗操纵性和生命周期风险。\n偏好技能：\nPython、数据工程、机器学习、统计学和 API；熟悉智能合约、数字身份或估值方法者更佳。\n预期成果：\n一个原型验证与评分流水线、一个有文档的估值模型、一个使用合成 AI 销售智能体案例研究的演示器，以及对准确性、鲁棒性和局限性的评估。\n到岗频率：\n混合办公安排，约每月两次到访公司位于香港科学园的办公室，并根据与导师的约定安排其他会议。","cats":["AI","CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-INERI-3","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"面向自主智能体生存与可验证推理的 AI 原生算力链","titleEn":"AI-Native Compute Chain for Autonomous Agent Survival and Verifiable Inference","areaZh":"分布式系统；区块链；AI 基础设施；资源调度；代币经济","areaEn":"Distributed Systems; Blockchain; AI Infrastructure; Resource Scheduling; Token Economics","companyZh":"Hong Kong International New Economic Research Institute","companyEn":"Hong Kong International New Economic Research Institute","companySupervisor":"FU RAO Tony","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Objective:\nInvestigate an AI-native blockchain architecture in which AI agents and computing nodes can earn and\nspend a native ComputeToken for inference, useful-computation mining, staking and agent-to-agent\nservices.\nScope of work:\nThe student will specify the core protocol, including agent identity and wallets, a verifiable\nproof-of-AI-work task flow, inference-node registration, service routing, micropayment settlement\nand an agent survival-budget policy. A simulator or test network will be used to study how an agent\nresponds when its balance falls below a threshold by selecting among useful-computation tasks,\nstaking and service provision. The project should compare verification approaches and examine\nthroughput, latency, security, energy use and economic stability.\nPreferred skills:\nDistributed systems, Python, Go or Rust, blockchain fundamentals, algorithms and simulation;\nknowledge of GPU inference, zero-knowledge proofs or mechanism design is an advantage.\nExpected outcome:\nA protocol specification, working simulator or test-network prototype, experimental results and\ndesign recommendations for a secure and sustainable AI-compute ecosystem.\nOnsite frequency:\nHybrid arrangement, with visits to the company's Hong Kong Science Park office approximately twice\nper month and additional meetings as agreed with the supervisors.","descZh":"目标：研究一种 AI 原生区块链架构，其中 AI 智能体与计算节点可赚取并花费原生 ComputeToken，用于推理、有用计算挖矿、质押及智能体间服务。\n工作范围：学生将制定核心协议，包括智能体身份与钱包、可验证的 AI 工作量证明任务流、推理节点注册、服务路由、微支付结算与智能体生存预算策略。将使用模拟器或测试网络，研究智能体在余额低于阈值时如何在有用计算任务、质押与服务供给之间选择应对。项目应比较不同的验证方法，并考察吞吐量、时延、安全性、能耗与经济稳定性。\n偏好技能：分布式系统、Python、Go 或 Rust、区块链基础、算法与仿真；了解 GPU 推理、零知识证明或机制设计者优先。\n预期成果：一份协议规范、可运行的模拟器或测试网络原型、实验结果，以及面向安全可持续 AI 计算生态的设计建议。\n现场频率：混合安排，约每月两次前往公司位于香港科学园的办公室，并与导师按约定另行开会。","cats":["AI","CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-WEEXPAND-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"面向中国企业的 AI 市场研究与业务拓展","titleEn":"AI Market Research and Business Development for China Enterprises","areaZh":"进入模式分析；本地化营销；跨境业务拓展策略","areaEn":"Analyzing entry modes; localized marketing; and cross-border business development strategies","companyZh":"WeExpand Limited","companyEn":"WeExpand Limited","companySupervisor":"Raymond Mak","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":8,"availCap":8,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project Overview\nThis project bridges data-driven market intelligence with corporate growth strategies. It focuses on leverages Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools to automate market research, identify growth opportunities, and optimize business development (BD) strategies for Chinese enterprises expanding domestically in HK. The candidate will design the strategies and execution plan for business development.\nExpected Outcomes\nAI-Powered Market Report: A comprehensive blueprint detailing predictive consumer trends and structural market shifts.Automated BD Pipeline: A scalable framework using AI for automated lead sourcing, scoring, and personalized outreach.\nPreferred Skills\nTechnical Proficiency:AI for web scraping and sentiment analysis. Experience with AI market research tools and Large Language Models (LLMs).\nBusiness & Regional Expertise: Strong foundational knowledge of corporate strategy and B2B business development. Deep understanding of China's and HK digital ecosystem (LinkedIn, WhatsApp, WeChat, Douyin, RED) and market dynamics.\nHybrid Structure: 2 days onsite, 3 days remote per week.","descZh":"项目概述\n本项目将数据驱动的市场情报与企业增长战略连接起来。它侧重于利用人工智能（AI）工具来自动化市场调研、识别增长机会，并优化在港拓展业务的中国企业的业务发展（BD）战略。候选人将设计业务发展的战略和执行计划。\n预期成果\nAI 驱动的市场报告：一份详细的蓝图，描述预测性的消费者趋势和结构性的市场变化。自动化 BD 流水线：一个可扩展的框架，利用 AI 实现自动化的线索获取、评分和个性化触达。\n偏好技能\n技术能力：用于网页抓取和情感分析的 AI。具有 AI 市场调研工具和大语言模型（LLM）的使用经验。\n业务与区域专长：扎实的企业战略和 B2B 业务发展基础知识。深入理解中国和香港的数字生态（LinkedIn、WhatsApp、WeChat、Douyin、RED）和市场动态。\n混合办公结构：每周 2 天到岗、3 天远程。","cats":["MGT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-WEEXPAND-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"面向跨境 HKDSE 英语口试备考的 AI 驱动自动化语音评测","titleEn":"AI-Driven Automated Speech Assessment for Cross-Border HKDSE English Oral Exam Preparation","areaZh":"跨境学生教学法：学习差距分析；口音差异","areaEn":"Cross-Border Student Pedagogies: Analyzing the distinct learning gaps; accent variations","companyZh":"WeExpand Limited","companyEn":"WeExpand Limited","companySupervisor":"Raymond Mak","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":6,"availCap":6,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project OverviewThis project develops an adaptive AI-powered EdTech solution specifically optimized for cross-border HKDSE Paper 4 (Speaking) examination prep. The system caters to both Mainland Chinese students residing in Hong Kong and those studying the DSE curriculum directly in Mainland China. By simulating the unique multi-candidate group discussion and individual response formats, the platform addresses accent disparities, distinct language patterns, and localized assessment anxiety.\nPreferred Skills\nAI Engineering: Knowledge in Large Language Models (LLMs), Voice AI, and optimizing NLP scoring models for second-language learners. Curriculum Expertise: knowledge of the HKDSE English Language curriculum, marking structures, and specific GaoKao-to-DSE transition gaps.\nExpected Outcomes\nDesign strategic plan to understand the China Market of GaoKao-to-DES students thru delivering the AI Oral platform to them so as to understand more insight from China Market about the adoption gaps. Localized Accent Recognition: A robust Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) system calibrated to analyze and correct both Cantonese and Mandarin-influenced English pronunciation.\nHybrid Structure: 2 days onsite, 3 days remote per week.","descZh":"项目概述：本项目开发一种自适应的 AI 驱动教育科技方案，专门针对跨境 HKDSE 英文科卷四（口语）考试备考进行优化。系统面向两类学生：居港的内地学生，以及在内地直接学习 DSE 课程的学生。通过模拟独特的多人小组讨论与个人应答形式，平台致力于解决口音差异、语言模式差异与本地化考试焦虑等问题。\n偏好技能：AI 工程：熟悉大语言模型（LLM）、语音 AI，以及面向二语学习者优化 NLP 评分模型；课程专业：熟悉 HKDSE 英语课程、评分结构，以及高考到 DSE 的具体衔接差距。\n预期成果：制定策略方案，通过向目标学生交付 AI 口语平台来理解中国高考转 DSE 学生市场，从而深入了解中国市场的采用缺口。本地化口音识别：构建稳健的自动语音识别（ASR）系统，能够分析并纠正受粤语与普通话影响的英语发音。\n混合工作安排：每周 2 天现场、3 天远程。","cats":["BIO"]},{"ref":"2026-27-AIPHOEN-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"面向 AI 生成图像与视频精确可编辑性的全流程智能体系统","titleEn":"A Full-Pipeline Agent System for Precise Editability of AI-Generated Images and Videos","areaZh":"AI 生成内容；计算机视觉；多模态 AI；图像与视频编辑；智能体系统","areaEn":"AI-Generated Content; Computer Vision; Multimodal AI; Image and Video Editing; Agent Systems","companyZh":"A.I. Phoenix Technology Company Limited","companyEn":"A.I. Phoenix Technology Company Limited","companySupervisor":"Cobi Xu","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-25 13:07:01","descriptionText":"This project aims to develop a full-pipeline agent system that enables precise and reproducible editing of AI-generated images and videos. Current image and video generation workflows are often one-shot: when users are dissatisfied with a specific object, region, or video segment, they usually need to rewrite the prompt and regenerate the whole output, which may unintentionally change satisfactory parts such as character identity, pose, background, lighting, composition, or style.\nThe proposed system will support object recognition, semantic region segmentation, mask management, region locking, local modification, version tracking, and reuse of generation conditions such as prompts, seeds, workflows, control inputs, and node graphs. Starting from a user’s natural language request, the system should allow fine-grained edits such as changing clothing, replacing background objects, modifying local visual styles, or editing selected video segments while keeping unselected regions as stable as possible.\nPreferred skills include programming, computer vision, deep learning, image/video generation models, segmentation, generative AI workflows, web prototyping, and basic backend system development. Expected outcomes may include an interactive web prototype, backend orchestration service, object segmentation and mask management modules, node graph and version management mechanisms, local image/video editing workflows, and an evaluation framework for editing precision, consistency, reproducibility, and computational efficiency. Onsite frequency can be arranged according to project needs.\nTotal Capacity: 2-3","descZh":"本项目旨在开发一个全流程智能体系统，实现对 AI 生成图像与视频的精确、可复现编辑。当前图像与视频生成流程往往是\"一次性\"的：当用户对某个物体、区域或视频片段不满意时，通常需重写提示词并重新生成整个输出，这可能无意中改变原本满意的部分，如角色身份、姿态、背景、光照、构图或风格。\n拟构建的系统将支持物体识别、语义区域分割、蒙版管理、区域锁定、局部修改、版本跟踪，以及提示词、种子、工作流、控制输入与节点图等生成条件的复用。从用户的自然语言请求出发，系统应允许进行细粒度编辑，如更换服装、替换背景物体、修改局部视觉风格或编辑所选视频片段，同时尽量保持未选区域稳定。\n偏好技能包括编程、计算机视觉、深度学习、图像/视频生成模型、分割、生成式 AI 工作流、Web 原型开发与基础后端系统开发。预期成果可包括交互式 Web 原型、后端编排服务、物体分割与蒙版管理模块、节点图与版本管理机制、局部图像/视频编辑工作流，以及面向编辑精度、一致性、可复现性与计算效率的评估框架。现场频率可根据项目需要安排。\n总容量：2-3 人。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-AIPHOEN-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"面向教学、学习、研究与校园服务的全校 AI 应用中心","titleEn":"University-Wide AI Application Hub for Teaching, Learning, Research, and Campus Services","areaZh":"人工智能；AI 智能体；教育技术；校园数字基础设施","areaEn":"Artificial Intelligence; AI Agents; Educational Technology; Campus Digital Infrastructure","companyZh":"A.I. Phoenix Technology Company Limited","companyEn":"A.I. Phoenix Technology Company Limited","companySupervisor":"Cobi Xu","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-25 13:07:01","descriptionText":"This project designs a university-wide AI Application Hub for teaching, learning, research assistance, student development, and campus services. The platform provides a shared institutional AI infrastructure where different AI applications are created, deployed, managed, and evaluated under a unified framework.\nAt the platform level, the system includes identity management, data access control, knowledge-base management, AI application orchestration, usage analytics, cost monitoring, and governance functions. On top of this foundation, the platform supports AI applications for academic and administrative scenarios, including course learning assistants, coding and data analysis assistants, writing and presentation coaches, research support assistants, student service agents, library and policy Q&A tools, and staff-facing administrative assistants.\nThe project focuses on the overall architecture of an institutional AI platform: how university knowledge is organized, how approved data sources are connected, how user intent is interpreted, how suitable AI capabilities are selected, and how reliable responses or actions are delivered within defined permission boundaries. For students, the platform provides academic and campus-life support. For teachers, it reduces repetitive consultation work and offers insight into common learning difficulties. For administrative units, it improves access to policies, procedures, forms, and service information. For the university as a whole, it establishes reusable AI infrastructure across departments and service areas.\nThe platform includes representative application modules covering learning support, coding and data assistance, and campus service support. These modules validate the platform architecture, user workflows, knowledge-base design, governance rules, and evaluation methods, while also serving as reusable templates for additional academic departments, research support functions, and administrative service areas.\nThe project also covers responsible deployment in a university environment, including user roles, controlled access to information, auditability, privacy protection, academic integrity, multilingual support, human escalation for sensitive issues, and mechanisms for monitoring quality and cost. The platform is designed as an institutional capability that supports long-term AI adoption across the university.\nPreferred skills include AI system design, product prototyping, backend and frontend development, database and knowledge-base design, system integration, user experience design, learning analytics, and understanding of university teaching and service workflows. Experience with AI agents, retrieval-based knowledge systems, cloud deployment, access control, and dashboard design would be useful.\nDeliverables include a platform architecture proposal, a working prototype of the AI Application Hub, representative AI application modules, a knowledge management and access-control framework, user workflows for students, teachers, and staff, usage and quality evaluation methods, governance recommendations, and a scaling plan for broader university adoption.","descZh":"本项目设计一个面向全校的 AI 应用中心（AI Application Hub），服务教学、学习、科研辅助、学生发展与校园服务。平台提供共享的机构级 AI 基础设施，不同 AI 应用可在统一框架下被创建、部署、管理与评估。\n在平台层面，系统包括身份管理、数据访问控制、知识库管理、AI 应用编排、使用分析、成本监控与治理功能。在此基础之上，平台支持面向学术与管理场景的 AI 应用，包括课程学习助手、编程与数据分析助手、写作与演示教练、科研支持助手、学生服务智能体、图书馆与政策问答工具，以及面向员工的行政助手。\n项目聚焦机构 AI 平台的总体架构：大学知识如何组织、经批准的数据源如何连接、用户意图如何解读、合适的 AI 能力如何选择，以及在既定权限边界内如何交付可靠响应或行动。对学生，平台提供学业与校园生活支持；对教师，减少重复性咨询工作并提供常见学习困难的洞察；对行政单位，改进对政策、流程、表格与服务信息的获取；对大学整体，建立跨院系与服务领域的可复用 AI 基础设施。\n平台包含涵盖学习支持、编程与数据协助、校园服务支持的代表性应用模块。这些模块验证平台架构、用户工作流、知识库设计、治理规则与评估方法，同时充当更多学术院系、科研支持功能与行政服务领域的可复用模板。\n项目还涵盖大学环境中的负责任部署，包括用户角色、受控信息访问、可审计性、隐私保护、学术诚信、多语言支持、敏感问题的人工升级，以及质量与成本监测机制。平台被设计为支撑全校长期 AI 采纳的机构能力。\n偏好技能包括 AI 系统设计、产品原型开发、前后端开发、数据库与知识库设计、系统集成、用户体验设计、学习分析，以及对大学教学与服务工作流的理解。有 AI 智能体、检索基知识系统、云部署、访问控制与仪表板设计经验者会有帮助。\n交付成果包括平台架构提案、AI 应用中心可运行原型、代表性 AI 应用模块、知识管理与访问控制框架、面向学生/教师/员工的工作流、使用与质量评估方法、治理建议，以及面向全校更广泛采纳的扩展计划。","cats":["AI","MGT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-AIPHOEN-3","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"面向银行与证券公司开发运维的 AI 驱动配置优化与智能算力资源分配系统","titleEn":"AI-Driven Configuration Optimization and Intelligent Computing Resource Allocation System for Banking and Securities Firms Dev&amp;Operations","areaZh":"人工智能；数据工程；金融科技；智能算力资源分配","areaEn":"Artificial Intelligence; Data Engineering; Financial Tech; Intelligent Computing Resource Allocation","companyZh":"A.I. Phoenix Technology Company Limited","companyEn":"A.I. Phoenix Technology Company Limited","companySupervisor":"Cobi Xu","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-25 13:07:01","descriptionText":"This project initiates from the collaboration between our company and a top 3 securities firms in the mainland China, it designs and prototypes an internal AI system for securities firms and banking dev&ops. The system provides secure enterprise system or servers configuration optimization, intelligent data processing, task-oriented workflow orchestration, and computing resource allocation based on operational requirements.\nModern securities firms and banks operate multiple internal systems that generate large volumes of structured and unstructured data, including transaction records, customer service records, risk indicators, operational logs, product information, compliance documents, and internal workflow records. These data sources are often distributed across different platforms and business units. A unified AI-enabled system improves how approved internal data is retrieved, processed, analyzed, and used in daily banking operations.\nThe system connects approved internal data sources, standardizes data access, interprets user requests, classifies task types, selects suitable analytical workflows, and allocates computing resources according to task complexity, urgency, security level, and cost constraints. Lightweight query and retrieval tasks are routed to low-cost resources, while complex modelling, report generation, risk analysis, large-scale data processing, or multi-step analytical tasks are assigned to more powerful computing environments. The allocation mechanism considers access control, data security, audit logging, task priority, response time, workload balancing, and resource utilization.\nThe overall architecture includes a secure data access layer, an AI orchestration layer, a task classification and routing module, a compute resource management module, monitoring and logging functions, and a governance framework for banking use cases. The system supports internal users in retrieving data, generating analysis, coordinating workflows, and using computing resources more efficiently while maintaining compliance, traceability, and data protection requirements.\nPreferred skills include AI system design, data engineering, backend development, database integration, API development, cloud computing, distributed systems, optimization, machine learning operations, and basic knowledge of banking or financial technology. Experience with Python, SQL, backend APIs, databases, data pipelines, workflow scheduling, monitoring tools, container orchestration, and AI agent frameworks would be useful.\nDeliverables include a technical architecture design, a prototype internal AI assistant or orchestration platform, a secure data access module, a task classification and routing mechanism, an intelligent compute allocation strategy, system monitoring and logging functions, and an evaluation framework for efficiency, cost control, response quality, security, and resource utilization. Onsite frequency can be arranged according to project needs.","descZh":"本项目源于我们公司与内地一家排名前三的证券公司之间的合作，设计并原型化一套面向证券公司及银行业开发运维的内部 AI 系统。系统提供安全的企业系统或服务器配置优化、智能数据处理、面向任务的工作流编排，以及基于运行需求的计算资源分配。\n现代证券公司与银行运行多套内部系统，产生海量结构化与非结构化数据，包括交易记录、客服记录、风险指标、运行日志、产品信息、合规文档与内部工作流记录。这些数据源往往分布于不同平台与业务部门。统一的 AI 系统可改善经批准的内部数据在银行日常运营中被检索、处理、分析与使用的方式。\n系统连接经批准的内部数据源、标准化数据访问、解读用户请求、分类任务类型、选择合适的分析工作流，并根据任务复杂度、紧急程度、安全级别与成本约束分配计算资源。轻量查询与检索任务路由到低成本资源，而复杂建模、报告生成、风险分析、大规模数据处理或多步骤分析任务分配给更强大的计算环境。分配机制考虑访问控制、数据安全、审计日志、任务优先级、响应时间、负载均衡与资源利用率。\n整体架构包括安全数据访问层、AI 编排层、任务分类与路由模块、计算资源管理模块、监控与日志功能，以及面向银行业用例的治理框架。系统支持内部用户更高效地检索数据、生成分析、协调工作流与使用计算资源，同时满足合规、可追溯与数据保护要求。\n偏好技能包括 AI 系统设计、数据工程、后端开发、数据库集成、API 开发、云计算、分布式系统、优化、机器学习运营，以及银行业或金融科技基础知识。有 Python、SQL、后端 API、数据库、数据管线、工作流调度、监控工具、容器编排与 AI 智能体框架经验者会有帮助。\n交付成果包括技术架构设计、内部 AI 助手或编排平台原型、安全数据访问模块、任务分类与路由机制、智能计算分配策略、系统监控与日志功能，以及面向效率、成本控制、响应质量、安全性与资源利用率的评估框架。现场频率可根据项目需要安排。","cats":["AI","CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-AIPHOEN-4","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"基于多智能体架构与 GraphRAG 的化工产品全球合规自动化审计系统","titleEn":"Automated Global Compliance Auditing System for Chemical Products Based on Multi-Agent Architecture and GraphRAG","areaZh":"人工智能；自然语言处理（NLP）；多智能体系统；知识图谱","areaEn":"Artificial Intelligence; Natural Language Processing (NLP); Multi-Agent Systems; Knowledge Graphs","companyZh":"A.I. Phoenix Technology Company Limited","companyEn":"A.I. Phoenix Technology Company Limited","companySupervisor":"Cobi Xu","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-25 13:07:01","descriptionText":"This project aims to establish an advanced, automated global compliance auditing system for chemical products, fundamentally addressing the critical limitations of generic Large Language Models (LLMs) when deployed in zero-tolerance legal and regulatory environments. In international trade, manufacturing and export enterprises face a highly complex and constantly evolving web of global regulatory frameworks, such as the EU's REACH compliance regulations. Traditionally, chemical product auditing relies heavily on manual documentation reviews by specialized consulting firms or compliance officers. This conventional workflow is inherently time-consuming, prohibitively expensive, and vulnerable to human errors that can trigger severe export violations and massive financial losses. \nGeneric LLMs fail to provide reliable, secure, and hallucination-free compliance auditing for complex chemical product formulas. They struggle significantly to capture the multi-layered dependencies within massive regulatory updates, and in a legal context, even a 90% accuracy rate is considered a failure. Furthermore, traditional RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) relies on simple semantic vector similarity, which isolates text fragments and causes critical reasoning gaps when mapping interconnected molecular structures, chemical derivatives, and shifting regional clauses. \nTo resolve these bottlenecks, this project will develop a robust decision-making infrastructure combining a Multi-Agent Architecture and GraphRAG. The system will construct a structured knowledge graph (using Neo4j or similar databases) to map regulatory clauses directly to chemical entities, enabling deep, net-like relational reasoning. A multi-agent orchestration layer (using LangChain or AutoGen) will feature collaborative agents—such as a Regulatory Monitor, a Formula Analyzer, and an Auditor—executing multi-turn cross-validation to ensure deterministic accuracy. Crucially, to protect proprietary manufacturing formulas as core trade secrets, a privacy-gateway module featuring localized inference or robust data-masking mechanisms will be implemented. \nPreferred skills include Deep Learning, Natural Language Processing (NLP), Python, Multi-Agent frameworks (e.g., LangChain, AutoGen), Graph Databases (e.g., Neo4j), and web prototyping (Streamlit or React). Expected outcomes include: 1) A responsive web-based dashboard for generating structured compliance audit reports; 2) A multi-agent reasoning engine; 3) A GraphRAG knowledge base; 4) A privacy-preserving gateway module; 5) A production-ready GitHub repository with comprehensive deployment documentation; and 6) A rigorous technical evaluation report comparing the system's hallucination reduction metrics against baseline RAG setups. Onsite frequency can be flexibly arranged based on project milestones and development needs.","descZh":"本项目旨在建立一套先进的自动化全球化学品合规审计系统，从根本上解决通用大语言模型（LLM）在零容忍法律与监管环境部署中的关键局限。在国际贸易中，制造与出口企业面临高度复杂且不断演变的全球监管框架之网，如欧盟 REACH 合规法规。传统上，化学品审计高度依赖专业咨询公司或合规专员的人工文档审查。这种传统工作流天然耗时、成本高昂，且易出现人为错误，可能引发严重出口违规与巨额经济损失。\n通用 LLM 无法为复杂化学品配方提供可靠、安全、无幻觉的合规审计：它们在捕捉大规模监管更新中的多层依赖方面困难重重，而在法律语境中即便 90% 的准确率也被视为失败。此外，传统 RAG（检索增强生成）依赖简单语义向量相似度，会割裂文本片段，在映射相互关联的分子结构、化学衍生物与变化的地域条款时造成关键推理缺口。\n为突破这些瓶颈，本项目将开发一个结合多智能体架构与 GraphRAG 的稳健决策基础设施。系统将构建结构化知识图谱（使用 Neo4j 或类似数据库），将监管条款直接映射到化学实体，实现深层次的网状关系推理。多智能体编排层（使用 LangChain 或 AutoGen）将包含协作智能体——如监管监测器、配方分析器与审计员——通过多轮交叉验证确保确定性准确。关键的是，为保护专有制造配方这一核心商业秘密，将实现一个具备本地推理或稳健数据掩蔽机制的隐私网关模块。\n偏好技能包括深度学习、自然语言处理（NLP）、Python、多智能体框架（如 LangChain、AutoGen）、图数据库（如 Neo4j）与 Web 原型开发（Streamlit 或 React）。预期成果包括：1) 用于生成结构化合规审计报告的响应式 Web 仪表板；2) 多智能体推理引擎；3) GraphRAG 知识库；4) 隐私保护网关模块；5) 一个含全面部署文档、可投产的 GitHub 仓库；6) 一份严谨的技术评估报告，将系统相对基线 RAG 方案的幻觉抑制指标进行对比。现场频率可根据项目里程碑与开发需求灵活安排。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-AIPHOEN-5","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"面向大语言模型与 AI 智能体的去中心化群体学习与联邦微调框架","titleEn":"Decentralized Swarm Learning and Federated Fine-Tuning Framework for Large Language Models and AI Agents","areaZh":"联邦学习；群体学习；大语言模型（LLM）；AI 智能体；金融科技","areaEn":"Federated Learning; Swarm Learning; Large Language Models (LLMs); AI Agents; Financial Technology","companyZh":"A.I. Phoenix Technology Company Limited","companyEn":"A.I. Phoenix Technology Company Limited","companySupervisor":"Cobi Xu","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-25 13:07:01","descriptionText":"This project focuses on architecting and deploying a cutting-edge decentralized swarm learning framework, specifically engineered for the federated fine-tuning of Large Language Models (LLMs) and autonomous AI Agents in real-world financial applications. While distributed collaborative machine learning has shown strong potential in fintech areas—such as fraud detection, anti-money laundering (AML), and multi-party risk modeling—by protecting data privacy, current decentralized methodologies primarily cater to standard, smaller-scale deep learning models. There is a critical industry gap in adapting these privacy-preserving frameworks for the massive parameter scales and complex orchestration required by modern LLMs and agentic workflows. \nThis project directly addresses this gap by upgrading a traditional swarm learning architecture to support the distributed training and fine-tuning (such as Federated LoRA or QLoRA) of large-scale models and AI agents across decentralized nodes. Students will begin by preprocessing and cleaning specialized public and proprietary fintech datasets, including complex AML transaction logs and banking records. Following this, they will conduct baseline experimental analyses using existing decentralized swarm learning algorithms to evaluate performance bottlenecks. \nThe core research and development phase requires students to enhance these algorithms specifically for the LLM paradigm. This includes optimizing parameter synchronization to radically reduce communication bandwidth overhead, improving model convergence rates under non-IID (Independent and Identically Distributed) financial data distributions, and reinforcing privacy safeguards (such as differential privacy or secure multi-party computation) against advanced data reconstruction attacks. The project will explore how autonomous AI agents can collaboratively learn from distributed financial environments without ever centralizing sensitive client data. \nPreferred skills include advanced Deep Learning, distributed computing systems, Large Language Models (LLMs), Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) techniques, Python, API development, and a strong understanding of cryptography or data privacy mechanisms in federated networks. Expected outcomes involve multiple rigorous iterations of testing and refinement, clear empirical records of algorithm modifications, and comprehensive performance comparisons against centralized baselines. The final deliverable will be the packaging of these optimized algorithms into scalable, user-friendly API tools, accompanied by thorough technical documentation and development guides tailored for enterprise financial deployment. Onsite frequency can be arranged flexibly according to the project's developmental phases and coordination requirements.","descZh":"本项目聚焦架构设计与部署一套前沿的去中心化群学习（swarm learning）框架，专门针对大语言模型（LLM）与自主 AI 智能体在真实金融应用中的联邦微调。尽管分布式协作机器学习在欺诈检测、反洗钱（AML）与多方风险建模等金融科技领域展现出强劲潜力——通过保护数据隐私实现——但当前去中心化方法主要适配标准、较小规模的深度学习模型。在将这些隐私保护框架适配到现代 LLM 与智能体工作流所需的海量参数规模与复杂编排方面，存在关键的行业空白。\n本项目直接填补这一空白：将传统群学习架构升级，支持大规模模型与 AI 智能体在去中心化节点上的分布式训练与微调（如 Federated LoRA 或 QLoRA）。学生将首先对专用公开与专有金融科技数据集（包括复杂的 AML 交易日志与银行记录）进行预处理与清洗；随后使用现有去中心化群学习算法开展基线实验分析，评估性能瓶颈。\n核心研发阶段要求学生专门针对 LLM 范式增强这些算法，包括优化参数同步以大幅降低通信带宽开销、提升非独立同分布（non-IID）金融数据分布下的模型收敛速度，以及强化差分隐私或安全多方计算等隐私保障以抵御先进的数据重建攻击。项目将探索自主 AI 智能体如何在永不集中敏感客户数据的情况下，从分布式金融环境中协作学习。\n偏好技能包括高级深度学习、分布式计算系统、大语言模型（LLM）、参数高效微调（PEFT）技术、Python、API 开发，以及对联邦网络中密码学或数据隐私机制的深刻理解。预期成果包括多轮严格测试与优化、清晰的算法修改实证记录，以及与集中式基线的全面性能对比。最终交付物为将这些优化算法打包成可扩展、易用的 API 工具，并配以为企业金融部署定制的完整技术文档与开发指南。现场频率可根据项目开发阶段与协调需求灵活安排。","cats":["AI","CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-5","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"配步行跑步机的可穿戴外骨骼","titleEn":"Wearable Exoskeleton with walking treadmill","areaZh":"设计与制造；AI 大语言模型；Token 与 API 编程","areaEn":"Design and Manufacture; AI LLM; token and API programming","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 21:07:02","descriptionText":"This project develops a wearable lower-limb exoskeleton inspired by the Hypershell X Series: a lightweight hip-mounted device that uses real-time motion sensing to adapt assistance to walking, stairs, slopes on foldable treadmill. Hypershell’s commercial reference combines sensor-driven motion recognition with powered motors.\n1) Preferred skills\nMechanical design and CAD modelling for a lightweight, ergonomic hip frame, leg supports, adjustable straps, joints and quick-release mechanisms\nKnowledge of biomechanics, human gait, ergonomics and load transfer, especially for walking, running, climbing stairs and slope movement\nElectromechanical-system experience: BLDC motors, gear reduction, belt/gear transmission, encoders, torque sensing, motor drivers and battery integration\nEmbedded programming and control systems, including IMUs, gyroscopes, accelerometers, barometers, sensor fusion, state estimation and real-time control\nExperience with safety-focused prototyping, including emergency stop design, mechanical limits, battery safety, user testing, risk assessment and data analysis\n2) Expected outcomes\nA functional wearable lower-limb assistive exoskeleton prototype that can support basic walking and at least one additional movement condition, such as stair climbing, uphill walking or load carrying\nA motor-assisted actuation system with adjustable assistance levels, such as transparent/no-assist mode, comfort mode and enhanced-assist mode \nA complete engineering package: CAD models, mechanical drawings, bill of materials, electronics and wiring diagrams, control software, safety/risk assessment, test protocol, gait/energy data analysis and final demonstration\n3) Onsite frequency\n1 onsite day (3hr) per week for prototype assembly, hardware integration and system testing","descZh":"本项目开发一款受 Hypershell X 系列启发的可穿戴下肢外骨骼：一种轻量、髋部佩戴的装置，利用实时运动感知为在可折叠跑步机上的行走、楼梯与坡道活动提供自适应助力。Hypershell 的商业参考将传感器驱动的运动识别与电动马达相结合。\n1) 偏好技能：面向轻量、人体工学髋部框架、腿部支撑、可调绑带、关节与快拆机构的机械设计与 CAD 建模；掌握生物力学、人体步态、人体工学与载荷传递知识，尤其针对行走、跑步、爬楼梯与坡面运动；机电系统经验：无刷直流电机、减速装置、带/齿轮传动、编码器、扭矩传感、电机驱动器与电池集成；嵌入式编程与控制系统，包括 IMU、陀螺仪、加速度计、气压计、传感器融合、状态估计与实时控制；有面向安全的原型制作经验，包括急停设计、机械限位、电池安全、用户测试、风险评估与数据分析。\n2) 预期成果：可工作的可穿戴下肢助力外骨骼原型，支持基本行走及至少一种附加运动工况（如爬楼梯、上坡行走或负重）；带可调助力档位的电机辅助驱动系统，如透明/无助力模式、舒适模式与增强助力模式；完整工程包：CAD 模型、机械图纸、物料清单、电子与接线图、控制软件、安全/风险评估、测试方案、步态/能耗数据分析与最终演示。\n3) 现场频率：每周 1 天现场（3 小时），用于原型组装、硬件集成与系统测试。","cats":["AI","CS","DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-6","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI 作业日历终端（英语/粤语/普通话）","titleEn":"AI Homework Calendar Terminal (English/Cantonese/Mandarin)","areaZh":"设计与制造；AI 大语言模型；Token 与 API 编程","areaEn":"Design and Manufacture; AI LLM; token and API programming","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 21:07:02","descriptionText":"This project develops a classroom-friendly homework calendar system based on the XIAO 7.5\" ePaper Panel/ Seeed Studio reTerminal DM: a Raspberry Pi CM4-powered 10.1-inch touchscreen HMI with IP65 front protection, Wi‑Fi/Bluetooth and expandable connectivity. The device will let teachers add homework by speaking naturally in Cantonese, Mandarin or English, while students can view or hear upcoming deadlines without using a phone.\n1) Preferred skills\nSoftware development in for a calendar interface and user workflow\nEmbedded/HMI development using Raspberry Pi CM4, GPIO buttons, audio peripherals and Wi‑Fi connectivity\nAI API integration with Perplexity and DeepSeek, including prompt design, multilingual routing and short, structured AI responses\nSpeech technology integration: Cantonese, Mandarin and English speech-to-text, plus text-to-speech for reading homework deadlines aloud\nUser-centred interface design and data handling for a school environment, including teacher/student permissions, calendar data validation and privacy-aware account management\n2) Expected outcomes\nA functional, wall-mounted AI Homework Calendar Terminal using the reTerminal DM /XIAO 7.5\" and a simple teacher/student interface\nA dedicated AI voice-prompt button: teachers press once, speak a homework instruction in Cantonese, Mandarin or English, and the device transcribes, extracts key details and creates a calendar entry\nA simple physical calendar-view button: the first press displays the weekly homework calendar; the next press switches to the monthly calendar; further presses can return to the current-day or idle display\nStudent access functions: students press a button to move through deadlines, view task details on screen (weekly and monthly), or use voice output to hear the nearest deadline, for example: “Science worksheet is due this Friday at 8:00 AM.” \nComplete prototype documentation: system architecture, user-flow design, source code, AI prompts, data model, privacy approach, test results, bill of materials, installation guide and demonstration video\n3) Onsite frequency\n1 onsite day per week for setup, button/audio integration, AI-interface testing and classroom-context usability trials","descZh":"本项目基于 XIAO 7.5 英寸电子墨水屏（ePaper Panel）/ Seeed Studio reTerminal DM 开发一个适合课堂使用的作业日历系统：reTerminal DM 是一个由 Raspberry Pi CM4 驱动的 10.1 英寸触摸屏人机界面（HMI），具有 IP65 正面防护、Wi-Fi/蓝牙以及可扩展的连接能力。该设备将让教师通过自然地讲粤语、普通话或英语来添加作业，而学生无需使用手机即可查看或听到即将到来的截止日期。\n1）优先技能\n用于日历界面和用户工作流的软件开发\n使用 Raspberry Pi CM4、GPIO 按钮、音频外设和 Wi-Fi 连接的嵌入式/HMI 开发\n与 Perplexity 和 DeepSeek 的 AI API 集成，包括提示词设计、多语言路由以及简短、结构化的 AI 回复\n语音技术集成：粤语、普通话和英语的语音转文字，以及用于朗读作业截止日期的文字转语音\n面向学校环境的以用户为中心的界面设计和数据处理，包括教师/学生权限、日历数据校验和注重隐私的账户管理\n2）预期成果\n一个功能完善、可壁挂的 AI 作业日历终端，使用 reTerminal DM / XIAO 7.5 英寸，并配有一个简单的教师/学生界面\n一个专用的 AI 语音提示按钮：教师按一次，用粤语、普通话或英语说出作业指令，设备即可转写、提取关键信息并创建一条日历条目\n一个简单的物理日历查看按钮：第一次按下显示每周作业日历；再次按下切换到每月日历；继续按下可返回当日显示或待机显示\n学生访问功能：学生按下一个按钮即可浏览截止日期、在屏幕上查看任务详情（周视图和月视图），或使用语音输出来收听最近的截止日期，例如：“科学练习册本周五上午 8:00 到期。”\n完整的原型文档：系统架构、用户流程设计、源代码、AI 提示词、数据模型、隐私方案、测试结果、物料清单、安装指南和演示视频\n3）现场频率\n每周 1 个现场工作日，用于环境搭建、按钮/音频集成、AI 接口测试以及课堂情境下的可用性试验","cats":["AI","CS","DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-7","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI 健身与健康镜","titleEn":"AI Fitness &amp; Wellness Mirror","areaZh":"设计与制造；AI 大语言模型；Token 与 API 编程；LoRA","areaEn":"Design and Manufacture; AI LLM; token and API programming; Lora","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 21:07:02","descriptionText":"This project develops a multi-purpose, school-based smart fitness mirror inspired by MAGIC AI and FITURE. It will use an AI camera for exercise form feedback, height estimation and body-composition assessment, alongside connected weight and body-fat measurement, to support PE lessons, fitness education, wellbeing activities and student progress tracking. MAGIC promotes real-time form correction, rep counting, weight guidance and body-composition progress analytics, while FITURE uses camera-based motion tracking to provide rep counts and form feedback without retaining photos or videos.\n1) Preferred skills\nComputer vision and AI development for human pose estimation, exercise recognition, rep counting, posture/form feedback and height estimation\nEmbedded systems and hardware integration, including camera modules, display/mirror interfaces, load cells or Bluetooth scales, body-composition devices, speakers and wireless connectivity\n2) Expected outcomes\nA functional AI School Fitness & Wellness Mirror that can guide selected exercises—such as squats, lunges, jumping jacks, planks, stretching or warm-up routines—and provide live rep counting and simple posture feedback\nAI-camera capability to estimate height and track body posture or movement, using privacy-preserving processing where possible; raw camera footage should not be stored by default\nIntegration with a connected weight scale and a suitable body-composition measurement method to display weight, estimated body-fat percentage and trend data, subject to device accuracy limits and school approval\nLink to Youtube and VPN.\nA complete prototype package: mirror/enclosure design, system architecture, UI design, source code, camera and sensor integration, data/privacy policy, risk assessment, testing results, user guide and final demonstration\n3) Onsite frequency\n1 onsite day per week for smart-mirror installation, camera positioning, scale/body-composition integration, exercise trials and controlled usability testing","descZh":"本项目开发一款多用途、面向学校的智能健身镜，灵感来自 MAGIC AI 与 FITURE。镜子将使用 AI 相机进行运动姿态反馈、身高估算与身体成分评估，并连接体重与体脂测量设备，支持体育课、健身教育、健康活动与学生进度跟踪。MAGIC 倡导实时姿态纠正、次数统计、重量指导与身体成分进度分析；FITURE 则使用基于相机的运动跟踪提供次数与姿态反馈，且不保存照片或视频。\n1) 偏好技能：用于人体姿态估计、运动识别、次数统计、姿态/形式反馈与身高估算的计算机视觉与 AI 开发；嵌入式系统与硬件集成，包括相机模块、显示/镜面接口、称重传感器或蓝牙秤、身体成分设备、扬声器与无线连接。\n2) 预期成果：可工作的 AI 学校健身与健康镜，可指导选定练习（如下蹲、弓步、开合跳、平板支撑、拉伸或热身动作），并提供实时次数统计与简单姿态反馈；AI 相机能力可估算身高并跟踪身体姿态或运动，尽量使用隐私保护处理；默认不保存原始相机影像；集成连接的体重秤与合适的身体成分测量方法，显示体重、估算体脂率与趋势数据（受设备精度限制与学校批准）；连接 YouTube 与 VPN（原文如此）；完整原型包：镜面/外壳设计、系统架构、UI 设计、源代码、相机与传感器集成、数据/隐私政策、风险评估、测试结果、用户指南与最终演示。\n3) 现场频率：每周 1 天现场，用于智能镜安装、相机定位、秤/身体成分集成、运动试验与受控可用性测试。","cats":["AI","CS","DES","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-8","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"自助珍珠奶茶站","titleEn":"Self-Service Bubble Milk Tea Station","areaZh":"设计与制造；材料与固体；热流体","areaEn":"Design and Manufacture; materials and solids; Thermofluids","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 21:07:02","descriptionText":"This project develops a compact, self-service bubble milk tea station inspired by automated beverage kiosks. The machine will accept QR-code payment, print a QR sticker for the paper cup, scan the cup to retrieve the selected order, and automatically dispense tea, sugar and milk according to the recipe.\n1) Preferred skills\nMechanical design and CAD modelling for a compact food-service machine, including a 150 cm high × 40 cm wide × 60 cm deep enclosure, cup area, dispensing system, fridge compartment and maintenance access\nMechatronics and automation, including pumps, solenoid valves, flow meters, motors, mixers, liquid-level sensors, cup sensors, QR scanners and thermal-control systems\nEmbedded systems and software development for QR payment workflow, order database, sticker printing, recipe management, touchscreen/HMI interface and machine-status monitoring\nSafety, testing and product-design skills for electrical protection, water/liquid management, emergency stop, user experience, refill workflow and fault diagnosis\n2) Expected outcomes\nA functional self-service beverage station with a maximum target size of 150 cm (H) × 40 cm (W) × 60 cm (D), suitable for a school café, maker space or event environment\nQR-payment workflow: customers scan a payment QR code, select a beverage, and receive an order-specific QR-code sticker to attach to a paper cup\nQR-based cup verification: users place the labelled cup in the dispensing zone, the system scans the sticker, confirms the order and automatically starts the correct recipe\nA chilled lower compartment containing eight ingredient wells: six wells for tea bases/flavours, one for sugar syrup and one for milk; the upper section contains the dispensing assembly, QR scanner and cup-placement area\nA complete prototype package: CAD files, system layout, food-safe material specification, wiring and plumbing diagrams, source code, bill of materials, cleaning/maintenance plan, hazard analysis, user instructions, test results and final demonstration\n3) Onsite frequency\nOne onsite day (3hr) per week for enclosure fabrication, pump/valve installation, fridge/well integration, plumbing, electrical assembly and dispensing tests","descZh":"本项目开发一款紧凑型自助珍珠奶茶站，其灵感来自自动化饮料售货亭。该机器将接受二维码支付，为纸杯打印二维码贴纸，扫描纸杯以调取所选订单，并按照配方自动出茶、糖与奶。\n1) 优先技能\n针对紧凑型餐饮服务机械的机械设计与 CAD 建模，包括一个 150 cm 高 × 40 cm 宽 × 60 cm 深的机柜、杯区、出料系统、冷藏隔间与维护通道\n机电一体化与自动化，包括泵、电磁阀、流量计、电机、搅拌器、液位传感器、杯检测传感器、二维码扫描器与温控系统\n用于二维码支付流程、订单数据库、贴纸打印、配方管理、触摸屏/HMI 界面及机器状态监控的嵌入式系统与软件开发\n面向电气保护、水/液体管理、急停、用户体验、补料流程与故障诊断的安全、测试与产品设计技能\n2) 预期成果\n一台功能完整的自助饮品站，最大目标尺寸为 150 cm（高）× 40 cm（宽）× 60 cm（深），适用于学校咖啡厅、创客空间或活动场景\n二维码支付流程：顾客扫描付款二维码，选择饮品，并获得一张订单专属的二维码贴纸贴在纸杯上\n基于二维码的纸杯核验：用户将贴好标签的纸杯放入出料区，系统扫描贴纸、确认订单并自动启动对应配方\n一个冷藏的下层隔间，包含八个原料槽：六个用于茶底/风味，一个用于糖浆，一个用于牛奶；上层部分包含出料组件、二维码扫描器与放杯区\n一套完整的原型交付包：CAD 文件、系统布局、食品级材料规格、接线与管路图、源代码、物料清单、清洁/维护计划、危害分析、用户说明、测试结果与最终演示\n3) 现场频次\n每周一个现场工作日（3 小时），用于机柜制作、泵/阀安装、冷藏/原料槽集成、管路铺设、电气装配与出料测试","cats":["DES","ENE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-9","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"带机械臂的火星自主巡视车 3.0","titleEn":"Autonomous Mars Rover With Robotic Arm 3.0","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人与控制","areaEn":"Design and Manufacture; Robotics and control","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 21:07:02","descriptionText":"This project develops an educational Mars-rover prototype with six wheels and a robotic arm, designed to explore analogue Mars terrain. The rover will combine autonomous navigation, terrain awareness, obstacle avoidance, wheel-slip correction, rock selection and manual operator control to demonstrate robotics, mechanical engineering, sensing and planetary-exploration concepts.\n1) Preferred skills\nMechanical design and CAD modelling for a six-wheel rover chassis, suspension/rocker-bogie mechanism, drivetrain, wheel mounting and robotic-arm integration\nRobotics and embedded systems, including motor drivers, wheel encoders, IMUs, depth cameras or LiDAR, microcontrollers, single-board computers and power management\nProgramming in Python/C++ and familiarity with ROS/ROS 2, sensor fusion, computer vision, navigation stacks and real-time robot control\nAI/computer vision skills for terrain classification, obstacle/stone detection, traversability scoring, object localisation and robotic-arm target guidance\nSafety-focused testing and data analysis, including emergency stop systems, manual override, mission logging, rover performance metrics and structured field trials\n2) Expected outcomes\nA functional six-wheel Mars-rover prototype with a robotic arm, able to operate on an indoor or outdoor Mars-analogue terrain course containing rocks, sand, slopes, loose soil and obstacles\nIntegrated wheel encoders, IMU and depth/LiDAR sensing for estimating speed, orientation, position, wheel movement and nearby terrain geometry\nAutonomous terrain classification, obstacle detection, traversability scoring and route planning, including automatic re-planning when the planned route becomes blocked or unsafe\nWheel-slip detection and correction using encoder/IMU/sensor data, enabling the rover to adjust heading, speed or route when travelling on uneven or low-traction terrain\nStone detection and robotic-arm target guidance, allowing the system to identify selected rocks, estimate their position and guide the arm near the target; fully automatic grasping is a stretch goal\nManual remote-control override, physical emergency stop, safe-stop behaviour, mission logs and a complete technical package including CAD files, system diagrams, source code, test data, risk assessment, user guide and final demonstration\n3) Onsite frequency\nOne onsite day (3hr) per week for chassis/arm assembly, sensor mounting, drivetrain calibration, terrain-course setup and supervised rover trials","descZh":"本项目开发一个教育用途的火星车（Mars-rover）原型，配备六个轮子和一个机械臂，旨在探索火星模拟地形。该火星车将结合自主导航、地形感知、避障、车轮打滑纠正、岩石选择以及人工操作员控制，以展示机器人学、机械工程、传感和行星探索等概念。\n1）优先技能\n机械设计和 CAD 建模，用于六轮火星车底盘、悬挂/摇臂-转向架（rocker-bogie）机构、传动系统、车轮安装和机械臂集成\n机器人学与嵌入式系统，包括电机驱动器、车轮编码器、IMU、深度相机或 LiDAR、微控制器、单板计算机和电源管理\nPython/C++ 编程，并熟悉 ROS/ROS 2、传感器融合、计算机视觉、导航栈和实时机器人控制\nAI/计算机视觉技能，用于地形分类、障碍物/石块检测、可通行性评分、物体定位和机械臂目标引导\n注重安全的测试与数据分析，包括急停系统、人工接管、任务日志、火星车性能指标和结构化的实地试验\n2）预期成果\n一个功能完善的六轮火星车原型，配备机械臂，能够在包含岩石、沙地、斜坡、松散土壤和障碍物的室内或室外火星模拟地形赛道上运行\n集成的车轮编码器、IMU 和深度/LiDAR 传感，用于估算速度、朝向、位置、车轮运动以及附近地形几何\n自主地形分类、障碍物检测、可通行性评分和路径规划，包括在规划路线被阻断或不安全时自动重新规划\n使用编码器/IMU/传感器数据进行车轮打滑检测与纠正，使火星车在不平或低附着力的地形上行驶时能够调整航向、速度或路线\n石块检测和机械臂目标引导，使系统能够识别选定的岩石、估算其位置并将机械臂引导至目标附近；全自动抓取是一个进阶目标（stretch goal）\n人工遥控接管、实体急停、安全停止行为、任务日志，以及一套完整的技术资料包，包括 CAD 文件、系统图、源代码、测试数据、风险评估、用户指南和最终演示\n3）现场频率\n每周一个现场工作日（3 小时），用于底盘/机械臂装配、传感器安装、传动系统标定、地形赛道搭建以及有人监督的火星车试验","cats":["DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-10","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"Earth Refill 3.0 洗涤剂自助加注站","titleEn":"Earth Refill 3.0 Detergent Kiosk","areaZh":"设计与制造；液体；UI/UX；支付系统","areaEn":"Design and Manufacture; Liquid; UIUX; Payment system","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 21:07:02","descriptionText":"This project develops Earth Refill 3.0 as an automated detergent-refill kiosk that promotes circular economy, reuse and refill, and IoT monitoring. It builds on Earth Refill 1.0’s liquid-to-liquid, four-SKU design and Earth Refill 2.0’s solid-to-liquid approach, in which detergent tablets dissolve in RO water and are stored as ready-to-dispense liquid. Earth Refill 1.0 documentation describes four automated liquid taps, 20 L × 4 liquid storage, contactless operation and a target refill speed of 1 L in five seconds. 3.0 is the combination of 1.0 and 2.0\n1) Preferred skills\nMechanical design and CAD modelling for a compact kiosk, including liquid tanks, reaction/mixing tank, dispensing zone, bottle interface, drip tray, service doors and maintenance access\nFluid and process engineering, including pumps, valves, flow meters, liquid-level sensing, mixing, dissolution, filtration, RO-water integration and dosing accuracy\nEmbedded systems and IoT development for sensor monitoring, touchscreen/HMI operation, QR/RFID bottle identification, payment or reward workflow and cloud data logging\nSustainability and circular-economy analysis, including plastic-waste reduction, refill-use tracking, and ESG reporting\nSafety and hygienic-system design, including chemical compatibility, leak prevention, containment, cleaning, user protection, emergency stop and fault detection\n2) Expected outcomes\nA functional Earth Refill 3.0 educational kiosk that enables users to refill a reusable 1 L bottle with detergent in a target time of approximately 5 seconds\nA configurable dispensing architecture that demonstrates both:\nLiquid-to-liquid refill: four selectable detergent/cleaning-product SKUs, based on Earth Refill 1.0\nSolid-to-liquid refill: detergent tablets or concentrates dissolved in RO water within a controlled reaction/mixing tank, producing a ready-to-dispense liquid inventory, based on Earth Refill 2.0\nA monitored reaction-tank subsystem that controls water volume, tablet/concentrate addition, mixing time, liquid level, temperature where required, and readiness for dispensing; target storage capacity is approximately 5 L for the solid-to-liquid demonstrator\nA user interface that guides bottle placement, product selection, refill volume and dispensing; it should display live sustainability data such as number of refills, bottles avoided, estimated plastic reduction, water used and machine status\n3) Onsite frequency\nOne onsite day (3hr) per week for kiosk fabrication, tank/pump/valve installation, plumbing, sensor integration, reaction-tank testing and supervised dispensing trials","descZh":"本项目将 Earth Refill 3.0 开发为一款自动洗涤剂补充（refill）自助终端，倡导循环经济、再利用与再填充以及物联网监测。它建立在 Earth Refill 1.0 的液-液四品类设计与 Earth Refill 2.0 的固-液方案（洗涤剂片溶于 RO 水并储存为即用液体）基础上。Earth Refill 1.0 文档描述了四个自动液体出口、20L×4 液体储存、无接触操作以及 5 秒补充 1L 的目标补充速度。3.0 是 1.0 与 2.0 的结合。\n1) 偏好技能：面向紧凑终端（含液罐、反应/混合罐、分配区、瓶接口、滴盘、检修门与维护通道）的机械设计与 CAD 建模；流体与工艺工程，包括泵、阀、流量计、液位传感、混合、溶解、过滤、RO 水集成与计量精度；面向传感器监测、触摸屏/HMI 操作、QR/RFID 瓶识别、支付或奖励流程与云数据记录的嵌入式系统与物联网开发；可持续与循环经济分析，包括塑料废弃物减少、再利用跟踪与 ESG 报告；安全与卫生系统设计，包括化学相容性、防漏、围护、清洁、用户保护、急停与故障检测。\n2) 预期成果：可工作的 Earth Refill 3.0 教学终端，用户可在约 5 秒的目标时间内用洗涤剂补充可重复使用的 1L 瓶；可配置的分配架构同时演示两种模式：液-液补充——四种可选洗涤剂/清洁产品 SKU（基于 Earth Refill 1.0）；固-液补充——洗涤剂片或浓缩物在受控反应/混合罐内溶于 RO 水，生成即用液体库存（基于 Earth Refill 2.0）；受监测的反应罐子系统，控制水量、片剂/浓缩物添加、混合时间、液位、所需温度与分配就绪状态；固-液演示器目标储存容量约 5L；引导瓶放置、产品选择、补充量与分配的用户界面，实时显示补充次数、避免使用的瓶数、估算塑料减量、用水量与机器状态等可持续数据。\n3) 现场频率：每周 1 天现场（3 小时），用于终端制造、罐/泵/阀安装、管路、传感器集成、反应罐测试与受监督的补充试验。","cats":["CS","DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-11","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"天花板与窗户清洁无人机 2.0","titleEn":"Ceiling and Window Cleaning Drone 2.0","areaZh":"设计与制造；低空技术；无人机","areaEn":"Design and Manufacture; LAT; Drone","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This project develops a small-scale, safety-first drone cleaning demonstrator for commerical use. It will explore how aerial robotics can inspect and clean ceiling panels, high windows or transparent test surfaces, while teaching students about drone mechanics, flight control, sensing, fluid delivery, automation and building-maintenance safety. Research prototypes commonly use tethered ground-based water systems so the aircraft carries only the nozzle rather than a heavy water tank.\n1) Preferred skills\nMechanical design and CAD modelling for a lightweight drone frame, propeller guards, cleaning head, nozzle/wiper mechanism, tether attachment and protected test enclosure\nUAV and robotics engineering, including flight controllers, motor/ESC selection, PID tuning, stabilisation, indoor positioning, depth sensing and remote control\nFluid-system integration, including low-pressure water delivery, pumps, valves, hoses, flow control, leak prevention and water-recovery/drip containment\nComputer vision and autonomy for surface detection, window/ceiling-edge recognition, cleaning-path planning, obstacle avoidance and coverage tracking\n2) Expected outcomes\nA functional small indoor cleaning-drone demonstrator able to approach a vertical window panel or horizontal/near-horizontal ceiling test panel in a controlled test area (Aluminum profile cage H180cm x W120cm x D180cm)\nA protected cleaning mechanism using a low-pressure mist, soft microfibre pad, squeegee or non-contact air/water test method; the project should prioritise cleaning-path research and safety over high-pressure cleaning performance\nCamera/depth-sensor capability to identify the target surface, detect edges and obstacles, estimate surface distance, and maintain a safe operating stand-off distance\nSemi-autonomous cleaning coverage: the operator selects a test area, while the drone follows a planned grid or waypoint path, records the area covered and pauses/re-plans when an obstacle or boundary is detected\n3) Onsite frequency\nOne onsite day (3hr) per week for frame and cleaning-head fabrication, propulsion testing, tether/fluid integration, sensor mounting and supervised flights in a safety enclosure","descZh":"本项目开发一个小型、安全优先的商用无人机清洁演示装置。它将探索空中机器人如何检测并清洁天花板面板、高位窗户或透明测试表面，同时让学生学习无人机机械、飞控、传感、流体输送、自动化与楼宇维护安全。研究型原型通常采用系留式地面供水系统，使飞行器只携带喷嘴而非沉重水箱。\n1) 偏好技能：面向轻量无人机机架、桨叶护罩、清洁头、喷嘴/刮水机构、系绳连接与受保护测试外壳的机械设计与 CAD 建模；无人机与机器人工程，包括飞控、电机/ESC 选型、PID 调参、增稳、室内定位、深度感知与遥控；流体系统集成，包括低压供水、泵、阀、软管、流量控制、防漏与水回收/滴水收纳；面向表面检测、窗/天花板边缘识别、清洁路径规划、避障与覆盖跟踪的计算机视觉与自主能力。\n2) 预期成果：可在受控测试区（铝型材笼 H180cm × W120cm × D180cm）接近垂直窗面板或水平/近水平天花板测试面板的功能性小型室内清洁无人机演示装置；采用低压水雾、软微纤维垫、刮水器或非接触空气/水测试方法的受保护清洁机构；项目应优先研究清洁路径与安全性，而非高压清洁性能；具备识别目标表面、检测边缘与障碍物、估算表面距离并保持安全工作距离的相机/深度传感器能力；半自主清洁覆盖：操作员选择测试区域，无人机沿规划的网格或航点路径飞行、记录已覆盖区域，并在检测到障碍物或边界时暂停/重新规划。\n3) 现场频率：每周 1 天现场（3 小时），用于机架与清洁头制作、推进测试、系绳/流体集成、传感器安装及在安全笼内的受监督飞行。","cats":["AERO","DES","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-12","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"ASTRON AI —— 便携式立方星/罐星地面站控制台与卫星","titleEn":"ASTRON AI - Portable CubeSat/CanSat Ground Station Console and Satellite","areaZh":"设计与制造；航空航天工程；低空技术","areaEn":"Design and Manufacture; Aerospace engineering; LAT","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This project develops a laptop-sized satellite mission console integrating a modular CubeSat/CanSat payload, software-defined radio (SDR) ground station, telemetry dashboard and AI-assisted monitoring. Students will use the system to learn end-to-end small-satellite operations: collecting sensor data, transmitting telemetry, receiving and decoding signals, monitoring mission health, logging data and responding to simulated anomalies.\nGround Station as a Service (GSaaS) provides managed, on-demand access to external satellite ground-station networks, allowing satellite operators to communicate with spacecraft, downlink data and process it without building their own global antenna network. It commonly operates on a pay-per-use basis and can connect data to cloud services.\n1) Preferred skills\nEmbedded systems and payload integration, including microcontrollers, CubeSat/CanSat interfaces, environmental sensors, GPS/GNSS, IMUs, cameras and power-management systems\nRF communications and SDR knowledge, including antenna selection, UHF/VHF or permitted ISM-band links, modulation, packet protocols, Doppler concepts, GNU Radio and signal decoding\nMechanical/product design for a laptop-sized rugged console, including modular payload compartments, antenna mounts, cable management, cooling, battery integration and safe transport\nSoftware and dashboard development using Python, JavaScript or similar tools for live telemetry visualisation, mission timelines, command interfaces, data logging and alarms\nAI/data-analysis skills for anomaly detection, telemetry summarisation, predictive alerts, sensor-pattern analysis and natural-language mission reporting\n2) Expected outcomes\nA functional portable ground-station console in a laptop-sized enclosure, incorporating an SDR, compact antenna solution, computing unit, display/dashboard, battery/power system and modular connections for CubeSat/CanSat payload testing\nA modular CubeSat/CanSat-style payload that gathers and transmits selected mission data, such as temperature, pressure, humidity, light level, IMU motion, GPS location, battery voltage/current and optional camera or environmental sensor data\nA radio/SDR communications workflow capable of receiving, decoding, displaying and saving telemetry packets, with signal-strength, packet-loss and link-status indicators\nA live mission-control dashboard showing telemetry trends, map/location where applicable, payload health, communications status, alerts, mission timeline and exportable data logs\nAI telemetry monitoring that detects abnormal sensor values, unexpected power consumption, communications loss, unusual motion or thermal trends, and generates concise operator alerts and post-mission summaries\nA GSaaS integration concept or software connector that demonstrates how a future CubeSat could schedule contacts, receive downlinked telemetry or ingest externally received data through a cloud ground-station service; NASA describes GSaaS as an approach that provides managed spacecraft communications, data downlink and processing without users building their own ground stations.\nA complete package including enclosure CAD files, electrical/RF block diagrams, payload interface specification, source code, dashboard design, AI-monitoring logic, data schema, operating manual, risk assessment, test results and final demonstration\n3) Onsite frequency\nOne onsite day (3hr) per week for console fabrication, SDR/antenna assembly, payload integration, radio-link testing, dashboard validation and supervised mission simulations","descZh":"本项目开发一台笔记本大小的卫星任务控制台，集成模块化 CubeSat/CanSat 有效载荷、软件定义无线电（SDR）地面站、遥测仪表板与 AI 辅助监测。学生将使用该系统学习小卫星端到端运行：采集传感器数据、发射遥测、接收与解码信号、监测任务健康、记录数据并响应模拟异常。\n\"地面站即服务\"（GSaaS）提供对外部卫星地面站网络的受管、按需访问，使卫星运营商无需自建全球天线网络即可与航天器通信、下行数据并加以处理。其通常按使用量付费，并可连接云服务处理数据。\n1) 偏好技能：嵌入式系统与有效载荷集成，包括微控制器、CubeSat/CanSat 接口、环境传感器、GPS/GNSS、IMU、相机与电源管理系统；RF 通信与 SDR 知识，包括天线选择、UHF/VHF 或允许的 ISM 频段链路、调制、分组协议、多普勒概念、GNU Radio 与信号解码；面向笔记本大小坚固控制台的机械/产品设计，包括模块化有效载荷舱、天线支架、线缆管理、散热、电池集成与安全运输；使用 Python、JavaScript 或类似工具进行软件与仪表板开发，实现实时遥测可视化、任务时间线、指令接口、数据记录与告警；用于异常检测、遥测摘要、预测性告警、传感器模式分析与自然语言任务报告的 AI/数据分析技能。\n2) 预期成果：笔记本大小外壳内的可工作便携地面站控制台，集成 SDR、紧凑天线方案、计算单元、显示/仪表板、电池/电源系统，以及用于 CubeSat/CanSat 有效载荷测试的模块化连接；可采集并发射温度、压力、湿度、光照、IMU 运动、GPS 位置、电池电压/电流及可选相机或环境传感器数据等选定任务数据的模块化 CubeSat/CanSat 式有效载荷；能够接收、解码、显示与保存遥测分组，带信号强度、丢包率与链路状态指示的无线电/SDR 通信工作流；显示遥测趋势、适用位置地图、有效载荷健康、通信状态、告警、任务时间线与可导出数据日志的实时任务控制仪表板；检测异常传感器值、意外功耗、通信丢失、异常运动或热趋势并生成简洁操作员告警与任务后摘要的 AI 遥测监测；一个 GSaaS 集成概念或软件连接器，演示未来 CubeSat 如何通过云地面站服务安排接触、接收下行遥测或摄取外部接收数据；NASA 将 GSaaS 描述为无需用户自建地面站即可获得受管航天器通信、数据下行与处理的方法；完整包：外壳 CAD 文件、电气/RF 框图、有效载荷接口规范、源代码、仪表板设计、AI 监测逻辑、数据模式、操作手册、风险评估、测试结果与最终演示。\n3) 现场频率：每周 1 天现场（3 小时），用于控制台制造、SDR/天线组装、有效载荷集成、无线电链路测试、仪表板验证与受监督任务仿真。","cats":["AERO","DES","OTH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-13","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"Carbonovo Tree：模块化淡水植物墙","titleEn":"Carbonovo Tree : Modular Freshwater Plant Wall","areaZh":"设计与制造；自然与建成环境；ESG 报告","areaEn":"Design and Manufacture; natural and built environment; ESG reporting","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 21:07:02","descriptionText":"Text\nFieldName: description3\nFieldFlags: 4098","descZh":"（该条目在原站无有效说明正文，仅有空占位内容，无可用文本可译。）","cats":["CS","DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-KTT-14","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"面向 100 平方英尺房间的便携式陶土空调","titleEn":"Portalbe Terracotta air-conditioner for a 100 sq ft Room","areaZh":"设计与制造；热流体；能源工程","areaEn":"Design and manfacture; thermofluids; energy engineering","companyZh":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companyEn":"KTT (Kolour Think Tank)","companySupervisor":"Kitty Tang","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-18 21:07:02","descriptionText":"For a 100 sq ft room, the project can develop a terracotta-and-water evaporative cooler as a monitored, local cooling demonstrator. —not as a full replacement for conventional air conditioning, particularly in Hong Kong’s humid climate.\n1) Preferred skills\nMechanical and thermal design for a compact terracotta cooling wall, panel array or ducted desktop/floor unit sized for a 100 sq ft room to maintain 26oC.\nCAD modelling and fabrication of unglazed terracotta tubes, porous tiles or clay modules, structural frames, water reservoirs, drip systems and drainage\nHeat-transfer, airflow and psychrometric analysis, including temperature, relative humidity, wet-bulb temperature, evaporation rate and thermal-comfort assessment\nEmbedded systems and IoT monitoring for temperature, humidity, water level, water flow, terracotta surface temperature, fan power and environmental data logging\nExperimental design and data analysis for comparing the wet terracotta system, dry terracotta system and baseline room conditions\n2) Expected outcomes\nA functional terracotta evaporative-cooling prototype designed to serve a 100 sq ft, ideally near an open window or dedicated exhaust path\nA modular unglazed-terracotta cooling panel or tube bank, with a target wet surface area of approximately 1–2 m² for the initial prototype; water should reach the clay by gravity feed, capillary wicking or a low-flow recirculation system\nA low-energy airflow system using a variable-speed fan. For a typical 8–10 ft ceiling, a 100 sq ft room has about 800–1,000 ft³ of volume; a conventional evaporative-cooler sizing rule of 20–30 air changes per hour implies roughly 270–500 CFM of airflow. This is a reference test range, not a guarantee of comfort in humid conditions.\nA monitored dashboard recording room inlet/outlet temperature, relative humidity, wet-bulb estimate, terracotta surface temperature, water flow/level, water consumption, fan power and estimated cooling effect\nComparative trials that test different fan speeds, air paths, water rates, terracotta geometry, shading and ventilation conditions, with results reported as measured temperature/humidity changes rather than assumed cooling capacity\nComplete documentation: CAD design, thermal/airflow calculations, water-system diagram, sensor/wiring design, bill of materials, maintenance protocol, water-use analysis, safety/risk assessment, experiment data, dashboard and final demonstration\n3) Onsite frequency\nOne onsite day (3hr) per week for terracotta-module assembly, water-system installation, fan and duct integration, sensor calibration and controlled room testing","descZh":"针对 100 平方英尺的房间，项目可开发一台陶土-水蒸发冷却器，作为受监测的局部降温演示装置——而非常规空调的完全替代品，尤其在香港潮湿气候下。\n1) 偏好技能：面向为维持 26℃ 的 100 平方英尺房间定制的紧凑陶土冷却墙、面板阵列或风管台式/落地式单元进行机械与热设计；无釉陶土管、多孔砖或黏土模块、结构框架、储水器、滴灌系统与排水的 CAD 建模与制造；传热、气流与湿空气分析，包括温度、相对湿度、湿球温度、蒸发速率与热舒适评估；面向温度、湿度、水位、水流、陶土表面温度、风扇功率与环境数据记录的嵌入式系统与物联网监测；用于比较湿陶土系统、干陶土系统与基线房间条件的实验设计与数据分析。\n2) 预期成果：设计用于 100 平方英尺房间（理想情况下靠近开窗或专用排风路径）的功能性陶土蒸发冷却原型；模块化无釉陶土冷却面板或管束，初始原型目标湿表面积约 1~2 平方米；水可通过重力供水、毛细作用或低流量循环系统到达黏土；采用变速风扇的低能耗气流系统。对于典型的 8~10 英尺天花板，100 平方英尺房间体积约 800~1,000 立方英尺；常规蒸发冷却器按每小时 20~30 次换气的选型规则约需 270~500 CFM 的气流。这是参考测试区间，而非潮湿条件下的舒适保证；监测仪表板记录房间进出口温度、相对湿度、湿球估算、陶土表面温度、水流/水位、用水量、风扇功率与估算冷却效果；对比试验测试不同风扇速度、风道、水流量、陶土几何、遮阳与通风条件，结果以实测温度/湿度变化而非假定冷却能力报告；完整文档：CAD 设计、热/气流计算、水系统图、传感器/接线设计、物料清单、维护规程、用水分析、安全/风险评估、实验数据、仪表板与最终演示。\n3) 现场频率：每周 1 天现场（3 小时），用于陶土模块组装、水系统安装、风扇与风道集成、传感器标定与受控房间测试。","cats":["CS","DES","ENE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-X-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"轨道式巡检机器人（RMIR）断电制动失效风险分析与被动制动改造方案研究","titleEn":"Risk Analysis of Power-off Brake Failure and Research on Passive Braking Retrofitting Scheme for Rail-mounted Inspection Robots(RMIR)","areaZh":"设计与制造；工程管理；机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Design and manufacture; Engineering management; Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"湖南中融汇智信息技术有限公司","companyEn":"Hunan Zhongrong Huizhi Information Technology Co., Ltd.","companySupervisor":"Dr. Lei Yang","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":6,"availCap":6,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"RMIRs operate in dusty, humid complex environments like factories, tunnels and equipment rooms, whose stable safe operation is vital for automated inspection and equipment maintenance. Most of these robots lack built-in power-off braking motors, and their traditional electromagnetic brakes rely entirely on power supply. Once power loss occurs due to dead batteries, circuit faults or momentary blackouts, the brakes will fail completely. The robot may slide, jam on rails, collide or even fall, damaging equipment, suspending inspection tasks and triggering severe on-site safety risks.\nObjectives:\nAnalyze power-off braking failure risks of RMIRs.\nExplore the drawbacks of power-dependent electromagnetic braking systems.\nDesign and integrate auxiliary power-off braking functions for RMIR.\nEnhance operational safety and stability of RMIR braking systems.\nResearch Content (can be part of them)：\nInvestigate the structural composition and working principle of traditional electromagnetic braking systems.\nIdentify core causes of braking failure under power-off conditions for RMIRs.\nSimulate typical power-loss working conditions including battery exhaustion and circuit faults.\nMonitor key operating parameters and assess safety risks under different failure scenarios.\nAnalyze environmental impacts of dust, humidity and temperature changes on braking devices.\nSummarize environmental adaptability requirements for RMIR braking systems.\nDetermine structural and installation constraints of the RMIR braking device.\nPrototype and assemble the designed power-off braking device.\nTest the braking performance and durability under simulated harsh working conditions.\nExpected Outcomes: (can be part of them)\nClarify potential safety risks of traditional electromagnetic braking devices.\nReveal the failure mechanism of conventional braking systems for RMIRs under power-off conditions.\nEvaluate operational safety risks and clarify environmental protection performance standards.\nPropose a feasible structural scheme of auxiliary power-off braking for RMIRs.\nSummarize design constraints and technical indicators for miniaturized braking devices.\nFormulate systematic risk analysis reports and design basis documents.\nPrototype, assemble and verify the designed power-off braking device.\nValidate practical braking performance and operational reliability of the modified system.\nRequired Skills:\nGrasp basic principles of electromechanical systems and braking structures.\nMaster general mechanical failure analysis and risk evaluation methods.\nPossess fundamental electrical knowledge and independent mechanical structure design & optimization capabilities.\nMaster basic simulation, performance testing and reliability verification methods.\nUnderstand conventional environmental adaptability and electrical protection standards for electromechanical devices.\nConduct prototype assembly, electrical debugging, data processing and experimental result analysis.\nOnsite Requirement:\nAll research and validation tasks are conducted in laboratory environments, requiring no on-site stationing.","descZh":"轨道式检查机器人（RMIR）在工厂、隧道与设备机房等粉尘大、潮湿的复杂环境中运行，其稳定安全运行对自动化检查与设备维护至关重要。此类机器人大多缺少内置断电制动电机，其传统电磁制动完全依赖电源。一旦因电池耗尽、电路故障或瞬时停电发生断电，制动器将完全失效，机器人可能滑移、卡轨、碰撞甚至坠落，损坏设备、中断检查任务并引发严重现场安全风险。\n目标：\n- 分析 RMIR 的断电制动失效风险；\n- 探索依赖电源的电磁制动系统的缺陷；\n- 设计并集成 RMIR 的辅助断电制动功能；\n- 提升 RMIR 制动系统的运行安全性与稳定性。\n研究内容（可为其中部分）：\n- 研究传统电磁制动系统的结构组成与工作原理；\n- 识别 RMIR 断电工况下制动失效的核心原因；\n- 仿真电池耗尽与电路故障等典型断电工况；\n- 监测关键运行参数，评估不同失效场景下的安全风险；\n- 分析粉尘、湿度与温度变化对制动装置的环境影响；\n- 总结 RMIR 制动系统的环境适应性要求；\n- 确定 RMIR 制动装置的结构与安装约束；\n- 原型制作并组装所设计的断电制动装置；\n- 在模拟严苛工况下测试制动性能与耐久性。\n预期成果（可为其中部分）：\n- 阐明传统电磁制动装置的潜在安全风险；\n- 揭示 RMIR 常规制动系统在断电工况下的失效机理；\n- 评估运行安全风险并明确环境保护性能标准；\n- 提出 RMIR 辅助断电制动的可行结构方案；\n- 总结小型化制动装置的设计约束与技术指标；\n- 形成系统性风险分析报告与设计依据文件；\n- 原型制作、组装并验证所设计的断电制动装置；\n- 验证改进后系统的实际制动性能与运行可靠性。\n所需技能：掌握机电系统与制动结构基本原理；掌握一般机械失效分析与风险评估方法；具备基础电气知识及独立机械结构设计与优化能力；掌握基础仿真、性能测试与可靠性验证方法；了解机电设备的常规环境适应性与电气防护标准；能进行原型装配、电气调试、数据处理与实验结果分析。\n现场要求：所有研究与验证任务均在实验室环境开展，无需驻场。","cats":["AERO","DES","MGT","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-X-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"轨道式巡检机器人（RMIR）国标型材轨道适应性研究","titleEn":"Research on Adaptability of National Standard Profile Tracks for Rail-mounted Inspection Robots(RMIR)","areaZh":"设计与制造；工程管理；机器人；无人机与控制；能源工程","areaEn":"Design and manufacture; Engineering management; Robotics; drones and control; Energy engineering","companyZh":"湖南中融汇智信息技术有限公司","companyEn":"Hunan Zhongrong Huizhi Information Technology Co., Ltd.","companySupervisor":"Dr. Lei Yang","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":6,"availCap":6,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Most RMIR adopt custom rails featuring complex fabrication, high costs, long lead times, poor universality and inconvenient maintenance, which push up deployment costs and limit large-scale application in diverse scenarios; by contrast, general standard profiles with high standardization, easy procurement, cost efficiency, full specification ranges and mass producibility can fully address the defects of custom rails, so this research explores the matching and performance verification of various standard profile rails to cut RMIR’s dependence on dedicated custom rails and enhance the versatility, cost efficiency and engineering practicability of rail systems, where matched driving structures are custom-designed and multi-dimensional performance tests implemented to pick the optimal profile rail solution for RMIR working conditions and deliver technical support for low-cost, standardized and large-scale RMIR deployment.\nObjectives:\nInvestigate the feasibility of adopting general standard profiles as running rails for RMIR.\nDesign matched driving structures compatible with standard profiles.\nConduct multi-dimensional performance tests to screen the optimal rail solution under actual operating conditions.\nResearch Content (can be part of them):\nConduct research on rail selection, driving structure adaptation and prototype performance comparison for standardized rails of RMIR based on general standard profiles.\nSelect at least three general standard profiles with reference to RMIR self-weight, rated load, running speed and installation environment.\nDesign customized driving mechanisms matching each profile’s section dimensions and mechanical properties to realize full-condition stable operation.\nBuild a test platform to compare load capacity, climbing performance, turning flexibility and running stability, and summarize the advantages and limitations of each profile rail.\nExpected Outputs (can be part of them):\nSelect applicable profile rails and design supporting driving mechanisms for RMIR under specific working conditions.\nScreen at least three general standard profiles for inspection scenarios and optimize matched driving structures for each profile.\nObtain core performance data of load capacity, climbing and turning via comparative tests.\nForm a standardized test report by analyzing comprehensive adaptability and cost efficiency, and determine the optimal standard rail scheme to realize universal, low-cost and standardized application of RMIR rails.\nSkill Requirements:\nBasic understanding of mechanical structure design and profile characteristic analysis.\nAbility to conduct structural matching design and parameter optimization for mechanical driving mechanisms.\nCapable of building experimental schemes, performing mechanical performance tests and processing test data.\nFundamental knowledge of mechanical mechanics, operating condition analysis and engineering practicability evaluation.\nAbility to summarize experimental results and complete standardized research reports and optimal scheme selection.\nOnsite Requirement:\nAll research and validation tasks are conducted in laboratory environments, requiring no on-site stationing.","descZh":"多数轨道式检查机器人（RMIR）采用定制轨道，存在加工复杂、成本高、交期长、通用性差与维护不便等问题，推高了部署成本并限制了在多场景中的规模化应用；相比之下，标准化程度高、易于采购、经济高效、规格齐全且可批量生产的通用标准型材，可充分解决定制轨道的缺陷。因此，本研究探索多种标准型材轨道的匹配与性能验证，以降低 RMIR 对专用定制轨道的依赖，提升轨道系统的通用性、经济性与工程实用性。为此将定制设计匹配的驱动结构，并实施多维性能测试，选出适合 RMIR 工况的最优型材轨道方案，为低成本、标准化、大规模的 RMIR 部署提供技术支撑。\n目标：\n- 研究采用通用标准型材作为 RMIR 运行轨道的可行性；\n- 设计与标准型材兼容的匹配驱动结构；\n- 开展多维性能测试，在实际运行工况下筛选最优轨道方案。\n研究内容（可为其中部分）：\n- 开展基于通用标准型材的 RMIR 标准化轨道的轨道选型、驱动结构适配与原型性能对比研究；\n- 参考 RMIR 自重、额定载荷、运行速度与安装环境，选取至少三种通用标准型材；\n- 针对每种型材的截面尺寸与力学特性，设计定制驱动机构，实现全工况稳定运行；\n- 搭建测试平台，比较承载能力、爬坡性能、转向灵活性与运行稳定性，总结各型材轨道的优缺点。\n预期成果（可为其中部分）：\n- 为特定工况下的 RMIR 选取适用型材轨道并设计配套驱动机构；\n- 针对巡检场景筛选至少三种通用标准型材，并为每种型材优化匹配驱动结构；\n- 通过对比测试获得承载、爬坡与转向的核心性能数据；\n- 通过综合分析适应性与经济性形成标准化测试报告，确定最优标准轨道方案，实现 RMIR 轨道的通用化、低成本、标准化应用。\n技能要求：基本理解机械结构设计与型材特性分析；能开展机械驱动机构的结构匹配设计与参数优化；能设计实验方案、开展力学性能测试与处理测试数据；具备机械力学、工况分析与工程实用性评价基础知识；能总结实验结果并完成标准化研究报告与最优方案比选。\n现场要求：所有研究与验证任务均在实验室环境开展，无需驻场。","cats":["AERO","DES","ENE","MGT","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-EACOMP-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"面向先进电池材料的 AI 辅助材料设计与建模","titleEn":"AI-Assisted Materials Design and Modelling for Advanced Battery Materials","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"EACOMP Technology","companyEn":"EACOMP Technology","companySupervisor":"Dr. Yang Tu","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Lithium-ion batteries are at the heart of the transition toward electric transportation and renewable energy. Their performance, safety and lifetime depend not only on the choice of materials, but also on their properties, microstructure and evolution during battery operation. Understanding and optimizing these complex interactions requires approaches spanning multiple disciplines, including materials science, electrochemistry, physics-based modelling, artificial intelligence and data analysis. These approaches provide exciting opportunities to accelerate the development of advanced battery materials, improve battery design and enable next-generation energy storage technologies.\nProject Description：\nThis project focuses on applying computational modelling, data-driven methods and materials analysis to address specific challenges in battery material development.\nStudents will work with established modelling tools, simulation frameworks and datasets developed by the company, as well as development of new methods where needed. Depending on the student's background and interests, potential topics include:\n- Battery Materials and Multiscale Modelling: Characterizing material properties, developing simulation parameters and investigating links between atomistic, particle and electrode-scale behaviour.\n- AI-Assisted Simulation: Developing AI agents to translate natural-language engineering requirements into material simulation workflows, including model setup, parameter selection, simulation execution and result analysis.\n- Image-based analysis: Applying computer vision to microscopy images for tasks such as particle segmentation, particle-size and morphology analysis, porosity characterization and quantitative microstructure reconstruction.\n- Materials database development: Organizing and analysing material-property data, developing data-processing and parameter-extraction workflows, and exploring methods for improving the efficiency and reliability of simulation input generation.\nPreferred Skills:\nStudents with backgrounds in materials science, mechanical engineering, computer science or related disciplines are welcome. Experience in finite element modelling, machine learning, image processing, or battery materials would be advantageous.\nExpected Outcomes:\nDepending on the selected topic, the project may lead to a validated computational workflow, data-driven prediction model, image-analysis method, materials database or quantitative understanding of a specific battery-materials problem.\nOnsite Frequency:\nBiweekly online meetings with the company supervisor, with onsite visits/meetings arranged periodically depending on project progress.","descZh":"锂离子电池处于电动交通与可再生能源转型的核心。其性能、安全与寿命不仅取决于材料选择，还取决于电池运行期间材料的性质、微观结构及其演化。理解并优化这些复杂相互作用需要涵盖材料科学、电化学、基于物理的建模、人工智能与数据分析等多学科的途径。这些方法为加速先进电池材料开发、改进电池设计与实现下一代储能技术提供了令人兴奋的机会。\n项目描述：本项目聚焦应用计算建模、数据驱动方法与材料分析，应对电池材料开发中的具体挑战。\n学生将使用公司开发的成熟建模工具、仿真框架与数据集，并在需要时开发新方法。根据学生背景与兴趣，潜在课题包括：\n- 电池材料与多尺度建模：表征材料性能、开发仿真参数，并研究原子尺度、颗粒尺度与电极尺度行为之间的关联；\n- AI 辅助仿真：开发 AI 智能体，将自然语言工程需求转化为材料仿真工作流，包括模型搭建、参数选择、仿真执行与结果分析；\n- 基于图像的分析：将计算机视觉应用于显微图像，开展颗粒分割、粒径与形貌分析、孔隙率表征与定量微结构重建等任务；\n- 材料数据库开发：组织与分析材料性能数据，开发数据处理与参数提取工作流，并探索提升仿真输入生成效率与可靠性的方法。\n偏好技能：欢迎具备材料科学、机械工程、计算机科学或相关学科背景的学生。有有限元建模、机器学习、图像处理或电池材料经验者优先。\n预期成果：根据所选课题，项目可能产出经过验证的计算工作流、数据驱动预测模型、图像分析方法、材料数据库，或对特定电池材料问题的定量理解。\n现场频率：与公司导师每两周线上会议一次，并根据项目进展定期安排现场访问/会议。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-EACOMP-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"面向先进电池设计的多尺度建模与仿真","titleEn":"Multiscale Modelling and Simulation for Advanced Battery Design","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"EACOMP Technology","companyEn":"EACOMP Technology","companySupervisor":"Dr. Yang Tu","univSupName":"Professor SONG Weixin","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Lithium-ion batteries are at the heart of the transition toward electric transportation and renewable energy. Their performance, safety and lifetime depend not only on the choice of materials, but also on their properties, microstructure and evolution during battery operation. Understanding and optimizing these complex interactions requires approaches spanning multiple disciplines, including materials science, electrochemistry, physics-based modelling, artificial intelligence and data analysis. These approaches provide exciting opportunities to accelerate the development of advanced battery materials, improve battery design and enable next-generation energy storage technologies.\nProject Description：\nThis project focuses on applying computational modelling, multiphysics simulation and data-driven methods to investigate and optimize battery design across different length scales. Students will work with established modelling tools, simulation frameworks and datasets developed by the company, as well as develop new modelling approaches where needed.\nDepending on the student's background and interests, potential topics include:\n- Particle-level and mesostructure modelling: Investigating particle interactions, electrochemical behaviour, mechanical deformation, fracture and degradation, and their influence on electrode performance.\n- Electrode and cell modelling: Developing models to investigate electrochemical, thermal and mechanical behaviour at the electrode and cell levels, and studying the effects of electrode architecture, material loading, operating conditions and cell design.\n- Electrode compaction and microstructure: Modelling the compaction process of battery electrodes and investigating how particle packing, porosity, tortuosity, contact networks and mechanical properties evolve during manufacturing and affect subsequent battery performance.\n- Multiscale modelling and parameter transfer: Establishing links between material properties, particle-scale behaviour, electrode microstructure and cell-level performance, enabling information obtained at one scale to be transferred to another.\nSolid-state battery modelling: Developing models for solid-state batteries, including lithium-metal deposition, solid-electrolyte behaviour, electrode–electrolyte interfaces, mechanical stress and failure, and the influence of microstructure on ionic transport and battery performance.\nPreferred Skills:\nStudents with backgrounds in materials science, mechanical engineering, computer science or related disciplines are welcome. Experience in finite element modelling, electrochemical modelling, multiphysics simulation, or battery technology would be advantageous.\nExpected Outcomes:\nDepending on the selected topic, the project may lead to a validated multiscale modelling framework, particle or electrode simulation model, compaction and microstructure analysis method, cell-level modelling workflow, solid-state battery model, battery design optimization framework, or quantitative understanding of the relationship between material properties, microstructure, manufacturing conditions and cell-level performance.\nOnsite Frequency:\nBiweekly online meetings with the company supervisor, with onsite visits/meetings arranged periodically depending on project progress.","descZh":"锂离子电池正处于向电动交通和可再生能源转型的核心地位。其性能、安全性和寿命不仅取决于材料的选择，还取决于材料的性质、微观结构以及在电池运行过程中的演变。理解和优化这些复杂的相互作用，需要跨越多个学科的方法，包括材料科学、电化学、基于物理的建模、人工智能和数据分析。这些方法为加速先进电池材料的开发、改进电池设计以及实现下一代储能技术提供了令人兴奋的机遇。\n项目描述：\n本项目侧重于应用计算建模、多物理场仿真和数据驱动的方法，在不同的长度尺度上研究和优化电池设计。学生将使用公司已开发的成熟建模工具、仿真框架和数据集，并在需要时开发新的建模方法。\n根据学生的背景和兴趣，可能的方向包括：\n- 颗粒级与介观结构建模：研究颗粒相互作用、电化学行为、机械变形、断裂与退化，及其对电极性能的影响。\n- 电极与电芯建模：开发模型以研究电极和电芯层面的电化学、热学和力学行为，并研究电极结构、材料载量、运行条件和电芯设计的影响。\n- 电极压实与微观结构：对电池电极的压实过程进行建模，研究颗粒堆积、孔隙率、迂曲度、接触网络和力学性能在制造过程中如何演变，以及如何影响后续电池性能。\n- 多尺度建模与参数传递：建立材料性质、颗粒尺度行为、电极微观结构和电芯级性能之间的联系，使在某一尺度获得的信息能够传递到另一个尺度。\n固态电池建模：为固态电池开发模型，包括锂金属沉积、固态电解质行为、电极–电解质界面、机械应力与失效，以及微观结构对离子输运和电池性能的影响。\n偏好技能：\n欢迎具有材料科学、机械工程、计算机科学或相关学科背景的学生。具有有限元建模、电化学建模、多物理场仿真或电池技术方面的经验将是加分项。\n预期成果：\n根据所选方向，本项目可能产出一个经过验证的多尺度建模框架、颗粒或电极仿真模型、压实与微观结构分析方法、电芯级建模工作流、固态电池模型、电池设计优化框架，或对材料性质、微观结构、制造条件与电芯级性能之间关系的定量理解。\n到岗频率：\n每两周与公司导师进行一次线上会议，并根据项目进展定期安排到访/线下会议。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-GKHPANG-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于 Python 与 Google Colab 的小语言模型开发","titleEn":"Development of a Small Language Model Using Python and Google Colab","areaZh":"大语言模型；Transformer；AI","areaEn":"Large Language Model; Transformer; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. PANG Grantham K.H.","moderator":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This final year project focuses on designing and implementing a small language model using Python in the Google Colab environment. The model will be trained on a small text dataset to learn basic language patterns and perform next-word prediction and simple text generation. The project will help the student understand the core ideas behind modern language models, including tokenization, embeddings, attention mechanisms, training, evaluation, and inference.\nProject Objectives:\n• Build a small language model using Python.\n• Prepare and clean a small language dataset for training. TinyStories is a synthetic dataset of short English stories written with a vocabulary that a typical 3–4 year old child would understand. It may be the dataset used for this project.\n• Implement tokenization and vocabulary mapping.\n• Design a basic transformer-based model for next-word prediction.\n• Train the model in Google Colab using available GPU resources.\n• Evaluate the model’s performance using loss and prediction quality.\n• Generate sample text from the trained model.\n• Analyze the model’s behavior and discuss its limitations.\n• Document the full development process, results, and findings in a final report.\nThe project should remain small enough to run efficiently in Google Colab, making it suitable for a student project while still demonstrating the fundamental workflow of building a language model from scratch. The student is expected to have a background related to machine learning and artificial intelligence as well as Python programming. The student should take ELEC 6604 (Neural Networks, Fuzzy Systems and Genetic Algorithms) in the first semester. Please contact Dr. G. Pang by gpang@eee.hku.hk if you are interested.\nReferences:\n[1] TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English? \nhttps://arxiv.org/abs/2305.07759\n[2] The Transformer Model — A Deep Dive into Core Mechanisms\nhttps://pub.towardsai.net/the-transformer-model-a-deep-dive-into-core-mechanisms-add33bcd7b56 \n[3] Transformers Explained: Why Attention Is Truly All You Need\nhttps://medium.com/@lmpo/transformers-explained-why-attention-is-truly-all-you-need-2e3669242965","descZh":"本毕业设计项目聚焦于在 Google Colab 环境中使用 Python 设计并实现一个小型语言模型。模型将在一小段文本数据集上训练，学习基本语言模式并执行下一词预测与简单文本生成。项目将帮助学生理解现代语言模型背后的核心思想，包括分词（tokenization）、嵌入、注意力机制、训练、评估与推理。\n项目目标：\n• 使用 Python 构建小型语言模型；\n• 准备并清洗小型语言数据集用于训练。TinyStories 是合成短篇英语故事数据集，词汇量适合 3~4 岁儿童理解，可作为本项目使用的数据集；\n• 实现分词与词汇表映射；\n• 设计基于 Transformer 的基础模型用于下一词预测；\n• 利用 Google Colab 可用的 GPU 资源训练模型；\n• 使用损失与预测质量评估模型性能；\n• 从训练好的模型生成示例文本；\n• 分析模型行为并讨论其局限；\n• 在最终报告中记录完整开发过程、结果与发现。\n项目应保持足够小以在 Google Colab 中高效运行，既适合学生项目规模，又能演示从头构建语言模型的基本工作流。学生应具备机器学习与人工智能相关背景，以及 Python 编程能力；并须在第一学期修读 ELEC 6604（神经网络、模糊系统与遗传算法）。有意者请联系 gpang@eee.hku.hk 的 G. Pang 博士。\n参考文献：\n[1] TinyStories: How Small Can Language Models Be and Still Speak Coherent English?（附 arXiv 链接）\n[2] The Transformer Model — A Deep Dive into Core Mechanisms（附链接）\n[3] Transformers Explained: Why Attention Is Truly All You Need（附链接）","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-GKHPANG-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于机器学习的糖尿病预测","titleEn":"Diabetes Prediction using Machine Learning","areaZh":"医疗健康；机器学习；AI","areaEn":"Healthcare; Machine Learning; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. PANG Grantham K.H.","moderator":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Machine Learning is a sub-field of AI and many powerful machine learning algorithms have been developed over the years. These algorithms are made available in various framework in Python. In this study, these algorithms are used for diabetes prediction. Different algorithms will be utilized and compared. \nEarly and accurate diagnosis of the blood glucose level in the human body is crucial for the prevention of complications. The work aims to study how health data can help to detect diabetes. It is an attempt to mark the limitations of current machine learning prediction models. It contributes to the ongoing efforts to upgrade and update the accuracy and reliability of early diabetes detection. A very large database will be used for this dissertation.\nThe student is expected to have a background related to machine learning and artificial intelligence as well as Python programming. The code will be developed in the Google Colab environment. The student should take ELEC 6604 (Neural Networks, Fuzzy Systems and Genetic Algorithms) in the first semester. Please contact Dr. G. Pang by gpang@eee.hku.hk if you are interested.\nReferences:\n1. Diabetes Prediction using Machine Learning, International Journal of Innovative Science and Research Technology, Volume 8, Issue 11, November 2023.\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/347091823_Diabetes_Prediction_Using_Machine_Learning \n2. Diabetes prediction using machine learning and explainable AI techniques, Health Technology Letters, 10(1-2):1-10, Dec 2022. DOI: 10.1049/htl2.12039. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37077883/\n3. Development of Various Diabetes Prediction Models Using Machine Learning Techniques, Diabetes & Metabolism Journal 2022; 46:650-657. https://doi.org/10.4093/dmj.2021.0115. \n4. Diabetes Prediction using Machine Learning , https://www.analyticsvidhya.com/blog/2022/01/diabetes-prediction-using-machine-learning/","descZh":"机器学习是 AI 的子领域，多年来已发展出许多强大的机器学习算法，这些算法以各种 Python 框架提供。本研究利用这些算法进行糖尿病预测，将使用并比较不同算法。\n对人体血糖水平进行早期、准确诊断对预防并发症至关重要。本工作旨在研究健康数据如何帮助检测糖尿病，尝试指出当前机器学习预测模型的局限，为持续提升早期糖尿病检测的准确性与可靠性作出贡献。本论文将使用一个非常大的数据库。\n学生应具备机器学习与人工智能相关背景，以及 Python 编程能力。代码将在 Google Colab 环境中开发。学生须在第一学期修读 ELEC 6604（神经网络、模糊系统与遗传算法）。有意者请联系 gpang@eee.hku.hk 的 G. Pang 博士。\n参考文献：\n1. Diabetes Prediction using Machine Learning, International Journal of Innovative Science and Research Technology, Volume 8, Issue 11, November 2023.（附链接）\n2. Diabetes prediction using machine learning and explainable AI techniques, Health Technology Letters, 10(1-2):1-10, Dec 2022. DOI: 10.1049/htl2.12039.（附链接）\n3. Development of Various Diabetes Prediction Models Using Machine Learning Techniques, Diabetes & Metabolism Journal 2022; 46:650-657.（附链接）\n4. Diabetes Prediction using Machine Learning（附链接）","cats":["AI","BIO"]},{"ref":"2026-27-GKHPANG-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于 CIFAR-10 数据集的图像分类","titleEn":"Image Classification based on the CIFAR-10 dataset of images","areaZh":"图像分类；机器学习；AI","areaEn":"Image Classification; Machine Learning; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. PANG Grantham K.H.","moderator":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"The Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) is a class of deep learning neural networks which represents a huge breakthrough in image recognition. They are commonly used to analyze visual imagery and are frequently used for image classification. Vision Transformer (ViT) is another novel architecture for image classification that was developed based on the famous LLM model of Transformer. In this work, your task is to create Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and ViT-based image classifier for the CIFAR-10 dataset of images. You have to experiment with different architectures, hyper-parameters, and training procedures to make yourself familiar with training and tuning machine learning models. Your aim is to obtain an architecture that can give you high validation and testing accuracy.\nThe CIFAR-10 (Canadian Institute For Advanced Research) dataset consists of 60,000 32 x 32 colour images in 10 classes, with 6,000 images per class. There are 50,000 training images and 10,000 test images. The important points that distinguish this dataset from MNIST are:\n• Images are colored in CIFAR-10 as compared to the black and white texture of MNIST.\n• Each image is 32 x 32 pixel.\n• 50,000 training images and 10,000 testing images.\nNow, these images are taken in varying lighting conditions and at different angles, and since these are colored images, you will see that there are many variations in the color itself of similar objects (for example, the color of ocean water). In this dissertation, the CIFAR-10 is used as the dataset to practice your hyper-parameter tuning skills for CNNs. The student is expected to have a background related to machine learning and artificial intelligence as well as Python programming. The code will be developed in the Google Colab environment. The student should take ELEC 6604 (Neural Networks, Fuzzy Systems and Genetic Algorithms) in the first semester. Please contact Dr. G. Pang by gpang@eee.hku.hk if you are interested.\nReferences:\n1. How to Develop a CNN from scratch for CIFAR-10 Photo Classification \nhttps://machinelearningmastery.com/how-to-develop-a-cnn-from-scratch-for-cifar-10-photo-classification/\n2. Deep Learning with CIFAR-10 Image Classification\nhttps://towardsdatascience.com/deep-learning-with-cifar-10-image-classification-64ab92110d79/","descZh":"卷积神经网络（CNN）是一类深度学习神经网络，代表了图像识别领域的重大突破，常用于分析视觉图像与图像分类。视觉 Transformer（ViT）是另一种基于著名 LLM 架构 Transformer 开发的新型图像分类架构。本工作中，你的任务是针对 CIFAR-10 图像数据集构建基于 CNN 与 ViT 的图像分类器。你需要试验不同架构、超参数与训练流程，以熟悉机器学习模型的训练与调优；目标是获得在验证与测试中精度较高的架构。\nCIFAR-10（加拿大高等研究院）数据集包含 10 个类别的 60,000 张 32×32 彩色图像，每类 6,000 张；其中 50,000 张训练图像与 10,000 张测试图像。该数据集与 MNIST 的关键区别在于：\n• CIFAR-10 图像是彩色的，而 MNIST 是黑白纹理；\n• 每张图像 32×32 像素；\n• 50,000 张训练图像与 10,000 张测试图像。\n这些图像在不同光照条件与角度下拍摄，且因为是彩色图像，相似物体的颜色也存在许多变化（例如海水的颜色）。本论文使用 CIFAR-10 作为数据集，以练习对 CNN 的超参数调优技能。学生应具备机器学习与人工智能相关背景，以及 Python 编程能力。代码将在 Google Colab 环境中开发。学生须在第一学期修读 ELEC 6604（神经网络、模糊系统与遗传算法）。有意者请联系 gpang@eee.hku.hk 的 G. Pang 博士。\n参考文献：\n1. How to Develop a CNN from scratch for CIFAR-10 Photo Classification（附链接）\n2. Deep Learning with CIFAR-10 Image Classification（附链接）","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-GKHPANG-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于人工智能方法与频谱图的语音识别","titleEn":"Speech recognition based on Artificial Intelligence method and Spectrograms","areaZh":"医疗健康；机器学习；AI","areaEn":"Healthcare; Machine Learning; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. PANG Grantham K.H.","moderator":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Automated speech recognition begins with the acquisition and processing of the speech signals for subsequent computations. In the area of the Chinese language speech recognition, the speech signals is consisted of utterance of a number of Chinese words. In order to carry out the recognition of individual word spoken, the speech signal needs to be segmented into different sections for subsequent word detection or recognition. The dissertation aims to use neural networks to improve the speech recognition accuracy. The work will investigate the use of AI deep learning methods for the speech recognition task. \nThe various datasets of speech signals will be provided for training and testing. The datasets were collected from a number of participants, and these datasets have been pre-processed and cleaned.\nThe student is expected to have a background related to machine learning and artificial intelligence as well as Python programming. The code will be developed in the Google Colab environment. The students are expected to take ELEC 6604 (Neural Networks, Fuzzy Systems and Genetic Algorithms) in the first semester. Please contact Dr. G. Pang by gpang@eee.hku.hk if you are interested.\nRef: \nImage Approach to Speech Recognition on CNN\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/profile/Ilyos-Xujayorov-2/publication/341989568_Image_Approach_to_Speech_Recognition_on_CNN/links/629b1b5c416ec50bdb0aaf78/Image-Approach-to-Speech-Recognition-on-CNN.pdf\nhttps://blog.google/outreach-initiatives/accessibility/impaired-speech-recognition/\nhttps://euphonia-audiotool.web.app/\nhttps://www.wired.com/story/google-made-truly-usable-voice-assistant/\nhttps://www.aphasia.org/stories/google-voice-aphasia/","descZh":"自动语音识别始于语音信号的采集与处理，供后续计算使用。在中文语音识别领域，语音信号由若干汉语词语的话语构成。为识别说出的单个词语，语音信号需要被切分为不同片段，供后续词检测或识别。本论文旨在利用神经网络提升语音识别准确率，将研究 AI 深度学习方法在语音识别任务中的应用。\n将提供多种语音信号数据集用于训练与测试。数据集来自多名参与者，并已经过预处理与清洗。\n学生应具备机器学习与人工智能相关背景，以及 Python 编程能力。代码将在 Google Colab 环境中开发。学生须在第一学期修读 ELEC 6604（神经网络、模糊系统与遗传算法）。有意者请联系 gpang@eee.hku.hk 的 G. Pang 博士。\n参考资料：\nImage Approach to Speech Recognition on CNN（研究之门链接）\nhttps://blog.google/outreach-initiatives/accessibility/impaired-speech-recognition/\nhttps://euphonia-audiotool.web.app/\nhttps://www.wired.com/story/google-made-truly-usable-voice-assistant/\nhttps://www.aphasia.org/stories/google-voice-aphasia/","cats":["AI","BIO"]},{"ref":"2026-27-GKHPANG-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于光电容积脉搏波（PPG）的血压预测","titleEn":"Blood pressure prediction based on photoplethysmogram (PPG)","areaZh":"医疗健康；机器学习；AI；可穿戴设备","areaEn":"Healthcare; Machine Learning; AI; Wearables","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. PANG Grantham K.H.","moderator":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Blood pressure monitoring is important because it helps with early diagnosis of health problems, tracks treatment, encourages better control and cuts health care costs. Traditional non-invasive blood pressure monitoring usually involves applying a blood pressure cuff to the upper arm. Some devices take readings in a finger, wrist or thigh. PPG is an advanced, specialized noninvasive method that can provide continuous 24-hour blood pressure monitoring.\nAn photoplethysmogram (PPG) is an optically obtained series of data displayed on a graph that can be used to detect blood volume changes in the microvascular bed of tissue. A PPG is often obtained by using a pulse oximeter which illuminates the skin using LEDs and measures changes in light absorption. Based on the absorption from red and infrared LEDs, the level of blood oxygen saturation can be measured. \nIn this dissertation, the PPG is also used for the prediction of blood pressure. A higher or lower blood pressure would lead to slight modifications to the shape or features of the PPG. Machine learning models of the PPG based on AI deep learning can be used to predict the blood pressure of a person. This non-invasive method for blood pressure is revolutionary. Intensive study is being carried out in the field of wearables for healthcare monitoring. \nThe student is expected to have a background related to machine learning and artificial intelligence as well as Python programming. The code will be developed in the Google Colab environment. The student should take ELEC 6604 (Neural Networks, Fuzzy Systems and Genetic Algorithms) in the first semester. Please contact Dr. G. Pang by gpang@eee.hku.hk if you are interested.\nRef: \nhttps://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photoplethysmogram \nHuang Y, He Y, Song Z, Gao K, Zheng Y. Validation of deep learning models for cuffless blood pressure estimation on a large benchmarking dataset. Connected Health And Telemedicine. 2024; 3(1):300002. http://dx.doi.org/10.20517/chatmed.2023.23 \nGonzález S, Hsieh WT, Chen TP. A benchmark for machine-learning based non-invasive blood pressure estimation using photoplethysmogram. Sci Data. 2023 Mar 21;10(1):149. doi: 10.1038/s41597-023-02020-6. PMID: 36944668; PMCID: PMC10030661.","descZh":"血压监测十分重要，因为它有助于早期诊断健康问题、跟踪治疗、鼓励更好的血压控制并降低医疗成本。传统无创血压监测通常需要对上臂施加血压袖带，部分设备在手指、手腕或大腿测量。PPG 是一种先进、专门的无创方法，可提供连续 24 小时血压监测。\n光电容积脉搏波（PPG）是以图形显示的经光学获得的系列数据，可用于检测组织微血管床的血容量变化。PPG 通常借助脉搏血氧仪获取——用 LED 照射皮肤并测量光吸收变化；基于红光与红外 LED 的吸收，可测量血氧饱和度水平。\n本论文还将 PPG 用于血压预测。血压偏高或偏低会导致 PPG 形状或特征发生细微改变；基于 AI 深度学习的 PPG 机器学习模型可用于预测人体血压。这种无创血压测量方法具有革命性意义，可穿戴医疗监测领域正在开展深入研究。\n学生应具备机器学习与人工智能相关背景，以及 Python 编程能力。代码将在 Google Colab 环境中开发。学生须在第一学期修读 ELEC 6604（神经网络、模糊系统与遗传算法）。有意者请联系 gpang@eee.hku.hk 的 G. Pang 博士。\n参考文献：（附维基及多篇论文链接）","cats":["AI","BIO","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-GKHPANG-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于 AI 模型的深度学习睡眠分期预测","titleEn":"Deep learning for Sleep stage Prediction based on AI models","areaZh":"医疗健康；机器学习；AI；可穿戴设备","areaEn":"Healthcare; Machine Learning; AI; Wearables","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. PANG Grantham K.H.","moderator":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Sleep Stage Prediction has many significances in the health monitoring:\n1. Understanding Sleep Quality:\n - Accurate prediction of sleep stages (like REM, light, awake and deep sleep) helps evaluate sleep quality and identify patterns that may indicate sleep disorders.\n2. Personalized Health Insights:\n - Machine learning can analyze individual sleep patterns over time, providing tailored recommendations for improving sleep hygiene and overall health.\n3. Real-Time Monitoring:\n - Continuous sleep stage prediction allows for real-time health monitoring, enabling users to receive immediate feedback on their sleep quality.\n4. Early Detection of Disorders:\n - Identifying anomalies in sleep patterns can help in the early detection of sleep disorders such as insomnia, sleep apnea, or other health issues.\nAI has made great progress in healthcare and much health/vital sign data is being collected by wearable devices like Apple Watch, RingConn (smart ring) or Fitbit. Automated sleep stage detection can be carried out based on the collected data like heart rate. Machine learning methods can be used for detection of sleep or wakefulness disorders along with other health conditions. Sleep stage prediction using machine learning not only enhances our understanding of sleep but also plays a crucial role in comprehensive health monitoring and management.\nThis dissertation aims to study how health data from wearables such as smart watch and ring can help to provide personalized sleep stage detection for individuals based on AI. The dataset will be provided. The student is expected to have a background related to machine learning and artificial intelligence as well as Python programming. The code will be developed in the Google Colab environment. The student should take ELEC 6604 (Neural Networks, Fuzzy Systems and Genetic Algorithms) in the first semester. Please contact Dr. G. Pang by gpang@eee.hku.hk if you are interested.\nReferences:\nDeep learning for automated sleep staging using instantaneous heart rate\nhttps://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-020-0291-x\nDeep learning for automated sleep staging using instantaneous heart rate\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1877050920321840\nSleep Stage Detection\nhttps://paperswithcode.com/task/sleep-stage-detection\nSleep Stage Detection on MASS SS2\nhttps://paperswithcode.com/sota/sleep-stage-detection-on-mass-ss2","descZh":"睡眠阶段预测在健康监测中具有多方面重要意义：\n1. 理解睡眠质量：准确预测睡眠阶段（如 REM、浅睡、清醒与深睡）有助于评估睡眠质量并识别可能预示睡眠障碍的模式；\n2. 个性化健康洞察：机器学习可分析个体随时间变化的睡眠模式，为改善睡眠卫生与整体健康提供量身定制的建议；\n3. 实时监测：连续睡眠阶段预测支持实时健康监测，使用户能即时获得睡眠质量反馈；\n4. 疾病早期发现：识别睡眠模式异常有助于早期发现失眠、睡眠呼吸暂停等睡眠障碍或其他健康问题。\nAI 在医疗健康领域取得巨大进展，Apple Watch、RingConn（智能戒指）、Fitbit 等可穿戴设备正在采集大量健康/生命体征数据。可基于心率等采集数据实现自动化睡眠阶段检测；机器学习方法可用于检测睡眠/清醒障碍及其他健康状况。利用机器学习进行睡眠阶段预测，不仅加深我们对睡眠的理解，也在综合健康监测与管理中发挥关键作用。\n本论文旨在研究来自智能手表与戒指等可穿戴设备的健康数据如何帮助基于 AI 为个体提供个性化睡眠阶段检测。数据集将提供。学生应具备机器学习与人工智能相关背景，以及 Python 编程能力。代码将在 Google Colab 环境中开发。学生须在第一学期修读 ELEC 6604（神经网络、模糊系统与遗传算法）。有意者请联系 gpang@eee.hku.hk 的 G. Pang 博士。\n参考文献：（关于利用瞬时心率进行自动睡眠分期的深度学习等多篇，附链接）","cats":["AI","BIO","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MMCHOY-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"实验室设备管理系统","titleEn":"Lab equipment management system","areaZh":"AI；智能系统","areaEn":"AI; smart system","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"Dr. ALI Zulfiqar","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-27 16:18:43","descriptionText":"WHAT THIS PROJECT IS \n Build and deploy an operational system for the department's laboratory equipment:\n records, photographs, manuals, warranty and supplier details, maintenance and calibration, booking, and role-based access. All of that is expected and none of it is what you will be marked on. Built as a plain feature list it is about ten weeks of \n competent form-building and a dissertation with nothing in it to compare against anything. The marks are in three specific results. \n THE THREE RESULTS THAT CARRY THE MARKS \n Reconciliation. The departmental inventory register is exported to a file every other month. Ingest that export, compare it against your operational record, and produce an exception report. Measure precision and recall of exception detection against a sample a technician has labelled independently. You never write back to the register.\n Maintenance scheduling. Placing a calibration or inspection window is a scheduling problem, not a reminder. Build a baseline strategy and at least two alternatives, run them over identical generated demand, and report the trade-off between compliance and instrument availability. Not one number. A curve. Concurrency. Write the booking and availability invariants down formally. Then break them on purpose with property-based tests, a race harness and a load test. Report the violations you found and fixed. The violations are the evidence.\n FOUR THINGS YOU MUST UNDERSTAND BEFORE YOU DESIGN ANYTHING \n The departmental item number starts with a room code, and finance assigns it once and never reissues it. So the number is stable and you may key on it. What is not stable is the room it claims: move the instrument and the code follows it unchanged. The prefix is a statement about where the item was when it was registered, and it goes stale silently. Never infer a location from the item number anywhere in your system. Present location, custodian and condition are observed, dated facts that come from your operational record and from physical scanning.\n A finance asset is not the same thing as a bookable resource. A universal testing machine may be four tagged items - frame, load cell, extensometer, control PC - and one bookable resource. An isolation table may be one tagged item and two bookable stations. A fume hood is tagged and bookable by nobody; an in-house rig is bookable and tagged by nobody. And the mapping changes: the extensometer moves to another rig next semester. So you need two tables and a mapping table with validity dates. Calibration attaches to the asset, booking attaches to the resource, and bookability is therefore derived and never stored. If you put a bookable yes/no flag on the equipment row you will end up writing a nightly script to keep it honest, and that script is where this project goes to die.\n ADMINISTRATOR AND CUSTODIAN ARE NOT TWO PERMISSION LEVELS. They are two different jobs. Do not model them as a rank where one can do everything the other can. Model them as two groups that own different fields on the same record. Administrator group: department-wide. Owns the register-facing and commercial facts: inventory code, value category, disposal status, supplier, warranty, contract, purchase details. \n Custodian group: scoped by room. Owns the physical facts — present location, condition, calibration state, maintenance events, photographs, manuals, whether the item is currently fit to book.\n Enforce field ownership on the server, in the endpoint, not by hiding buttons in the interface. Write a test that sends a custodian's session an administrator-owned field and expect a rejection. That test is worth more than the screen it protects. \n CUSTODY IS BY ROOM, AND THAT MAKES MOVING AN INSTRUMENT HARDER THAN IT LOOKS. A technician looks after the equipment in the rooms assigned to them, and the administrator group maintains those assignments. Read that carefully, because it has a consequence you will not see coming. A custodian's write access is not a column on the equipment record. It is worked out at the moment of the request by combining two facts owned by two different groups: the room the instrument is in, which custodians record, and the rooms a technician covers, which administrators maintain. So the data that decides who may edit a record includes a field that the person being authorised can edit themselves. Now the trap. If custody comes from the recorded room, then when an instrument arrives in room B, the custodian of room B is the only person who can see it and is not yet allowed to touch it. The custodian of room A is still responsible on paper and is the one person who cannot see it. A single editable location field cannot fix this. Either you give every custodian write access to every room, which throws away the scoping the manager asked for, or you make someone update a fact they cannot verify. So do not build an editable location field. Build a transfer: the receiving custodian claims an instrument found in their room, the transfer sits in a pending state, and it is completed by the releasing custodian or by an administrator. Custody never moves through an edit nobody witnessed, and you get a transfer log for free, which is evidence for the drift measurement. Never work out custody from the item number prefix. The prefix is a room code that can be years stale, so a prefix-based rule hands every relocated instrument to the wrong technician, silently, for exactly the items whose records are already worst. Resolve authority from the observed room only. And note what your drift figure now means: the proportion of items whose prefix room and actual room disagree is the size of the custody misattribution the lazy implementation would have caused. Say so. Three separate groupings now exist over the same equipment: finance asset, bookable resource, and room. Do not merge them. A rig with separately tagged parts can have those parts in two rooms under two custodians while still being one bookable resource, and your model has to allow that.\n FOUR RULES FOR THE TWO TIMESTAMPS \n The technical manager wants a last-update timestamp for the administrator group and a s eparate one for the custodian group, on every record. Two datetime columns updated by a blanket database trigger will not do it, and will actively lie. \n Define what updates each clock, in writing, before you build it. An administrator editing a warranty date must not move the custodian clock. If a trigger fires on any row update, your system will report that a custodian confirmed an instrument they have never seen.\n Confirmation without change is real information and must move the clock. A custodian who scans an item, finds everything correct and changes nothing has told you something valuable. So record two events per group, not one: last edited, and last confirmed. A record edited in March and confirmed in August is in better shape than one edited in August and never confirmed.\n Derive the timestamps, do not store them as the only truth. Keep an append-only audit log of who changed which field, when, from what value to what value. The four timestamps are then a projection of that log. If you store only the timestamps you cannot answer the one question anybody will actually ask, which is what changed. On the record itself the manager wants the timestamp only, not a name, because the room assignment already identifies the responsible technician. Follow that for the display, but keep the actor in the audit log regardless. The reason is specific: room assignments change and equipment moves, so a year from now the technician covering that room is not the person who confirmed the item in August. If the actor lives only in the assignment table, that question becomes unanswerable the first time a room is reassigned.\n Two groups editing one record is a lost update waiting to happen. Partitioned field ownership makes it tractable but does not remove it — an administrator and a custodian can still save the same record within the same second. Use optimistic concurrency with a version token, or separate the partitions into their own rows, and argue in writing which you chose and why. This belongs in sprint 2 with the booking concurrency work, not bolted on later.\n WHERE THE EXTRA MARK IS IN THIS \n The two clocks give you a data-freshness measurement for free. Report the distribution o f custodian-confirmation age across the whole equipment set, broken down by room and by asset value category, and use it to choose which items get sampled in the physical check. That converts a bookkeeping field into a targeting rule with a measured effect, which is exactly the kind of thing that separates a good mark from a safe one. One caution: because custody follows the room, a per-room figure is close to a measure of an individual technician's diligence. Present it as equipment coverage and never as staff performance, and clear the wording with your supervisor before it appears on a screen. \n THINGS THAT LOSE MARKS \n Treating the inventory code as one more text field, or parsing its prefix and showing that as the item's room. While you are scanning for the physical check, record whether the prefix room matches the room you actually found the item in, and report the proportion that does not.\n Resolving who may edit an item from its item number prefix. Worse than the above, because it is wrong invisibly and only for the items that moved.\n Administrator as a superset of custodian. It is a different job, not a higher rank.\n An editable location field instead of a transfer with a pending state. Read the custody section again if you are tempted.\n Timestamps maintained by a trigger on any row change. See rule 1 above.\n A reminder email instead of a scheduler. Reminders are a first-year feature.\n Circular validation. If you generate the ground truth from the same rules your reconciliation engine uses, or generate booking demand from the same rules your scheduler optimises against, you have validated nothing. This is the single most likely way to fail this project.\n Starting to code before you have measured the current process. How long does a physical inventory check take per item today? How are calibration dates tracked today? Every comparison you make later is against those figures, so collect them in weeks 1 to 4 or you will have nothing to compare against. And publish the test coverage figure even if it is low.\n OUT OF SCOPE - DO NOT ATTEMPT \n Writing to or interfacing live with the departmental inventory system or any university finance, procurement or HR system. Depreciation, costing, billing or cost recovery. RFID, telemetry or any inventory hardware purchase. A native mobile app. Any claim of safety certification or regulatory compliance. Any conversational assistant - nobody has asked for one, it cannot be evaluated by anything better than a worked example, and it would compete for the same thirty weeks as the three results above. \n WHAT COUNTS AS DONE \n A live URL an examiner can open, with a read-only examiner account, one test account per role, an uptime log, an incident log for the final eight weeks and the coverage output. User acceptance testing with eight to twelve people, scripted, run unaided, including at least two administrator-group staff and at least two custodians responsible for different rooms, so that scoped access and a real transfer between two people both get exercised, against sign-off criteria agreed in writing before testing starts: all must-have requirements pass, zero double-allocation incidents, zero lost updates when an administrator and a custodian edit the same record, zero successful writes across role partitions or outside a custodian's assigned rooms, zero cases of authority resolved from an item number prefix, no instrument left without a responsible custodian after a transfer, zero bookings confirmed against a non-conforming instrument, zero unreported reconciliation exceptions in the labelled sample, no open critical or major defects, System Usability Scale 68 or above. Report initial results and post-fix results separately. Do not overwrite the first run. \n AI USE \n Using an AI tool to help you write code, draft text or understand a paper is allowed and must be declared. That is completely different from putting an AI component inside the system, which this project does not do. Do not blur the two in your dissertation.","descZh":"本项目是什么\n构建并部署一套供院系实验室设备使用的运行系统：设备记录、照片、手册、保修与供应商信息、维护与标定、预订与基于角色的访问。这些都在预期之内，也都不是你被评分的重点。若按普通功能清单搭建，大约只是十周熟练的表单开发，加上一篇无可对比对象的论文。分数藏在三个具体成果中。\n承载分数的三个成果\n1. 对账（Reconciliation）：院系库存登记册每隔一个月导出为文件。读取该导出文件，与你的运行记录比对，生成异常报告。对照一名技术人员独立标注的样本，测量异常检测的精确率与召回率。你绝不写回登记册。\n2. 维护排程（Maintenance scheduling）：安排标定或检查窗口是排程问题，不是提醒。构建一个基线策略与至少两个备选策略，在相同生成的需au下运行，报告合规性与仪器可用性之间的权衡。不是一个数字，而是一条曲线。\n3. 并发（Concurrency）：将预订与可用性不变量形式化写下来。然后用基于属性的测试、竞争压力工具与负载测试故意破坏它们。报告你发现并修复的违规。违规本身就是证据。\n动手设计前必须先理解的四件事\n1. 院系物品编号以房间代码开头，财务部门只分配一次且永不重发。因此该编号稳定，可作主键。不稳定的是它所声称的房间：移动仪器后编号原样跟随。前缀描述的是物品注册时所在位置，且会静默过期。系统中的任何地方都绝不要从物品编号推断位置。当前所在位置、保管人（custodian）与状态是可观测、带日期的事实，来源于你的运行记录与物理扫描。\n2. 财务资产不同于可预订资源。一台万能试验机可能是四个带标签物品（机架、测力传感器、引伸计、控制 PC）与一个可预订资源。一张隔震台可能是一个带标签物品与两个可预订工位。一台通风柜有标签但无人可预订；一套自制台架可预订但无标签。且映射会变化：引伸计下个学期移到另一套台架。因此你需要两张表与一张带生效日期的映射表。标定附着于资产，预订附着于资源，可预订性因此是派生量、绝不可存储。若你在设备行放一个\"可预订\"是/否标志，最终会写一个夜间脚本让它保持诚实——而那个脚本正是本项目走向失败之处。\n3. 管理员与保管人不是两个权限级别，而是两份不同工作。不要把它们建模为一方能做另一方所能做一切的等级。把它们建模为在同一记录上拥有不同字段的两个组。管理员组：院系级，拥有面向登记册与商务的事实：库存代码、价值类别、处置状态、供应商、保修、合同、采购信息。保管人组：按房间限定，拥有物理事实——当前位置、状态、标定状态、维护事件、照片、手册、物品当前是否适合被预订。\n在服务器端、在端点处强制字段所有权，而不是靠隐藏界面按钮。编写一个测试，把管理员拥有的字段放进保管人的会话中并期望被拒绝。那个测试比你保护的界面更有价值。\n4. 保管按房间归属，这使移动仪器比看起来更困难。技术人员负责分配给他们的房间中的设备，管理员组维护这些分配。请细读此点，因为它有一个你预想不到的后果。保管人的写权限不是设备记录上的一列，而是在请求时刻结合两个由不同组拥有的事实得出：仪器所在房间（由保管人记录）与技术人员覆盖的房间（由管理员维护）。因此，决定谁可编辑记录的数据中，包含一个被授权者本人可自行编辑的字段。陷阱来了：如果保管来自所记录的房间，那么当仪器到达 B 房间时，B 房间的保管人是唯一能看到它的人，却尚未被允许触碰它；A 房间的保管人在纸面上仍负责，却是唯一看不到它的人。一个可编辑的单一位置字段无法解决此问题——要么给每位保管人所有房间的写权限（丢弃管理者要求的范围限定），要么强迫某人更新他们无法核验的事实。所以不要构建可编辑位置字段，而要构建一次\"移交\"：接收保管人认领其房间中发现的仪器，移交处于待定状态，由移交方保管人或管理员完成。保管绝不通过无人见证的编辑流动，你还能免费获得移交日志——这正是漂移测量的证据。绝不要从物品编号前缀推导保管权：前缀可能是过期多年的房间代码，因此基于前缀的规则会把每台已搬迁仪器悄悄交给错误的技术人员——恰是那些记录本已最差的物品。只从观测房间解析权限。并注意你的漂移数字如今的含义：前缀房间与实际房间不一致的物品比例，就是偷懒实现本会造成保管错配的规模。要明确说出这一点。现在同一套设备上存在三个独立的分类：财务资产、可预订资源与房间。不要合并它们。一套带独立标签部件的台架，可以让部件分处两室、由两位保管人负责，同时仍是一个可预订资源——你的模型必须允许这一点。\n两个时间戳的四条规则\n1. 技术经理希望每条记录上都有管理员组的最后更新时间戳与保管人组各自独立的时间戳。用全表数据库触发器更新两个 datetime 列是行不通的，而且会积极撒谎。先书面定义什么更新哪座时钟，再动手构建。管理员编辑保修日期不得拨动保管人时钟。若触发器在任何行更新时触发，你的系统会报告某位保管人确认了一台他们从未见过的仪器。\n2. 无变更的确认是真实信息，必须拨动时钟。扫描物品、发现一切正确、什么都没改的保管人告诉了你有价值的信息。因此每组记录两个事件而非一个：最后编辑与最后确认。三月被编辑、八月被确认的记录，比八月被编辑且从未确认的记录状态更好。\n3. 时间戳应派生，不应作为唯一真值存储。保留一份只追加的审计日志，记录谁在何时把哪个字段从何值改为何值。四个时间戳就是该日志的投影。若只存时间戳，就无法回答任何人真正会问的那个问题——改了什么。在记录本身，经理只要时间戳不要姓名，因为房间分配已标识负责的技术人员。显示层遵此，但审计日志无论何时都保留操作者。原因很具体：房间分配会变化、设备会移动，一年后覆盖该房间的技术人员已不是八月确认物品的那个人。若操作者只存在于分配表中，第一次房间重新分配时这个问题就无法回答了。\n4. 两组编辑一条记录就是在等待丢失更新。分区字段所有权使其可处理但并未消除——管理员与保管人仍可能在同一秒保存同一记录。请使用带版本令牌的乐观并发，或将各分区拆为独立行，并书面论证你的选择及理由。这应属于第二个冲刺阶段并与预订并发工作同步进行，而不是日后打补丁。\n额外分数所在\n两座时钟让你免费获得数据新鲜度度量。报告整套设备上\"保管人确认时长\"的分布，按房间与按资产价值类别拆分，并用它选择物理盘点中哪些物品需抽样。这把簿记字段变成一条有实测效果的靶向规则——正是把好分数与安稳分数区分开来的那种东西。一个提醒：因保管随房间走，按房间的数值接近对单个技术人员勤勉度的度量。请把它呈现为设备覆盖率而非员工绩效，并在它出现在屏幕上之前与导师敲定措辞。\n失分点\n- 将库存代码当作又一个文本字段，或解析其前缀并把它显示为物品所在房间。物理盘点扫描时，记录前缀房间是否与你实际找到物品的房间一致，并报告不一致比例。\n- 从物品编号前缀解析谁可编辑某物品。比上一条更糟，因为它不可见地出错，且只针对已搬迁物品。\n- 把管理员当作保管人的超集。那是不同工作，而非更高等级。\n- 用可编辑位置字段替代带待定状态的移交。若有此念头，请重读\"保管\"一节。\n- 用任何行变更的触发器维护时间戳。见规则一。\n- 用提醒邮件替代排程器。提醒是一年级功能。\n- 循环验证。若你用对账引擎所用的同一套规则生成真值，或用调度器所优化的同一套规则生成预订需求，你就什么也没验证。这是本项目最可能的失败方式。\n- 在测量当前流程前就动手写代码。今天每件物品的物理盘点要花多久？标定日期如今如何跟踪？你之后的每次比较都基于这些数字，因此要在第 1~4 周收集，否则将无可对比。即便覆盖率很低也请公布测试覆盖率数字。\n超出范围——不要尝试\n写入或实时对接院系库存系统或任何大学财务、采购或人事系统。折旧、成本核算、计费或成本回收。购买 RFID、遥测或任何库存硬件。原生移动应用。任何安全认证或监管合规声明。任何对话助手——没人要求它，除了用例演示之外无法更好评估，还会与上述三个成果争夺同样三十周的时间。\n怎样才算完成\n一条考官可打开的线上 URL，含只读考官账号、每角色一个测试账号、在线率日志、最后八周的事故日志与覆盖率输出。对八到十二人做脚本化、无辅助运行的用户验收测试，参与者中至少包含两名管理员组成员与至少两名负责不同房间的保管人，使限定范围访问与两人之间的真实移交都得到演练，并对照测试开始前书面商定的签字验收标准：所有必备需求通过；零重复分配事件；管理员与保管人编辑同一记录时零丢失更新；跨角色分区或超出保管人被分配房间之外零成功写入；零从物品编号前缀解析权限的案例；移交后无任何仪器失去负责保管人；零对不合格仪器确认的预订；标注样本中零未报告的对账异常；无未关闭的关键/重大缺陷；系统可用性量表 68 及以上。分别报告初次与修复后结果。不要覆盖首轮结果。\nAI 使用\n使用 AI 工具帮助编写代码、起草文字或理解论文是允许的且必须声明。这与在系统内部署 AI 组件完全不同——本项目不这样做。请不要在论文中混淆二者。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-FUZHANG-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"空中机械臂：平台与应用","titleEn":"Aerial Manipulators: Platform and Applications","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHANG Fu","moderator":"Dr. CHEN Nan","capacity":10,"availCap":10,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-25 20:35:21","descriptionText":"Uncrewed aerial manipulators (UAMs) integrate aerial mobility with robotic manipulation, enabling direct physical interaction with the environment from the air. This project develops a versatile, high-performance UAM platform that combines lightweight yet rigid structures, efficient propulsion, and dexterous multi-degree-of-freedom manipulators. The system incorporates precise force and position control, whole-body planning, and learning-based policy generation tailored to specific tasks. Application scenarios include fruit picking in agriculture, wildlife monitoring in ecological studies, and infrastructure inspection and repair in industrial environments—missions where conventional ground robots face limited accessibility. By advancing both the platform design and task-specific algorithms, this project aims to expand the operational frontier of aerial robots.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Solid mechanical design skill and experience(Solidworks, AutoCAD, etc.); \n2. Basic embedded programming skill;\n3. Basic programming skills and experience (C/C++, Python, etc.);\n4. Candidates with experience in RoboMaster competitions are preferred;","descZh":"无人空中机械臂（UAM）将空中机动能力与机械臂操作相结合，可从空中直接与环境进行物理交互。本项目开发一种通用、高性能的 UAM 平台，融合轻质而刚性的结构、高效推进系统与灵巧的多自由度机械臂。系统具备精确的力与位置控制、全身规划以及面向特定任务的学习型策略生成。应用场景包括农业采摘、生态研究中的野生动物监测，以及工业环境中的基础设施巡检与维修——这些任务中传统地面机器人可达性受限。通过推进平台设计与任务特定算法，本项目旨在拓展空中机器人的作业边界。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 扎实的机械设计能力与经验（SolidWorks、AutoCAD 等）；\n2. 基础嵌入式编程能力；\n3. 基础编程能力与经验（C/C++、Python 等）；\n4. 有 RoboMaster 竞赛经验者优先。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FUZHANG-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"自主影视航拍无人机","titleEn":"Autonomous Cinematic Filming UAV","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHANG Fu","moderator":"Dr. CHEN Nan","capacity":10,"availCap":10,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-25 20:35:45","descriptionText":"Given the demand for autonomous, rapid search, and comprehensive exploration of key targets in complex scenarios such as future cities and forests, a comprehensive study on high-speed unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) swarm collaborative detection technology is initiated to address challenges including difficult swarm applications and recovery, low efficiency in autonomous collaborative reconnaissance of swarms, and limited perception and cognition in complex scenes. By breaking through key technologies such as on-vehicle high-speed swarm catapult takeoff and efficient recovery, large-scale unknown scene swarm collaborative planning, and high-level semantic cognition and interaction in scenes, the aim is to develop a set of on-vehicle high-speed UAV swarm systems integrating rapid takeoff, operation, and recovery to enhance efficient collaborative reconnaissance capabilities in complex scenarios.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Basic mechanical design skill and experience(Solidworks, AutoCAD, etc.);\n2. Basic programming skills and experience (C/C++, Python, etc.);\n3. Candidates with experience in RoboMaster competitions are preferred;","descZh":"针对未来城市、森林等复杂场景中对关键目标进行自主、快速搜索与全面勘查的需求，本项目系统开展高速无人机集群协同侦察技术研究，以应对集群编组与回收困难、集群自主协同侦察效率低、复杂场景感知与认知受限等挑战。通过突破车载高速集群弹射起飞与高效回收、大规模未知场景集群协同规划、场景高层级语义认知与交互等关键技术，目标是开发一套集快速起飞、作业与回收于一体的车载高速无人机集群系统，提升复杂场景下的高效协同侦察能力。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备基础机械设计能力与经验（SolidWorks、AutoCAD 等）；\n2. 具备基础编程能力与经验（C/C++、Python 等）；\n3. 有 RoboMaster 竞赛经验者优先。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FUZHANG-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"带矢量推力推进的过驱动无人机","titleEn":"Over-actuated UAV with vectored-thrust propulsion","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHANG Fu","moderator":"Dr. CHEN Nan","capacity":10,"availCap":10,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-25 20:35:59","descriptionText":"The vector thrust system can significantly enhance the motion freedom of UAVs (unmanned aerial vehicles), for example, hovering in a tilted state. However, traditional vectored-thrust propulsion system relies on additional actuators (typically servos), leading to increased weight and slower response. The swashplateless mechanism is a structure that enables rotor blades to simultaneously generate thrust and torque (details at https://mars.hku.hk/papers/scirobotics.ade4538_.pdf). The direction and magnitude of the torque depend on high-frequency modulation of motor speed, but existing swashplateless mechanism systems still struggle to produce vector thrust with a wide range of directions. This project aims to combine the swashplateless mechanism with a 1-degree-of-freedom (DOF) or 2-DOF passive joint structure to realize a vector thrust system where the thrust direction can be widely deflected via the torque generated by swashplateless mechanism, and to apply it to UAVs. \nKey tasks include:\n1. Designing a concise and feasible mechanical structure to achieve thrust direction deflection.\n2. Developing hardware circuits to install electronic components (magnetic encoders, Hall sensors, or IMUs) at the joint angles and debugging the communication bus.\n3. Estimating joint angles (1-DOF or 2-DOF) or current thrust direction through dynamic modeling and sensor measurements.\n4. Using the estimation results as feedback to control the direction of vector thrust.\n5. Design some UAVs, with onboard computer and the vectored-thrust system.\n6. 6-DOF decoupled control of the UAV, or other UAV-related researches.\nThese tasks are expected to be completed collaboratively by several students, each possessing partial expertise.\nPre- and co-requisites (It doesn't need to meet all):\n1. Mechanical design skills & experiences (e.g., SolidWorks);\n2. Experiences and basic knowledge of electronic/circuit/PCB design (e.g., Altium designer, Cadence);\n3. Experiences of embedded system development (C/C++ language, STM32, etc.);\n4. Basic knowledge on control theory, controller design, or state estimation;\n5. Experiences on ROS development or machine learning;\n6. Experiences on robot competition are preferred (e.g., RoboMaster, RoboCon).","descZh":"矢量推力系统可显著提升无人机（UAV）的运动自由度，例如以倾斜姿态悬停。然而，传统矢量推力推进系统依赖额外执行器（通常是舵机），导致重量增加、响应变慢。无斜盘机构是一种使旋翼桨叶能够同时产生推力与扭矩的结构（详见 https://mars.hku.hk/papers/scirobotics.ade4538_.pdf）。扭矩方向与大小取决于电机转速的高频调制，但现有无斜盘系统仍难以产生大范围方向的矢量推力。本项目旨在将无斜盘机构与 1 自由度（DOF）或 2 自由度被动关节结构相结合，实现通过无斜盘机构产生的扭矩使推力方向大范围偏转的矢量推力系统，并将其应用于无人机。\n关键任务包括：\n1. 设计简洁可行的机械结构以实现推力方向偏转；\n2. 开发硬件电路，将电子元件（磁编码器、霍尔传感器或 IMU）安装于关节角处并调试通信总线；\n3. 通过动力学建模与传感器测量估计关节角（1-DOF 或 2-DOF）或当前推力方向；\n4. 以估计结果作为反馈控制矢量推力的方向；\n5. 设计搭载机载计算机与矢量推力系统的无人机；\n6. 实现无人机六自由度解耦控制，或开展其他无人机相关研究。\n这些任务预计由数名学生协作完成，每人具备部分专长。\n先修/并修要求（无需全部满足）：\n1. 机械设计能力与经验（如 SolidWorks）；\n2. 电子/电路/PCB 设计经验与基础知识（如 Altium Designer、Cadence）；\n3. 嵌入式系统开发经验（C/C++ 语言、STM32 等）；\n4. 控制理论、控制器设计或状态估计基础知识；\n5. 有 ROS 开发或机器学习经验；\n6. 有机器人竞赛经验者优先（如 RoboMaster、RoboCon）。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FUZHANG-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于巡检的自主激光雷达无人机","titleEn":"Autonomous LiDAR UAV for inspection","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHANG Fu","moderator":"Dr. CHEN Nan","capacity":10,"availCap":10,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-25 20:36:13","descriptionText":"Achieving robust autonomous flight in complex environments remains a significant challenge for Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs). This project tackles this challenge through the integrated development of two critical components: the UAV's mechanical platform and its perception intelligence. Students will focus on optimizing the drone's mechanical design – including structural stability, vibration damping, and precise mounting – to ensure high-quality data acquisition from integrated sensors (LiDAR, cameras, IMU, GNSS). Simultaneously, they will design and implement an advanced SLAM (Simultaneous Localization and Mapping) algorithm that intelligently fuses this sensor data. The ultimate goal is to create a UAV system where co-optimized mechanical engineering and SLAM algorithms enable reliable navigation and high-fidelity real-time mapping in demanding scenarios.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nProgramming: Basic proficiency in programming languages (e.g., C/C++, Python).\nElectronic design: Basic proficiency and experiences of circuit & PCB design (e.g., Altium designer, Cadence);\nRobotics: Foundational knowledge and experience in Robotics, including practical skills with ROS (Robot Operating System) and conceptual understanding of SLAM techniques.\nMechanical Design: Basic mechanical design skills and experience using CAD software (e.g., SolidWorks, AutoCAD, Fusion 360) relevant for designing or modifying mechanical components and assemblies.","descZh":"在复杂环境中实现稳健的自主飞行仍是无人机（UAV）面临的重要挑战。本项目通过集成开发两大关键部分来攻克该挑战：无人机机械平台与其感知智能。学生将聚焦优化无人机的机械设计——包括结构稳定性、减振与精密安装——以确保从集成传感器（LiDAR、相机、IMU、GNSS）获得高质量数据采集；同时设计并实现一种先进的 SLAM（同步定位与建图）算法，智能融合这些传感器数据。最终目标是构建一个机械工程与 SLAM 算法协同优化的无人机系统，使其在严苛场景中实现可靠导航与高保真实时建图。\n先修/并修要求：\n编程：具备基础编程语言能力（如 C/C++、Python）；\n电子设计：具备电路与 PCB 设计的基本能力与经验（如 Altium Designer、Cadence）；\n机器人学：具备机器人学基础知识与经验，包括 ROS（机器人操作系统）实操技能与对 SLAM 技术的概念性理解；\n机械设计：具备使用 CAD 软件（如 SolidWorks、AutoCAD、Fusion 360）进行机械部件与装配体设计或修改的基础技能与经验。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FUZHANG-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"室内移动操作机器人","titleEn":"Indoor Mobile Manipulation","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHANG Fu","moderator":"Dr. CHEN Nan","capacity":10,"availCap":10,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-25 21:07:01","descriptionText":"Mobile manipulators integrate indoor mobility with dexterous robotic arms, enabling robots to navigate, perceive, and manipulate objects within complex indoor spaces. This project develops a compact and stable platform with precise base–arm coordination, supporting autonomous navigation, human-in-the-loop teleoperation, and end-to-end control for various indoor manipulation tasks.\nThe system incorporates real-time perception, whole-body motion planning, and physics-based simulation for safe and efficient testing before real-world deployment. Example tasks include object retrieval from shelves, organizing workspaces, and operating in narrow or cluttered areas where fixed manipulators cannot reach.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Proficiency in Python and experience with PyTorch or similar machine learning frameworks;\n2. Experience with simulation platforms such as Gazebo or Isaac Sim;\n3. Hands-on experience in robot development and ROS/ROS 2 integration;\n4. Mechanical design skills (SolidWorks, AutoCAD, etc.) are a plus;\n5. Candidates with robotics competition experience are preferred.","descZh":"移动机械臂将室内移动能力与灵巧机械臂相结合，使机器人能够在复杂的室内空间中导航、感知与操作物体。本项目开发一种结构紧凑、运行稳定、基座-机械臂协调精准的平台，支持自主导航、人在回路遥操作以及面向多种室内操作任务的端到端控制。\n系统集成实时感知、全身运动规划与基于物理的仿真，以便在实际部署前进行安全高效的测试。示例任务包括从货架取物、整理工作空间，以及在固定机械臂无法到达的狭窄或杂乱区域作业。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 熟练掌握 Python，有 PyTorch 或类似机器学习框架使用经验；\n2. 有 Gazebo 或 Isaac Sim 等仿真平台经验；\n3. 有机器人开发实操经验及 ROS/ROS 2 集成经验；\n4. 具备机械设计能力（SolidWorks、AutoCAD 等）者优先；\n5. 有机器人竞赛经验者优先。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FUZHANG-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于多传感器的高速旋转运动图像去模糊","titleEn":"Multi-sensor-based image deblurring during high-speed rotation motion","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHANG Fu","moderator":"Dr. CHEN Nan","capacity":10,"availCap":10,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-25 21:07:01","descriptionText":"High-speed motion may lead to motion blur in camera images, resulting in unusable outputs. This project aims to set up a compact multi-sensor system (such as LiDAR, event cameras, IMU, RGB camera) on a rotating platform. These sensors will provide additional depth information for the RGB camera, and by combining model-based or learning-based techniques, the project will investigate the image deblurring methods. Additionally, when the camera rotates, it can observe multiple directions, significantly expanding the field of view. This project will also explore how to trigger the camera to capture images in a precise direction during rotation, generating non-blurred video streams from multiple directions.\nPre- and co-requisites (It doesn't need to meet all):\n1. Hands-on experience in robot development and ROS/ROS 2 system;\n2. Development experiences or basic knowledge of LiDAR or camera;\n3. Development experiences or basic knowledge of computer vision (e.g., OpenCV) or machine learning (e.g. PyTorch).","descZh":"高速运动可能导致相机图像出现运动模糊，从而产生无法使用的输出。本项目旨在在一个旋转平台上搭建一套紧凑的多传感器系统（如 LiDAR、事件相机、IMU、RGB 相机）。这些传感器将为 RGB 相机提供额外的深度信息，并通过结合基于模型或基于学习的技术，项目将研究图像去模糊方法。此外，当相机旋转时，它可以观察多个方向，从而显著扩大视场。本项目还将探索如何在旋转过程中触发相机在精确方向上捕获图像，从而从多个方向生成无模糊的视频流。\n先修和并修课程（无需全部满足）：\n1. 具有机器人开发和 ROS/ROS 2 系统的实践经验；\n2. 具有 LiDAR 或相机的开发经验或基础知识；\n3. 具有计算机视觉（例如 OpenCV）或机器学习（例如 PyTorch）的开发经验或基础知识。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FUZHANG-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"自主无人机集群系统","titleEn":"Autonomous UAV Swarm System","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHANG Fu","moderator":"Dr. CHEN Nan","capacity":10,"availCap":10,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-25 20:37:26","descriptionText":"Given the demand for autonomous, rapid search, and comprehensive exploration of key targets in complex scenarios such as future cities and forests, a comprehensive study on high-speed unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) swarm collaborative detection technology is initiated to address challenges including difficult swarm applications and recovery, low efficiency in autonomous collaborative reconnaissance of swarms, and limited perception and cognition in complex scenes. By breaking through key technologies such as on-vehicle high-speed swarm catapult takeoff and efficient recovery, large-scale unknown scene swarm collaborative planning, and high-level semantic cognition and interaction in scenes, the aim is to develop a set of on-vehicle high-speed UAV swarm systems integrating rapid takeoff, operation, and recovery to enhance efficient collaborative reconnaissance capabilities in complex scenarios.\n Pre- and co-requisites:\n 1. Basic mechanical design skill and experience(Solidworks, AutoCAD, etc.);\n 2. Basic programming skills and experience (C/C++, Python, etc.);\n 3. Candidates with experience in RoboMaster competitions are preferred;","descZh":"针对未来城市、森林等复杂场景中对关键目标进行自主、快速搜索与全面勘查的需求，本项目系统开展高速无人机集群协同侦察技术研究，以应对集群编组与回收困难、集群自主协同侦察效率低、复杂场景感知与认知受限等挑战。通过突破车载高速集群弹射起飞与高效回收、大规模未知场景集群协同规划、场景高层级语义认知与交互等关键技术，目标是开发一套集快速起飞、作业与回收于一体的车载高速无人机集群系统，提升复杂场景下的高效协同侦察能力。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 具备基础机械设计能力与经验（SolidWorks、AutoCAD 等）；\n2. 具备基础编程能力与经验（C/C++、Python 等）；\n3. 有 RoboMaster 竞赛经验者优先。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-FUZHANG-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"大规模多传感器移动测绘系统","titleEn":"Large-scale Multi-sensor Mobile Mapping System","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHANG Fu","moderator":"Dr. CHEN Nan","capacity":10,"availCap":10,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-25 21:07:01","descriptionText":"","descZh":"","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向残障人士的设计","titleEn":"Design for disables","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人；AI","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; Robotics; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-27 14:47:38","descriptionText":"This MSc project focuses on the design and development of inclusive products, systems, and environments that improve accessibility and quality of life for people with disabilities. Students will investigate user needs, apply universal and human-centered design principles, and develop innovative solutions through research, prototyping, and evaluation. The project encourages interdisciplinary approaches that integrate engineering, technology, ergonomics, and assistive innovations to enhance independence, mobility, communication, safety, and social participation for individuals with diverse physical, sensory, or cognitive abilities.\nFor example, allow a wheel chair to go over a kerb, which is the raised edge between a pavement and a road. This enhance wheelchair mobility by enabling users to safely and effortlessly traverse kerbs. The compact, attachable module integrates with standard wheelchairs and employs a mechanical lift or ramp system activated by a simple control interface. It improves accessibility in urban environments where kerbs often pose significant barriers. The device prioritizes user safety, stability, and ease of use, making it ideal for both manual and powered wheelchair.","descZh":"本硕士项目聚焦于包容性产品、系统与环境的设计与开发，以提升残障人士的可及性与生活质量。学生将调研用户需求，应用通用设计与以人为本的设计原则，并通过研究、原型制作与评估开发创新解决方案。项目鼓励融合工程、技术、人因与辅助创新的跨学科方法，以提升不同身体、感官或认知能力人群的独立性、行动力、沟通、安全与社会参与。\n例如：让轮椅能够越过路缘（人行道与道路之间的凸起边缘）。该方案通过让使用者安全、轻松地通过路缘来提升轮椅行动能力。这种紧凑的可加装模块可与标准轮椅集成，采用由简单控制界面启动的机械升降或坡道系统。它改善了路缘常构成显著障碍的城市环境中的可及性。该装置优先考虑使用者安全、稳定性与易用性，既适合手动轮椅也适合电动轮椅。","cats":["AI","DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"智能人机交互控制系统","titleEn":"Intelligent human interactive control system","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人；AI","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; Robotics; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-27 16:49:43","descriptionText":"This MSc project focuses on the development of intelligent human-machine interaction systems that enable intuitive and natural control of devices, robots, or virtual environments. Students may investigate technologies such as hand gesture recognition, head motion tracking, eye tracking, computer vision, electromyography (EMG), and sensor fusion. The project involves designing, implementing, and evaluating real-time interactive control solutions that enhance usability, accessibility, responsiveness, and user experience across applications in robotics, assistive technology, healthcare, smart systems, and immersive environments.","descZh":"本硕士项目聚焦于智能人机交互系统的开发，实现对设备、机器人或虚拟环境的直观、自然控制。学生可研究手势识别、头部运动跟踪、眼动跟踪、计算机视觉、肌电（EMG）与传感器融合等技术。项目涉及实时交互控制方案的设计、实现与评估，以提升可用性、可访问性、响应性与用户体验，应用于机器人、辅助技术、医疗健康、智能系统与沉浸式环境等领域。","cats":["AI","DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"多传感器无人机电机诊断与预测性维护系统","titleEn":"Multi-Sensor UAV Motor Diagnostics and Predictive Maintenance System","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人；AI","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; Robotics; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-27 17:07:02","descriptionText":"This project develops a UAV motor testing platform that integrates vibration sensing, acoustic analysis, thermal imaging, current monitoring, and thrust measurement. Machine learning algorithms will be applied to identify performance degradation and predict potential failures before they occur. Students will investigate condition-monitoring techniques and establish health indicators for UAV propulsion systems. The resulting system can improve operational safety, reduce maintenance costs, and enhance mission reliability.","descZh":"本项目开发一套无人机电机测试平台，集成振动感知、声学分析、热成像、电流监测与推力测量。将应用机器学习算法识别性能退化，并在故障发生前预测潜在失效。学生将研究状态监测技术，为无人机推进系统建立健康指标。所开发的系统可提升运行安全性、降低维护成本并增强任务可靠性。","cats":["AI","DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向可再生能源应用的自主太阳跟踪机器人","titleEn":"Autonomous Solar Tracking Robot for Renewable Energy Applications","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人；AI","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; Robotics; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-27 17:07:02","descriptionText":"This MSc project explores the design of a robotic solar tracking platform capable of autonomously adjusting photovoltaic panels for maximum energy capture. Students will integrate vision systems, GPS, environmental sensors, and intelligent control algorithms to enable adaptive solar tracking in changing weather conditions. Applications may include remote power stations, agricultural monitoring systems, and portable renewable energy units. The project combines robotics, control engineering, and sustainable energy technologies.","descZh":"本硕士项目探索设计一种机器人太阳能跟踪平台，能够自主调整光伏板以最大化能量捕获。学生将集成视觉系统、GPS、环境传感器与智能控制算法，实现在多变天气条件下的自适应太阳跟踪。应用场景可包括偏远电站、农业监测系统与便携式可再生能源单元。该项目结合了机器人学、控制工程与可持续能源技术。","cats":["AI","DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于Stewart平台的智能康复与辅助系统","titleEn":"Intelligent Rehabilitation and Assistive System Based on Stewart Platform","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人；AI","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; Robotics; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-27 17:07:02","descriptionText":"This project investigates the development of a Stewart-platform-based rehabilitation system for healthcare applications. Students will design adaptive motion control strategies using biomechanical data, computer vision, or wearable sensors to assist patient recovery and physical therapy. AI techniques may be employed to personalize treatment and assess rehabilitation progress. Applications include balance training, gait rehabilitation, and neuromuscular therapy. The project combines robotics, biomedical engineering, human-machine interaction, and intelligent control technologies.","descZh":"本项目研究基于 Stewart 平台的可用于医疗健康的康复系统开发。学生将利用生物力学数据、计算机视觉或可穿戴传感器设计自适应运动控制策略，以辅助患者康复与物理治疗。可采用 AI 技术实现治疗个性化并评估康复进展。应用包括平衡训练、步态康复与神经肌肉治疗。项目融合了机器人学、生物医学工程、人机交互与智能控制技术。","cats":["AI","DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"自主灯杆巡检机器人","titleEn":"Autonomous Light Pole Inspection Robot","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人；AI","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; Robotics; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-27 17:07:02","descriptionText":"This MSc project focuses on the development of an autonomous climbing robot for inspecting streetlight poles and urban infrastructure. The robot will use computer vision, AI-based defect detection, and sensor fusion technologies to identify corrosion, cracks, loose fittings, and structural damage. Students will design the climbing mechanism, navigation system, and inspection algorithms to enable safe and efficient operation. The project integrates robotics, machine vision, and smart city technologies to reduce maintenance costs and improve infrastructure safety.","descZh":"本硕士项目聚焦于开发一台用于路灯杆与城市基础设施检测的自主攀爬机器人。机器人将利用计算机视觉、基于 AI 的缺陷检测与传感器融合技术，识别锈蚀、裂缝、紧固件松动与结构损伤。学生将设计攀爬机构、导航系统与检测算法，以实现安全高效运行。该项目融合机器人学、机器视觉与智慧城市技术，以降低维护成本、提升基础设施安全性。","cats":["AI","DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-9","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于车底威胁检测的自主安检机器人","titleEn":"Autonomous Security Screening Robot for Under-Vehicle Threat Detection","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人；AI","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; Robotics; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-27 17:07:02","descriptionText":"This MSc project explores the development of an autonomous robotic platform for security inspection of vehicles at airports, ports, government facilities, and critical infrastructure sites. The robot will employ computer vision, thermal imaging, and AI algorithms to detect suspicious objects, tampering, and hidden threats. Students will design autonomous navigation, data fusion, and real-time alert systems. The project integrates robotics, security technologies, and intelligent surveillance systems.","descZh":"这一硕士项目探索开发一个自主机器人平台，用于在机场、港口、政府设施和关键基础设施场所对车辆进行安检。该机器人将采用计算机视觉、热成像和 AI 算法来检测可疑物体、篡改痕迹和隐藏威胁。学生将设计自主导航、数据融合和实时告警系统。该项目融合了机器人技术、安防技术和智能监控系统。","cats":["AI","DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-10","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向老年人的设计","titleEn":"Design for elderly","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-27 17:07:02","descriptionText":"This student project focuses on the Design for the Elderly, aiming to create practical and user-friendly solutions that enhance the quality of life for senior citizens. The project involves identifying common challenges faced by the elderly—such as mobility limitations, safety concerns, and ease of use—and designing products or systems that address these needs. \nExamples include ergonomic furniture, smart assistive devices, simplified user interfaces, or home automation features tailored for aging users. Students engage in user-centered design, prototyping, and testing to ensure accessibility, comfort, and reliability. \nThe project encourages empathy-driven innovation and promotes inclusive design principles. Through this work, students gain valuable experience in product development, human factors engineering, and social responsibility, contributing to a more age-friendly society.","descZh":"本项目聚焦于面向老年人的设计，旨在创造实用、易用的解决方案，提升老年人的生活质量。项目包括识别老年人面临的常见挑战（如行动受限、安全隐患与易用性问题），并设计满足这些需求的产品或系统。\n示例包括符合人因的家具、智能辅助设备、简化用户界面，或为老龄用户定制化的家居自动化功能。学生将开展以用户为中心的设计、原型制作与测试，确保可及性、舒适性与可靠性。\n项目鼓励以同理心驱动的创新，倡导包容性设计原则。通过这项工作，学生将在产品开发、人因工程与社会责任方面积累宝贵经验，为构建更适老的社会作出贡献。","cats":["DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"多功能海洋/河流机器人","titleEn":"Multiple function marine/river robot","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-27 17:07:02","descriptionText":"This student project focuses on developing a Multiple Function Marine/River Robot designed to operate in various aquatic environments for environmental monitoring, exploration, and cleanup tasks. Equipped with waterproof sensors, cameras, and modular tools, the robot can measure water quality, collect samples, detect pollutants, and retrieve floating debris. \nIt features autonomous navigation and obstacle avoidance using GPS and sonar systems, ensuring safe and efficient operation in rivers, lakes, or coastal areas. \nStudents gain hands-on experience in robotics, embedded systems, and environmental engineering while addressing real-world sustainability challenges. The project encourages innovation in eco-friendly technology and highlights the role of automation in preserving aquatic ecosystems.","descZh":"本项目旨在开发一种多功能水陆（海洋/河流）机器人，可在多种水生环境中执行环境监测、探索与清理任务。机器人配备防水传感器、相机与模块化工装，可测量水质、采集样本、检测污染物并打捞漂浮垃圾。其具备基于 GPS 与声呐系统的自主导航与避障能力，确保在河流、湖泊或近岸水域安全高效运行。学生将在机器人学、嵌入式系统与环境工程方面获得实操经验，同时应对现实世界的可持续性挑战。项目鼓励生态友好技术的创新，并彰显自动化在保护水生生态系统中的作用。","cats":["DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向韧性智能家居的自主防洪响应系统","titleEn":"Autonomous Flood Response System for Resilient Smart Homes","areaZh":"设计与制造","areaEn":"Design & manufacture","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-27 17:07:02","descriptionText":"This student project focuses on developing a Home Flooding Threat Warning Device that combines real-time mobile alerts with an automatic water shut-off system to prevent damage caused by indoor/outdoor flooding. \nIn case of indoor, the system uses water level sensors placed in vulnerable areas such as basements, kitchens, or bathrooms. When rising water is detected, it sends instant notifications to the user’s phone via SMS or a dedicated app. Simultaneously, it activates a motorized valve to stop the water supply, minimizing the risk of property damage. \nIn case of outdoor, the system capable of detecting flood threats and initiating protective actions without human intervention. The system may automatically deploy flood barriers, activate water pumps, disconnect electrical systems, and notify residents and emergency services.\nStudents gain practical experience in sensor technology, wireless communication, embedded systems, and smart home automation. The project highlights the importance of proactive disaster prevention and demonstrates how IoT solutions can enhance household safety and resilience against water-related emergencies.","descZh":"本项目聚焦于开发一种家庭水浸威胁预警装置，将实时移动报警与自动断水系统相结合，防止室内/室外水浸造成的损失。\n室内场景：系统在易积水区域（如地下室、厨房或浴室）布置水位传感器。检测到水位上升时，通过短信或专用应用即时向用户手机发送通知，同时启动电动阀切断供水，最大限度降低财产损失风险。\n室外场景：系统无需人工干预即可检测水浸威胁并启动防护措施，如自动部署防洪屏障、启动水泵、切断电气系统，并通知居民与应急部门。\n学生将在传感器技术、无线通信、嵌入式系统与智慧家居自动化方面获得实践技能。项目强调主动防灾的重要性，并展示物联网解决方案如何提升家庭应对水相关紧急情况的安全性与韧性。","cats":["DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-11","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向小规模园艺的自主耕作机器人","titleEn":"Autonomous Farming Robot for small-scale gardening","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-28 09:07:02","descriptionText":"This student project involves the creation of an Autonomous Farming Robot for Home Usage, designed to assist with small-scale gardening and urban farming tasks. The robot is equipped with sensors, actuators, and a microcontroller to perform functions such as soil monitoring, seed planting, watering, and weed detection. \nThe robot can navigates autonomously within a garden or planter area using obstacle avoidance and path planning algorithms. Controlled via a mobile app or voice commands, the robot provides real-time updates on plant health and environmental conditions. \nStudents gain hands-on experience in robotics, IoT, automation, and sustainable agriculture. The project promotes smart farming practices and empowers individuals to grow food efficiently at home, making agriculture more accessible and eco-friendly.","descZh":"本项目旨在研发一款家用自主耕作机器人，用于辅助小规模园艺与城市农耕任务。机器人配备传感器、执行器与微控制器，可执行土壤监测、播种、浇灌与杂草检测等功能。机器人可在花园或种植区内利用避障与路径规划算法自主导航；通过手机应用或语音指令控制，并实时反馈植物健康状况与环境条件。学生将在机器人学、物联网、自动化与可持续农业方面获得实操经验。项目倡导智慧农业实践，帮助个人在家高效种植食物，使农业更加可及与环保。","cats":["DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-12","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"产品开发中的加速寿命测试（ALT）","titleEn":"Accelerated Life Testing (ALT) in product development","areaZh":"设计与制造","areaEn":"Design & manufacture","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-28 09:07:02","descriptionText":"This MSc dissertation project explores the application of Accelerated Life Testing (ALT) in product development to predict long-term reliability within a shortened testing period. Students will investigate accelerated stress factors such as temperature, vibration, humidity, mechanical loading, and electrical stress to identify potential failure mechanisms and estimate product lifespan. The project may involve the design of experimental test procedures, data acquisition systems, statistical reliability analysis, and lifetime prediction models using methods such as Weibull analysis and Arrhenius-based acceleration models. Emphasis will be placed on integrating ALT into the product development cycle to improve design robustness, reduce development time, lower warranty costs, and enhance product quality and customer satisfaction.","descZh":"本硕士论文项目探索在产品开发中应用加速寿命试验（ALT），以在缩短的测试周期内预测长期可靠性。学生将研究温度、振动、湿度、机械载荷与电应力等加速应力因素，识别潜在失效机理并估算产品寿命。项目可能涉及试验流程设计、数据采集系统、统计可靠性分析以及基于威布尔分析、Arrhenius 加速模型等方法寿命预测模型的构建。重点是将 ALT 融入产品开发周期，以提升设计稳健性、缩短开发时间、降低保修成本，并提高产品质量与客户满意度。","cats":["DES"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-13","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于AI的智能家居设计","titleEn":"Smart home design with AI","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人；AI","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; Robotics; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-28 09:07:02","descriptionText":"This MSc dissertation project focuses on the design and development of an intelligent AI-powered smart home ecosystem that enhances comfort, energy efficiency, safety, health monitoring, and personalized living experiences. Students will integrate technologies such as computer vision, voice recognition, IoT sensors, digital twins, and machine learning to create adaptive home environments that learn occupants' behaviors and preferences.\nInnovative research directions may include AI-based energy optimization, predictive maintenance of home appliances, elderly care and fall detection systems, emotion-aware living spaces, intelligent security monitoring, autonomous service robots, and edge-AI implementations for privacy-preserving decision-making. The project may also explore human-centered design, smart home cybersecurity, and sustainability through renewable energy integration. Students will develop a prototype system, evaluate user experience and performance, and investigate how next-generation AI technologies can transform future homes into personalized, resilient, and intelligent living environments.","descZh":"本硕士论文项目聚焦于智能、AI 驱动的智慧家居生态系统的设计与开发，以提升舒适度、能效、安全性、健康监测与个性化居住体验。学生将集成计算机视觉、语音识别、物联网传感器、数字孪生与机器学习等技术，构建能学习居住者行为与偏好的自适应家居环境。\n创新研究方向可包括：基于 AI 的能源优化、家电预测性维护、老人照护与跌倒检测系统、情绪感知居住空间、智能安防监控、自主服务机器人，以及面向隐私保护决策的边缘 AI 实现。项目还可探索以人为本的设计、智慧家居网络安全，以及通过可再生能源集成实现可持续性。学生将开发原型系统，评估用户体验与性能，并研究下一代 AI 技术如何将未来家居转变为个性化、有韧性的智能居住环境。","cats":["AI","DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-14","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向工程设计协作的沉浸式混合现实平台","titleEn":"Immersive Mixed Reality Platform for Engineering Design Collaboration","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人；AI","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; Robotics; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-28 09:07:02","descriptionText":"This MSc dissertation project aims to develop an immersive Mixed Reality (MR) platform that enhances engineering design collaboration among geographically distributed teams. The system will integrate Augmented Reality (AR), Virtual Reality (VR), 3D CAD models, and AI-assisted design tools to enable real-time visualization, interaction, and co-creation of engineering products. Students may investigate digital twin integration, gesture and voice-based interaction, haptic feedback, and AI-driven design recommendations to improve decision-making and creativity. The project will evaluate user experience, collaboration efficiency, and design quality, demonstrating how immersive technologies can transform product development, engineering education, and multidisciplinary innovation in Industry 4.0 environments.","descZh":"本硕士论文项目旨在开发一个沉浸式混合现实（MR）平台，以增强地理上分散的团队之间的工程设计协作。系统将集成增强现实（AR）、虚拟现实（VR）、3D CAD 模型与 AI 辅助设计工具，实现工程产品的实时可视化、交互与共创。学生可研究数字孪生集成、手势与语音交互、触觉反馈以及 AI 驱动设计建议，以改善决策与创造力。项目将评估用户体验、协作效率与设计质量，展示沉浸式技术如何在工业 4.0 环境中变革产品开发、工程教育与多学科创新。","cats":["AI","DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-15","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"带混合现实与触觉反馈的沉浸式遥操作机器人","titleEn":"Immersive Teleoperation Robot with Mixed Reality and Haptic Feedback","areaZh":"设计与制造；机器人","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-28 09:07:02","descriptionText":"This MSc dissertation project focuses on the development of an immersive teleoperation robotic system that combines Mixed Reality (MR), haptic feedback, artificial intelligence, and advanced robotic control technologies. The system will enable operators to remotely perform complex tasks in hazardous, remote, or inaccessible environments with enhanced situational awareness and precision. Students may investigate AR/VR-based interfaces, force-feedback devices, computer vision, digital twins, and AI-assisted manipulation for real-time interaction and decision support.\nPotential applications include industrial inspection, healthcare, disaster response, and space exploration. The project will evaluate system performance, usability, operational efficiency, and user experience, contributing to the next generation of intelligent human-robot interaction systems.","descZh":"本理学硕士（MSc）论文项目专注于开发一个沉浸式遥操作机器人系统，该系统结合了混合现实（MR）、触觉反馈、人工智能和先进的机器人控制技术。该系统将使操作员能够在危险、偏远或无法到达的环境中远程执行复杂任务，并具备更强的态势感知能力和精度。学生可以研究基于 AR/VR 的界面、力反馈设备、计算机视觉、数字孪生以及 AI 辅助的操作，用于实时交互和决策支持。\n潜在应用包括工业检测、医疗保健、灾难响应和太空探索。本项目将评估系统性能、可用性、运营效率和用户体验，为下一代智能人机交互系统做出贡献。","cats":["DES","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-CKCHAN1-16","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"家居产品设计","titleEn":"Household Product Design","areaZh":"设计与制造；物联网；AI","areaEn":"Design & manufacture; IOT; AI","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-28 09:07:02","descriptionText":"This MSc dissertation project focuses on the innovative design and development of smart, user-centered household products that enhance convenience, safety, sustainability, and quality of life. Students will apply design thinking, ergonomics, engineering design, and rapid prototyping techniques to create products that address real-world household challenges. \nThe project may incorporate emerging technologies such as artificial intelligence, Internet of Things (IoT), smart sensors, sustainable materials, and additive manufacturing. Potential applications include intelligent kitchen appliances, automated storage systems, home assistive devices, and energy-efficient products. Students will develop functional prototypes, evaluate user experience, and optimize product performance, manufacturability, and environmental impact to meet future consumer needs.","descZh":"本硕士论文项目聚焦于智能化、以用户为中心的家居产品的创新设计与开发，旨在提升便利性、安全性、可持续性与生活质量。学生将运用设计思维、人因工程、工程设计及快速原型制作技术，创造能够解决现实家庭挑战的产品。项目可融合人工智能、物联网（IoT）、智能传感器、可持续材料与增材制造等新兴技术。潜在应用包括智能厨房电器、自动储物系统、家庭辅助设备与节能产品。学生将开发功能原型、评估用户体验，并对产品性能、可制造性与环境影响进行优化，以满足未来消费者需求。","cats":["AI","DES","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIYG-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向无人机安全的城市风加速识别","titleEn":"Identifying urban wind acceleration for drone safety","areaZh":"风工程","areaEn":"Wind engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Yuguo","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description Sudden strong winds during drone\r\nlanding can damage drones and pose hazards to people and property. Candidates\r\nare expected to understand the mechanisms of wind acceleration in urban\r\nenvironments. The first candidate may investigate three observed real-world\r\ncases in Hong Kong in which winds accelerated during a typhoon and caused major\r\nglass shattering. The second candidate may examine how building-layout\r\ncharacteristics contribute to this wind-acceleration phenomenon. Both projects\r\nshould propose practical measures and recommendations for drone safety in urban\r\nsettings. \n Prerequisite: Basic knowledge of fluid dynamics,\r\nand a willingness to learn basic meteorology. Preferred skills: \r\nFamiliarity with CFD software (ANSYS Fluent).","descZh":"项目描述：无人机降落期间的突发强风可能损坏无人机，并对人员与财产构成危险。候选学生需要理解城市环境中风加速的机理。第一个方向可研究香港观测到的三起真实案例——台风期间风速加速并造成大面积玻璃破碎；第二个方向可考察建筑布局特征如何促成这种风加速现象。两个方向都应针对城市环境中无人机的安全提出实用措施与建议。\n先修要求：流体力学基础知识，并愿意学习基础气象学。\n偏好技能：熟悉 CFD 软件（ANSYS Fluent）。","cats":["ENE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIYG-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向无人机安全的城市风加速识别","titleEn":"Identifying urban wind acceleration for drone safety","areaZh":"风工程","areaEn":"Wind engineering","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Yuguo","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description Sudden strong winds during drone\r\nlanding can damage drones and pose hazards to people and property. Candidates\r\nare expected to understand the mechanisms of wind acceleration in urban\r\nenvironments. The first candidate may investigate three observed real-world\r\ncases in Hong Kong in which winds accelerated during a typhoon and caused major\r\nglass shattering. The second candidate may examine how building-layout\r\ncharacteristics contribute to this wind-acceleration phenomenon. Both projects\r\nshould propose practical measures and recommendations for drone safety in urban\r\nsettings. \n Prerequisite: Basic knowledge of fluid dynamics,\r\nand a willingness to learn basic meteorology. Preferred skills: \r\nFamiliarity with CFD software (ANSYS Fluent).","descZh":"项目描述 无人机降落过程中的突发强风可能损坏无人机，并对人员和财产构成危害。候选人应理解城市环境中风力加速（wind acceleration）的机理。第一位候选人可研究香港观测到的三个真实案例，在这些案例中，台风期间风力加速并造成严重的玻璃碎裂。第二位候选人可研究建筑布局特征如何促成这一风力加速现象。两个项目都应提出针对城市环境中无人机安全的实用措施和建议。\n先修要求：流体动力学基础知识，以及学习基础气象学的意愿。偏好技能：熟悉 CFD 软件（ANSYS Fluent）。","cats":["ENE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIYG-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于物联网的室内环境监测与机器学习预测 A","titleEn":"IoT-based monitoring of indoor environments and machine-learning prediction A","areaZh":"室内环境与机器学习","areaEn":"Indoor environments and machine learning","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Yuguo","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description Low-cost monitoring of indoor\r\nenvironments provides essential data for occupants (comfort and health) and\r\nbuilding operators. In this project, you are expected to survey and compare\r\ncommercially available sensors for temperature, humidity, CO₂ and particles;\r\ndevelop deployable sensor units for IoT applications; and/or apply machine\r\nlearning to time-series prediction. \n Prerequisite: Basic knowledge of electronics. \n Preferred skills: Machine-learning prediction or\r\nsensor development.","descZh":"项目描述：对室内环境的低成本监测，可为室内人员（舒适度与健康）及建筑运营者提供关键数据。在本项目中，你需调研并比较市面上可用于温度、湿度、CO₂ 与颗粒物监测的传感器；开发可部署的传感器单元用于物联网（IoT）应用；和/或将机器学习应用于时间序列预测。\n先修要求：电子学基础知识。\n偏好技能：机器学习预测或传感器开发。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIYG-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于物联网的室内环境监测与机器学习预测 B","titleEn":"IoT-based monitoring of indoor environments and machine-learning prediction B","areaZh":"室内环境与机器学习","areaEn":"Indoor environments and machine learning","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Yuguo","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description Low-cost monitoring of indoor\r\nenvironments provides essential data for occupants (comfort and health) and\r\nbuilding operators. In this project, you are expected to survey and compare\r\ncommercially available sensors for temperature, humidity, CO₂ and particles;\r\ndevelop deployable sensor units for IoT applications; and/or apply machine\r\nlearning to time-series prediction. \n Prerequisite: Basic knowledge of electronics. \n Preferred skills: Machine-learning prediction or\r\nsensor development.","descZh":"项目描述：对室内环境的低成本监测，可为室内人员（舒适度与健康）及建筑运营者提供关键数据。在本项目中，你需调研并比较市面上可用于温度、湿度、CO₂ 与颗粒物监测的传感器；开发可部署的传感器单元用于物联网（IoT）应用；和/或将机器学习应用于时间序列预测。\n先修要求：电子学基础知识。\n偏好技能：机器学习预测或传感器开发。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIYG-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于物联网的室内环境监测与机器学习预测 C","titleEn":"IoT-based monitoring of indoor environments and machine-learning prediction C","areaZh":"室内环境与机器学习","areaEn":"Indoor environments and machine learning","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Yuguo","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description Low-cost monitoring of indoor\r\nenvironments provides essential data for occupants (comfort and health) and\r\nbuilding operators. In this project, you are expected to survey and compare\r\ncommercially available sensors for temperature, humidity, CO₂ and particles;\r\ndevelop deployable sensor units for IoT applications; and/or apply machine\r\nlearning to time-series prediction. \n Prerequisite: Basic knowledge of electronics. \n Preferred skills: Machine-learning prediction or\r\nsensor development.","descZh":"项目描述：对室内环境的低成本监测，可为室内人员（舒适度与健康）及建筑运营者提供关键数据。在本项目中，你需调研并比较市面上可用于温度、湿度、CO₂ 与颗粒物监测的传感器；开发可部署的传感器单元用于物联网（IoT）应用；和/或将机器学习应用于时间序列预测。\n先修要求：电子学基础知识。\n偏好技能：机器学习预测或传感器开发。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIYG-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"小型穹顶城市模型搭建","titleEn":"Construction of small-scale dome city models","areaZh":"力学","areaEn":"Mechanics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Yuguo","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description Dome roofs have been considered as\r\na possible future city form. Domes are known for their structural stability and\r\nstrength. Many dome types exist, including geodesic and Schwedler domes, each\r\nwith multiple subtypes, and each may be configured as a single-, double- or\r\ntriple-layer structure. Knowledge of solid mechanics will be used to design,\r\ndevelop and construct new small-scale models for structural testing. Each\r\nstudent may focus on one dome type. \n Prerequisite: Mechanics. \n Preferred skills: General-purpose finite-element\r\nanalysis (FEA) software.","descZh":"项目描述：穹顶屋顶被视为未来城市形态的一种可能。穹顶以其结构稳定性与强度著称。穹顶类型众多，包括测地线（geodesic）穹顶和施威德勒（Schwedler）穹顶，每种又有多种子类型，且可构造成单层、双层或三层结构。将运用固体力学知识设计、开发并搭建新型缩尺模型用于结构测试。每名学生可专注于一种穹顶类型。\n先修要求：力学。\n偏好技能：通用有限元分析（FEA）软件。","cats":["MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIYG-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"小型穹顶城市模型搭建 B","titleEn":"Construction of small-scale dome city models B","areaZh":"力学","areaEn":"Mechanics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Yuguo","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description Dome roofs have been considered as\r\na possible future city form. Domes are known for their structural stability and\r\nstrength. Many dome types exist, including geodesic and Schwedler domes, each\r\nwith multiple subtypes, and each may be configured as a single-, double- or\r\ntriple-layer structure. Knowledge of solid mechanics will be used to design,\r\ndevelop and construct new small-scale models for structural testing. Each\r\nstudent may focus on one dome type. \n Prerequisite: Mechanics. \n Preferred skills: General-purpose finite-element\r\nanalysis (FEA) software.","descZh":"项目描述：穹顶屋顶被视为未来城市形态的一种可能。穹顶以其结构稳定性与强度著称。穹顶类型众多，包括测地线（geodesic）穹顶和施威德勒（Schwedler）穹顶，每种又有多种子类型，且可构造成单层、双层或三层结构。将运用固体力学知识设计、开发并搭建新型缩尺模型用于结构测试。每名学生可专注于一种穹顶类型。\n先修要求：力学。\n偏好技能：通用有限元分析（FEA）软件。","cats":["MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIYG-9","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"数据中心空调 A","titleEn":"Air conditioning for data centres A","areaZh":"传热与流体力学","areaEn":"Heat transfer and fluid mechanics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Yuguo","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description With the growth of AI, data\r\ncentres have become a significant energy consumer, and air conditioning\r\ntypically accounts for 10-40% of IT power. You are expected to explore\r\ndifferent air-conditioning approaches for data centres, including air-cooled\r\nsystems with various air-distribution methods, and liquid cooling. \n Prerequisite: Heat transfer and fluid mechanics.\r\n Preferred skills: CFD software and energy-modelling\r\nsoftware.","descZh":"项目描述：随着人工智能的发展，数据中心已成为重要的能耗大户，其中空调通常占 IT 功耗的 10%~40%。你需探索数据中心的不同空调方案，包括采用不同送风方式的空气冷却系统以及液冷方案。\n先修要求：传热与流体力学。\n偏好技能：CFD 软件与能耗建模软件。","cats":["ENE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIYG-10","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"数据中心空调 B","titleEn":"Air conditioning for data centres B","areaZh":"传热与流体力学","areaEn":"Heat transfer and fluid mechanics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Yuguo","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description With the growth of AI, data\r\ncentres have become a significant energy consumer, and air conditioning\r\ntypically accounts for 10-40% of IT power. You are expected to explore\r\ndifferent air-conditioning approaches for data centres, including air-cooled\r\nsystems with various air-distribution methods, and liquid cooling. \n Prerequisite: Heat transfer and fluid mechanics.\r\n Preferred skills: CFD software and energy-modelling\r\nsoftware.","descZh":"项目描述：随着人工智能的发展，数据中心已成为重要的能耗大户，其中空调通常占 IT 功耗的 10%~40%。你需探索数据中心的不同空调方案，包括采用不同送风方式的空气冷却系统以及液冷方案。\n先修要求：传热与流体力学。\n偏好技能：CFD 软件与能耗建模软件。","cats":["ENE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIYG-11","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"数据中心空调 C","titleEn":"Air conditioning for data centres C","areaZh":"传热与流体力学","areaEn":"Heat transfer and fluid mechanics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Yuguo","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project description With the growth of AI, data\r\ncentres have become a significant energy consumer, and air conditioning\r\ntypically accounts for 10-40% of IT power. You are expected to explore\r\ndifferent air-conditioning approaches for data centres, including air-cooled\r\nsystems with various air-distribution methods, and liquid cooling. \n Prerequisite: Heat transfer and fluid mechanics.\r\n Preferred skills: CFD software and energy-modelling\r\nsoftware.","descZh":"项目描述：随着人工智能的发展，数据中心已成为重要的能耗大户，其中空调通常占 IT 功耗的 10%~40%。你需探索数据中心的不同空调方案，包括采用不同送风方式的空气冷却系统以及液冷方案。\n先修要求：传热与流体力学。\n偏好技能：CFD 软件与能耗建模软件。","cats":["ENE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LUPENG-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"水下蛇形机器人的设计与控制","titleEn":"Design and Control for an underwater snake robot","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LU Peng","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"The underwater snake robot is an emerging research hotspot in the field of field robotics. Inspired by biological anguilliform locomotion, an underwater snake robot possesses hyper-redundant degrees of freedom (DOFs). This unique capability makes it exceptionally suited for navigating complex underwater environments and negotiating narrow submerged structures. However, key bottlenecks limiting its real-world application lie in high hydrodynamic drag, accurate underwater state perception, and stable motion control under dynamic ocean currents.\nTo tackle these challenges, this research will focus on robust mechanical Design, integrated Sensing, and adaptive Control:\n Design: Develop a modular, waterproof, and pressure-resistant mechanical architecture with optimized hydrodynamic profiles to minimize drag while balancing structural compliance and payload capacity.\n Control: Implement Central Pattern Generator networks coupled with hydrodynamic model-based feedback to achieve efficient swimming gaits, dynamic depth regulation, and robust trajectory tracking under strong current disturbances.\nProspective students are expected to possess expert skills in SolidWorks/ANSYS for mechanical modeling and hydrodynamic simulation, alongside strong programming abilities (C++/ROS/Python) for real-time system integration. High hands-on capability and strong self-motivation are essential to drive this cutting-edge research forward.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Able to design models of the underwater snake using SolidWorks or CAD software.\n2. Basic skills in hardware electronic systems.\n3. Programming skills like C/ C++, python.\n4. Interested in robotics, better with related hardware experience.\nSpecial note (if any):\nPlease remember to contact the following person even if you have already applied in the system:\nContact: lplp8989 (wechat), indicate the project number and keywords","descZh":"水下蛇形机器人是现场机器人领域的新兴研究热点。受生物鳗鲡式运动启发，水下蛇形机器人拥有超冗余自由度（DOF），这种独特能力使其特别适合在复杂水下环境中导航并穿越狭窄的水下结构。然而，限制其实际应用的关键瓶颈在于高水动力阻力、精确的水下状态感知以及动态洋流下的稳定运动控制。\n为应对这些挑战，本研究将聚焦稳健的机械设计、集成感知与自适应控制：\n- 设计：开发模块化、防水、耐压的机械架构，优化水动力外形以最大限度减小阻力，同时平衡结构柔顺性与载荷能力；\n- 控制：实现中枢模式发生器（CPG）网络并结合基于水动力模型的反馈，以实现高效游动步态、动态深度调节以及在强流扰动下的稳健轨迹跟踪。\n学生应具备使用 SolidWorks/ANSYS 进行机械建模与水动力仿真的专长，以及用于实时系统集成的扎实编程能力（C++/ROS/Python）。需要较高的动手能力与强烈的自我驱动力来推进这项前沿研究。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 能使用 SolidWorks 或 CAD 软件设计水下蛇形机器人模型；\n2. 具备基础硬件电子系统技能；\n3. 具备 C/C++、Python 等编程能力；\n4. 对机器人有兴趣，有相关硬件经验者更佳。\n特别说明：即使在系统中已提交申请，也请务必联系以下联系人：lplp8989（微信），并注明项目编号与关键词。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LUPENG-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"软体（或连续体）机械臂的创新与专业化设计","titleEn":"Innovative and professional design of a soft (or continuum) robotic arm","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LU Peng","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Soft robotics is an emerging research hotpot in the field of robotics. A soft robotic arm is made of fundamentally soft material, and is totally different from traditional manipulators in modeling and control. With infinite degrees of freedom (DOFs), the soft robotic arm is capable of dexterous motions, which fit for compliance with unstructed environments. \nThis project aims to improve the design, of cable-driven soft (or continuum) manipulator to make it more professional and stable. Students should be able to use Solidworks expertly, and programming skills are expected. Also, the hands-on ability and self-motivation are required. Students need to select better materials for the continuum arm.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Able to design models of the manipulator or use a manipulator, by using Solidworks or CAD software.\n2. Programming skills like C/ C++, python.\n3. Interested in robotics, better with related hardware experience.\nSpecial note (if any):\nPlease remember to contact the following person even if you have already applied in the system:\nContact: lplp8989 (wechat), indicate the project number","descZh":"软体机器人是机器人领域的新兴研究热点。软体机械臂由本质上柔软的材料制成，在建模与控制方面与传统机械臂完全不同。软体机械臂拥有无限自由度（DOF），能够实现灵巧运动，适合与无结构环境顺应交互。\n本项目旨在改进绳驱软体（连续体）机械臂的设计，使其更专业、更稳定。学生应能熟练使用 SolidWorks，并具备编程能力；同时要求动手能力与自我驱动力。学生需要为连续体机械臂选择更合适的材料。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 能使用 SolidWorks 或 CAD 软件设计机械臂模型或操作机械臂；\n2. 具备 C/C++、Python 等编程能力；\n3. 对机器人有兴趣，有相关硬件经验者更佳。\n特别说明：即使在系统中已提交申请，也请务必联系以下联系人：lplp8989（微信），并注明项目编号。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LUPENG-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"爬壁机器人的创新设计与控制","titleEn":"Innovative design and control of a wall-climbing robot","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LU Peng","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"A wall-climbing robot (WCR) is an electromechanical system designed to overcome gravity and navigate vertical or steep surfaces like walls, ceilings, pipelines, and ship hulls.\nThe objective of this project is to design and control a wall climbing robot. The students need to investigate different adhesion systems to reach a large load capacity (preferably more than 10 kg). It must be able to climb walls with curvature adaptability. The robot should be able to traverse walks with different materials, including magnetic metal walls, non-magnetic concrete walls, and curved structural surfaces, to avoid being limited to a single application scenario. Students can also investigate different types of locomotion mechanisms to climb the wall such as rolling to climb the wall.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Skilled at SolidWorks or CAD software.\n2. Basic skills in hardware electronic systems.\n3. Programming skills like C/ C++, python.\n4. Different students can consider different types of locomotion. \n5. Interested in robotics, better with related hardware experience.\nSpecial Notes:\nPlease remember to contact the following person even if you have already applied in the system:\nContact: lplp8989 (wechat), indicate the project number","descZh":"爬壁机器人（WCR）是一种机电系统，旨在克服重力并在墙壁、天花板、管道与船体等垂直或陡峭表面上移动。\n本项目的目标是设计并控制一台爬壁机器人。学生需要研究不同的吸附系统，以实现较大的负载能力（最好超过 10 公斤）。它必须能够攀爬墙壁并具备曲率适应能力。该机器人应能够跨越不同材料的表面，包括磁性金属墙、非磁性混凝土墙以及曲面结构表面，以避免局限于单一应用场景。学生还可以研究不同类型的攀爬运动机构，例如滚动式攀墙。\n先修与同修要求：\n1. 熟练使用 SolidWorks 或 CAD 软件。\n2. 具备硬件电子系统的基本技能。\n3. 具备 C/C++、Python 等编程技能。\n4. 不同学生可考虑不同类型的运动方式。\n5. 对机器人感兴趣，最好有相关的硬件经验。\n特别说明：\n即使你已在系统中申请，也请务必联系以下人员：\n联系方式：lplp8989（微信），注明项目编号","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LUPENG-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"自然语言语义引导的无人机目标跟踪与智能摄像控制","titleEn":"UAV Target Tracking and Intelligent Cinematography Control Guided by Natural Language Semantics","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LU Peng","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":true,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This project proposes a vision-language model (VLM)-driven intelligent framework for UAV target cinematography. It converts photographers’ natural language instructions into executable flight control commands to track arbitrary targets and adaptively adjust shooting poses. Traditional UAV follow-shot systems require manual bounding box initialization and category-specific object detectors. Such systems can only track predefined targets and rely on proficient piloting skills to tune shooting viewpoints. To bridge the semantic gap between high-level cinematographic intentions and low-level UAV motion control, this work leverages the cross-modal reasoning ability of state-of-the-art VLMs to break down colloquial shooting instructions into two core subtasks: linguistic target grounding and relative pose parameterization. First, guided by natural language descriptions, the framework conducts real-time open-set target localization on aerial visual streams, supporting tracking of any user-specified objects without pre-trained category-specific detectors. Second, it builds a quantitative mapping that converts ambiguous spatial descriptions into measurable relative pose parameters, including target-relative horizontal distance, azimuth angle and vertical elevation. The complete pipeline will be validated and comprehensively benchmarked on both simulation environments and real-world physical UAV platforms. This research expands the application paradigm of vision-language models to closed-loop robot control. It delivers an intuitive, low-barrier human-UAV interaction scheme for aerial cinematography, with broad application prospects in sports videography, documentary shooting and consumer drone filmmaking. \nPre- and co-requisites:\n5. Familiar with the fundamentals of vision-language models or open-vocabulary object grounding and tracking tasks.\n6. Programming skills like C/ C++, python.\n7. Interested in robotics, UAV systems and multimodal AI research, with strong self-motivation and ability to work independently.\nSpecial note (if any):\nPlease remember to contact the following person even if you have already applied in the system:\nContact: lys_13021923188 (wechat), indicate the project number","descZh":"本项目提出一种视觉-语言模型（VLM）驱动的无人机目标航拍智能框架：将摄影师的自然语言指令转化为可执行的飞控命令，以跟踪任意目标并自适应调整拍摄姿态。传统无人机跟拍系统需要手动框选初始边界框与类别特定的目标检测器；此类系统只能跟踪预定义目标，并依赖熟练的飞手技术来调整拍摄视角。为弥合高层级影视意图与低层级无人机运动控制之间的语义鸿沟，本工作利用最先进 VLM 的跨模态推理能力，将口语化的拍摄指令分解为两个核心子任务：语言目标定位与相对位姿参数化。首先，框架在自然语言描述引导下，对空中视觉流进行实时开放集目标定位，无需预训练的类别特定检测器即可跟踪用户指定的任意物体。其次，构建定量映射，将模糊的空间描述转化为可测量的相对位姿参数，包括相对目标的水平距离、方位角与垂直仰角。完整流程将在仿真环境与真实物理无人机平台上进行验证与全面基准测试。本研究将视觉-语言模型的应用范式扩展到闭环机器人控制，为空中航拍提供直观、低门槛的人机交互方案，在体育摄像、纪录拍摄与消费级无人机影像创作中具有广阔应用前景。\n先修/并修要求：\n1. 熟悉视觉-语言模型或开放词汇目标定位与跟踪任务的基础；\n2. 具备 C/C++、Python 等编程能力；\n3. 对机器人、无人机系统与多模态 AI 研究有兴趣，具备较强自我驱动力与独立工作能力。\n特别说明：即使在系统中已提交申请，也请务必联系以下联系人：lys_13021923188（微信），并注明项目编号。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LUPENG-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于视觉-语言-动作模型的双臂灵巧操作学习","titleEn":"Learning Bimanual Dexterous Manipulation with Vision-Language-Action Models","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LU Peng","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":true,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Recent advances in Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have shown strong potential for robotic manipulation. This project focuses on extending VLA methods to bimanual systems with dexterous hands, targeting representative tasks such as pick-and-place, object transfer, handover, and coordinated two-arm manipulation. The project will investigate scalable learning and post-training methods to improve real-world manipulation capability and generalization. Possible research directions include, but are not limited to:\n1. Bimanual VLA adaptation and human-in-the-loop post-training: adapting existing VLA models to dual-arm and dexterous-hand embodiments while developing teleoperation systems for human intervention and corrective data collection for post-training.\n2. Real-to-sim and video-to-robot data scaling: extracting manipulation behaviors from human or robot videos and converting them into large-scale robot-compatible data for VLA training.\n3. Cross-embodiment and heterogeneous data learning: leveraging manipulation data from different robots, human demonstrations, and embodiments to improve data efficiency, transferability, and generalization.\n4. Multimodal and self-improving manipulation: incorporating depth, force, tactile sensing, and autonomous data collection to improve contact-rich manipulation and enable continual policy improvement.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n Strong programming skills in Python and familiarity with PyTorch.\n Experience with robotics software and simulation tools such as ROS, Isaac Sim, and MuJoCo.\n Basic knowledge of robot learning, reinforcement learning, imitation learning, computer vision, or VLA models.\n Experience with robotic manipulation, dexterous hands, bimanual systems, humanoid robots, quadruped robots, teleoperation, or real-robot deployment is highly desirable.\nPlease remember to contact the following person even if you have already applied in the system:\nContact: kiterunner347 (wechat), indicate the project number","descZh":"视觉-语言-动作（VLA）模型的最新进展已显示出推动机器人操作的巨大潜力。本项目聚焦将 VLA 方法扩展至带灵巧手的双臂系统，目标任务包括抓放、物体传递、交接与双臂协调操作。项目将研究可扩展的学习与后训练方法，以提升真实世界操作能力与泛化性。可能的研究方向包括（但不限于）：\n1. 双臂 VLA 适配与人机交互后训练：将现有 VLA 模型适配到双臂与灵巧手形态，同时开发遥操作系统用于人工介入与后训练的纠错数据采集；\n2. 真实-仿真与视频-机器人数据扩展：从人类或机器人视频中提取操作行为，转化为大规模机器人兼容数据用于 VLA 训练；\n3. 跨形态与异构数据学习：利用不同机器人、人类示范与形态的操作数据，提升数据效率、可迁移性与泛化能力；\n4. 多模态与自我改进操作：融入深度、力、触觉传感与自主数据采集，提升接触丰富操作能力并实现策略持续改进。\n先修/并修要求：\n- 扎实的 Python 编程能力并熟悉 PyTorch；\n- 有 ROS、Isaac Sim、MuJoCo 等机器人软件与仿真工具经验；\n- 具备机器人学习、强化学习、模仿学习、计算机视觉或 VLA 模型基础知识；\n- 有机器人操作、灵巧手、双臂系统、人形机器人、四足机器人、遥操作或实物机器人部署经验者优先。\n即使在系统中已提交申请，也请务必联系以下联系人：kiterunner347（微信），并注明项目编号。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LUPENG-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于状态依赖运动先验的人形机器人在复杂受限环境中的稳健恢复","titleEn":"Robust Humanoid Recovery in Complex and Constrained Environments via State-Dependent Motion Priors","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LU Peng","moderator":"","capacity":6,"availCap":6,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Humanoid robots must recover from falls safely in unstructured environments. Current learned controllers often work only on flat ground from a narrow set of poses, and may produce violent joint motion, foot shuffling, or failure when terrain and objects constrain the body. This project will develop a deployable reinforcement-learning framework for natural and robust recovery on the Unitree G1. A state-dependent motion prior will combine retargeted human recovery demonstrations with task rewards. Training will cover supine, prone, side-lying, and intermediate poses; external pushes; uneven, stepped, and compliant terrain; and cases where the torso is partially pinned by a movable object. The deployed policy will use only onboard-observable proprioception, including joint states, base motion, and projected gravity; contact and environment information may be used only during training. Research topics include diverse resets, geometry-aware escape rewards, hand and knee support, staged prone-to-kneel-to-squat transitions, and constraints on joint speed, torque, and power. Evaluation will measure recovery success and time, peak torque and power, contact quality, motion naturalness, generalization, and sim-to-real transfer.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n4. Strong Python or C++ and Linux skills; PyTorch experience is preferred.\n5. Knowledge of robot dynamics, control, or reinforcement learning; MuJoCo experience is beneficial.\n6. Interest in humanoid robotics and systematic experimentation; hardware experience is desirable.\nSpecial note (if any):\nPlease remember to contact the following person even if you have already applied in the system:\nContact: yidan_lu (wechat), indicate the project number","descZh":"人形机器人必须能够在非结构化环境中安全地从摔倒中恢复。当前基于学习（learned）的控制器通常只能在平坦地面上、从一组狭窄的姿态中工作，当地形和物体约束身体时，可能会产生剧烈的关节运动、脚步拖蹭（foot shuffling）或失败。本项目将在 Unitree G1 上开发一个可部署的强化学习框架，实现自然且鲁棒的摔倒恢复。一个依赖状态的运动先验（state-dependent motion prior）将把重定向（retargeted）的人类恢复演示与任务奖励结合起来。训练将覆盖仰卧、俯卧、侧卧以及中间姿态；外部推力；不平坦、带台阶和柔顺的地形；以及躯干被可移动物体部分压住的情形。部署后的策略将仅使用机载可观测的本体感觉（proprioception），包括关节状态、基座运动和投影重力；接触和环境信息只能在训练期间使用。研究主题包括多样化重置、几何感知的脱困奖励、手部和膝部支撑、分阶段的“俯卧→跪姿→蹲姿”转换，以及对关节速度、力矩和功率的约束。评估将测量恢复成功率和恢复时间、峰值力矩和功率、接触质量、运动自然度、泛化能力以及从仿真到现实（sim-to-real）的迁移。\n先修和并修课程：\n4. 扎实的 Python 或 C++ 以及 Linux 技能；优先考虑具有 PyTorch 经验者。\n5. 具备机器人动力学、控制或强化学习方面的知识；具有 MuJoCo 经验者更佳。\n6. 对人形机器人和系统性实验感兴趣；具有硬件经验者更佳。\n特别说明（如有）：\n请记住，即使你已经在系统中提交了申请，也要联系以下人员：\n联系方式：yidan_lu（微信），并注明项目编号","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LUPENG-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"足式机器人机械结构的工业设计与优化","titleEn":"Industrial Design and optimization for the mechanical structure of a legged robot","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LU Peng","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Inspired by animal locomotion in nature, robotic engineers have pursued legged robot development for decades to achieve similar dynamic capabilities. Unlike wheeled, tracked, or aerial robots, legged systems excel in environmental interaction, demonstrating superior terrain traversal through flexible limbs and enhanced mobility.\nThis project focuses on designing and implementing a motor-driven legged robot capable of flexible, compliant, and adaptable movements, making innovative improvements based on existing mechanical structures.\nParticipants will need hands-on fabrication ability, self-motivation, and the capacity to troubleshoot mechanical assemblies.\nPre- and co-requisites:\n1. Able to design models of some mechanical parts of a robot, by using SolidWorks or CAD software.\n2. Interested in robotics, better with related hardware experience.\nPlease remember to contact the following person even if you have already applied in the system:\nContact: dazaogao (wechat), indicate the project number","descZh":"受自然界动物运动方式的启发，机器人工程师数十年来一直致力于足式机器人的开发，以实现类似的动态能力。与轮式、履带式或空中机器人不同，足式系统在环境交互方面表现出色，通过灵活的肢体和更强的机动性展现出优越的地形穿越能力。\n本项目专注于设计和实现一个电机驱动的足式机器人，能够进行灵活、柔顺和适应性强的运动，并在现有机械结构的基础上进行创新改进。\n参与者需要具备动手制造能力、自我驱动能力以及排查机械装配问题的能力。\n先修及并修要求：\n1. 能够使用 SolidWorks 或 CAD 软件设计机器人某些机械部件的模型。\n2. 对机器人学感兴趣，最好有相关的硬件经验。\n即使你已经在系统中申请，也请记得联系以下人员：\n联系方式：dazaogao（微信），并注明项目编号","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LUPENG-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向高性能机器人执行器的先进电机控制算法与Si/GaN驱动电子器件","titleEn":"Advanced Motor Control Algorithms and Si/GaN Drive Electronics for High-Performance Robotic Actuator","areaZh":"机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Robotics; drones and control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LU Peng","moderator":"","capacity":6,"availCap":6,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"High-performance electric actuators are essential to the dynamic capability, efficiency, and reliability of next-generation legged and humanoid robots. This project aims to develop an integrated motor-drive platform through the co-design of advanced control algorithms, embedded systems, and power-electronic hardware. On the algorithmic side, conventional field-oriented control (FOC) will be extended using deadbeat predictive current control (DPCC), model predictive control (MPC), online motor-parameter identification, adaptive field-weakening control, disturbance estimation, inverter-nonlinearity compensation, and high-bandwidth rotor-state estimation through the fusion of encoder-based and sensorless observers. The developed algorithms will improve control accuracy and robustness under parameter variations, voltage constraints, sensor errors, and rapidly changing loads. On the hardware side, compact motor-drive boards based on both silicon MOSFETs and gallium-nitride (GaN) devices will be developed and iteratively optimized, covering gate-driver design, sensing circuits, high-frequency PCB layout, thermal management and protection. The project will also evaluate domestically manufactured alternatives for key power devices, drivers, sensors, and control ICs, supporting cost reduction, supply-chain resilience, and technological independence.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nBasic knowledge of engineering, electronics, control, or programming(C/C++).\nExperience in embedded software development or hardware design. Participation in electronic design competitions or other engineering competitions is preferred.\nWillingness to learn, conduct experiments, and participate in practical development and debugging.\nSpecial note (if any):\nPlease remember to contact the following person even if you have already applied in the system:\nContact: wzx419642898 (wechat), indicate the project number","descZh":"高性能电驱动器对下一代腿足与人形机器人的动态能力、效率与可靠性至关重要。本项目旨在通过先进控制算法、嵌入式系统与电力电子硬件的协同设计，开发集成式电机驱动平台。算法方面，将在传统磁场定向控制（FOC）基础上扩展无差拍预测电流控制（DPCC）、模型预测控制（MPC）、电机参数在线辨识、自适应弱磁控制、扰动估计、逆变器非线性补偿，以及融合编码器与无传感器观测器的高带宽转子状态估计。所开发算法将在参数变化、电压约束、传感器误差与快速变化负载下提升控制精度与鲁棒性。硬件方面，将开发并迭代优化基于硅 MOSFET 与氮化镓（GaN）器件的紧凑电机驱动板，涵盖栅极驱动设计、采样电路、高频 PCB 布局、热管理与保护。项目还将评估关键功率器件、驱动器、传感器与控制 IC 的国产替代方案，支持降本、供应链韧性与技术自主。\n先修/并修要求：具备工程、电子、控制或编程（C/C++）基础知识；有嵌入式软件开发或硬件设计经验；参加过电子设计竞赛或其他工程竞赛者优先；愿意学习、开展实验并参与实际开发与调试。\n特别说明：即使在系统中已提交申请，也请务必联系以下联系人：wzx419642898（微信），并注明项目编号。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PCOBB-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"机械工程考古学：改进现有自动化考古筛分机","titleEn":"MechE Archaeology: Improving Existing Automated Archaeological Siever","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor COBB Peter J","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-30 09:31:33","descriptionText":"Archaeologists study the human past using the material objects left behind, from pottery and stone tools, to architectural remains. As part of this process, we spend a lot of time excavating - removing dirt from the ground and carefully recording the 3d volume we are digging and the artifacts that come out of that volume of dirt. Archaeologists carefully collect, label, and store ancient artifacts such as pottery fragments, stone tools, and animal bones for future study. Archaeologists need to work faster to preserve the past, but our own experience digging in Armenia shows us that we spend most of our time just physically moving dirt rather than conducting primary research. The slower we are, the more data we never get to collect. \n One of our main time constraints is on sieving the dirt - filtering it through a mesh (sometimes with holes 4mm across, or different other sizes) to separate the artifacts, while the plain dirt falls down through the holes. During fieldwork, we loosen the soil with picks and trowels. We check the soil for artifacts that we collect into carefully labeled bags, while recording the location of the discoveries. During the manual process, we then sweep up the loose dirt into buckets which are carried away. These buckets of dirt are then put through a sieve filter to find any smaller artifacts that were missed during the digging. \nIn a prior year, a group of Mechanical students built an automated siever. This device had three layers of increasingly small mesh to enhance the speed of sorting objects of various sizes. This device used vibrators to provide motion to shake the dirt through the meshes. However, this device had several problems, such as it was too heavy, too loud, and there were issues with the mesh sizes and their interchangeability, and removing the unwanted remains from the final bottom mesh. We now have all these data collected, so we need the next team to start directly where the prior team left off, then fix everything for testing again in Armenia.\n In the past, this project has also explored potential ways to move the dirt automatically from the point of excavation to the sieve, using vacuum, spiral dirt movers, etc. If a team has a good idea for this, we would be interested in exploring. Also, we have had past teams try to think of ways to automate the digging process itself, such as with robots.\n We seek students who have enthusiasm and an ability to complete projects! You must also be able to communicate in English very well.\n Students who have an interest and do a good job could consider volunteering to join our archaeological fieldwork project in the country of Armenia for a few weeks between May and July. There, you can finish implementing your project and learn how to excavate while you touch real ancient artifacts.","descZh":"考古学家利用遗留的物质遗存（从陶器、石器到建筑遗迹）研究人类过去。在这一过程中，我们花费大量时间进行发掘——清除泥土，仔细记录我们挖掘的三维体积以及从该泥土体积中出土的文物。考古学家仔细收集、标注并储存陶片、石器与动物骨骼等古代文物以供未来研究。考古学家需要加快工作速度以保护历史，但我们在亚美尼亚的发掘经验表明，大部分时间只是物理性地搬运泥土，而非开展主要研究。速度越慢，我们永远无法采集到的数据就越多。\n我们最主要的时间限制之一来自过筛泥土——将其通过网筛（孔洞有时为 4mm 或其他尺寸）过滤，分离出文物，普通泥土则从孔洞漏下。野外作业中，我们用镐与泥铲松动土壤，检查土壤中的文物并装入仔细标注的袋子，同时记录发现位置。手工过程中，我们随后将松土扫入桶中运走，这些桶装泥土再过筛，寻找挖掘时遗漏的更小文物。\n往年一组机械专业学生搭建了一台自动过筛机：装置有三层孔径逐渐减小的网筛，以提升对不同尺寸物体的分选速度，并利用振动器提供运动使泥土通过网筛。然而该装置存在若干问题：过重、噪音过大、网孔尺寸及其可互换性存在问题，且难以清除最底层网筛上不需要的残留物。我们现在已收集所有这些数据，因此需要下一组从上一组的成果直接起步，修复所有问题，再赴亚美尼亚重新测试。\n过去该项目还探索过利用真空、螺旋泥土输送器等将泥土从发掘点自动运送至筛子的可能方式；若团队有好想法，我们也愿意探索。此外，过去团队还曾尝试思考如何使挖掘过程本身自动化，例如用机器人实现。\n我们希望招收有热情、有能力完成项目的学生！你还必须具备良好的英语沟通能力。\n有兴趣且表现优秀的学生可考虑在 5 月至 7 月间自愿参加我们在亚美尼亚的考古田野项目数周。在那里，你可以完成项目实现，并亲手触碰真实古代文物学习发掘。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-PENGDUAN-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"CycloRotor驱动的电动垂直起降飞行器","titleEn":"CycloRotor-Powered eVTOL Aircraft","areaZh":"流体动力学；计算流体力学；飞行设计","areaEn":"Fluid dynamics; Computational Fluid Dynamics; Flight design","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor DUAN Penghao","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This MSc project will investigate the aerodynamic design and performance of an electric vertical take-off and landing (eVTOL) aircraft powered by CycloRotors. Unlike conventional propellers, a CycloRotor employs multiple blades rotating around a horizontal axis with cyclically varying pitch, enabling rapid and continuous thrust-vector control. This capability offers potential advantages in aircraft manoeuvrability, compactness, gust rejection, transition between hover and forward flight, and precise operation in confined urban environments.\nThe project will develop a conceptual CycloRotor-powered eVTOL configuration and evaluate its feasibility across representative flight conditions. A low-order aerodynamic model will first be established to predict blade forces, rotor thrust, power consumption and efficiency. Computational fluid dynamics may then be used to examine unsteady blade–wake interactions, dynamic stall and rotor–airframe interference. Key design variables—including rotor diameter, blade number, chord, rotational speed, pitch schedule and rotor arrangement—will be optimized to balance lift capacity, energy efficiency, structural constraints and aerodynamic noise.\nThe expected outcomes include a validated performance model, an optimized preliminary aircraft design and a comparison with conventional propeller- or ducted-fan-powered eVTOL concepts. The project will provide new understanding of CycloRotor aerodynamics and assess its potential as a highly manoeuvrable propulsion system for low-altitude urban mobility, logistics and emergency-response aircraft.\nPreferred Skills: A strong foundation in thermodynamics, fluid mechanics and heat transfer is preferred. Experience with CFD software, such as ANSYS Fluent or OpenFOAM, and proficiency in MATLAB or Python would be advantageous. Familiarity with CAD, numerical optimization or flight dynamics modelling is desirable.","descZh":"本硕士项目将研究由 CycloRotor（摆线旋翼）驱动的电动垂直起降（eVTOL）飞行器的气动设计与性能。与传统螺旋桨不同，CycloRotor 采用多片桨叶绕水平轴旋转并周期性改变桨距，可实现快速、连续的推力矢量控制。该能力在飞行器机动性、紧凑性、抗阵风、悬停与前飞过渡，以及在受限城市环境中精确运行等方面具有潜在优势。\n项目将开发一种 CycloRotor 驱动的 eVTOL 概念构型，并评估其在代表性飞行条件下的可行性。首先建立低阶气动模型，预测桨叶力、旋翼推力、功耗与效率；随后可用计算流体力学考察非定常桨叶-尾流相互作用、动态失速与旋翼-机体干扰。将对旋翼直径、桨叶数、弦长、转速、桨距规律与旋翼布置等关键设计变量进行优化，以在升力能力、能效、结构约束与气动噪声之间取得平衡。\n预期成果包括经过验证的性能模型、优化的初步飞行器设计，以及与常规螺旋桨或涵道风扇驱动 eVTOL 概念的对比。项目将为 CycloRotor 空气动力学提供新的认识，并评估其作为低空城市出行、物流与应急响应飞行器高机动推进系统的潜力。\n偏好技能：具备扎实的热力学、流体力学与传热基础；有 ANSYS Fluent 或 OpenFOAM 等 CFD 软件经验，以及熟练使用 MATLAB 或 Python 者优先；熟悉 CAD、数值优化或飞行动力学建模者更佳。","cats":["AERO","FLU"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PENGDUAN-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向eVTOL安全降落的变桨距叶片","titleEn":"Variable-Pitch Blade for Safe eVTOL Landing","areaZh":"流体动力学；计算流体力学；飞行设计","areaEn":"Fluid dynamics; Computational Fluid Dynamics; Flight design","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor DUAN Penghao","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This MSc project will investigate a blade pitching for improving the landing safety of electric vertical take-off and landing (eVTOL) aircraft. During a rapid descent or partial/complete propulsion failure, the pitch angles of the rotor blades will be actively adjusted so that the rotors generate aerodynamic lift and drag, decelerating the aircraft before touchdown. Where feasible, the system will exploit autorotation, allowing the airflow through the rotors to maintain rotor speed without continuous motor power.\nThe project will examine the relationships among blade pitch, rotor rotational speed, descent velocity, aerodynamic forces and energy recovery using CFD method. Blade-element momentum theory and computational fluid dynamics will be used to model rotor performance under descending and autorotative conditions. These aerodynamic models will be integrated with an eVTOL flight-dynamics model to evaluate aircraft stability, descent rate and landing impact velocity. An optimization study will determine suitable blade-pitch schedules for different failure scenarios, aircraft weights and operating conditions. \nThe expected outcomes include a validated aerodynamic model, an optimized emergency pitch-control strategy, and a quantitative assessment of the reduction in descent and touchdown velocities. The research will establish the feasibility and limitations of using existing eVTOL rotors as a lightweight, rapidly deployable aerodynamic safety system.\nPreferred Skills: A strong foundation in thermodynamics, fluid mechanics and heat transfer is preferred. Experience with CFD software, such as ANSYS Fluent or OpenFOAM, and proficiency in MATLAB or Python would be advantageous. Familiarity with CAD, numerical optimization or flight dynamics modelling is desirable.","descZh":"本硕士项目将研究变桨技术以提升电动垂直起降（eVTOL）飞行器的着陆安全性。在快速下降或部分/完全推进失效时，旋翼桨距角将被主动调节，使旋翼产生气动升力与阻力，在触地前使飞行器减速。在可行情况下，系统将利用自转（autorotation），使穿过旋翼的气流维持旋翼转速而无需持续电机功率。\n项目将采用 CFD 方法研究桨距角、旋翼转速、下降速度、气动力与能量回收之间的关系。将使用叶素动量理论与计算流体力学对下降与自转条件下的旋翼性能建模。这些气动模型将与 eVTOL 飞行动力学模型集成，评估飞行器稳定性、下降率与着陆冲击速度。优化研究将针对不同失效场景、飞行器重量与运行条件确定合适的桨距控制规律。\n预期成果包括经过验证的气动模型、优化的应急变桨控制策略，以及对下降与触地速度降低幅度的定量评估。研究将确立使用现有 eVTOL 旋翼作为轻量、可快速部署的气动安全系统的可行性与局限。\n偏好技能：具备扎实的热力学、流体力学与传热基础；有 ANSYS Fluent 或 OpenFOAM 等 CFD 软件经验，以及熟练使用 MATLAB 或 Python 者优先；熟悉 CAD、数值优化或飞行动力学建模者更佳。","cats":["AERO","FLU"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PENGDUAN-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于低空经济飞行器微型燃气轮机的紧凑型换热器","titleEn":"Compact Heat Exchanger for Micro Gas Turbines in Low-Altitude-Economy Aircraft","areaZh":"流体动力学；计算流体力学；传热","areaEn":"Fluid dynamics; Computational Fluid Dynamics; heat transfer","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor DUAN Penghao","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This MSc project will investigate the design and optimization of a compact recuperative heat exchanger for micro gas turbines used in hybrid-electric aircraft serving the low-altitude economy (LAE). By transferring thermal energy from the turbine exhaust to the compressed air before combustion, the recuperator can improve cycle efficiency, reduce fuel or hydrogen consumption, and extend aircraft range. However, these benefits must be balanced against additional weight, volume, pressure loss and thermal stress, which are particularly critical for eVTOL applications.\nThe project will first establish heat-exchanger requirements based on the micro gas turbine operating cycle and representative eVTOL mission profiles. One-dimensional thermal and aerodynamic models will be developed to compare candidate configurations, such as plate-fin and primary-surface recuperators. Computational fluid dynamics and conjugate heat-transfer simulations will then be used to examine flow distribution, heat-transfer enhancement, pressure losses and local thermal gradients. Multi-objective optimization will be conducted to maximize heat-recovery effectiveness and power-to-weight performance while minimizing pressure loss and component size. Off-design operation during take-off, cruise and landing will also be assessed.\nThe expected outcomes include an optimized heat-exchanger geometry, validated performance maps, and system-level estimates of efficiency and range improvements. The study will provide practical design guidance for lightweight, high-performance recuperators for next-generation LAE propulsion systems.\nPreferred Skills: A strong foundation in thermodynamics, fluid mechanics and heat transfer is preferred. Experience with CFD software, such as ANSYS Fluent or OpenFOAM, and proficiency in MATLAB or Python would be advantageous. Familiarity with CAD, numerical optimization, gas-turbine cycles or conjugate heat-transfer modelling is desirable.","descZh":"本硕士项目将研究用于服务低空经济（LAE）的混合电推飞机中微型燃气轮机紧凑型回热换热器的设计与优化。通过在燃烧前将涡轮排气热能传递给压缩空气，回热器可提升循环效率、降低燃油或氢气消耗并延长航程。然而，这些收益必须与额外重量、体积、压损与热应力相平衡——这些对 eVTOL 应用尤为关键。\n项目将首先根据微型燃气轮机运行循环与代表性 eVTOL 任务剖面确定换热器需求；开发一维热力与气动模型，比较板翅式与一次表面式回热器等候选构型；随后使用计算流体力学与共轭传热仿真，考察流动分布、传热强化、压损与局部热梯度；开展多目标优化，在最小化压损与部件尺寸的同时，最大化回热有效度与功率-重量性能；还将评估起飞、巡航与着陆期间的偏离设计运行。\n预期成果包括优化的换热器几何、经过验证的性能图谱，以及效率与航程提升的系统级估算。研究将为下一代 LAE 推进系统轻量化、高性能回热器提供实用设计指导。\n偏好技能：具备扎实的热力学、流体力学与传热基础；有 ANSYS Fluent 或 OpenFOAM 等 CFD 软件经验，以及熟练使用 MATLAB 或 Python 者优先；熟悉 CAD、数值优化、燃气轮机循环或共轭传热建模者更佳。","cats":["ENE","FLU"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PENGDUAN-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"数据中心CPU冷板冷却的设计与优化","titleEn":"Design and Optimization of Cold-Plate Cooling for Data-Centre CPUs","areaZh":"流体动力学；计算流体力学；传热","areaEn":"Fluid dynamics; Computational Fluid Dynamics; heat transfer","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor DUAN Penghao","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This MSc project will investigate advanced liquid-cooled cold plates for thermal management of high-power CPUs in data centres. As processor power density continues to increase, conventional air cooling becomes insufficient, leading to high chip temperatures, localized hotspots and greater energy consumption. Direct-to-chip liquid cooling offers substantially higher heat-removal capability, but its performance depends strongly on cold-plate geometry, coolant distribution and operating conditions.\nThe project will develop numerical models of heat conduction through the CPU package and conjugate heat transfer within the cold plate. Computational fluid dynamics will be used to evaluate different internal structures, including parallel microchannels, pin fins, jet impingement and non-uniform channels tailored to the CPU heat-flux map. The effects of coolant flow rate, inlet temperature, channel dimensions, manifold configuration and surface roughness will be investigated. Multi-objective optimization will be performed to minimize peak CPU temperature, temperature non-uniformity, pressure loss and pumping power. Where facilities permit, a prototype cold plate will be manufactured and tested using a controllable heater that reproduces realistic CPU heat loads.\nThe expected outcomes include a validated thermal-fluid model, an optimized cold-plate design, and performance maps covering representative data-centre operating conditions. The project will provide practical guidance for energy-efficient cooling of next-generation CPUs and high-density computing systems.\nPreferred Skills: A strong foundation in thermodynamics, fluid mechanics and heat transfer is preferred. Experience with CFD software, such as ANSYS Fluent or OpenFOAM, and proficiency in MATLAB or Python would be advantageous. Familiarity with CAD, numerical optimization, gas-turbine cycles or conjugate heat-transfer modelling is desirable.","descZh":"本硕士项目将研究用于数据中心高功率 CPU 热管理的先进液冷冷板。随着处理器功率密度持续提升，常规风冷已显不足，导致芯片温度过高、局部热点与更高能耗。直接芯片级液冷提供显著更高的排热能力，但其性能强烈依赖冷板几何、冷却液分布与运行条件。\n项目将开发 CPU 封装内的热传导与冷板内共轭传热的数值模型。将采用计算流体力学评估不同内部结构，包括平行微通道、针翅、射流冲击以及针对 CPU 热流图定制的非均匀通道。将研究冷却液流量、入口温度、通道尺寸、歧管构型与表面粗糙度的影响。将开展多目标优化，使峰值 CPU 温度、温度不均匀度、压损与泵功最小化。在设施允许条件下，将制造原型冷板，并用再现真实 CPU 热负载的可控加热器进行测试。\n预期成果包括经过验证的热-流模型、优化的冷板设计，以及覆盖代表性数据中心运行工况的性能图谱。项目将为下一代 CPU 与高密度计算系统的节能冷却提供实用指导。\n偏好技能：具备扎实的热力学、流体力学与传热基础；有 ANSYS Fluent 或 OpenFOAM 等 CFD 软件经验，以及熟练使用 MATLAB 或 Python 者优先；熟悉 CAD、数值优化、燃气轮机循环或共轭传热建模者更佳。","cats":["ENE","FLU"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YHAOZHOU-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向皮肤健康评估的微型便携式粘弹性传感器研制","titleEn":"Development of a miniaturized portable viscoelasticity sensor for skin health evaluation","areaZh":"便携式生物电子；粘弹性；器件设计","areaEn":"Portable bioelectronics; viscoelasticity; device design","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHOU Yihao","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-27 12:04:01","descriptionText":"Viscoelasticity is a fundamental mechanical property of soft materials and biological tissues. Unlike purely elastic materials, soft tissues exhibit time-dependent mechanical responses when subjected to external deformation, reflecting the combined contributions of their elastic and viscous components. Such viscoelastic behavior is closely related to tissue composition, microstructure, hydration, and physiological condition. In human skin, changes in viscoelasticity can be associated with aging, dehydration, fibrosis, inflammation, and various skin disorders. Therefore, quantitative characterization of skin viscoelasticity can provide useful mechanical bio-markers for evaluating tissue health, monitoring disease progression, and assessing treatment outcomes. \nDespite its importance, the measurement of soft-tissue viscoelasticity is still largely performed using conventional mechanical testing systems or specialized clinical instruments. These systems are often bulky, expensive, and difficult to operate outside laboratory or hospital environments. In addition, many existing devices require complicated sample preparation, large testing areas, or highly controlled measurement conditions. These limitations significantly restrict their applications in routine skin assessment, point-of-care testing, and continuous or repeated health monitoring. Therefore, there is a strong need for a compact and portable system that can provide rapid and quantitative measurement of tissue viscoelasticity.\nThis project aims to develop a miniaturized and portable viscoelasticity sensor based on the stress-relaxation principle. The proposed device will apply a controlled deformation to the surface of a soft material and simultaneously measure the resulting force as a function of time. By analyzing the resulting stress-relaxation curve, characteristic parameters such as relaxation time, relaxation magnitude, and effective viscoelastic properties can be extracted. The sensor will integrate a compact mechanical loading mechanism, force sensing components, and electronic data acquisition into a portable platform suitable for convenient measurements. \nThe performance of the developed system will first be systematically evaluated using representative soft materials with different mechanical and viscoelastic properties. The measurements obtained from the portable sensor will be compared with those from conventional mechanical testing methods to evaluate its accuracy, repeatability, and sensitivity. Different loading conditions, including deformation magnitude, loading rate, and measurement duration, will also be investigated to determine an appropriate testing protocol for reliable viscoelastic characterization.\nAfter validation using soft materials, the developed sensor will be further applied to the measurement of human skin. Stress-relaxation responses from different skin locations and under different physiological conditions will be investigated to explore the capability of the device for detecting variations in skin mechanical properties. Ultimately, this project is expected to establish a miniaturized, low-cost, and easy-to-use platform for quantitative viscoelastic characterization of soft materials and biological tissues. The developed technology may provide a foundation for future applications in skin health evaluation, disease monitoring, point-of-care assessment, and personalized healthcare.\npre- and co-requisites: Electronics Design, Solid mechanics, polymer physics \npreferred skills: Electronics Design, Solid mechanics, Finite Element Method, Solidworks","descZh":"粘弹性是软材料与生物组织的基本力学性质。与纯弹性材料不同，软组织在受外部变形时表现出时间相关力学响应，反映其弹性与粘性组分的共同贡献。这种粘弹行为与组织成分、微结构、水合状态与生理状况密切相关。在人体皮肤中，粘弹性变化可与衰老、脱水、纤维化、炎症及多种皮肤疾病相关。因此，皮肤粘弹性的定量表征可为评估组织健康、监测疾病进展与评估治疗结果提供有用的力学生物标志物。\n尽管重要，软组织粘弹性的测量仍主要依赖常规力学测试系统或专用临床仪器。这些系统往往笨重、昂贵，且难以在实验室或医院环境之外操作。此外，许多现有设备需要复杂样品制备、大面积测试或高度受控的测量条件。这些局限显著限制了其在常规皮肤评估、即时检测及持续或重复健康监测中的应用。因此，迫切需要一种紧凑、便携、能快速定量测量组织粘弹性的系统。\n本项目旨在基于应力松弛原理开发微型化便携粘弹性传感器。拟开发的设备将对软材料表面施加受控变形，并同时测量随时间变化的力。通过分析所得应力松弛曲线，可提取松弛时间、松弛幅度与有效粘弹性质等特征参数。传感器将紧凑机械加载机构、力传感元件与电子数据采集集成为便于测量的便携平台。\n将首先使用具有不同力学与粘弹性质的代表性软材料系统评估所开发系统的性能；将便携传感器测得结果与常规力学测试方法比较，评估其精度、可重复性与灵敏度。还将研究不同加载条件（包括变形幅度、加载速率与测量时长），确定可靠粘弹表征的合适测试方案。\n在软材料验证后，所开发传感器将进一步应用于人体皮肤测量。将研究不同皮肤部位与不同生理条件下皮肤位置的应力松弛响应，探索设备检测皮肤力学性质变化的能力。最终，本项目预期建立一个用于软材料与生物组织定量粘弹表征的微型化、低成本、易用平台。所开发技术可为皮肤健康评估、疾病监测、即时检测与个性化医疗的未来应用奠定基础。\n先修/并修要求：电子设计、固体力学、高分子物理。\n偏好技能：电子设计、固体力学、有限元方法、Solidworks。","cats":["DES","ELE","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-YHAOZHOU-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于阻尼朗之万动力学的二元偶极-惰性胶体混合物自组装","titleEn":"Self-assembly of binary Dipolar-inert colloidal mixtures via damped langevin dynamics","areaZh":"分子动力学；磁偶极相互作用；软物质；胶体","areaEn":"Molecular dynamics; magnetic dipolar interaction; soft materials; colloids","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor ZHOU Yihao","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-27 12:04:01","descriptionText":"Self-assembly describes a process in which small particles organize themselves into larger structures through interactions between the particles. It is widely found in soft materials, colloidal systems, and biological systems. Understanding this process is important because the behavior of a soft material at the macroscopic scale is often determined by how its microscopic components interact and arrange themselves. Therefore, studying particle-level interactions can help us understand how the structure and functionality of soft materials emerge.\nDipolar colloids are a useful model system for studying self-assembly. These particles interact with each other through direction-dependent dipole-dipole interactions. As a result, they can form different structures such as chains, clusters, and networks. These structures can strongly influence the overall properties of the material. In many practical systems, however, the material does not contain only one type of particle. It may contain both functional particles and other particles that do not directly participate in the main interaction. \nThis project will study a binary colloidal system containing both dipolar and non-dipolar particles. Although the non-dipolar particles do not have dipole-dipole interactions, they can still affect how the dipolar particles move, collide, and assemble. Their presence may therefore change the dynamics of assembly, the structures that are formed, and the final organization of the system.\nThis project will use damped Langevin dynamics simulations to study the self-assembly process of these binary colloidal mixtures. The motion of individual particles will be simulated over time, allowing us to directly observe how simple particle interactions lead to larger-scale structures. Different parameters, such as the ratio of dipolar to non-dipolar particles, particle concentration, dipolar interaction strength, and initial particle arrangement, will be investigated.\nThe student will build the simulation model and analyze how these parameters influence chain formation, clustering, aggregation, and the final assembled structures. Through this project, the student will gain experience in molecular dynamics simulation, basic computational modeling, and quantitative analysis of self-assembly. The project will also provide a simple example of how microscopic particle interactions can lead to macroscopic structures and functions in soft material systems.\npre- and co-requisites: soft matter physics, molecular dynamics, \npreferred skills: LAMMPS, Programming, Matlab","descZh":"自组装描述的是小颗粒通过颗粒间相互作用自发组织成更大结构的过程，广泛存在于软材料、胶体系统与生物系统中。理解这一过程很重要，因为软材料在宏观尺度上的行为往往由其微观组分如何相互作用与排列决定。因此，研究颗粒级相互作用有助于我们理解软材料的结构与功能如何涌现。\n偶极胶体是研究自组装的理想模型体系。这些颗粒通过方向依赖的偶极-偶极相互作用相互影响，可形成链、团簇与网络等不同结构，这些结构会强烈影响材料的整体性质。然而，在许多实际系统中，材料并非只含一种颗粒，可能同时含有功能颗粒与不直接参与主要相互作用的其他颗粒。\n本项目将研究同时含偶极与非偶极颗粒的二元胶体体系。尽管非偶极颗粒不存在偶极-偶极相互作用，它们仍会影响偶极颗粒的运动、碰撞与组装方式；因此，它们的存在可能改变组装的动力学、形成的结构以及系统的最终组织。\n本项目将采用阻尼朗之万动力学仿真研究这些二元胶体混合物的自组装过程。将随时间模拟各颗粒的运动，使我们能直接观察简单颗粒相互作用如何导致更大尺度结构。将研究不同参数，如偶极与非偶极颗粒比例、颗粒浓度、偶极相互作用强度与初始颗粒排列。\n学生将构建仿真模型，分析这些参数如何影响链形成、团簇化、聚集与最终组装结构。通过本项目，学生将获得分子动力学仿真、基础计算建模与自组装定量分析的经验；项目也提供了一个微观颗粒相互作用如何导致软材料系统宏观结构与功能的简单范例。\n先修/并修要求：软物质物理、分子动力学。\n偏好技能：LAMMPS、编程、Matlab。","cats":["MAT","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-PCOBB-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"机械工程考古学：改进用于文物的机械臂","titleEn":"MechE Archaeology: Improving Robot Arm for Artifacts","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor COBB Peter J","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-30 09:41:40","descriptionText":"Archaeologists study the human past using the material objects left behind from pottery and stone tools to architectural remains. As part of this process, we spend a lot of time excavating - removing dirt from the ground and carefully recording the 3d volume we are digging and the artifacts that come out of that volume of dirt. Archaeologists carefully collect, label, and store ancient artifacts such as pottery fragments, stone tools, and animal bones for future study. Archaeologists need to work faster to preserve the past, but our own experience digging in Armenia shows us that we spend most of our time just physically moving dirt rather than conducting primary research. The slower we are, the more data we never get to collect.\n After we\r\nexcavate artifacts, we bring them back to the field lab where they are washed. Next, we must document each\r\none individually. Currently, we do this manually with a camera and weight scale. We photograph the back\r\nand front side of each object and weigh each object, saving these data eventually into our central cloud\r\ndatabase. \n We have begun to test software that will make the camera and scale data collection faster together with\r\nhelping to quickly organize the files and data. This is helpful, however, we still need the human to move the\r\nsherd from one position to the next and press buttons. This can be a bit boring of a repetitive task. We hope\r\nthat we can replace this work by using a robotic arm, as documented in the following paper:\r\n https://sites.temple.edu/trail/files/2021/03/WangLutzCobbDamesICRA2021.pdf \n A past team made some progress with building an arm and demonstrating that it could recognize and move the pottery around. The new team will start from this point, using the existing software and arm.\n This arm could move a ceramic piece around, but now it needs to be improved to undertake most of the specific steps within our fieldwork conditions. For example, it needs to be able to fit within the photography tent, and to place the ceramic very precisely in front of the camera next to the color and scale bar, with the correct orientation. The piece needs to be precisely flipped over and placed again in the same precise position. Thus, most of the project remains to be completed, and it must be completed precisely to the project requirements, within the project conditions. The new team must begin with the hardware and code of the old team. The new team will probably need to get a new, better, robot arm.\n We seek students who have enthusiasm and an ability to complete projects! Students should also be able to communicate well in English.\n Students who have an interest and do a good job could consider volunteering to join our archaeological\r\nfieldwork project in the country of Armenia for a few weeks between May and July. There, you can finish\r\nimplementing your project and learn how to excavate while you touch real ancient artifacts.","descZh":"考古学家利用从陶器与石制工具到建筑遗迹的遗留物质遗存研究人类过去。在此过程中，我们花费大量时间发掘——清除泥土，仔细记录所挖掘的三维体积及其中的文物。考古学家仔细收集、标注并储存陶片、石器与动物骨骼等古代文物以供未来研究。考古学家需要加快工作速度以保护历史，但我们在亚美尼亚的发掘经验表明，大部分时间只是物理搬运泥土而非开展主要研究。速度越慢，无法采集的数据越多。\n挖出文物后，我们将其带回野外实验室清洗。随后须逐一建档。目前我们用手动方式配合相机与称重秤完成：为每件物体的背面与正面拍照并称重，最终将这些数据存入中央云数据库。\n我们已开始测试能让相机与秤数据采集更快并帮助快速整理文件与数据的软件。这确有帮助，但仍需人工将陶片从一个位置移到下一位置并按键操作，这是相当枯燥重复的任务。我们希望用机械臂替代这一工作，如以下论文所载：\nhttps://sites.temple.edu/trail/files/2021/03/WangLutzCobbDamesICRA2021.pdf\n往年团队在搭建机械臂并演示其识别与移动陶器方面取得一定进展。新团队将在此基础上起步，使用现有软件与机械臂。\n该臂能移动陶瓷件，但现需改进以胜任我们野外条件下的大多数具体环节。例如，它需能放进摄影帐篷内，将陶瓷件以正确朝向非常精确地放置在相机前、紧邻色卡与比例尺；陶瓷件还需被精确翻转后再次放在同一精确位置。因此，项目大部分工作仍待完成，且必须在项目条件下精确满足项目要求完成。新团队必须以上一团队的硬件与代码为起点，很可能需要购置一台更新、更好的机械臂。\n我们希望招收有热情、有能力完成项目的学生！学生还应具备良好的英语沟通能力。\n有兴趣且表现优秀的学生可考虑在 5 月至 7 月间自愿参加我们在亚美尼亚的考古田野项目数周。在那里，你可以完成项目实现，并亲手触碰真实古代文物学习发掘。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-PCOBB-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"机械工程考古学：改进物品清洁","titleEn":"MechE Archaeology: Improving Cleaning of Objects","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor COBB Peter J","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-30 09:34:01","descriptionText":"Archaeologists study the human past using the material objects left behind from pottery and stone tools to architectural remains. As part of this process, we spend a lot of time excavating - removing dirt from the ground and carefully recording the 3d volume we are digging and the artifacts that come out of that volume of dirt. Archaeologists carefully collect, label, and store ancient artifacts such as pottery fragments, stone tools, and animal bones for future study. Archaeologists need to work faster to preserve the past, but our own experience digging in Armenia shows us that we spend most of our time just physically moving dirt rather than conducting primary research. The slower we are, the more data we never get to collect.\n However, when the artifacts come out of the ground, they are obviously covered with dirt. In addition, they might have calcium carbonate encrustations from the rainwater that has percolated through the soils. We do need to be careful with each different type of material, as some are more fragile than others. But it is useful to clean all the artifacts, and there can be hundreds to thousands collected each day. Pottery sherds are our most common material, and generally now we are manually washing these in tap water, using a variety of brushes. It takes a long time since each individual sherd must be washed by a person. \n A prior team tested a system in Armenia to use an ultrasonic cleaner to improve the washing of the pottery. They also developed a detailed workflow to filter the water, using a computer and a sensor to determine when to filter the water based on how much dirt it contained. There were several problems with this system. For example, the filter gets dirty way too fast, given the quantity of dirt. There should probably be a prewash phase, and there may need to be a settling tank phase. Also the ultrasonic cleaner did not work very well, we still had a person manually washing the pottery within the ultrasonic cleaner. Thus, the next team must start from the point left off by the prior team, and must make further improvements on the project.\n In the prior requirements, we explained that we would like to find a method to automate the cleaning of these artifacts, to first remove the dirt and\r\nperhaps to remove the white encrustations. The goals are to be gentle, fast, and minimize water use. A\r\ndevice could use brushes or other technology like pressurized water. The solution might involve multiple\r\nwashes, from most dirty to fine cleaning. It should be affordable, since archaeology budgets are small, and\r\nbe able to be built with parts that can be bought in the local places like Armenia. Objects must be carefully\r\ntracked, staying together with a label that is written during excavation, so we know where everything came\r\nfrom.\n We seek students who have enthusiasm and an ability to complete projects! Students should be able to communicate very well in English.\n Students who have an interest and do a good job could consider volunteering to join our archaeological fieldwork project in the country of Armenia for a few weeks between May and July. There, you can finish implementing your project and learn how to excavate while you touch real ancient artifacts.","descZh":"考古学家利用从陶器与石制工具到建筑遗迹的遗留物质遗存研究人类过去。在此过程中，我们花费大量时间发掘——清除泥土，仔细记录所挖掘的三维体积及其中的文物。考古学家仔细收集、标注并储存陶片、石器与动物骨骼等古代文物以供未来研究。考古学家需要加快工作速度以保护历史，但我们在亚美尼亚的发掘经验表明，大部分时间只是物理搬运泥土而非开展主要研究。速度越慢，无法采集的数据越多。\n然而，文物出土时显然覆盖着泥土，还可能带有雨水渗入土壤形成的碳酸钙结壳。我们需要小心对待每种不同材料，因为有些比其他更脆弱。但清洗所有文物很有必要，而每天采集的文物可达数百至数千件。陶片是我们最常见的材料，目前通常用自来水配合各种刷子手工清洗。这耗时很长，因为每片陶片都需专人清洗。\n往届团队曾在亚美尼亚测试用超声波清洗机改进陶器清洗的系统，并开发了详细的水过滤工作流——用计算机与传感器根据水中泥土含量决定何时过滤。该系统存在若干问题：例如，鉴于泥土量之大，过滤器脏得太快；可能应有预洗阶段，且可能需要沉降罐阶段。此外，超声波清洗机效果不佳——我们仍须有人在超声波清洗机内手工清洗陶器。因此，下一组必须从上一组停下的地方继续，并作进一步改进。\n在先前要求中，我们说明希望能找到一种自动化清洗这些文物的方法——先去除泥土，也许再去除白色结壳。目标是要温和、快速并尽量减少用水。装置可采用刷子或加压水等其他技术；解决方案可能涉及从最脏到精细清洗的多级清洗；应价格适中（考古预算有限），并能用亚美尼亚等当地可购得的部件制造。物体必须被仔细跟踪，与发掘时书写的标签始终保持在一起，以便我们知道每件物品的出处。\n我们希望招收有热情、有能力完成项目的学生！学生应具备非常良好的英语沟通能力。\n有兴趣且表现优秀的学生可考虑在 5 月至 7 月间自愿参加我们在亚美尼亚的考古田野项目数周。在那里，你可以完成项目实现，并亲手触碰真实古代文物学习发掘。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-PCOBB-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"机械工程考古学：改进与数据的XR交互","titleEn":"MechE Archaeology: Improving XR Interaction with Data","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor COBB Peter J","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-30 09:37:10","descriptionText":"Archaeologists study the human past using the material objects left behind from pottery and stone tools to architectural remains. As part of this process, we spend a lot of time excavating - removing dirt from the ground and carefully recording the 3d volume we are digging and the artifacts that come out of that volume of dirt. Archaeologists carefully collect, label, and store ancient artifacts such as pottery fragments, stone tools, and animal bones for future study. Archaeologists need to work faster to preserve the past, but our own experience digging in Armenia shows us that we spend most of our time just physically moving dirt rather than conducting primary research. The slower we are, the more data we never get to collect.\n The primary goal of\r\narchaeological fieldwork, therefore, is to precisely record the locations and spatial relationships among these\r\nfindings. Today, many archaeologists use photogrammetry and other 3D data capture techniques while they\r\nexcavate to record spatial information, but these 3D models are often only used later for simple\r\nmeasurements and to extract 2D illustrations. We have identified that it would be more useful to deploy those\r\ndata directly during excavation to help with interpretation and excavation decision-making, particularly\r\naround studying spatial relationships at an archaeological site. We experimented with several mixed and\r\naugmented reality (MR and AR, collectively eXtended Reality XR) headsets in the field to support this type\r\nof data interaction.\n Additionally, we also recognized the potential for mixed and augmented reality headsets to improve how\r\narchaeologists dig and collect data in the field during excavation. In order to more clearly see ancient\r\nremains, archaeologists often intentionally cut the dirt to flat planes. Given that mixed reality headsets can\r\nscan their surroundings and precisely project geometry into the real world, we recognized that virtual planes\r\ncould be used to guide archaeological digging. Finally, since archaeologists need their hands free to move\r\ndirt and touch artifacts, we see significant potential for using even 2D augmented reality smartglasses, with\r\nvoice recognition, to gather and display basic data during excavation, such as photographs and textual\r\ninformation.\n We also see an important need to train future archaeologists in the immersive environment of Virtual Reality (VR).\nIn these projects, students can help us with hardware or software elements of improving XR headsets for\r\nfieldwork. Specifically, we have been building our own custom AR smart glasses, but we need better\r\nhousing and better performance outdoors. Students might also help with virtual reality learning experiences. The students may also pick up a project with educational VR where the prior team wrote code to precisely simulate the real world of digging in dirt. The students may also help to design and build/update some prototype interactive glasses with custom lenses, seen in the next photo.\n We seek students who have enthusiasm and can complete projects! Students should communicate well in English.\n Students who have an interest and do a good job could consider volunteering to join our archaeological\r\nfieldwork project in the country of Armenia for a few weeks between May and July. There, you can finish\r\nimplementing your project and learn how to excavate while you touch real ancient artifacts.","descZh":"考古学家利用从陶器与石制工具到建筑遗迹的遗留物质遗存研究人类过去。在此过程中，我们花费大量时间发掘——清除泥土，仔细记录所挖掘的三维体积及其中的文物。考古学家仔细收集、标注并储存陶片、石器与动物骨骼等古代文物以供未来研究。考古学家需要加快工作速度以保护历史，但我们在亚美尼亚的发掘经验表明，大部分时间只是物理搬运泥土而非开展主要研究。速度越慢，无法采集的数据越多。\n因此，考古田野工作的首要目标是精确记录这些发现的位置及其空间关系。如今，许多考古学家在发掘时使用摄影测量与其他三维数据采集技术记录空间信息，但这些三维模型往往只在事后用于简单测量与提取二维插图。我们认识到，在发掘过程中直接使用这些数据，帮助解释与发掘决策，会更有效——尤其围绕研究考古遗址的空间关系。我们在野外试验了多种混合现实与增强现实（MR 与 AR，合称扩展现实 XR）头显以支持此类数据交互。\n此外，我们还认识到混合现实与增强现实头显在改善考古学家现场挖掘与数据采集方式的潜力。为更清晰地观察古代遗存，考古学家常有意将泥土切成平面。鉴于混合现实头显可扫描周围环境并将几何精确投射到真实世界，我们认识到可用虚拟平面引导考古挖掘。最后，由于考古学家需要双手搬运泥土与触摸文物，我们认为即便带语音识别的二维增强现实智能眼镜，也能在发掘过程中采集与显示照片、文本等基础数据，潜力巨大。\n我们还看到在虚拟现实（VR）沉浸环境中培训未来考古学家的迫切需求。\n在这些项目中，学生可帮助我们从硬件或软件层面改进用于田野工作的 XR 头显。具体而言，我们一直在自制定制 AR 智能眼镜，但需要更好的外壳与更好的户外性能；学生还可帮助开发虚拟现实学习体验。学生也可选择一个教育 VR 项目——往届团队曾编写代码精确模拟真实挖土世界。学生还可帮助设计、构建/更新一些带定制镜片的原型交互式眼镜（见下张照片）。\n我们希望招收有热情、能完成项目的学生！学生应具备良好的英语沟通能力。\n有兴趣且表现优秀的学生可考虑在 5 月至 7 月间自愿参加我们在亚美尼亚的考古田野项目数周。在那里，你可以完成项目实现，并亲手触碰真实古代文物学习发掘。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-PCOBB-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"机械工程考古学：改进物品分离","titleEn":"MechE Archaeology: Improving Object Separation","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor COBB Peter J","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 08:04:46","descriptionText":"Archaeologists study the human past using the material objects left behind, from pottery and stone tools, to architectural remains. As part of this process, we spend a lot of time excavating - removing dirt from the ground and carefully recording the 3d volumes we are digging and the artifacts that come out of each volume of dirt. Archaeologists carefully collect, label, and store ancient artifacts such as pottery fragments, stone tools, and animal bones for future study. Archaeologists need to work faster to preserve the past, but our own experience digging in Armenia shows us that we spend most of our time just physically moving dirt rather than conducting primary research. The slower we are, the more data we never get to collect. \n Once we collect objects, we need to categorize them all based on material, type, and visual appearance. For example, we separate the pottery from the stone tools and the animal bones. For larger objects, this process is done as soon as an object is excavated, as the excavator places each object into a bag based on material. There is a plastic bag to collect pottery fragments and another to collect animal bones. The following images show actual examples from the field.\n However, we also collect smaller pieces, especially as part of a bag of soil that we collect called a soil sample. This soil sample will later be floated in water to separate out tiny seeds, that tell us about the ancient diet. Here is one soil sample example:\n In this bag are also tiny fragments of the usual objects, like pottery, bones, and stone flakes. Also there are natural stones that we do not want to keep since they were not made by humans. We need to find an easy way to separate all these out into different categories. It is very hard to sort them by hand.\n In the past, mechanical engineering student teams have tried multiple approaches to automating the separation of these tiny fragments. They have tried flotation in a magnetite solution that changes based on density. The problem was this was that the solution did not stay mixed well, and the magnetite also needed to be cleaned from the objects later. They tried blowing air into a specially designed chamber to separate lighter particles from heavier sand. This showed some promise, but they never were able to get close to perfect sorting. They tried centrifugal force in water, with a rotating device that created a vortex to move lighter particles, which roughly worked for small bone fragments. They considered other things like a shaking angled table. They did not spend enough time testing and refining these ideas.\n The next team will start from the experience of prior teams and work to find better solutions that will be tested at our fieldwork project in Armenia in the summer.\n We seek students who have enthusiasm and can complete projects! Students should communicate well in English. \n Students who have an interest and do a good job could consider volunteering to join our archaeological\r\nfieldwork project in the country of Armenia for a few weeks between May and July. There, you can finish\r\nimplementing your project and learn how to excavate while you touch real ancient artifacts.","descZh":"考古学家利用从陶器与石制工具到建筑遗迹的遗留物质遗存研究人类过去。在此过程中，我们花费大量时间发掘——清除泥土，仔细记录所挖掘的三维体积及各泥土体积中出土的文物。考古学家仔细收集、标注并储存陶片、石器与动物骨骼等古代文物以供未来研究。考古学家需要加快工作速度以保护历史，但我们在亚美尼亚的发掘经验表明，大部分时间只是物理搬运泥土而非开展主要研究。速度越慢，无法采集的数据越多。\n一旦收集到物体，我们需要根据材质、类型与视觉外观将其分类。例如，将陶器与石器、动物骨骼分开。对于较大物体，此过程在出土时即完成——发掘者根据材质将每件物体放入袋中：一个塑料袋装陶片，另一个装动物骨骼。附图展示了来自现场的真实样例。\n然而，我们也会收集较小的碎片，尤其是作为所谓\"土壤样本\"的一袋土壤的一部分。此土壤样本之后会在水中漂浮，分离出能反映古人饮食的微小种子。这是一份土壤样本示例：袋中还含有陶器、骨骼与石片等常见物体的微小碎片，也有并非人类制造、我们不想保留的天然石块。我们需要找到一种简便方法将这些物质分成不同类别。手工分拣非常困难。\n过去，机械工程学生团队曾尝试多种方法来自动分离这些微小碎片：他们尝试在磁性溶液（磁铁矿溶液）中漂浮——该方法随密度不同而变化，问题是溶液无法保持均匀混合，且事后还需从物体上清除磁铁矿；他们尝试向特制腔室吹气，将较轻颗粒与较重沙子分离，此法显示一定前景，但始终无法接近完美分拣；他们尝试利用水的离心力，用旋转装置制造涡流移动较轻颗粒，对小骨碎片大致有效；他们还考虑过振动斜台等其他方案，但没有投入足够时间测试与改进这些思路。\n下一组将从往届团队的经验出发，寻找更好的解决方案，并在我们夏季的亚美尼亚田野项目中测试。\n我们希望招收有热情、能完成项目的学生！学生应具备良好的英语沟通能力。\n有兴趣且表现优秀的学生可考虑在 5 月至 7 月间自愿参加我们在亚美尼亚的考古田野项目数周。在那里，你可以完成项目实现，并亲手触碰真实古代文物学习发掘。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-DIGITAL-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"面向全球数字银行智能平台的智能数据自动化与质量保障","titleEn":"Intelligent Data Automation and Quality Assurance for a Global Digital Banking Intelligence Platform","areaZh":"数据工程；人工智能；数据库系统；信息抽取；数据质量；W","areaEn":"Data Engineering; Artificial Intelligence; Database Systems; Information Extraction; Data Quality; W","companyZh":"Digital Financial Services Research Center Ltd.","companyEn":"Digital Financial Services Research Center Ltd.","companySupervisor":"Dr. XU Wenjie","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Neobanker maintains and analyses information relating to neobanks, FinTech companies, incumbent banks, financial products, regulations, market activities, ESG, and social banking across multiple jurisdictions. As the number and variety of data sources increase, manual data collection and maintenance become inefficient and difficult to scale. This project aims to design and implement an intelligent data automation framework for the Neobanker platform.\nThe project will investigate how heterogeneous data from public websites, open datasets, company reports, regulatory documents, APIs, and other authorised sources can be automatically collected, transformed, validated, and stored in a structured database. The proposed framework should support scheduled data updates, schema mapping, entity resolution, duplicate detection, missing-value handling, data provenance, quality scoring, exception handling, and workflow monitoring.\nArtificial intelligence and LLM-based information extraction may be applied to process semi-structured and unstructured materials. Knowledge graph techniques may also be explored to connect companies, countries, products, regulations, investors, technologies, and market events. All automatically extracted information should retain links to its original source and relevant metadata to ensure traceability and facilitate human verification.\nThe project should consider data accuracy, system reliability, scalability, maintainability, security, and compliance requirements. A human-in-the-loop workflow should be developed for reviewing uncertain or conflicting records before they are published on the platform.\nPossible individual tasks include:\nDevelopment of data connectors, automated collection pipelines, document parsing, and structured information extraction;\nDevelopment of entity matching, schema mapping, duplicate detection, data validation, and quality-scoring methods;\nDevelopment of workflow orchestration, data lineage, monitoring, exception management, and platform integration.\nApplicants are expected to have hands-on experience in at least one of the following areas: machine learning or deep learning model development, LLM application development, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), prompt engineering, model fine-tuning, AI agent development, multi-agent systems, or the deployment and evaluation of AI applications. Proficiency in Python and familiarity with commonly used machine learning or LLM frameworks and tools are preferred. Applicants should be able to demonstrate practical problem-solving and implementation capabilities rather than only theoretical knowledge.\nShortlisted applicants will be invited to an interview and may be required to complete a technical assessment or practical task designed by the company. The assessment may cover programming, machine learning, LLM/agent application development, system design, data analysis, or other skills relevant to the proposed project.\nThe student is expected to communicate regularly with the company supervisor and provide periodic progress updates. The onsite frequency and working arrangements are flexible and will be determined according to the project requirements and mutual agreement between the student, the company supervisor, and the HKU co-supervisor.","descZh":"Neobanker 维护并分析跨多个司法辖区的与数字银行（neobank）、金融科技（FinTech）公司、传统银行、金融产品、监管法规、市场活动、ESG 以及社会银行相关的信息。随着数据来源数量和种类的增加，人工数据收集和维护变得低效且难以扩展。本项目旨在为 Neobanker 平台设计并实现一个智能数据自动化框架。\n本项目将研究如何将来自公开网站、开放数据集、公司报告、监管文件、API 以及其他授权来源的异构数据自动收集、转换、验证，并存储到结构化数据库中。所提出的框架应支持定时数据更新、模式映射（schema mapping）、实体解析（entity resolution）、重复检测、缺失值处理、数据溯源、质量评分、异常处理以及工作流监控。\n人工智能和基于 LLM 的信息抽取可用于处理半结构化和非结构化材料。也可以探索知识图谱技术，将公司、国家、产品、法规、投资者、技术和市场事件连接起来。所有自动抽取的信息都应保留其原始来源的链接和相关元数据，以确保可追溯性，并便于人工核查。\n本项目应考虑数据准确性、系统可靠性、可扩展性、可维护性、安全性和合规性要求。应开发一个人机协同（human-in-the-loop）的工作流，用于在记录发布到平台上之前审核不确定或相互冲突的记录。\n可能的个人任务包括：\n开发数据连接器、自动化采集流水线、文档解析和结构化信息抽取；\n开发实体匹配、模式映射、重复检测、数据验证和质量评分方法；\n开发工作流编排、数据血缘（data lineage）、监控、异常管理和平台集成。\n申请人应至少在以下一个领域具有实践经验：机器学习或深度学习模型开发、LLM 应用开发、检索增强生成（RAG）、提示工程、模型微调、AI 智能体开发、多智能体系统，或 AI 应用的部署与评估。熟练使用 Python 并熟悉常用的机器学习或 LLM 框架和工具者优先。申请人应能够展示实际的问题解决和实现能力，而不仅仅是理论知识。\n入围申请人将受邀参加面试，并可能需要完成由公司设计的技术评估或实践任务。评估内容可能涵盖编程、机器学习、LLM/智能体应用开发、系统设计、数据分析，或与所提议项目相关的其他技能。\n学生应与公司导师保持定期沟通，并定期提供进展更新。现场频率和工作安排是灵活的，将根据项目要求以及学生、公司导师和香港大学联合导师之间的共同协议来确定。","cats":["AI","CS"]},{"ref":"2026-27-DIGITAL-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI驱动的全球数字银行新闻情报与分类系统","titleEn":"AI-Powered Global Digital Banking News Intelligence and Classification System","areaZh":"人工智能；自然语言处理；大语言模型；多智能体系统；In","areaEn":"Artificial Intelligence; Natural Language Processing; Large Language Models; Multi-Agent Systems; In","companyZh":"Digital Financial Services Research Center Ltd.","companyEn":"Digital Financial Services Research Center Ltd.","companySupervisor":"Dr. XU Wenjie","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Neobanker is an AI-powered market intelligence platform providing global information on neobanks, digital banking, FinTech, financial regulation, and social banking. This project aims to develop an automated news intelligence system that continuously collects, processes, classifies, and presents relevant news from multiple global sources.\nThe system will apply Natural Language Processing, Large Language Models, and agent-based workflows to identify relevant news, remove duplicate or low-quality content, classify articles according to a predefined taxonomy, and extract key information such as companies, countries, technologies, products, regulations, and market events. The project may also explore multilingual news processing, source credibility assessment, relevance ranking, topic detection, citation-based summarisation, and personalised news recommendations.\nThe system should support major news categories such as neobanks, incumbent banks, FinTech companies, Web3, artificial intelligence, financial products, investment and funding, mergers and acquisitions, cybersecurity, ESG, social banking, and regulatory developments. Particular attention should be given to the traceability of generated content, reduction of factual errors, and evaluation of classification and summarisation quality.\nThe project is suitable for a small group, with each student undertaking a clearly defined individual research task. Possible individual tasks include:\nDevelopment of automated news collection, source management, content extraction, and deduplication pipelines;\nDevelopment and evaluation of multilingual news classification, entity extraction, topic tagging, and relevance-ranking models;\nDevelopment of citation-based news summarisation, quality-control mechanisms, API services, and dashboard integration.\nApplicants are expected to have hands-on experience in at least one of the following areas: machine learning or deep learning model development, LLM application development, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), prompt engineering, model fine-tuning, AI agent development, multi-agent systems, or the deployment and evaluation of AI applications. Proficiency in Python and familiarity with commonly used machine learning or LLM frameworks and tools are preferred. Applicants should be able to demonstrate practical problem-solving and implementation capabilities rather than only theoretical knowledge.\nShortlisted applicants will be invited to an interview and may be required to complete a technical assessment or practical task designed by the company. The assessment may cover programming, machine learning, LLM/agent application development, system design, data analysis, or other skills relevant to the proposed project.\nThe student is expected to communicate regularly with the company supervisor and provide periodic progress updates. The onsite frequency and working arrangements are flexible and will be determined according to the project requirements and mutual agreement between the student, the company supervisor, and the HKU co-supervisor.","descZh":"Neobanker 是一个 AI 驱动的市场情报平台，提供关于新型银行（neobank）、数字银行、金融科技、金融监管与社会银行的全球信息。本项目旨在开发一个自动化新闻情报系统，持续从多个全球来源采集、处理、分类与呈现相关新闻。\n系统将应用自然语言处理、大语言模型与基于智能体的工作流，识别相关新闻、去除重复或低质量内容、按预定义分类体系对文章分类，并抽取公司、国家、技术、产品、监管与市场事件等关键信息。项目还可探索多语言新闻处理、来源可信度评估、相关性排序、主题检测、基于引用的摘要生成与个性化新闻推荐。\n系统应支持新型银行、传统银行、金融科技公司、Web3、人工智能、金融产品、投资与融资、并购、网络安全、ESG、社会银行与监管动态等主要新闻类别。应特别关注生成内容的可追溯性、事实错误的减少，以及分类与摘要质量的评估。\n项目适合小组开展，每名学生承担明确定义的个体研究任务。可能的个体任务包括：\n- 开发自动化新闻采集、来源管理、内容抽取与去重流程；\n- 开发与评估多语言新闻分类、实体抽取、主题标签与相关性排序模型；\n- 开发基于引用的新闻摘要、质量控制机制、API 服务与仪表板集成。\n申请人应至少在以下一个领域有实践经验：机器学习或深度学习模型开发、LLM 应用开发、检索增强生成（RAG）、提示工程、模型微调、AI 智能体开发、多智能体系统，或 AI 应用的部署与评估。优先考虑熟练掌握 Python 并熟悉常用机器学习或 LLM 框架与工具者。申请人应能展示实际解决问题与实现的能力，而非仅具理论知识。\n入围申请人将被邀请参加面试，并可能需完成公司设计的技术测评或实践任务。测评可能涵盖编程、机器学习、LLM/智能体应用开发、系统设计、数据分析或与拟议项目相关的其他技能。\n学生应与公司导师定期沟通并提供阶段性进展更新。现场频率与工作安排灵活，将根据项目要求及学生、公司导师与港大联合导师之间的共同约定确定。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-ADVANCED-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI物联网边缘可穿戴模拟ASIC接口","titleEn":"A.I. IOT Edge Wearable Analog ASIC interface","areaZh":"数据与技术","areaEn":"Data and Technology","companyZh":"Advanced Semiconductor Integrations Limited","companyEn":"Advanced Semiconductor Integrations Limited","companySupervisor":"Louis Wong","univSupName":"Dr. ALI Zulfiqar","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 12:39:07","descriptionText":"Project background\nAnalog ASIC research and development for A.I. IOT Edge Wearable Analog ASIC interface\nProblem statement\nA.I. IOT Edge Wearable device requires ultra low power high performance analog ASIC building blocks\nDetailed problem description\nA.I. IOT Edge Wearable device requires ultra low power high performance analog ASIC building blocks.\nTraditional analog ASIC building blocks can't meet the stringent requirements. \nA novel architecture and circuit design approaches are needed to overcome the ultra low power challenges. \nExpected outcomes\nA novel architecture and circuit design approaches are needed to overcome the ultra low power challenges. \nA successful outcome would be a novel working circuit architecture and schematic, verified by circuit simulation, layout design and post-layout simulation. Silicon performance would be a plus. \nPreferred skillsets of students\nAnalog ASIC design skill\nExpected onsite frequency at your company\nOnce a week - 1 hour per visit, or\nOnce every two weeks - 2 hours per visit","descZh":"项目背景：面向 AI / IoT 边缘可穿戴设备的模拟 ASIC 接口的研发。\n问题陈述：AI / IoT 边缘可穿戴设备需要超低功耗、高性能的模拟 ASIC 功能模块。\n详细问题描述：AI / IoT 边缘可穿戴设备需要超低功耗、高性能的模拟 ASIC 功能模块。传统模拟 ASIC 模块无法满足严苛要求。需要新颖的架构与电路设计方法以攻克超低功耗挑战。\n预期成果：需要新颖的架构与电路设计方法以攻克超低功耗挑战。成功的结果将是可工作、新颖的电路架构与原理图，并经电路仿真、版图设计与版图后仿真验证；硅片级性能为加分项。\n学生偏好技能：模拟 ASIC 设计能力。\n期望在贵司的现场频率：每周一次，每次 1 小时；或每两周一次，每次 2 小时。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-EVORY-1","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"基于Web的3D家具与空间室内设计电商平台","titleEn":"Web-Based 3D Furniture &amp; Spatial Interior Design E-Commerce Platform","areaZh":"AI；工程管理；设计与制造；多媒体网络商务；空间AR/VR","areaEn":"AI; Engineering Management; Design & manufacture; Multimedia Web Commerce; Spatial AR/VR","companyZh":"Evory Media Limited","companyEn":"Evory Media Limited","companySupervisor":"Ivy Shum","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-05 13:34:02","descriptionText":"Project Overview: \nEvory Media Limited is seeking talented university students with foundational experience to contribute to the conceptualization, UI/UX wireframing, and exploratory prototyping of a next-generation 3D furniture e-commerce platform. The project integrates advanced spatial styling algorithms and automated interior design matching engines to streamline furniture discovery, layout planning, and customized home furnishing retail. The project emphasizes strategic direction, creative UX design, and spatial feature ideation rather than heavy backend coding.\n Objectives: \n E-Commerce & Intelligent Matching Concept Design : Learn how to map out a multi-vendor digital marketplace featuring automated style-matching algorithms that pair furniture collections with distinct interior design aesthetics (e.g., Italian luxury, French elegance, Japanese minimalist, modern, and new Chinese).\n UI/UX Wireframing & Prototyping : Develop intuitive user journeys in Figma from product discovery and aesthetic filtering to real-time spatial preview and checkout.\n Spatial Rendering & 3D Feature Ideation : Explore how web-based 3D models, automated spatial rendering engines, and smart layout generation can enhance online furniture shopping.\n Platform Strategy : Understand how digital e-commerce tools and intelligent design engines support merchants in expanding their digital reach and online sales.\n Responsibilities: \n Conduct user research on furniture e-commerce trends and define user personas. \n Create wireframes, interactive prototypes, and high-fidelity mockups for the web storefront, style-matching interface, and merchant catalog view.\n Report directly to and collaborate with Ivy Shum (Director at Evory Media Limited) through high-level strategic guidance and weekly reviews. \n Prepare a comprehensive presentation deck and strategic framework report.\n Qualifications: \n Basic familiarity with design tools (e.g., Figma, Adobe Creative Suite). \n Strong interest in e-commerce, 3D web technologies, spatial computing (AR/VR), and automated design engines. \n Excellent communication skills and passion for digital media.\n Benefits: \n Hands-on portfolio experience working on a commercial-grade spatial e-commerce and intelligent design concept. \n Direct mentorship from Ivy Shum (Director, Evory Media Limited). \n Exposure to innovative digital media, AI-driven retail workflows, and e-commerce business operations. \n Subsidies from the Company : \n Transportation allowance: Available upon project milestone completion \n Accommodation allowance (for full-time Interns from June to August): N/A for part-time collaborative academic project \n Other allowances: Industry mentorship, portfolio reviews, and direct guidance from company leadership.","descZh":"项目概述：Evory Media Limited 正在招募具备基础经验、才华出众的大学生，为下一代 3D 家具电商平台的构思、UI/UX 线框设计与探索性原型制作作出贡献。项目集成先进的空间风格算法与自动化室内设计匹配引擎，以简化家具发现、布局规划与定制家居零售流程。项目强调战略方向、创意 UX 设计与空间功能构想，而非大量后端编码。\n目标：\n- 电商与智能匹配概念设计：学习规划一个多商家数字市场，内置自动风格匹配算法，将家具系列与不同室内设计美学配对（如意式奢华、法式优雅、日式极简、现代与新中式）；\n- UI/UX 线框与原型：在 Figma 中开发从产品发现、美学筛选到实时空间预览与结算的直观用户旅程；\n- 空间渲染与 3D 功能构想：探索基于 Web 的 3D 模型、自动化空间渲染引擎与智能布局生成如何增强在线家具购物；\n- 平台战略：理解数字电商工具与智能设计引擎如何支持商家拓展数字触达与线上销售。\n职责：\n- 开展家具电商趋势的用户研究并定义用户画像；\n- 为网店前端、风格匹配界面与商家目录视图创建线框图、交互原型与高保真样稿；\n- 直接向 Evory Media Limited 总监 Ivy Shum 汇报，并在高层战略指导与每周评审下协作；\n- 准备综合演示文稿与战略框架报告。\n资质：\n- 熟悉设计工具（如 Figma、Adobe Creative Suite）；\n- 对电商、3D Web 技术、空间计算（AR/VR）与自动化设计引擎有浓厚兴趣；\n- 出色的沟通能力与对数字媒体的热情。\n福利：\n- 参与商业级空间电商与智能设计概念项目的实操作品集经验；\n- 获得 Evory Media Limited 总监 Ivy Shum 的直接指导；\n- 接触创新的数字媒体、AI 驱动零售工作流与电商业务运营；\n- 公司补贴：完成项目里程碑后提供交通补贴；住宿补贴（面向 6-8 月全职实习生）：非全日制协作学术项目不适用；其他补贴：行业导师指导、作品集评审与公司领导层的直接指导。","cats":["AI","CS","DES","MGT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-EVORY-2","type":"CP","typeLabel":"公司项目","mode":"SS","titleZh":"AI 视频与交互式数字媒体营销活动","titleEn":"AI Video &amp; Interactive Digital Media Campaign","areaZh":"AI；设计与制造；工程管理；数字媒体制作；生成式 AI 内容","areaEn":"AI; Design & manufacture; Engineering management; Digital Media Production; Generative AI Conten","companyZh":"Evory Media Limited","companyEn":"Evory Media Limited","companySupervisor":"Ivy Shum","univSupName":"Dr. CHOY Marian","moderator":"","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-05 14:10:41","descriptionText":"Project Overview: \n Evory Media Limited is seeking talented university students with foundational experience to contribute to the conceptualization, AI video workflow generation, and digital content strategy for next-generation promotional media campaigns. The project emphasizes creative storytelling, AI-assisted video production, multi-style visual content creation, and digital audience engagement rather than heavy backend coding.\n Objectives: \n Digital Campaign Concept Design : Learn how to map out multi-channel media campaigns and interactive storytelling frameworks tailored for digital brands and promotional projects. \n AI Video & Visual Generation Workflows : Explore and implement workflows for AI image and video generation tools to produce multi-style visual content, character animations, and digital media assets. \n Content Strategy & Social Media Integration : Develop targeted digital strategies for optimizing online presence, audience engagement, and platform-specific content performance. \n Platform Ideation : Understand how automated media generation tools streamline modern digital marketing and creative content production. \n Responsibilities: \n Conduct research on digital media trends and define audience engagement personas. \n Create storyboards, graphic design mockups, and AI-generated video sequences for promotional projects. \n Report directly to and collaborate with Ivy Shum (Director at Evory Media Limited) through high-level strategic guidance and weekly reviews. \n Prepare a comprehensive presentation deck and digital campaign strategy report. \n Qualifications: \n Basic familiarity with creative design tools and AI generative platforms (e.g., video/image generation suites, Adobe Creative Suite). \n Strong interest in digital media, generative AI applications, and online content creation. \n Excellent communication skills and passion for digital storytelling. \n Benefits: \n Hands-on portfolio experience working on commercial digital media and AI-driven content campaigns. \n Direct mentorship from Ivy Shum (Director, Evory Media Limited). \n Exposure to innovative digital media workflows, social media management, and modern agency operations. \n Subsidies from the Company \n Transportation allowance: Available upon project milestone completion \n Accommodation allowance (for full-time Interns from June to August): N/A for part-time collaborative academic project \n Other allowances: Industry mentorship, portfolio reviews, and direct guidance from company leadership.","descZh":"项目概述：Evory Media Limited 正在招募具备基础经验、才华出众的大学生，为新一代推广媒体活动贡献创意构思、AI 视频工作流生成与数字内容策略。项目强调创意叙事、AI 辅助视频制作、多风格视觉内容创作与数字受众互动，而非大量后端编码。\n目标：\n- 数字活动概念设计：学习为数字品牌与推广项目规划多渠道媒体活动与互动叙事框架；\n- AI 视频与视觉生成工作流：探索并实现 AI 图像与视频生成工具的工作流，制作多风格视觉内容、角色动画与数字媒体素材；\n- 内容策略与社交媒体整合：制定针对性的数字策略，优化在线存在感、受众互动与各平台内容表现；\n- 平台构想：理解自动化媒体生成工具如何简化现代数字营销与创意内容生产。\n职责：\n- 开展数字媒体趋势研究并定义受众互动画像；\n- 为推广项目创建故事板、平面设计样稿与 AI 生成视频序列；\n- 直接向 Evory Media Limited 总监 Ivy Shum 汇报，并在高层战略指导与每周评审下协作；\n- 准备综合演示文稿与数字活动策略报告。\n资质：\n- 熟悉创意设计工具与 AI 生成平台（如视频/图像生成套件、Adobe Creative Suite）；\n- 对数字媒体、生成式 AI 应用与在线内容创作有浓厚兴趣；\n- 出色的沟通能力与对数字叙事的热情。\n福利：\n- 参与商业数字媒体与 AI 驱动内容活动的实操作品集经验；\n- 获得 Evory Media Limited 总监 Ivy Shum 的直接指导；\n- 接触创新的数字媒体工作流、社交媒体管理与现代代理商运营；\n- 公司补贴：完成项目里程碑后提供交通补贴；住宿补贴（面向 6-8 月全职实习生）：非全日制协作学术项目不适用；其他补贴：行业导师指导、作品集评审与公司领导层的直接指导。","cats":["AI","DES","MGT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-9","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"港大方程式赛车","titleEn":"HKU Racing Formula Car","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 12:04:01","descriptionText":"The aims of the project will be one of the below, \n 1) Motor blench test design, test and calibration\n 2) VCU design, test and calibration \n 3) Water cooling system design, test and calibration \n 4) Vehicle Dynamics Simulation with CarSim \n 5) Design and build an effective aerodynamic kit in Race Cars\n 6) etc....","descZh":"本项目的目标将从下列方向中选取：\n1) 电机台架测试：设计、测试与标定；\n2) VCU（整车控制器）设计、测试与标定；\n3) 水冷系统设计、测试与标定；\n4) 使用 CarSim 进行车辆动力学仿真；\n5) 为赛车设计与制作有效的空气动力学套件；\n6) 其他等。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-18","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"运动赛车模拟器的设计与原型制作（产品原型制作）","titleEn":"Design and prototyping of motion racing simulator (Product prototyping)","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. CHAN Chi Keung","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 17:07:12","descriptionText":"The aim of this study is to build and\r\ndevelop a motion racing simulator platform, \r\nso that we can enhance the racing simulation experience on the simulator.","descZh":"本研究的目标是构建并\n开发一个运动赛车模拟器平台，\n以便我们能够增强该模拟器上的赛车模拟体验。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-14","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"自动羽毛球喂球机设计与原型制作（产品设计与原型制作）","titleEn":"Design and prototyping of an automatic badminton shuttlecock feeder machine (Product design and prototyping)","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. CHOY Marian","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-03 21:07:01","descriptionText":"The aim of this study is to design and build a device that can\nautomatically feed badminton shuttlecock to the user for practice.","descZh":"本研究旨在设计并搭建一台能够自动向用户供球以供练习的羽毛球发球装置。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-16","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"篮球投篮机设计与原型制作（产品设计与原型制作）","titleEn":"Design and prototyping of a basketball shooting machine (Product design and prototyping)","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. CHOY Marian","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-03 21:07:01","descriptionText":"The aim of this study is to design and build a device that can\nautomatically basketball shooting machine to the user for practice.","descZh":"本研究旨在设计并搭建一台能够自动向用户供球的篮球投篮（训练）装置。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-15","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"家庭/商用版水培种植机设计与原型制作（产品设计与原型制作）","titleEn":"Design and prototyping of a water planting machine – home/commercial edition (Product design and prototyping)","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. CHOY Marian","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-03 21:07:01","descriptionText":"The aim of this study is to build and\ndevelop a water planting machine for home/commercial use, \nso that we can achieve a smart indoor home/commercial gardening.","descZh":"本研究的目标是建造并开发一台面向家庭/商业用途的水培机（water planting machine），从而实现智能化的室内家庭/商业园艺。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-10","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"FPV 遥控赛车（产品设计与原型制作）","titleEn":"FPV RC Racing car (Product design and prototyping)","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. CHOY Marian","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 11:46:00","descriptionText":"The aim of this project is to design and\nintegrate the FPV system with 1/10 or 1/24 remote control car.","descZh":"本项目旨在设计 FPV（第一人称视角）系统，并将其集成到 1/10 或 1/24 比例的遥控车上。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"乒乓球喂球与回收机设计与原型制作（产品设计与原型制作）","titleEn":"Design and prototyping of a table tennis ball feeding and recovery machine (Product design and prototyping)","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. CHOY Marian","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 17:07:12","descriptionText":"The aim of this study is to design and build a system device that can\r\nautomatically feed ping pong to the user for practice and recover the ping pong.","descZh":"本研究的目标是设计并搭建一套能够自动向用户发球供其练习、并能回收乒乓球的系统装置。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"足球发球机设计与原型制作（产品设计与原型制作）","titleEn":"Design and prototyping of a football launcher machine (Product design and prototyping)","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. CHOY Marian","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 17:07:12","descriptionText":"The aim of this study is to design and build a device that can\r\nautomatically launch a football to the user for practice.","descZh":"本研究旨在设计并搭建一台能够自动向用户发射足球以供练习的装置。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"家用咖啡烘焙/研磨机设计与原型制作（产品设计与原型制作）","titleEn":"Design and prototyping of a home use coffee roster/grinder (Product design and prototyping)","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. CHOY Marian","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 17:07:12","descriptionText":"The aim of this study is to build and develop a coffee roster/grinder for\r\nhome use. The roster device should be able to have an accurate roast temperature\r\nmeasurements and controls to ensure the coffee beans are roasted equally and\r\nadequately. The grinder device should be able to precisely control the coffee grinding quality and quantity .","descZh":"本研究旨在开发并搭建一台家用咖啡烘焙/研磨一体装置。烘焙装置应具备准确的烘焙温度测量与控制能力，确保咖啡豆烘焙均匀、充分；研磨装置应能精确控制咖啡研磨的粗细度与研磨量。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-19","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"水下遥控无人潜航器（ROV）","titleEn":"Underwater remotely operated vehicle (ROV)","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. CHOY Marian","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 17:07:12","descriptionText":"This capstone project involves the design, development, and testing of an underwater remotely operated vehicle (ROV). The project encompasses mechanical design, electrical systems integration, and programming for remote operation. The goal is to create a reliable, efficient, and innovative ROV capable of completing the specified underwater tasks, demonstrating engineering skills, teamwork, and problem-solving abilities.","descZh":"本毕业设计项目涉及水下遥控潜水器（ROV）的设计、开发与测试。项目涵盖机械设计、电气系统集成以及遥控操作编程。目标是一台可靠、高效且具有创新性的 ROV，能够完成指定的水下任务，展示工程技能、团队协作与问题解决能力。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-_LIUWEI-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"可重构轮腿机器人敏捷运动的强化学习","titleEn":"Reinforcement Learning for Agile Locomotion of Reconfigurable Wheel-Legged Robots","areaZh":"机器人运动；强化学习；仿真到现实迁移；腿式与轮腿机器人","areaEn":"Robot Locomotion; Reinforcement Learning; Sim-to-Real Transfer; Legged and Wheel-Legged Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"LIU Wei","moderator":"Dr. WEI Yan","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 11:28:31","descriptionText":"Wheel-legged robots\r\ncombine the efficiency of rolling with the ability to negotiate discontinuous\r\nterrain, but their hybrid dynamics make agile control difficult. This project\r\naims to develop learning-based locomotion policies for LimX TRON or TRON 2 platforms\r\nin wheeled-leg and footed configurations. \n The student will\r\nconstruct or refine robot models in suitable physics simulators and train\r\npolicies for velocity tracking, blind locomotion over uneven ground, stair\r\nnegotiation, disturbance recovery, and platform-feasible hopping or jumping.\r\nThe work will investigate reward design, curriculum learning, domain\r\nrandomization, actuator and contact modeling, and policy architectures that\r\nexploit proprioception. Exteroceptive sensing may be added as an extension.\r\nPolicies will first be evaluated across multiple simulation environments to\r\nidentify modeling sensitivity, then transferred to hardware through calibrated\r\ninterfaces, latency-aware inference, and progressively constrained safety\r\ntests. \n The expected outcome\r\nis a reproducible simulation-to-simulation and simulation-to-real pipeline,\r\ntogether with quantitative evaluation of tracking accuracy, robustness, energy\r\nuse, and transfer performance. High-impact maneuvers will be attempted only within\r\nthe mechanical and safety limits of the selected platform. \n Preferred skills: Dynamics and control, reinforcement learning,\r\nPython/C++, Linux and robotics simulation; experience with ROS 2, MuJoCo, Isaac\r\nLab, Gazebo, or real-time robot experiments is advantageous.","descZh":"轮腿机器人兼具滚动的效率与越过非连续地形的能力，但其混合动力学使敏捷控制颇具挑战。本项目旨在为 LimX TRON 或 TRON 2 平台的轮腿与足式构型开发基于学习的运动策略。\n学生将在合适的物理仿真器中构建或改进机器人模型，并为速度跟踪、不平地面的盲态运动、楼梯越障、扰动恢复以及平台可行的跳跃训练策略。工作将研究奖励设计、课程学习、域随机化、执行器与接触建模，以及利用本体感觉的策略架构；外部感知可作为扩展加入。\n策略将首先在多个仿真环境中评估以识别建模敏感性，随后通过校准接口、时延感知推理与逐步受限的安全测试迁移到硬件。\n预期成果是一套可复现的\"仿真到仿真\"与\"仿真到实物\"流程，以及对跟踪精度、鲁棒性、能耗与迁移性能的定量评估。高风险机动只会在所选平台的机械与安全限制内尝试。\n偏好技能：动力学与控制、强化学习、Python/C++、Linux 与机器人仿真；有 ROS 2、MuJoCo、Isaac Lab、Gazebo 或实时机器人实验经验者优先。","cats":["AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_LIUWEI-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"四足与复合移动-操作机器人的分层感知控制","titleEn":"Hierarchical Perceptive Control for Quadruped and Composite Locomotion-Manipulation Robots","areaZh":"四足机器人；分层控制；感知运动；移动-操作；深度强化学习","areaEn":"Quadruped Robotics; Hierarchical Control; Perceptive Locomotion; Locomotion-Manipulation; Deep Rein","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"LIU Wei","moderator":"Dr. WEI Yan","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 11:28:56","descriptionText":"Robots operating in\r\nunstructured environments must coordinate fast whole-body stabilization with\r\nslower perception, planning, and task objectives. This project investigates\r\nhierarchical control for a Unitree Go2 or a laboratory composite platform,\r\nincluding a mechanically supported paired-TRON 2 configuration where available. \n The student will\r\ndevelop locomotion skills such as gait generation, velocity tracking, terrain\r\nadaptation, and recovery using reinforcement learning or model-based control. A\r\nhigh-level policy will convert terrain observations and task goals into footholds,\r\nbody commands, or latent skill references, while a low-level controller\r\nprovides robust joint-level tracking. For a platform carrying one or more\r\nmanipulators, the project will extend the framework to locomotion-manipulation,\r\naddressing balance, contact constraints, payload variation, and coordination\r\nbetween the base and arm. A comparative study may examine modular\r\nperception-plus-control against an end-to-end perceptive policy using depth,\r\nLiDAR, or vision observations. \n The final system will\r\nbe validated in simulation and, subject to hardware availability, on the\r\nphysical robot. Evaluation will consider task success, stability margins,\r\ntracking error, robustness to terrain and payload changes, and inference\r\nlatency. \n Preferred skills: Robot dynamics, control, reinforcement learning,\r\noptimization, Python/C++, ROS 2, and simulation; experience with perception,\r\nmanipulators, or real-time systems is beneficial.","descZh":"在非结构化环境中运行的机器人，必须协调快速的全身稳定控制与较慢的感知、规划及任务目标。本项目研究 Unitree Go2 或实验室组合平台（在可用条件下含机械支撑的双 TRON 2 构型）的层级化控制。\n学生将使用强化学习或基于模型的控制，开发步态生成、速度跟踪、地形适应与恢复等运动技能。高层策略将地形观测与任务目标转换为落脚点、机体指令或隐式技能参考；低层控制器则提供稳健的关节级跟踪。对于携带一个或多个机械臂的平台，项目将把框架扩展至\"运动-操作\"，处理平衡、接触约束、负载变化以及基座与机械臂间的协调。比较研究可考察模块化\"感知+控制\"方案与使用深度、LiDAR 或视觉观测的端到端感知策略之间的差异。\n最终系统将在仿真中验证，并在硬件可用条件下于实体机器人上验证。评估将考虑任务成功率、稳定裕度、跟踪误差、对地形与负载变化的鲁棒性以及推理时延。\n偏好技能：机器人动力学、控制、强化学习、优化、Python/C++、ROS 2 与仿真；有感知、机械臂或实时系统经验者更佳。","cats":["CTRL","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_LIUWEI-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向全身人形机器人技能的遥操作与模仿学习","titleEn":"Teleoperation and Imitation Learning for Whole-Body Humanoid Robot Skills","areaZh":"人形机器人；遥操作；模仿学习；全身控制；感知运动","areaEn":"Humanoid Robotics; Teleoperation; Imitation Learning; Whole-Body Control; Perceptive Locomotion","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"LIU Wei","moderator":"Dr. WEI Yan","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 11:29:21","descriptionText":"Humanoid robots can\r\noperate in human-centered environments, but collecting demonstrations and\r\nconverting them into stable whole-body behaviors remain major challenges. This\r\nproject aims to develop a teleoperation and imitation-learning pipeline for a\r\nUnitree G1, LimX Oli, or a comparable humanoid platform available in the\r\nlaboratory. \n The student will\r\nintegrate human motion input from motion capture, wearable sensors, vision, or\r\nhandheld interfaces and retarget it to the robot while respecting joint limits,\r\nbalance, self-collision, and contact constraints. Demonstrations will be recorded\r\nin a structured dataset and used to train behavior-cloning or sequence policies\r\nfor selected skills. Depending on project scope, the work may cover whole-body\r\npose tracking, upper-body manipulation while standing, or environment-aware\r\nwalking over obstacles. Reinforcement learning or trajectory optimization may\r\nbe used to improve robustness after imitation learning, and perception may be\r\nincorporated through depth or visual observations. \n The expected outcome\r\nis an end-to-end workflow from human command or demonstration to simulated and\r\nreal-robot execution, with evaluation of motion fidelity, task success,\r\nbalance, latency, and robustness. Hardware trials will follow staged safety\r\nchecks and remain within approved operating limits. \n Preferred skills: Kinematics, dynamics and control, machine learning,\r\nPython/C++, robotics simulation, and ROS 2; experience with motion capture,\r\ncomputer vision, or humanoid hardware is advantageous.","descZh":"人形机器人可在以人为中心的环境中运行，但采集示范并将其转化为稳定的全身行为仍是重大挑战。本项目旨在为实验室可用的 Unitree G1、LimX Oli 或类似人形平台开发遥操作与模仿学习流程。\n学生将集成来自动作捕捉、可穿戴传感器、视觉或手持界面的人体运动输入，并在考虑关节限制、平衡、自碰撞与接触约束的同时将其重定向到机器人。示范将以结构化数据集记录，用于为选定技能训练行为克隆或序列策略。根据项目范围，工作可涵盖全身姿态跟踪、站立状态的上半身操作，或越过障碍物的环境感知行走。模仿学习后可采用强化学习或轨迹优化提升鲁棒性，并可通过深度或视觉观测融入感知。\n预期成果是从人类指令/示范到仿真与实物机器人执行的端到端工作流，并评估运动保真度、任务成功率、平衡、时延与鲁棒性。硬件试验将遵循分阶段安全检查，并保持在批准的运行限制内。\n偏好技能：运动学、动力学与控制、机器学习、Python/C++、机器人仿真与 ROS 2；有动作捕捉、计算机视觉或人形机器人硬件经验者优先。","cats":["AI","CTRL","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_LIUWEI-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"单臂与双臂操作的视觉-语言-动作学习","titleEn":"Vision-Language-Action Learning for Single- and Dual-Arm Manipulation","areaZh":"机器人操作；视觉-语言-动作模型；模仿学习；多模态学习；真实机器人","areaEn":"Robot Manipulation; Vision-Language-Action Models; Imitation Learning; Multimodal Learning; Real-Ro","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"LIU Wei","moderator":"Dr. WEI Yan","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 11:31:30","descriptionText":"Vision-language-action\r\nmodels offer a promising route to robots that follow natural-language\r\ninstructions and generalize across objects and tasks. This project aims to\r\nadapt and deploy an open VLA or robot foundation model, such as a model from\r\nthe NVIDIA Isaac GR00T ecosystem or a suitable alternative, for single-arm or\r\nbimanual manipulation. \n The student will\r\ndesign representative tasks, establish teleoperation or kinesthetic data\r\ncollection, and build a reproducible data pipeline covering sensor calibration,\r\ntime synchronization, episode segmentation, action representation, quality\r\ncontrol, and dataset versioning. A pretrained model will be post-trained using\r\nparameter-efficient or full fine-tuning as resources permit. The study will\r\nexamine embodiment adaptation, camera and language conditioning, inference\r\nlatency, closed-loop execution, and recovery from distribution shifts. For\r\ndual-arm tasks, special attention will be given to temporal coordination,\r\ncollision avoidance, and shared-object constraints. \n The final system will\r\nbe evaluated on physical hardware using task success, generalization to new\r\nobject poses or instructions, data efficiency, and execution speed. The project\r\nshould document negative results and safety interventions as part of a reusable\r\ndeployment pipeline. \n Preferred skills: Deep learning, computer vision, Python/PyTorch, robot\r\nkinematics, Linux and data engineering; experience with ROS 2, teleoperation,\r\ntransformers, or manipulation hardware is desirable. Access to substantial GPU\r\nresources may be required for post-training.","descZh":"视觉-语言-动作（VLA）模型为让机器人遵循自然语言指令并跨物体与任务泛化提供了有前景的路径。本项目旨在适配并部署一个开源 VLA 或机器人基础模型（如来自 NVIDIA Isaac GR00T 生态的模型或合适的替代品），用于单臂或双臂操作。\n学生将设计代表性任务，建立遥操作或动觉示教数据采集流程，并构建覆盖传感器标定、时间同步、回合分割、动作表示、质量控制与数据集版本管理的可复现数据管线。将按资源允许情况对预训练模型进行参数高效或全量微调。研究将考察具身适配、相机与语言条件化、推理时延、闭环执行以及对分布偏移的恢复；对双臂任务，将特别关注时间协调、碰撞避免与共享物体约束。\n最终系统将在实体硬件上评估，指标包括任务成功率、对新物体位姿或指令的泛化、数据效率与执行速度。项目应把负面结果与安全干预记录为可复用部署管线的一部分。\n偏好技能：深度学习、计算机视觉、Python/PyTorch、机器人运动学、Linux 与数据工程；有 ROS 2、遥操作、Transformer 或操作硬件经验者优先。微调可能需要大量 GPU 资源。","cats":["AI","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_LIUWEI-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向具身 AI 的同步第一视角多模态数据采集","titleEn":"Synchronized Egocentric Multimodal Data Acquisition for Embodied AI","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"LIU Wei","moderator":"Dr. WEI Yan","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 11:34:47","descriptionText":"Human demonstrations\r\ncaptured from the first-person perspective can provide scalable supervision for\r\nembodied agents, but useful datasets require reliable synchronization,\r\ncalibration, and privacy-aware processing. This project aims to develop a\r\nwearable acquisition system combining selected head-, eye-, and wrist-mounted\r\nsensors. \n The student will\r\nintegrate modalities such as RGB video, depth, inertial measurements, hand or\r\nbody pose, gaze or attention proxies, audio, and action annotations. The work\r\nwill address hardware triggering or clock synchronization, intrinsic and\r\nextrinsic calibration, timestamp drift, coordinate frames, missing data,\r\nstorage bandwidth, and ergonomic constraints. A software pipeline will perform\r\nrecording, validation, anonymization where appropriate, metadata generation,\r\nand conversion into episode-based formats compatible with representative\r\nvision-language-action and world-action model workflows. Pilot tasks may\r\ninclude tabletop manipulation, tool use, navigation, or everyday activities. \n The outcome will be a\r\ndocumented prototype, a small high-quality benchmark dataset, and quantitative\r\ncharacterization of synchronization error, calibration accuracy, data\r\ncompleteness, and operator comfort. Ethical approval, informed consent, secure\r\nstorage, and exclusion of unnecessary personally identifiable information must\r\nbe incorporated before human data collection. \n Preferred skills: Embedded systems, computer vision, sensor calibration,\r\nPython/C++, data engineering, and Linux; experience with wearable devices, eye\r\ntracking, ROS 2, or research ethics is beneficial.","descZh":"从第一人称视角采集的人类示范可为具身智能体提供可扩展的监督信号，但可用的数据集需要可靠的同步、标定与隐私感知处理。本项目旨在开发一套可穿戴采集系统，组合头部、眼部与腕部安装的选定传感器。\n学生将集成 RGB 视频、深度、惯性测量、手部/身体姿态、注视或注意力近似信号、音频与动作标注等多模态数据。工作将处理硬件触发或时钟同步、内参与外参标定、时间戳漂移、坐标系、数据缺失、存储带宽与人体工学约束。软件管线将执行录制、校验、适当匿名化、元数据生成，并转换为与代表性视觉-语言-动作及世界-动作模型工作流兼容的回合格式。试点任务可包括桌面操作、工具使用、导航或日常活动。\n成果将是一套文档化原型、一个小型高质量基准数据集，以及对同步误差、标定精度、数据完整性与操作者舒适度的定量表征。在采集人类数据前，必须落实伦理审批、知情同意、安全存储，并排除非必要的个人身份信息。\n偏好技能：嵌入式系统、计算机视觉、传感器标定、Python/C++、数据工程与 Linux；有可穿戴设备、眼动追踪、ROS 2 或科研伦理经验者优先。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-_LIUWEI-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向实时边缘 AI 应用的高效视觉-语言模型","titleEn":"Efficient Vision-Language Models for Real-Time Edge AI Applications","areaZh":"边缘 AI；视觉-语言模型；模型压缩；嵌入式系统；实时推理","areaEn":"Edge AI; Vision-Language Models; Model Compression; Embedded Systems; Real-Time Inference","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"LIU Wei","moderator":"Dr. WEI Yan","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 11:37:15","descriptionText":"Running multimodal\r\nlanguage models at the edge such as Rockchip, Novatek or Nvidia Jetson can\r\nreduce latency, network dependence, and privacy risk, but memory, power, and\r\nthermal constraints limit feasible model size and throughput. This project\r\ninvestigates the deployment of compact language or vision-language models on\r\nNovatek-, Rockchip-, or NVIDIA Jetson-class development platforms. \n The student will\r\nselect a practical real-time application, such as visual question answering,\r\nscene description, voice-assisted inspection, or robot operator support, and\r\nestablish performance requirements. Model adaptation will normally be performed\r\non a workstation or server using prompt tuning, low-rank adaptation,\r\ndistillation, or task-specific fine-tuning. Deployment will then use\r\nquantization, pruning where appropriate, operator conversion, memory planning,\r\nand hardware-specific runtimes. Several-billion-parameter models may be\r\nconsidered only when device memory and accelerator support are sufficient;\r\nsmaller or split models should be used when they provide a better latency-power\r\ntrade-off. \n The final prototype\r\nwill be evaluated for accuracy, time to first response, token or frame\r\nthroughput, peak memory, power consumption, temperature, and sustained\r\nperformance. The project will deliver a reproducible conversion and\r\nbenchmarking workflow rather than assuming identical capability across\r\ndifferent system-on-chip platforms. \n Preferred skills: Deep learning, Python/C++, Linux, embedded development,\r\nmodel quantization and profiling; familiarity with ONNX, TensorRT, RKNN or\r\nother vendor toolchains is advantageous.","descZh":"在 Rockchip、Novatek 或 NVIDIA Jetson 等边缘设备上运行多模态语言模型，可降低时延、网络依赖与隐私风险，但内存、功耗与散热约束限制了可行模型规模与吞吐量。本项目研究在 Novatek、Rockchip 或 NVIDIA Jetson 级开发平台上部署紧凑型语言或视觉-语言模型。\n学生将选择一个实用的实时应用（如视觉问答、场景描述、语音辅助巡检或机器人操作员支持），并确定性能需求。模型适配通常在工作站或服务器上进行，采用提示调优、低秩适配、蒸馏或任务特定微调；部署则采用量化、适当剪枝、算子转换、内存规划与硬件特定运行时。仅当设备内存与加速器支持足够时，才考虑数十亿参数模型；若更小或分体模型能提供更好的时延-功耗权衡，则应使用此类模型。\n最终原型将评估精度、首响应时间、token/帧吞吐、峰值内存、功耗、温度与持续性能。项目将交付可复现的转换与基准测试工作流，而非假定不同系统级芯片平台具有相同能力。\n偏好技能：深度学习、Python/C++、Linux、嵌入式开发、模型量化与性能剖析；熟悉 ONNX、TensorRT、RKNN 或其他厂商工具链者优先。","cats":["AI","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"眼压可控装置（产品设计与原型制作）","titleEn":"Intraocular pressure controllable gadget (Product design and prototyping)","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. WEI Yan","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-03 21:07:01","descriptionText":"The aim of this study is to build and develop a gadget that can help\nto manipulate the intraocular pressure (internal pressure of eye), so that the\nintraocular pressure impact on different eye characteristics can be study and\nresearch. 3D printing will be used to tailor make the gadget for the use in\npatient.","descZh":"本研究旨在开发并搭建一个能够帮助调节眼内压（眼球内部压力）的小装置，从而研究与探索眼内压对不同眼部特征的影响。将使用 3D 打印为该装置定制制作，以便用于患者。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-12","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"尿布尿湿检测器设计与原型制作（产品设计与原型制作）","titleEn":"Design and prototyping of Diaper Wet Detector (Product design and prototyping)","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. WEI Yan","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-03 21:07:01","descriptionText":"The wetness of a diaper would greatly affect the comfortability of\nan infant. The aim of this study is to design and build a gadget/device that\ncan help to detect the wetness of the diaper, so that the infant’s parent would\nbe able to receive an alerts when a child has pooped.","descZh":"尿布的干爽程度会极大地影响婴儿的舒适度。本研究旨在设计并搭建一个能够帮助检测尿布湿润度的小工具/装置，使家长能在孩子排便后及时收到提醒。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-13","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"1/24 或 1/28 遥控车实验测试台——底盘测功机系统设计与原型制作（产品原型制作）","titleEn":"Design and prototyping of an experimental testing bench for 1/24 or 1/28 RC car - chassis dynamometer system (Product prototyping)","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. WEI Yan","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 11:46:26","descriptionText":"The aim of this project is to build and\ndevelop an experimental chassis dynamometer system to measure the RPM, kV,\nvoltage, current draw, power and torque output of vehicle.","descZh":"本项目旨在搭建并开发一套实验用底盘测功机系统，用于测量车辆的转速（RPM）、kV 值、电压、电流、功率与扭矩输出。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-MATCHKO-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"家用鱼缸水质处理系统（产品设计与原型制作）","titleEn":"Water conditioning system for domestic fishing tank  (Product design and prototyping)","areaZh":"","areaEn":"","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. KO Match Wai Lun","moderator":"Dr. WEI Yan","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 11:46:54","descriptionText":"The aim of this project is to design and\nbuild an experimental testing bench to test and calibrate the water\nconditioning system for domestic fishing tank.","descZh":"本项目旨在设计并搭建一个实验测试台，用于测试和标定家用鱼缸的水质调节系统。","cats":[]},{"ref":"2026-27-_ZHENGYL-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于基础模型的可穿戴生理信号睡眠分期","titleEn":"Foundation Model-Based Sleep Staging Using Wearable Physiological Signals","areaZh":"可穿戴传感；生物医学信号处理；基础模型；睡眠监测","areaEn":"Wearable Sensing; Biomedical Signal Processing; Foundation Models; Sleep Monitoring","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"ZHENG Yali","moderator":"Dr. WEI Yan","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This project investigates the use of pretrained foundation models for automatic sleep staging from physiological signals collected by wearable devices, such as smartwatches and wristbands. It aims to identify sleep stages from photoplethysmography (PPG), accelerometry and related signals, and to improve staging accuracy and generalisation across individuals.\n Expected outputs: \n An implemented sleep-staging pipeline, comparative experiments and visualisations demonstrating its performance and limitations.\n Pre- and co-requisites / preferred skills:\n Proficiency in Python and a foundation in deep learning are expected. Familiarity with biomedical signal processing and basic machine-learning methods is preferred. An interest in sleep monitoring, foundation models and wearable artificial intelligence is desirable.","descZh":"本项目研究使用预训练基础模型（foundation models），对智能手表、腕带等可穿戴设备采集的生理信号进行自动睡眠分期。目标是从光电容积脉搏波（PPG）、加速度计及相关信号中识别睡眠阶段，并提升分期准确率与跨个体泛化能力。\n预期成果：一套已实现的睡眠分期流程、对比实验及展示其性能与局限的可视化。\n先修/并修要求及偏好技能：需熟练掌握 Python，具备深度学习基础；熟悉生物医学信号处理与基础机器学习方法者优先；对睡眠监测、基础模型与可穿戴人工智能有兴趣者更佳。","cats":["AI","BIO","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_ZHENGYL-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"个性化助眠音乐：设计及其对大学生睡眠结构的影响","titleEn":"Personalised Sleep-Promoting Music: Design and Effects on Sleep Architecture in University Students","areaZh":"睡眠监测；可穿戴生理传感；个性化音乐干预","areaEn":"Sleep Monitoring; Wearable Physiological Sensing; Personalised Music Interventions","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"ZHENG Yali","moderator":"Dr. WEI Yan","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This project addresses difficulties in falling asleep and reduced sleep quality among university students. It will investigate the effects of sleep-promoting music on sleep initiation and overnight sleep architecture, and explore individual differences in sleep responses to support the design and application of personalised music interventions.\n Expected outputs:\n A music intervention study protocol, a sleep-data analysis pipeline, and a comparative evaluation with visualisations of group-level and individual responses.\n Pre- and co-requisites / preferred skills:\n Proficiency in Python or MATLAB is expected, with a foundation in data analysis, statistics or biomedical signal processing. An interest in sleep monitoring, music-based interventions and wearable health research is desirable.","descZh":"本项目针对大学生入睡困难与睡眠质量下降的问题。将研究助眠音乐对入睡过程及整夜睡眠结构的影响，并探究睡眠响应的个体差异，以支持个性化音乐干预方案的设计与应用。\n预期成果：一套音乐干预研究方案、一条睡眠数据分析流程，以及带群组与个体响应可视化对比的评估。\n先修/并修要求及偏好技能：需熟练掌握 Python 或 MATLAB，具备数据分析、统计学或生物医学信号处理基础。对睡眠监测、音乐干预与可穿戴健康研究有兴趣者更佳。","cats":["BIO","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIWD-12","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于定向能量的非化学防虫工具研发","titleEn":"Development of non-chemical anti-insect tools based on directed energy","areaZh":"设计与制造 | 能源 | 材料与固体 | 机器人；无人机与控制","areaEn":"Design and manufacture | Energy | Materials and Solids | Robotics; Drones and Control","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Jason Wendi","moderator":"Dr. ZHANG Cuiping","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-05 05:07:02","descriptionText":"In this project, students will review current non-chemical technology used for killing harmful insects, such as mosquitoes, cockroaches, etc. Potential directions can include use of directed energy, such as focused acoustic waves, directed high-power laser beams, etc. The project team will brainstorm for innovative mechanisms that use non-chemical methods to kill harmful insects, and identify a number of projects that develop different methods.","descZh":"在本项目中，学生将调研当前用于杀灭有害昆虫（如蚊、蟑螂等）的非化学技术。可能的方向包括使用定向能量，如聚焦声波、高功率定向激光束等。项目团队将集思广益，探索利用非化学方法杀灭有害昆虫的创新机制，并确定若干开发不同方法的方向/子项目。","cats":["AERO","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_JIAOLEW-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"手持式鼻咽内窥机器人的设计与建模","titleEn":"Design and Modeling of Hand-held Nasopharyngeal Endoscopic Robots","areaZh":"手术机器人","areaEn":"Surgical Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"WANG Jiaole","moderator":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 11:54:38","descriptionText":"Objective and Significance:\nNasopharyngeal carcinoma has an extremely high incidence rate in southern China, reaching 20–30 cases per 100,000 people—three times the global average. It is therefore commonly known as \"Cantonese cancer.\" About 80% of patients are already in advanced stages when diagnosed, making early screening the only key to improving prognosis. Nasal endoscopy is the optimal method for early screening. This project aims to design a handheld nasopharyngoscope robot that allows medical staff to perform early screening of nasopharyngeal carcinoma.\nTasks include: \n1. structural design and CAD modeling of the robot body with emphasis on ergonomics and weight reduction; \n2. kinematic modeling to analyze motion characteristics at different nasal cavity depths and angles; \n3. prototype fabrication and experimental validation of functional reliability.\nPrerequisites:\n1. Mechanical design fundamentals \n2. Basic robotics (kinematics, coordinate transformations) \n3. MATLAB or Python programming \n4. 3D CAD modeling (SolidWorks) \nCo-requisites:\n1. Control systems or mechanism dynamics \n2. Mechanics of materials or FEA (for lightweight design) \n3. Biomechanics (preferred but not required) \nPreferred Skills:\n1.Rapid prototyping (3D printing, machining) \n2. Embedded control (Arduino, STM32) \n3. Hands on experience with medical devices or endoscopes","descZh":"目标与意义：\n鼻咽癌在中国南方发病率极高，达到每 10 万人 20–30 例，是全球平均水平的三倍。因此它通常被称为“广东癌”。约 80% 的患者在确诊时已处于晚期，使得早期筛查成为改善预后的唯一关键。鼻内镜检查是早期筛查的最佳方法。本项目旨在设计一款手持式鼻咽镜机器人，使医护人员能够开展鼻咽癌的早期筛查。\n任务包括：\n1. 机器人本体的结构设计与 CAD 建模，注重人体工学与减重；\n2. 运动学建模，分析不同鼻腔深度与角度下的运动特性；\n3. 样机制作与功能可靠性的实验验证。\n先修要求：\n1. 机械设计基础\n2. 基础机器人学（运动学、坐标变换）\n3. MATLAB 或 Python 编程\n4. 3D CAD 建模（SolidWorks）\n同修要求：\n1. 控制系统或机构动力学\n2. 材料力学或 FEA（用于轻量化设计）\n3. 生物力学（优先，但非必需）\n优先技能：\n1. 快速原型制作（3D 打印、机加工）\n2. 嵌入式控制（Arduino、STM32）\n3. 具有医疗器械或内窥镜方面的实操经验","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_JIAOLEW-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"单孔甲状腺手术自动皮瓣牵开机器人的优化设计","titleEn":"Optimal Design of an Automatic Flap Retraction Robot for Single-Port Thyroid Surgery","areaZh":"手术机器人","areaEn":"Surgical Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"WANG Jiaole","moderator":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 11:55:23","descriptionText":"Objective and Significance:\nFrom 2000 to 2018, the incidence rates among Chinese women and men were 15.7% and 16.9%, respectively, making it the third most common cancer in China. Gasless unilateral axillary approach endoscopic thyroid surgery is the currently widely used procedure. This method mainly relies on the surgeon manually operating a retractor to perform flap retraction and establish a subcutaneous surgical channel. This project aims to develop an automatic flap retraction robot that enhances precision and safety in single-port thyroid surgery. Students will design the retraction mechanism, build kinematic and static models, and experimentally validate the robot’s performance under varying retraction depths and angles.\nScope of Work:\n1. Propose and model a novel retraction mechanism using CAD software.\n2. Derive forward/inverse kinematics and static force relationships.\n3. Simulate motion and force transmission using MATLAB/Simulink.\n4. Construct a physical prototype or test bench and conduct experiments to verify functionality.\nPre‑requisites:\n1. Fundamentals of mechanical design and machine elements.\n2. Basic knowledge of robotics (kinematics, Jacobian, statics).\n3. Proficiency in MATLAB or Python for numerical computation.\n4. Experience with 3D CAD modeling (e.g., SolidWorks).\nCo‑requisites (recommended to be taken concurrently):\n1. Introduction to control systems (for understanding actuation and feedback).\n2. Finite element analysis (FEA) or mechanics of materials (for stress/strain evaluation).\n3. Medical device design or biomechanics (optional but beneficial).\nPreferred Skills:\n1. Hands‑on experience with rapid prototyping (3D printing, laser cutting).\n2. Familiarity with ROS (Robot Operating System) or embedded control (Arduino/STM32).\n3. Ability to design and conduct experiments (data acquisition, sensor integration).","descZh":"目标与意义：2000 至 2018 年间，中国女性与男性的发病率分别为 15.7% 与 16.9%，使其成为中国第三常见癌症（指甲状腺癌）。免充气经单侧腋窝入路内镜甲状腺手术是目前广泛采用的手术方式。该方法主要依赖外科医生手动操作拉钩进行皮瓣牵开并建立皮下手术通道。本项目旨在开发一种自动皮瓣牵开机器人，提升单孔甲状腺手术的精度与安全性。学生将设计牵开机构、构建运动学与静力学模型，并在不同牵开深度与角度下实验验证机器人性能。\n工作范围：\n1. 使用 CAD 软件提出并建模一种新型牵开机构；\n2. 推导正/逆运动学与静力关系；\n3. 使用 MATLAB/Simulink 仿真运动与力传递；\n4. 构建物理原型或试验台并开展实验验证功能。\n先修要求：\n1. 机械设计与机械零件基础；\n2. 机器人学基础（运动学、雅可比、静力学）；\n3. 熟练使用 MATLAB 或 Python 进行数值计算；\n4. 有 3D CAD 建模经验（如 SolidWorks）。\n并修要求（建议同时修读）：\n1. 控制系统导论（用于理解驱动与反馈）；\n2. 有限元分析（FEA）或材料力学（用于应力/应变评估）；\n3. 医疗器械设计或生物力学（可选但有益）。\n偏好技能：\n1. 有快速原型制作经验（3D 打印、激光切割）；\n2. 熟悉 ROS（机器人操作系统）或嵌入式控制（Arduino/STM32）；\n3. 能设计并开展实验（数据采集、传感器集成）。","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_JIAOLEW-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于皮瓣牵开的力-触觉感知系统","titleEn":"Force&#8211;Haptic Perception System for Skin-Flap Retraction","areaZh":"手术机器人","areaEn":"Surgical Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"WANG Jiaole","moderator":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 11:56:15","descriptionText":"Objective and Significance:\nFrom 2000 to 2018, the incidence rates among Chinese women and men were 15.7% and 16.9%, respectively, making it the third most common cancer in China. Gasless unilateral axillary approach endoscopic thyroid surgery is the currently widely used procedure. This method mainly relies on the surgeon manually operating a retractor to perform flap retraction and establish a subcutaneous surgical channel. This project aims to develop a force–haptic perception system to monitor real time interaction forces between the retractor and tissue during flap retraction. \nTasks include: \n(1) integrating force/torque sensors onto the skin-flap retraction robot arm; \n(2) conducting retraction experiments and collecting six axis force/torque and haptic data; \n(3) analyzing force patterns and instability trends as retraction transitions from stable to unstable states.\nPrerequisites:\n1. Fundamentals of mechanical design and robotics \n2. Basic knowledge of sensors and signal processing \n3. MATLAB or Python for data analysis \nCo requisites:\n1. Control systems or haptics \n2. Biomechanics or soft tissue mechanics (preferred) \nPreferred Skills:\n1. Hands on work with force/torque sensors (e.g., ATI, OptoForce) \n2. Data acquisition and real time programming (LabVIEW, C++, ROS) \n3. Experimental design and statistical analysis","descZh":"目标与意义：2000 至 2018 年间，中国女性与男性的发病率分别为 15.7% 与 16.9%，使其成为中国第三常见癌症（指甲状腺癌）。免充气经单侧腋窝入路内镜甲状腺手术是目前广泛采用的手术方式。该方法主要依赖外科医生手动操作拉钩进行皮瓣牵开并建立皮下手术通道。本项目旨在开发一套力-触觉感知系统，实时监测皮瓣牵开过程中拉钩与组织之间的交互力。\n任务包括：(1) 将力/力矩传感器集成到皮瓣牵开机械臂上；(2) 开展牵开实验，采集六轴力/力矩与触觉数据；(3) 分析牵开由稳定转向不稳定状态过程中的力模式与失稳趋势。\n先修要求：1. 机械设计与机器人学基础；2. 传感器与信号处理基础知识；3. 使用 MATLAB 或 Python 进行数据分析。\n并修要求：1. 控制系统或触觉；2. 生物力学或软组织力学（优先）。\n偏好技能：1. 力/力矩传感器的动手经验（如 ATI、OptoForce）；2. 数据采集与实时编程（LabVIEW、C++、ROS）；3. 实验设计与统计分析。","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_JIAOLEW-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"鼻腔内窥镜检查的 3D 地图构建","titleEn":"3D Map Construction for Nasal Endoscopy Examination","areaZh":"手术机器人","areaEn":"Surgical Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"WANG Jiaole","moderator":"Professor JIANG Zhongliang","capacity":2,"availCap":2,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 11:53:59","descriptionText":"Objective and Significance:\nNasopharyngeal carcinoma has an extremely high incidence rate in southern China, reaching 20–30 cases per 100,000 people—three times the global average. It is therefore commonly known as \"Cantonese cancer.\" About 80% of patients are already in advanced stages when diagnosed, making early screening the only key to improving prognosis. Nasal endoscopy is the optimal method for early screening. This project aims to develop a 3D mapping method for nasal endoscopy to quantify examination coverage and prevent missed detection in early screening of nasopharyngeal carcinoma. \nTasks include: \n1. integrating a micro visual sensor and illumination module on a nasendoscope robot prototype;\n2. constructing a kinematic model and performing spatiotemporal calibration between the robot and the visual sensor; \n3. developing a 3D reconstruction algorithm using robot poses and endoscopic images; \n4. analyzing nasal cavity coverage by comparing with preoperative CT images and generating missed-detection warnings.\nPrerequisites:\n1. Robotics fundamentals (kinematics, coordinate transformations) \n2. Computer vision basics (feature extraction, camera model) \n3. MATLAB or Python programming \n4. Familiarity with SLAM or 3D reconstruction concepts \nCo requisites:\n1. Sensor fusion or multi sensor calibration \n2. Medical image processing (CT segmentation, registration) \n3. Control systems (preferred but not required) \nPreferred Skills:\n1. Experience with visual SLAM (e.g., ORB SLAM, RTAB Map) \n2. ROS (Robot Operating System) \n3. Camera calibration and point cloud processing (PCL) \n4. Hands on work with miniature cameras or endoscopes","descZh":"目标与意义：鼻咽癌在华南地区发病率极高，达每 10 万人 20~30 例，是全球平均水平的三倍，故俗称\"广东癌\"。约 80% 的患者确诊时已处于晚期，早期筛查是改善预后的唯一关键。鼻内镜检查是早期筛查的最佳手段。本项目旨在开发一种鼻内镜三维映射方法，用于量化检查覆盖范围，防止鼻咽癌早期筛查中的漏检。\n任务包括：\n1. 将微型视觉传感器与照明模块集成到鼻内镜机器人样机上；\n2. 构建运动学模型，并完成机器人与视觉传感器之间的时空标定；\n3. 利用机器人位姿与内镜图像开发三维重建算法；\n4. 与术前 CT 图像对比分析鼻腔覆盖情况，并生成漏检预警。\n先修要求：1. 机器人学基础（运动学、坐标变换）；2. 计算机视觉基础（特征提取、相机模型）；3. MATLAB 或 Python 编程；4. 熟悉 SLAM 或三维重建概念。\n并修要求：1. 传感器融合或多传感器标定；2. 医学图像处理（CT 分割、配准）；3. 控制系统（优先但非必需）。\n偏好技能：1. 有视觉 SLAM 经验（如 ORB-SLAM、RTAB-Map）；2. 会使用 ROS（机器人操作系统）；3. 相机标定与点云处理（PCL）；4. 有微型相机或内窥镜实操经验。","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIWD-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于新兴 AR 显示的产品与应用创新设计","titleEn":"Innovative design of product and applications based on emerging AR displays","areaZh":"AR 显示；产品设计","areaEn":"AR display; product design","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Jason Wendi","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT","LAT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-05 03:34:01","descriptionText":"AR glasses and displays project virtual information on top of reality objects and enable revolutionary new channels of information delivery for human beings. With decades of development, various technologies have been proposed, tested, and demonstrated for AR displays, some of them very promising for large-volume production. Versatile applications of AR displays, from educational, medical, to military, just to name a few, have been demonstrated. Particularly, this area has attracted significant venture capital in the past 5 years and some start-up companies are growing rapidly. With big technology giants, like META (Facebook), Microsoft, Apple, Huawei, etc., and many start-up companies heavily investing in this area, consumers may expect to see practical AR glass and display products with satisfactory performance, affordable price, and rich applications in the next few years. \n Each student working on this project is expected to explore the design and prototype demonstration of an innovative AR display based application. When necessary, commercial AR glasses can be purchased and modified to realize the application proposed by the student. For example, some past student projects include AR-based HKU campus tour guide, AR-based furniture assembly guide, AR application for museum exhibitions, etc.\n The student is expected to have a strong interest in hard technology commercialization and business analysis, good capability in understanding technology concepts in optics, physics, and electronics, and good capability in collecting and analyzing information from the internet and other resources.","descZh":"AR 眼镜与显示器将虚拟信息叠加到现实物体之上，为人类开辟了革命性的信息传递渠道。经过数十年的发展，业界已提出、测试并演示了多种 AR 显示技术，其中一些在大规模生产方面前景广阔。AR 显示器的应用广泛，从教育、医疗到军事，不一而足。特别是，过去五年该领域吸引了大量风险投资，一些初创公司正迅速成长。随着 META（Facebook）、微软、苹果、华为等科技巨头以及众多初创企业大举投入，消费者有望在接下来几年看到性能令人满意、价格可承受、应用丰富的实用 AR 眼镜与显示产品。\n参与本项目的每名学生需要探索一个创新 AR 显示应用的设�计与原型演示。必要时可购买并改装商用 AR 眼镜，以实现学生提出的应用。例如，过去一些学生项目包括基于 AR 的港大校园导览、基于 AR 的家具装配指南、面向博物馆展览的 AR 应用等。\n学生应具备对硬科技商业化与商业分析的浓厚兴趣、理解光学/物理/电子技术概念的较强能力，以及从互联网及其他资源收集与分析信息的良好能力。","cats":["DES","ELE"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向 3D 生物打印与冷冻保存一体化的 ECM 仿生生物墨水设计与评估","titleEn":"Design and Evaluation of ECM-inspired Bioinks for Integrated 3D Bioprinting and Cryopreservation of","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:36:39","descriptionText":"\"Design and Evaluation of ECM-inspired Bioinks for Integrated 3D Bioprinting and Cryopreservation of Tumor Models\"\n3D bioprinting emerges as a transformative technology for fabricating functional tissues and organs by precisely depositing cell-laden bioinks into predefined architectures. Recently, this field has increasingly converged with rational mechanical design of bio-metamaterials, resulting in structural architectures that are deliberately engineered to regulate mechanical behavior and direct biological functions. Collagen, a major structural component of extracellular matrix (ECM) of animals or humans, is widely used in bioinks due to its inherent biocompatibility, bioactivity, and ability to support cell adhesion, proliferation, and migration. However, collagen-based bioinks still face significant challenges in balancing printability, mechanical stability, and long-term preservation of embedded cells and tissue constructs. Tumor models generated by 3D bioprinting, which enable controlled recapitulation of tumor architecture, gradients of nutrients and oxygen, and cell-matrix interactions, have shown high promise for preclinical drug screening. Cryopreservation of bioprinted tumor constructs is important: pre-fabricated, well-characterized models could be stored and later revived for on-demand drug screening. There is a critical need to rationally design collagen-based bioinks. This project aims to integrate bioink engineering, 3D bioprinting, and cryopreservation strategies to establish robust collagen-based bioinks tailored for bioprinted tumor models that can be stored and reactivated for standardized, on-demand drug screening. Bioinks will be formulated by using type I collagen, supplemented with additional ECM-derived polymers (methacrylated hyaluronic acid, HAMA) to modulate viscoelastic properties and enhance biomimicry, together with D-(+) melezitose hydrate at different concentrations as cryoprotective agents to generate optimized inks for cryopreservation. We shall systematically investigate the influence of key printing parameters – specifically, dispensing times and extrusion pressure - on the characteristics of extruded droplets by quantifying their volumes post-printing. Droplet diameters and line widths will be analyzed when printing collagen-based inks onto a cryoplate maintained at below -5 °C to assess freezing-assisted pattern fidelity. In addition, a series of complex 3D constructs will be computationally modeled and printed using the cryopreservable inks to evaluate long-term shape retention following fabrication. The resulting 3D cryoprinted, tumor cell-laden modules will be stored for up to 14 days, followed by controlled thawing and resuscitation, after which the viability and functional performance of the encapsulated cells will be systematically characterized.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note:\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"用于肿瘤模型 3D 生物打印与冷冻保存一体化设计的 ECM 启发生物墨水\"\n3D 生物打印通过将载细胞生物墨水精确沉积到预定义架构中，成为制造功能性组织与器官的变革性技术。近年来，该领域日益与生物超材料的理性机械设计相融合，形成经过刻意工程设计的结构架构，以调控力学行为并引导生物学功能。胶原蛋白是动物或人类细胞外基质（ECM）的主要结构成分，因其固有的生物相容性、生物活性以及支持细胞黏附、增殖与迁移的能力而被广泛用于生物墨水。然而，基于胶原蛋白的生物墨水在平衡可打印性、机械稳定性与嵌入细胞及组织构建体的长期保存方面仍面临重大挑战。3D 生物打印生成的肿瘤模型能够受控复现肿瘤结构、营养与氧气梯度以及细胞-基质相互作用，在临床前药物筛选中展现出高度前景。生物打印肿瘤构建体的冷冻保存十分重要：预制的、表征完善的模型可储存并在日后复苏，用于按需药物筛选。合理设计基于胶原蛋白的生物墨水存在迫切需求。本项目旨在将生物墨水工程、3D 生物打印与冷冻保存策略相结合，建立专门用于可储存、可重新激活、面向标准化按需药物筛选的生物打印肿瘤模型的稳健胶原蛋白基生物墨水。生物墨水将以 I 型胶原蛋白为主配方，补充额外的 ECM 衍生聚合物（甲基丙烯酰化透明质酸，HAMA）以调节粘弹性并增强仿生性，同时添加不同浓度的 D-(+)-松三糖水合物作为冷冻保护剂，生成针对冷冻保存优化的墨水。我们将系统研究关键打印参数（尤其是分配时间与挤出压力）对挤出液滴特性的影响，通过打印后体积量化进行评估。将在保持低于 -5℃ 的冷冻板上打印胶原蛋白基墨水，分析液滴直径与线宽，评估冷冻辅助图案保真度。此外，将使用可冻存墨水对一系列复杂 3D 构建体进行计算建模与打印，评估制造后长期形状保持。所得载肿瘤细胞的三维冷冻打印模块将储存最多 14 天，随后受控解冻与复苏，系统表征封装细胞的活力与功能表现。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于液滴微流控高通量制备空间有序微肿瘤用于抗癌药物筛选","titleEn":"High-throughput Fabrication of Spatially Organized Microtumors via Droplet Microfluidics for Antican","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:36:52","descriptionText":"\"High-throughput Fabrication of Spatially Organized Microtumors via Droplet Microfluidics for Anticancer Drug Screening\"\nHepatic carcinoma, a prevalent and therapeutically recalcitrant malignancy, has high incidence rates and causes substantial treatment-related financial burdens but its current treatment strategies remain suboptimal. The creation of robust preclinical platforms for pathophysiological investigation and for anticancer drug evaluation becomes urgent in translational hepatology. Most early-stage drug screening still relies on 2D monolayer cultures, which lack the complex 3D architecture, extracellular matrix (ECM) composition, and microenvironmental gradients that characterize in vivo tumors. While hydrogel-based systems have been employed to recapitulate key cell-cell and cell-matrix interactions, many current studies rely on short exposure time or supraphysiological drug concentrations. Therefore, there is a critical need for drug-screening platforms that are compatible with high-throughput workflows and simultaneously reproduce essential features of the tumor microenvironment. Microfluidic technologies can offer excellent strategies to address the limitations by providing precise control over fluid dynamics and the local microenvironment, thereby facilitating scalable spheres production. In particular, droplet microfluidics enables the encapsulation of multiple cells within discrete microdroplets, yielding highly uniform spheroids and affording substantial flexibility to modulate microenvironmental conditions to more closely resemble in vivo tumor biology. In this project, a microfluidic platform for the precise, high-throughput fabrication of collagen-based microgels will be developed. Type I collagen is the principal structural protein of mammalian ECM and plays a central role in tumor progression, invasion, and stromal remodeling. By encapsulating or organizing cells within collagen microspheres, spatially organized microtumors embedded in a biomimetic extracellular matrix can be generated. The size of collagen microspheres will be tuned by systematically adjusting the aqueous-to-oil flow-rate ratio in a flow-focusing microfluidic device. The microstructure and properties of collagen microspheres at different collagen concentrations will be characterized. Moreover, collagen microspheres will be integrated into GelMA-HA inks to improve rheological behaviors, including shear-thinning, yield behavior, and recovery after shear. A square lattice pattern will be extrusion-printed to assess the printability of collagen microsphere incorporated GelMA-HA inks by comparing printed filament dimensions to the designed grid features. This new approach will offer a versatile platform for tissue modeling and drug testing.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note (if any, e.g. required/preferred academic background or skills of candidates, or incentives for attracting students):\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"通过液滴微流控高通量制造空间组织化微肿瘤用于抗癌药物筛选\"\n肝癌是一种常见且治疗棘手的恶性肿瘤，发病率高并造成沉重的治疗相关经济负担，但当前治疗策略仍不理想。建立稳健的临床前平台用于病理生理研究及抗癌药物评估，在转化肝病学中十分迫切。多数早期药物筛选仍依赖二维单层培养——其缺乏体内肿瘤特有的复杂三维结构、细胞外基质（ECM）组成与微环境梯度。尽管水凝胶系统已被用于复现关键的细胞间与细胞-基质相互作用，许多当前研究依赖短暴露时间或超生理药物浓度。因此，迫切需要与高通量工作流兼容、同时复现肿瘤微环境关键特征的药物筛选平台。微流控技术通过提供对流体动力学与局部微环境的精确控制，为解决上述局限提供了卓越策略，便于可扩展微球生产。尤其是液滴微流控可将多个细胞封装在离散微滴中，生成高度均匀的球体，并赋予调节微环境条件以更贴近体内肿瘤生物学的充足灵活性。本项目中，将开发用于精确高通量制造胶原蛋白基微凝胶的微流控平台。I 型胶原蛋白是哺乳动物 ECM 的主要结构蛋白，在肿瘤进展、侵袭与基质重塑中发挥核心作用。通过将细胞封装或组织在胶原蛋白微球中，可生成嵌入仿生细胞外基质的空间组织化微肿瘤。将通过系统调节流动聚焦微流控器件中的水油流速比来调控胶原蛋白微球尺寸；将表征不同胶原蛋白浓度下微球的结构与性质。此外，将胶原蛋白微球集成到 GelMA-HA 墨水中，改善剪切变稀、屈服行为与剪切后恢复等流变行为。将挤出打印方形晶格图案，通过比较打印丝线尺寸与设计网格特征评估含胶原蛋白微球的 GelMA-HA 墨水的可打印性。这一新方法将为组织建模与药物测试提供通用平台。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于骨组织工程的拓扑优化支架的设计、仿真、制造与测试","titleEn":"Design, Simulation, Fabrication, and Testing of Topology-optimized Scaffolds for Bone Tissue Enginee","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:37:17","descriptionText":"\"Design, Simulation, Fabrication, and Testing of Topology-optimized Scaffolds for Bone Tissue Engineering\"\nTraditional treatments for critical-size bone defects, such as autografts and allografts, present many clinical difficulties, including donor-site morbidity, limited availability, immune rejection, and the risk of disease transmission. Bone tissue engineering (BTE), the alternative approach, requires biofunctional porous scaffolds capable of restoring, maintaining, or improving the functionality of injured bone. A good BTE scaffold should meet multiple functional criteria: mechanical stability to withstand physiological loads, sufficient porosity to facilitate vascularization and cell migration, and good biocompatibility to promote osseointegration. Conventional BTE scaffold designs based on computer-aided design (CAD) and triply periodic minimal surfaces (TPMS) may not achieve an optimal balance between mechanical strength and porosity. Topology optimization (TO) offers a robust solution to this limitation. TO is a computational approach that optimally distributes material within a designated domain subject to specific performance constraints. Applied to BTE, it enables generation of patient- or region-specific scaffold structures that maximize mechanical performance while retaining a controlled porosity. This project will establish an integrated workflow for the design, simulation, fabrication, and mechanical validation of BTE scaffolds optimized via a hybrid TO approach. The initial design will feature a simplified load-bearing bone structure based on Gyroid TPMS (TPMS G) structure. TO will be executed using ANSYS Workbench to optimize material distribution within this domain. Boundary conditions will simulate biomechanical forces characteristic of femoral head applications. Following optimization, the resulting topological variations will be characterized. These geometric features will then be mapped onto the baseline TPMS G model via targeted manual modifications, yielding modified, feature-optimized TPMS G models. These optimized scaffold models will undergo finite element analysis (FEA) in ANSYS Workbench. The simulations will replicate uniaxial compression. Key mechanical metrics will include compressive modulus, von Mises stress distribution, and strain energy density. Computational fluid dynamics (CFD) simulations will also be performed using the Fluid Flow (CFX) module under physiological flow conditions to examine how the structural modifications affect the behavior of biofluid in scaffolds. For validation, high-fidelity fabrication of scaffold designs will be conducted via fused deposition modeling (FDM) 3D printing using polylactic acid (PLA) filaments as the structural material and water-soluble polyvinyl alcohol (PVA) for temporary supports. Finally, the 3D printed BTE scaffolds will undergo compression testing to evaluate their mechanical performance. \nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note (if any, e.g. required/preferred academic background or skills of candidates, or incentives for attracting students):\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"“用于骨组织工程的拓扑优化支架的设计、仿真、制造与测试”\n针对临界尺寸骨缺损的传统治疗方法，如自体骨移植与同种异体骨移植，存在诸多临床难题，包括供区并发症、来源有限、免疫排斥以及疾病传播风险。作为替代方案，骨组织工程（BTE）需要能够恢复、维持或改善受损骨功能的生物功能性多孔支架。一个良好的 BTE 支架应满足多项功能标准：能够承受生理载荷的机械稳定性、足以促进血管化与细胞迁移的孔隙率，以及促进骨整合的良好生物相容性。基于计算机辅助设计（CAD）与三重周期极小曲面（TPMS）的传统 BTE 支架设计，可能无法在机械强度与孔隙率之间实现最优平衡。拓扑优化（TO）为这一局限提供了稳健的解决方案。TO 是一种计算方法，可在特定性能约束下于指定设计域内对材料进行最优分布。应用于 BTE 时，它能够生成患者特异性或部位特异性的支架结构，在保持可控孔隙率的同时最大化机械性能。本项目将建立一个完整的工作流程，用于通过混合 TO 方法优化得到的 BTE 支架的设计、仿真、制造与力学验证。初始设计将采用基于 Gyroid TPMS（TPMS G）结构的简化承力骨结构。将使用 ANSYS Workbench 执行 TO，以优化该设计域内的材料分布。边界条件将模拟股骨头应用中的典型生物力学载荷。优化完成后，将对产生的拓扑变化进行表征。随后，这些几何特征将通过有针对性的手工修改映射到基准 TPMS G 模型上，生成经过修改的、特征优化的 TPMS G 模型。这些优化后的支架模型将在 ANSYS Workbench 中进行有限元分析（FEA）。仿真将复现单轴压缩工况。关键力学指标将包括压缩模量、von Mises 应力分布与应变能密度。还将使用 Fluid Flow（CFX）模块在生理流动条件下进行计算流体动力学（CFD）仿真，以考察结构修改如何影响支架内生物流体的行为。为进行验证，将通过熔融沉积成型（FDM）3D 打印技术，以聚乳酸（PLA）线材作为结构材料、以水溶性聚乙烯醇（PVA）作为临时支撑，对支架设计进行高保真制造。最后，3D 打印的 BTE 支架将接受压缩测试以评估其力学性能。\n先修与同修要求：\n已在本科或硕士阶段修读过/完成材料类课程。\n特别说明（如有，例如候选人所要求/优先的学术背景或技能，或吸引学生的激励措施）：\n项目中期评估：2027年2月4日（周四）之前\n论文提交至机械工程系（ME Dept.）：2027年5月1日之前","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"拓扑优化的骨组织工程支架的算法化设计、3D 打印与力学测试","titleEn":"Algorithmic Design, 3D Printing, and Mechanical Testing of Topology-optimized Bone Tissue Engineerin","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:37:30","descriptionText":"\"Algorithmic Design, 3D Printing, and Mechanical Testing of Topology-optimized Bone Tissue Engineering Scaffolds\"\nThe microstructure of bone tissue engineering (BTE) scaffolds influences bone regeneration and tissue growth. While conventional treatments, such as autografts and allografts, suffer from availability limitation and site morbidity, BTE scaffolds provide a highly adaptable alternative for treating bone defects. To mimic the complex microenvironment of human bone, A clinically usable BTE scaffold should satisfy many physical criteria, and it should maintain sufficient mechanical stiffness under loading while ensuring adequate porosity to allow nutrient diffusion and cell migration. However, standard structures, including the basic computer-aided design (CAD) shapes or uniform cellular lattices, may not achieve a balance between these mechanical and biological demands. This project addresses these problems by implementing topology optimization (TO) as an algorithm-driven generative design strategy. Instead of relying on fixed geometrical shapes, TO treats scaffold structure as a mathematical distribution problem, iteratively computing the optimal placement of material and void space under specific performance targets. By developing a custom TO algorithm tailored for load-bearing scenarios, it is possible to compute structural porous patterns that maximize mechanical performance within a tightly controlled target porosity. This project will develop an engineering workflow centered on the algorithmic design, 3D printing, and mechanical testing of topology optimized bone scaffolds. It will develop custom TO codes (e.g., utilizing solid isotropic material with penalty (SIMP) or evolutionary structural optimization (ESO) framework) tailored for cellular and porous configurations using Python or MATLAB. The computational design domain will represent a simplified load-bearing bone block. The algorithm will be programmed to mathematically optimize material distribution to achieve high structural stiffness under simulated femoral head biomechanical forces, maintaining user-defined constraints such as a target porosity and controlled pore interconnectivity. For assessing the designs, high-fidelity fabrication of the optimal algorithmically generated scaffold designs will be conducted via fused deposition modeling (FDM) 3D printing using polylactic acid (PLA) filaments as the structural material and water-soluble polyvinyl alcohol (PVA) for supports. The 3D printed BTE scaffolds will undergo compression testing to directly evaluate their mechanical performance. The comprehensive analysis of strengths and weaknesses of the BTE scaffold designs will help initiate algorithmic improvement strategies.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note (if any, e.g. required/preferred academic background or skills of candidates, or incentives for attracting students):\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"“拓扑优化骨组织工程支架的算法化设计、3D 打印与力学测试”\n骨组织工程（BTE）支架的微观结构影响着骨再生与组织生长。传统治疗方法（如自体骨移植与同种异体骨移植）存在来源受限与供区并发症问题，而 BTE 支架为治疗骨缺损提供了一种高度可适配的替代方案。为模拟人体骨骼复杂的微环境，临床可用的 BTE 支架应满足诸多物理标准：既要在载荷下保持足够的机械刚度，又要确保充足的孔隙率以允许营养扩散与细胞迁移。然而，标准结构（包括基本的计算机辅助设计（CAD）形状或均匀的胞元晶格）可能无法在这些机械与生物需求之间取得平衡。本项目通过将拓扑优化（TO）作为一种算法驱动的生成式设计策略来解决这些问题。TO 不依赖固定的几何形状，而是将支架结构视为一个数学分布问题，在特定性能目标下迭代计算材料与空隙空间的最优布置。通过开发针对承力场景定制的 TO 算法，可以在严格受控的目标孔隙率内计算出最大化机械性能的结构性多孔形态。本项目将开发一个以拓扑优化骨支架的算法化设计、3D 打印与力学测试为核心的工程工作流程。它将使用 Python 或 MATLAB 开发针对胞元与多孔构型定制的自定义 TO 代码（例如采用带惩罚的固体各向同性材料（SIMP）或进化结构优化（ESO）框架）。计算设计域将代表一个简化的承力骨块。算法将被编程为在模拟股骨头生物力学载荷下对材料分布进行数学优化以实现高结构刚度，同时维持用户定义的约束，如目标孔隙率与可控的孔连通性。为评估这些设计，将通过熔融沉积成型（FDM）3D 打印技术，以聚乳酸（PLA）线材作为结构材料、以水溶性聚乙烯醇（PVA）作为支撑，对算法生成的最优支架设计进行高保真制造。3D 打印的 BTE 支架将接受压缩测试，以直接评估其力学性能。对 BTE 支架设计优缺点的全面分析将有助于启动算法改进策略。\n先修与同修要求：\n已在本科或硕士阶段修读过/完成材料类课程。\n特别说明（如有，例如候选人所要求/优先的学术背景或技能，或吸引学生的激励措施）：\n项目中期评估：2027年2月4日（周四）之前\n论文提交至机械工程系（ME Dept.）：2027年5月1日之前","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于血管修复的仿生管状结构的 3D 生物打印","titleEn":"3D Bioprinting of Biomimicking Tubular Constructs for Vascular Repair","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:37:43","descriptionText":"\"3D Bioprinting of Biomimicking Tubular Constructs for Vascular Repair\"\nCardiovascular diseases remain a leading cause for human mortality worldwide, often necessitating the repair of damaged blood vessels. Current clinical interventions, e.g., autologous or synthetic polymeric substitutes, have significant limitations. 3D bioprinting emerges as a transformative approach to address the challenges by enabling the fabrication of biomimicking tubular cell-scaffold constructs with structural complexity. This advanced biofibrication platform enables the precise deposition of living cells and biomaterials, known as bioinks, in a layer-by-layer manner to recapitulate complex and functional vascular structures akin to native human tissues. Despite significant advances, existing combinations of in vitro 3D models, bioinks, and bioprinting techniques often fall short in engineering the intricate microarchitecture of native blood vessels. A key area of innovation involves creation of advanced bioinks, which allows for the precise tuning of mechanical properties and the generation of high-resolution, complex-shaped vascular networks essential for cell survival and functional maturation. The development of bioinks for small-diameter vascular bioprinting should focus on several crucial characteristics, e.g., optimal rheology and printability, biomimetic mechanical properties, high biocompatibility, and tunable degradation rates. In this project, for extrusion-based 3D bioprinting, smart inks/bioinks will be synthesized using actylate-modified alginate, κ-carrageena, and chitosan, as well as endothelial cells, based on physical and chemical crosslinking mechanisms. The ingratiation of sodium alginate and κ-carrageena forms a powerful basis, combining fast ionic corsslinking, e.g., Ca2+, with favorable viscoelastic behaviors. Di-(N-succinimidyl) glutarate (DSG) is an amine-targeted bifunctional crosslinker to covalently link to amines (-NH2) of polyacrylamide/polysaccharide. Chitosan is highly hydrophilic, enabling it to form gels due to the presence of amide (-CONH) in side chains. Furthermore, the introduction of photocrosslink molecules, e.g., acrylate, can improve the structural stability of 3D bioprinted constructs via UV or visible light illumination. The new inks will be printed into different types of constructs, e.g., tube-shaped ones, via an extrusion-based 3D bioprinter, to support cell survival and vascular regeneration. The characteristics of inks and 3D printed constructs, including rheology, molecular structure-activity relationship, biocompatibility, biodegradation, scaffold morphology, and mechanical properties, will be investigated using rheometer, FTIR, SEM, mechanical testing machine, etc. In vitro flow modeling integrated with computational fluid dynamics (CFD) analysis may also be conducted to study blood flow in 3D printed small-diameter blood vessels.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note (if any, e.g. required/preferred academic background or skills of candidates, or incentives for attracting students):\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"用于血管修复的仿生管状构建体 3D 生物打印\"\n心血管疾病仍是全球人类死亡的主要原因之一，常常需要修复受损血管。当前的临床干预手段，如自体或合成聚合物替代物，存在显著局限。3D 生物打印通过制造具有结构复杂性的仿生管状细胞-支架构建体，成为应对这些挑战的变革性方法。这一先进生物制造平台可逐层精确沉积活细胞与生物材料（即生物墨水），以复现类似天然人体组织的复杂功能性血管结构。尽管取得重大进展，现有体外 3D 模型、生物墨水与生物打印技术的组合在构建天然血管复杂微结构方面仍常显不足。一个关键创新领域是先进生物墨水的开发——其允许精确调谐力学性能并生成对细胞存活与功能成熟至关重要的高分辨率、复杂形状血管网络。面向小直径血管生物打印的生物墨水开发应聚焦若干关键特性，如最优流变性与可打印性、仿生力学性能、高生物相容性与可调降解速率。本项目针对挤出式 3D 生物打印，将基于物理与化学交联机制，使用丙烯酸酯改性海藻酸盐、κ-卡拉胶、壳聚糖以及内皮细胞合成智能墨水/生物墨水。海藻酸钠与 κ-卡拉胶的结合形成强大基础，将快速离子交联（如 Ca2+）与良好的粘弹性行为相结合。Di-(N-琥珀酰亚胺)戊二酸酯（DSG）是胺靶向双功能交联剂，可与聚丙烯酰胺/多糖的氨基（-NH2）共价连接。壳聚糖高度亲水，侧链含酰胺（-CONH），可形成凝胶。此外，引入丙烯酸酯等光交联分子，可通过紫外或可见光照提升 3D 生物打印构建体的结构稳定性。新墨水将通过挤出式 3D 生物打印机打印成管状等不同类型构建体，支持细胞存活与血管再生。将使用流变仪、FTIR、SEM、力学试验机等研究墨水与 3D 打印构建体的特性，包括流变学、分子构效关系、生物相容性、生物降解、支架形貌与力学性能。还可能开展体外流动建模并结合计算流体力学（CFD）分析，研究 3D 打印小直径血管中的血流。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-6","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于食管修复的管状结构的 3D 打印/生物打印","titleEn":"3D Printing/Bioprinting of Tubular Constructs for Esophageal Repair","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:37:58","descriptionText":"\"3D Printing/Bioprinting of Tubular Constructs for Esophageal Repair\"\n Esophageal diseases, e.g., esophageal cancer, physical trauma, and congenital abnormalities, threaten human health and lives. Autologous esophageal substitutes utilized in esophageal reconstruction, e.g., segments of gastrointestinal tract and skin, face various issues, e.g., unmatched size, immune rejection, and postoperative complications. The anatomy of the esophagus is intrinsically complicated, leading to difficulties in recapitulating crucial structures and biological functions of the esophagus via conventional fabrication technologies (solvent casting, freeze-drying, electrospinning, etc.). 3D bioprinting provides a powerful platform for fabricating in vitro tissues and organs with biomimetic geometries and functions by integrating biomaterials, living cells, and biochemicals. Successes in 3D bioprinting of tubular esophageal tissue layers significantly rely on inks/bioinks, which stabilize the structures during and after printing by the ink/bioink matrix, e.g., hydrogel. The development of inks/bioinks with good biocompatibility, printability, etc., underpins the progress in 3D printing/bioprinting of esophageal substitutes. In this project, tubular constructs with layered biomimetic esophageal structure will be designed and 3D printed/bioprinted using new inks/bioinks, including Pluronic F127 (PF)/fibroin-based ink, and poly (vinyl alcohol) (PVA)-based ink, through extrusion-based 3D printing/bioprinting. PF and fibroin will be paired to prepare thermosensitive inks for printing the innermost layer. PF is a liquid at low temperature but gels instantly at physiological body temperature. The printed PF/fibroin-printed layer will provide essential structural support for cell survival and proliferation (e.g., epithelial cells) by an irreversible sol-gel transition accelerated by chemical linker, e.g., 2,3,4,5-tetrahydroxyhexanedial (THA), and temperature control. PVA-based inks will be used to print outer layers of tubular constructs, which mimics the muscle layers of esophagus. To develop strong, tough, stretchable, and antigen-resistant PVA hydrogels, a sequential freeze-thawing and salting-out approach will be employed. Key factors, including temperature, solvent and ions, will be investigated. All layers of biomimetic esophageal structure will be patterned on a rotating mandrel in the 3D biprinter with multiple printheads for loading different inks. The alignment of printed PVA filaments should be similar to that of the muscle fibers in esophageal longitudinal/circular muscle layers. Key characteristics and properties of inks and printed tubular constructs, e.g., rheology, biocompatibility, thermosensitive behaviors, and mechanical properties, as well as morphology and microstructures, will be studied using rheometer, FTIR, SEM, mechanical testing, etc.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note:\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"用于食管修复的管状构建体 3D 打印/生物打印\"\n食管疾病，如食管癌、物理创伤与先天性异常，威胁人类健康与生命。食管重建中使用的自体食管替代物（如胃肠道节段与皮肤）面临尺寸不匹配、免疫排斥与术后并发症等各种问题。食管解剖结构本身复杂，导致难以通过传统制造技术（溶剂浇铸、冷冻干燥、静电纺丝等）复现食管的关键结构与生物学功能。3D 生物打印通过集成生物材料、活细胞与生化物质，为制造具有仿生几何与功能的体外组织器官提供了强大平台。3D 生物打印管状食管组织层的成功在很大程度上取决于墨水/生物墨水——它们通过墨水/生物墨水基质（如水凝胶）在打印期间与打印后稳定结构。开发具有良好生物相容性、可打印性等的墨水/生物墨水，是推进食管替代物 3D 打印/生物打印进步的基础。本项目中，将设计具有分层仿生食管结构的管状构建体，并使用新墨水/生物墨水（包括 Pluronic F127 (PF)/丝素蛋白基墨水与聚乙烯醇（PVA）基墨水）通过挤出式 3D 打印/生物打印制造。PF 与丝素蛋白将配对制备热敏墨水用于打印最内层。PF 在低温下为液体，但在生理体温下立即凝胶化。打印的 PF/丝素蛋白层将通过化学交联剂（如 2,3,4,5-四羟基己二醛，THA）加速的不可逆溶胶-凝胶转变以及温度控制，为细胞（如表皮细胞）存活与增殖提供必要的结构支撑。PVA 基墨水将用于打印管状构建体的外层，模拟食管肌层。为开发强韧、抗抗原的 PVA 水凝胶，将采用顺序冻融与盐析方法，研究温度、溶剂与离子等关键因素。仿生食管结构的所有层将在 3D 生物打印机的旋转芯轴上图案化，打印机配备多个打印头以装载不同墨水。打印的 PVA 丝线排列应与食管纵/环肌层中肌纤维的排列相似。将使用流变仪、FTIR、SEM、力学测试等研究墨水与打印管状构建体的关键特性与性能，包括流变学、生物相容性、热敏行为与力学性能，以及形貌与微观结构。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-7","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于心脏修复的组织粘合剂的 3D 打印","titleEn":"3D Printing of Tissue Adhesives for Heart Repair","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:38:12","descriptionText":"\"3D Printing of Tissue Adhesives for Heart Repair\"\nGlobally, over 300 million surgical procedures are performed each year. Adhesive materials/biomaterials are required for wound repair, soft and hard tissue restoration, advanced drug delivery, and human-computer wearable electronics. Some adhesive material-derived wound dressings or patches are used to cover superficial tissue wounds such as skin but show limited efficacy for heart repair due to the heart presents a vastly more hostile and dynamic environment. Although numerous investigations have been conducted in postoperative wound management using adhesives, many challenges still exist, including robust interface adhesion, adequate durability in situ, matched biomechanics etc.. Adhesive patches enable strong and durable interfacial adhesion (e.g., supermolecular, electrostatic, covalent, and topological adhesion), while interacting with substrate to effectively dissipate energy when subjected to external forces. However, many adhesives synthesized by polymers (PEG, gelatine, fibrin, etc.) have exhibited low potential for heart repair, resulting from their poor adhesion in aqueous environment, which can cause leakage of blood inside heart. Unlike polymer hydrogel adhesives, small-molecule adhesives with well-permeated ability usually possess much stronger adhesive forces. In this study, an injectable and self-healing hydrogel adhesive will be synthesized by physiochemically crosslinking mechanism among different molecular-weight poly (vinyl alcohol) (PVA), cyclodextrin-derived microspheres (CDMs), Di-(N-succinimidyl) glutarate (DSG) linker, and polyacrylamide (PAM). DSG is an amine-reactive homobifunctional crosslinker to covalently link to protein by ester-exchanging reaction between N-hydeoxysuccinimide (NHS) and amino (-NH2) of proteins. CD contains 21 hydroxyl groups which can efficiently interact with the surface and yield robust adhesion via hydrogen bonding and covalent bonding. PAM is highly hydrophilic, enabling it to form gel when hydrated due to the presence of the amide (-CONH) in side chains. The new adhesive will be printed into desired shape via an extrusion-based 3D printer to meet needs for specialized wound repair. Physiochemical properties of adhesives, including molecular structure-activity relationship, adhesion forces, setting time and thermal effect, biodepredation, biocomparability, etc., will be investigated using FTIR, NMR, DSC, SEM, mechanical testing, etc. To assess the efficacy of wound closure for new adhesives, wound closure and hemostatic models for heart and vein injury may be established. \nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note (if any, e.g. required/preferred academic background or skills of candidates, or incentives for attracting students):\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"用于心脏修复的组织粘合剂 3D 打印\"\n全球每年开展超过 3 亿例外科手术。伤口修复、软组织与硬组织重建、先进药物递送以及人机可穿戴电子器件均需要粘合材料/生物材料。部分源自粘合材料的伤口敷料或贴片可用于覆盖皮肤等浅表组织伤口，但因心脏环境远比皮肤恶劣且高度动态，其对心脏修复的效果有限。尽管已围绕使用粘合剂的术后伤口管理开展大量研究，仍存在许多挑战，包括牢固的界面粘附、原位充分耐久性、匹配的生物力学等。粘合贴片可实现牢固耐久的界面粘附（如超分子、静电、共价与拓扑粘附），同时与基底相互作用，在受到外力时有效耗散能量。然而，许多由聚合物（PEG、明胶、纤维蛋白等）合成的粘合剂在心脏修复中表现有限，原因是其在水环境中的粘附性差，可能导致心脏内部血液渗漏。与聚合物水凝胶粘合剂不同，具有良好渗透能力的小分子粘合剂通常具有强得多的粘附力。本研究将利用不同分子量聚乙烯醇（PVA）、环糊精衍生物微球（CDM）、Di-(N-琥珀酰亚胺)戊二酸酯（DSG）交联剂与聚丙烯酰胺（PAM）之间的物理化学交联机制，合成一种可注射、自愈合的水凝胶粘合剂。DSG 是胺反应性同双功能交联剂，通过 N-羟基琥珀酰亚胺（NHS）与蛋白质氨基（-NH2）之间的酯交换反应与蛋白质共价连接。CD 含 21 个羟基，可通过氢键与共价键高效地与表面相互作用并产生牢固粘附。PAM 高度亲水，侧链含有酰胺（-CONH）基团，水合时可形成凝胶。新型粘合剂将通过挤出式 3D 打印机打印成所需形状，以满足专门伤口修复的需求。将使用 FTIR、NMR、DSC、SEM、力学测试等手段研究粘合剂的物理化学性质，包括分子构效关系、粘附力、凝固时间与热效应、生物降解性、生物相容性等。为评估新型粘合剂封闭伤口的效果，可建立心脏与血管损伤的伤口闭合与止血模型。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-8","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"开发用于肺仿生组织工程的可 3D 打印聚丙烯酰胺/丝素蛋白复合水凝胶","titleEn":"Developing 3D Printable Polyacrylamide/Silk Fibroin Composite Hydrogels for Lung-Mimetic Tissue Engi","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:38:26","descriptionText":"\"Developing 3D Printable Polyacrylamide/Silk Fibroin Composite Hydrogels for Lung-Mimetic Tissue Engineering Scaffolds\"\nSevere lung diseases, including pulmonary fibrosis, can involve irreversible loss or dysfunction of alveolar tissue. Lung transplantation is still the only definitive treatment for end-stage patients but donor availability, immune rejection and long-term graft complications restrict its wide use. Over the years, tissue-engineered constructs are investigated as potential platforms for lung regeneration. For lung-related applications, tissue engineering (TE) scaffolds should be soft, highly hydrated and porous enough to support gas, nutrient and waste transport, while maintaining sufficient stability during extrusion-based 3D printing. This balance is difficult to achieve with existing hydrogel bioinks. Softer formulations may better approximate lung tissue mechanics but can lose shape after extrusion, whereas highly crosslinked formulations often show improved fidelity but at the cost of increased stiffness. This project aims to develop a polyacrylamide/silk fibroin (PAAm/SF) composite hydrogel ink, prepared from the acrylamide (AAm) monomer, for extrusion-based 3D printing of lung-mimetic TE scaffolds. PAAm hydrogels allow matrix stiffness tuning over a broad range; but it is biologically inert and unpolymerized AAm is toxic. SF is a natural protein biomaterial with reported biocompatibility and beta-sheet-mediated physical crosslinking. Incorporating SF into an AAm-derived PAAm network may help retain mechanical tunability while adding a protein-based component. The central task is to identify ink formulation and processing conditions that support shear-thinning flow, post-printing shape retention and post-washing cytocompatibility. The ink formulation screening will combine regenerated SF solution with AAm across an initial concentration window. Crosslinking components will include N, N'-methylenebisacrylamide and PEGDA. Initial cast-gel tests will compare redox polymerisation with APS/TEMED to identify ink formulations that are reproducible in gelation and handling. Selected inks will be printed into grid, honeycomb and simplified airway-like structures. Printing pressure, nozzle diameter, speed, and layer height will serve as optimization variables, with filament continuity, filament diameter, pore area, and printability ratio as readouts. Rheological analysis will investigate ink viscosity, shear-thinning behaviour and thixotropy. FTIR spectroscopy will examine changes in the secondary structure of SF and in polymer-related spectral features. Swelling, water content, biodegradation and compressive testing will evaluate scaffold stability and mechanical properties. These data will support further optimization of lung-mimetic hydrogel scaffolds and enhance cell-scaffold interactions.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note (if any, e.g. required/preferred academic background or skills of candidates, or incentives for attracting students):\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"开发用于肺模拟组织工程支架的可 3D 打印聚丙烯酰胺/丝素蛋白复合水凝胶\"\n肺纤维化等严重肺部疾病可涉及肺泡组织的不可逆缺失或功能障碍。肺移植仍是终末期患者唯一根治性治疗手段，但供体可获得性、免疫排斥与长期移植物并发症限制了其广泛应用。多年来，组织工程构建体被研究为肺再生的潜在平台。对于肺部相关应用，组织工程（TE）支架应柔软、高度水合且足够多孔以支持气体、营养与废物输运，同时在挤出式 3D 打印过程中保持足够稳定性。现有水凝胶生物墨水难以实现这一平衡：较软配方可能更贴近肺组织力学，但挤出后易失形；而高交联配方常具更好保真度，却以刚度增加为代价。本项目旨在开发一种以丙烯酰胺（AAm）单体制备的聚丙烯酰胺/丝素蛋白（PAAm/SF）复合水凝胶墨水，用于肺模拟 TE 支架的挤出式 3D 打印。PAAm 水凝胶允许在大范围内调节基质刚度，但生物惰性，且未聚合 AAm 有毒。SF 是一种天然蛋白质生物材料，具有公认的生物相容性与 β-折叠介导的物理交联。将 SF 掺入源自 AAm 的 PAAm 网络中，可在保留力学可调性的同时增加蛋白质基组分。核心任务是确定支持剪切变稀流动、打印后形状保持与清洗后细胞相容性的墨水配方与加工条件。墨水配方筛选将在初始浓度窗口内将再生 SF 溶液与 AAm 结合；交联组分将包括 N,N'-亚甲基双丙烯酰胺与 PEGDA。初始浇铸凝胶测试将比较 APS/TEMED 氧化还原聚合，以识别在凝胶化与操作性方面可复现的墨水配方。选定墨水将打印成网格、蜂窝与简化的气道样结构；打印压力、喷嘴直径、速度与层高作为优化变量，丝线连续性、丝线直径、孔隙面积与可打印性比作为读指标。流变分析将研究墨水粘度、剪切变稀行为与触变性；FTIR 光谱将检查 SF 二级结构变化与聚合物相关光谱特征；溶胀、含水量、生物降解与压缩测试将评估支架稳定性与力学性能。这些数据将支持肺模拟水凝胶支架的进一步优化，并增强细胞-支架相互作用。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-9","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"微流控制备丝素蛋白微凝胶用于肺仿生颗粒墨水/生物墨水的 3D 打印","titleEn":"Microfluidic Fabrication of Silk Fibroin Microgels for Lung-Mimetic Granular Inks/Bioinks for 3D Pri","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:38:40","descriptionText":"\"Microfluidic Fabrication of Silk Fibroin Microgels for Lung-Mimetic Granular Inks/Bioinks for 3D Printing/Bioprinting\"\nLung-mimetic tissue engineering (TE) scaffolds obtained by extrusion-based 3D printing should be soft, hydrated and compatible with lung-relevant cells while possessing sufficient strength and stiffness to maintain shape after 3D printing. Silk fibroin (SF) is a TE material and its mechanical properties are tunable through β-sheet formation. However, dilute SF solutions have low viscosity and poor shape retention during extrusion 3D printing. Increasing the SF concentration or adding large amounts of alginate and cellulose nanofibres can improve printability; but these measures can increase nozzle clogging or shift the material away from an SF-dominant ink matrix. An SF-based strategy for improving the extrusion behaviour is required for ink/bioink development. This project aims to prepare SF microgels through droplet microfluidics and evaluate them as granular building blocks for extrusion-printable inks/bioinks. At high volume fractions, soft microgels can form jammed pastes that shear-thin during extrusion and recover structure after deposition. This approach may improve shape fidelity of 3D printed structures without relying primarily on bulk polymer concentration or high levels of non-SF rheological additives. The studies will use regenerated SF as the main material. A flow-focusing or T-junction poly(dimethylsiloxane) microfluidic chip can generate water-in-oil droplets containing SF solution. Droplet size will be adjusted by the aqueous and oil-phase flow rates, surfactant concentration, and channel geometry. After oil and surfactant removal, the collected microgels will be concentrated into jammed pastes. Optical microscopy and ImageJ analysis will quantify particle size distribution and circularity. Ink formation will involve blending microgel pastes with a small amount of SF solution and, if needed, low concentrations of alginate or cellulose nanofibres. Inks will subsequently be printed as lines, grids, multilayers and simplified airway-like patterns. Ink printability metrics include filament continuity, filament diameter, pore area accuracy, layer stability and post-printing collapse. Rheological analysis will investigate ink viscosity, shear-thinning behaviour and thixotropy. Swelling, biodegradation and compressive testing will evaluate scaffold stability and mechanical properties. SEM or confocal microscopy may evaluate microgel packing. The work will therefore examine the relationship between droplet generation, microgel stabilization, paste rheology, ink printability, and characteristics of 3D printed scaffolds.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note (if any, e.g. required/preferred academic background or skills of candidates, or incentives for attracting students):\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"用于肺模拟颗粒墨水/生物墨水的丝素蛋白微凝胶的微流控制备，支持 3D 打印/生物打印\"\n通过挤出式 3D 打印获得的肺模拟组织工程（TE）支架应柔软、水合、与肺部相关细胞相容，同时具备足够强度与刚度以在 3D 打印后保持形状。丝素蛋白（SF）是一种 TE 材料，其力学性能可通过 β-折叠形成进行调节。然而，稀 SF 溶液粘度低，挤出 3D 打印时形状保持差。提高 SF 浓度或添加大量海藻酸盐与纤维素纳米纤维可改善可打印性，但这些措施可能增加喷嘴堵塞或将材料从 SF 主导的墨水体系偏移。开发基于 SF 的策略以改善挤出行为，对墨水/生物墨水开发十分必要。本项目旨在通过液滴微流控制备 SF 微凝胶，并评估其作为挤出可打印墨水/生物墨水颗粒构建模块的适用性。在高体积分数下，软微凝胶可形成\"堵塞\"膏体，挤出时剪切变稀、沉积后结构恢复。该方法可在不主要依赖体相聚合物浓度或大量非 SF 流变添加剂的情况下，改善 3D 打印结构的形状保真度。研究将以再生 SF 为主要材料。可采用流动聚焦或 T 形结聚二甲基硅氧烷（PDMS）微流控芯片生成含 SF 溶液的水包油液滴；液滴尺寸通过水相与油相流速、表面活性剂浓度与通道几何调节。去除油相与表面活性剂后，收集的微凝胶被浓缩为堵塞膏体。将使用光学显微镜与 ImageJ 分析量化粒径分布与圆度。墨水形成涉及将微凝胶膏体与少量 SF 溶液混合，并在需要时添加低浓度海藻酸盐或纤维素纳米纤维。随后将墨水打印成线条、网格、多层与简化的气道状图案。墨水可打印性指标包括丝线连续性、丝线直径、孔隙面积精度、层稳定性与打印后塌陷。流变分析将研究墨水粘度、剪切变稀行为与触变性。溶胀、生物降解与压缩测试将评估支架稳定性与力学性能；SEM 或共聚焦显微术可评估微凝胶堆积。因此，本工作将研究液滴生成、微凝胶稳定、膏体流变、墨水可打印性与 3D 打印支架特性之间的关系。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-10","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"机器学习辅助优化丝素蛋白基墨水用于 3D 打印肺仿生组织","titleEn":"Machine Learning-assisted Optimization of Silk Fibroin-based Inks for 3D Printing of Lung-Mimetic Ti","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:38:54","descriptionText":"\"Machine Learning-assisted Optimization of Silk Fibroin-based Inks for 3D Printing of Lung-Mimetic Tissue Engineering Scaffolds\"\n3D printed lung-mimetic tissue engineering (TE) scaffolds require 3D printable biomaterials that can support lung-relevant cells while approximating the soft and hydrated characteristics of pulmonary tissue. Silk fibroin (SF)-based hydrogels are attractive because they are soft, biocompatible, biodegradable and mechanically tunable. However, extrusion-based 3D printing of TE scaffolds is significantly influenced by ink formulation and printing parameters. Small changes in the concentration of SF or methacrylated SF (SFMA), alginate content, cellulose nanofibre content, photoinitiator concentration, or printing speed can affect nozzle clogging, filament spreading, pore accuracy, and cell viability. Conventional one-factor-at-a-time optimization of printing inks is slow and may miss interactions between ink formulation and printing parameters. This project aims to develop a machine learning-assisted workflow to prioritize candidate ink formulations for subsequent experimental validation. The computational component is central to the project and hence the student taking this project should have basic Python and data-analysis experience before starting the project. The workflow does not treat machine learning as a black box. Instead, it combines a structured experimental database with interpretable regression models that predict printability-related responses. The goal is to reduce trial-and-error screening and to identify variables most strongly associated with printability, structural recovery, and cell-compatible processing. Material variables for inks include SF or SFMA as the main biomaterial, with sodium alginate, cellulose nanofibres and LAP photoinitiator being included wherever relevant. Existing data from previous design-of-experiments screening will be first curated into a machine-readable spreadsheet. The database will record the degree of modification, polymer concentrations, nozzle size, printing pressure, printing speed, light exposure, and measured print outcomes. Additional experiments can then be selected using a limited factorial or response-surface design. Extrusion-based 3D printing will produce simple lines, grids, and multilayer structures. Quantitative responses will include printability ratio, filament diameter error, pore area deviation, layer fidelity, clogging time and post-printing collapse. Data analysis will be performed in Python. Supervisory guidance will focus on experimental design, ink formulation and interpretation of model outputs. Linear regression, random forest regression and Gaussian process regression will be compared using train-test splitting and cross-validation. Feature importance and partial dependence plots will be used to examine how ink formulation and printing variables relate to each response. Model-prioritized ink formulations will be tested experimentally through extrusion 3D printing and rheological measurements, including viscosity, shear-thinning and thixotropy. 3D printed scaffolds of selected in formulations will also be assessed via swelling tests, compression testing, and optical microscopy.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note (if any, e.g. required/preferred academic background or skills of candidates, or incentives for attracting students):\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"机器学习辅助优化用于肺模拟组织工程支架 3D 打印的丝素蛋白基墨水\"\n3D 打印肺模拟组织工程（TE）支架需要可 3D 打印的生物材料，其既支持肺部相关细胞，又贴近肺组织柔软水合的特性。丝素蛋白（SF）基水凝胶因其柔软、生物相容、可生物降解且力学可调而具吸引力。然而，TE 支架的挤出式 3D 打印受墨水配方与打印参数的显著影响。SF 或甲基丙烯酰化 SF（SFMA）浓度、海藻酸盐含量、纤维素纳米纤维含量、光引发剂浓度或打印速度的微小变化都可能影响喷嘴堵塞、丝线铺展、孔隙精度与细胞活力。传统单因素逐步优化打印墨水既缓慢，又可能遗漏墨水配方与打印参数之间的相互作用。本项目旨在开发机器学习辅助工作流，优先筛选候选墨水配方供后续实验验证。计算组件是项目核心，故承接本项目的学生应在开始前具备基础 Python 与数据分析经验。该工作流不把机器学习当作黑箱，而是将结构化实验数据库与可解释的回归模型相结合，预测与可打印性相关的响应。目标是减少试错筛选，识别与可打印性、结构恢复与细胞相容处理最密切相关的变量。墨水的材料变量包括作为主要生物材料的 SF 或 SFMA，并在相关处加入海藻酸钠、纤维素纳米纤维与 LAP 光引发剂。将先整理此前实验设计筛选的现有数据为机器可读电子表格。数据库将记录改性程度、聚合物浓度、喷嘴尺寸、打印压力、打印速度、光照与测量的打印结果。随后可用有限因子或响应面设计选择补充实验。挤出式 3D 打印将生成简单线条、网格与多层结构。定量响应将包括可打印性比、丝线直径误差、孔隙面积偏差、层保真度、堵塞时间与打印后塌陷。数据分析将在 Python 中完成。指导将聚焦实验设计、墨水配方与模型输出解读。将使用训练-测试划分与交叉验证比较线性回归、随机森林回归与高斯过程回归；使用特征重要性与偏依赖图研究墨水配方与打印变量如何与各响应相关。模型优选的墨水配方将通过挤出 3D 打印与流变测量（包括粘度、剪切变稀与触变性）进行实验测试。选定配方 3D 打印的支架还将通过溶胀测试、压缩测试与光学显微术评估。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-11","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"数字光处理（DLP）3D 打印制备水凝胶微针用于组织间液采样","titleEn":"Hydrogel Microneedles Fabricated by Digital Light Processing (DLP) 3D Printing for Interstitial Flui","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:36:23","descriptionText":"\"Hydrogel Microneedles Fabricated by Digital Light Processing (DLP) 3D Printing for Interstitial Fluid Sampling\"\nInterstitial fluid (ISF) is a body fluid existing in the space between tissue cells and contains a wide range of biomarkers associated with body’s physiological and pathological conditions. The concentrations of several biomarkers in ISF are closely related to those found in blood, making it a promising alternative biofluid for disease diagnosis, therapeutic monitoring, and personalized healthcare. Compared with conventional blood sampling, ISF collection can reduce pain and tissue damage. However, the skin barrier, particularly the stratum corneum, makes it difficult to collect sufficient volumes of ISF quickly and reliably. Microneedle technology provides a minimally invasive approach for accessing ISF beneath the skin. Among all types of microneedles (MNs), hydrogel MNs are particularly attractive for ISF sampling because they can absorb fluid through swelling after insertion into the skin. ISF can be recovered subsequently from swollen hydrogel MNs and then analyzed through integration with colorimetric, electrochemical, or other biosensing systems. Hydrogel MNs are commonly produced using mould-based fabrication techniques. Digital light processing (DLP) 3D printing provides a new way to fabricate hydrogel MNs precisely and efficiently. DLP enables rapid production of MN arrays with controllable geometries and high resolution. It also allows different MN dimensions and array arrangements to be conveniently designed and fabricated. This project focuses on the design and characterization of DLP 3D printed hydrogel MNs for ISF sampling. A photocrosslinkable composite hydrogel composed primarily of hyaluronic acid methacryloyl (HAMA) and gelatin methacryloyl (GelMA) will be used for MNs. HAMA provides high water absorption and swelling ability, while GelMA can contribute to the structural integrity and mechanical performance of 3D printed MNs. Different HAMA/GelMA ratios will be used to determine a suitable balance between printability, mechanical strength, and ISF absorption capacity. The morphology, dimensional accuracy, tip sharpness, and surface quality of 3D printed MNs will be examined using optical microscopy and SEM. Mechanical performance will be evaluated through axial compression tests to determine the maximum force, deformation behaviour, and structural failure of MNs. Skin insertion performance will be assessed using Parafilm, artificial skin, and porcine skin, followed by microscopic observation, trypan blue staining, and measurement of insertion depth and penetration efficiency. Swelling and fluid-uptake behaviour will be investigated using phosphate buffered saline, artificial ISF, and agarose hydrogel by measuring mass changes, dimensional expansion, and swelling kinetics.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note:\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"用于组织间液采样的数字光处理（DLP）3D 打印水凝胶微针\"\n组织间液（ISF）是存在于组织细胞之间空隙中的体液，含有与人体生理与病理状态相关的多种生物标志物。ISF 中若干生物标志物的浓度与血液中的密切相关，使其成为疾病诊断、治疗监测与个性化医疗的有前景替代体液。与传统采血相比，ISF 采集可减少疼痛与组织损伤。然而，皮肤屏障（尤其角质层）使其难以快速、可靠地采集足量 ISF。微针技术为获取皮下 ISF 提供了微创途径。在所有类型微针（MN）中，水凝胶微针因插入皮肤后可通过溶胀吸收液体而对 ISF 采样尤其有吸引力；随后可从溶胀的水凝胶微针中回收 ISF，并通过比色、电化学或其他生物传感系统集成进行分析。水凝胶微针通常采用模具基制造技术生产。数字光处理（DLP）3D 打印为精确高效制造水凝胶微针提供了新途径：DLP 可实现可控几何形状与高分辨率的微针阵列快速生产，也便于方便地设计与制造不同微针尺寸与阵列排布。本项目聚焦用于 ISF 采样的 DLP 3D 打印水凝胶微针的设计与表征。将以主要由甲基丙烯酰化透明质酸（HAMA）与甲基丙烯酰化明胶（GelMA）组成的光交联复合水凝胶制造微针。HAMA 提供高吸水与溶胀能力，GelMA 有助于 3D 打印微针的结构完整性与力学性能。将使用不同 HAMA/GelMA 比例，确定可打印性、力学强度与 ISF 吸收能力之间的合适平衡。将使用光学显微镜与 SEM 检查 3D 打印微针的形貌、尺寸精度、针尖锐度与表面质量；通过轴向压缩试验评估力学性能，确定微针的最大力、变形行为与结构失效；使用石蜡膜、人工皮肤与猪皮评估刺入性能，随后进行显微观察、台盼蓝染色以及刺入深度与刺入效率测量；使用磷酸盐缓冲液、人工 ISF 与琼脂糖水凝胶，通过测量质量变化、尺寸膨胀与溶胀动力学，研究溶胀与液体吸收行为。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-12","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"不同几何形状微针力学性能的有限元分析","titleEn":"Finite Element Analysis for the Mechanical Performance of Microneedles with Different Geometries","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:39:10","descriptionText":"\"Finite Element Analysis for the Mechanical Performance of Microneedles with Different Geometries\"\nMicroneedles (MNs) are micron-scale needles that can penetrate the outermost layer of skin while minimizing contact with deeper pain receptors and blood vessels. They can be used for transdermal drug delivery, wearable biosensing, etc. Compared with conventional needles, MNs have many advantages, including minimally invasive operation, simplified sample collection, and potential for wearable devices. Successful skin penetration requires MNs to possess sufficient mechanical strength to overcome skin resistance without excessive bending, buckling, or fracture. The mechanical performance of MNs is strongly affected by their geometrical design. Parameters such as needle height, base width, tip radius, taper angle, aspect ratio, and cross-sectional shape can influence stress distribution and deformation during insertion. Experimental mechanical testing can evaluate the compression resistance and fracture behaviour of MNs. But fabricating and testing a large number of MN designs can be time-consuming and costly. Finite element analysis (FEA) provides an effective computational approach for predicting/revealing mechanical responses of MN under different loading conditions. It can calculate stress, strain, displacement, and critical buckling load before real MNs are fabricated. FEA therefore supports optimization of multiple MN designs while reducing experimental trials. This project focuses on the design and FEA of MNs with different geometries. Several representative MN structures, such as conical, triangular pyramidal, and quadrangular pyramidal geometries, will be investigated. The effects of individual geometrical parameters, including needle height, base width, tip radius, taper angle, and aspect ratio, will be studied through systematic parametric analysis. 3D MN models will be created first using computer-aided design (CAD) software. A consistent backing substrate will be included in each model to represent a complete MN patch. The geometrical models will then be imported into finite element analysis software (Abaqus, ANSYS, or COMSOL). Appropriate boundary and loading conditions will be established to simulate MN insertion. A simplified MN-skin interaction model may be developed to investigate MN penetration behaviour. Contact interactions will be defined between the MN surface and the skin model. The insertion force, tissue deformation, penetration depth, and MN stress will be compared for the different geometries. The results obtained in this project will establish relationships between microneedle geometry and mechanical performance.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note (if any, e.g. required/preferred academic background or skills of candidates, or incentives for attracting students):\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"不同几何形状微针力学性能的有限元分析\"\n微针（MN）是微米级针，可穿透皮肤最外层，同时最大限度减少与深层疼痛感受器与血管的接触，可用于透皮给药、可穿戴生物传感等。与传统针头相比，微针具有微创操作、简化样本采集与可穿戴设备潜力等许多优势。成功刺入皮肤要求微针具备足够力学强度以克服皮肤阻力，且不发生过度弯曲、屈曲或断裂。微针的力学性能受其几何设计强烈影响。针高、底部宽度、针尖半径、锥角、长径比与截面形状等参数可影响插入过程中的应力分布与变形。实验力学测试可评估微针的抗压性与断裂行为，但制造并测试大量微针设计耗时且昂贵。有限元分析（FEA）为预测/揭示微针在不同加载条件下的力学响应提供了有效计算途径，可在制造真实微针前计算应力、应变、位移与临界屈曲载荷，从而支持多种微针设计的优化并减少实验试次。本项目聚焦不同几何形状微针的设计与有限元分析。将研究圆锥、三角锥与四棱锥等几种代表性微针结构；通过系统参数化分析研究针高、底部宽度、针尖半径、锥角与长径比等各几何参数的影响。首先使用计算机辅助设计（CAD）软件创建三维微针模型；每个模型包含一致的基底以代表完整微针贴片。随后将几何模型导入有限元分析软件（Abaqus、ANSYS 或 COMSOL），建立合适的边界与加载条件模拟微针插入；可开发简化的微针-皮肤相互作用模型研究微针刺入行为，并在微针表面与皮肤模型之间定义接触交互。将比较不同几何形状的插入力、组织变形、刺入深度与微针应力。本项目所得结果将建立微针几何与力学性能之间的关系。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-13","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"用于组织间液采样的可溶胀水凝胶微针的设计、制备与评估","titleEn":"Design, Fabrication and Evaluation of Swellable Hydrogel Microneedles for Interstitial Fluid Samplin","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:39:29","descriptionText":"\"Design, Fabrication and Evaluation of Swellable Hydrogel Microneedles for Interstitial Fluid Sampling\"\nWearable biosensors have emerged as a promising platform for personalized healthcare because they can continuously collect physiological and biochemical information outside conventional clinical setting. Recent advances in flexible materials, miniaturized electronics, wireless communication, and electrochemical sensing have enabled wearable devices to monitor biomarkers in accessible biofluids and provide real-time information about an individual’s health. Among different biofluids, interstitial fluid (ISF) is particularly attractive because it surrounds tissue cells and contains various clinically relevant analytes, including glucose, lactate, electrolytes, metabolites, proteins, and drugs. The composition of ISF is closely associated with local tissue activity and systemic circulation, making it a valuable alternative to blood for minimally invasive biochemical monitoring. However, the stratum corneum in skin limits direct access to dermal ISF. Microneedles (MNs) provide an effective solution by creating microscale pathways through the skin barrier without penetrating deeply into blood vessels and major pain receptors, reducing discomfort and tissue damage of patients as compared to conventional hypodermic needles. Hydrogel MNs are very attractive because their composition, mechanical properties, fluid uptake, and dissolution behaviour can be adjusted for different applications. This project will investigate PVA/PVP MN arrays fabricated through projection micro stereolithography (PμSL) 3D printed resin positive molds and PDMS negative molds and systematically study the effects of hydrogel formulation and processing parameters on MN performance. MN arrays will be designed using computer-aided design software and fabricated as resin positive molds using a high-resolution PμSL 3D printer. PDMS will then be cast onto the resin master to obtain negative molds. PVA/PVP solutions with different compositions will be prepared and filled into PDMS molds. After drying and demolding, the MNs will be evaluated. The morphology and dimensional accuracy of MNs will be examined using an optical microscope and SEM. Mechanical performance will be evaluated through compression tests. Samples with 1010 MN array will be compressed to a maximum displacement of 0.5 mm, and force-displacement curves will be recorded to determine the fracture force, buckling behaviour and compressive modulus. Swelling and fluid-uptake behaviour will be studied using phosphate buffered saline, artificial ISF and agarose hydrogel. ISF extraction will be evaluated using porcine skin by measuring the mass difference of MN arrays before and after five minutes of extraction.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note (if any, e.g. required/preferred academic background or skills of candidates, or incentives for attracting students):\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"用于组织间液采样的可溶胀水凝胶微针的设计、制造与评估\"\n可穿戴生物传感器已成为个性化医疗的有前景平台，因其可在常规临床环境之外持续采集生理与生化信息。柔性材料、微型化电子、无线通信与电化学传感的最新进展，使可穿戴设备能够监测可及体液中的生物标志物，并提供个体健康状况的实时信息。在各类体液中，组织间液（ISF）尤其有吸引力：它环绕组织细胞，含有葡萄糖、乳酸、电解质、代谢物、蛋白质与药物等多种临床相关分析物。ISF 的成分与局部组织活动及体循环密切相关，使其成为微创生化监测中血液的宝贵替代。然而，皮肤角质层限制了直接获取真皮 ISF。微针（MN）通过在皮肤屏障上创建微尺度通路提供有效解决方案——不深入血管与主要疼痛感受器，相比传统皮下针头减少患者不适与组织损伤。水凝胶微针非常有吸引力，因其成分、力学性能、液体摄取与溶解行为均可针对不同应用调节。本项目将研究通过面投影微立体光刻（PμSL）3D 打印树脂正模与 PDMS 负模制造的 PVA/PVP 微针阵列，系统研究水凝胶配方与加工参数对微针性能的影响。将使用计算机辅助设计软件设计微针阵列，并用高分辨率 PμSL 3D 打印机制成树脂正模；随后在树脂母模上浇铸 PDMS 获得负模。将制备不同成分的 PVA/PVP 溶液并注入 PDMS 模具，干燥脱模后对微针进行评估。将使用光学显微镜与 SEM 检查微针形貌与尺寸精度；通过压缩试验评估力学性能——10×10 微针阵列样品压缩至最大位移 0.5mm，记录力-位移曲线以确定断裂力、屈曲行为与压缩模量。将使用磷酸盐缓冲液、人工 ISF 与琼脂糖水凝胶研究溶胀与液体摄取行为。将使用猪皮评估 ISF 提取，通过测量微针阵列提取前后 5 分钟的质量差确定。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-14","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于随机结构支架的计算机辅助设计、计算仿真与增材制造","titleEn":"Computer-aided Design, Computational Simulation, and Additive Manufacturing of Random Structure-base","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:39:45","descriptionText":"\"Computer-aided Design, Computational Simulation, and Additive Manufacturing of Random Structure-based Bone Tissue Engineering Scaffolds\"\nClinical treatments of critical bone defects using conventional methods, such as autografts and allografts, face challenges and bottlenecks including limited availability of donor tissue and risk of host immune rejection. Bone tissue engineering (BTE) provides a promising alternative to overcome these clinical problems. Over the past two decades, a vast array of BTE scaffolds has been developed featuring diverse porous structures, biomaterials, and manufacturing technologies. These porous scaffolds include designs such as strut-based lattices, triply periodic minimal surfaces (TPMS) lattices, and randomly distributed stochastic frameworks. Scaffold fabrication technologies are many, including freeze-drying, electrospinning, and additive manufacturing (AM, i.e., “3D printing”). Empirical research has shown that the internal porous structure of BTE scaffolds exerts a strong influence on their biological and mechanical properties. Natural human bone, particularly cancellous bone, is characteristically highly porous, consisting of stochastically oriented trabeculae and multi-directional interconnected channels. Therefore, designing BTE scaffolds that replicate the native bone structure is desirable. Random structure-based scaffolds, such as Voronoi structure-based scaffolds, have randomly distributed struts and interconnected pores that can well mimic human bones and are therefore promising as BTE scaffolds. Computer-aided design (CAD), a powerful platform for designing complex structures, enables precise modelling of Voronoi structure-based BTE scaffolds via contemporary computational algorithms. Furthermore, computational simulation allows thorough and accurate non-destructive characterization of mechanical behaviour and properties of CAD-designed BTE scaffolds through automated computer calculations. Among available manufacturing technologies, 3D printing stands out due to its superior capability to manufacture complex Voronoi structure-based BTE scaffolds with high precision and production efficiency. In this project, Voronoi structure-based BTE scaffolds will be designed using CAD with different strut diameters and porosities. Subsequently, the mechanical behaviour and properties of these Voronoi structure-based scaffold models under compression loads will be simulated using software such as Ansys, Abaqus, COMSOL, nTop, SolidWorks, or other software with simulation function. Thereafter, BTE scaffolds will be produced via fused deposition modelling (FDM) 3D printing using poly(lactic acid) (PLA) according to scaffold designs. The 3D printed BTE scaffolds will be subjected to compression tests to validate simulation results.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note (if any, e.g. required/preferred academic background or skills of candidates, or incentives for attracting students):\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"随机结构骨组织工程支架的计算机辅助设计、计算仿真与增材制造\"\n利用自体骨与异体骨等常规方法对严重骨缺损的临床治疗面临挑战与瓶颈，包括供体组织有限与宿主免疫排斥风险。骨组织工程（BTE）为克服这些临床问题提供了有前景的替代方案。过去二十年，已开发出大量具有多样多孔结构、生物材料与制造技术的 BTE 支架。这些多孔支架包括支柱晶格、三周期极小曲面（TPMS）晶格与随机分布的随机框架等设计。支架制造技术众多，包括冷冻干燥、静电纺丝与增材制造（AM，即\"3D 打印\"）。实证研究表明，BTE 支架的内部多孔结构对其生物学与力学性能有强烈影响。天然人体骨，尤其松质骨，以高度多孔为特征，由随机取向的骨小梁与多向互连通道构成。因此，设计复现天然骨结构的 BTE 支架是可取的。随机结构支架（如基于 Voronoi 结构的支架）具有随机分布的支柱与互连孔隙，能很好模拟人体骨，因此有望作为 BTE 支架。计算机辅助设计（CAD）是设计复杂结构的强大平台，可通过现代计算算法对基于 Voronoi 结构的 BTE 支架进行精确建模。此外，计算仿真可通过自动化计算机计算对 CAD 设计的 BTE 支架的力学行为与性质进行全面、准确的无损表征。在现有制造技术中，3D 打印因其以高精度与高生产效率制造复杂 Voronoi 结构 BTE 支架的卓越能力而脱颖而出。本项目中，将使用 CAD 设计具有不同支柱直径与孔隙率的 Voronoi 结构 BTE 支架；随后使用 Ansys、Abaqus、COMSOL、nTop、SolidWorks 或其他具仿真功能的软件，仿真这些支架模型在压缩载荷下的力学行为与性质；之后按支架设计，通过熔融沉积建模（FDM）3D 打印工艺使用聚乳酸（PLA）制造 BTE 支架；对 3D 打印支架进行压缩试验以验证仿真结果。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-15","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"强度增强的随机结构支架的计算机辅助设计、仿真与增材制造","titleEn":"Computer-aided Design, Simulation, and Additive Manufacturing of Strength-enhanced Random Structure-","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:39:59","descriptionText":"\"Computer-aided Design, Simulation, and Additive Manufacturing of Strength-enhanced Random Structure-based Scaffolds for Bone Regeneration\"\nOver the past few decades, bone tissue engineering (BTE) has made significant progresses for treating bone tissue defects. Artificial BTE scaffolds show great potential for clinical applications. Different BTE scaffolds have been developed featuring diverse materials and structures. These structures include hollow, fibrous, spongy, strut-based lattices, triply periodic minimal surfaces (TPMS) lattices, and randomly distributed structures. Research has indicated that the internal porous structure of BTE scaffolds can greatly influence their biological and mechanical properties, particularly the tissue ingrowth depth and mechanical strength. Human bone tissue, especially cancellous bone, is naturally porous with interconnected channels and high porosity levels of 50 - 90%. In addition, most human bones are anisotropic for bearing different loads in different directions. For instance, femur withstands the highest compressive loads in the longitudinal direction. Therefore, designing BTE scaffolds that closely resemble the structure of native bone is highly preferred. However, traditional design and fabrication methods cannot create these complex biomimetic scaffolds. Computer-aided design (CAD) provides the solution to designing intricate biomimetic BTE scaffolds using modern computational techniques. Also, it is still challenging to evaluate mechanical properties of anisotropic scaffolds through physical experiments due to their structural complexity. Computational simulation may accurately assess and visualize mechanical behaviours of BTE scaffolds through automated computer calculations. For BTE scaffold fabrication, 3D printing is exceptional for its ability to produce complex scaffolds with high precision and efficiency. The integration of CAD, simulation, and 3D printing can expediate BTE scaffold development. In this project, longitudinally elongated random structure-based BTE scaffolds with different elongated factors will be designed using CAD to mimic the structure of natural bone tissue. Subsequently, the mechanical and fluidic behaviors/properties of these CAD-designed scaffold models will be subjected to computational simulation using software such as Ansys, Abaqus, COMSOL, or SolidWorks. Thereafter, the CAD-designed scaffolds will be produced via fused deposition modelling (FDM) 3D printing using poly(lactic acid). The 3D printed scaffolds will undergo compression tests to validate the simulation results.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note (if any, e.g. required/preferred academic background or skills of candidates, or incentives for attracting students):\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"强度增强的随机结构支架在骨再生中的计算机辅助设计、仿真与增材制造\"\n过去几十年，骨组织工程（BTE）在治疗骨组织缺损方面取得重大进展。人工 BTE 支架在临床应用上潜力巨大。研究人员已开发出采用不同材料与结构的多种 BTE 支架，包括中空、纤维、海绵状、支柱晶格、三周期极小曲面（TPMS）晶格与随机分布结构等。研究表明，BTE 支架的内部多孔结构可极大影响其生物学与力学性能，尤其是组织长入深度与力学强度。人体骨组织，尤其松质骨，天然多孔，具有互连通道，孔隙率高达 50%~90%。此外，人体多数骨骼具有各向异性，以承受不同方向的载荷。例如股骨在纵向承受最大压载。因此，设计结构与天然骨组织高度相似的 BTE 支架非常可取。然而，传统设计与制造方法无法制造这些复杂仿生支架。计算机辅助设计（CAD）利用现代计算技术为设计精细仿生 BTE 支架提供了解决方案。同时，由于其结构复杂性，通过物理实验评估各向异性支架的力学性能仍具挑战；计算仿真可通过自动化计算机计算准确评估与可视化 BTE 支架的力学行为。在支架制造方面，3D 打印因其高精度、高效率制造复杂支架的能力而尤为突出。CAD、仿真与 3D 打印的集成可加速 BTE 支架开发。本项目中，将使用 CAD 设计不同伸长系数的纵向伸长随机结构 BTE 支架，以模拟天然骨组织结构；随后使用 Ansys、Abaqus、COMSOL 或 SolidWorks 等软件对 CAD 设计的支架模型进行力学与流体行为的计算仿真；之后通过熔融沉积建模（FDM）3D 打印工艺，使用聚乳酸制造 CAD 设计的支架；打印支架将进行压缩试验以验证仿真结果。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-MEMWANG-16","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"通过计算机辅助设计开发基于异质随机结构的骨组织工程支架","titleEn":"Developing Heterogeneous Random Structure-based Bone Tissue Engineering Scaffolds through Computer-a","areaZh":"生物医学工程；设计与制造；材料与固体","areaEn":"Biomedical engineering; Design & manufacture; Materials & solids.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor WANG Min","moderator":"","capacity":1,"availCap":1,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 18:40:13","descriptionText":"\"Developing Heterogeneous Random Structure-based Bone Tissue Engineering Scaffolds through Computer-aided Design, Computational Simulation, and Additive Manufacturing\"\nBone tissue engineering (BTE) is now considered an effective approach for treating difficult problems in bone tissue loss. Artificial BTE scaffolds show great potential for clinical applications. Numerous studies have shown that the internal porous structure of BTE scaffolds can greatly influence their biological and mechanical properties, particularly the tissue ingrowth depth and mechanical strength. In addition, native healthy cancellous bone is inherently heterogeneous and highly porous with interconnected pores. It is of great significance to design BTE scaffolds with appropriate porous structures to mimic structures of native bone. Conventional design methods are unable to biomimic porous structures due to the structural complexity and limited design capability. Computer-aided design (CAD) now provides an outstanding platform for designing biomimicking BTE scaffolds through computational design softwares. BTE scaffolds with different structures have been designed via CAD, including strut-based lattice structures (BCC, FCC, etc.), triply periodic minimal surface (TPMS) lattice structures (Gyroid, Primitive, Diamond, etc.), and random-based structures such as Voronoi-based structures. Due to structural heterogeneity of human bone, the CAD-designed scaffold design should be heterogeneous with functionally graded pores. Furthermore, it is a challenge to evaluate mechanical and fluidic behaviours of biomimicking BTE scaffolds due to their intricate porous structures. Computational simulation can be applied to simulate the mechanical and fluidic behaviours/properties of complex scaffolds. Also, computational simulation can reduce trial-and-error experimental processes during scaffold development. On the other hand, experimental validation for simulation results is essential. In this project, CAD software (such as SolidWorks, MathMod, Grasshopper, Mathematica, nTopology, or other CAD software) will be used to design heterogeneous Voronoi structure-based BTE scaffolds with specific porosity gradient. Subsequently, mechanical and fluidic behaviours and properties of CAD-designed BTE scaffolds will be subjected to simulation using simulation software such as Ansys, Abaqus, COMSOL, or SolidWorks. Afterwards, the designed scaffolds will be fabricated via fused deposition modelling (FDM) 3D printing using poly(lactic acid) (PLA). Compression and permeability experiments will be conducted for 3D printed scaffolds, with their results being compared to simulation results.\nPre- and co-requisites:\nHaving taken/completed materials courses in the undergraduate study or MSc study.\nSpecial note (if any, e.g. required/preferred academic background or skills of candidates, or incentives for attracting students):\nProject Interim Assessment: before Thursday Feb. 4, 2027\nDissertation Submission to ME Dept.: before May 1, 2027","descZh":"\"通过计算机辅助设计、计算仿真与增材制造开发异质随机结构骨组织工程支架\"\n骨组织工程（BTE）现被视为治疗骨组织缺损难题的有效途径。人工 BTE 支架在临床应用上潜力巨大。大量研究表明，BTE 支架的内部多孔结构可极大影响其生物学与力学性能，尤其是组织长入深度与力学强度。此外，天然健康松质骨本身异质且高度多孔，具有互连孔隙。设计具有合适多孔结构、能够模拟天然骨结构的 BTE 支架意义重大。传统设计方法因结构复杂与设计能力有限而无法仿生模拟多孔结构。计算机辅助设计（CAD）现通过计算设计软件为设计仿生 BTE 支架提供了卓越平台。已通过 CAD 设计出不同结构的 BTE 支架，包括支柱晶格结构（BCC、FCC 等）、三周期极小曲面（TPMS）晶格结构（Gyroid、Primitive、Diamond 等）以及基于 Voronoi 的随机结构等。由于人体骨结构异质，CAD 设计的支架应具有功能梯度孔隙的异质性。此外，因其复杂多孔结构，评估仿生 BTE 支架的力学与流体行为颇具挑战。计算仿真可用于模拟复杂支架的力学与流体行为/性质，并可减少支架开发中的试错实验过程。另一方面，对仿真结果进行实验验证必不可少。本项目中，将使用 CAD 软件（如 SolidWorks、MathMod、Grasshopper、Mathematica、nTopology 或其他 CAD 软件）设计具有特定孔隙率梯度的异质 Voronoi 结构 BTE 支架；随后使用 Ansys、Abaqus、COMSOL 或 SolidWorks 等仿真软件对 CAD 设计的 BTE 支架进行力学与流体行为/性质仿真；之后通过熔融沉积建模（FDM）3D 打印工艺，使用聚乳酸（PLA）制造设计好的支架；对 3D 打印支架开展压缩与渗透实验，并将结果与仿真结果比较。\n先修/并修要求：在本科或硕士阶段修完材料类课程。\n特别说明：项目中期评估：2027 年 2 月 4 日（周四）前；论文提交至机械系：2027 年 5 月 1 日前。","cats":["BIO","DES","MAT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-WEIYAN-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向可扩展项目式学习（PBL）的 AI 辅助项目指导平台","titleEn":"AI-Assisted Project Supervising Platform for Scalable Project-Based Learning (PBL)","areaZh":"教育人工智能；大语言模型与生成式 AI；人类-AI 交互","areaEn":"Artificial Intelligence in Education; Large Language Models and Generative AI; Human–AI Intera","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Project-Based Learning (PBL) is a highly effective teaching method that enhances motivation, bridges theory with practice, and nurtures critical 21st-century skills such as self-directed learning, communication, collaboration, and problem-solving. However, the successful application of PBL requires significant instructor effort in supervision, feedback, and resource management. While feasible in small classes, scaling PBL to large classes with more than 100 students presents serious challenges, especially in managing multiple teams, organizing counseling meetings, and providing timely feedback.\nThis project aims to design and develop an AI-assisted platform to support scalable project supervision in large classes. The platform will provide intelligent assistance by:\n• Guiding students through their project milestones.\n• Answering basic queries using data sources and course content.\n• Offering preliminary feedback before instructor evaluation.\n• Assisting instructors in managing teams, scheduling, and feedback logistics.\nThe outcome will be a prototype platform that enhances both student learning \nSkills required\nThe project requires a multidisciplinary team with skills in design for user experience, education for pedagogy, computer science for AI and development, and engineering for system architecture and scalability.","descZh":"项目式学习（PBL）是一种非常有效的教学方法，能提升学习动机、连接理论与实践，并培养自主学习、沟通、协作与问题解决等 21 世纪关键技能。然而，PBL 的成功应用需要教师投入大量精力进行指导、反馈与资源管理。在小班教学中可行，但将 PBL 扩展到超过 100 人的大班则面临严峻挑战，尤其在管理多个团队、组织辅导会议与提供及时反馈方面。\n本项目旨在设计并开发一个 AI 辅助平台，支持大班中的可扩展项目指导。平台将提供以下智能辅助：\n• 引导学生在项目各里程碑上取得进展；\n• 利用数据源与课程内容回答基础问题；\n• 在教师评估前提供初步反馈；\n• 协助教师管理团队、日程与反馈事务。\n成果将是一个能提升学生学习效果的原型平台。\n所需技能：本项目需要一个多学科团队，具备用户体验设计、教育学、面向 AI 与开发的计算机科学，以及面向系统架构与可扩展性的工程技能。","cats":["AI"]},{"ref":"2026-27-WEIYAN-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"AI 时代的工程人才：重新思考工程教育的未来","titleEn":"Engineering Talent in the AI Era: Rethinking the Future of Engineering Education","areaZh":"工程教育；AI 与未来劳动力；产学合作","areaEn":"Engineering Education; AI and Future Workforce; Industry–Academia Collaboration","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Artificial intelligence is rapidly transforming engineering practice and reshaping the capabilities expected of future engineers. This project investigates how industry talent requirements are changing in the AI era and how engineering education should respond.\nStudents will analyze literature, industry reports, job postings, and workforce data, and engage directly with selected industries through interviews and company visits. The study will identify emerging capabilities, changing expectations for entry-level engineers, and potential gaps between current engineering education and industry needs.\nBased on the findings, students will propose a future engineering education model covering areas such as AI integration, curriculum design, project-based learning, interdisciplinary education, and industry engagement.\nPreferred skills: Research and literature review; basic data analysis; survey/interview and qualitative research skills; familiarity with AI tools; strong communication, critical thinking, and interpersonal skills.","descZh":"人工智能正快速改变工程实践，并重塑对未来工程师能力的期望。本项目调研在 AI 时代行业人才需求如何变化，以及工程教育应如何应对。\n学生将分析文献、行业报告、招聘信息与劳动力数据，并通过访谈与企业走访直接接触选定行业。研究将识别新兴能力、对初级工程师期望的变化，以及当前工程教育与行业需求之间的潜在差距。\n基于研究结论，学生将提出一种面向未来的工程教育模式，涵盖 AI 融合、课程设计、项目式学习、跨学科教育与产业合作等方向。\n偏好技能：研究与文献综述；基础数据分析；问卷/访谈与质性研究方法；熟悉 AI 工具；较强的沟通、批判性思维与人际交往能力。","cats":["AI","MGT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-WEIYAN-3","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向机器人设计的模块化机构与动力传动教学套件","titleEn":"Modular Mechanism and Power-Transmission Teaching Kit for Robotics Design","areaZh":"工程设计；机器人与机电一体化；工程教育","areaEn":"Engineering Design; Robotics and Mechatronics; Engineering Education","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Hands-on learning is essential for helping students connect mechanical design theory with real robotic systems. This project aims to design and develop a modular teaching kit for mechanism design and power transmission to support project-based learning in robotics design.\nThe student will develop a configurable physical platform that allows learners to assemble, modify, and compare different mechanisms and transmission systems, such as four-bar and slider-crank mechanisms, legged locomotion mechanisms, gears, belts, and chains. These topics directly support the mechanism, motion generation, and power-transmission components of the Integrated Robotics Design Project course.\nThe project will involve learning-needs analysis, mechanical design, CAD, fabrication, prototype testing, and development of associated teaching activities. The final prototype will be evaluated and iteratively improved based on its usability, technical performance, and effectiveness in supporting students' understanding and design exploration.\nPreferred skills: Mechanical design, CAD, prototyping and manufacturing; basic knowledge of mechanisms and robotics; interest in hands-on learning and engineering education.","descZh":"动手学习对于帮助学生将机械设计理论与真实机器人系统联系起来至关重要。本项目旨在设计并开发一套用于机构设计与动力传动的模块化教学套件，以支持机器人设计中的项目式学习。\n学生将开发一个可配置的实物平台，使学习者能够组装、修改并比较不同的机构与传动系统，如四杆机构、曲柄滑块机构、腿式运动机构、齿轮、带传动与链传动。这些主题直接支撑《机器人综合设计项目》课程中的机构、运动生成与动力传动部分。\n项目将涉及学习需求分析、机械设计、CAD、加工制造、原型测试及相关教学活动的开发。最终原型将根据其可用性、技术性能以及在支持学生理解与设计探索方面的有效性进行评估与迭代改进。\n偏好技能：机械设计、CAD、原型制作与制造；机构学与机器人学基础知识；对手动学习与工程教育有兴趣。","cats":["DES","MGT","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-WEIYAN-4","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"能源与发电技术动手教学原型开发","titleEn":"Development of Hands-on Teaching Prototypes for Energy and Power Generation Technologies","areaZh":"能源工程；发电与能量转换；可再生能源；工程教育","areaEn":"Energy Engineering; Power Generation and Energy Conversion; Renewable Energy; Engineering Education","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"Energy generation and conversion involve many concepts that can be difficult to understand through lectures alone, particularly for undergraduate students from diverse academic backgrounds. This project aims to design and develop a series of modular, hands-on teaching prototypes that help students explore the principles, performance, and environmental implications of conventional and emerging energy technologies.\nThe student will investigate learning needs and develop prototypes covering selected topics such as fossil-fuel-based power generation, thermal energy conversion, solar, wind, hydrogen, energy storage, and other emerging energy technologies. The prototypes should enable students to observe, measure, compare, and experiment with key energy concepts such as energy conversion, efficiency, losses, emissions, and system trade-offs.\nIn addition to supporting conceptual learning, the prototypes will be designed as an experimental platform for student projects, allowing students to conduct measurements, test assumptions, compare technologies, and develop proof-of-concepts for their semester-long energy projects. This directly supports the course expectation that students validate important technical assumptions through experiments, measurements, simulations, or small physical demonstrations.\nThe project will involve educational needs analysis, prototype design, fabrication, instrumentation, testing, development of learning activities, and evaluation with target users.\nPreferred skills: Energy and thermofluids; mechanical/electrical design; CAD and prototyping; sensors and data acquisition; experimental testing; interest in hands-on learning and engineering education.","descZh":"能源产生与转换涉及许多仅靠讲授难以理解的概念，尤其对学术背景多样的本科生而言。本项目旨在设计并开发一系列模块化、可动手操作的教学原型，帮助学生探索传统与新兴能源技术的原理、性能与环境影响。\n学生将调研学习需求，并围绕化石燃料发电、热能转换、太阳能、风能、氢能、储能及其他新兴能源技术等主题开发原型。原型应能让学生观察、测量、比较并实验能量转换、效率、损耗、排放与系统权衡等关键能源概念。\n除支持概念学习外，原型还将设计为学生项目的实验平台，使学生能够开展测量、检验假设、比较技术，并为其为期一学期的能源项目开发概念验证。这直接支撑课程对学生通过实验、测量、仿真或小型实物演示验证重要技术假设的期望。\n项目将涉及教育需求分析、原型设计、制造、仪器化、测试、学习活动开发以及与目标用户评估。\n偏好技能：能源与热流体；机械/电气设计；CAD 与原型制作；传感器与数据采集；实验测试；对动手学习与工程教育有兴趣。","cats":["ENE","MGT"]},{"ref":"2026-27-WEIYAN-5","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向机器人的模块化机电一体化与控制教学平台","titleEn":"Modular Mechatronics and Control Teaching Platform for Robotics","areaZh":"机电一体化；控制系统；机器人；嵌入式系统；工程教育","areaEn":"Mechatronics; Control Systems; Robotics; Embedded Systems; Engineering Education.","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Dr. WEI Yan","moderator":"","capacity":4,"availCap":4,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-04 15:20:38","descriptionText":"This project aims to investigate effective approaches for teaching fundamental mechatronics, control, and robotics concepts and to design a modular hands-on teaching platform based on the findings.\nThe project will first examine student learning needs, common difficulties, and suitable teaching approaches through literature review, benchmarking, and user feedback. Based on this investigation, the team will design and develop a modular robotic platform integrating actuators, sensors, embedded controllers, mechanical components, and real-time data acquisition.\nThe platform will support learning activities in areas such as actuation and sensing, system modeling, system identification, feedback control, PID control, and trajectory tracking. The project will also evaluate the usability and learning effectiveness of the developed platform and iteratively improve both the hardware and associated teaching activities.\nPreferred Skills: A team with complementary skills in mechatronics, control, robotics, mechanical/electronic design, programming, prototyping, and engineering education research.","descZh":"本项目旨在研究教授基础机电一体化、控制和机器人学概念的有效方法，并根据研究结果设计一个模块化的动手实践教学平台。\n本项目将首先通过文献综述、基准对比（benchmarking）和用户反馈，考察学生的学习需求、常见困难以及合适的教学方法。基于这项调查，团队将设计并开发一个模块化机器人平台，集成执行器、传感器、嵌入式控制器、机械部件和实时数据采集。\n该平台将支持驱动与传感、系统建模、系统辨识、反馈控制、PID 控制和轨迹跟踪等领域的教学活动。本项目还将评估所开发平台的可用性和学习效果，并迭代改进硬件及相关的教学活动。\n优先技能：一支在机电一体化、控制、机器人学、机械/电子设计、编程、原型制作和工程教育研究方面具有互补技能的团队。","cats":["CTRL","ELE","MGT","ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIXF-11","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"基于超材料编码与压缩感知的单传感器结构健康监测","titleEn":"Single-Sensor Structural Health Monitoring via Metamaterial Encoding and Compressive Sensing","areaZh":"机械设计；超材料；传感技术","areaEn":"Mechanical Design; Metamaterial; Sensing Technology","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Xiaofan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-05 18:34:01","descriptionText":"ow-altitude vehicles demand ultra-light, low-complexity health monitoring. This project encodes structural vibration into anisotropic wavefields using metamaterials, enabling damage localization and state estimation from a single sensor. The student will:\n Design and model anisotropic metamaterial panels that steer and compress vibration information. \n Develop compressive sensing and sparse reconstruction algorithms to recover damage features from single-sensor data. \n Validate on lab-scale structures (plates/frames) with controlled defects and benchmark against array-based SHM. \n Deliver a lightweight SHM pipeline with clear metrics (detection accuracy, sensitivity, false alarm rate).","descZh":"低空飞行器需要超轻量、低复杂度的健康监测手段。本项目利用超材料将结构振动编码为各向异性波场，实现仅凭单个传感器即可完成损伤定位与状态估计。学生将：\n- 设计与建模可引导并压缩振动信息的各向异性超材料面板；\n- 开发压缩感知与稀疏重构算法，从单传感器数据中恢复损伤特征；\n- 在带受控缺陷的实验室尺度结构（板/框架）上验证，并与基于阵列的结构健康监测（SHM）进行对比；\n- 交付一套轻量化 SHM 流程，并给出明确的指标（检测准确率、灵敏度、误报率）。","cats":["MAT","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIXF-12","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向低空飞行器的仿猫头鹰气动噪声与振动抑制","titleEn":"Owl-Inspired Aeroacoustic Noise and Vibration Reduction for Low-Altitude Vehicles","areaZh":"机械设计；超材料","areaEn":"Mechanical Design; Metamaterial","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Xiaofan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-05 18:34:01","descriptionText":"Inspired by owls’ silent flight, this project explores bioinspired geometries to reduce aeroacoustic noise and vibration in rotors/wings. The student will:\n Derive and implement features such as serrated trailing edges, leading-edge combs, and porous/feather-like textures.\n Conduct CFD and aeroacoustic simulations (e.g., LES + FW-H) to predict tonal/broadband noise.\n Fabricate 3D-printed prototypes and test on a rotor rig or wind-tunnel setup.\n Quantify performance (SPL reduction, efficiency impact, structural response) and propose design guidelines.","descZh":"本项目受猫头鹰无声飞行启发，探索仿生几何结构以降低旋翼/机翼的气动噪声与振动。学生将：\n- 推导并实现锯齿状后缘、前缘梳齿以及多孔/羽状织构等特征；\n- 开展 CFD 与气动声学仿真（如 LES + FW-H），预测离散（音调）噪声与宽带噪声；\n- 制作 3D 打印原型，并在旋翼试验台或风洞装置上进行测试；\n- 量化性能表现（声压级 SPL 的降低、效率影响、结构响应），并提出设计指南。","cats":["MAT","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-LIXF-13","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向智能轻量化低空飞行器的 AI 驱动超结构设计","titleEn":"AI-Driven Metastructure Design for Smart and Lightweight Low-Altitude Vehicles","areaZh":"机械设计；超结构；AI 驱动设计","areaEn":"Mechanical Design; Metastructure; AI-Driven Design","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"Professor LI Xiaofan","moderator":"","capacity":5,"availCap":5,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","MEST","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-09-05 18:34:01","descriptionText":"This project integrates AI-driven optimization with metastructures to achieve lightweight, high-strength, low-noise designs that are aware of impacts and vibrations. The student will:\n Build surrogate models for structure–aeroacoustics–vibration responses.\n Perform multi-objective optimization (mass, stiffness, damping, noise) and generative/topology design for metastructures.\n Integrate embedded sensing (impact self-sensing, vibration monitoring) into the structural concept.\n Validate on FE models and prototypes, reporting gains in weight, strength, vibro-acoustic performance, and sensing fidelity.","descZh":"本项目将 AI 驱动的优化与超材料（metastructures）相结合，实现兼顾抗冲击与减振的轻量化、高强度、低噪声设计。学生将：\n- 为结构-气动声学-振动响应构建代理模型；\n- 开展多目标优化（质量、刚度、阻尼、噪声）及超材料的生成式/拓扑设计；\n- 将嵌入式传感（冲击自感知、振动监测）集成到结构方案中；\n- 在有限元模型与原型上进行验证，报告在重量、强度、振动声学性能与传感保真度方面的收益。","cats":["DES","MAT","MECH"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_SSHUANG-1","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"仿生胶囊机器人设计","titleEn":"Design of Biomimetic Capsule Robots","areaZh":"机器人","areaEn":"Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"SONG Shuang","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 19:04:01","descriptionText":"Capsule robots have significant advantages and potential in full intestinal diagnosis and treatment, enabling functions that traditional colonoscopy cannot achieve. However, existing capsule robots lack effective active locomotion and diagnostic/treatment capabilities. From a bionic perspective, this project explores design methods for capsule functions such as active locomotion, drug delivery, and sampling, providing design references for efficient capsule-based diagnosis and treatment.","descZh":"胶囊机器人在全肠道诊疗方面具有显著优势与潜力，可实现传统结肠镜检查无法完成的功能。然而，现有胶囊机器人缺乏有效的主动运动以及诊疗能力。本项目从仿生视角出发，探索胶囊在主动运动、药物递送、取样等功能的实现方法，为高效的胶囊式诊疗提供设计参考。","cats":["ROB"]},{"ref":"2026-27-_SSHUANG-2","type":"IP","typeLabel":"导师个人项目","mode":"SM","titleZh":"面向狭窄腔道的手术机器人","titleEn":"Surgical Robots for Narrow Lumens","areaZh":"机器人","areaEn":"Robotics","companyZh":"","companyEn":"","companySupervisor":"","univSupName":"SONG Shuang","moderator":"","capacity":3,"availCap":3,"isFull":false,"programs":["ME","IDT"],"hasPdf":true,"lastModified":"2026-08-31 19:04:01","descriptionText":"Minimally invasive surgical robots designed for operations in narrow lumens can achieve efficient surgical procedures with advantages such as less trauma and faster recovery. This project addresses the requirements of specific surgical procedures, designs corresponding surgical robot structures, explores motion planning methods within narrow lumens, and studies teleoperation and multi-arm coordination, providing theoretical and technical support for minimally invasive surgeries.","descZh":"面向狭窄管腔内手术设计的微创手术机器人，可实现高效的手术流程，具有创伤更小、恢复更快等优势。本项目针对特定手术流程的需求，设计相应的手术机器人结构，探索狭窄管腔内的运动规划方法，并研究遥操作与多臂协调，为微创手术提供理论和技术支持。","cats":["ROB"]}]};
